                            +\n                                                                                    +\n > The Law of Faith Must Be Obeyed                                                  +\n                                                                                    +\n > “If It Be Thy Will” Should Be Forgotten                                          +\n                                                                                    +\n > Will Men Die if God Always Heals Them?                                           +\n                                                                                    +\n > What is Included in Christ’s Will and Testament?                                 +\n                                                                                    +\n > No Excuse for Ignorance of God’s Will                                            +\n                                                                                    +\n > Everyone Can Have All That is Promised                                           +\n                                                                                    +\n > All Answers to Prayer Are for God’s Glory                                        +\n                                                                                    +\n > What is God’s Desire for Men?                                                    +\n                                                                                    +\n > Who Has Been Authorized to Change God’s Will?                                    +\n                                                                                    +\n > Both Testaments Promise the Same Blessings                                       +\n                                                                                    +\n > We Have Exceeding Great and Precious Promises                                    +\n                                                                                    +\n > Common Sense Must Be Used                                                        +\n                                                                                    +\n > Ten Questions For Supplement Twenty-five. Do You Know:                           +\n                                                                                    +\n Lesson 51: The New Heaven and the New Earth                                        +\n                                                                                    +\n > I. The Dispensation of the Redeemed                                              +\n                                                                                    +\n > II. The New Heaven and the New Earth (Rev. 21:1–22:5)                            +\n                                                                                    +\n > III. New Lands (Rev. 21:1)                                                       +\n                                                                                    +\n > IV. The New Jerusalem (Rev. 21:2–22:4)                                           +\n                                                                                    +\n > V. The New Peoples (Rev. 21:3-4, 24-26)                                          +\n                                                                                    +\n > VI. New Conditions in the New Earth (Rev. 21:3-8)                                +\n                                                                                    +\n > VII. “Behold, I Make All Things New” (Rev. 21:5)                                 +\n                                                                                    +\n > VIII. The Full Restitution of All Things (Acts 3:21)                             +\n                                                                                    +\n > IX. Eternity Merely the Continuation of Time                                     +\n                                                                                    +\n > X. No Perfect Age Before the New Earth                                           +\n                                                                                    +\n > Questions on Lesson Fifty-one                                                    +\n                                                                                    +\n Lesson 52: The Bride of Christ                                                     +\n                                                                                    +\n > I. Is the Bride of Christ Israel?                                                +\n                                                                                    +\n > II. Is the Bride of Christ Only a Part of the Church?                            +\n                                                                                    +\n > III. Is the Bride of Christ Only the New Testament Church?                       +\n                                                                                    +\n > IV. Is the New Testament Church Now Married to Christ?                           +\n                                                                                    +\n > V. What is the Bride of Christ?                                                  +\n                                                                                    +\n > Questions on Lesson Fifty-two\n\n---\n\nYou now have a complete map of Scripture — dispensations, covenants, prophecies, doctrines, all in their proper place in God's unfolding plan. But a map is only as valuable as the journey it guides. So here's the final question this course leaves you with: Of everything you've studied — fifty-two lessons of God's eternal plan — what is the one truth that has most changed the way you understand yourself, your relationship with God, and what He's asking of you today?	\N	\N	\N
cmq76y46e0001w0ylpqtowclk	cmq76xgul000jhks877ughys4	Why I Wrote This Part of the Course	READING	\N	0	\N	t	![Why I Wrote This Part of the Course](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsUMeYxi6edALvbYMWXfx7ZoQswlOpr26nuJ1t)\n\n## Why I Wrote This Part of the Course\n\nI am not writing this section as a researcher. I am writing it as someone who needed it.\n\nThere was a period in my life — late teens into my early twenties — when I was genuinely curious about the spiritual world, and the church I grew up in had no language for it. Nobody sat me down and explained that the curiosity was legitimate, that the spiritual world was real, and that the doorways I was opening were documented in Scripture by a God who had specifically prohibited them. Nobody explained the difference between encountering the living God and encountering the entities that masquerade as Him. So I did what curious people do when they have no guide: I explored.\n\nI read the books. I experimented with the practices. I had experiences that I could not dismiss — experiences that felt genuinely spiritual, genuinely illuminating, genuinely larger than my ordinary life. That was the most dangerous part. The experiences were real. They were just coming from the wrong source.\n\nIt took years before I understood what I had been in contact with. It took longer before I understood why certain patterns in my life — patterns of oppression, of specific fears, of a persistent sense that something was wrong that I could not name — were downstream of those years of exploration. And it took serious, specific, Spirit-directed prayer before those patterns broke.\n\nI wrote the 44 lessons in this Part of the course because I have been to enough of these places to know what they feel like from the inside. I wrote them because I have watched people I love spend years in bondage to systems they entered innocently and could not find their way out of. I wrote them because the church I grew up in — well-meaning, genuinely loving — did not give me the information that could have protected me, and I do not want that to be your story.\n\n---\n\n## What Part VII Covers and Why the Order Matters\n\nThe 44 lessons in this Part are organized into seven progressive waves. They move from the accidental to the deliberate, from the cultural to the covenantal, from the individual to the systemic. They escalate for a reason.\n\n**The progression is designed so that by the time you reach the most serious material, you have the theological framework to understand what you're looking at.**\n\nHere is the map:\n\n| Wave | Theme | Lessons |\n|------|-------|---------|\n| **Wave 1** | Cultural Entry Points | Halloween, Occult Entertainment, Music Industry, Celebrity/Political Idolatry, Astrology, Digital Divination |\n| **Wave 2** | The Wellness Trojan Horse | Yoga, Crystals, Law of Attraction, TM/Mindfulness, Feng Shui, Reiki, Chakras, The Enneagram, Hypnosis, Smudging |\n| **Wave 3** | Spiritual Seeking Gone Wrong | New Age, Spirit Guides, Contemplative Prayer, Labyrinths, Paranormal Investigation, Psychedelics, Astral Projection |\n| **Wave 4** | Active Occult Practice | Tarot, Psychics, Ouija, The Occult, Witchcraft, Wicca, Santería, Occult Tattoos, Idols in the Home |\n| **Wave 5** | Covenant Traps | Freemasonry, Curses, Generational Curses, False Gospels |\n| **Wave 6** | Personal Bondages | Adultery, Pornography, Self-Harm, Eating Disorders |\n| **Wave 7** | End-Times Systemic Deceptions | Aliens/UFOs, Burning Man, Transhumanism, AI Spiritual Deception |\n\nEach wave ends with a short checkpoint quiz. Between Wave 7 and the Final Quiz, you will find two supplements: the theological framework that explains why all of these attacks work (Legal Ground), and the complete prayer guide for closing every door this course has identified.\n\n---\n\n## How to Use This Part of the Course\n\nWork through the lessons in order. The sequence is intentional. Do not skip waves because the topic seems irrelevant to you personally — the lesson may be the key to understanding someone you love, or to recognizing something in your family history you have not yet named.\n\nAt the end of each lesson, there is a Community Discussion question. Engage with it honestly. The discussion prompts are designed to be specific enough to generate real conversation and safe enough to share in community. The enemy's primary tool in these areas is secrecy. Community is the antidote.\n\nAt each checkpoint quiz, if you miss a question, return to the relevant lesson before continuing.\n\nThe Freedom Prayer Guide in Supplement 12 is designed to be used as many times as needed. Once through the sequence for a comprehensive pass — then returned to for specific areas as the Holy Spirit surfaces them.\n\n---\n\n## One Thing Before You Begin\n\nThe God who prohibited these practices prohibited them for the same reason a loving father tells his children not to play in traffic. Not because He is controlling. Because He can see what you cannot, and He has been watching these entities operate since before the world was made.\n\n[Deuteronomy 18:12](data-scripture="Deuteronomy 18:12"): *"For all that do these things are an abomination unto the LORD."* The severity of that language is not moral condemnation for its own sake. It is the measured response of a God who knows exactly what these practices invite and exactly what they cost.\n\nHe also wrote [Isaiah 61:1](data-scripture="Isaiah 61:1"): *"The Spirit of the Lord GOD is upon me... to proclaim liberty to the captives, and the opening of the prison to them that are bound."*\n\nBoth verses are true. Both are relevant to Part VII. The first explains why the door should not have been opened. The second is why you are taking this course.\n\n> **Community Discussion:** *What is the experience or practice that brought you to Part VII specifically? What were you hoping to find — and what do you expect this section to cost you?*	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk4bq001tewhj8kdfxzbv	cmnknk4bp001qewhjk5bcqnfj	Supplement 19 — For Lessons 37 & 38	SUPPLEMENT	1203	2	\N	t	The dead are not in some vague spiritual limbo — Scripture places them specifically. And there are seven distinct judgments in Scripture, each with different subjects, different timing, different outcomes. This supplement makes those truths practical: How do you grieve the dead? How do you live in light of judgment?\n\nThis fact alone is enough to prove that God naturally loves you and makes abundant provision for you in this life as well as the next. He provides for the lesser creations, as you can see on every hand—so how much more will He provide for you?\n\n![Supplement 19 — For Lessons 37 & 38](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsCl8P34Ze78vbPqVLiKnDy6up93CGaNzkh5Hs)\n\n## Man and Evil Spirits Are Responsible\n\nOf all earthly creatures, we humans are the only ones responsible for our present state of sin and bondage, poverty and want, pain and disease. We have voluntarily submitted our domain to the devil and evil spirit forces, and we—along with them—are responsible for these conditions.\n\nGod is not responsible for them in any sense. These conditions would not curse the human race today if God could get every person to obey Him. They should not be in the life of any child of God, and they won't be, when believers come to know the truth that sets them free.\n\nWhy would God will such conditions in the lives of His own children whom He has so dearly purchased by the death of His own Son?\n\n## Sin and Demons Hinder Man's Blessings\n\nThe Bible is very clear that sin and demon powers are what hinder you from receiving God's blessings: "The thief cometh not, but for to steal, and to kill, and to destroy: I am come that they might have life, and that they might have it more abundantly" (Jn. 10:10).\n\nFaith comes by hearing, and hearing by the Word of God (Rom. 10:17). But faith is often hindered because the devil takes the word of faith out of your heart and mind before it can germinate and bring forth fruit (Mt. 13:19; 2 Cor. 4:3-4).\n\nThe Bible also makes clear that we ourselves permit the devil to do this—otherwise it couldn't be done (Mt. 13:15; Jas. 4:7; 1 Pet. 5:8-9; 2 Cor. 4:3-4).\n\n## God's Abundant Promises and Provision\n\nThere is no reasonable excuse for the unbelief in Christendom concerning this truth. Paul said, "He that spared not His own Son, but delivered him up for us all, how shall he not with him also freely give us all things?" (Rom. 8:32).\n\nThe Psalmist said, "They that seek the Lord shall not want any good thing" and "No good thing will he withhold from them that walk uprightly" (Ps. 34:10; 84:11). Jesus said, "If ye then, being evil, know how to give good gifts unto your children, how much more shall your Father which is in heaven give good things to them that ask him" (Mt. 7:7-11).\n\nMany other promises plainly state that you can ask what you will and it shall be done (Mk. 9:23; 11:22-24; Jn. 14:12-15; 15:7, 16; 16:23-26).\n\n## God is Always True to His Word\n\nYou would think every Christian would have enough faith in God because of His promises to tackle their own problems, resist the devil, and receive the many benefits they need so much in life.\n\nBut many times, instead of believing God, people believe the devil. Instead of holding to the fact that God's promises are true, they argue against them and seek every excuse under the sun to disbelieve them. Instead of fighting the demons who seek to rob them of God's blessings, they actually cooperate with those demons in destroying faith in God.\n\nMeanwhile, they claim they love God with their whole heart and that the devil has nothing to do with their failures. They generally lay any blame on God when they fail to get what is promised in the gospel.\n\nThey're quick to accept the idea that it's not God's will to bless them if there's the least delay or hindrance. It never seems to dawn on them that such opposition could be from the devil and not from God. Even if they mentally acknowledge this opposition as satanic, they don't put forth the least effort to defeat the powers of darkness.\n\nThey seem satisfied to do without these things—they'd rather go without than fight a little for them. On the other hand, they'll fight a good fight of unbelief and maintain a consistent faith that such blessings may not be God's will, and thus hinder the answer to their prayers.\n\nEven when they hear many plain, simple, scriptural statements from the Word of God saying they can have the benefits of the promises, many are still hard to convince that these things are true.\n\n## One Thing God Requires of Everyone\n\nEvery person who professes to believe in God will agree that everyone should lay aside all arguments of unbelief and have faith that God will do what He has promised and answer prayer. Yet in spite of mentally acknowledging this fact, it seems so hard to get many of the same people to put forth the necessary effort to believe what God has promised.\n\nIt is a fact that God will always do what He has promised. It is also a fact that He requires every person to have faith that He will do what they ask of Him.\n\nGod demands the same act of unwavering faith from everyone alike. He is no respecter of persons, and He has made it very clear what each one must do to get what they desire. The one thing God requires is faith—unwavering faith (Heb. 11:5; Jas. 1:4-8).\n\n## No Efficacy in Human Works or Good Deeds\n\nThere's a prevailing idea among people in general—even among the best of Christians—that you must become good enough for God to hear you, or that long service to God earns you an answer to prayer.\n\nMany pride themselves in the fact that they've been Christians for many years and have numerous Christian accomplishments to their credit. They seem to think that God owes them answers to prayer because of this.\n\nMany times an older Christian is puzzled because God answers the cry of sinners and heals them or otherwise answers their prayers when they come to God, while their own prayers go unanswered.\n\nThe reason is simple. The sinner knows he has no human merits or goodness to offer as a basis for claiming the benefits of the gospel. He comes to God as a sinner—unworthy, undone, and helpless. In simple, child-like faith, he surrenders himself to God and throws himself upon God's mercy, asking for those things Jesus died to give him.\n\nHe is immediately heard by God and goes away with what he asked for.\n\nOn the other hand, some believer who has served God for years comes to God asking and pleading for some answer to prayer—and goes away unanswered. This seems unfair to him and to others who know how well such people have lived.\n\nBut in such cases, remember: God does not and will not answer because of human merit, good works, clean living, a perfect character, or a wonderful reputation. God answers solely when a person realizes their human nothingness and unworthiness, and expresses simple, child-like faith in God through Jesus Christ.\n\n## Man Must Learn His Lesson\n\nSooner or later, you must learn the lesson that you are unworthy of God's blessings, that you cannot possibly merit any favor with God in yourself, and that only by repentance and absolute dependence upon the merits of Christ's work are you blessed.\n\nThink of it like this: a child who comes to a loving parent with empty hands and a humble heart receives far more than one who comes with a list of chores they've done, expecting payment. The parent wants to give freely—not because of what the child has earned, but because of love.\n\nThis is the sum total of the Christian faith as far as getting things from God is concerned. Christian men can pray and beg and ask and seek God for something on any other basis, and they will never be heard. Don't miss this truth—it's the cornerstone of prayer and its answer.\n\nWhen the Pharisee prayed and bragged on himself, he was not heard by God. But when the publican smote upon his breast and said, "God be merciful to me a sinner," he went down to his house justified (Lk. 18:9-14).\n\nLong Christian service, winning souls, godly living, a good character, a perfect reputation, or the most wonderful stage of human living will never be the basis of answered prayer. Learn this lesson once and forever—it will save many heartaches and anxieties in Christian life.\n\nNo one will ever get good enough for God to answer any prayer, and no one will ever get bad enough for God to turn them down if they come in faith and repentance through the gospel.\n\n## It is Simple to Believe God\n\nSo many say that it's hard to believe God, or that it's hard to have enough faith to get an answer to prayer. But this is a lie and a slander against God and His character.\n\nWhy should it be hard to believe a God who has always been faithful to every person who has ever lived? Why would it be hard to trust in a God who has never failed anyone? Why should anyone tolerate such theories and express them in words and acts when there is no foundation for such lies?\n\nWe take each other's word without proof. Children believe their parents. Most people will believe the word of those over them in any realm—except in religion. Many won't believe the preacher who declares that God will answer prayer, that God is true, that He will never fail anyone, and that He is under obligation to meet every need according to His promises.\n\nChildren won't doubt their parents, and people will seldom doubt those over them—but when it comes to believing the preacher concerning the gospel, that's another question.\n\n## It is Always God's Will to Help Men\n\nHow could God be hindered except by unbelief? How could He fail to do as He has promised and still keep the respect of His own creation? How could He fail those for whom Christ died?\n\nHow could He will to see them suffer defeat, poverty, want, pain, sickness, sin, and unhappiness? No normal earthly father would get pleasure out of seeing his own children suffer these things if he could help them.\n\nHow much more will God help His own children whom He sent Jesus to die for? How much more is it God's will for His children to be happy in life, victorious over sin and evil, free from sickness and pain, and from poverty and want?\n\nGod can help His children out of such conditions—He is not helpless like human parents. God has promised to help His children, but only upon the one condition of faith. So it is up to each person to get from God those things that belong to them by virtue of being partakers of gospel redemption through Jesus Christ.\n\n## There is No Excuse for Children of God to be Defeated\n\nThis is as true as can be. God is stronger than the devil, fallen angels, men, and all demon forces. The devil is already a defeated foe.\n\nDemons have no power over Christians if they will use the means of grace God has provided for them. Speaking of saved people, the Bible says, "Greater is he that is in you, than he that is in the world" (1 Jn. 4:4). If Christians would believe and use the power of God that comes through the Holy Spirit, there could not possibly be any defeat.\n\nGod's promises are so abundant and all-inclusive that every believer can get what they want in life—not only what they need. So there cannot possibly be an excuse for anyone to be sick, sinful, helpless, and defeated in life if they will cooperate with God and fight against the devil (Mk. 9:23; 11:22-24).\n\n## The Proper Action to Take\n\nIf you could only realize that these facts are true, you would become stirred up to attain to the benefits for which Christ died. But as long as you remain in ignorance and unbelief, you won't become concerned enough to get rid of sins and sicknesses through Christ.\n\nThe thing to do is quit complaining if there is any defeat in your life. If you don't sincerely desire these benefits promised by God, then quit laying the blame on God for such defeats. Lay the blame on the devil and the enemies of God who seek to hinder you from getting what God wants you to have.\n\nIf you want the devil to get glory and have right of way in your life, then that's what will happen. But if you want God to receive glory from your life and have the right of way in you, then accept the truth that will make you free.\n\nYou not only don't need to be sick and sinful, but you don't need to be in want all your days, for God has made full provision for you to get what you want in material things and all other good blessings of life. This we have seen in Lesson Ten and other lessons.\n\nAll you need to do to get what you want and need in life is follow the instructions we have repeatedly given in past supplements and in the lessons themselves. Don't fail to follow instructions to the letter.\n\nAbove all, never doubt that you are on the right track and that you are going to receive what God has promised. It doesn't matter what your needs are or how impossible it seems to you. God is true to you and will fulfill His will in you if you will fulfill His will by having faith and humility.\n\n## God is Not Responsible\n\nGod is responsible for providing all good things for you. He is responsible for making so many unlimited promises concerning every known need of the human race, here and now. He will meet His part of the responsibility in giving you all the benefits of the promises.\n\nBut He is not responsible for your attitude toward these blessings. He is not responsible for your reception of these wonderful truths. You are the only one robbing yourself of these fortunes of grace. You are the one holding back the floodgates of blessing in your life.\n\nGod has done His part, and you must do your part if you want the good things which you are freely promised. God cannot remain just and righteous to you if He broke His laws to give you these good things contrary to the conditions He has given in connection with the promises.\n\nThe conditions are clear. God's Word has gone out of His mouth—He cannot and will not lie. He will do for every person what He has promised all believers. He cannot fail to do His part, so if there is any failure in getting what is promised, the responsibility is on the individual and not on God.\n\nLet the people who want what God has promised do as He requires: have faith in God, nothing wavering, as is plainly stated in Mt. 21:21-22; Mk. 11:22-24; Heb. 11:6; Jas. 1:5-8.\n\nIf unbelief is the hindrance, then let it be corrected by a definite refusal to question the will of God, the promises of God, the love of God, and the faithfulness of God. Let there be a dogged faith maintained.\n\nIt's similar to how you might stubbornly hold onto something you deeply care about in other areas of life—use that same stubborn determination in connection with faith. Simply reject unbelief and doubts, believe God, and the answer will materialize.\n\n## Ten Questions For Supplement Nineteen. Do You Know:\n\n- That one-third of God's own angels rebelled against Him when Lucifer rebelled? This is clearly taught in Rev. 12:1-12. In this passage the great red dragon (the devil, as explained in verse 9) is seen drawing one-third of the stars of the heavens down to the Earth. These stars are called "his angels" in the same passage. Since God originally created them, they were His angels. So if they are now the angels of the devil, then they rebelled with him in the original rebellion, as we have seen in Lesson Seven.\n\n- That there are other kingdoms on other planets? It is plainly stated in Col. 1:15-18 that God through Christ created thrones, dominions, principalities, and powers, both visible and invisible, that are in Heaven and in Earth. These dominions and principalities are not all located on Earth, for it is stated that some are in Heaven. The word "Heaven" should be understood as the whole of the heavens, which are inhabited as proven in Supplement One, Question 7. There must be life on all the habitable worlds, and if this is true, then they are organized into dominions and kingdoms as we understand it on this planet.\n\n- That a day never means a year and a year never means a day in prophecy? This theory is purely of human origin based upon the fact that God punished men forty years according to the number of days the spies searched Canaan (Num. 13:25; 14:34) and according to the number of days Ezekiel lay upon his side (Ezek. 4:1-13). People have used this as a basis for changing a day to a year or a year to a day in Scripture as their fancy led them. This has led to innumerable fallacies concerning Bible dates and prophetic truths. There is no basis for such a theory, for in the above cases a day always means a day and a year always means a year. And so it is in all Scripture. Forty days will always be forty days and not forty years, and forty years will always be forty years and not forty days. To deviate from these facts is to follow foolish reasoning concerning Bible interpretation.\n\n- That this age cannot end any day as some teach? There are many prophecies yet to be fulfilled and a number of years to come to pass before this age ends. There will be at least seven years that Antichrist will be here before the age ends (Dan. 9:27; Rev. 13:5). How many more years there will be before he comes is not clear, so it is impossible for this age to end any day, as taught by many Bible teachers. Many Scriptures will be given to prove this in the next two lessons.\n\n- That the Church does not have to go through the Tribulation to be purified? There is no such statement in Scripture. If there was, then it would be necessary for all the dead saints to be resurrected and live in the Tribulation in order to go through the same process of cleansing—and this no one teaches. It is the blood that cleanses from sin and not a period of tribulation, and this will always be the case (Eph. 1:7; 1 Jn. 1:7; Rev. 1:5). The Church is ready and always has been ready, for all the true believers in Christ are new creatures in Christ, and as long as they stay in Christ they are ready. Those out of Christ are never ready, and there is no statement in Scripture that Christ cannot come back for those that are ready until certain ones who are not saved get ready. On the contrary, all in Christ are ready at all times and will be raptured together at the proper time (1 Thess. 4:13-17; 1 Cor. 15:23; Eph. 5:27).\n\n- That a literal earthly kingdom will continue on this planet forever? This is proven from the following Scriptures (Dan. 2:44-45; 7:9-14, 18; Isa. 9:6-7; Lk. 1:32-33; Rev. 1:5; 5:10; 11:15; 22:5-6; Zech. 14; etc.).\n\n- That the Earth will not be desolate during the Millennium? There is not one statement in Scripture to support such a theory. On the contrary, Rev. 20 speaks of the saints reigning on the Earth over natural men, and at the end of the Millennium many people will rebel against the government and will be destroyed by fire. At the second coming of Christ, before the Millennium, the kingdom will be set up and it will continue forever (Mt. 13:42-43; 25:31-46; Rev. 19:11-21; 20:1-15; Zech. 14; Dan. 2:44-45; 7:9-14, 18; Heb. 12:25-28). People will go up to Jerusalem to worship year by year in the Millennium (Isa. 2:1-4; Zech. 14:16-21), so there will be plenty of people on Earth during that time.\n\n- That the next form of government on Earth will be a theocracy? This means government by God through Jesus Christ and the saints as taught in Dan. 2:44-45; 7:13-14, 18; Zech. 14; Mt. 25:31-46; Lk. 1:32-33; Rev. 11:15; 20:1-10; 22:5-6.\n\n- That Jerusalem will soon be the world's center of law and religion? This will be in the Millennium and eternal ages when Christ reigns in Jerusalem. Then the law and the Word of the Lord shall go out from Jerusalem to the ends of the Earth (Isa. 2:1-4; Mic. 4; Zech. 14; Joel 3; Ezek. 43:7; Jer. 17:25).\n\n- That over one billion people who have never heard of Jesus Christ will be evangelized in less than one hundred years? There is no question but what we are in the very last days of this age, and we will be in the Millennium in less than one hundred years. It will be then that the knowledge of the Lord will fill the Earth as the waters cover the sea (Isa. 2:1-4; 11:9). This means that when Jesus comes, the billion or more people who have never heard the gospel will hear and be evangelized.\n\n---\n\nThe dead are present with the Lord or awaiting judgment — there is no neutral zone, no purgatory, no soul sleep. And for the believer, the judgment to come is not of guilt but of works. So here's the question: Is there someone you've lost who you're not sure died in faith? And for yourself: if you stood before the Bema Seat today, what record of works would you present — and what would be consumed by fire because it was built for the wrong reasons?	\N	\N	\N
cmq76xook000h8071qs86l2ws	cmq76xgul000jhks877ughys4	The Music Industry	READING	\N	3	\N	t	![The Music Industry](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsaG7pp6EhtesUMivpomO8x4WSPknRFTuAI75Q)\n\n## The Music Industry\n\nHe had grown up in the church. He could still sing every hymn from his childhood by heart. He also had a playlist of 800 songs that he called his "motivation music" — the songs that made him feel powerful, focused, and alive. He listened to it for approximately four hours every day: commuting, working out, cooking.\n\nHe had never analyzed the lyrics. He absorbed them the way you absorb advertising — below the threshold of conscious evaluation. When a pastor friend asked him to listen to five songs from his playlist and describe what they were actually saying, he spent an uncomfortable evening with lyrics in front of him for the first time. Four of the five were explicit invitations to darkness, revenge, lust, and self-deification.\n\nHe had been meditating on those messages for four hours a day for eleven years. He did not understand why the fruits of the Spirit felt so difficult to cultivate.\n\n---\n\n## What It Is\n\nMuch of mainstream music — heavy metal, certain hip-hop, pop, and electronic genres — contains occult symbolism, references to demonic entities, explicit celebration of sexual immorality, and direct invocations of darkness. Behind the commercial surface lies a deeper layer: concert events designed as mass spiritual experiences, award show ceremonies saturated with occult ritual, and an industry culture of explicit spiritual compromise.\n\nMajor artists have openly acknowledged making covenantal agreements with dark forces. Album artwork, music videos, and stage performances use all-seeing eyes, Baphomet imagery, mock crucifixions, and ritualized occult ceremonies before audiences of tens of thousands.\n\nThis is not conspiracy theory. It is what the artists themselves, and the industry that produces them, openly state.\n\n## The Demonic Deception\n\nMusic is spiritually powerful in a way that other media are not. It bypasses the analytical mind and speaks directly to the emotional and spiritual center of the person. This is why every culture uses music in worship — it has a direct channel to the soul.\n\n[Ephesians 5:19](data-scripture="Ephesians 5:19"): *"Speaking to yourselves in psalms and hymns and spiritual songs, singing and making melody in your heart to the Lord."* The instruction exists because the inverse is also true: music that directs the soul toward darkness creates the same depth of formation as music that directs it toward God. What you sing, you become. What you meditate to, you are formed by.\n\nConcert environments combine overwhelming sound, lighting designed to induce altered states, mass crowd energy, and frequently chemical substances — creating a corporate spiritual experience that functions as a massive simultaneous opening of thousands of people to demonic influence.\n\n## How It Opens the Door\n\n**Lyric meditation forms the mind.** The brain rehearses whatever it repeatedly hears. Lyrics that celebrate death, self-deification, sexual conquest, and rebellion do not remain in the "entertainment only" compartment — they become the furniture of the practitioner's inner world.\n\n**Artist transference is real.** Musicians who are heavily involved in the occult transmit something through their recordings and performances. This is not physically explicable. The documented experience of many deliverance ministers is that listening to specific artists produces consistent, specific spiritual effects in those who engage with the content — distinct from the content's lyrical or thematic influence.\n\n**Backmasking and embedded content.** Messages recorded backward bypass conscious analysis and implant suggestions in the subconscious. This is well-documented in the music of several major artists from the 1970s onward. The technique is not merely an urban legend.\n\n## What This Means for You Right Now\n\nAudit your playlist. Not casually — with lyrics in front of you. Ask: what is this song actually saying? What is it training me to desire, celebrate, and become?\n\nThen apply [Philippians 4:8](data-scripture="Philippians 4:8") as a filter: *whatsoever things are true, honest, just, pure, lovely, of good report.* Music that does not pass this filter is not a neutral leisure choice. It is soul formation in a direction away from God.\n\nReplace it. Not with exclusively Christian music necessarily — with music that celebrates truth, beauty, love, courage, and the created world God made. Music is the background of your soul's formation. Make it count.\n\n---\n\n*The industry makes gods of its vessels. The next lesson examines what happens when the audience starts worshipping the vessel instead of God.*\n\n> **Community Discussion:** *Name an artist you have loved whose work contains occult imagery. How do you plan to approach that listening going forward?*	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk44k0006ewhjvo5au3s7	cmnknk44i0001ewhju56drbw5	Lesson 3 — How to Interpret the Bible	READING	3126	4	\N	t	![Lesson 3 — How to Interpret the Bible](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsXgNGUHx3hkKszq9AHmfWrCLc6tDpSgVyF1n0)\n\n# LESSON THREE\n\n## 3. HOW TO INTERPRET THE BIBLE\n\nHere's a dangerous idea that's quietly wrecked more Bible students than almost anything else: the belief that Scripture can mean whatever you need it to mean. Under this view, interpretation is just creative reading — a personal conversation between you and the text with no objectively right answer. Pastors quote it one way. Scholars quote it another. Denominations split over it. So why not just decide for yourself? Because if that's true, the Bible isn't a divine revelation — it's a mirror. And you'll never be changed by a mirror.\n\nLast time we established that the Bible is a God-breathed document that has proven its divine origin across 1,800 years and 40 authors. Now the question becomes: if it has one author with one intended meaning, how do we find it? That's the whole science of biblical interpretation — and it's simpler than most people have been led to believe.\n\n## I. The Bible is the Simplest Book to Understand\n\nThis probably sounds ridiculous at first. But if you take a moment to consider a few straightforward facts, I think you'll see how sensible this really is.\n\n### 1. BECAUSE THE BIBLE IS A REVELATION\n\nMost people assume that because the Bible is a divine book, it must be divinely cryptic — that its truths require special spiritual keys to unlock, accessible only to priests, scholars, or people with extraordinary spiritual gifts. But this gets the logic completely backwards.\n\nWe saw in Lesson Two that the Bible is an inspired revelation from God. Think about what "revelation" actually means: it's an unveiling. Like a sculptor pulling the cloth off a finished statue so a crowd can see it. The curtain is gone. What was hidden is now visible. The only reason someone wouldn't see what's been uncovered is if they simply refuse to look.\n\nIf something has truly been revealed, it's meant to be clear. Otherwise, the whole point of revealing it has failed. A "revelation" that requires another revelation to understand isn't a revelation at all — it's another mystery.\n\n### 2. BECAUSE OF ITS REPEATED TRUTHS\n\nThe Bible repeats its truths over and over so that "in the mouth of two or three witnesses every word may be established" (Dt. 17:6-7; 19:15; Mt. 18:16; 2 Cor. 13:1; 1 Tim. 5:19; Heb. 10:28). Any doctrine that isn't plainly stated in Scripture is best left alone.\n\nIf God didn't say anything about a particular question, then we have no right to teach anything about it as though it were in the Bible. We should limit our teaching to a "thus saith the Lord" or simply stay quiet. Our personal opinions carry no weight in proving something the Bible doesn't teach.\n\nOn the other hand, if God did address a subject, you'll find it mentioned in several places—so you won't be left guessing. All you need to do is collect everything God says on a topic, and it becomes so clear that no special interpretation is necessary.\n\nWhen you do this honestly, nothing needs to be added to or taken from Scripture to arrive at the truth. Just find out where "it is written" and believe it. Always make your ideas conform to the Bible—never twist Scripture to fit your ideas. Anyone who presumes to know more than what God has said is putting themselves in God's place as the Author of Scripture.\n\n### 3. BECAUSE THE BIBLE IS WRITTEN IN THE SIMPLEST HUMAN LANGUAGE POSSIBLE\n\nAnyone who understands basic human language can understand what the Bible says. Every time any group of people reads a particular passage, they all read the same words. If they read it again, it will still say the same thing.\n\nIf you asked them to tell you what the passage says, they could all do it. And if they can tell you what it says—if they can read what it says—then they can all believe what it says. That's really all that's necessary to understand the Bible.\n\nWhat's so hard about something everyone can read and believe alike without needing special interpretation?\n\n### 4. BECAUSE GOD IS THE AUTHOR OF THE BIBLE\n\nGod Himself authored Scripture (2 Tim. 3:15-17; 2 Pet. 1:21). If God is the author, we have every right to expect it to be clear. No human being can make a book simpler than God can.\n\nIf God could have made a book as easy to understand as any person could—and chose not to—then we'd have to conclude He didn't want us to understand His Word. But why then would He give it to us? In that case, we'd have to throw out the Bible and accuse God of injustice for judging us based on something we can't comprehend.\n\nSince we can't imagine God acting that way, we're left with one conclusion: the Bible is simple enough for everyone to understand.\n\nA God who couldn't make Himself clear—or who needed interpreters every time He spoke—would be no God at all. Almost any human being can express themselves clearly enough to be understood! And a God who could make Himself clear but deliberately chose to confuse us wouldn't be worth listening to.\n\nThink of it like a parent giving their child instructions for something important, then deliberately making those instructions impossible to follow—and punishing the child anyway. That wouldn't be a loving parent; that would be a tyrant. The same logic applies to God.\n\nLet's reject these slanderous concepts of God! It's the devil's work to get people believing such things about God and His eternal revelation. Satan wants us to twist God's Word into something false so we'll face judgment for not believing what it actually says.\n\nLet's wake up to this deception before it's too late. Satan knows that God means what He says—so he wants to deceive us into being lost.\n\nLet's believe, like sensible people, that God can and did speak to us in the simplest human language. He meant exactly what He said and said exactly what He meant. He expects us to understand His Word using the same common-sense principles we use with any other book.\n\nHe will hold us responsible for what He says—not for what others interpret His words to mean. He has every right to judge people who constantly twist His words and listen to satanic theories instead of what He actually said.\n\nJesus Himself warned us: "He that rejecteth me, and receiveth not my words, hath one that judgeth him: the word that I have spoken, the same shall judge him in the last day" (Jn. 12:48; Rev. 20:11-15).\n\nShouldn't this be enough warning to stop the foolishness of making God's Word mean whatever we want? It's the height of arrogance to claim we can interpret the Bible better than God Himself. The Bible needs no interpretation apart from its own explanation of its contents. Scripture interprets its own terms, symbols, parables, allegories, and figures of speech.\n\nSo in these lessons, we're taking a different approach. We'll let God's own Word be the final authority on every question. We'll let what God says mean exactly what He says—and reject any contradicting human theory.\n\n### 5. THE BIBLE IS SIMPLE BECAUSE IT WAS GIVEN BY GOD TO BE UNDERSTOOD BY THE SIMPLE\n\nFollowing the logical argument that a perfect God cannot make anything imperfect, we can say scripturally that God did not fail in His purpose of giving mankind a simple revelation (Deut. 29:29; Ps. 119:104, 130; Prov. 1:1-4; 2 Tim. 3:15-17).\n\nPaul speaks of "the simplicity that is in Christ" (2 Cor. 11:3). Jesus thanked God that biblical truths were hidden from the worldly-wise who refused to believe, saying God has "revealed them unto babes" (Mt. 11:25-27).\n\nWhy are truths hidden from some people? Because they refuse to humble themselves to believe and conform to the Bible (Mt. 13:10-17). Jesus speaks of the devil taking the Word from people's hearts before it can bear fruit (Mt. 13:19-23). Paul describes Satan blinding people's minds and people willfully handling God's Word dishonestly (2 Cor. 4:1-6).\n\nThe simplest beginner can understand the Bible one line at a time—that's how it was given, and it's the best way to understand it (Isa. 28:9-13). No one can grasp the vastness of the Bible all at once. It's this seemingly infinite scope that makes some people think the Bible is difficult.\n\nPicture a man named Thomas, a new believer in first-century Corinth, who received a letter from the Apostle Paul. Thomas had never read a word of the Old Testament. He had no seminary training, no concordance, no commentary. He simply read the letter — one sentence at a time. And he understood it. Not every nuance, not every layer, but enough to be transformed and to transform others. That's how Scripture has always worked. That's how it was designed to work.\n\nIt's similar to someone claiming they can't understand water because they can't drink the entire ocean in one sitting. Naturally, it takes time to gain a working knowledge of the whole Bible. But here's our point: it cannot be hard to understand if you take it one line at a time, one verse at a time, one truth at a time.\n\nYou can't look at any big book and absorb its entire contents at a glance. Anyone who says the Bible is hard to understand before they've actually dug into it is being foolish. Once you do, you'll find truths opening up beyond your wildest dreams.\n\nThose who insist the Bible is hard to understand generally fall into these categories:\n\n> (1) Those who are ignorant of its contents.\n\n> (2) Those who are too lazy to master its contents.\n\n> (3) Those who are biased against its contents (Mt. 15:7-14; Jn. 8:43).\n\n> (4) Those who refuse to believe what it says and get tangled up in hopeless theories and interpretations they've been taught.\n\n> (5) Those who've heard the old theory that it's hard to understand and give up before they even try.\n\n> (6) Those raised in the wrong school of thought about the Bible who wonder if God really means what He says (Mt. 16:6-12; Lk. 24:25; Mk. 16:14).\n\n> (7) Those who are too worldly-wise, thinking they know more than the Bible, discounting its contents through unbelief and pride.\n\n> (8) Those who are unstable and unlearned and twist the scriptures to their own destruction (2 Pet. 3:16).\n\n> (9) Those who willfully handle God's Word deceitfully to cover up their own sins, which the Bible condemns (2 Cor. 4:1-4).\n\n> (10) Those who let Satan steal the Word from their lives, causing them to be blind to truth (Mt. 13:19-23; 2 Cor. 4:1-4).\n\n> (11) Those who make money by fighting the Word of God (Mt. 15:7-14; 2 Pet. 2:1-3).\n\n> (12) Those who refuse to be converted and become as little children (Mt. 18:3; 28:9-15; Isa. 6:9-10; Acts 28:24-29).\n\n### 6. THE BIBLE IS SIMPLE BECAUSE READING AND BELIEVING WITH A SIMPLE HEART IS ALL GOD CONSIDERS NECESSARY TO UNDERSTAND IT\n\nGod commands all people to be saved and to understand His Word (Ps. 32:9; Prov. 4:5; 8:4-10). We're not to add to or take from the Bible in any detail—we must take it as written (Deut. 4:2-6; 12:32; Rev. 22:18-19).\n\nWe're instructed to search and study the Bible and apply our hearts to it—which means we must be able to understand it (Jn. 5:39; Ps. 1:2-3; 90:12; 2 Tim. 2:15; 3:15-17). God created us with a spirit capable of understanding His Word. He made both us and His Word—and they fit together like a lock and key (Job 32:8; 38:3-6; Jn. 1:4-9).\n\nEven unbelievers can understand if they truly want to (Rom. 1:16-20). So the old theory that you must be saved and spiritual to understand the Bible isn't scriptural. All the references above apply to everyone—saved and unsaved alike.\n\nAnyone can understand the letter of the Bible. And if you want the experiences it teaches, you can understand them through the letter well enough to attain them. Because some don't live up to the light they receive, God has a just basis to judge them.\n\nSome people point to 1 Cor. 2:14 to argue that the Bible is hard to understand. But that passage says the "natural man" cannot receive the things of the Spirit—it doesn't teach that sinners can't understand the written Word. Others use 2 Pet. 3:16-18 similarly, but that passage refers to the "unstable and unlearned" who twist scriptures to their own destruction.\n\nIf sinners couldn't understand the Bible, how could they get saved or know what God requires? How could God judge them for things they can't comprehend? The reality is that many sinners understand the Bible better than some believers because they're sensible enough to take it at face value.\n\nIf all believers did the same, they'd have a distinct advantage—because they have God's Spirit to illuminate them, while unbelievers don't (Jn. 14:17; Rom. 8:9).\n\n### 7. THE BIBLE IS A SIMPLE BOOK TO UNDERSTAND BECAUSE MOST OF IT IS HISTORY AND SIMPLE INSTRUCTIONS ABOUT HOW TO LIVE\n\nAbout 25,007 verses—around 80 percent of the Bible—contain straightforward history, commands, warnings, promises, rebukes, and plain instructions for understanding God's will. What could be in any of these verses that a normal person couldn't understand? Anyone with common intelligence should be able to comprehend them.\n\nThe other 6,207 of the Bible's 31,214 verses are prophecy, written in the same simple human language used for history. Of these, 3,299 have already been fulfilled—meaning they're now history too. The remaining 2,908 verses are unfulfilled prophecy, but they're just as easy to understand as history.\n\nPicture it this way: prophecy is simply history written beforehand. If you can understand what has happened by reading a clear record of it, you should be able to understand what will happen by reading the same kind of clear record.\n\nWhat's hard about either history or prophecy? What's hard about any part of the Bible? We conclude that the Bible is remarkably simple to understand—if we believe it as written and let it guide our faith and practice.\n\n## II. Definition of Terms\n\n### 1. INTERPRET\n\nAs we use this word, it means to state the true sense of God's message exactly as He expresses it. It means giving readers the exact statements of Scripture without changes to prove any point discussed—stating precisely what God says and where He says it. We consider this all-sufficient.\n\nThe modern way of interpreting the Bible often means changing what's written to suit personal preferences and make Scripture fit human theories. We call this "how not to interpret the Bible" because it violates every sensible principle of true interpretation and elevates human authority above God regarding His own Word.\n\nThe Bible is clear in itself when traditions, wrong interpretations, changes, and spiritualizing are set aside. The average person is blind to many simple truths of Scripture because they've been covered over with human traditions and interpretations designed to serve a church, a party, or personal whims.\n\nOur purpose in this lesson is to expose this overlay and draw attention to what God actually says—which is always clear in itself.\n\n### 2. HERMENEUTICS\n\nThis is the science or art of interpretation and explanation. It comes from the Greek word *ermeneuo*, meaning to explain, expound, and interpret (Jn. 1:38-42; 9:7; Heb. 7:2). It's the science that establishes and classifies the principles, methods, and rules for discovering an author's intended meaning.\n\nHow you interpret any piece of literature depends on what kind of writing it is. Poetry, history, fiction—each requires different rules. Rules for interpreting fiction wouldn't work for history. Similarly, the rules for Bible interpretation depend on the character of its different types of writing, just as with other books.\n\nSince the Bible is written in human language like other books, it must be interpreted like other literature. If heavenly, supernatural, and spiritual truths are expressed in human language, we must understand them on that basis. We interpret words and expressions in the Bible the same way we would if we found them outside it.\n\nThere's no special Bible logic, rhetoric, or grammar. The laws of grammar apply to Scripture just as they do to other writings.\n\nChrist and His disciples demonstrated this method consistently and without apology. In about 400 quotations from the Old Testament in the New Testament, the phrase "It is written" is treated as entirely sufficient to prove God's message. Not "It is written, and here's my elaborate interpretation." Just: "It is written." End of argument.\n\nWhen Satan tempted Jesus in the wilderness, Jesus didn't respond with theological reasoning or personal spiritual experience. He responded three times with a single phrase: "It is written" (Mt. 4:4, 7, 10). The plain text of Scripture was Jesus's entire defense against the most sophisticated deceiver in the universe. If that was enough for Him, it settles every doctrinal point today — if we want to be Christ-like.\n\nNot one example exists in Scripture where the plain, literal sense was replaced by allegorical, mystical, speculative, spiritualizing, or symbolizing methods so common today. We must abandon such approaches if we want true knowledge of the Bible.\n\n### 3. BIBLICAL HERMENEUTICS\n\nThis is the science that establishes and classifies the principles, methods, and rules by which God's Word is made plain.\n\n### 4. EXEGESIS\n\nThis is applying the rules of biblical hermeneutics to unfold the meaning of a Scripture passage. Interpretation expresses exactly another's mind and thoughts—it's purely reproductive, involving no originality from the interpreter.\n\nExegesis uses the science of interpretation to reproduce God's thoughts as expressed in Scripture. Being a faithful exegete carries great responsibility and great reward. But being an eisegete—someone faulty in explaining Scripture—may cost both the interpreter's soul and those who follow them.\n\n## III. Reasons for Biblical Hermeneutics\n\n> 1. The Bible is a heavenly message conveyed in human language, so the same interpretation principles used with all human language must apply to Scripture.\n\n> 2. The Bible's original languages differ in some ways from English in grammatical structure and idiomatic usage. Understanding these differences is necessary for certain passages.\n\n> 3. The Bible is a composite book of 66 books.\n\n> 4. The Bible is a religious book for this life and the life to come.\n\n> 5. The Bible is varied—an oriental library containing all forms of human expression and literature.\n\n> 6. The Bible comes from many lands and peoples with habits and customs different from ours.\n\n> 7. The changing nature of English and its imperfect translation of every Hebrew and Greek nuance makes certain rules necessary for understanding particular passages.\n\n## IV. Two Seemingly Contradictory Ways to Read the Bible\n\n> 1. Read it like any other book—from beginning to end—grasping each writer's thought, the meaning of words and unique expressions, the customs of Bible lands and times, God's purpose in each message, and the particular audience addressed.\n\n> 2. Read it differently from any other book because it's inspired. Much of it is direct revelation from God (2 Tim. 3:15-17). Read it slowly, prayerfully, frequently, reverently, meditatively, searchingly, perseveringly, believingly, and obediently.\n\n## V. Historical Sketch of Hermeneutics\n\nA brief understanding of interpretation history has great value. It helps guard against repeating others' errors and shows what influences caused those mistakes. The science of interpretation has gone through many false concepts, just like other sciences. But thankfully, like other sciences, we're starting to demand reasons for particular interpretations.\n\nPast efforts have demonstrated the foolishness of abandoning Scripture's plain literal sense. The belief that the Bible was divine almost completely blinded ancient interpreters to its human elements—its literary and grammatical construction, its history, and its literal, intended meaning.\n\nBoth Jews and Christians sought hidden meanings in the smallest jot and tittle of the text. They didn't consider God's original purpose. Like many people today, their main focus seemed to be proving their own speculations, regardless of how much they contradicted the Bible.\n\nThrough this abuse, the Bible came to be seen as a mysterious book beyond ordinary understanding. Among Jews, only rabbis were thought capable of understanding it. Among Christians, only select church leaders could supposedly unravel its mysteries. Millions today are still taught that common people can't understand the Bible and should leave interpretation to priests and preachers. This is one of Christianity's greatest fallacies.\n\n### 1. THE JEWISH METHOD OF INTERPRETATION\n\nJewish interpretation from Ezra to Christ can be traced through the Apocrypha, Philo's works, Josephus, and the Talmud. Interpreters of this period valued each letter and believed each contained great mysteries. They assigned numerical values to letters and imposed fantastic meanings on plain historical statements.\n\nFor example, the letters in "Eliezer" have a numerical value of 318 in Hebrew. Gen. 14:14 says Abraham had 318 trained servants—so this was interpreted to mean Eliezer equaled all these servants in value. "Keturah" means "sweet odor" in Hebrew. Abraham married Keturah—which was interpreted to mean he wedded a holy life.\n\nGen. 25 tells us Abraham had six sons by Keturah. Following Jewish interpretation, we couldn't believe the literal account that Abraham married a woman and had children with her!\n\nThe scribes carefully guarded against text errors, but they established oral tradition that eventually was considered equal to Scripture. Christ swept away all these traditions and accepted the plain, literal written Word as the only truth (Mk. 7:1-13). Paul likewise rebuked the Jews for prioritizing tradition over God's Word (Gal. 1:13-14; Col. 2:8; 1 Tim. 1:4; 6:20; 2 Tim. 2:14-16; Titus 1:14; 3:9).\n\n### 2. THE ALLEGORICAL METHOD OF INTERPRETATION\n\nInstead of following Christ and the apostles in taking Scripture's plain literal meaning, the early Church Fathers largely followed Jewish methods. The literal sense was buried under allegorical, moral, and spiritual interpretations.\n\nOrigen gave all Scripture a threefold meaning corresponding to body (literal), soul (moral), and spirit (spiritual or mystical). His influence lasted centuries. Clement interpreted Rahab's scarlet cord as Christ's blood. Chrysostom believed Solomon's throne's six steps represented six steps to God. Nearly everything was treated this way—the literal and historical meaning was almost entirely replaced by spiritual and mystical meanings.\n\nThis approach continued until the Reformation, when Scripture was increasingly freed from earlier spiritualizing tendencies and studied more literally. Despite this freedom, many ministers today have reverted to the unintelligent methods of the past. We should completely reject such foolishness.\n\nThese interpreters disregard the common meaning of words, grammatical construction, and God's literal intention. They force whatever meaning they fancy into Scripture, making themselves equal to God and their interpretations superior to His plain Word.\n\n### 3. THE RATIONALISTIC METHOD OF INTERPRETATION\n\nSeveral modern interpretation methods are rationalistic in spirit—substituting reason for faith and human speculation for divine revelation. They explain away the supernatural, miracles, eternal judgment, atonement, resurrection, and all biblical essentials.\n\nThese methods let people choose their own Scripture meanings—which are never what's actually written. They're lawless and irrational, with no agreement among adherents. Sin, Satan, sickness, and the realities of this life and the next are explained away as errors of mortal mind and unreal things. God's Word is nullified by human theories.\n\n## VI. Summary of the False Methods of Interpretation\n\n> 1. An excessive reverence for Scripture that manifests in searching for hidden meanings in every letter and word. This amounts to idolatry of the letter and leads away from Scripture's real meaning. This is why so many today find more written "between the lines" than in the lines themselves. They can seemingly prove doctrines never once mentioned in Scripture, yet can't seem to prove those stated repeatedly.\n\n> 2. A hostile attitude toward the text, resulting in vain attempts to eliminate the supernatural and redemption through Jesus Christ. This utterly destroys God's Word as far as real benefit in this life and the next.\n\n## VII. The True Method of Bible Interpretation\n\nThe chief fundamental principle is gathering from Scripture itself the precise meaning the writers intended. We apply to the Bible the same principles, rules, grammatical processes, common sense, and reason that we use for other books.\n\nIn doing this, take the Bible literally whenever possible. When you encounter a statement that can't possibly be literal—like Jesus being a "door," or a woman "clothed with the sun and standing on the moon with a crown of twelve stars," or "land animals coming out of the sea"—you know the language is figurative.\n\nIn such cases, find the literal truth conveyed by the figurative language. That truth will be just as real as if expressed in literal terms. After all, figurative language expresses literal truth just as surely as non-figurative language does.\n\nIn general, the true method encompasses:\n\n> 1. The primary meaning of words, their common use in the particular era, and the importance of synonyms.\n\n> 2. The grammatical construction and idiomatic peculiarities of the Bible's languages, and the meaning of both immediate and remote context.\n\n> 3. Comparison of parallel passages on the same subject.\n\n> 4. The purpose or object of each writer in each particular book.\n\n> 5. The historical background of each writer and the circumstances of writing.\n\n> 6. The general plan of the entire Bible and its moral and spiritual teachings.\n\n> 7. Scripture's agreement in its several parts, plus prophecies and their fulfillment.\n\n> 8. The manners and customs of each writer's particular age and land.\n\n> 9. Understanding how to interpret prophecy, poetry, allegories, symbols, parables, figures of speech, types, and all other forms of human expression.\n\n> 10. The different classes of people and institutions dealt with in Scripture, and applying the appropriate principles and rules.\n\nWhen you keep all these facts in mind and interpret Scripture accordingly, there simply can't be any misunderstanding. Remember this: take the Bible literally whenever possible. When language can't be taken literally, it's figurative. Then find the literal truth the figurative language conveys, just as if it were expressed in literal terms.\n\n## VIII. General Rules of Biblical Interpretation\n\n1. The entire Bible came from God and possesses unity of design and teaching. We therefore consider both Testaments equally inspired.\n\n2. We can assume no one speaks or writes without having an idea to express, and they'll use words and expressions familiar to their audience. If they use a word or figure differently from its common meaning, they'll make that clear.\n\n3. The Bible cannot contradict itself. Its teachings in one part must agree with its teachings elsewhere. Any interpretation making the Bible inconsistent with itself rests on false principles.\n\n4. Passages on Christian experience can't be fully understood beyond the letter until we enter into those experiences ourselves. Christian experience should be founded on the Bible—not Scripture interpreted through experience.\n\n5. No meaning should be extracted from the Bible except what fair, honest, grammatical, and historical interpretation yields.\n\n6. Language is an accumulation of words used to exchange thoughts. To understand a speaker or writer, you must know what their words mean. True word meanings yield true understanding. This is as true of the Bible as any other book.\n\n7. Often, fully understanding a passage requires knowing the scope or plan of the entire book. Sometimes the design is clearly stated—Proverbs (1:1-4), Isaiah (1:1-3), John (20:31), Revelation (1:1). When not explicitly stated, determine the purpose from the contents and the Bible's overall design, as clear in Jn. 5:39; 2 Tim. 2:15; 3:16-17. Some apparent contradictions clear up when this rule is followed. The difference between Paul and James is easily understood when you recognize their books' different purposes. In Romans, Paul proves a person isn't saved by works; in James, he shows a person can't remain saved without producing good works.\n\n8. Sometimes the connection is obscured through virtual dialogue between writers and unseen persons, as in Ps. 15; Isa. 52:13; 63:1-6; Rom. 3; etc.\n\n9. One of the most fundamental rules is comparing Scripture with Scripture. By strictly and honestly observing this rule, you can find the true meaning when everything else has failed. Before concluding on any topic, collect all the Scriptures on that subject and read them together. If questions remain, go over everything again until every question is resolved. A major fault among many people is accepting only part of Scripture on a subject while rejecting passages that contradict their theory. This is dishonest with the Bible and leads to darkness rather than light.\n\n10. Not only should all passages be compared until perfectly harmonized, but the words different writers use should be compared too. Words often change meaning from one age to another. The Bible was written in different lands, some parts about 1,800 years apart, so word comparison is essential to see if the same words mean the same thing across different eras. The subject matter always helps determine this.\n\n11. Sometimes a brief, seemingly obscure statement on a subject becomes perfectly clear through a longer passage on the same topic. Always explain the seemingly difficult with the simpler Scriptures. No doctrine founded on a single verse contains the whole subject, so don't be dishonest—don't force a meaning into a passage that isn't clearly there or in parallel passages. Be honest, open-minded, studious, and zealous to arrive at the whole truth and nothing but the truth. Determine whether the language is literal or figurative, whether you understand the right meaning of words and terms, and whether they have only one meaning. Choose the meaning that best harmonizes with the passage itself and all other passages on the same subject.\n\n12. Recognize the progressive character of revelation and gradual development of truth. Some truths found in seed form in the Old Testament are fully developed in the New. For example, blood sacrifices were progressively developed from Abel's time until fully culminating in Christ's sacrifice at Calvary.\n\n13. Don't carry a New Testament word or phrase meaning back into Old Testament doctrine unless both Testaments warrant it. Water baptism, the Lord's Supper, and other New Testament doctrines aren't found in the Old Testament. It's not proper to ask whether David was baptized in water or whether Saul was a Christian—these are New Testament terms.\n\n14. Don't spiritualize obviously literal passages. For example, making Canaan's natural blessings represent Heaven's spiritual blessings, or regarding Noah's ark as salvation through Christ, and countless similar interpretations. You may draw lessons and illustrations from historical passages for sermons, but in interpretation, take them literally—don't spiritualize them. Such lessons from Old Testament events form the basis of many church doctrines in some circles. Some can't discuss Christ and His bride without referencing Adam and Eve, Isaac and Rebekah, etc. If you want to prove a church doctrine, get plain passages on the subject—don't base proof on historical events that literally happened and weren't recorded as types. Always get two or three plain Scriptures to prove a doctrine, or let it go.\n\n15. Note the dispensational character of Scripture so you can place every passage in a definite period of God's plan.\n\n16. Note the three classes of people dealt with in Scripture: Jews, the Church, and Gentiles. Up to Gen. 12, the race as a whole is addressed. From Gen. 12 to the New Testament, Jews and Gentiles are addressed. In the New Testament, these plus the Church of God (made up of Jews and Gentiles) are addressed (1 Cor. 10:32).\n\n17. In all doctrinal study, keep the practical aspect in view (2 Tim. 3:16-17).\n\n18. Emphasize the comparative importance of truth. Study positive truths more than negative ones. It's more important to have faith than unbelief, to know God better than Satan. So learn more about faith and God than about unbelief and Satan.\n\n19. General familiarity with the Bible as a whole is very important. Keep reading through the Bible until its overall contents become familiar. The more you remember of what you've read, the clearer the Bible becomes.\n\n20. Keep some "musts" and "must nots" in mind while studying Scripture. Don't handle God's Word deceitfully (2 Cor. 4:1-4). Don't insist the Bible is hard to understand. Don't misapply Scripture to a subject or age it doesn't address. Don't misinterpret Scripture. Let the Bible be its own interpreter and be satisfied accepting its own authority on any subject's meaning.\n\nIn our Lord's time, accepting the mass of Jewish reasonings, traditions, and interpretations piled on Scripture was considered orthodox. Returning to the authority of Scripture itself was heterodox—this was Jesus' most serious offense to the Jews (Mt. 15:1-9; 16:6-12; 23:1-36; Mk. 3:1-6; 7:1-13; Jn. 5-8).\n\nToday, church council decisions, church leader decrees, and fanciful spiritual interpretations have nearly nullified God's Word and Scripture's literal sense. Some groups dismiss scriptures contrary to their theories by saying "that's a parable" or "that's figurative language"—as if such language means nothing.\n\n21. Words of Scripture must agree with the content and subject matter in their passages. No meaning should be given to a word that would be out of harmony with any Scripture. For example, "seen" in Jn. 1:18 should be understood as "comprehended" to harmonize with all scriptures stating that men saw God with their natural eyes.\n\n22. Pay careful attention to connecting words that link events—words like "when" and "then" in Mt. 24:15-16, 21, 23, 40; 25:1.\n\n23. Pay careful attention to prepositions, definite articles, names of different persons and places with the same name, same persons and places with different names, and different spellings by different authors in different books.\n\n24. Ascertain the exact meaning of Scripture's words. How a word is used, the subject matter, and the context often determine the true meaning.\n\n25. Note Hebrew and Greek idioms. Sometimes a person with a peculiar characteristic, or subject to a peculiar evil, or destined to a particular destiny is called the "child" of that evil or destiny (Lk. 10:6; Eph. 2:1-3; 2 Thess. 2:3). "Father" is applied to the originator of any custom or inventor of something (Gen. 4:20-21; Jn. 8:44) and is also used for ancestor (1 Chron. 1:17). "Son" and "daughter" are sometimes used for descendants or in-laws (Gen. 46:22; Lk. 3:23). "Brother" and "cousin" are sometimes used for relatives and countrymen (Gen. 14:16 with 11:31; Lk. 1:36, 58). Names of parents are used for posterity (1 Ki. 18:17-18).\n\n26. Preference is sometimes expressed by the word "hate" (Lk. 14:26; Rom. 9:13).\n\n27. A peculiar idiom concerning numbers must be understood. Sometimes round numbers rather than exact numbers are used (Judg. 20:35, 46). This explains seem\n\n---\n\nYou now have the tools: interpret Scripture as you would any other written document — in its plain, literal, grammatical, historical sense, letting the Bible define its own terms and interpret its own symbols. But tools only matter if you actually use them. So here's the honest question: Is there a doctrine or belief you're holding right now that you've never actually verified with the open Bible in your hand — that you've simply trusted because a teacher, a tradition, or a church told you so? What would change if you checked?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk4bq001wewhj9qfudjdy	cmnknk4bp001qewhjk5bcqnfj	Supplement 20 — For Lessons 39 & 40	SUPPLEMENT	1969	5	\N	t	Daniel's prophecies are the most precisely verifiable in Scripture. And Daniel's seventieth week — the final seven years — is the most detailed prophetic period in the Bible. This supplement works through the prophetic timeline so you can locate yourself in it and understand what's coming.\n\n![Supplement 20 — For Lessons 39 & 40](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsoaeVS0WxwEUd728la9hHriMsfOkGYRKDCuLZ)\n\n## God is Not Responsible for the Conditions of the Fall\n\nMany modern preachers teach that God is the one responsible for original sin and for the sufferings of His children — that He actually appoints and calls people to suffer and even determines their suffering and how far it will go. This theory completely ignores all the satanic opposition and the conditions caused by the willful rebellion of man and the Fall. It places all the blame for these miserable conditions upon God and reasons that since they exist, they must be God's will — and that Christians should accept these things as "blessings in disguise."\n\nThe Bible does not teach any of the above theories. Such teaching impeaches the character of God and makes Him the most unjust, heartless, and despotic Being in the universe. The arguments used by these modern teachers to prove such fallacies are given and refuted in the points below.\n\n### Suffering May Arise From Doing Good\n\nThis is no doubt true in the case of Christ and many others whose purpose in life was to do good. Because of holy living and preaching truth that condemned the lives of people around them, they had to undergo much suffering. But such suffering was not caused by God — it was caused by evil men and demons.\n\nNeither was such suffering that of physical sickness, sin, and failure in life. Some of them had to go without material needs at times because Christianity was a new religion and had to become established in the world. However, today, since it is the prevailing religion of our country, there is no need for Christians to suffer for lack of material needs and undergo persecutions like some of the founders of Christianity did.\n\nTherefore, such an argument — that suffering may result from doing good — does not prove that God wills and sends sin, sickness, poverty, and failure to Christians in order to keep them humble and godly.\n\nIf there is ever again persecution of Christians similar to what early believers endured, it will be necessary to suffer hardships, poverty, beatings, stonings, killings, and many other things people naturally suffer in such times. But even then, it will not be necessary for Christians to suffer sin, sickness, and failure in attaining the benefits of the gospel that belong to them under all circumstances.\n\n### Bible Characters Furnish No Excuse to Fail God\n\nThe cases of Paul, Timothy, and Epaphroditus are given by some men today to prove that all Christians must suffer. It is true all must suffer some trials, but this does not mean Christians must suffer sickness, sin, and poverty, as we have seen in Lesson Fourteen, Point VIII, 6-9.\n\nThere is no statement in Scripture that Paul had sore eyes or some disease of the body. He was beaten with rods, stoned to death, and suffered many physical sufferings at the hands of mobs — and to these infirmities he does refer (2 Cor. 11:23-31; 12:7-9). But he has never even hinted in one passage that he bore sicknesses in his own body that Christ died to heal him of.\n\nNo other case is given in Scripture where a man was in the total will of God and yet was constantly sick because of God's will. All sicknesses, sins, and failures to get what the gospel provides are not the work of God. All such is the work of the devil and the failure of man to cooperate with God and have faith for the benefits of the Gospel.\n\n### Two Kinds of Suffering\n\nConsider the suffering of Paul and Silas "because of casting out the devil from the fortune-telling girl" (Acts 16:16-24); "the suffering and death of Stephen because he preached the truth to the Jews" (Acts 7:54-59); "suffering as the result of right living" (1 Pet. 3:14; 2 Tim. 3:12); "suffering persecution for the gospel" (2 Tim. 1:8; 1 Pet. 4:14); "suffering because of a life of prayer" (Dan. 6:10-12); "suffering because of fearless preaching" (Mt. 14:1-11). All like sufferings are listed by many Bible writers as proof that it is the will of God for Christians to live in sin and to suffer pain, sickness, and poverty.\n\nThere are two kinds of suffering, and it seems that some teachers do not know the difference between the two. Naturally, all Christians will suffer persecution to the extent they are different from the world. They will suffer like the early Christians when they get power from God to cast out demons and defy modern religion in certain churches who deny the many great truths of the gospel.\n\nThey will suffer in any definite and marked conflict between themselves and the ungodly of the world — and especially between themselves and the church members who oppose the gospel. But while it is true they will always suffer these things, it does not mean that they are to live in sin, suffer sicknesses, diseases, and pains for which Christ died to take away from believers. It does not mean that Christians cannot be prosperous during this present time of equal opportunity in business between themselves and sinners.\n\nThink of it like two employees at the same company: one faces pushback from coworkers for doing the right thing, while the other avoids conflict by compromising. The first employee may face hardship for integrity — but that's different from the company itself making them sick or poor. Persecution for righteousness is one thing; bondage to sin and sickness is another entirely.\n\nIf Christ and the gospel cannot save from sin and deliver one from its power and dominion, then all men are sinners and all will go to Hell. If there is no power in Christianity to make men live free from sin and to make them different from sinners, then there is no difference and all are lost because of sinful lives.\n\nIf the gospel does not provide healing for the body and help in financial problems, Christians have no greater blessings than do sinners — so of what benefit is the gospel? If all the promises in the Bible are lies and God did not mean what He said, then the Bible should be thrown away and rejected as the rule of faith and practice. If God will not answer prayers when His people need Him, then they are no better off than sinners.\n\n### Sin, Sickness, and Poverty Not God's Will\n\nWhen men sin, it is not the will of God. It is their will in the matter, or sin would not be committed. When men are sick or fail in business and do not get answers to prayer, this does not prove that they are following the will of God.\n\nOn the contrary, it demonstrates that they are failing to appropriate the benefits of God's promises, and for this reason they are not blessed of God according to the gospel. All suffering for sin is not the will of God regardless of what kind of suffering it is. If God's will would have been done in the first place, there would be no suffering and defeat in life.\n\nIf men will only obey God and the gospel, they can and will be brought out of the sufferings which Christ bore to take away from them in this life. God does not promise to take away persecutions and warfare with demons and evil men, but He has promised to take away sin, disease, and failure in life.\n\nIf men want to suffer these things by following the doctrines of unbelief and failure — thinking they cannot live godly and humble lives — that is their loss. Many are seeing the truth and are getting free from sin, sickness, and poverty, and all other men can enjoy these blessings if they follow the truth of the Bible.\n\n### God Permits or Allows Suffering\n\nGod permits or allows all kinds of sufferings, but this does not mean that He sends them. He does not send persecutions or warfare with demons and evil in the world, but He does permit it because His people live in a fallen and rebellious world.\n\nTherefore, we repeat it again: this kind of suffering is not the same as the needless suffering of Christians when they give in to the devil, making them sinful, sick, and defeated in life, and which robs them of the benefits God has promised to give His people while in this life.\n\nIf Christians suffer sin, sickness, or failure in this life, it is not that it is sent by God. It is allowed only in the sense that God does not interfere with people who choose to live in defeat. God allows it in the same way that He would allow a person to deliberately jump in front of a train to commit suicide. Certainly God allows it just because He does not break His own laws to forcefully keep a man from doing such a thing. He allows His people to sin if they want to, or to get sick if they want to.\n\nIt's similar to a parent who warns their teenager not to touch a hot stove but doesn't physically restrain them every moment. If the teen touches it anyway, the burn isn't the parent's doing — it's the natural consequence of ignoring the warning.\n\nWhen we use the argument that God allows certain sufferings and therefore that such must be His will and His best for men, this demonstrates ignorance of truth. God allows the devil and demons and fallen men to continue in rebellion, but this is not His will. He will take action in due time to put down this rebellion, but until that time comes they are simply allowed to operate as they can to defeat God and His plan.\n\nThat God allows men to rebel does not mean that He is personally responsible for the sin and rebellion or their effects. So let us be sensible and realize that certain things are the result of wars between good and evil, and as long as two opposing forces have power to fight each other, certain conditions will be created that could not be otherwise.\n\n### What to Do to Get Deliverance\n\nIf Christians are attacked by the devil or if they have contracted some disease, and they fail to immediately rebuke such attacks, they are liable to normal consequences. Children of God should learn how to ward off such attacks and resist the devil (Jas. 4:7; 1 Pet. 5:8-9).\n\nThey can be immune from sicknesses and diseases (Ps. 91; Lk. 10:19). They can pray and resist business failure just as much as they can sins and sicknesses. They can pray for wisdom and God will give it to them liberally and He will not upbraid them for asking (Jas. 1:4-8).\n\nSuch wisdom will enable the child of God to be a business success and it will help him in every problem of life. These blessings are for all children of God alike, and if only some avail themselves of them, it does not prove that these benefits are only for a special few. It simply proves that some have faith and some do not, that some understand the truth and others do not, and that some cooperate with God and others do not.\n\n### Christians Are Not to Display Satan's Works\n\nIt is argued by one writer that Paul's sufferings and afflictions as stated in 2 Cor. 6:4, 5 made him a public display of the sufficiency of God's grace, and that he proved it by the afflictions he carried around and displayed before men. This author teaches that Paul had sore eyes and bodily diseases as part of his display to prove to men that God's grace is sufficient to help people be true to God in sickness and to be satisfied that they are in the will of God.\n\nThis is nothing more than an accusation against God. This theory alleges that God is the author of sickness and that He sends His people out into the world to demonstrate the work of the devil before men. What a concept of God! How could we think that God would be guilty of such a thing just to prove to men how wonderful He is and how He loves His children?\n\nThe very devil himself blushes at such ignorance of truth, and he is happy to get men deceived enough to blame God for his own work. Such teaching only glorifies Satan, and such a demonstration of sickness is a mark of the work of the devil instead of the work of God.\n\nNo human being could believe that any parent would show any love or kindness at all for an offspring that he deliberately makes sick, helpless, poor, defeated, and full of pain and misery. Human beings would rise up against any parent who would deliberately cause these conditions in the life of one child. Such a parent would be dealt with by law and the child would be taken from him. No person knowing of such a condition would or could have any respect for a parent that would act in such a low and degraded manner.\n\n### The Effect of Such Teaching on Sinners\n\nIs it any wonder that multiplied thousands of sinners — in observing how the children of God display the wares of the devil as being of God — are little interested in God or His religion? Think of it: sickly, helpless, painful, miserable, sinful, depraved, and cursed people are running around claiming to be Christians and claim to be truly displaying the wares of God as taught by the Bible.\n\nSinners continually reason that if this is a sample of how God loves His children, and if that is the lot they are expected to choose if they quit sin and the devil to serve God, they do not want any part of it. Sinners see how much better off they are than Christians. They see that many sinners are prosperous in life while they hear preachers in almost every church say that Christians should not expect to have good things in life to enjoy and that they are to be poor and almost God-forsaken in order to qualify for Heaven.\n\nSinners see the saints pray and pray, and seldom if ever are their prayers answered; thus they wonder what it is all about. They wonder whether God is in existence or not and why He is so far off. They wonder why it is so hard to get Him to do the least thing for His children who have forsaken all for Him.\n\nThey see the Christians who are sick and miserable while many of them are well and healthy, and they naturally wonder whether they ever want that kind of religion or not. And one cannot blame them, for if that is the kind of blessing God bestows upon men who give their all to Him, it certainly is not very inviting to become a Christian.\n\nEven if some Christians recognize that the devil is responsible for some of these conditions while they themselves are responsible for the rest, there is very little difference in what sinners really see in these Christians and in the others who claim that God is responsible for all these curses. They see that both classes of Christians are being defeated by the demons who are the main cause of such conditions. They reason that if Christians cannot get these benefits, why should they try to get them.\n\nThe picture of the true conditions of Christians in modern churches is very black and discouraging to sinners. They should see — not the demonstration of satanic powers in cursing men with pain, sickness, poverty, and sin — but mighty demonstrations of the power of the Holy Spirit in destroying these works of the devil in the lives of believers.\n\n### Christians Should Be Free from Satan's Works\n\nChristians do not have to demonstrate the works of the devil, and it is the greatest curse to the Christian religion for them to do so. They can live free from sin, free from sickness and pain, free from want and poverty, and free from failures in life along every line. The Christian should be an outstanding example of the power of God in living free from these curses, as we have proven in Lesson Fourteen.\n\nPaul's afflictions are listed in 2 Cor. 11:23-31 as being the result of mobs, being shipwrecked, and of perils and persecutions of all kinds by men — instead of being the work of the devil in bodily diseases. It was in these necessary sufferings for the gospel that he sought to be approved of God, and this is all right if anyone is ever called to undergo such persecutions for the cause of Christ.\n\nBut who in present-day Christianity in so-called Christian lands are called to go through these sufferings? The modern Church has substituted the works of the devil that God wants to deliver them from as the sufferings Paul and all Christians endure. This is not true, and it never will be true. This is the deceptive work of the devil to make believers satisfied to carry about in their bodies the works of demons under the guise of suffering for the gospel like early believers did.\n\n### God is Not Glorified by Sickness\n\nSome preachers contend that God gets glory from sickness and even death, and they give as proof the case of Lazarus, of whom it was said, "This sickness is not unto death, but for the glory of God, that the Son of God might be glorified thereby" (Jn. 11:4). They contend that many souls have been saved through sick Christians.\n\nBe that as it may, it cannot be said that sickness ever saved anyone. People are not saved through sickness but through faith and repentance to God through Christ (Eph. 2:8, 9; Rom. 10:9, 10). Sickness might have caused some to turn to God just like other calamities that have come to people.\n\nTo use Lazarus as an example that God gets glory through sickness — and thus encourage men to accept diseases as the means of glorifying God — is foolish in the extreme. The truth is that God never would have received glory from Lazarus if He had not healed him. If Lazarus had remained dead, God would not have been glorified.\n\nSo it is with others: God cannot get glory as long as men are sick or dead. If they want God to get glory, then they should permit God to heal them, so He can get glory by manifesting His power over the power of the devil. But men use Lazarus as an excuse for their unbelief and to teach that God gets glory while men are sick.\n\nIf men who are Christians want to remain sick and thereby glorify Satan, that is their business. But it should be clearly understood by them that they are voluntarily yielding to the devil and permitting his work in them and that they are robbing God of the glory that is due Him.\n\nIf Christians would only acknowledge the truth concerning the true source of their sickness as being of the devil — and that God longs to heal them so that He can get glory — many of them would be stirred up to active cooperation with God to get healed.\n\n### Modern Experiences Unsafe to Follow\n\nOne preacher writes, "What difference does it make if we are sick or if we are well, just so we are what we are for the glory of God. It is not a pleasant thing to be sick. The writer knows, for he has suffered eighteen years with that dreadful affliction—arthritis. Even now, as I write this book, I know what pain and suffering is, and I know what it is to have burning joints, aches and pain. I know what it is to have sleepless nights and a weak body. But why should I complain because I have arthritis, why should I rebel against God's plan and God's will for my life? If God can get more glory by my being sick and afflicted, Amen! . . . through my afflictions I have been used of God to lead many souls to Christ . . . Some of God's greatest soul winners have weak bodies, and are sickly."\n\nWhat ridiculous teaching! The very devil himself could not give better teaching to the Church to suit his purpose than this preacher has given. The devil is highly pleased with such leaven in our Christian Church and in "gospel" (?) literature. This is purely one of the doctrines of the demons that Paul predicted would be followed in the last days (1 Tim. 4; 2 Tim. 3:5).\n\nThis same preacher and thousands like him are reaping the fruit of their own teaching. If I taught that God gets glory out of sickness, pain, failure, and needless sufferings that glorify Satan more than God, I would want to become more sick in order to get deeper in the will of God in order to glorify God all the more.\n\nBut this preacher says that he has had three operations and has spent much money in trying to get well. By his own teachings, wouldn't we have a right to conclude that he is trying to get well in order to get out of the will of God and in order to rob God of the glory that He gets by having His children sick? What utter foolishness and fallacy!\n\n### God is Glorified More By Healthy Bodies\n\nWhat difference does it make whether a child of God is sick or well? It makes all the difference in the world! God can naturally get more glory out of healing than in refusing to heal. He can get more glory out of well bodies that can labor more for Him than out of sick, sinful, painful, helpless bodies that can only lay around groaning under the so-called loving-kindness of God.\n\nHe can get more glory out of people who demonstrate by their lives that God is alive and answers prayer — and out of healing bodies and thus proving to the world that God does care and make provision for the welfare of His people.\n\nThe above-mentioned preacher has suffered needlessly for eighteen years and has caused perhaps thousands of people to become unbelievers in God and in answered prayer by his testimony that God has not heard his prayer and has permitted him to suffer that long.\n\nSuppose he has led a few souls to Christ in spite of, not because of, his suffering — would he not have won many more to Christ if he had been well enough to devote more time and strength to winning souls? Any cripple can do some things in spite of his handicap, but how much more could he do if he was not so helpless? So it is with deceived Christians who are content to carry about in their bodies the works of the devil just because they get a little Christian work done.\n\n### Christians Are Deceived By Satan\n\nThe devil is a slick one. He not only makes men sick but he makes them believe that his work in them is the work of God and the will of God and that such glorifies the Heavenly Father.\n\nHe gets men to teach that because some souls are saved through men who suffer, it must be the will of God for them to be in this state or souls would not be saved. He gets them to believe that sick people can win more souls than well people. He gets them to teach everyone they can to accept sickness and needless sufferings as the will of God because God blesses them in such unbelieving, helpless, sickly, and defeated condition.\n\n### It is Time to Wake Up\n\nMy friends, sooner or later we are going to have to wake up to such deceptions of the devil — and why not do it now, once and forever? Let us throw off such a yoke of bondage and such slanderous teaching against God and His truth. Let us reject such unscriptural theories that cause us to spend sleepless nights, suffer untold pain and agony, and be defeated by the demons of Hell who blame God for their own work.\n\nGod gives His beloved sleep, sweet sleep, and not sleepless and painful nights (Ps. 4:8; 127:1-2; Prov. 3:21-24). God wants His workers to be free from burning joints, aches, and pain, so they can carry on His work (Ps. 91; 103:1-4; Mt. 7:7-11; 21:21-22; Mk. 9:23; 11:22-24; Jn. 14:12-15; 15:7, 16; 16:23-26; 2 Cor 1:20).\n\nFor God to will otherwise or refuse to make health provisions for His workers is to place Him on a lower and more inhuman and unjust plane than ordinary business corporations which make the best provisions possible for their workmen so that they can get the highest degree of service out of them.\n\nGod wants every child of God — and even sinners — to rebel against sin, sickness, pain, want, and poverty in life as not being the will and plan of God for man. So when preachers resign to the work of the devil in them to the point where they seek to get others to become resigned to accept the work of the devil as the work of God, we are in need of better and more enlightened leadership in Christendom.\n\nIt is not God's plan or will that any man should be sick and defeated in life. God cannot get more glory out of anyone who is in this state than if he were free from these hell-destroying forces. It is a lie that any man can win more souls for God while under physical handicaps than he could if he were free from such.\n\nJust because some sickly people might work for God more than some well persons — and thereby win more souls than do those who are well — does not prove that one can win more souls to God while he is sick than if he was well. This is just another trick of the devil to deceive men.\n\n### Christ is Our Example in Life\n\nJesus Christ is our example — and not some modern unbelieving, deceived preacher who seeks to hide his failure and his unbelief by such theories as those above. Jesus was not a sickly, helpless human being. He was full of life and vitality.\n\nHe knew no sin and experienced no sickness until the time He bore the sins and sicknesses of all men in His own body on the cross (Mt. 8:16, 17; 1 Pet. 2:24; Isa. 53). If not one apostle or one Christian at any time in this whole age ever got the full benefits of the gospel or followed the example of Christ to the letter, Jesus would still be the one to pattern after, for "as he is, so are we in this world" (1 Jn. 4:4, 17; 1 Pet. 2:21). Every Christian can get what he wants from God just as Jesus did (Mt. 7:7-11; 21:21-23; Mk. 9:23; 11:22-24; Jn. 15:7, 16).\n\nJesus taught us that God is more loving and good to His children than earthly parents are (Mt. 7:7-11; Lk. 11:1-13). James taught us that every good and perfect gift comes from the Father of lights and that God would give all things liberally to them that would ask in faith (Jas. 1:4-21).\n\nPaul taught that men can have all the gifts of the Holy Spirit and that they do not need to "come behind" in any gift from God (1 Cor. 1:7; 12:1-11). He taught that all the promises of God are yea and amen to them that believe (2 Cor. 1:20), that men can be filled with all the fullness of God and get what they want from God (Eph. 1:3-19; 3:13-20; 6:10-18), and that men can get from God exceeding abundantly above all that they ask or think (Eph. 3:20; Phil. 1:6; 4:5-7).\n\nIn his writings Paul refers to the sufferings experienced by the early saints. It is clear that the sufferings he spoke of were not the pains of sicknesses and disease brought on by the devil, but were persecutions from enemies of the gospel.\n\nWe covet for you the simple faith and the true knowledge of the truth so that you can be made free from every work of the devil, every spirit of bondage, and every fallacy that so many people consider to be of God. If you will read the references themselves, you can see that we are sticking to what is plainly written in the Bible.\n\nYou are getting the full truth of God's Word on every question. The responsibility of getting the benefits promised by God is up to you as an individual. If you fail to get what is for you, do not blame God. Always clear Him of all wrong whatever you do. God does not fail men nor go back on His Word, so if you fail to get what God has promised, be honest to acknowledge the source of your trouble as being from demons and from unbelief and failure on your part to conform to truth.\n\n## Ten Questions For Supplement Twenty. Do You Know:\n\n- **That there will be no second chance for the dead to be saved?** The so-called Jehovah's Witnesses claim that all the wicked dead will be resurrected and given a second chance to live and be saved in the Millennium. Then those who do not accept salvation in this period will be annihilated. This is a false teaching, as are practically all the teachings of this cult. The Bible teaches that it is appointed unto men once to die and after this the judgment, not after this a second chance to be saved (Heb. 9:27). It further teaches that the wicked dead will not be resurrected until after the Millennium and then they will be judged and cast into the Lake of Fire, not given a second chance (Rev. 20:4-15). The wicked will never be annihilated, as we have already proven in Lesson Thirty-seven.\n\n- **That animal sacrifices will again be offered to God in the near future?** The Jews will build a temple at Jerusalem and will again offer sacrifices to God until the middle of Daniel's Seventieth Week, at which time the Antichrist will break his seven-years covenant with them and will do away with these sacrifices and set up himself in the temple as God (Dan. 9:27; Mt. 25:15; Rev. 11:1-2; 13). The Jews were to be without sacrifices for many years until they returned to their native land again (Hos. 3:4-5). This prophecy will soon be fulfilled to the letter.\n\n- **That modern Syria, Iraq, and Iran will become one state?** All these modern states were parts of the Syrian division of the old Grecian Empire. As we have seen in Lesson Thirty-nine, this original division of Greece will be one of the ten kingdoms of Dan. 7:23-24 and one of the four kingdoms of Dan. 8:20-23; 11:1-45 from which the Antichrist will come. Syria must gain control of the original territories that it ruled over in days of old in order to fulfill the above prophecies. Antichrist will reign from the future city of Babylon on the River Euphrates until he breaks his covenant with the Jews and takes Jerusalem as his capital. He is called the king of Babylon in Isa. 14. Since Babylon must be rebuilt to fulfill Scripture and since it will be located in modern Iraq and will be the capital of Syria in the future, Iraq must become a part of Syria. Since the other states mentioned above were parts of ancient Syria, they must also become a part of the greater Syria of the future in order to fulfill prophecy.\n\n- **That the Arabs will protect Israel during the great tribulation?** This seems impossible as we look at the present enmity between the Jews and Arabs, but present enemies will make friends in the latter days. The Antichrist will seek to control all of Europe, Asia, and Africa when he comes. He will gain control of the ten future kingdoms that will be formed inside the Roman Empire. He will enter Palestine and make Jerusalem his capital during the last 3½ years of this age. The Jews will have to flee into the wilderness of Edom and Moab, or modern Arabia and Trans-Jordan, which part of the world escapes the Antichrist (Dan. 11:40-45; Isa. 16:1-5; Ezek. 20:33-39; Hos. 2:14-16; Mt. 24:15-16; Rev. 12:6, 13-16). This will be the only part of the world next to Palestine that will escape the Antichrist and it will be the only place that the Jews from Judea can flee for protection.\n\n- **That priests and Levites will again be prominent in the new nation of Israel?** This is clear from the fact that animal sacrifices will be offered in the future in the Jewish temple as stated in Question 2, above. Then too, in Ezek. 40:1–48:35 we have several chapters giving the dimensions of the future temple and of the sacrifices, feasts, and all the solemnities of the house of Israel that will be observed in the Millennium. Priests and Levites are mentioned as continuing with such worship forever (Ezek. 43:7, 19, 24; 44:5, 9-31; 45:17; 46:14; 48:35; Isa. 66:21).\n\n- **That the Dead Sea will soon become clear water and a great place for fishing?** This will be in the Millennium following the second coming of Christ. At His coming the Mount of Olives will pass away and a very great valley will be made. The Dead Sea will be raised and an outlet will be made permitting the water to be kept fresh. That it will be a great fishing place is clear from Ezek. 47:1-12.\n\n- **That King David will soon again reign in Jerusalem over the whole house of Israel?** This will be true in the Millennium and all succeeding ages. There are a number of passages which mention David as being resurrected from the dead to reign again over all Israel (Jer. 30:9; Ezek. 34:24; 37:24-28; Hos. 3:4-5). This proves that David will have a higher position in the eternal kingdom than will the twelve apostles who will have only one tribe each (Lk. 22:28-30). Jesus Christ will be over David and all other kings and priests of that period. He will be King of Kings and Lord of Lords (Rev. 17:14; 19:12, 16).\n\n- **That a literal river will be made between the Mediterranean and the Dead seas and fish will go from one sea to the other forever?** This is clearly stated in Ezek. 47:1-12; Zech. 14:8. In these passages we have the fact that living waters will go forth from Jerusalem flowing into both seas, that fish will go from one sea to the other, and that there will be fish in the Dead Sea as there are in the Mediterranean. This river will start from under the Millennial temple and will flow south through Jerusalem and then south of the city. It will part into two branches and half of it will flow into the Mediterranean and half of it into the Dead Sea, causing the waters of the sea to be healed.\n\n- **That all the people on Earth will soon have but one religion?** This event will take place after the second coming of Jesus in the Millennium and forever. Malachi predicts that the name of the Lord will be praised from one part of the Earth to the other and that it will be honored among the heathen in every place (Mal. 1:11). Zechariah speaks of the Lord being king over all the Earth and that all nations shall go up from year to year to worship the Lord at Jerusalem (Zech. 14:9, 16-21). Isaiah speaks of the universal knowledge of the Lord\n\n---\n\nDaniel wrote six centuries before Christ and predicted events that history has confirmed to the year. The same precision applies to the events that haven't happened yet — including the final seven years. So here's the question: Given the precision and reliability of Daniel's prophecies, how does that level of divine foreknowledge change the way you hold uncertainty in your own life? If God knows the end from the beginning at a prophetic level, what does He know about your situation?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk44k0007ewhjk1o0sfhw	cmnknk44i0001ewhju56drbw5	Lesson 4 — The Truth About God	READING	4275	5	\N	t	What if the God most people believe in — even most Christians — isn't the God the Bible actually describes? Not in the big things like omnipotence or love, but in the specific, material details: what He looks like, where He exists, how He relates to the Son and the Spirit, what kind of Being He actually is. These aren't abstract philosophical puzzles. How you picture God shapes how you pray, how you trust, and whether you expect Him to show up in any concrete way in your life.\n\nLast time we established that the Bible interprets itself in plain language — that we don't need to spiritualize or mystify what God says plainly. That principle becomes critical now, because the doctrines we're about to examine are among the most spiritualized and mystified in all of theology. Let's let Scripture speak for itself.\n\nIt seems like many think the best way to honor God is to turn Him into a big mystery. This is one of the greatest errors in the Christian Church today.\n\nBut here's the thing: there's simply no good excuse for this kind of ignorance about God. The Bible contains over 20,000 references about Him, spelling out in remarkable detail what He's like, what He can and cannot do, and what He has done and still plans to do. We'll magnify God best by believing and teaching everything the Bible actually says about Him. We can develop a comprehensive knowledge of God from these many references—if we'll just believe what they say and stop treating everything God says about Himself as somehow untrue.\n\nOver the centuries, people have spiritualized and twisted so many statements about God in Scripture that they've essentially emptied the Bible's true revelation of Him. Think of it like taking a clear photograph and applying so many filters that you can't recognize the original image anymore. That old approach hasn't given us a true, sensible, simple knowledge of God, so why not try something different?\n\nWhy not believe what God says about Himself in the same straightforward way we understand similar language when it's used about other people? Why not trust that God means what He says? He should know more about Himself than any human being does!\n\nWouldn't it be easier to believe what He actually says about Himself than to believe things He never said? If God didn't mean what He said, why would He say such things in the first place? Why should it be some kind of unforgivable error to believe the Bible literally on this subject, just as we believe it literally on other topics? Taking God at His word simply makes better sense—it gives us a clearer, more "common sense" understanding of who He is.\n\nWe've all been taught that God cannot be understood, and nearly every theology book makes the subject of God vague and unreachable for ordinary people. Such books are quite good at explaining away what the Bible actually says about God! For once, let's set aside all that confusion and simply stand with what the Bible plainly states. That approach certainly can't make things any more mysterious than they already seem.\n\nWe can't go wrong if we simply believe what God reveals about Himself. Since we've already seen how to understand the Bible literally, let's be wise and follow this practice with every subject in Scripture—not just the ones we've chosen to take at face value.\n\nHere's something that strikes me as odd: people can understand all about the Lord's Supper, baptism, or any other topic from just a handful of passages. But when it comes to God Himself, suddenly they claim they can't grasp anything about Him—even though there are thousands of passages on the subject! That attitude just doesn't make sense.\n\nSo let's study God from a different angle. Let's believe what the Bible says and see for ourselves that this subject has been greatly misunderstood.\n\n---\n\n![Lesson 4 — The Truth About God](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8Oss4w1BqnIFYWHUNhmRXnATM2qSZzVGaodwQ5D)\n\n## I. Definition of the Terms Used\n\n### 1. GOD\nThis word simply means deity or divinity. It's a general term used both for false gods and for the true God. You can't determine how many persons are in the true deity just from the word itself—you need clear scriptures on the subject to settle that question.\n\n### 2. GODHEAD\nThis term simply means "that which is divine." It's used of Jesus in Col. 2:9, showing that He has all the qualities of divinity in His manifestation of God to humanity. It's also used of all three persons in the deity in Rom. 1:20.\n\n### 3. ONE\nThe Hebrew word for "one" in scriptures like "one Lord" (Dt. 6:4-6) and "one God" (Mal. 2:10) is *achad*, meaning to unify, collect, or be united as one. It's frequently used to describe unity: "they shall be one flesh" (Gen. 2:24); "the people is one" (Gen. 11:6).\n\nThe Greek word for "one" in "one Lord" and "one God" in Mk. 12:29, 32 is *heis*, which means to gather together in one (Jn. 11:52) and to be one in unity (Jn. 10:30; 17:11, 21-23; 1 Jn. 5:7-8). Our English word "one" can also mean "one in unity," as these passages show. Whether a particular passage means "one in unity" or "one in number" must be determined by Scripture, not by the word's meaning alone.\n\n### 4. IN\nThis word means "in union with." When used of persons, it doesn't mean physically entering into another body—except in the case of disembodied spirits or demons.\n\nWe read of God being in Christ (2 Cor. 5:19) and Christ being in God (Jn. 14:10-11, 20); of believers being in Christ (2 Cor. 5:17) and Christ being in believers (Rom. 8:10); of believers being in the Spirit and the Spirit being in believers (Rom. 8:9); and of Satan entering into someone (Jn. 13:27). But none of these mean physical entrance into another body, because all these persons have bodies and can't literally get inside each other.\n\nWhen Paul said, "I have you in my heart" and "ye are in our hearts" (2 Cor. 7:3; Phil. 1:7), he could only mean "in union with," not physically inside. It's similar to how we might say a loved one is "always in my heart"—we don't mean they're literally living inside our chest!\n\nThe Bible's teaching about interpenetration means the union of two or more persons together for the same purpose. People can be "one with" each other toward a common goal without literally getting inside each other or becoming a single person. Being united doesn't require physical contact or losing one's individual personality. You can be "in" someone and "one with" them even if you're thousands of miles apart.\n\nPeople influence each other to the point of being unified in purpose. The same is true of God and Satan, who influence people toward union with their respective purposes. So when God dwelled in Christ and Christ dwelled in God, it didn't mean they were one person or that they lived bodily inside each other. They were one in union—united toward the same purpose, in the same way that believers and Christ, or believers with each other, "dwell in" one another. "He that is joined unto the Lord is one Spirit" (1 Cor. 6:17).\n\n### 5. PERSON\nA person is anyone who can act, think, and feel; anyone capable of self-consciousness and self-determination; any individual with legal rights and duties; a rational being with bodily presence, soul passions, and spirit faculties.\n\nIn grammar, it refers to one of three separate relations distinguished by certain pronouns: the speaker (first person), the one spoken to (second person), and the one spoken of (third person).\n\nFirst, second, and third personal pronouns are all used of God the Father, God the Son, and God the Holy Spirit when they speak to and about each other, as we'll see in Lesson Twenty-seven. Both singular and plural personal pronouns are used as well.\n\n**God is a Real Person:**\n\n> (1) Personal names are given Him (Gen. 1:1; Exod. 3:13-15; 6:3; Ps. 68:4), and His names appear over 19,000 times in Scripture.\n\n> (2) Personal statements are made about God, just as with other persons. See Over 500 Scriptures Proving a Divine Trinity in Lesson Twenty-seven.\n\n> (3) Personal relationships are ascribed to God (2 Cor. 6:1; 13:14; 1 Jn. 1:3; 3:1; Rev. 21:3-7).\n\n> (4) Personal plans are ascribed to Him (Lesson One).\n\n> (5) A personal body, soul, and spirit are ascribed to Him (Point II, 7-9).\n\n> (6) Personal works are ascribed to Him. Many personal acts are recorded in the first two chapters of the Bible.\n\n> (7) Personal pronouns are used of all three persons of the divine Trinity, both singular and plural (Gen. 1:26; 3:22; 11:7; Isa. 6:8; Jn. 17:11-21). See Point I, Lesson Twenty-seven.\n\n### 6. INCARNATION\nThis means a person taking on a body as his very own, dwelling inside that body and not existing in any sense outside of it (Ps. 40:6-10; Heb. 10:5-10).\n\n### 7. FATHER AND SON\nA father is one who has brought a child into existence. A son is the one brought into being by a father. It takes two separate persons to be a father and a son—they could never be one person, but they can certainly be one in unity, just as any two people can be.\n\n### 8. TRINITY\nThis means the union of three persons—the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit—in one (unified) Godhead or divinity. All three persons are one in unity and eternal substance, yet three separate and distinct persons in their individuality (1 Jn. 5:7-8; Dan. 7:9-14; Mt. 3:16-17; 28:19; Acts 7:56-59).\n\n### 9. BODY, SOUL, AND SPIRIT\nGod the Father, God the Son, God the Holy Spirit, each angel and human being, and every separate person in the universe has a personal body, soul, and spirit, distinct from all others.\n\n> (1) **The body** of any being is the outward form or dwelling place where the soul and spirit reside (Gen. 2:7, 19; Jn. 5:28-29; Mt. 27:52; 1 Cor. 15:34-58; Jas. 2:26; 1 Thess. 5:23; Heb. 10:5-10). There are spiritual bodies and natural bodies—heavenly and earthly bodies—and both kinds are real (1 Cor. 15:40-49).\n\n> (2) **The soul** is that invisible part of all living beings that feels—the seat of affections, emotions, passions, and desires—that which gives self-consciousness and makes one a sentient being (Lev. 23:43; 1 Sam. 22:2; 30:6; 2 Sam. 13:39; 2 Ki. 4:27; 23:3; Ps. 107:5, 9, 18, 26; Mk. 12:33; Mt. 26:38; Jn. 12:27; Heb. 4:12; 10:38).\n\n> (3) **The spirit** is that invisible part of all living beings that knows—the seat of intellect, mind, and will—that which gives self-determination and makes one a free moral agent and rational being (1 Cor. 2:11; Mt. 26:41; Exod. 35:21; Job 38:8, 18; Prov. 20:27; Phil. 1:27; Heb. 4:12; Jas. 2:26; 1 Thess. 5:23).\n\nThe Bible teaches that unsaved people do not have God's Spirit in them (Rom. 8:9-16; 1 Cor. 12:13; 2 Cor. 1:22; 6:16-18; Gal. 3:2-5; 4:6; Eph. 1:13; 2 Tim. 1:7; 1 Jn. 4:4-6); that God's Spirit isn't received until the New Birth (Jn. 3:1-5; 14:17; Rom. 8:9-16); that there's a difference between the natural spirit of humans and animals (Eccl. 3:21); that there's a difference between breath and spirit (Job 34:15; Ps. 19:7; Lk. 12:19; 1 Cor. 5:5; 2 Cor. 7:1); and that each creature has a separate spirit which is not part of God (Num. 16:22; 27:17; Eccl. 3:21; Ezek. 21:7; Dan. 2:1-3; Mal. 2:15-16; Prov. 16:2; 1 Cor. 2:10-12; 14:32; Heb. 12:9, 23; 1 Thess. 5:23).\n\nTry substituting "breath" for "soul" and "spirit" in all those passages—you'll quickly see how ridiculous that meaning becomes!\n\n---\n\n### The Invisibility of God\n\nWhen we use the word "invisible," we shouldn't think of it only in terms of substance, but mainly as distance—beyond eyesight, beyond natural visibility. God has been seen with natural eyes many times, so His invisibility isn't something beyond spiritual or physical possibility (Point II, 3, below).\n\nIn eternity, He will be seen by people as clearly as we see each other now (Rev. 21:27; 22:4-5; Ezek. 43:7; 48:35). He can appear visible or invisible to natural eyes right now; and when our eyesight is adjusted to see spiritual things, spiritual sight will be as natural and normal as physical sight is today. Angels and other spirit-beings have also been seen by natural eyes (Lesson Six, VI).\n\nThink of it like this: our eyes currently aren't equipped to see certain things—even some material things—that we'll be able to see when God removes the veil of darkness spread over all nations since humanity's fall (Isa. 24:21-22; 25:7; 1 Cor. 13:12). At that time, the sun's light will increase sevenfold, and the moon's light will be as bright as our current sunlight (Isa. 24:23; 30:26).\n\nEven the invisible soul and spirit, or inner person, has been and can be seen (Lk. 16:19-31; Rev. 6:9-11). Therefore, it's possible for all spirits and spiritual things to be seen. Sin has dimmed our natural sight so that now we see "through a glass darkly" (1 Cor. 13:12).\n\nNo one can claim with scriptural authority that God consists of some kind of invisible substance that can never be seen or touched. In fact, God will live among people in visible form forever (Rev. 21:3-7; 22:4-5).\n\n---\n\n## II. True Interpretation of God as Spirit (Jn. 4:24)\n\nIf we can come to understand what spirit-beings are actually like, we can begin to comprehend God as Spirit. Hundreds of clear scriptures help us gain this knowledge.\n\n### 1. Heavenly and Earthly Bodies\n\nThe Bible declares that there are heavenly and earthly bodies—"there is a natural body, and there is a spiritual body" (1 Cor. 15:35-58). From this passage we learn that all created things have their own distinct bodies: bodies for grain, fish, birds, animals, humans, and every living thing on earth; bodies for the sun, moon, stars, and all celestial objects; bodies for angels, cherubim, seraphim, and all spirit-beings.\n\nNowhere in the Bible is there an exception stating that God alone, of all beings in the universe, lacks a body.\n\nWhen Jesus said, "a spirit hath not flesh and bones, as ye see me have" (Lk. 24:39), He certainly wasn't implying that spirit-bodies aren't real and tangible. He was simply teaching that spirit-bodies aren't made of earthly flesh and bone. He couldn't have meant that God lacks a real spirit-body, because He taught elsewhere that God has a voice and a shape (Jn. 5:37). He also showed John in Rev. 4 and 5 that God has a body and sits on a throne just like anyone else.\n\nThe Greek word for "shape" in Jn. 5:37 is *eidos*, meaning form, appearance, shape, fashion, or sight—referring to outward form or what can be seen with the eyes, as is clear from Lk. 3:22; 9:29; 2 Cor. 5:7.\n\n### 2. Made in God's Image\n\nMoses declared that humans were made in God's image and likeness (Gen. 1:26-27; 9:6). The Hebrew word for "image" is *tselem*, meaning shape, shadow, resemblance, figure, or bodily form, as proven throughout Scripture where it's used (Gen. 5:3; 9:6; Exod. 20:4; Lev. 26:1; Ps. 73:20; 106:19; Isa. 40:19-20; 44:9-17; 45:20; 48:5; Jer. 10:14; 51:17).\n\nThe Hebrew word for "likeness" is *demooth*, meaning model, shape, fashion, or bodily resemblance, as proven in Gen. 5:1, 3; Isa. 40:18; Ezek. 1:5, 10, 13, 16, 22, 26, 28; 10:1, 10, 21-22.\n\nThe Fenton translation of Gen. 1:26-27 reads: "Let us make men under our shadow, as our representatives... So God created men under his own shadow, creating them in the shadow of God." Anything that casts a shadow must be real!\n\nPaul said that man is "the image and glory of God" (1 Cor. 11:7). The Greek word for "image" here is *eikon*, meaning likeness, profile, statue, or bodily resemblance, as proven wherever it's used (Mt. 22:20; Acts 19:35; Rom. 1:23; 8:29; 11:4; 1 Cor. 15:49; 2 Cor. 4:4; Col. 1:15; Heb. 10:1; Rev. 13:14-15; 14:9-11; 15:2; 16:2; 19:20; 20:4).\n\nThere's no question that humans were made in God's moral and spiritual likeness, but that's not what these passages are referring to—they're talking about bodily form and shape. If humans were made in God's bodily image and likeness, then God must have a body with outward form and shape.\n\nIt would make just as much sense to argue that "image and likeness" when used of idols means moral and spiritual qualities rather than physical form—yet the same Hebrew and Greek words are used in both cases! That these words refer to what can be seen with natural eyes is clear from Gen. 5:3; Isa. 40:19-20; 44:9-17; Ezek. 1:5-28; Acts 19:35; and other passages.\n\n### 3. Eyewitness Accounts\n\nBible writers not only stated that God has a body—they testified that they've seen it with their natural eyes!\n\n- Abraham prepared dinner for God and two angels, and they actually ate food (Gen. 18).\n- Jacob had an all-night physical wrestling match with God (Gen. 32:24-30).\n- Moses talked with God face to face (Exod. 33:11-23).\n- Seventy-four elders of Israel had a banquet with God on Mount Sinai (Exod. 24:9-11).\n- Joshua and all Israel saw God with a sword in His hand (Josh. 5:13-15).\n- Gideon (Judg. 6:11-23), Manoah and his wife (Judg. 13:3-23), David (1 Chron. 21:16-17), Job (Job 42:5), Isaiah (Isa. 6:1-13), and Amos (Amos 9:1) all saw God standing on the ground, sitting on thrones, and having bodily parts like humans.\n- Ezekiel saw God on a chariot and described Him as having "the appearance of a man" with loins and upper and lower body parts like a human (Ezek. 1:26-28; 10:1, 20; 40:3).\n- Daniel saw both God the Father and the Son of Man as two separate beings at the same time and place. God was on a throne wearing white clothes, with white hair. The Son of Man also had a body, wore clothes, and had hair (Dan. 7:9-14; 10:5-6).\n- Stephen saw both God and Christ at the same place with the same eyes (Acts 7:56-59).\n- John saw God on a throne, Christ symbolized as a lamb, and the Holy Spirit symbolized as lamps of fire and seven horns and eyes—all at the same time (Rev. 4:2-5; 5:1, 5-7).\n\n### 4. The Promise of Seeing God\n\nThe Bible predicts that all the redeemed will see God's face (Greek *prosopon*—countenance, outward appearance, person) with their eyes (Rev. 7:9; 14:5; 21:3; 22:4-5). The word "face" is used in Mt. 6:17; 18:10; 26:39, 67; Acts 6:15; 20:25, 38; 1 Cor. 13:12; Rev. 4:7; 6:16; 10:1; 20:11 for both God and humans—so both must have a face.\n\nAre we going to call all these witnesses liars just to match what modern interpreters say—interpreters who have never seen God themselves? Wouldn't it be wiser to trust the testimony of those who actually saw God and knew what they were talking about?\n\nModern doctrine writers assert that no one can understand God, that no one has ever seen God, and that God has no body with parts and passions to be seen. I've quoted only from prominent leaders who have had wide influence in major denominations. We can personally admire these men for their great work, but that doesn't change the fact that they're wrong on this most important subject.\n\nIt shows the modern trend to make God too mystical to understand. One writer says: "It is clearly revealed in Scripture that God is one being constituted by three persons. We give this complex Person the name Trinity... It would be folly to seek to explain this startling revelation... We can only say that we believe it because we do not comprehend it... The doctrine of the Trinity bewilders the most astute and is frankly beyond the comprehension of the most learned."\n\nWe quote from widely-used books on great Bible doctrines: "God as a spirit is incorporeal, invisible reality; has no body or parts like human beings; nothing of a material or bodily nature... God cannot be seen with the material eyes; nothing on earth to resemble Him; without parts, without body, without passions... God cannot be comprehended by the senses, but by the soul; and is above sensuous perceptions... The image of God consists only in intellectual and moral likeness; when God is spoken of as having hands, feet, eyes, hair, and other bodily parts, these are figures of speech and mere human expressions trying to convey some idea of God."\n\nSuch statements are, frankly, foolish and unscriptural. No wonder these writers can't comprehend the Trinity the way they describe it! They make such impossible claims about God that understanding becomes impossible.\n\nIf we take the Bible instead of these statements, we *can* comprehend God. The Bible does not say that God is one person made up of three persons—that could never be true. But God *can* be three distinct persons, as separate and distinct as any three people we know. This is understandable! The other idea isn't, because there's simply no such thing as three persons inside one person.\n\nGod Himself couldn't explain it that way because that's not how He explained it, nor is it how He asks us to understand it.\n\nThe Bible also doesn't say that God's bodily parts are figures of speech or mere human attempts to convey ideas about God, or that these references do away with the reality of God's body. All figures of speech actually emphasize and make the ideas they express as real—or more real—than literal language would. There can be no true figure of speech unless the idea it conveys is real. So if God's bodily parts are figures of speech, they're true figures representing real bodily parts.\n\nSince we don't have God's authority for claiming He's a spiritual "nothing," and since we only have human opinions, let's forget the doctrine that God is unreal and simply believe the Bible literally about God—just as we do about other subjects.\n\nThe statements quoted above directly conflict with Scripture:\n- God *can* be seen and *has* been seen with natural eyes.\n- God *does* have a tangible body that can be touched and can do everything any other body can do.\n- God *does* have bodily parts and passions.\n- God *can* be comprehended by the senses (the senses are part of the soul).\n- God consists of more than intellectual and moral qualities.\n- The expressions describing God's bodily parts are real and literal, not figurative.\n\n### 5. Angels and Spirit-Beings Have Bodies\n\nThe Bible teaches that angels, seraphim, cherubim, and all spirit-beings have bodies, souls, and spirits like humans, as we'll see in the next two lessons.\n\nAngels have always appeared as men in real spirit-bodies when seen with human eyes. They have bodily parts and can do everything humans can do. They can engage in physical combat. They can be bound by chains and confined to material places. They have feelings and passions. They drive horses, guard gates, and perform many activities (Lesson Six).\n\nIf they're spirit-beings with bodies, soul passions, and spirit faculties, why couldn't God also have a real spirit-body with soul passions and spirit faculties and still be divine?\n\n### 6. Understanding God Through Creation\n\nPaul makes it very clear that people *can* understand God. He gives us a simple illustration of the Godhead and says even sinners can comprehend God:\n\n"Because that which may be known of God is manifest in them; for God hath shewed it unto them. For the invisible things of him from the creation of the world are clearly seen, being understood by the things that are made, even his eternal power and godhead; so that they are without excuse" (Rom. 1:19-21).\n\nIf the invisible things, the eternal power, and the Godhead are clearly seen through visible creation, then all we need is a little common sense combined with plain Scripture to understand what the Godhead is really like. If sinners can understand God, certainly believers should be able to!\n\nThis certainly doesn't match the opinions that no one can comprehend God. It doesn't sound like God is such a mystery. If He can be clearly seen by something visible in this world, what visible thing gives us a picture of the invisible God?\n\nThe answer must be humanity. Humans have body, soul, and spirit; so if we're the visible thing that clearly illustrates God, then God must also have a personal body, soul, and spirit.\n\nIf the Godhead consists of three separate and distinct persons, as plainly stated in 1 Jn. 5:7-8, then we should understand that each person has a personal body, soul, and spirit—just like each human. If there are three persons in the Godhead existing as "one," we must understand this oneness the same way we understand several people being "one"—one in unity, as in Jn. 17:11, 21-23; Mt. 19:5; Heb. 2:11; 1 Cor. 6:17; Acts 4:32.\n\nWhat's hard to understand about this, since we have concrete examples on earth of every fact stated here?\n\n- What's hard about three persons in the Godhead being three separate persons in the same sense as any other three persons?\n- What's hard about three persons being one in unity, just as we conceive of any number of people being unified?\n- Do we have to believe that three persons must become one person to be "three in one"? Is that what happens when three people become unified in purpose?\n\nIf people can retain their individuality while being one in unity, couldn't this be true of the Godhead? Couldn't God exist as three separate persons with three separate bodies, souls, and spirits, and still be one in unity?\n\nThen why claim this can't be comprehended when we have concrete examples of this kind of unity all around us?\n\nThe Father is called God (1 Cor. 8:6); the Son is called God (Isa. 9:6; Heb. 1:8; Jn. 20:28); and the Holy Spirit is called God (Acts 5:3-4). All three persons of the Godhead are divine and can be spoken of individually as God and collectively as one God in the sense of unity.\n\nEach one is called Lord, and collectively all three can be called one Lord in the sense of unity. The Father and Son are both called Lord and God in the same passages, yet they're clearly distinguished as two separate persons (Heb. 1:8-9; Ps. 110:1; Gen. 19:24; 1 Cor. 11:3). It's similar to how any family members could individually or collectively be called by the family name.\n\nTo sum it all up: everything that could be spoken collectively of God could be spoken of each person in the Godhead individually. However, there are certain things said of each individual person regarding position, office, and work that couldn't be said of the other members:\n- Only the Father is the Father and the head of Christ (1 Cor. 11:3)\n- Only the Son was begotten of the Father (2 Jn. 3)\n- Only the Holy Spirit proceeds from both the Father and the Son (Jn. 14:16, 26; 15:26; 16:7-15; Acts 2:34)\n\nThere are many other distinctions between the three persons of the Godhead that will be covered in Lessons Twenty-one, Twenty-five, and Twenty-seven.\n\nAccording to all these points, a spirit is far different from what we've been taught. Spirit-beings can and do have real, material, tangible spirit-form, shape, and size, with bodily parts, soul passions, and spirit faculties. Their material bodies are of spiritual substance and are just as real as human bodies. This will be abundantly proven in Lesson Six.\n\nEven human bodies after resurrection will be able to pass through material substances, as Jesus did after His resurrection. He had a flesh and bone body (Lk. 24:39), yet in its changed, spiritualized, glorified state, Jesus could transform His body from one form to another at will. He could appear and disappear, pass through material substances (including closed doors), and be visible or invisible (Lk. 24:16, 31, 34, 36-45; Mk. 16:12-14; Jn. 20:14-18).\n\nIf this is true of glorified human bodies, then we can see how God, angels, and other spirit-beings can have bodies of material spiritual substance that are just as real as spiritualized human bodies.\n\n### 7. God's Spirit-Body with Bodily Parts\n\nGod has a spirit-body with bodily parts like humans. This is proven by hundreds of clear scriptures that don't need interpretation—they're too plain and literal to misunderstand. All we can do is either deny what the Bible says or believe it.\n\nIf we're not going to believe, let's at least be honest and admit we don't believe the Bible. It doesn't make sense to pretend to believe the Bible while refusing to trust it, except for a few things we've traditionally been taught to accept. If we haven't been taught to believe the Bible, we can decide right now to become full believers in everything plainly written in Scripture. If we don't, God will hold us accountable on Judgment Day.\n\nWhat does it matter if some preachers and teachers don't believe the Bible's simplicity? We can choose to believe.\n\nSpace doesn't allow quoting every passage, so we'll simply state what they reveal about God. If you want to verify these facts, read each passage yourself and see if they're not plainly written in your own Bible.\n\n**God is a person who is Spirit—infinite, eternal, immutable, self-existent, omnipresent, omniscient, omnipotent, invisible, perfect, impartial, immortal, absolutely holy and just, full of knowledge and wisdom, in whom all things have their source, support, and end.**\n\nGod is known in Scripture by over 200 names. He is described as having a body, soul, and spirit (Job 13:8; Heb. 1:3; Dan. 7:9-14; 10:5-7). He is a Spirit-being with:\n\n- A body (Dan. 7:9-14; 10:5-6, 9-19; Exod. 24:11; Gen. 18; 32:24-32; Ezek. 1:26-28; Acts 7:54-59; Rev. 4:2-4; 5:1, 5-7; 22:4-5)\n- Shape (Jn. 5:37)\n- Form (Phil. 2:5-7; same Greek word as in Mk. 16:12, referring to bodily form)\n\n---\n\nThe God of Scripture is not a vague cosmic force or a theological abstraction — He is a specific, personal Being with form, with name, with eternal self-existence, in three distinct Persons who each have their own identity while remaining in perfect unity. That's the God you're invited to know. So here's the question worth sitting with: In your actual experience of prayer and trust, which of these truths about God do you genuinely believe — and which have remained ideas you intellectually accept without ever expecting them to make a concrete difference?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk44k0009ewhji9q7qhu6	cmnknk44i0001ewhju56drbw5	Lesson 5 — The Original Creations of God	READING	1806	7	\N	t	What was happening before Genesis 1:1? Most people assume the answer is “nothing” — that God existed in some empty timeless void and then decided to create. But Scripture tells a far stranger and more dramatic story. Before Adam, before Eden, before the six days of re-creation, there was a world that existed, flourished, rebelled, and was destroyed. And the ruins of that first world are buried in one of the most overlooked verses in all of Scripture: “And the earth was without form, and void” (Gen. 1:2).\n\nLast time we examined the nature of God Himself — the eternal self-existent Being who created all things. Now we go back to the very beginning of that creation: what God originally made in the dateless past, and what happened to it.\n\nWhen we talk about God's original creations, we're looking at everything He brought into existence at the very beginning—the heavens, the earth, and everything in them. Genesis 1:1 gives us the summary: "In the beginning God created the heaven [Hebrew, heavens] and the earth."\n\nThis takes us back to the dateless past. It covers only a portion of what we call the creative ages (as we discussed in Lesson One, Point III, 1, (4))—specifically from the first moment of creation until the chaotic period of Gen. 1:2, when the earth and its original inhabitants were destroyed by the first flood.\n\nYou'll find many other scriptures that reference this same period (Job 38; Ps. 8:3-8; 19:1-6; Prov. 8:22-31; Jn. 1:3, 10; Acts 17:24-26; Col. 1:15-18; Heb. 1:1-12; 11:3; Rev. 4:11).\n\n---\n\n![Lesson 5 — The Original Creations of God](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8Osh950MK2RWrNFBdIuiJlO67t4A5yKbkf8vT0P)\n\n## I. God in the Eternal Past\n\nWhat exactly was God doing throughout the eternal past? Honestly, we don't know—He hasn't chosen to reveal all His activities from infinity (Deut. 29:29). What we do know is that He has existed from all eternity, and at some point in that dateless past, He brought the heavens, the earth, and everything originally in them into existence.\n\nThe things we see on earth today were created during six days—but that came after the destruction of the first creation of free moral agents who lived here (Gen. 1:3–2:25).\n\nScripture makes it abundantly clear that God has existed forever: "Before the mountains were brought forth, or ever thou hadst formed the earth and the world, even from everlasting to everlasting, thou art God" (Ps. 90:2). "Thy throne is established of old: thou art from everlasting" (Ps. 93:2; Prov. 8:22-31; Mic. 5:2; Heb. 9:14; Jn. 1:1-3; Rev. 1:4-8; 4:8; 22:5, 13).\n\nAnd Scripture also makes clear that God created the heavens, the earth, and everything in them sometime in that eternal past. "In the beginning"—whenever that was—points to the dateless past because no date is given. Anyone who tries to pin down exactly when that beginning was is adding to the Bible.\n\nSo let's leave it as it stands. It could have been millions or billions of years ago, or it could have been a shorter time. If geologists can prove the earth is as old as they claim, why argue with them? Whether they're right or wrong, it doesn't contradict Scripture since the Bible doesn't give us a time element for the original creation.\n\nHere's what we can say with certainty from Scripture: the earth is more than 6,000 years old, and there were inhabitants on this planet before Adam's day. We'll prove this from many clear scriptures in Lessons Seven and Eight. Keep in mind that the heavens and earth were created in the dateless past (Gen. 1:1), the earth was cursed and flooded because of sin (Gen. 1:2), and all of this happened before the six days of re-creation (Gen. 1:3–2:25).\n\nLucifer was already a fallen creature when he showed up in Adam's Eden. He had already ruled the earth, already rebelled, and already caused the first flood before Adam ever drew breath. Don't miss the next two lessons where we prove these facts!\n\nAnother certainty: God made His plan for the heavens, the earth, and everything in them before He began bringing them into existence. And it's equally certain that God brought them into existence—they are not a product of evolution, as some foolish people teach in their opposition to the Bible.\n\nThe Bible opens with God as Creator of all things and closes with God reigning over all things after rebellion against His moral government has been put down (Gen. 1:1; 1 Cor. 15:24-28; Eph. 1:10; Phil. 2:9-11; Rev. 21–22).\n\n---\n\n## II. The Drafting of God's Plan\n\nAs we'll see in Supplement Five, God's plan was made by and is being carried out by three separate and distinct persons. Scripture shows us that all three had their part in designing the plan now revealed in the Bible. The three members of the deity consulted together at various points in working out this plan.\n\nIn Gen. 1:1 and about 2,700 other places in the Hebrew Bible, the word for God is *Elohim*—a uni-plural noun meaning "Gods." It's actually translated "Gods" 239 times in the King James Version (Gen. 3:5; Exod. 22:28; 1 Sam. 4:8; Dan. 2:11; 4:6-9; 5:11, 14; etc.). It's the plural of *Eloah*, meaning deity or God.\n\nIf we translated this literally into English, Gen. 1:1 would read: "In the beginning Gods created the heaven and the earth." Sometimes it's used with plural pronouns, like "And Gods said, Let us make man in our image"—showing not only plurality of persons, but that all persons had the same kind of image and likeness, and that not just one of them had a spirit-body.\n\nGen. 3:22 puts it this way: "man is become as one of us," showing that all three persons in the deity were alike. The phrase "as one of us" means "like each one person of several persons of the same kind"—that's what it means wherever it's used in Scripture or any other literature. In Gen. 11:7, the Gods said, "Let us go down, and there confound their language."\n\nPlural pronouns are used of God in other places throughout both Testaments (Isa. 6:8; Ps. 2:3; Jn. 14:23; 17:11, 22). It's perfectly scriptural to use the plural word "Gods" when speaking of three separate persons in the Godhead, as explained in Lesson Four, Point I, 3, and Point II, 6.\n\nThink of it like a business partnership where three equal partners work together on every major decision. There's no sense in talking about "unity" if only one person exists. So it's perfectly natural that all three persons in the deity would participate and consult each other in drafting the plan.\n\nWhere did the Godhead dwell in all eternity? What did they all do? When and where was the plan drafted? We don't know these things. But Scripture does tell us that God planned the worlds (ages) by Christ (Heb. 1:1-3; 11:3), and by Him all things were brought into being (Col. 1:15-18; Eph. 3:9; Jn. 1:1-3, 10).\n\nThe following passages prove that the plan was made and known to God from the very beginning: "Declaring the end from the beginning, and from ancient times" (Isa. 46:10-11); "This is the purpose that is purposed upon the whole earth" (Isa. 14:26-27); "Thou hast established the earth, and it abideth. They continue this day according to thine ordinances" (Ps. 119:89-91); "Known unto God are all his works from the beginning of the world" (Acts 15:18). See also Eph. 1:4-11; 2:7; 3:5-11; 1 Pet. 1:20.\n\n---\n\n## III. The Dispensational Plan of God from Eternity through Eternity (Eph. 3:11)\n\nThe following points give you a bird's-eye view of God's entire plan as revealed in the Bible—from the eternal past to the eternal future. This outline of just thirty points is worth memorizing so you'll always have the general scope of God's plan of the ages and dispensations in your mind (as illustrated by the author's Plan of the Ages bible chart).\n\nIt's helpful to study this plan alongside the chart so you can grasp God's program from eternity to eternity. Then you can begin rightly dividing the Word of Truth by connecting various scriptures to their proper periods, ages, and dispensations.\n\n> 1. God in the eternal past (Ps. 90:2; 93:2; Mic. 5:2; Heb. 9:14).\n\n> 2. The drafting of God's plan (Heb. 1:3; 11:3; Eph. 3:11; 1 Pet. 1:20).\n\n> 3. Creation of the heavens, including the sun, moon, and stars (Gen. 1:1; Col. 1:15-18; Ps. 8:3; 90:2; 95:5; 102:25; Isa. 40:12, 26; 45:12; 48:13).\n\n> 4. Creation of the spirit world (Job 38:4-7; Col. 1:15-18).\n\n> 5. Creation of the earth. The earth made perfect the first time (Gen. 1:1; Job 38:4-7; Isa. 45:18; Heb. 11:3).\n\n> 6. Creation of "the world [kosmos, social order] that then was" (2 Pet. 3:5-9; Isa. 14:12-14; 45:18; Jer. 4:23-26; Ezek. 28:11-17).\n\n> 7. Lucifer's reign over "the world that then was" (Isa. 14:12-14; Jer. 4:23-26; Ezek. 28:11-17; Col. 1:15-18). The length of his rule is unknown.\n\n> 8. Other thrones, dominions, principalities and powers placed over other parts of the universe (Col. 1:15-18; Eph. 1:21; 3:10; 1 Pet. 3:22).\n\n> 9. The kingdom of God universal; God the Supreme Moral Governor of the universe, and all in harmony with Him (Job 38; Dan. 4:17, 25, 32, 35). The length of the rule of the universal harmonious kingdom before Lucifer started his rebellion is unknown.\n\n> 10. Lucifer, the original ruler of the planet earth, conceives the idea that he can get the cooperation of other angelic beings of the universe, dethrone God, and become the exalted supreme ruler of the universe (Isa. 14:12-14; Ezek. 28:11-17; 1 Tim. 3:6).\n\n> 11. Lucifer carries out his plans and through pride falls and incites rebellion by slander and accusations against the Almighty. He causes his own earth-kingdom subjects and over one-third of God's angels (Rev. 12:7-12) to rebel against Him. The earth enters its first sinful career (Isa. 14:12-14; Ezek. 28:11-17; 1 Tim. 3:6). The length of the uprising is unknown.\n\n> 12. Lucifer instigates rebellion and persuades everyone possible to rebel. He openly breaks relations with God and His government, and leads his rebels from the appointed place of mobilization on earth into heaven to dethrone God; but he is met by Michael and the faithful angels, and is defeated and cast as lightning back to the earth (Isa. 14:12-14; Ezek. 28:11-17; Lk. 10:18).\n\n> 13. God completely destroys Lucifer's kingdom on earth, and curses the earth by destroying every bird, animal, fish, city, inhabitant, and all vegetation. He then turns the earth upside down and by means of a great flood makes it empty and a waste (Gen. 1:2; Ps. 104:5-9; Isa. 14:12-14; Jer. 4:23-26; Ezek. 28:11-17; 2 Pet. 3:5-9). The length of the pre-Adamite flood on the earth is unknown.\n\n> 14. The Spirit of God begins to move upon the flooded earth and in the darkness which covered the waters to restore the earth to a habitable state, to create new land animals, fish, fowls, vegetation, and Adam as the new ruler of the earth (Gen. 1:2; Ps. 104:6-9). Length of the re-creation is six days (Gen. 1:3–2:25; Exod. 20:8-11; 31:15).\n\n> 15. The earth is made perfect a second time and all things in the universe are again in harmony with God as before Lucifer's rebellion; except Lucifer and his spirit-rebels are still at large in the heavenlies to further God's plan in the probationary periods of the human race should man fall (Gen. 1:3–2:25; Eph. 2:1-3; 6:10-18; Job 1:6; 2:1; Rev. 12:7-12).\n\n> 16. THE FIRST PROBATIONARY PERIOD—The Dispensation of Innocence. The length of this period was about six days. (Gen. 2:17–3:24).\n\n> 17. Lucifer (now the devil or adversary of God and man called Satan) enters the restored earth, tempts man and causes his fall, thus regaining dominion of the earth and all things therein (Gen. 3; Lk. 4; Jn. 12:31; 2 Cor. 4:4; Rev. 11:15; 20:1-10).\n\n> 18. Rebellion starts again on the earth by the second ruler of earth. Man is judged, the earth is again cursed and enters its second sinful career, and all creatures are brought under the bondage of sin and corruption (Gen. 3; Rom. 5:12-21; 8:19-23).\n\n> 19. "The heavens and the earth which are now" since the restoration work of the six days, and since the new curse on the earth await the time of the second renovation, and the third perfect state of the earth, called the New Heavens and the New Earth (2 Pet. 3:5-13; Rom. 8:19-23; Heb. 1:10-12; 12:24-28; Rev. 21–22).\n\n> 20. THE SECOND PROBATIONARY PERIOD—The Dispensation of Conscience (Gen. 3:1–8:14). The length of this period was 1,656 years (Gen. 5:1-29; 7:11).\n\n> 21. THE THIRD PROBATIONARY PERIOD—The Dispensation of Human Government (Gen. 8:15–11:9). The length of this period was 427 years (Gen. 11:10–12:9).\n\n> 22. THE FOURTH PROBATIONARY PERIOD—The Dispensation of Promise (Gen. 11:10—Exod. 12:51). The length of this period was 430 years (Exod. 12:40; Gal. 4:30).\n\n> 23. THE FIFTH PROBATIONARY PERIOD—The Dispensation of Law (Exod. 13:1—Mt. 4:1; 11:11; Lk. 16:16). The length of this period was over 1,718 years from Moses to Christ.\n\n> 24. THE SIXTH PROBATIONARY PERIOD—The Dispensation of Grace (Jn. 1:17; Mt. 4:1—Rev. 19:21). The length of this period will be from the first advent to the second advent of Christ, and the binding of Satan in the abyss at the end of this age. It has already lasted over 2,000 years.\n\n> 25. THE SEVENTH PROBATIONARY PERIOD—The Dispensation of Divine Government or Millennium (Rev. 20:1-10). The length will be 1,000 years (Rev. 20:1-10).\n\n> 26. At the end of the Millennium Satan will be loosed from the abyss to deceive the nations on the earth, then will come the last rebellion on the earth and the destruction of all human rebels (Rev. 20:7-10).\n\n> 27. The second resurrection and final judgment at the end of the Millennium (Rev. 20:11-15).\n\n> 28. The end of the earth's second sinful career by the renovation of the immediate heavens and the earth and the removal of the curse in its entirety and all its effects (2 Pet. 3:10-13; Rom. 8:19-23; Heb. 1:10-12; 12:24-28).\n\n> 29. The confinement of all spirit and human rebels of all ages in the Lake of Fire forever (Mt. 25:41, 46; Rev. 14:9-11; 20:10-15; Isa. 66:22-24).\n\n> 30. The New Heavens and the New Earth—the eternal perfect state with God all-in-all again, as before rebellion started in the universe with Lucifer and Adam (1 Cor. 15:24-28). The earth will be made perfect the third time (Isa. 65:17; 66:22-24; 2 Pet. 3:13; Rev. 21–22).\n\n---\n\n## IV. The Creation of the Material Universe (Gen. 1:1)\n\nWhen we talk about the creation of the material universe, we mean the creation of the heavens, the earth, and everything originally made in them. The heavens were created first, then the earth—that's proven in Gen. 1:1; Job 38:4-7.\n\nThe word "beginning," as we've seen, refers to the dateless past when no material universe yet existed. Otherwise, the heavens and earth couldn't be described as "created" or "brought into existence" at that time. There was no day and night yet, no time on earth or in heaven, because they hadn't been created.\n\nWhether day and night or times and seasons existed where God lived before that—Scripture doesn't say. Speculation about it is pointless since we can't prove it one way or the other.\n\nThe word "created" comes from the Hebrew word *bara*, meaning to create, to make new, or to bring into existence without using pre-existing material. This is certainly true of the materials from which the heavens and earth were formed.\n\nHebrews 11:3 tells us that "things which are seen [the visible things] were not made of things that do appear" or that were visible. If the heavens and earth were brought into existence, then at one time they clearly didn't exist.\n\n*Bara* appears 49 times in the Hebrew Bible. It's translated "create" 8 times; "created" 33 times; "make" 4 times; "Creator" 3 times; and "createth" once. The core idea is bringing something new into existence—even if that something new is made from already existing material. Perfection is generally implied, and always implied when anything is a creation of God.\n\nMoses said, "His work is perfect" (Deut. 32:4). David said, "His way is perfect" (2 Sam. 22:31). Solomon said that God "made everything beautiful in his time" (Eccl. 3:11). A perfect God simply cannot make anything imperfect.\n\n*Bara* is used only seven times in Gen. 1:1–2:4—the passage recording all the creative ages. It's correctly translated "created" each time. In all other verses of this passage, the word "made" is used. That comes from the Hebrew *asah*, meaning to make something out of already existing material, as we'll explain in Point VII below.\n\nIn Gen. 1:1, the heavens and earth are created or brought into existence. In Gen. 1:21, the sea creatures are created or brought into existence. In Gen. 1:28, man is created or brought into being. So *bara* is reserved for introducing each of the three great spheres of existence: the world of matter, the world of natural life (all living creatures), and the world of spiritual life (represented by man).\n\nThe heavens and earth were brought into existence "in the beginning," while living creatures and man were brought into existence on the fifth and sixth days during the restoration of the earth. All other work done in the six days wasn't creative in nature—it was making things from already existing material that had been created during the various periods "in the beginning."\n\nThe Hebrew word for heaven is *shamayim*, meaning lofty, sky, or the higher ether where celestial bodies revolve. It appears 657 times, and in most places should have been translated "heavens." God created the heavens—including the sun, moon, stars, all inhabitants of heaven, and everything in them. Then He created the earth with its inhabitants and everything in it.\n\nThat the heavens are inhabited is clear from Rev. 12:12; 13:6; Col. 1:15-18; Dan. 4:35. There are at least three heavens, and all were created and inhabited "in the beginning." The third heaven is God's dwelling place—a real created planet like earth, as proven in Gen. 1:1; 2 Cor. 12:1-3; Heb. 11:10-16; Col. 1:15-18; Deut. 10:14; Ps. 115:16.\n\nHere's something fascinating: astronomers have discovered planets about 150 million light years from earth. That's roughly 14,690,062,800,000,000,000 miles—the distance light travels in 150 million years at 186,000 miles per second. Astronomers say there may be other universes beyond this one!\n\nThey also tell us that each star is actually a sun to other planets, but they're so far from our solar system that they appear as small lights. About six billion stars have been discovered, and they're all suns to other planets.\n\nIf this is true, the universe is vast beyond what we can conceive. And this makes sense when you think about it—it's hard to imagine God existing from all eternity doing nothing until a few thousand years ago when He finally decided to make something. It's much easier to picture God always busy, always creating throughout the eternal past.\n\nThe Bible itself says the heavens cannot be measured and the host of heaven cannot be counted (Jer. 31:37; 33:22-25).\n\nThe Hebrew word for earth is *erets*, meaning dry ground: "And God called the dry land earth" (Gen. 1:9-10). So we can read Gen. 1:1 as: "In the beginning God created the heavens and dry land." In Gen. 1:2, that dry land is then wet land—proving it was flooded after its original creation (Ps. 104:6-9; 2 Pet. 3:5-6). We'll see this more clearly in the next two lessons.\n\n*Erets* is translated "earth" 708 times; "land" 1,523 times; "ground" 102 times; "country" 102 times; and "countries" 53 times. The heavens and earth were completed and inhabited, and then the earth was flooded as in Gen. 1:2—all before the beginning of the six days in Gen. 1:3–2:25.\n\n---\n\n## V. The Creation of the Spirit World\n\nJob 38:4-7 makes it clear that the spirit world—angels, seraphim, cherubim, and other spirit-beings—was created before the earth. The "sons of God" were present and shouting for joy when God laid the foundations of the earth.\n\nThis passage also shows that the heavens, including the sun, moon, and stars, were created before the earth, since "the morning stars" sang together when God laid the earth's foundations.\n\nScripture doesn't specifically say whether spirit-beings were created before the heavens. But if we take God's work in creating the earth as a pattern for His work in the heavens, then it seems clear the heavens were created first, then their inhabitants.\n\nThink of it like building a house before moving in the furniture. That's the logical order—make the place before creating inhabitants to live there. Since the inhabitants were part of creation and not part of the Creators (Col. 1:15-18), they naturally had to be created after their dwelling places were made. This is exactly how it happened on earth during the six days when God restored it from chaos to its second habitable state (Gen. 1:3–2:25).\n\n---\n\n## VI. How God Created and Made the Material Universe\n\nGod undoubtedly created and made the different parts of the material universe—and each thing in it—with the same care and time He used in the six days when He restored planet earth and made a new order of creatures.\n\nDuring the six days, Scripture tells us God formed each living creature and man with His hands out of the dust of the ground (Gen. 1:20-27; 2:7-25; Job 26:13; Rom. 9:20; 1 Tim. 2:13). It's clear that God not only created the heavens and earth and everything "in the beginning" (each in its own period), but He also formed everything with His hands.\n\nGod formed both light and darkness (Isa. 45:7). He didn't do this on the first day of Gen. 1:3-5—at that point He merely divided them. So they must have been created and formed before the first day.\n\nScripture also says God formed the earth with His hands (Ps. 8:3, 6; 90:2; 95:5; 102:25; Isa. 40:12); the heavens (Ps. 8:3; 19:1; 102:25; Isa. 40:12); the planets (Ps. 8:3; Isa. 40:26; 45:12; 45:13; Heb. 1:10); and all things (Prov. 26:10).\n\nWhen you study all these scriptures together with those on creation, the picture becomes clear: by the Word of God, materials were brought into existence, and then with His hands He formed those materials into everything we see. Picture it this way: as God spoke, materials came into existence, and as fast as they materialized, He used them to form all things with His hands (Ps. 8:3; 2 Pet. 3:3-9; Prov. 26:10).\n\n---\n\n## VII. When God Created and Made the Material Universe\n\nThe Bible's answer? "In the beginning [literally, by periods or ages] God created the heaven [Hebrew, heavens] and the earth"—not "Six thousand years ago God created the heavens and the earth," as many commonly teach.\n\nCan we pinpoint exactly when the beginning was? If we can, we know more than God has revealed. If we can't, then we obviously don't know and shouldn't teach that the beginning was 6,000 years ago. For all we know, it could have been six billion years ago just as easily as 6,000.\n\nWhen we talk about the six days and the creation of present life on earth, we can speak with definite biblical authority that it was about 6,000 years ago. You can see this by adding up the lengths of the various dispensations since Adam in the outline in Point III above. But nowhere does Scripture tell us the heavens and earth were originally created during those six days, about 6,000 years ago at the time of Adam.\n\nSome people use Exod. 20:8-11; 31:17 to argue that the heavens and earth were created during the six days of Gen. 1:3–2:25, making them about 6,000 years old. But these passages say nothing about the original creation of the heavens and earth.\n\nIn these verses, the Hebrew word *asah* is used—meaning to make out of already existing material—instead of *bara*, to create. These verses picture the re-creation work of the six days, not the original creation "In the beginning."\n\n*Asah* never means to create. It's translated "made" 659 times; "make" 449 times; "maketh" 59 times; "makest" 16 times; "maker" 13 times; "making" 11 times; and "madest" 3 times.\n\nWhen God said, "In six days the Lord made heaven and earth," He was referring to the restoration of the heaven (the firmament or clouds) and the earth to a habitable state—like it was before Lucifer's kingdom was destroyed by the flood of Gen. 1:2. We'll dig deeper into this in the next two lessons.\n\n---\n\n## VIII. How Long Was God Creating and Making the Material Universe?\n\nBased on what was accomplished in six days, it appears God took a very long time to originally create and form each sun, moon, star, planet, and creature inhabiting the vast universe.\n\nConsider this: God took one day just to divide light from darkness on this one little planet earth. How much time did He originally take to bring into existence all the darkness and light throughout the entire vast universe? We don't know, but evidently much longer than a day.\n\nGod took one day to divide the waters covering the earth and restore the firmament. He took another day to restore the dry land and set boundaries for the seas. Naturally, He would have taken much longer to originally bring all those materials into existence and form the waters, firmament, and earth with its mountains and valleys.\n\nGod took one day to complete solar regulation in connection with the restored earth. But He evidently used a much longer period to originally create and form with His own hands the vast heavens and all the countless suns, moons, stars, and planets.\n\nGod took two days to form the bodies of fish, fowl, beasts, man, and woman. He naturally took much longer to originally create and form every inhabitant of the vast heavens and all the animals and creatures who lived on earth during Lucifer's kingdom—long before the chaos of Gen. 1:2 and the six days of Gen. 1:3–2:25.\n\nIt's similar to comparing a home renovation project with building an entire neighborhood from scratch. If God took six days to restore one little planet and form new inhabitants, He would naturally take much longer to originally create and form the entire universe with its innumerable suns, planets, and inhabitants.\n\n---\n\n## IX. Why God Created and Made the Material Universe\n\nGod created the material universe to be inhabited by intelligent, free moral agents to whom He could reveal Himself—beings who could enjoy all the rich blessings of life and the goodness of the Creator forever. He also created plant and animal life, along with everything else necessary to sustain life throughout the universe (Ps. 8:3-8).\n\nIsaiah 45:18 tells us: "For thus saith the Lord that created the heavens, God Himself that formed the earth and made it; he hath established it, he created it not in vain [Heb. *tohu*, desolate, as in Gen. 1:2] he formed it to be inhabited."\n\nPaul explained it this way: "All things were created by him, and for him . . . that in all things he might have the preeminence . . . that we should be holy and without blame before him in love that in the ages to come he might shew the exceeding riches of his grace in his kindness toward us through Jesus Christ" (Col. 1:15-18; Eph. 1:3-11; 2:7; 3:9-11).\n\nRevelation 4:11 puts it beautifully: "Thou hast created all things, and for thy pleasure they are and were created."\n\n---\n\n## X. Creation of the Original Social System\n\nFor an unknown period, an original social system existed on this earth ruled by Lucifer—before the social system created in Adam's day. This might be a new concept for you, so we'll provide plenty of scriptural proof in Lesson Seven.\n\nFor now, it's enough to say there had to be a social order on earth for Lucifer to have a kingdom here, as proven in Isa. 14:12-14; 45:18; Jer. 4:23-26; Ezek. 28:11-17; 2 Pet. 3:5-7.\n\nAlong with this social system, there were land animals, birds, fish, and other creatures—all destroyed in the flood of Gen. 1:2; Ps. 104:5-9; 2 Pet. 3:5-7. All fossils and remains of ancient animals belong to this original period, as do the geologic formations of the earth. Demons also belong to this period, as they are not the souls of human beings—we'll explore this in the next lesson.\n\nBetween these original creations and the creations of the six days, Scripture reveals: Lucifer's rule on earth for an unknown period; his rebellion against God; the earth's first sinful career; Lucifer's defeat by God; and the overthrow of his kingdom by the first recorded flood in Gen. 1:2.\n\nLucifer was the first ruler on earth. He had already completed his period of earth rule and experienced his fall by the time of Adam. When he entered Adam's Eden, he was already a fallen creature.\n\n---\n\n## Questions on Lesson Five\n\n- What do the original creations of God include?\n- How much of the creative ages is covered by this period?\n- Discuss God in the eternal past.\n- Prove from\n\n---\n\nBefore the world you know was re-created, there was a world made perfect, handed to an anointed ruler, and destroyed by his rebellion. That’s the backdrop against which your story takes place. So here’s the question worth sitting with: How does knowing that God’s original plan was sabotaged by a creature He created perfect — yet God still chose to restore and redeem — change the way you understand your own failures and God’s willingness to restore you?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk4bq001zewhji2xzseek	cmnknk4bp001qewhjk5bcqnfj	Supplement 21 — For Lessons 41 & 42	SUPPLEMENT	699	8	\N	t	Revelation is a book of victory, not confusion — once you have the framework. And the resurrection is bodily: same person, glorified body, real existence in a real place forever. This supplement connects the macro (the sweep of Revelation) with the personal (your resurrection body).\n\nHere's the thing: there are certain earmarks of true Christians that you can always detect and confirm. These show whether a person has genuinely been born-again. There are also certain earmarks of the ungodly that are easy to spot — clear evidence that they oppose true Christianity and the Bible.\n\n![Supplement 21 — For Lessons 41 & 42](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsT8uV94uzI0Sg3uieQlJxkWsZOR6bnzGDtEw9)\n\n### "Ye Shall Know Them by Their Fruits"\n\nThis statement comes directly from Jesus Christ Himself (Mt. 7:15-23). There's never been a truer statement made.\n\nIt plainly teaches that there are ways for everyone to know who is right and who is wrong. You can discern who is for the truth and who is against it, who is a true Christian and who isn't, and who is a true teacher versus a false one.\n\nThe way to discern people is "by their fruits" — not by their outward appearance and refinement, which can be incredibly deceptive. Think of it like a rotten apple with an attractive coating of wax on the outside, colored with beautiful paint. It looks perfect, but a bite into it would be a bite into corruption.\n\nA perfect and beautiful fruit tree on the outside will sometimes be so diseased inside that it produces the worst kind of fruit. A good tree cannot bring forth corrupt fruit, and a corrupt tree cannot produce good fruit. Every tree brings forth according to its own kind — whether good or bad.\n\nSo it is with false teachers and those who follow them. Such people can appear so good outwardly. They may be more refined and correct in their behavior than many true Christians. But this doesn't prove they're of God.\n\nWe've all seen false teachers and rebels against God and the Bible who are incredibly nice, refined, polite, well-mannered, polished, and poised. You'd think they were gifts from Heaven! But in reality, they were destitute of all truth and spiritual experience — bitter enemies of the Bible.\n\n### "Beware of False Prophets"\n\nNot only are we told to beware of false prophets, but we're also warned to "Beware of men" who will cast true believers out of churches and even persecute them to death (Mt. 10:17-20).\n\nIt was the refined and popular religious leaders of the first century who crucified Christ (Jn. 18:3, 19-40; 19:6-15; Acts 2:22-24, 36). Jesus and His gospel were always at variance with the accepted religion of His day (Mt. 16:6-12; 23:13-39; Mk. 8:15; 12:38).\n\nIt was organized, backslidden religionists who persecuted the early disciples (Acts 4:1-31; 5:17-42; 6:8-15; 7:51-60; 8:1-3; 9:1-2, 21; 12:1-4; 13:44-52; 14:19; 17:5-9; 18:6).\n\n### Apostasy Predicted for the Last Days\n\nThe Bible is crystal clear that there will be a great falling away from the true faith in the last days. Let's just quote the Scriptures — that's all we need to clarify this point:\n\n> "Take heed that no man deceive you. For many shall come in my name, saying, I am Christ; and shall deceive many . . . Then shall they deliver you up to be afflicted, and shall kill you: and ye shall be hated of all nations for my name's sake. And then shall many be offended, and shall betray one another, and shall hate one another. And many false prophets shall rise, and shall deceive many. And because iniquity shall abound, the love of many shall wax cold . . . For there shall arise false Christs, and false prophets, and shall shew great signs and wonders; insomuch that, if it were possible, they shall deceive the very elect. Behold, I have told you before" (Mt. 24:4-12, 24-25; Lk. 21:8-11).\n\n> "Now we beseech you, brethren, by the coming of our Lord Jesus Christ, and by our gathering together unto him. That ye be not soon shaken in mind, or be troubled, neither by spirit, nor by word, nor by letter as from us, as that the day of Christ is at hand. Let no man deceive you by any means: for that day shall not come, except there come a falling away first, and that man of sin be revealed . . . as his coming is after the working of Satan with all power and signs and lying wonders, and with all deceivableness of unrighteousness in them that perish; because they received not the love of the truth, that they might be saved. And for this cause God shall send them strong delusion, that they should believe a lie; that they all might be damned who believed not the truth, but had pleasure in unrighteousness" (2 Thess. 2:1-12).\n\n> "Now the Spirit speaketh expressly, that in the latter times some shall depart from the faith, giving heed to seducing spirits, and doctrines of devils; speaking lies in hypocrisy; having their conscience seared with a hot iron; forbidding to marry, and commanding to abstain from meats, which God hath created to be received with thanksgiving of them which believe and know the truth. For every creature of God is good, and nothing to be refused, if it be received with thanksgiving: for it is sanctified by the word of God and prayer. If thou put the brethren in remembrance of these things, thou shalt be a good minister of Jesus Christ, nourished up in the words of faith and of good doctrine, whereunto thou hast attained" (1 Tim. 4:1-8).\n\n> "This know also, that in the last days perilous [hard] times shall come. For men shall be lovers of their own selves, covetous, boasters, proud, blasphemers, disobedient to parents, unthankful, unholy, without natural affection, trucebreakers, false accusers, incontinent, fierce, despisers of those that are good, traitors, heady, highminded, lovers of pleasure more than lovers of God; having a form of godliness, but denying the power thereof: from such turn away . . . Ever learning, and never able to come to the knowledge of the truth . . . men of corrupt minds, reprobate concerning the faith . . . Yea, and all that will live godly in Christ Jesus shall suffer persecution. But evil men and seducers shall wax worse and worse, deceiving and being deceived" (2 Tim. 3:1-14).\n\n> "I charge thee therefore before God . . . Preach the word; be instant in season, out of season; reprove, rebuke, exhort with all longsuffering and doctrine. For the time will come when they shall not endure sound doctrine; but after their own lusts shall they heap to themselves teachers, having itching ears: and they shall turn away their ears from the truth, and shall be turned into fables" (2 Tim. 4:1-5).\n\n### Ten Questions For Supplement Twenty-one. Do You Know:\n\n- That resurrected men will eat and drink forever like we do in this life? This is clearly revealed in passages that need no interpretation at all. See Lk. 22:14-30; Rev. 2:7, 17; 19:1-10; 22:1-3.\n\n- That the large oceans will cease to exist within 1,000 to 1,100 years? At the end of the thousand years' reign of Christ, the Earth will be purified by fire. The oceans will cease to exist, for John saw no more sea in the New Earth (Rev. 21:1-2). The Millennium isn't far off. In fact, prophecies are being fulfilled now that prove we're at the very end of this Age of Grace. When Christ comes, there will be only 1,000 years before the Earth is made new — without most of it being under water, as we'll see in Lessons Forty-nine through Fifty-two.\n\n- That spirit-beings are soon to appear on Earth to make war on men? In Rev. 9, we have the prediction that two kinds of spirits out of the Abyss will appear on Earth to torment men for five months and to slay one-third of men at a certain hour. This will happen during the first 3½ years of Daniel's Seventieth Week, as we studied in Lesson Forty. In Rev. 12:7-12, we have the prediction that the devil and his angels will be cast down to Earth in the middle of the Week and will destroy men during the last 3½ years of the Week. So we can say with definite assurance that spirit-beings will soon be seen on Earth and will do exactly what these Scriptures say they will do.\n\n- That millions of human bodies will be created in a moment of time in the near future? Scripture states that at the rapture, God will give a body to each resurrected saint in a moment of time (1 Cor. 15:51-58; 1 Thess. 4:13-17; Phil. 3:20-21). This will require the actual creation of millions of bodies in one moment. God will give each one a body as it has pleased Him, and to every one his own body (1 Cor. 15:35-58).\n\n- That the souls and spirits of the dead will never be resurrected out of the graves? The souls of the wicked and the righteous don't go into the graves when their bodies are buried. Therefore, they won't come out of the graves in the resurrection. Only the bodies die and go into the graves (Jas. 2:26). The souls and spirits of the wicked go to Hell, and the souls and spirits of the righteous go to Heaven until the resurrection of the bodies, as we proved in Lesson Thirty-seven.\n\n- That some of you reading these lessons may continue to live forever on Earth in your natural bodies? In other words, we're living so near the end of this Age of Grace that it's possible for some living in this generation of young people to be alive on Earth at the second coming of Jesus Christ. They could enter the Millennium as earthly, natural people, helping replenish the Earth during Christ's thousand years' reign. That there will be people continuing from this age into the Millennium is clear from Zech. 14:1-21; Isa. 2:1-4; Mt. 25:31-46 and other Scriptures. If any person alive today continues to live through the future tribulation and is worthy of entrance into the Millennium — and if they conform to God's will throughout the Millennium and don't rebel with Satan at the end of the thousand years — they'll be worthy of continuing to live on the New Earth forever. That there will be eternal generations of natural people on Earth has already been proven in Supplement Two, Question 2. In the Millennium, human life will be extended forever for those who obey God and conform to His whole will, as we'll see in Lessons Forty-nine through Fifty-two.\n\n- That one man carried on a twenty years' war single-handed against a whole nation and killed many thousands of the enemy? This man was Samson, who fought against the Philistines for twenty years. He killed from 4,000 to 6,000 men in this period by himself (Judg. 14:4, 19; 15:8, 15, 20; 16:23-31).\n\n- That one man slew 102 men while sitting down? This was Elijah, who sat on a hill and called fire from Heaven to slay two companies of fifty men and their captains (2 Ki. 1:8-16).\n\n- That two men did more for God in their death than they did in life? Samson was one of these men. He slew more of God's enemies in his death than he did in his life (Judg. 16:23-31). He killed all the leaders of the nation who held the children of Israel in bondage. Jesus was the other man. Through His death, He conquered death, Hell, and the grave, liberating all men and redeeming them to God by His blood (Col. 2:14-17; Rev. 1:5; 5:8-10; Jn. 3:16). He did more by His death than during His entire life on Earth.\n\n- That Adam was not the first son of God to be created? There were many sons of God called angels who were created before Adam. They were present when God laid the foundations of the Earth (Job 38:4-7; Col. 1:16-18). Adam was the first son of God created of the human race (Lk. 3:38), but there were innumerable created sons of God in Heaven and on Earth before Adam. The pre-Adamite races and all spirit-beings were created by God and were therefore His sons by creation, as we've seen in Lessons Six and Seven.\n\n---\n\nRevelation ends with a wedding, a city, a river, and eternal life. And your resurrection body is as real as the body you have now — only without death, decay, or pain. So here's the question: How does the concrete, physical reality of resurrection and the New Jerusalem — not 'spiritual' in the vague sense, but real and embodied — change the way you hold suffering, loss, and mortality in this present life?	\N	\N	\N
cmq76xoo40003807171yulqax	cmq76xgul000jhks877ughys4	The Law of Attraction	READING	\N	10	\N	t	![The Law of Attraction](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsZn3V6WdWQAzP5eOGYXjM0itdfBSUE9IN6u8o)\n\n## The Law of Attraction\n\nShe spent $4,000 on the manifesting course. She made a vision board with $10,000 in cash fanned in the center, the apartment she wanted, the man she wanted, the body she wanted. Every morning she stood before it and said the affirmations: *I am abundant. I am magnetic. What I desire, I attract.*\n\nWithin a year, she had the apartment. She had met someone who matched the vision board. She had lost thirty pounds.\n\nShe told everyone about manifesting. She became a coach. She charged others $4,000 for the same course. She called what had happened "alignment with the universe."\n\nShe did not examine too closely what the universe had asked for in exchange for the apartment and the relationship and the body — or why, despite having everything on the vision board, the hollow place at her center was wider than it had ever been.\n\n---\n\n## What It Is\n\nThe Law of Attraction teaches that focused thought and spoken declaration attract matching realities into one's life. *The Secret* (2006), the movement's most famous text, sold over 30 million copies. Its core claim: your thoughts and words create your reality. The mechanism: "vibrational frequency" — your dominant emotional and cognitive state determines what the universe sends you.\n\nThe practice involves vision boards, spoken affirmations, visualization of desired outcomes, and "acting as if" the desired reality already exists. It undergirds a vast industry of life coaching, business coaching, and self-help, and has infiltrated word-faith theology in Christianity.\n\n## The Demonic Deception\n\n[Genesis 3:5](data-scripture="Genesis 3:5"): *"Ye shall be as gods."* The serpent did not offer rebellion. He offered divinity — the capacity to determine reality through will and declaration. The Law of Attraction is that same offer, repackaged with New Thought philosophy and self-help branding.\n\nBiblical faith trusts God's will and timeline. Manifesting demands that the universe comply with human decree. It replaces prayer — *Your will be done* — with decree — *I declare this is mine.* The practitioner becomes the sovereign. God becomes irrelevant.\n\nDemons sometimes grant manifestations. When the vision board "works" — when the apartment materializes, when the relationship arrives — it is not because the universe responded to vibrational frequency. It is because an entity operating in the domain of provision decided that granting this request would deepen the practitioner's commitment to a system that bypasses God and creates a functional covenant with darkness. Every successful manifestation is potential evidence of demonic empowerment, not divine confirmation.\n\n## How It Opens the Door\n\n**Spoken declarations are spiritual acts.** [Proverbs 18:21](data-scripture="Proverbs 18:21"): *"Death and life are in the power of the tongue."* This principle works in all directions. Repeated spoken decrees addressed to "the universe" — which is a spiritual system, not a neutral mechanism — are spiritual transactions that accumulate into a covenant relationship with whatever is responding.\n\n**The practitioner becomes the god.** A worldview that attributes creative power to your words and your vibrational state has installed you at the center of your spiritual universe. There is no room for submission, repentance, or a Savior in that cosmology. And the entity that wants you at the center, crowding God out, is the same one that offered the same position in Genesis 3.\n\n**"Failures" create accusation.** When manifestation does not work, the system tells you your thoughts were not positive enough, your frequency was too low. This is the Spirit of Accusation working through the system's built-in failure protocol — driving practitioners into obsessive self-monitoring and deeper dependency.\n\n## What This Means for You Right Now\n\nLook at the outcomes manifesting produced in your life. Then look at the spiritual state those outcomes produced. The apartment is real. The relationship is real. The body is real. But is the God who created those things more present in your life, or less?\n\n[Matthew 6:33](data-scripture="Matthew 6:33"): *"But seek ye first the kingdom of God, and his righteousness; and all these things shall be added unto you."* The promise is real — but the mechanism is seeking God, not declaring outcomes to the universe. The one who seeks the kingdom first and finds that all things are added is not a manifestor. They are a child whose Father provides.\n\nRepent specifically for each manifesting practice. Renounce the spoken declarations and the spiritual covenants they created. If outcomes you "manifested" are now present in your life, bring them before God and ask Him to clarify whether they are His provision or the enemy's counterfeit.\n\n---\n\n*The Law of Attraction fills the mind with desire and calls it faith. The next lesson examines the opposite technique — emptying the mind and calling that faith too.*\n\n> **Community Discussion:** *Have you used a vision board, read The Secret, or practiced manifestation affirmations? What did you believe you were doing spiritually?*	\N	\N	\N
cmq76y46v0005w0yllsr9l6yj	cmq76xgul000jhks877ughys4	Wave 2 Checkpoint: The Wellness Trojan Horse	QUIZ	\N	18	\N	t	## Wave 2 Checkpoint: The Wellness Trojan Horse\n\nTen wellness practices examined. Three questions before Wave 3.\n\n---\n\n**1. The course's core argument about yoga, Reiki, TM, and chakra work is that they share a common spiritual mechanism. What is it?**\n\nA) They all originated in Asia and therefore reflect spiritual systems incompatible with Western monotheism\nB) They all invoke or manipulate spiritual forces from systems outside Jesus Christ — regardless of the user's intent, the fitness studio branding, or the therapeutic framing\nC) They are all ineffective — their perceived benefits are entirely psychosomatic\nD) They are only dangerous when practiced in group settings\n\n---\n\n**2. What does this course identify as the specific spiritual danger of the Enneagram?**\n\nA) The Enneagram creates pride by overemphasizing personality differences\nB) The nine-type system is mathematically based on numerology and therefore occultic\nC) The Enneagram's origins in Gurdjieff's occultic channeled material and its adoption of a fixed, pre-conversion identity framework conflict with the new creation theology of 2 Corinthians 5:17\nD) Churches that use the Enneagram inevitably drift toward liberalism\n\n---\n\n**3. The Law of Attraction and Transcendental Meditation are theologically opposed in method but united in destination. What destination do they share?**\n\nA) Buddhist philosophy as their theological source\nB) The replacement of God as the source of provision and guidance with either the self (manifestation) or the emptied mind (TM) — both of which create an open channel for demonic spiritual influence\nC) The promotion of materialism and self-interest at the expense of community\nD) Debt and financial instability through misapplied prosperity thinking\n\n---\n\n**Answer Key**\n\n1. **B** — The spiritual mechanism is the same regardless of marketing. The entities invoked do not change because the studio uses soft lighting.\n2. **C** — [2 Corinthians 5:17](data-scripture="2 Corinthians 5:17"): the new creation in Christ cannot be adequately mapped to a pre-conversion personality type.\n3. **B** — [Matthew 12:43-45](data-scripture="Matthew 12:43-45"): the swept and empty house is not safe. Both methods empty the self of the right occupant.\n\n---\n\n*Wave 3 opens with the theological framework that explains where all ten of these practices were pointing.*	\N	\N	\N
cmq76y46x0007w0yl63wrdhr7	cmq76xgul000jhks877ughys4	Wave 3 Checkpoint: Spiritual Seeking Gone Wrong	QUIZ	\N	26	\N	t	## Wave 3 Checkpoint: Spiritual Seeking Gone Wrong\n\nSeven forms of sincere spiritual seeking that arrived at the wrong destination. Three questions.\n\n---\n\n**1. The course describes spirit guides as fallen angels presenting in an appealing form. What Scripture does it cite as the primary proof text?**\n\nA) [Ephesians 6:12](data-scripture="Ephesians 6:12") — spiritual wickedness in high places\nB) [2 Corinthians 11:14](data-scripture="2 Corinthians 11:14") — Satan himself is transformed into an angel of light\nC) [1 Peter 5:8](data-scripture="1 Peter 5:8") — the devil as a roaring lion\nD) [Revelation 12:9](data-scripture="Revelation 12:9") — the great dragon cast out\n\n---\n\n**2. The course identifies a specific theological problem with the Contemplative Prayer Movement. What is it?**\n\nA) It was developed by Catholics rather than Protestants\nB) Silent prayer is inherently less effective than spoken prayer\nC) Centering prayer techniques produce the same emptied-mind state as Transcendental Meditation — a state of cognitive passivity that [Matthew 12:43-45](data-scripture="Matthew 12:43-45") warns creates a spiritual vacancy, and the movement's theological framework draws from sources outside Scripture\nD) Prayer without words violates [Romans 8:26](data-scripture="Romans 8:26") which requires verbal groaning\n\n---\n\n**3. What does this course identify as the specific spiritual danger of psychedelic plant medicine, beyond the legal and health risks?**\n\nA) Psychedelics cause permanent brain damage that impairs the ability to experience genuine spiritual encounters\nB) Psychedelics are addictive and therefore lead to a lifestyle incompatible with Christian discipleship\nC) Psychedelics forcibly open spiritual doors through chemical means — producing real encounters with demonic entities that the person did not consent to and cannot control, and creating lasting spiritual access points that persist after the substance wears off\nD) The shamanic contexts in which plant medicine is used are culturally appropriative\n\n---\n\n**Answer Key**\n\n1. **B** — [2 Corinthians 11:14](data-scripture="2 Corinthians 11:14"): the beauty and wisdom of the encounter is not evidence of its source.\n2. **C** — Method matters. The theological problem is the technique, not the silence itself.\n3. **C** — The door opened chemically does not close when the chemistry ends. [1 Corinthians 6:19-20](data-scripture="1 Corinthians 6:19"): the body is the temple.\n\n---\n\n*Wave 4 drops the subtlety. What follows is explicit occult practice — the enemy named by his own names.*	\N	\N	\N
cmq76y4700009w0yllvrnu53y	cmq76xgul000jhks877ughys4	Wave 4 Checkpoint: Active Occult Practice	QUIZ	\N	36	\N	t	## Wave 4 Checkpoint: Active Occult Practice\n\nNine forms of explicit occult practice. Three questions.\n\n---\n\n**1. According to this course, what is the theological basis for the claim that Wicca and witchcraft are not merely false religions but spiritually harmful practices?**\n\nA) Wicca is a relatively new religion without genuine spiritual power — its practitioners are deceived but not spiritually endangered\nB) [Galatians 5:19-20](data-scripture="Galatians 5:19-20") lists witchcraft (*pharmakeia*) as a work of the flesh, and [Deuteronomy 18:10-12](data-scripture="Deuteronomy 18:10-12") prohibits each Wiccan practice specifically — not because they are ineffective, but because they work, and the entities they invoke are real\nC) Wicca is dangerous primarily because it leads practitioners away from community and into isolation\nD) The Horned God and Triple Goddess are not spiritually real — the danger is purely theological error\n\n---\n\n**2. The course states that Ouija board experiences are not explained by the ideomotor effect (unconscious muscle movement). What evidence does it use to support this?**\n\nA) Scientific studies have disproved the ideomotor hypothesis in controlled conditions\nB) The board produces verifiable information that none of the participants consciously possess, and the consistent pattern of entity behavior — escalation, attachment, personality — is inconsistent with unconscious self-generated movement\nC) The Catholic Church has officially declared Ouija boards demonically active\nD) Ouija boards produce physical phenomena (levitation, temperature changes) that cannot be explained by muscle movement\n\n---\n\n**3. What specific mechanism makes occult tattoos spiritually significant, according to this course?**\n\nA) The artistic glorification of occultic symbols influences the viewer subliminally\nB) Tattoos violate [Leviticus 19:28](data-scripture="Leviticus 19:28") categorically, regardless of the symbol depicted\nC) The blood involved in tattooing creates a covenant transaction, and symbols dedicated to demonic entities function as permanent spiritual declarations of allegiance — marking the body as claimed territory — regardless of whether the bearer currently believes in those entities\nD) Tattoos with occult symbols attract demonic attention to the bearer from outside, like a spiritual beacon\n\n---\n\n**Answer Key**\n\n1. **B** — The prohibition is because the practices work, not because they don't. The entities are real.\n2. **B** — Information the participants don't consciously know, and behavioral patterns too consistent for unconscious movement.\n3. **C** — Blood covenant through the tattooing process, plus the symbol as a standing spiritual declaration that persists beyond belief.\n\n---\n\n*Wave 5 moves from individual practice to generational binding — the traps that don't just affect one person.*	\N	\N	\N
cmq76y472000bw0yl8li7slv2	cmq76xgul000jhks877ughys4	Wave 5 Checkpoint: Covenant Traps	QUIZ	\N	41	\N	t	## Wave 5 Checkpoint: Covenant Traps\n\nFour systems that bind beyond the individual. Three questions.\n\n---\n\n**1. What is the specific mechanism by which Freemasonry creates spiritual effects in the children and grandchildren of Masons?**\n\nA) Masons pass down occult knowledge and practices deliberately through family instruction\nB) The blood oaths taken in Masonic degrees create covenantal spiritual agreements that, according to [Exodus 20:5](data-scripture="Exodus 20:5"), establish generational spiritual assignments that follow the bloodline until specifically broken in the name of Jesus Christ\nC) Children of Masons are statistically more likely to become Masons themselves, creating a generational cycle of occult involvement\nD) The Masonic financial network creates generational dependency that makes it difficult for descendants to leave the fraternal system\n\n---\n\n**2. Proverbs 26:2 is a key text in the lesson on Curses. What principle does it establish?**\n\nA) Curses spoken by non-believers have no spiritual effect on believers\nB) Curses that are deserved will land; those that are undeserved will not — establishing that sin creates vulnerability and righteousness creates protection, but not that believers are automatically immune to all curses\nC) Only God has the authority to speak a genuine curse\nD) Curses spoken in anger dissipate within 24 hours\n\n---\n\n**3. According to the False Gospels lesson, why is the prosperity gospel specifically described as capable of damning its adherents?**\n\nA) It creates a culture of financial exploitation that destroys community trust\nB) The prosperity gospel's emphasis on positive confession displaces faith in the atoning death of Christ\nC) The prosperity gospel presents a Christ who guarantees health and wealth — a Christ who does not exist in Scripture — meaning the trust placed in that Christ is not placed in the Christ who saves, and the salvation being offered cannot be delivered\nD) Prosperity gospel churches teach works-based salvation through the discipline of tithing\n\n---\n\n**Answer Key**\n\n1. **B** — Covenant oaths create generational legal ground. [Exodus 20:5](data-scripture="Exodus 20:5") is the mechanism; [Galatians 3:13](data-scripture="Galatians 3:13") is the remedy.\n2. **B** — The causeless curse cannot land — but the curse that has cause (open legal ground through sin) can.\n3. **C** — A gospel that cannot save is not the gospel. [Galatians 1:8-9](data-scripture="Galatians 1:8-9"): anathema.\n\n---\n\n*Wave 6 turns inward. The attacks that follow do not come from outside — they work through the most intimate systems God designed.*	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk461000qewhjsvwknmpd	cmnknk45y000gewhjn5pl0kw9	Lesson 15 — The Dispensation of Promise	READING	3542	9	\N	t	God called one man out of a pagan city in Mesopotamia and told him to walk into an unknown country on nothing but a promise. No contract, no map, no timeline — just the word of a God Abram had presumably never personally encountered before. And everything that followed — the nation of Israel, the covenants, the Messiah, the Church, the eternal kingdom — hinges on that one moment of obedience.\n\nLast time we established that healing is written into the atonement alongside forgiveness. Now we turn to a new dispensation: the age that began when God made an unconditional covenant with one man and through him changed the trajectory of the entire human race.\n\n![Lesson 15 — The Dispensation of Promise](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8Os73vSbMRlSEtNozZx4rmO6UfQcdhyY2pDqwX3)\n\n## I. The Definition of Promise\n\nThis dispensation gets its name from the promises and covenants God made with Abraham and his descendants. These promises became the foundation for everything God did with His chosen people during this era.\n\nHere's what makes this period so significant: it's the first time God began to emphasize that Christ would come through a specific branch of the human family. Now, it's true that looking back at history, we can trace Christ's lineage through Seth and his descendants as listed in Gen. 5 and Lk. 3:23-38. But before Abraham, God never prophesied that the Messiah would definitely come through any particular line.\n\nThe only clear prophecy of Christ up to this point had been given to Adam and Eve in the Garden after the Fall (Gen. 3:15). But that promise didn't specify which of Adam's sons would carry the line. Think of it like an announcement that a royal heir would be born somewhere in a vast kingdom — the promise was certain, but the location was yet to be revealed.\n\nThe prophecy Noah gave concerning Shem (Gen. 9:24-27) is often understood as the next prediction of Christ's coming. But that passage doesn't actually mention the Messiah directly. It simply predicts that God would have a special relationship with Shem. We understand it as referring to Christ now, in light of what has happened since, but at the time it wasn't clear that the Messiah would come through Shem.\n\nStarting with Abraham, everything changed. God singled out his branch of the race as the very one through whom the seed of the woman would come. Over this period of 430 years, God gave many promises and prophecies about the coming Messiah through Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, and his descendants.\n\nIn Gen. 12:1-3, we have the first prophecy in the Bible that still awaits complete fulfillment. When will it be fully realized? In the Millennium and forever, when "In thee [Christ, the seed of Abraham] shall all families of the earth be blessed."\n\nThis prophecy and promise was confirmed later:\n- To Abraham (Gen. 17:19; 21:12; 22:17-18)\n- To Isaac (Gen. 26:3-4)\n- To Jacob (Gen. 28:3-4, 13-15)\n- To Judah (Gen. 49:8-12)\n\n## II. The Length of the Dispensation of Promise: 430 Years\n\nAccording to Exod. 12:40 and Gal. 3:14-17, this dispensation lasted exactly 430 years — from the call of Abraham to Moses. Here's how those years break down:\n\n1. From the 75th year of Abraham to the birth of Isaac (Gen. 12:4; 21:5) — **25 years**\n2. From Isaac's birth to that of Jacob (Gen. 25:26) — **60 years**\n3. From Jacob's birth to his death (Gen. 47:28) — **147 years**\n4. From Jacob's death to that of Joseph (Gen. 37:2; 41:46; 47:27; 50:22) — **54 years**\n5. From Joseph's death to the exodus from Egypt (Exod. 12:40) — **144 years**\n\n**TOTAL: 430 years**\n\nNow, you might wonder about the 400 years mentioned in Gen. 15:13 and Acts 7:6. Those years are calculated from a different starting point — from the confirmation of Isaac as the seed when Ishmael was cast out (Gen. 21:12; Gal. 4:30). This happened five years after Isaac's birth.\n\nThe 430 years, on the other hand, are counted from Abraham's departure from Haran, which was twenty-five years before Isaac was born. Here's an important detail: Abraham's seed was actually in Egypt for only 215 years. The entire "sojourn" in various "strange" countries — Canaan, Philistia, Egypt, and others — totaled about 400 years in round numbers, or precisely 430 years from Gen. 12:1-3.\n\nYou can trace this sojourn through many passages: Gen. 12:1-20; 13:1-18; 15:13-14; 20:1-18; 21:22-34; 23:4; 26:3-35; 28:10; 29:1; 31:13-55; 35:6; 37:1; 46:1-7; 47:27; 50:22-26; Exod. 1-12; Heb. 11:8-10.\n\n## III. The Favorable Beginning of the Dispensation of Promise\n\nThe Dispensation of Promise started with God's call of Abraham. God revealed Himself to Abraham and made an eternal covenant with him — to bless him and his descendants forever and to give them the land of Canaan as an everlasting possession (Gen. 12:1-3; 13:14-18; 15:17-21; 17:7-19).\n\nAbraham had the true knowledge of God and understood how to worship Him properly (Gen. 12:4-9; 13:1-4; 14:17-24; 15:1-21; 17:4-19). He had the gospel of Christ and possessed faith in God and in the blood of Christ for redemption from all sin (Gal. 3:6-9; Rom. 4:1-25).\n\nLet this sink in: Abraham was a saved and born-again man in the same sense that you and I can be saved and born-again today (Rom. 4; Jas. 2:21-24). He had the promise of eternal life and an eternal home. He looked forward to a city to come, where he could live forever with God (Heb. 11:8-19).\n\nAbraham believed in the resurrection of the dead and personally experienced many aspects of the gospel we enjoy now (Heb. 11:19; Rom. 4). He also believed in divine healing and was greatly used by God in the healing of an entire nation (Gen. 20:7, 17).\n\nAbraham lived separated from the idolatrous nations and served as God's representative on Earth. Think of him like an ambassador stationed in a foreign land — set apart, yet positioned to influence everyone around him.\n\nThe promises he received were remarkable:\n- All nations would be blessed through him\n- His descendants would be as numerous as the stars and the sand of the sea\n- He would have a great name on Earth\n- He would be the father of a great nation\n- Kings would come from his line\n- His natural descendants would be eternal\n- He would be great and wealthy on Earth\n\nAbraham had a personal relationship with God that few people have ever experienced. He received many revelations about this life and the one to come. He became wealthy, and God caused everything he touched to prosper.\n\nThere simply couldn't have been a better or more favorable beginning for anyone starting their stewardship in God's plan for this age. If you read Genesis from chapter twelve onward, you'll see just how many favorable conditions existed for people in this era.\n\n### Human Government Enlarged\n\nMany laws of human government were added beyond those God originally gave to Noah in Gen. 9. As sins against society emerged, new laws became necessary. Some of these laws are recorded in Genesis and concerned:\n\n- Monogamy (12:18; 16:1)\n- Adultery (12:18; 20:3-9)\n- Priesthood (14:18)\n- Tithes (14:20; 28:22)\n- Covenant-making (15:10-18; 21:27-34; 31:44-55)\n- Circumcision (17:10)\n- Hospitality (18:1-8; 19:8)\n- Licentiousness (18:20-21)\n- Fornication (34:7)\n- Oaths (21:23; 24:41)\n- Birthright (25:33)\n- Anointing oil (28:18; 31:13)\n- Vows (28:18)\n- Idolatry (31:32-35)\n- A brother's widow (38:8)\n- Dowry (34:12)\n\nSome of these laws came directly from God, while others were man-made. Many of them were later included in the laws of Moses as God continued to expand human government and hold people more accountable for their sins against others.\n\nSome of these civil laws were part of the code established by Amraphel, the ruler of Babylon, which governed various peoples from Persia to the Caspian Sea and the Mediterranean Sea. Gen. 14 mentions the vast Babylonian Empire that ruled many peoples in those early days after the flood.\n\nOther man-made civil laws concerned:\n- Adoption (Gen. 15:1-6)\n- Concubines (Gen. 16:1-3; 30:1-24)\n- Burial places (Gen. 23)\n- Execution for stealing (Gen. 31:32)\n- Execution by burning (Gen. 38:24)\n- Death for stealing in a palace (Gen. 44:9)\n- A special portion allotted to a favorite son (Gen. 48:22)\n\nYou'll discover more of these as you read carefully through Genesis.\n\nHere's an important principle: God didn't do away with human government or man's responsibility to execute laws against crime just because He began to more fully introduce the gospel of grace. Human government is here to stay. It will continue even in the Millennium and the New Earth, as we'll see in Lessons Forty-nine through Fifty-two.\n\n## IV. The Test in the Dispensation of Promise\n\nThe specific test for people in this age was to believe God's promises and obey the gospel as revealed in the Abrahamic Covenant. This test primarily concerned Abraham's chosen family.\n\nGod recognized that He could no longer deal with humanity as a whole because people were determined to rebel against Him, perpetuate idolatry, and live lives devoted to self-gratification. His new approach was to work through His chosen people of this period — Abraham's family.\n\nTo them He gave the specific promises and covenants, along with the responsibility of remaining separated from all other peoples and being wholly consecrated to the true God and His worship. God required them to leave their relatives behind and sojourn in a strange land, remaining set apart as His representatives on Earth.\n\nBeyond their particular calling, God also required His chosen people to obey all civil laws that didn't conflict with His divine plan for them.\n\nWhat about the other nations? Their test in this period was to obey human government laws and also have faith in God, conforming to the gospel as revealed to them by the chosen race and by the conscience within every person (Rom. 2:12-16).\n\nNobody in this period had any excuse for not knowing they were lost or what God required for salvation. Knowledge of God existed among many peoples. All the inhabitants of Canaan knew about God because of Abraham, Melchisedec, Isaac, Jacob, and others who lived among them.\n\nGod dealt personally with many groups:\n- The Egyptians (Gen. 13:10-20; 39:1–50:26)\n- Hagar (Gen. 16)\n- The cities of the plain (Gen. 19)\n- Philistia (Gen. 20 and 26)\n- Rebekah's family (Gen. 24:1-67; 28:1–31:55)\n\nAnd there were others throughout this age. The founders of the nations of Moab, Ammon, Edom, Midian, and other branches of the race were all raised in the households of Lot and Abraham.\n\nAbraham's family sojourned for 430 years in various lands, and all these peoples received testimony about God from them. By the end of this age, many nations witnessed God's mighty power in the plagues of Egypt, in the wilderness, and in Canaan and surrounding countries — miracles that only God could perform.\n\nGod has never been without witnesses among the nations in any age. People have always been required to live according to the light they received, and they will be judged accordingly.\n\n## V. The Purpose of God in the Dispensation of Promise\n\nBecause humanity had utterly failed God in every test — proving sinful, idolatrous, and rebellious — the Lord saw that He could no longer deal with humanity as a whole. So He purposed in this dispensation to call out one man and make him and his descendants God's representatives among all nations.\n\nGod planned to give them the promised land as an everlasting possession and as a base of operations for His greater purpose: the coming of the seed of the woman and the defeat of the giants and other satanic enemies on Earth. This land would be where the Messiah would be born to rule all nations forever.\n\nGod's plan was to keep the chosen race as pure Adamite stock, free from corruption by the giant races that were filling the land. This way, there would be no failure in bringing the seed of the woman into the world to redeem humanity and carry out His eternal purpose.\n\nHere's something crucial to understand: the second eruption of the sons of God had already occurred before Abraham's call. God knew Satan's purpose in this — it was the same as before the flood: to corrupt the human race and eliminate pure Adamite stock so the seed of the woman couldn't come into the world. If Satan succeeded, it would avert his own doom and that of the fallen angels, allowing their rulership of Earth to continue.\n\nAlready, the sons of God were marrying human women and producing giants on Earth, just as before the flood. These beings had taken possession of Canaan and surrounding territories — the very lands God had chosen as the base for His missionary and governmental operations among the nations.\n\nBut God had a plan. He would let Israel multiply in another land until they became strong enough to destroy these giants and possess the lands He intended. In Lesson Seventeen, we'll see how these giants were destroyed by Israel's sword, accomplishing what God planned in this dispensation. (See Lessons Eleven and Thirteen for more on the sons of God and daughters of men before and after the flood.)\n\nThrough choosing Abraham and his descendants, God purposed to make them His physical, financial, and spiritual representatives on Earth. He wanted to demonstrate to all the heathen nations what serving the true and living God would be like.\n\nPicture it this way: Israel was meant to be a living advertisement — a demonstration of the dramatic contrast between the blessings of serving a living God versus the dead idols the nations worshiped.\n\nGod wanted Israel to be:\n- A spiritual power in His hands to defeat Satan's forces and show all people they could use God's power to conquer sin and sickness\n- Perfect in physical health, proving it's God's will for all to be free from disease\n- Prosperous and wealthy, proving it's God's will for all to be free from want and poverty\n- Happy and victorious over all enemies\n- A true example of God's highest will for fallen humanity\n- The ideal nation — people who were happy, prosperous, sinless, and healthy, living in perfect peace and blessedness\n\nAll of this would draw the heathen nations back to God and lead them to accept Him as the rightful Moral Governor of the universe.\n\nThrough this actual demonstration of God's blessings on Israel, God planned to make His will perfectly clear to all mankind. Israel was called to be missionaries for God to all nations. If they had remained faithful, the Earth could have been evangelized centuries before Christ came!\n\nAll people could have seen definite proof of truth demonstrated in the bodies, souls, and spirits of the chosen nation — in their religion and government. They would have been the head, not the tail. They would have been the esteemed leaders of all Gentiles, and all nations would have been blessed alongside them — just as they will be in the Millennium and forever.\n\nWe would already have the days of Heaven on Earth, which won't fully come until Jesus returns the second time, as we'll see in Lesson Forty-nine.\n\nGod planned to banish poverty, want, sickness, sin, and all misery from Earth in the lives of everyone who followed Israel in serving the true God and believing the gospel of Christ.\n\nNo nation has ever had a better opportunity to be used by God among all other nations than Israel did — from Abraham's call to Christ's first coming. No people on Earth have ever been given a greater program than what God had in mind when He called Abraham and chose his descendants to represent truth.\n\nHere's something wonderful: most of what we now include in the gospel of grace actually had its beginning and purpose in the Age of Promise. God preached the gospel beforehand to Abraham and his seed, saying, "In thee shall all the nations be blessed" (Gal. 3:7-9).\n\nAll the wonderful doctrines of the New Testament are found in the promises and covenant made with Abraham:\n- Salvation in all its aspects\n- Remission of sin\n- Conversion\n- Repentance\n- Faith\n- Consecration\n- Eternal life\n- Spiritual revelations\n- Redemption\n- Sanctification\n- Justification\n- Bodily healing\n- Health\n- Grace\n- Heirship\n- Reconciliation\n- Imputation of righteousness\n- Answers to prayer\n- Bodily resurrection\n- Fruit of the Spirit\n\nAnd many other parts of the present gospel were experienced by Abraham's descendants before the cross. We'll explore this more in Lessons Thirty-three and Thirty-four.\n\n## VI. The Means of God in Accomplishing His Purpose\n\nWhat tools did God use to accomplish His purpose in this age? The call of God, the covenant and promises (which included "the gospel" — Gal. 3:6-9, 13, 14), and personal dealings and revelations of His plan to Abraham and his descendants.\n\nGod had confidence in Abraham that he would remain separate and represent Him to all other nations according to the gospel. He trusted Abraham and his seed to preserve their family line and remain free from mixture with the giants, so that the seed of the woman could eventually come and bruise the serpent's head.\n\nAbraham had no idea at first what all God had in mind when He called him. But he was obedient, following God step by step as God revealed His plan. He left his own country and went into the land of the giants to live as a sojourner.\n\nIn that land, God revealed many things to him. One important revelation was that his descendants would sojourn in Egypt, become a great nation, and then be brought back to possess the land (Gen. 15:13-21). This was fulfilled in this Dispensation of Promise.\n\nHis descendants multiplied slowly at first — only 70 souls in 215 years. But in the next 215 years, they grew rapidly to 600,000 men of war, plus their wives, children, and elderly. Many Egyptian converts also joined the nation (Exod. 12:38; Num. 11:4; Neh. 13:3). There must have been over six million people when they left Egypt!\n\nGod wanted them strong enough to destroy the giants occupying the promised land and eliminate all abnormal beings that came from the sons of God and daughters of men since the flood (Gen. 6:4). In Gen. 15:16, we learn that even in Abraham's time, God purposed to destroy these giants.\n\nThe sword of Israel was God's instrument for destroying the mighty race of giants that had emerged since the flood, as we explained in Lesson Eleven. Israel became a great nation in Egypt and was used powerfully by God to fulfill His prophetic Word concerning Egypt, Canaan, and surrounding lands (Gen. 15:13-16; Deut. 20:17; Josh. 3:10).\n\nWhen Israel surrendered to God's will through obedience, they demonstrated before all nations God's power over sickness, poverty, want, and sin. God made the entire nation healthy — there wasn't one feeble person among all their tribes (Ps. 105:37; 107:20). He promised continued freedom from sickness and disease (Exod. 15:26; 23:25; Ps. 91). He promised wealth and happiness forever, and Israel enjoyed these blessings as long as she walked in God's will (Lev. 26; Deut. 28).\n\nDuring the reigns of David, Solomon, and a few other kings of Judah, all nations were drawn to the God of Israel. This showed, at least in part, God's purpose for Israel throughout the period from Abraham to Christ.\n\n## VII. The Failure of Men in the Dispensation of Promise\n\nThe history of Abraham and his descendants recorded in Genesis 12–50 and Exod. 1–12 reveals the failure of Israel in particular and all humanity in general. Let's examine these failures:\n\n### 1. THE FAILURE OF ABRAHAM (Gen. 12:11–25:11)\n\nAbraham's first failure was not leaving all his relatives behind when he went to the Promised Land. God had clearly told him: "Get thee out of thy country, and from thy kindred, and from thy father's house" (Gen. 12:1-3). But Abraham allowed Terah, his father, to lead the journey into Canaan — not what God commanded (Gen. 11:31-32).\n\nSeveral family members started from Ur of the Chaldees, and they were delayed in Haran until Terah's death. Then some family members stayed in Haran while others continued with Abraham to Canaan (Gen. 12:1-6).\n\nAbraham's next failure was showing unbelief in God's protection. He left Canaan to go to Egypt during a famine. This move itself might have been permitted, but his fear that the Egyptians would kill him for Sarah's sake and his deception of Pharaoh was inexcusable (Gen. 12:10-20).\n\nIt was a clear failure to believe God's promise that his descendants would bless all nations. He should have realized that if God's promises were true, the Egyptians couldn't kill him — he had no children yet, and he would have to live to have any! In Egypt, Satan tried to corrupt Sarah and frustrate God's purpose in bringing the seed of the woman through Abraham, but God intervened, protected Sarah, and exposed Abraham's deception. Abraham was expelled from Egypt, so his time there was clearly outside God's will.\n\nGod was with Abraham when he returned to Canaan. He was finally separated from Lot, and then God promised him descendants as numerous as the stars and sand of the sea (Gen. 13:1-18).\n\nAbraham delivered Lot from his enemies as recorded in Gen. 14. Then God appeared again, promising a son and offspring as countless as the stars and sand (Gen. 15).\n\nFifteen years passed with no promised son. Sarah came up with an idea to "help" God fulfill His word. She suggested Abraham take a second wife, giving him her handmaid Hagar, who bore Ishmael (Gen. 16). This wasn't God's plan for producing the promised seed, and it caused tremendous family trouble. Abraham had failed God again by not having faith for a child through Sarah.\n\nFourteen more years went by — still no promised seed. God appeared to Abraham and renewed the covenant, confirming it by commanding circumcision. Abraham was 99 years old, and Ishmael was thirteen when they were circumcised (Gen. 17). A few weeks later, God appeared personally and promised a son through Sarah, who was 89 years old. Both were past the age of having children, but God renewed His promise — they would have a child within the year.\n\nAround this same time, Abraham went to Philistia and failed God again through unbelief. He gave Sarah to the king for his harem. This was another attempt by Satan to corrupt Sarah, but God intervened by making all the women in the nation barren. If God hadn't protected Sarah, any child she bore could have been suspected as the king's, not Abraham's — causing endless scandal and family trouble. God rescued Sarah so that Abimelech never touched her, ensuring Abraham would know without doubt that Isaac was truly his — the long-promised child.\n\nFrom that point on, Abraham no longer staggered at God's promise through unbelief. He became strong in faith, giving glory to God, counting Him faithful to His word. God renewed the youth of both Abraham and Sarah, and Isaac was born (Gen. 21; Rom. 4:12-21; Heb. 11:11-12).\n\nAt this same time, God appeared to Abraham, ate a meal with him, and told him about the coming destruction of Sodom and Gomorrah (Gen. 18-19). Lot was rescued from Sodom, though the last we read of him concerns his shameful incident with his two daughters, from which came Moab and Ammon (Gen. 19:33-38).\n\nHagar and Ishmael were sent away when Isaac was old enough to be weaned (Gen. 21). Abraham and Abimelech made a covenant together, and Abraham lived in Philistia for many days. After this, Abraham agreed to offer Isaac as a sacrifice, but God intervened and provided another sacrifice (Gen. 22).\n\nSarah died when Isaac was thirty-seven years old and was buried in the cave of Machpelah (Gen. 23). At forty years of age, Isaac married Rebekah, whom Abraham's servant found among his relatives in Haran (Gen. 24).\n\nAbraham married Keturah and had six sons by her, plus other children by his concubines. He left all his wealth to Isaac, except for gifts given to his other children, whom he sent away to the East country. Abraham died at 175 years of age and was buried by Isaac and Ishmael beside Sarah in the cave of Machpelah (Gen. 25).\n\nSo Abraham wasn't absolutely perfect, as many people today assume. For about twenty-four years, he struggled with faith and patience concerning the promised son. After several failures to believe God without wavering, in the twenty-fifth year he became strong in faith with no more recorded failures (Rom. 4:17-25).\n\nAfter that, there was no need for faith in a promised son — he had what was promised (Rom. 8:24-25). He lived as a good man, full of experience, for seventy-five more years before dying in faith toward God.\n\n### 2. THE FAILURE OF ISAAC (Gen. 21:1–35:29)\n\nIsaac seemed less spiritual than Abraham. He had fewer revelations than his father. He loved Esau more than Jacob, which later caused much family trouble.\n\nLike Abraham, Isaac failed in faith when famine came. He went to Philistia and committed the same sin his father had concerning his wife. This was Satan's attempt to corrupt Rebekah, through whom the Messiah was to come, but God intervened, and she was spared from sinning against God and man (Gen. 26).\n\nGod had promised that Isaac's descendants would be as numerous as the stars and sand. If Isaac had believed God, no man could have killed him to get his wife!\n\nIsaac experienced more conflicts with the land's inhabitants than Abraham did and also had more trouble with his children. Overall, we see a decline in spiritual and home life in Isaac's family compared to Abraham's.\n\nTwenty years passed after Isaac's marriage with no child. He prayed to God, and twins were born (Gen. 25:19-34). God predicted that Jacob would be the greater of the two boys, and the Messiah would come through him. Yet despite this, Isaac loved Esau and tried to give him the blessing, but was thwarted by Rebekah's scheming (Gen. 27).\n\nAfter this, Isaac lived in the land for many years — the twenty years Jacob was away and many years after Jacob's return. He died at 180 years of age, and Jacob and Esau buried him (Gen. 35:27-29).\n\n### 3. THE FAILURE OF JACOB AND HIS SONS (Gen. 25:24–49:33)\n\nJacob was less spiritual than Isaac. His disposition always drove him to get ahead of everyone else. In fact, he was called "Jacob" or "supplanter" because in the womb he tried to get ahead of his brother and be firstborn (Gen. 25:24-26).\n\nHis story begins with twins struggling in Rebekah's womb and the prophecy that he would be greater than his brother (Gen. 25:22-23). When grown, he bought his brother's birthright and stole his blessing (Gen. 25:27-34; 27:1-46). For this, he had to flee to Haran to escape Esau's anger (Gen. 28).\n\nIn Haran, he raised a large family and schemed constantly to get his father-in-law's wealth, tampering with nature and working against him behind his back (Gen. 29-30). This caused Laban's sons to plot against Jacob, which prompted his decision to return home. He fled from Laban, who pursued and caught him in Gilead and would have harmed him if God hadn't intervened (Gen. 31).\n\nWhen Jacob heard Esau was coming after him with 400 armed men, he failed God by being afraid he would be killed. He wrestled with God all night, and his entire life and disposition were transformed (Gen. 32). God made Esau friendly, and Jacob settled in Canaan (Gen. 33).\n\nThere, he began reaping what he had sown through years of trying to get ahead of everyone:\n- His daughter was defiled, causing her brothers to kill all the men of Shechem\n- Some family members became idolaters (Gen. 35:2-5)\n- Rachel, his beloved wife, and Isaac, his father, both died (Gen. 35)\n- Reuben committed adultery with his father's concubine (Gen. 35:22)\n- His sons became lawless, unfaithful, and deceitful\n- They sold Joseph into Egypt (Gen. 37)\n- Judah brought family shame through his daughter-in-law and by marrying outside the chosen family (Gen. 38)\n\nJacob ended his life in Egypt with his sons, dying at 147 years of age (Gen. 47).\n\nJacob's descendants were generally less spiritual than he was, except for Joseph. Over 215 years, they became almost completely backslidden and had forgotten God. For this, God allowed them to be oppressed by the Egyptians, hoping their troubles would bring them back to repentance.\n\nTheir oppression did produce repentance — they cried out to God for mercy. God heard them and raised up Moses to deliver them. You can trace their backsliding in passages like Exod. 2:23-25; 3:13-18; 4:1-9, 29-31; 5:1-23.\n\nSo we see that throughout these failures, the devil tried repeatedly to defeat God's plan for bringing the seed of the woman into the world:\n- He tried three times to corrupt Sarah and Rebekah\n- He tried several times to make Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, and his sons fear death before God's promises were fulfilled\n- He tried famine, unbelief, fear, war, deceit, lying, slavery, and murder\n- He tried cutting off male children to destroy the race\n- He tried provoking Moses into attempting a premature deliverance so Israel's chosen leader would be killed\n- He tried hardships and discouragements of all kinds\n- He tried causing Israel to sin so God would destroy them\n\nAll to keep the seed of the woman from coming into the world as God promised.\n\nSatan was defeated every time. The specific line through whom the Messiah would come was preserved until Christ actually appeared and defeated him on the cross. This battle between God and Satan over the seed of the woman continued through this age and carried on through the Dispensation of Law in many forms, with God always victorious, as we'll see.\n\n### 4. THE FAILURE OF ALL MEN IN GENERAL\n\nNot only did the chosen family fail God on many occasions to live up to their best light, but the Gentile nations among whom Israel lived rebelled as a whole against God. Few righteous people are mentioned in this period.\n\nWe can name Melchisedec, Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, Joseph, Sarah, Moses, Job, and a few others that Scripture identifies as righteous during this time. But as a whole, the nations were given over to idolatry and sin.\n\nSome cities and nations are specifically called out as very sinful: the Amorites, Sodom, Gomorrah, Egypt, and others (Gen. 13:13; 15:13-16; 19:1-25; 31:30).\n\nThe majority of people in every age have failed to live up to the light they received. For this, they will receive God's judgment (Rom. 2:12-16).\n\n## VIII. The Judgment of God upon Men in the Dispensation of Promise\n\nThe judgment ending this age was twofold:\n1. Upon Israel, the chosen family who had special responsibility as God's representatives on Earth during this period\n2. Upon Egypt because of their persecution of Israel\n\n### 1. THE JUDGMENT UPON ISRAEL (Exod. 1:7–6:30)\n\nBecause of Israel's continual spiritual decline throughout this age and their almost universal backsliding from the original faith given to Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, God allowed His chosen people to be greatly oppressed by the Egyptians. His purpose was to bring them to repentance.\n\nThe oppression worked. It brought them to repentance, and they cried out to God for mercy. God heard them and raised up Moses for their deliverance (Exod. 2:1–4:31; Acts 7:17-36; Heb. 11:23-29).\n\n### The Beginning of "the Times of the Gentiles"\n\nThis term refers to the period when the Gentiles served as God's rod of discipline upon Israel to further His purposes concerning them.\n\nIt began with Israel's first oppression by the Gentiles in Egypt and continues through Israel's history even into this Dispensation of Grace. It will end at Christ's return in glory, when He will deliver Israel from the Gentiles under Antichrist and exalt them as the head of all nations in the Millennium and forever (Lk. 21:24; Rev. 11:1-2).\n\n### The Door of Mercy Always Open to Gentiles\n\nThis dispensation of the Gentiles is mentioned only in Lk. 21:24 and referred to in Rom. 11:25 as "the\n\n---\n\nGod's covenant with Abraham was unconditional — God alone passed through the pieces, binding only Himself. Abraham didn't earn it. He simply believed it. So here's the question worth sitting with: What promise of God are you currently waiting for that you've quietly stopped believing — not because Scripture changed, but because circumstances made it seem impossible? And how different is that from Abraham's situation when he was a hundred years old and still waiting?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk461000rewhjil16pno4	cmnknk45y000gewhjn5pl0kw9	Lesson 16 — Asking and Receiving from God	READING	3383	10	\N	t	Most Christians pray. Far fewer expect an answer. There's a kind of spiritual resignation that gets dressed up in reverent language — 'Whatever is God's will,' 'If it pleases Him,' 'Perhaps in His timing' — that functions as theological cover for the belief that answered prayer is rare and unpredictable. But Jesus made a statement that has no room for that kind of ambiguity: 'Ask, and it shall be given you; seek, and ye shall find; knock, and it shall be opened unto you' (Mt. 7:7). That's not a suggestion. That's a guarantee with conditions.\n\nLast time we traced the Dispensation of Promise and God's unconditional covenant with Abraham. Now we get practical: how do you actually access God's provision through prayer and faith?\n\nHere's the good news: if you truly master these truths and commit to practicing them faithfully, you'll never have to wonder why your prayers go unanswered like so many people do. You really can get what you want from God if you simply do what He says. The certainty of receiving from Him is as solid as anything can possibly be—if you follow the instructions laid out here.\n\nGod cannot fail. His promises cannot fail. And you cannot fail if you're just as determined to refuse failure as most people seem determined to accept it. Let's dive into these great lessons on prayer until asking becomes second nature and receiving becomes your normal experience.\n\n![Lesson 16 — Asking and Receiving from God](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8Osd1URbmXCHEavRrA2ePyuNDOn3wIQ7Y9mCfTk)\n\n## I. What Prayer Is\n\nSo what exactly is prayer? It's bringing your desires for things you need, things that are lawful, and things God has promised—with a humble confidence that you'll receive them through Jesus Christ, for God's glory and your good.\n\nThink of it like pleading your case in God's courtroom. It's reaching out for help in matters beyond your own power. It's a personal appeal to a personal, present God—based on His will, His Word, and your legitimate desires.\n\nPrayer is cooperating with God's willingness to show His goodness to everyone who has faith in Him and depends on Him for help. Simply put, prayer is asking and receiving from God—and that's what this whole lesson is about.\n\n## II. General Facts about Asking and Receiving\n\n**1. COMMANDS TO PRAY:** (1 Chron. 16:11; 2 Chron. 7:14; Ps. 105:4; Isa. 55:6; Mt. 7:7-11; Eph. 6:18; Phil. 4:6; 1 Thess. 5:17; Heb. 4:16).\n\n**2. TIME TO PRAY:** "daily," in the morning (Ps. 5:3; 88:13; 143:8); "twice daily" (Ps. 88:1); "thrice daily" (Ps. 55:17; Dan. 6:10); "without ceasing" (1 Thess. 5:17); "at night" (Mk. 1:35; Lk. 6:12); "night and day" (Lk. 2:37).\n\n**3. ATTITUDES IN PRAYER:** "standing" (Lk. 18:13); "sitting" (Acts 1:14; 2:2; Luke 10:13); "lying" (2 Sam. 12:16-20); "kneeling" (Ps. 95:6; Dan. 6:10; Lk. 22:41; Acts 9:40; 20:36; 21:5); "weeping" (Ezra 10:1; 1 Sam. 1:10); "talking" (Gen. 18:23-33); "meditating" (Ps. 5:1); "agonizing" (Lk. 22:44); "groaning" (Rom. 8:26-27); and "in the Spirit" (Eph. 6:18; Jude 20).\n\n**4. WHERE TO PRAY:** "in closets" (Mt. 6:6); "on mountains" (Mt. 14:23); and in homes, in temples and everywhere (Acts 1:13-14; 2:46; 3:1; 12:12; 1 Tim. 2:8).\n\n**5. HOW NOT TO PRAY:** Jesus taught that you shouldn't pray like the hypocrites who want to be seen by others, and not like the heathen who think God hears them because of their many words (Mt. 6:5-8).\n\n**6. THE PURPOSE OF PRAYER:** The main goal of all prayer should be to glorify God and to make your joy complete (Jn. 14:13-15; 16:24).\n\n**7. GROUND FOR ASKING AND RECEIVING:** The true foundation for prayer and its answer is being born-again and having a right relationship with God. Only true, saved people have the right to ask and receive whatever they want from God.\n\nSinners who rebel and refuse to surrender their lives to God have no real grounds to approach Him. They have no foundation for their faith. Now, God in His mercy does sometimes help sinners and hear their cries of despair—but He's not obligated to do for them what He's promised until they obey Him. He helps them simply because He wants to prove His love and mercy, even to rebels.\n\nPaul said, "the goodness of God leadeth thee to repentance" and "God commendeth his love toward us, in that, while we were yet sinners Christ died for us" (Rom. 2:4; 5:8).\n\nThe first promise a sinner can legally claim through Christ's death is forgiveness and cleansing from all sin (Acts 2:38; 3:19; 1 Jn. 1:9). Once someone truly repents and gets born-again, they're immediately on praying ground for anything else God has promised. A right relationship with God is the true foundation for asking and receiving (Mt. 7:7-11; Lk. 11:1-13; Jn. 14:12-15; 15:7, 16; 16:23-26; 1 Jn. 3:20-24; 5:13-15).\n\nWith the proper relationship with God, it becomes your family right, your legal right, your redemptive right, your gospel right, your needful right, and your creative right to ask for and receive anything the Father has promised His children. There should be no question about your personal rights or the outcome of prayer—all things belong to God and His children, and His sons will inherit all things when rebellion is finally put down (Rom. 8:17-18; 1 Pet. 1:2-5; Rev. 21:7).\n\nIf all things belong to us and we'll eventually inherit them, why not get what we want of them here and now? At the very least, why shouldn't it be our right to receive what God has already promised and provided?\n\n## III. The Certainty of Answered Prayer\n\nAccording to Jesus, asking is receiving: "Ask, and it shall be given you; seek and ye shall find; knock, and it shall be opened unto you: for every one that asketh receiveth; and he that seeketh findeth; and to him that knocketh it shall be opened" (Mt. 7:7-8).\n\nHere's something interesting: the first letters of "ask," "seek," and "knock" spell A-S-K. This isn't just a coincidence—it's a divine reminder that all you have to do is ask. But asking presupposes a God who hears and answers.\n\nIf you ask in mockery or just going through the motions without faith, you're not truly asking. You must ask in faith, nothing wavering, and then you'll receive (Jas. 1:5-9; Heb. 11:6). There can be no failure, or God isn't true—so don't doubt it, because God is true.\n\nJesus continued by showing us the infinite fatherhood of God. He explained that God loves His children more than any human parent could possibly love their own flesh and blood.\n\nThis means it's not—and cannot be—God's will for any one of His children to be sinful, rebellious, defeated, unhappy, sick, poor, wanting, worrying, complaining, or discouraged about anything in life. No earthly parent would want these things for their children, and they would do everything possible to deliver them from such troubles. How much more does God want to help you?\n\nJesus proved God loves His children more than earthly parents when He said: "Or what man is there of you, whom if his son ask bread, will he give him a stone? Or if he asks a fish, will he give him a serpent? If ye then, being evil, know how to give good gifts unto your children, how much more shall your heavenly Father which is in Heaven give good things to them that ask him?" (Mt. 7:9-11).\n\nWhat more could you want than such a broad, all-inclusive promise of God's infinite fatherhood? What more proof could you need that it's God's will for you to ask for anything—wealth, health, cleansing from sin, keeping from sin, or anything else you desire in life?\n\nIn the same Sermon on the Mount, Jesus taught us to pray, "Thy will be done in earth, as it is in heaven" (Mt. 6:10). Now ask yourself: Is it God's will for people to be sick and sinful in Heaven? Is it His will for people to be defeated, poverty-stricken, and unhappy there? Is it His will for people to be tormented by demons and carry around the works of the devil forever in Heaven?\n\nYou know the answer. If these things aren't God's will in Heaven, they're not God's will on Earth either. You can have freedom from these things here just as much as you'll be free from them there—if you'll just ask and receive according to God's will, right here and now.\n\nMay God Himself wake us up to see our privileges in the gospel here and now when we need so many things! Prayer will never fail when it's done right. Heaven and Earth will pass away more easily than prayer will become powerless when people pray correctly.\n\nPrayer never fails when you never fail to believe God. Prayer is absolute and unfailing when faith is the same. At the grave of Lazarus, Jesus said, "Father, I thank thee that thou hast heard me. And I knew that thou hearest me always" (Jn. 11:41-42). This is a normal, truthful attitude for every child of God to take and maintain.\n\nYou can say just as easily, "I can, I will, I do believe that God hears me and answers my prayer," as you can say, "It's hard to believe, I don't know if I can, I hope God hears me." You can form right words and cause them to come out of your mouth just as easily as you could tell a lie—and if you're that kind of person, it's infinitely easier to tell the truth.\n\nAll statements of unbelief, all doubts about whether God answers prayer—these are from the devil. Anyone who gives in to these ideas is cooperating with the enemy. You're making God a liar and hindering Him from answering your prayers.\n\nGod will not, on any occasion or in any case, transgress His law of faith and work contrary to it. If you believe God will answer, He will. If you believe He won't, He won't. This is truth, plain and simple (Heb. 11:6; Jas. 1:5-9).\n\nIn Detroit, Michigan, about three years ago, a deacon took me to pray with a man who had been paralyzed on one side and hadn't walked for about eight months. I spoke to him about faith, and his very first words were, "I believe in healing all right, but I can't seem to have faith for my healing."\n\nI got up, walked over to his bed, and said, "Don't you say that again. That's a lie. You can have faith, and you must have faith if you expect to get healed." I quoted Mk. 11:22-24, emphasizing it this way: "Believe you've got it and you shall have it." I said that several times and got him to promise to believe God would heal him. Then I prayed.\n\nWhile I was praying, the paralyzed man said, "If I've got it, what am I lying here for?" With that, he jumped out of bed and shouted all over the place! I told him, "Now stay up until bedtime, go to bed, get up in the morning perfectly well, and forget you ever had paralysis."\n\nIt's true—God will meet faith to the full extent of that faith. Jesus would sometimes say, "According to your faith so be it done unto you" or "as thou hast believed, so be it done unto thee" (Mt. 8:13; 9:29). It's the same today. Aggressive faith produces aggressive results. Living faith produces life. Dead, passive faith produces death and failure.\n\nIn Jn. 14:12, Jesus promised power for praying and working. He promised that each believer would do what He did—and even greater works—upon one condition: "He that believeth on me."\n\nWe can argue that this isn't for us today or that there must be some mistake about such a broad promise. But the fact that He said it still faces us, and we dare not make Him a liar if we expect to see Him in peace. This passage is plain; it means what it says. It will pay us to believe it, whether we want the experience of it or not.\n\nIn many other passages, Jesus gave believers full power of attorney to use His name to get anything in life they want (Jn. 14:12-15; 15:7, 16; 16:23-26; Mk. 16:16-20). We'll explore this more fully in Lesson Twenty-two. For now, just understand the absolute certainty of answered prayer for anything and everything you want in life.\n\nThere are no impossibilities with God or with the believer who will believe Him (Mk. 9:23; 11:22-24; Mt. 17:20; 21:21-22; Jn. 14:12-15; 15:7, 16). Possibilities in prayer are vast, and we should wake up to attain the blessings of the gospel. The world is dying for this kind of program.\n\nGod is going to hold modern believers accountable. He will judge every preacher, teacher, and the least person who claims to believe the gospel for not boldly setting aside unbelief and striving for the faith once delivered to the saints (Jude 3).\n\nThe certainty of answered prayer is also expressed in the Old Testament:\n\n"The eyes of the Lord are upon the righteous, and his ears are open unto their cry. The righteous cry, and the Lord heareth, and delivereth them out of ALL their troubles" (Ps. 34:15-17).\n\n"Delight thyself also in the Lord; and he shall give thee the desires of thine heart. Commit thy way unto the Lord; trust also in him; and he shall bring it to pass" (Ps. 37:4-5).\n\n"He shall call upon me, and I will answer him: I will be with him in trouble; I will deliver him, and honour him" (Ps. 91:15).\n\n"No good thing will he withhold from them that walk uprightly" (Ps. 84:11).\n\n## IV. Scientific Proof of Answered Prayer\n\nPrayer and its answer can be tested scientifically just like anything else. Now, we're not saying God bows to every whim of unbelieving people or that He meets every test imposed by mere curiosity or skepticism.\n\nJesus went about answering prayer and performing all kinds of miracles in the moral, spiritual, material, and physical realms. But when unbelieving Jews sought a sign from Heaven, He didn't satisfy their unbelief (Mt. 12:39). Some skeptics set up their own arbitrary "prayer gauge," and anything that doesn't meet their requirements doesn't count as an answered prayer to them. People have dared God to strike them dead, thinking He wouldn't—and when He didn't, they went off boasting there was no God.\n\nBut God has answered legitimate tests in the material realm. When Elijah had a contest with the prophets of false gods, God answered powerfully (1 Ki. 18:36-38). In Egypt, God manifested His power so all the Egyptians would know He was the true and living God (Exod. 7:5; 8:10, 22-23; 9:16, 29; 11:7).\n\nGod answered the sign Gideon asked for (Judg. 6:37-40). He answered Hezekiah with a sign, turning the sun backward ten degrees (Isa. 38:5-8). He even rebuked Ahaz for not asking a sign "either in the depth, or in the height above" (Isa. 7:10-13). Jesus promised that people could move literal mountains, just as He cursed the fig tree and performed other signs before witnesses (Mt. 21:21-22). But this was never done to satisfy mere unbelief and curiosity—it can only happen in sincerity and faith.\n\nWhen we have thousands of definite, concrete answers to prayer on record—both in the Bible and throughout history—we don't need to obligate God to perform some sign just to satisfy skeptics. The proof of answered prayer fills many pages of history, and this evidence is sufficient to convince any honest inquirer.\n\nThe Lord heard Abraham and healed a whole nation (Gen. 20:7, 17). He heard Eliezer and gave Isaac a wife (Gen. 24). He heard Isaac, and children were born (Gen. 25:21-26). He heard Moses, and Israel was spared from death with many signs on many occasions (Exod. 5:22–6:18; Exod. 32). He heard judges, prophets, priests, and leaders in Israel—and early Christians—times without number (Num. 12:13-15; Ps. 3:4; 6:8; 18:6; 21:2-4; 22:24; 28:6; 30:2-3; 31:22; 34:4-6; 40:1; 66:19-20; 77:1; 81:7; 99:6-8; 106:44; 107:6-7, 13-20; 116:1-2; 118:5, 21; 138:3; Hos. 12:4; Jonah 2:1; Lk. 23:42-43; Jn. 1:42; Acts 4:31; Jas. 5:17; etc.).\n\nThese and scores of other testimonies in the Bible prove that God has heard prayer for people of all past ages.\n\nThe history of the Church and of people's lives throughout this age overflow with testimonies of answered prayer. We have more testimony proving answered prayer than we have for any scientific fact ever proven. If there's any fact capable of proof, it's that God answers prayer.\n\nHundreds of thousands of people today testify to answered prayer in every area of life. Let any skeptic go to any church, and they'll find people who will affirm under oath that God has answered their prayers. Space won't permit sharing all the testimonies I've personally experienced or know that others have had. If you're doubtful, you can get proof from your next-door neighbor. Ask any true Christian, and you can collect all the evidence you need. Now let's move on to the practical help in prayer that people need today.\n\n## V. The Secrets of Asking and Receiving\n\n### 1. LEARN TO PRAY RIGHT\n\nThis is one of the greatest lessons to learn about prayer. You can't get an answer unless you pray right.\n\nIn Lk. 11:1, we see the disciples watching how Jesus prayed. They knew God always heard Him. There was such absolute simplicity and assurance when He prayed that a hunger grew in them to know how to pray like that. They asked Him, "Lord, teach us to pray, as John also taught his disciples."\n\nThis should be the daily longing of every child of God. The need to know how to pray right is the need of the hour.\n\nChrist is the greatest teacher of prayer—He practiced it Himself. We must learn to pray like Jesus, and God will always hear us, just as He always heard Him. God is no respecter of persons.\n\nThe following points will enable anyone to learn the art of prayer as far as knowing what to do. But they'll never give you practical experience in prayer—that comes only through much practice, doggedly following what you've learned. Knowing what to do and actually doing it are two different things. You need both knowledge and obedience to get answers.\n\nSince we know our need and we have the greatest teacher to help us, let's apply ourselves until prayer becomes second nature—just as it was with Him.\n\n### 2. THE MODEL PRAYER (Lk. 11:2-4)\n\nThe so-called Lord's Prayer is really a prayer for disciples—learners of how to pray. It was given when the disciples asked, "Lord, teach us to pray, as John also taught his disciples" (Lk. 11:1-4).\n\nAll believers can consider themselves learners and model their prayers after this one until they graduate from the school of prayer—or at least until they get every prayer answered. With that as our goal, we can all consider ourselves in the same school, learning the same lessons. Here are several things we need to learn:\n\n> **(1) "OUR FATHER."** This teaches a new and right relationship to God through the new birth (Jn. 3:1-8; 2 Cor. 5:17; Gal. 5:24; Jn. 15:7; 1 Jn. 2:29; 3:7-10; 5:1-4, 13-18).\n>\n> It's generally taught that people in Old Testament days never dared address God as "Father" because they weren't born-again. But searching Scripture actually proves the contrary. People of all past ages were born-again by faith in the coming Redeemer and His blood that was to be shed, just as we're born-again today by faith in the blood that has been shed.\n>\n> This is clear from Deut. 14:1; 32:18; 2 Sam. 7:14; 2 Chron. 28:6; Isa. 1:2; 63:16; Hos. 1:10; Ps. 82:6; Gal. 4:28-30; Rom. 4:1-25; Heb. 11. People in past ages asked and received on the same grounds we do today, as the Scriptures under Point IV show.\n>\n> The right relationship to God means everything and is one of the greatest secrets of answered prayer. Why would anyone be so presumptuous as to approach God stained with sin and in open rebellion, then expect Him to do what He's promised only to His children?\n>\n> Not only should we have the right relationship, but we must learn to whom prayer should be addressed. God the Father—our Father—is the one we should address in prayer. Jesus taught this in Jn. 16:23-26: "In that day [when I go back to the Father] ye shall ask me nothing . . . Whatsoever ye shall ask the Father in my name, he will give it you. Hitherto have ye asked nothing in my name: ask, and ye shall receive, that your joy may be full . . . At that day ye shall ask in my name."\n>\n> God the Father is the head of Christ and the Holy Spirit, and therefore He should be the one directly addressed in prayer (1 Cor. 11:3; Eph. 3:14-16).\n>\n> The habit of praying directly to Jesus or to the Holy Spirit should be broken, because we have no authority for such praying. God overlooks ignorance of this fact, just as He does other things—but if we want to learn to pray right, we should learn correctly and practice what we learn. God's Word shouldn't be ignored here any more than anywhere else.\n>\n> Children of God sometimes, because of the freedom they've found in Christ, willfully ignore certain technical truths and never seem concerned to obey fully. This may be why so much prayer goes unanswered—"to him that knoweth to do good, and doeth it not, to him it is sin" (Jas. 4:17).\n>\n> Some people decide they're not going to pay attention to "little details" like this. But we warn you: it's best to keep the letter of the Word, since an attitude that disregards what's written certainly isn't the spirit of the Word either. God commands obedience to His Word, not to what we think is right. It won't be a sin to obey. When we transgress the letter, we obey neither the spirit nor the letter. Saul learned this, and his lesson was recorded for our benefit (1 Sam. 15:22-30).\n>\n> We're not saying you shouldn't recognize Christ or the Holy Spirit in prayer and communion. Give them their proper scriptural place—worship all three persons of the Godhead and commune with all three appropriately. But address all petitions to the Father, in the name of Jesus, by the power of the Holy Spirit. Praying in Jesus' name to Jesus is foolish, and praying to God without using Jesus' name has no promise of reward. Any prayer that ignores Jesus or the Holy Spirit in their proper place isn't truly biblical and has no assurance of being heard.\n\n> **(2) "WHICH ART IN HEAVEN."** This teaches that we're associated with God in Heaven itself and identifies where God dwells, as we saw in Lesson Four, Point 9.\n>\n> Paul said, "our conversation [citizenship] is in heaven; from whence we look for the Saviour" (Phil. 3:20-21). As God's child, you should constantly recognize the source of your blessings and your citizenship, which guarantees certain rights—just like being a citizen of any country.\n\n> **(3) "HALLOWED BE THY NAME."** This teaches the proper manner of approaching God. Worship, adoration, and praise should come before your petitions and continue alongside them (Phil. 4:6; Heb. 13:15-16). This pattern runs throughout the Psalms.\n>\n> If our praise balanced our asking, we'd receive more. Praise demonstrates faith, and faith brings the answer. If you don't seem to have faith, praise God much for what you've received and what you're going to receive—and watch faith grow until you dare believe God for anything.\n\n> **(4) "THY KINGDOM COME."** This puts God's interests and His kingdom first—the needs of others before your own desires. This is loving your neighbor as yourself. The kingdom of God and its interests are the chief subjects for prayer.\n\n> **(5) "THY WILL BE DONE, AS IN HEAVEN, SO IN EARTH."** This accepts and agrees with the Father's will in all things. It's submission to God's will in prayer, yet it refuses to be denied concerning everything He's promised as His will.\n>\n> If we were just as stubborn in refusing failure in prayer as we are in accepting it, there would be very little failure. (See Point III above concerning God's will.)\n\n> **(6) "GIVE US DAY BY DAY OUR DAILY BREAD."** After you've followed the instructions above—you have the right relationship with God, you've directed your prayer to the right source, you've worshipped Him not just to get from Him but because you love Him, you've put kingdom interests first, you have definite promises before you that cover your needs, and you accept what you're praying for as God's will—then you're ready to make your petition known.\n>\n> Sadly, most people who pray have their own interests first and always. All they know about prayer is "Give me, give me, give me"—and that's about all God hears. This is fine in its proper place, but we must show God we have His interests at heart as well as others', that we love Him, and that we want to bless others regardless of our personal needs. When Solomon took this attitude, God heard him (1 Ki. 4:4-15).\n>\n> When Charles Page, a multi-millionaire from Tulsa, Oklahoma, prayed for his wife's healing and tried to bargain with God—promising to help the poor if God would heal her—he got no answer. He went back to God, confessed his bargaining, and promised to help the poor because it was right, not because he wanted something in return. His wife was instantly healed, and he gave millions to the poor over many years.\n\n> **(7) "AND FORGIVE US OUR SINS; FOR WE ALSO FORGIVE EVERY ONE THAT IS INDEBTED TO US."** Confession of sins and making things right with others goes a long way in getting answers to prayer. You can't expect to keep sinning or have a wrong attitude toward others and still be heard by God.\n>\n> If people would quit the sin business, live in peace, and love others as the gospel teaches, answers could be expected without going through a long process of rebuilding faith. In Mt. 6:14, Jesus based answered prayer on showing mercy to others. When we forgive, we can expect God to forgive. When we're kind, good, and merciful, we can expect the same from God.\n\n> **(8) "LEAD US NOT INTO TEMPTATION, BUT DELIVER US FROM EVIL."** Guidance and deliverance from God should be part of your prayer life—and you can expect it when you've faithfully met all the conditions above.\n\n### 3. THE BOLDNESS OF GOD'S FRIENDS (Lk. 11:5-8; Heb. 4:14-16; 10:19-23)\n\nOne of the greatest secrets of asking and receiving is being bold enough to come to the throne of grace. This shows both your immediate need and your friendship with God.\n\nWhen people are afraid to approach God boldly, that fear indicates lack of faith and trust—which in turn suggests either that the heart isn't right or that the person is under direct attack from demons. When you live clean and holy before God, there's a natural confidence that comes from walking closely with Him.\n\nIf you're living a true Christian life and still feel afraid or timid about coming to God, you may be under demonic attack. In that case, resist the devil, ignore those feelings, and approach God with full confidence. You can always tell whether you've sinned and are under conviction or not. If there's been no known sin, then evil spirits are likely trying to counterfeit conviction where no sin has been committed.\n\nJesus illustrates this boldness in friendship in Lk. 11:5-8. One friend went to another for bread at midnight because he had to have it right then—not the next day. He got it not because of friendship alone, but because of his bold persistence. Think of it like this: friendship creates the boldness to make demands on a friend. The greater the friendship, the greater the boldness in times of need.\n\nIn Heb. 4:14-16, we're commanded to come boldly to the throne of grace to obtain mercy and help in time of need. This boldness is based on the fact that we have a great high priest who is touched by our weaknesses and has been tempted in all points just like us. With such a mediator between God and us, with such a friend representing us before the Father, how could we fail? (Rom. 8:34; 1 Tim. 2:4-6).\n\nThe basis for coming to God in Heb. 10:19-23 is the blood of Jesus Christ, which gives us a new way of approach so we can "draw near to God with a true heart in full assurance of faith, having our hearts sprinkled from an evil conscience . . . Let us hold fast the profession of our faith without wavering; for he is faithful that promised."\n\n### 4. FAITH IN THE CERTAINTY OF ANSWERED PRAYER AND THE INFINITE FATHERHOOD OF GOD (Lk. 11:9-13)\n\nThis has already been covered in Point III above.\n\n### 5. THE ALL-COMPREHENSIVE GIFT (Lk. 11:9-13)\n\nJesus promised that God's children have a greater gift due them than what they received in the new birth. The gift of the Holy Spirit is promised here to every child of God, and His help is one of the secrets of successful praying.\n\nThe fact that this gift is promised only to God's children proves it's not the same as receiving the Holy Spirit in measure at the new birth, which every child of God receives when born-again (Rom. 8:9, 14-16).\n\nThis all-inclusive gift refers to the fullness of the Holy Spirit in the believer's life after the new birth. You must be a child of God before you can receive the fullness of the Spirit. This was true for Jesus (Mt. 3:16-17; Lk. 4:18-21; Jn. 3:34), the disciples (Acts 2), the Samaritans (Acts 9:5-25), Paul (Acts 9:1-17), and the Ephesians (Acts 19:1-7).\n\nIn Cornelius's case, God saved them and gave them the Spirit baptism all at once (Acts 10:43-48; 11:14-18; 15:7-11)—so you can receive the Spirit baptism at the same time you receive the new birth if you believe. If you don't, which is true of many, then you should ask God for it, as Jesus taught: "How much more shall your heavenly Father give the Holy Spirit to them that ask him?" (Lk. 11:13).\n\nThis whole passage (Lk. 11:9-13) proves the subject is gifts for people who are already children. God gave this gift to Cornelius and his household when they were saved to prove there's not necessarily a time gap between various blessings of God. If there is a gap, it's because individual knowledge and faith limit how God can work.\n\nThe Holy Spirit is one of our great helpers in prayer: "Likewise the Spirit also helpeth our infirmities: for we know not what we should pray for as we ought: but the Spirit himself maketh intercession for us with groanings which cannot be uttered" (Rom. 8:26-27, 34).\n\nWith both Christ and the Holy Spirit as our helpers, how could there possibly be failure in prayer? When we receive the all-comprehensive gift, we receive the all-comprehensive Helper in prayer. So let's have faith and boldness in prayer for all we want in this life and the life to come.\n\n### 6. THE POWER OF PERSEVERING PRAYER (Lk. 18:1-8)\n\n---\n\nThe conditions for answered prayer are clear: ask in faith, according to God's will as revealed in Scripture, without unconfessed sin blocking the channel. When those conditions are met, God answers. So here's the question to take with you: Think of one specific thing you've been asking God for without receiving an answer. Which of the conditions for answered prayer — faith, alignment with Scripture, clean conscience, persistence — have you genuinely, honestly met? And which are you still working around?	\N	\N	\N
cmq76y474000dw0ylnkdz72me	cmq76xgul000jhks877ughys4	Wave 6 Checkpoint: Personal Bondages	QUIZ	\N	46	\N	t	## Wave 6 Checkpoint: Personal Bondages\n\nFour forms of intimate personal bondage. Three questions — and these may be the hardest ones in the course to answer honestly.\n\n---\n\n**1. The course argues that pornography creates soul ties comparable to those created by physical sexual union. What is the theological basis for this?**\n\nA) The neurological impact of pornography on the brain is equivalent to the neurological impact of physical intimacy\nB) Scripture does not distinguish between lust in the heart and physical act — [Matthew 5:28](data-scripture="Matthew 5:28") — and the soul tie mechanism of [1 Corinthians 6:16](data-scripture="1 Corinthians 6:16") operates through the covenantal intention of sexual union, which pornography activates even without physical contact\nC) Pornography use and physical adultery produce identical legal ground because both involve explicit consent to sexual sin\nD) Soul ties form whenever sexual imagery is viewed, regardless of whether the viewer desires the connection\n\n---\n\n**2. The self-harm lesson identifies a spiritual assignment specifically at work in cutting and self-injury. What is it?**\n\nA) The Spirit of Rejection — the entity that drives the sufferer to punish themselves for perceived inadequacy\nB) The Spirit of Addiction — which drives escalation and dependency on the neurochemical response\nC) The Spirit of Death — which uses the blood shed in self-harm as a covenant transaction, establishing legal claim over the body with a specific long-term assignment toward lethal completion\nD) The Spirit of Control — which gives the sufferer a false sense of agency over their emotional pain\n\n---\n\n**3. What is the course's core argument about why eating disorders resist treatment in ways that standard addiction models do not fully explain?**\n\nA) Eating disorders involve cognitive distortions that are uniquely resistant to behavioral intervention\nB) The medical profession's materialist framework excludes the spiritual dimension — the demonic assignment operating through the disorder — which means even successful psychological treatment leaves the spiritual layer untreated and the pattern eventually returns\nC) Eating disorders are rooted in childhood trauma that requires specialized trauma therapy rather than addiction treatment\nD) Eating disorders involve both psychological and physical components that require simultaneous treatment, unlike most other addictions\n\n---\n\n**Answer Key**\n\n1. **B** — The soul tie mechanism operates through covenantal intention. Pornography activates that mechanism.\n2. **C** — The Spirit of Death. Blood is covenant currency. Self-harm spends it in the wrong direction.\n3. **B** — The spiritual dimension requires spiritual treatment. Psychology addresses real layers; deliverance addresses others. Both are necessary.\n\n---\n\n*Wave 7 is the final wave — and the most important for the generation currently alive. What follows has never been possible before.*	\N	\N	\N
cmq76y476000fw0yl0z9rw0lc	cmq76xgul000jhks877ughys4	Wave 7 Checkpoint: End-Times Systemic Deceptions	QUIZ	\N	51	\N	t	## Wave 7 Checkpoint: End-Times Systemic Deceptions\n\nFour attacks that require 21st-century infrastructure. Three questions.\n\n---\n\n**1. This course interprets the UFO/UAP phenomenon through a specific biblical category. What is it?**\n\nA) The phenomena are fabricated by human governments as a distraction from domestic policy failures\nB) Extraterrestrial life is real and represents God's creation beyond Earth — the church must develop a theology of the cosmos\nC) UAP phenomena are real, but the correct category is fallen angels — interdimensional entities who adapt their presentation to the dominant mythological frame of the culture they are targeting, currently presenting as extraterrestrials to a post-Christian culture that has no other category for non-human intelligence\nD) UAP phenomena are natural atmospheric events misidentified due to cognitive bias\n\n---\n\n**2. The course identifies a specific spiritual danger in Burning Man distinct from the drug use and sexual behavior. What is it?**\n\nA) Burning Man creates financial dependency through the ticket and travel costs required for participation\nB) The festival's "radical self-expression" principle normalizes moral relativism in ways that persist after the event\nC) Burning Man functions as a temporary pagan sacred space — a location dedicated to the spiritual principles of death and renewal through fire sacrifice — and the spiritual atmosphere of that space does not leave its visitors unchanged, regardless of their stated reason for attending\nD) The Burning Man community functions as a cult that maintains social control over members year-round through online community norms\n\n---\n\n**3. What specific mechanism makes AI "pastoral" tools spiritually dangerous beyond simple doctrinal error?**\n\nA) AI systems are directly programmed by technologists with anti-Christian worldviews\nB) AI cannot understand spiritual language and therefore cannot provide useful theological guidance\nC) AI pastoral tools are trained on the full corpus of human writing — including every false theology and demonic doctrine ever published — and deliver synthesis of that material in personalized, warm, contextually appropriate language, creating the emotional architecture of spiritual relationship without the Holy Spirit; while familiar spirits can influence outputs toward spiritually targeted deception for specific users\nD) The data collected by AI systems creates privacy risks for users who share spiritual struggles\n\n---\n\n**Answer Key**\n\n1. **C** — Fallen angels adapting their presentation. [2 Thessalonians 2:9-11](data-scripture="2 Thessalonians 2:9-11"): lying wonders.\n2. **C** — The sacred space effect is real regardless of personal intent. The spiritual atmosphere of a place does not ask why you came.\n3. **C** — The emotional architecture of spiritual relationship without the Spirit. [1 John 2:27](data-scripture="1 John 2:27"): the anointing teaches. Code does not.\n\n---\n\n*All seven waves complete. The Supplement that follows provides the theological framework for why every attack in this course worked — and the prayer guide for closing each door specifically.*	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk48i000zewhj95yqtw9l	cmnknk48e000xewhj49ao5az0	Lesson 20 — The New Testament Program for the Modern Church	READING	1691	1	\N	t	The early Church, in three centuries, turned the Roman Empire upside down without a marketing budget, without buildings, without a professional clergy class, and while being actively persecuted by the state. The modern church has all of those things — and is losing ground in every Western nation. Something changed. And it wasn't the culture. It was the program.\n\nLast time we established what it means to live under grace in this present dispensation. Now the question becomes practical: what was Jesus's actual program for His Church — and are we still running it?\n\nSo what's different? It's not the program — it's us. The Church has drifted.\n\nThe good news is that Scripture spells out this program clearly, and history shows us exactly how powerfully it worked in the lives of early believers. There's really no mystery here if you're honestly looking for the truth. Anyone who can read simple language can discover it right in the New Testament.\n\n![Lesson 20 — The New Testament Program for the Modern Church](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OskV3juz8rZcMaXYe27SivEmH1t8Ad0OozjUKI)\n\n## I. What the New Testament Program for the Modern Church Is\n\nThis program isn't complicated at all. It boils down to two main things: **Most churches today operate a come-to-us model: build an attractive service, make people comfortable, hope they bring their neighbors. But Jesus never modeled or commanded this. His program was simpler and more demanding: preach the gospel** and **confirm the gospel**. That's it.\n\nLet's look at how Jesus Himself modeled this. "The former treatise have I made, O Theophilus, of all that Jesus began both to do and to teach" (Acts 1:1-2). Notice that — He both *did* and *taught*.\n\n"Jesus went about all Galilee, teaching in their synagogues, and preaching the gospel of the kingdom, and healing all manner of sickness and all manner of diseases among the people" (Mt. 4:23-24). See the pattern? Preaching *and* healing went hand in hand.\n\nWhen John the Baptist sent messengers to ask if Jesus was the Messiah, Jesus pointed to the evidence: "Go and shew John again the things which ye do hear and see. The blind receive their sight, and the lame walk, the lepers are cleansed, and the deaf hear, the dead are raised up, and the poor have the gospel preached unto them" (Mt. 11:1-6).\n\nMark records the same two-part ministry: "He healed many that were sick of divers diseases, and cast out many devils . . . And he preached in their synagogues" (Mk. 1:34-39).\n\nJesus Himself declared His mission this way: "The Spirit of the Lord is upon me, because he hath anointed me TO PREACH the gospel to the poor; he hath sent me to heal . . . to set at liberty them that are bruised, to preach the acceptable year of the Lord" (Lk. 4:16-19).\n\n### The Disciples Carried On This Same Work\n\nPreaching and healing was the great work Christ started, and it's exactly what He commanded His disciples to continue — both before and after the cross.\n\nLook at what happened before the crucifixion: "He called unto him his twelve disciples [Judas included], he gave them power against unclean spirits, to cast them out, and to heal all manner of sickness and all manner of disease . . . These twelve [Judas included] Jesus sent forth and commanded them . . . as ye go preach, saying, The kingdom of heaven is at hand. Heal the sick, cleanse the lepers, raise the dead, cast out devils: freely ye have received, freely give" (Mt. 10:1-7).\n\nMark tells us: "He ordained twelve [Judas included], that they should be with him, and that he might send them forth to preach, and to have power to heal sicknesses, and to cast out devils" (Mk. 3:13-15).\n\nAnd they actually did it! "And they [the twelve, Judas included] went out, and preached that men should repent. And cast out many devils, and anointed with oil many that were sick and healed them" (Mk. 6:7-13).\n\nLuke confirms: "He sent them [the twelve, Judas included] to preach the kingdom of God, and to heal the sick . . . and they departed, and went through the towns, preaching the gospel, and healing every where" (Lk. 9:1-6).\n\n### The Seventy Were Also Empowered\n\nIt wasn't just the twelve apostles. Christ ordained seventy others and sent them out two by two to do the exact same work. He told them: "into whatsoever city ye enter . . . heal the sick that are therein, and say [preach] unto them, The kingdom of God is come nigh unto you" (Lk. 10:1-24).\n\n### The Great Commission Continues This Pattern\n\nWhen Jesus was ready to return to Heaven, He specifically commanded His followers to carry on what He "began both to do and to teach" (Acts 1:1-2).\n\nHe declared: "All power [authority] is given unto me in heaven and in earth." Then He commissioned them: "Go ye therefore, and teach all nations baptizing them in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Ghost: Teaching them to observe all things whatsoever I have commanded you: and, lo, I am with you alway, even unto the end of the world" (age, Mt. 28:18-20).\n\nBut first, He told them to wait for power: "but tarry ye in the city of Jerusalem, until ye be endued with power from on high" (Lk. 24:49; Acts 1:4-8).\n\n### The Promise of Signs Following\n\nJesus didn't just send them out hoping for the best. He promised specific results: "These signs shall follow them that believe; In my name shall they cast out devils; they shall speak with new tongues; they shall take up serpents [see Lesson Fourteen, Point V, 11 for the doctrine of taking up serpents]; and if they drink any deadly thing, it shall not hurt them; they shall lay hands on the sick, and they shall recover" (Mk. 16:15-18).\n\n"Ye shall be baptized with the Holy Ghost not many days hence . . . Ye shall receive power, after that the Holy Ghost is come upon you: and ye shall be witnesses unto me . . . unto the uttermost parts of the earth" (Acts 1:4-8).\n\nJesus made this stunning promise: "He that believeth on me, the works that I do shall he do also; and greater works than these shall he do; because I go unto my Father. And whatsoever ye shall ask in my name, that will I do, that the Father may be glorified in the Son. If ye shall ask any thing in my name, I will do it . . . ye shall ask what ye will, and it shall be done unto you . . . I have chosen you, and ordained you, that ye should go and bring forth fruit, and that your fruit should remain: that whatsoever ye shall ask of the Father in my name, he may give it you" (Jn. 14:12-15; 15:7, 16).\n\n### No Limitations on God's Promises\n\nThese promises — and hundreds more like them — were made to believers. You won't find limitations or qualifications attached to them. They're unlimited and unqualified, so that "all things are possible to him that believeth" (Mk. 9:23; 11:22-24).\n\nThink of it like this: God didn't fill up the Bible with promises just to make it a bigger book. He gave these promises to give you absolute assurance that you can carry on the work Jesus "began both to do and to teach" and confirm the Word of God as you preach it.\n\n### The Early Church Demonstrated This Program\n\nThe Lord backed up the preaching of the early Church with power, proving that He wanted everyone in this age to know that preaching is only *part* of the program. Confirmation of what's preached is equally essential.\n\nHere's the evidence from Scripture:\n\n"They cast out many devils, and anointed with oil many that were sick, and healed them" (Mk. 6:7-13).\n\n"They departed, and went through the towns, preaching the gospel, and healing everywhere" (Lk. 9:16).\n\n"And the seventy returned again with joy, saying, Lord, even devils are subject unto us through thy name" (Lk. 10:17).\n\nAfter Jesus ascended: "So then after the Lord had spoken unto them, he was received up into heaven, and sat on the right hand of God. And they went forth, and preached everywhere, the Lord working with them, and confirming the word with signs following" (Mk. 16:19-20).\n\nThe book of Acts is full of this pattern: "And many wonders and signs were done by the apostles . . . such as I have give I unto thee: In the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth rise up and walk . . . and immediately his feet and ankle bones received strength" (Acts 2:43; 3:6-11).\n\n"And when they had prayed the place was shaken . . . and they were all filled with the Holy Ghost . . . and by the hands of the apostles were many signs and wonders wrought among the people . . . Insomuch that they brought forth the sick into the streets, and laid them on beds and couches, that at least the shadow of Peter passing by might overshadow some of them. There came also a multitude . . . bringing sick folks, and them that were vexed with unclean spirits: and they were healed every one" (Acts 4:29-31; 5:12-16).\n\n"Peter said unto him, Jesus Christ maketh thee whole: arise, and make thy bed. And he arose immediately . . . Tabitha, arise. And she opened her eyes: and when she saw Peter, she sat up" (Acts 9:32-43).\n\n### Not Just the Twelve Apostles\n\nSome people — even entire denominations — argue that only the twelve apostles received the baptism in the Spirit and had power to confirm God's Word. But Scripture proves this is false.\n\nIn Acts 6, we see seven men chosen from among ordinary believers specifically because they were "full of the Holy Ghost." One of them, Stephen, "full of the Holy Ghost and power, did great wonders and miracles among the people" (Acts 6:3, 5-10).\n\nPhilip, another of these business elders, had a great revival and did great miracles among the people in Samaria (Acts 8:5-20).\n\nWhen believers were scattered by persecution, "went everywhere preaching the word . . . And the hand of the Lord was with them: and a great number believed, and turned to the Lord" (Acts 8:4; 11:19-21). The phrase "the hand of the Lord was with them" proves they did miracles, as is clear from Acts 4:30; 13:11. People turned to the Lord in great numbers *because* of miracles (Acts 9:35; 15:12, 19).\n\nAnanias — not one of the twelve — was filled with the Holy Ghost and had power to lay hands on Paul, baptize him in the Spirit, and heal him of blindness (Acts 9:10-19).\n\nPaul and Barnabas did great signs and wonders (Acts 13:11; 14:3, 8-20; 15:12; 16:16-34; 19:1-6). God worked special miracles through Paul so that handkerchiefs and aprons from his body healed the sick and cast out demons (Acts 19:11-20; 28:7-9; Rom. 15:18, 29).\n\nPaul's immunity from a snakebite is one of the signs Jesus said would follow all believers (Acts 28:1-6; Mk. 16:17-20). Timothy, one of Paul's converts, did the same kind of miracles Paul did (1 Cor. 16:10).\n\nThe pattern continues throughout the New Testament. Ordinary elders of all the churches were to be called to heal the sick (Jas. 5:14-16).\n\nPaul issued this warning: none of us will escape God's judgment if we neglect so great a salvation "Which at the first began to be spoken by the Lord, and was confirmed unto us by them that heard him; God also bearing them witness, both with signs and wonders, and divers miracles, and gifts of the Holy Ghost, according to His own will" (Heb. 2:3-4).\n\nIn 1 Cor. 12, Paul describes nine gifts of the Spirit distributed among all believers who earnestly desire them. So the teaching that miracles only happened among the twelve apostles — and that we can't experience the same things today — is simply false.\n\n### This Program Is for Today\n\nThe New Testament program of preaching and confirming the Word is clearly established for the modern Church — if believers actually want these experiences.\n\nPeople throughout this age were commanded to observe all that Jesus commanded His disciples (Mt. 28:20). The same baptism in the Spirit is promised to everyone who believes in Christ (Mt. 3:11; Jn. 7:38-39; Acts 2:38-39; 5:32). All the promises of God are yea and amen to those who believe (2 Cor. 1:20).\n\nHere's what we've established: Christ started this program. The disciples were commanded to continue it. They were promised power to make it successful. They *did* make it successful, and they commanded believers throughout this age to continue such a program and contend for the faith once delivered to the saints (Jude 3).\n\nWhy did many in the early Church fail? Why has the Church failed as a whole from the apostolic period until today? That's explained in Lesson Nineteen, Point VII, 2 and 3.\n\n## II. The Power Every Believer in Christ Should Exercise\n\nAccording to Jesus's promise, every believer should have power to do the works of Christ — and even greater works than He did (Jn. 14:12-15; 15:7, 16).\n\nThe apostles promised that all believers could have the same baptism in the Spirit they received (Acts 2:38-39; 5:32; 2 Cor. 1:20) and that they could have the gifts of the Holy Ghost (1 Cor. 1:7; 12:1-11, 31; 14:1; Rom. 1:11; 12:3-8; Eph. 4:7-16; 1 Tim. 4:14; 2 Tim. 1:6).\n\nI have watched for years as our churches have drifted from these truths, and I'm passionate about seeing them return to the old ways. Millions of people are leaving Christian churches and turning to false religions because the modern church program offers them nothing.\n\nIt's similar to a store that advertises amazing products but has empty shelves. Eventually, people stop coming and go somewhere else — even if what they find elsewhere is counterfeit.\n\nFalse religions are demonstrating more power and getting people more results — physically, materially, and spiritually — than the average modern church. So people are turning to whatever offers them tangible benefits.\n\nI'm against these false religions. I'm *for* the churches and want to help all of them rediscover the original type of Christianity that will draw these people back.\n\nPeople are hungry for supernatural power to heal when they need it. They want answers to prayer. They want material help. They want freedom from sin and failure.\n\nIf they can't get it from one source, they'll accept it from another. If they can't get the real thing the gospel offers, they'll settle for Satan's substitute. Satan knows he can deceive people with his counterfeit power, and he's winning millions who should be in Christian churches — and would be, if ministers would wake up to their responsibility and get God into the churches in a supernatural way.\n\n## III. Christian Doctrines and Experiences Seldom Seen in Modern Churches\n\nYears ago, I became deeply troubled by something: God was not doing for modern believers what I read about in the New Testament. Being someone who values sincerity, honesty, and at least ordinary intelligence, I decided to find out what was wrong.\n\nThink about it this way: when a machine won't run or something fails to carry out its purpose, you know something needs to be fixed before it will work properly. I applied this same common sense to spiritual things. I had read the New Testament enough to see a massive difference between the modern church and the early one.\n\nSo I developed a plan: investigate and experiment with the spiritual truths taught in the New Testament. I was pastoring a church whose members were nearly all honest and open to all the Bible's teachings.\n\nI said to the church, "Let's gather night after night and read the New Testament through to discover what it teaches that we're not doing and what kind of spiritual experiences it describes that we haven't received. Let's bring pencils and paper and write down everything we find that we're not practicing as a church and every blessing the early believers had that we don't have today."\n\nWe gathered on Monday night and read through Matthew 10. That first night, we discovered why we didn't have the power the early disciples had. Christ "gave them power" and said, "heal the sick, cleanse the lepers, raise the dead, cast out devils: freely ye have received, freely give" (Mt. 10:1-8).\n\nI stopped and commented that we'd found what we were looking for the very first night. The reason we couldn't freely give was that we hadn't freely received.\n\nTo make a long story short, when we finished reading through the entire New Testament and honestly listing everything we weren't practicing and every experience that wasn't normal in our lives, I had **160 points** on my list.\n\nI could hardly believe my eyes — and yet there was chapter and verse for every single point. I knew these things weren't happening in our church because I had never seen them.\n\nFrom that moment, I became a different man. I had to choose: either remain honest, face the facts, and acknowledge the truth I had discovered — or reject my findings, turn dishonest, and make excuses for why we don't have these blessings today.\n\nI decided — as I always have and always will — to be honest with the eternal Word of God and with my own soul and the souls of everyone I teach. I have received a measure of power from God and am expecting the fullness in my life so I can help all people get free from the devil and all his works.\n\nHere's the truth: John 14:12 states the divine standard for every believer. Until a believer can do the works of Christ, they're not a normal New Testament Christian in terms of power.\n\nYou may have many other blessings from God, but if you don't have power like Christ's, you're falling short of your privileges. There's nothing to be gained by denying this fact.\n\nThis doesn't mean you're not saved or not going to Heaven. It simply means you're not up to the New Testament standard of power with God. Anything short of this power reveals a human limitation to the promises. The New Testament standard of normal power for every believer is clear — and we can have it if we want it and if we obey all the New Testament to the letter.\n\nThese teachings and practices, along with scores of others from the New Testament, are more or less foreign to the average church member. Even the average preacher knows little about them.\n\nIs it any wonder the modern church is powerless? Is it any wonder multitudes are chasing counterfeit power and outward refinement in false religions?\n\nMany thousands of people in local churches are following leaders who deny that New Testament doctrines and experiences are even for us today. Until the modern church again believes in the faith once delivered to the saints, it cannot contend for that faith. So it will continue in helplessness and defeat. It will continue in worldliness and formality — and people searching for the supernatural will keep leaving, even if it means denying Christ and the Bible.\n\nThe modern church must wake up to its responsibilities and privileges in the gospel, or millions more will be lost to false religions and demonic powers.\n\nTime is short. What the church must do, it must do quickly.\n\nLet everyone who names the name of Christ depart from iniquity and contend for the fullness of power and faith promised to those who believe the gospel of Jesus Christ, the only begotten Son of God.\n\nGod will back us up by the power of the Holy Ghost and all the hosts of Heaven. The devil is already a defeated foe, and his power has been broken. He's already been proven in the Court of Heaven as a usurper and intruder into humanity's dominion, holding people in his power only as they consent to it.\n\nAll that remains is to carry out the execution of the sentence against him. This will be done at the second advent of Christ. But right now, believers can defeat him and all his works in their personal lives and enjoy the fullness of the blessing of the gospel of Christ.\n\nLet all who read these lessons unite to pray and work for New Testament power in all our churches.\n\n## IV. Questions the Modern Church Must Answer\n\n> 1. Is this program for the modern church?\n\n> 2. Does God desire to carry on this program today?\n\n> 3. Do we need such a program in the modern church?\n\n> 4. Why does not God carry on this program more fully than He does now?\n\n> 5. Has God a lower standard of world-attraction for His gospel than in the beginning?\n\n> 6. Is God able to confirm the gospel today as recorded in the New Testament?\n\n> 7. Are supernatural powers necessary today to confirm the Word of God?\n\n> 8. Does the Word of God need confirmation today before the unbelieving world?\n\n> 9. Are there any sick, lame, demon-possessed, and defeated people today?\n\n> 10. Why would not God want to see men delivered today from the devil as in days of old?\n\n> 11. Why did Jesus die for all men of all ages to deliver them from these troubles if His work was to be limited only to the early Church period?\n\n> 12. Why did the Holy Spirit come to abide forever with men if He was to work only in the lives of the apostles and a few special pets of God in the early Church?\n\n> 13. Why were the hundreds of promises given that all men of all ages should be victorious over all the works of the devil if they were to be limited to the early Church?\n\n> 14. Why would God desire to see His people today tormented of the devil any more than He desired it in the days of Christ and the apostles?\n\n> 15. Why would God want His children oppressed by His enemy when no other father would tolerate such a thing if he could help it?\n\n> 16. Are we receiving new light about God's New Testament program for the Church?\n\n> 17. What should be the attitude of the Church if these things be true?\n\n> 18. What is the responsibility of the Church in view of these facts?\n\n> 19. How can we get the New Testament program into operation today?\n\n> 20. What would be the results of such a program in the modern Church?\n\nThese questions — and many others — must be answered by modern believers. By now, it should be clear to you what the answers are.\n\nIf this program was for the early Church, it's still for the Church today. The apostles were commanded to teach all nations to observe everything Christ commanded them (Mt. 28:19-20). Jesus Christ is the same today as ever (Heb. 13:8).\n\nNowhere in Scripture did God say this program would change when the apostles died or that it was only for a certain period. On the contrary, Christ promised to be with the Church until the end of the age (Mt. 28:20). Paul declared that all the promises of God are yea and amen to those who believe (2 Cor. 1:20).\n\nGod is the same. The devil is the same. Sin is the same. Sickness and demon-possessed people are still with us.\n\nThe power of God needs to be manifest today as much as ever. So we must conclude that it's God's will for the modern Church to face the facts and contend for the faith once delivered to the saints (Jude 3).\n\nThe work of the Spirit, the power of the blood, and the Word of God were meant to continue being effective in the lives of all believers throughout this age. Even if the Church as a whole refuses to accept these truths or put forth effort to carry on this program, you as an individual can get benefits through faith.\n\n### Why Doesn't God Do More Today?\n\nThe only reason God doesn't carry on this program more fully today is the Church's failure to cooperate with Him. There's no question that such a program is needed to bless people according to their needs — and that God needs it to attract multitudes to His gospel.\n\nGod works only through people who have faith and who will permit Him to work in their lives. This has always been true and always will be (Rom. 10:10-21).\n\nGod hasn't lowered His standard of world attraction to His gospel. He's waiting for the Church to pray for a spiritual revival that will produce signs and wonders again. God is just as able today to save and heal multitudes — if preachers would wake up to their true calling and work at it.\n\n### The Contest Is On\n\nDemon-inspired religions are being evidenced and confirmed by the supernatural power of the devil. The need for God's greater power is obvious — to prove to people that Christianity promises and delivers everything they need in this life as well as the life to come.\n\nPicture it this way: there's a contest happening between the actual results and power of the gospel versus false religions. And right now, false religions are showing up with demonstrations while many churches show up with empty talk.\n\nThe Word of God needs confirmation today in the sense of proving that the gospel is the same power of God unto salvation today as it was in the beginning. The unbelieving world has come to believe in supernatural powers, and multitudes are attracted wherever there's a real manifestation of the supernatural — whether it's the devil's power or God's.\n\n### The Church's Opportunity\n\nThe Church stands today on the threshold of great and mighty achievements if it will wake up and wage an aggressive fight against Satan and his demon-forces. The Church can prove to the world once again that God's power is greater than Satan's.\n\nThere are sick and demon-possessed people in nearly every home who would gladly turn to God and be healed and saved from sin — if all the churches would stop fighting each other and the gospel itself, and start teaching people how to receive the benefits God promises.\n\nJesus died so that this program could be carried on throughout this age. The Holy Spirit was sent to carry it on. The Word of God was given to teach us what the program is and how to get its full benefits.\n\nThe program was started right, as demonstrated by Christ and the early Church. It can be fully revived again if we'll accept the truth.\n\nGod has always been ready to defeat Satan and his works in the lives of His children. When the Church gets ready and anxious to see God work, then our desires will be granted. God told Jeremiah that if people would call upon Him, He would show them great and mighty things (Jer. 33:3). Jesus and others promised that these things would happen in the lives of every believer — so there's no excuse but hardness of heart and the sin of unbelief.\n\nThis might sound impossible, like an idle dream. But just as sure as God is in Heaven and His Word is true, these changes and many others will literally come to divided and worldly Christendom today — if all churches will adopt the program of the New Testament as it is.\n\n## Questions on Lesson Twenty\n\n- Has the New Testament program changed, or has the modern Church? Prove.\n\n- How can we get to know what the New Testament program is?\n\n- What is the New Testament program? Prove from Scripture.\n\n- Who started the program? Who were commanded to carry it on?\n\n- How long was the program to continue? Prove.\n\n- Who was sent from Heaven to carry on this program?\n\n- Has He failed? Who has? Why?\n\n- Is there a limitation to the needful benefits of this life in the promises?\n\n- Why were the promises given and for whom?\n\n- Through whom did God continue the program in the first century?\n\n- Were the apostles the only ones who carried on the program? Prove.\n\n- To what extent was the program successful in winning souls in the beginning?\n\n- What kind of power should every believer have in Christ? Prove.\n\n- Why do not all Christians have this power?\n\n- Why are multitudes of professed Christians going after demon religions?\n\n- What should the Church do to counteract this exodus from Christ and to attract men again?\n\n- Name a number of Christian experiences seldom seen in the average church of today.\n\n- What verses of Scripture state the normal standard of New Testament power?\n\n- Name a number of Christian principles and practices seldom seen in churches.\n\n- Discuss the modern revivals of the power of God.\n\n- What should the Church awaken to?\n\n- What are some of the questions the modern church must answer?\n\n- Give your own answer to these questions by giving Scripture to prove the answers.\n\n- Why is not God fully carrying out this program today?\n\n- How are false religions attracting people?\n\n- Should the Church be satisfied to let demon powers work more than God?\n\n- What should believers do who are getting more light?\n\n- What should they do if preachers and churches reject truth?\n\n- What kind of results can the Church see if it will obey the Bible?\n\n- Is this program with the results pictured possible today? How?\n\n---\n\nThe New Testament program has never been cancelled and never been improved upon: preach the gospel and confirm it with power. The question is not whether it still works — it's whether we're still running it. So here's the honest question for you personally: When did you last share the gospel with someone outside the church? And when did you last pray for a specific person by name to be saved — not in general terms, but with the expectation that God would answer?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk48i0011ewhjtbauau0c	cmnknk48e000xewhj49ao5az0	Lesson 21 — The Truth About Jesus Christ	READING	5623	3	\N	t	Every major world religion has an opinion about Jesus. He's a prophet, a wise teacher, a good man, a symbol of divine love, an enlightened being, a reformer. What almost no one outside orthodox Christianity is willing to say is what Jesus actually claimed about Himself: that He was God in the flesh, that He existed before Abraham, that He and the Father are one, and that no one comes to the Father except through Him. These are not the claims of a good man. They're either the claims of God or of a madman.\n\nLast time we examined the New Testament program for the Church: preach and confirm the gospel. Now we focus on the center of that gospel — the person of Jesus Christ Himself. Who is He, really? And what does Scripture say that most people — even most Christians — don't fully grasp?\n\nThen something remarkable happened—one of the three divine persons took on human form to redeem the world. This is what sets Jesus apart from the other members of the Godhead during these past 1,900 years. Let's explore who Jesus Christ really is as the visible manifestation of the invisible God among us.\n\n![Lesson 21 — The Truth About Jesus Christ](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsEfRqlQtdLWqJHyu8Q5MfmIRAjonSBKDvC0V2)\n\n## I. The Pre-Existence of Jesus Christ\n\nThe Bible makes it absolutely clear that the person we now know as Jesus Christ was one of the three divine persons of the deity. As God, He had no beginning. When we talk about His "pre-existence," we're referring to the time before He became a man.\n\nTechnically speaking, there's no such thing as existence "before" Him as God—but He certainly existed before becoming human. Mic. 5:1-2 speaks of Him as existing from all eternity. John describes Him as existing in the very beginning with the Father (Jn. 1:1-5).\n\nJesus Himself spoke of being before Abraham and before the world was even created (Jn. 8:58; 17:5, 24). Paul tells us He existed before all things and that He is both the Creator and Upholder of everything (Col. 1:15-18; Heb. 1:1-3, 8; 2:10). God the Father created all things through Him (Eph. 3:9) and the Holy Spirit (Gen. 1:2).\n\n## II. The Deity of Jesus Christ\n\n### 1. Divine Names and Titles Are Ascribed to Him\n\nThe names and titles given to Jesus in Scripture prove beyond doubt that He is divine by nature and a full member of the Godhead. Think of it like someone's credentials—if a person holds every title of a particular office, they clearly occupy that position.\n\nHe is called "God" and "Immanuel" (Mt. 1:23; Jn. 1:1; 20:28; Acts 20:28); "Lord" (Lk. 19:34; Acts 2:36); "Lord of All" (Acts 10:36); "Lord of Glory" (1 Cor. 2:8); "Wonderful, Counselor, Mighty God, Everlasting Father, Prince of Peace" (Isa. 9:6-7); "Christ the Lord" (Lk. 2:26); "The Son of God" (Mt. 4:3; 14:33; Lk. 22:70; Jn. 1:34; Rom. 1:4); "His Son" (Mt. 22:45; Jn. 3:16-18); "My Son" (Mt. 3:17); "The Only Begotten Son" (Jn. 1:18; 3:16-18; 1 Jn. 4:9).\n\nHe's also called "The First and the Last, Alpha and Omega, The Beginning and the Ending" (Rev. 22:12, 13, 16); "The Lord" (Acts 9:17); "The Son of the Highest" (Lk. 1:32; Mk. 14:61); "The Bread of God" (Jn. 6:33); "The Holy One of God" (Mk. 1:24); "Thy Holy Child Jesus" (Acts 4:30); "King of Kings and Lord of Lords" (Rev. 19:16); "Lord and Saviour" (2 Pet. 3:2); and "The Word of God" (Rev. 19:13).\n\nThese names and titles—along with many others throughout Scripture—prove Christ's deity. Some of them appear hundreds of times in the Bible. If we're going to believe the Bible, we must believe in the divinity of Christ.\n\n### 2. Divine Attributes Are Ascribed to Him\n\nThis becomes crystal clear in Phil. 2:5-11, where Paul explains that Christ existed in God's form. He then laid aside this form and limited His divine attributes and powers to become a man. After His earthly limitation, these powers were restored to Him, as we'll see in Point VIII below.\n\n### 3. Divine Offices Are Ascribed to Him\n\nJesus holds offices that belong only to God. He is called "The Creator" (Jn. 1:3; Col. 1:16; Heb. 1:1-3); "Mediator" (1 Tim. 2:4-5; Heb. 8:6); "Head of the Church" (Eph. 1:22; Col. 1:16-24); "Saviour" (2 Pet. 3:2); "Judge" (2 Tim. 4:1); "Preserver" (Heb. 1:1-3); "Life Giver" (Jn. 10:28; 17:2); "Lord and Christ" (Acts 2:36); "The Resurrection and the Life" (Jn. 11:25).\n\nThese offices and works prove that Christ is divine and one with the Father as part of the deity. Scripture calls Him the fellow and equal of God in divinity (Zech. 13:7; Jn. 5:17-23; 10:30-38; 17:10).\n\n### 4. Divine Character Is Ascribed to Him\n\nAll ordinary human beings are sinners by nature (Ps. 51:5; Eph. 3:1-3; Rom. 5:12-21). But Christ is different. He is "holy by birth" (Lk. 1:35); "righteous" (Isa. 53:11; Heb. 1:9); "faithful" (Isa. 11:5; 1 Thess. 5:24); "true" (Jn. 1:14; 14:6); "just" (Jn. 5:30); "guileless" (1 Pet. 2:22); "sinless" (2 Cor. 5:21); "spotless" (1 Pet. 1:19); "innocent" (Mt. 27:4); "harmless" (Heb. 7:26).\n\nHe was "obedient to God" (Jn. 15:10; Heb. 5:8-10) and "His earthly parents" (Lk. 2:51); "zealous" (Jn. 2:17); "meek" (Mt. 11:29); "lowly in heart" (Mt. 11:29); "merciful" (Heb. 2:17); "patient" (Isa. 53:7); "long-suffering" (1 Tim. 1:16); "compassionate" (Mt. 15:32); "benevolent" (Acts 10:38); "loving" (Jn. 15:13); "self-denying" (2 Cor. 8:9); "humble" (Phil. 2:5-11); "resigned" (Lk. 22:42); and "forgiving" (Lk. 23:34).\n\n### 5. The Works of God Are Ascribed to Him\n\nScripture attributes God's works directly to Christ (Jn. 1:3; Col. 1:15-18; Heb. 1:1-3, 10; Jn. 5:19-23; Rev. 3:14).\n\n### 6. Divine Worship Was Given to Him\n\nBoth angels and humans worship Jesus—yet both refuse worship for themselves (Mt. 4:9-10; 14:33; 28:9; Lk. 24:52; Jn. 5:23; 14:14; Acts 7:59; Rom. 10:9-13; Heb. 1:6; Phil. 2:10-11; Rev. 5:12-14). When people tried to worship Peter or when John attempted to worship an angel, they were immediately stopped (Acts 10:25-26; Heb. 1:6; Rev. 22:8-9). But Jesus accepted worship—because He deserves it as God.\n\n### 7. His Name Is Associated with the Father and Holy Spirit\n\nJesus' name is joined with the Father and the Holy Spirit as one of the three divine persons in the deity (Mt. 28:19; Jn. 5:19-23; 14:1, 23; 17:3; Rom. 1:7; 2 Cor. 13:14; 1 Jn. 5:7-8; Rev. 5:13; 7:10; 20:6).\n\n### 8. Equality with God Is Definitely Stated\n\nScripture explicitly declares His equality with God in divinity (Jn. 5:19-29; Phil. 2:5-11).\n\n### 9. Divine Characteristics Are Ascribed to Him\n\nThe Bible attributes divine characteristics to Christ (Jn. 5:19-29; 14:26; Heb. 1:9).\n\n### 10. He Is Expressly Called "God" and "Lord"\n\nScripture directly calls Jesus "God" and "Lord" (Jn. 1:1-3; 20:28; Acts 2:36; 20:28).\n\n## III. The Humanity of Jesus Christ\n\n### 1. Human Names Are Ascribed to Him\n\nJesus bears human names that prove His humanity: "Rabboni" (Jn. 20:16); "Messiah" or "Christ" (Jn. 1:41; 4:25; Lk. 2:26); "Jesus" (Mt. 1:21); "Master" (Mt. 9:19); "Son of man" (Mt. 8:20); "Son of Mary" (Mk. 6:3); "Son of Abraham and David" (Mt. 1:1); "Seed and Offspring of David" (Rom. 1:3; Rev. 5:5; 22:16); "The Second Man" and "The Last Adam" (1 Cor. 15:45-47); "The King of the Jews" (Mt. 2:2); "Lamb of God" (Jn. 1:29).\n\n### 2. He Is Called a "Babe," a "Child," and a "Man"\n\nScripture refers to Him with these distinctly human terms (Lk. 2:16; Isa. 9:6; Acts 17:31; 1 Tim. 2:4-5; Rom. 5:12-21; Jn. 8:40; Acts 2:22; 1 Cor. 15:21, 45-47).\n\n### 3. Prophecy of His Human Birth Proves His Humanity\n\nThe Old Testament predicted He would be born of a human mother (Gen. 3:15; Isa. 7:14; 9:6-7; 11:1; 53:1-12; Ps. 22).\n\n### 4. Historical Record of His Birth Proves His Humanity\n\nThe New Testament records His conception and birth from a woman (Mt. 1:18-25; 2:2; Lk. 1:32-35; 2:1-52; Gal. 4:4).\n\n### 5. He Had Flesh and Blood Like All Other People\n\nJesus possessed a physical body just like ours (Jn. 1:14; Heb. 2:14-15; 1 Jn. 4:1-6; Lk. 24:39; Jn. 19:34).\n\n### 6. He Had a Human Body Even After the Resurrection\n\nHis resurrected body was still physical (Lk. 24:39; Jn. 20:27).\n\n### 7. He Had Human Limitations and Passions Like Us\n\nJesus experienced everything we experience: He "wept" (Jn. 11:35); "hungered" (Mt. 4:1-11); "thirsted" (Jn. 4:7; 19:28); "slept" (Mt. 8:24); "grew weary" (Jn. 4:6); "sorrowed" (Isa. 53:3-4); "suffered physical agony" (Lk. 22:44); "craved sympathy" (Mt. 26:36-40; Lk. 22:15-27); "was tempted in all points as men" (Heb. 4:14-16); "suffered physical death" (Jn. 19:30; 1 Cor. 15:3). He endured many human sufferings just as we do.\n\n### 8. He Was Human in All Things\n\nJesus was subject to the physical, mental, and moral conditions of human existence just like the rest of us (Heb. 2:14-17; 4:14-16).\n\n### 9. He Lived a Normal Human Life\n\nHe lived in total dependence upon God through prayer and faith for daily grace for body, soul, and spirit—exactly as all human beings should (Jn. 5:30-46; 6:57; 7:16; 8:27-29; Heb. 5:7-9; Phil. 2:5-10).\n\n### 10. He Was Limited Like Other Humans\n\nJesus was limited in wisdom, knowledge, and power like other men and was subordinate to the Father, as we'll see in Point VIII below. In fact, His human nature is denied only by antichrists and demons (1 Jn. 2:18-23; 4:1-6).\n\n## IV. The Union of the Two Natures of Jesus Christ\n\nEverything we've studied about Christ's divinity and humanity proves He was a divine human being. The orthodox understanding holds that both natures of Christ were complete in themselves, yet so organically and permanently united that no third nature was formed. We must not divide His person or confuse His two natures.\n\nThink of it like this: because He is truly divine, He perfectly represents God; because He is truly human, He perfectly represents us.\n\nChrist always spoke of Himself as a single person, not as two persons in one. There's no back-and-forth conversation between His two natures as if they were separate people talking to each other. The attributes and powers of both natures belong to one person and operate as part of a single individual. There's no double personality—just one unified being with characteristics of both the human and the divine.\n\nIt's similar to how a father and mother each contribute traits to their child, creating a single person with characteristics from both parents. In the same way, the human and divine united in the one person of Jesus Christ—with one body, one soul, one spirit, one consciousness, and one will.\n\nThe Fatherhood of God and the motherhood of Mary produced a single personality. Remember that God made human beings with the same bodily parts He has in His Spirit-body—only our bodies are earthly and human while His is spiritual and divine. He made us with the same kind of soul with feelings, emotions, passions, desires, and appetites, capable of the same soul-acts as He is—only our souls are finite and His is infinite. He made us with spirits having all the attributes and powers that He has—only our spirits are finite and His is infinite.\n\nIn other words, we're endowed with the same traits, characteristics, attributes, powers, feelings, and passions as God—just on a finite scale.\n\nUnderstanding this helps us see that the soul and spirit faculties born in Jesus Christ by a divine Father and human mother were exactly the same as in any being like God. When Christ exercised any attribute or power as a man, it was like God exercising those same aspects—only His faculties were perfectly untainted by the Fall and its effects. When Christ acted, He was like man before the Fall, not like sinful humanity after it.\n\nEvery fallen person who is recreated in Christ and made a new creature becomes capable of properly exercising their faculties in holy and lawful ways. (See Lesson Four, Point II, 8 and 9.)\n\nHere's another way to think about it: unfallen humans acted exactly like God in exercising their faculties—just with limited attributes and powers. What is finite in us is infinite in God. When holy people are energized and endued with supernatural powers, they can exercise their natural attributes to a supernatural degree, depending on how much they yield to and are energized by the Spirit of God.\n\nFor example, Christ and the disciples, when endued with power from on High, were capable of God-action to destroy sin and sickness as effectively as if God Himself were doing the work without using them as instruments.\n\nRemember also that when people are born again, they become partakers of the divine nature. To the extent that nature controls and works through their created faculties, they live divine lives and do divine works. In such people, their faculties are freed from evil acts and evil powers and become channels of divine energy through the Holy Spirit.\n\nJust as Christ was perfectly helpless in Himself and acted, spoke, worked, and lived entirely through the Holy Spirit's anointing, believers who become like Christ become God-inspired, God-energized, and God-operated. (See Point V, 31 for Scriptures about a perfectly divine life in human form.) Christians fully living in God's fullness live a divine-human life in the Holy Spirit through God's very presence and power in their soul and spirit.\n\nIf we can grasp these truths, we can certainly understand how God could become so perfectly human while remaining so perfectly divine—a perfect union of God and man in one personality.\n\nWhether Christ's divine attributes and powers were limited, and to what extent, remains a significant question in Christian circles. Whether He laid them aside entirely for a time or retained them but voluntarily limited their use will always be debated. However, this much is settled: He was limited during His days in the flesh, as we'll see in Point VIII.\n\nWhether this limitation was constitutional or voluntary isn't really the main point. If it was done constitutionally, it was still voluntary, as stated in Jn. 10:18; Heb. 10:5-9. He wasn't forced to do anything—everything was a voluntary action on His part. Whether the limitation was built into His incarnation or whether He retained all divine powers and chose not to use them—the practical result was the same: He was limited as a man.\n\nIt was crucial that He limit Himself as a man to set the right example for us, so we can be inspired to live like God on Earth using the same means Christ used. Despite all the theological debates about whether He had two personalities, two natures in one personality, or some other arrangement—the fact remains that He was both human and divine. Even if some can't understand the "how" of it, the "fact" of it can be believed and must be believed if we want all the Scriptures to harmonize.\n\nOne certainty stands: His human nature had no separate existence before its union with the divine and is not in itself a separate personality from the divine person who became incarnated in human flesh.\n\nHis twofold nature was important not only for our sake, but also for God's sake, so He could be a true mediator between God and humanity. His dual nature gives Him fellowship with both parties and the ability to represent both in reconciliation.\n\nAs God, He upholds the dignity of deity. As man, He is truly sympathetic and able to meet human needs. Because He is God, His atonement has infinite value and effect. We'll discuss His dual natures further in Point VIII below.\n\n## V. Jesus Christ Is Not the Father or the Holy Ghost\n\nMany people are misled into making Jesus the only person in the Godhead—more than what the Bible says He is. In doing so, they rob the Father and the Holy Spirit of their own separate personalities and make them less than what Scripture reveals, depriving them of their rightful and separate places in the unity of God.\n\nIt's vitally important to know whether there are three persons in the Godhead so we can give each His rightful place, proper love, and worship—and so we can understand the Bible correctly without being led astray into false doctrines that come from twisting Scripture to fit human church creeds.\n\nThe following points prove that Jesus Christ is not the Father or the Holy Ghost:\n\n### 1. The Father Was in Heaven While Jesus Was on Earth\n\nThe Father remained in Heaven the entire time Jesus walked the Earth, so the Father could not have been incarnated in Jesus (Mt. 5:16, 45, 48; 6:1, 9; 7:21; 16:17; 18:10; 23:9).\n\n### 2. Jesus Would Confess Men Before His Father\n\nJesus said He would confess people "before my Father" and "before the angels"—impossible if He were the same person as the Father or the angels (Mt. 10:32-33; Lk. 12:8-9; Rev. 3:2-5). The word "before" means "in the presence of" or "face to face with," requiring both the Father and angels to be distinct persons from Jesus. This kind of language couldn't be used if only one person were involved, any more than it could in the 1,767 similar expressions throughout Scripture (Mt. 14:6; 17:2; 1 Tim. 5:19-20; 6:13; Rev. 4:5-6; 5:8; 7:9, 11, 15; 8:2; etc.).\n\n### 3. Jesus Always Prayed to the Father\n\nJesus consistently prayed to the Father and addressed Him as a separate person (Mt. 11:25; 26:39, 42-46; Lk. 10:21; 22:42; 23:34; Jn. 11:41; 12:28; 17:1-25). Nowhere do we read of the Father praying to anyone—but the Son constantly prays to someone outside Himself.\n\n### 4. The Father Protected Jesus from Outside His Body\n\nThe Father was outside Jesus' body, protecting Him, so He couldn't have been incarnated inside Jesus or be "all of God" inside Jesus as some teach (Mt. 2:12-23; 3:16-17; 17:5; Lk. 22:39-46; Jn. 12:27-30).\n\n### 5. Old Testament Prophets Distinguish Father and Son\n\nAll the Old Testament prophets quoted in the New Testament prove that the Father is separate from the Son. It was the Father who spoke "by the prophets" and "through the Spirit" concerning the Son (Heb. 1:1-3; Acts 3:21; Rom. 1:1-4; 1 Pet. 1:1-16; 2 Pet. 1:21).\n\nNote the speaker and the person spoken of in Mt. 2:15, 23; 4:6; 12:17-21; 22:41; 27:9-11; Lk. 4:16-21; 24:27, 44-46; Jn. 18:9; Acts 2:22-34; 3:13-24; 4:25-31; 7:2-50; 8:32-37; 10:34-43; 13:23-41; Heb. 2:3-9; 5:5-10. Human language is meaningless if two or three persons aren't understood in these statements.\n\n### 6. Both Jesus and Satan Referred to the Father as Separate\n\nBoth Jesus and Satan spoke of the Father as a separate person from the Son: "He [one person] shall give his angels charge concerning thee" refers to Jesus, the Son of the Father, another person (Mt. 4:6).\n\n### 7. Jesus Constantly Distinguished Himself from the Father\n\nJesus consistently referred to the Father as a separate person dwelling in Heaven, as far from Earth as Heaven is above Earth (Mt. 7:21; 10:32-33; 11:27; 15:13; 16:17, 27; 18:10-35; 20:23; Mk. 12:32; Jn. 5).\n\n### 8. God Is Called "The God and Father of Our Lord Jesus Christ"\n\nNew Testament writers called the Father "The God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ"—impossible if He were the Lord Jesus Christ (Eph. 1:3, 17; 3:14; 1 Pet. 1:3; Mt. 27:46; Jn. 20:17).\n\n### 9. Terms Like "Son of the Father" Require Two Persons\n\nPhrases like "the Son of the Father" (2 Jn. 3), "his Father" (Mt. 16:27; Rev. 1:6; 14:1), "my Father" (used 57 times, Mt. 7:21; Jn. 15:1; Rev. 2:27; 3:5; etc.), and "my God" (Jn. 20:17; Rev. 3:12) couldn't be true if Jesus were the Father and the only person called God.\n\nIf Jesus spoke of God the Father as being His Father and His God, then He either lied or told the truth. Such language demands we believe in another person who is the God and Father of Jesus Christ. Not once did Jesus or any Bible writer use unscriptural terms like "Jesus, the Father," "the Father Jesus," "spirit-Jesus," "Father Jesus," "one person in the Godhead," or "Jesus only."\n\n### 10. Jesus' Parables Show His Relationship to the Father\n\nIn His parables, Jesus illustrates His relationship to the Father as that of a Son—a separate person from the Father (Mt. 21:33-46; Lk. 20:9-18; Jn. 15:1-10).\n\nTo believe only one person is both Father and Son in these passages would make Jesus a plain liar. If He said He was "the vine" and the Father was "the husbandman" (Jn. 15), and if God the Father is compared to "a certain householder" while the Son is compared to "his son" and "heir," then this relationship is the truth—distinguishing two persons known as "the Father" and "the Son" (Mt. 21:33-46; Lk. 20:9-18).\n\n### 11. Jesus Taught Us to Pray to the Father, Not to Himself\n\nJesus instructed us to go directly to the Father in prayer and not to pray to Him at all: "Ye shall ask me nothing . . . ask the Father in my name, he will give it you" (Jn. 14:12-15; 15:16; 16:23-28).\n\nWhat could be clearer? If we're commanded to "ask me nothing" but to "ask the Father" instead, then Jesus is not the Father. It's unreasonable to teach that Jesus and His Father are the same person—with the body being the Son and the inner man being the Father.\n\nIt would be absurd to ask us to pray to one part of a person in the name of another part of the same person, or to call different parts by different names. Even more problematic, some people ignore the Father entirely and pray only to the Son. If Jesus' language doesn't refer to two persons, we'd have to conclude He didn't know how to use human language.\n\n### 12. Jesus Thanked the Father by Looking Up to Heaven\n\nOn certain occasions, Jesus thanked the Father "looking up to Heaven," where the Father dwelt—not looking inside Himself to a Father within (Jn. 11:41; Mt. 26:25-27; Mk. 8:6; 14:23). Was Jesus giving thanks to Himself and teaching us self-praise? Or was there a real Father outside of Him who dwelt in Heaven as a separate person?\n\n### 13. Many Statements About the Father Couldn't Be True of Jesus\n\nConsider these distinctions: The Father was in Heaven while Jesus was on Earth (Mt. 5:1, 48). The Father knew things Jesus didn't know (Mt. 10:29-31; Mk. 13:32; Acts 1:7; Rev. 1:1). The Father was "good," but Jesus didn't claim that quality for Himself (Mt. 19:17). The Father was on a throne, and Jesus was not (Mt. 23:22). Jesus is coming in the glory of the Father, not His own glory (Mt. 16:27).\n\nJesus prayed to the Father, never to Himself (Mt. 26:39-42; Jn. 17). He prophesied He would be exalted at the Father's right hand (Mt. 26:64), and the apostles later confirmed He was there (Acts 2:33-36; Eph. 1:20; Col. 3:1; Heb. 1:3; 8:1; 12:2; Rom. 8:34). Stephen actually saw Jesus with his own eyes at God's right hand (Acts 7:56-59). Jesus committed His spirit to God the Father at death, proving He died while the Father did not (Lk. 23:46). Others also saw Jesus as a separate person from the Father (Dan. 7:9-14; Rev. 5:1-7).\n\n### 14. Jesus Claimed to Be Sent by God\n\nJesus said He was sent by God, came from God, and was going back to God (Mt. 15:24; Jn. 3:16-18, 34; 5:30, 36-37; 6:29-40, 44, 57; 7:16, 28-29; 8:16-18, 29, 42; 9:4; 10:36; 11:42; 12:45, 49; 15:21; 16:5; 17:3, 8, 21-25; Gal. 4:4; 1 Jn. 4:9).\n\nThese Scriptures wouldn't make sense if only one person were referred to. The sense in which God sent Jesus is the same sense in which Jesus sent His disciples (Jn. 17:18; 20:21) and the same sense in which the Father and Son sent the Spirit (Jn. 14:16-17, 26; 15:26; 16:7-15).\n\nBeing sent doesn't make someone the same person as the sender—otherwise the disciples would have become Jesus when He sent them. If being sent proves two persons (sender and sent), then both Jesus and the Holy Spirit are separate persons from each other and from the Father, since both were sent by the Father.\n\n### 15. Jesus Was Flesh and Blood, Not a Spirit\n\nJesus plainly told Peter that His Father in Heaven was not "flesh and blood," and He told the Samaritan woman His Father and God was "Spirit" (Mt. 16:16-17; Jn. 4:24; 19:34).\n\nBecause Jesus was flesh and blood and didn't claim to be "spirit" even in His resurrected state, He couldn't be the Father (Lk. 24:39; Jn. 19:34; Rom. 8:3).\n\n### 16. The Father's Voice Came from Heaven\n\nPeter received a revelation from the Father in Heaven about Jesus' Sonship (Mt. 16:17) and actually heard the Father's voice from Heaven saying of the Son on Earth, "This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased, hear ye him" (Mt. 17:5).\n\nPeter later testified this voice came from Heaven—not from inside Jesus through some kind of ventriloquism. He said it came "from God the Father . . . from the excellent glory . . . from heaven" (2 Pet. 1:16-18). John the Baptist also heard this voice from Heaven while Jesus was on Earth (Mt. 3:16-17). They never said or believed Jesus was the Father in Heaven speaking.\n\n### 17. The Jews Understood Jesus Claimed to Be the Son, Not the Father\n\nThe Jews never understood Jesus to claim He was the Father—they understood He claimed to be the Son, thus making Himself equal with God (Mt. 26:64; 27:40-43; Jn. 5:17-35; 6:45; 8:13-38; 10:34-39; 19:7).\n\nIf He had claimed to be the Father, the only God, all of God, and the only person of the Godhead, they would have had a legitimate case against Him—because not one prophet ever foretold this doctrine. They did prophesy that God would have a Son as a separate person from Himself.\n\n### 18. Jesus Called the Father "My God" Even After the Resurrection\n\nEven after rising from the dead, Jesus called the Father "my God" (Jn. 10:17; Rev. 3:12; Ps. 22:1-10). He couldn't be His own Father and His own God. If He were the only person in the Godhead, this would be a false statement.\n\n### 19. The Angel Gabriel Distinguished Jesus from God\n\nThe angel Gabriel—"the angel of the Lord" (whom some sects claim was God Himself)—didn't know Jesus was the only person in the Godhead. He spoke of a God still in Heaven and called Jesus only "the Son of God" and "the Son of the Highest" (Mt. 1:18-25; Lk. 1:19, 27-38; 2:21).\n\n### 20. All the Angels Distinguished Jesus from God\n\nAll the angels in Heaven shared Gabriel's understanding, praising and giving glory to a "God in the highest" who was outside the baby Jesus in the manger (Lk. 2:8-16).\n\nIt's no sin for us to believe that these angels—fresh from Heaven, having come\n\n---\n\nJesus Christ is not one option among many for approaching God — He is the only way, the truth, and the life. That claim is either the most important fact in the universe or the most dangerous lie ever told. There is no comfortable middle ground. So here's the question this lesson leaves you with: Not whether you believe Jesus existed or was a remarkable person — but do you actually live as though He is what He claimed to be: the eternal Son of God, risen from the dead, sitting at the right hand of the Father, with all authority in heaven and on earth? What in your life would look different if you did?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk48i000yewhjn7siysw1	cmnknk48e000xewhj49ao5az0	Lesson 19 — The Dispensation of Grace	READING	5404	0	\N	t	Grace is the most used and least understood word in Christianity. Ask ten believers to define it and you'll get ten different answers — most of them partial, several of them subtly wrong in ways that quietly undermine how those believers live. The Dispensation of Grace is the age you're living in right now. Knowing precisely what grace means — and what it doesn't mean — is not a theological nicety. It's the difference between spiritual freedom and spiritual paralysis.\n\nLast time we saw that God has always had a called-out people, even before the New Testament church. Now we enter the age we're living in: what does it mean to live under grace, and how is this dispensation different from everything that came before it?\n\nWe call this period "the Dispensation of Grace" because God's grace shines through everything He does during this time. As John put it, "The law was given by Moses, but grace and truth came by Jesus Christ" (Jn. 1:16, 17).\n\nLet's take a closer look at what grace actually means and how it works in every age.\n\n![Lesson 19 — The Dispensation of Grace](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsZssClKdWQAzP5eOGYXjM0itdfBSUE9IN6u8o)\n\n### 1. The Definition of the Word "Grace"\n\nAt its heart, grace means the free, eternal, and completely unearned love and favor God shows toward the beings He created—whether human or angelic—who are capable of knowing Him and being morally responsible.\n\nThink of it like this: grace is the spring, the source, the very fountainhead from which all of God's blessings flow to everything He has made (Jn. 1:14-17; 3:16; Rom. 3:24; 5:17-21; 11:5, 6; 2 Cor. 9:8; Eph. 1:6, 7; 2:5-8; Jas. 4:6; 1 Pet. 5:5).\n\nThe Greek word *charis* appears 156 times in the New Testament. It's translated "grace" 130 times, "favor" six times, "thank" and "thankworthy" twelve times, "pleasure" twice, and "acceptable," "benefits," "gift," "gracious," "joy," and "liberality" once each.\n\nInterestingly, you won't find this word in Matthew or Mark. But it shows up eight times in Luke, four times in John, sixteen times in Acts, a whopping 110 times in Paul's letters, sixteen times in James through Jude, and twice in Revelation—once at the beginning and once at the end.\n\nGrace also describes the favor people show each other (Gen. 32:5; 33:8-15; 34:11; 39:4; 47:25, 29; Ruth 2:10; Esther 2:17).\n\nHere's something important to understand: we can't limit grace to just God's New Testament dealings with humanity. Wasn't God just as gracious and loving to angels and people in Old Testament times as He is to us today? Of course He was!\n\nHe couldn't be any other way toward His creation—except when they were in rebellion and sin. The very fact that creatures exist and continue to exist is itself an act of grace. Grace even extends to animals, providing everything that sustains life. The animals don't earn this any more than we do. Grace flows freely to all creatures, whether they realize it or not.\n\n### 2. The Benefits of Grace\n\nEvery good thing from God comes through His amazing grace. We deserve nothing, yet He gives us everything!\n\nGrace moves God to act for the best and eternal good of all creation. You see grace at work in His acts of judgment just as much as in His acts of mercy. It works for the benefit of the few and the many alike. All living creatures have an eternal guarantee of God's benefits and loving care through grace.\n\nThrough grace, we receive "every good and perfect gift" and "all things that pertain unto life and godliness" (Jn. 3:16; Rom. 3:24; 5:2, 17-21; 6:14; 8:32; 2 Cor. 8:9; 9:8; Eph. 1:6-7; 2:5-8; Jas. 1:17; 4:6; 1 Pet. 5:5; 2 Pet. 1:3-4).\n\nBut here's the key: we receive these benefits only when we humble ourselves and depend entirely on God by faith. We must recognize that we are nothing, that God is everything, and that His blessings come completely apart from our works (Rom. 3:24-31; 4:1-4, 16; 5:15-21; 6:14, 15; 11:6; Gal. 2:16; 3:1-12; Eph. 2:7-9).\n\n### 3. The Grace of God in All Ages\n\nNobody can dispute that God showed grace in every age. He has always been gracious, loving, and merciful to all people. Every act of mercy from God has flowed from His grace. Every favor He shows comes through grace.\n\nWhen John wrote that "grace and truth came by Jesus Christ" (Jn. 1:16-17), he couldn't have meant there was no grace before Jesus came. That would be like saying "the law was given by Moses" means there was no law at all before Moses—but we know there were many laws before him (see Lesson Thirteen).\n\nThere was grace before Christ came to earth, but it was the *fullness* of grace that arrived through Him (Jn. 1:14-17). God has shown different graces—different favors—to people at different times. The idea that grace never brought spiritual blessings before Christ comes from unbelief and predetermined ideas designed to prop up false doctrines.\n\nAll through the Old Testament, people received God's grace and favor in many ways. Some lost that favor and never got it back. Others maintained it, more or less, throughout their lives (Gen. 6:8; 19:19; Exod. 33:12-17; 34:9; Ps. 84:11; Prov. 3:34; Lk. 2:40).\n\nIn Lesson Eighteen, Point IX, we saw that Old Testament saints enjoyed nearly every blessing that we receive through grace today. The only real difference between New Covenant blessings and those before or under the Old Covenant is this: we can have in fullness what they had in part.\n\nThey had the Holy Spirit in a measure; we can have Him in all fullness (Jn. 3:34; 7:37-39). They had grace in a measure; we can have it fully through Jesus Christ (Jn. 1:17). They had God's glory in part; we can experience it in a greater way (2 Cor. 3:6-15).\n\nWe've seen that each dispensation began in God's favor and ended in His judgment because people lost that favor. Every unsaved soul in all of history proves there was a fall from original grace. Millions have never regained their standing in grace.\n\nGrace provided the means of reconciliation even in Old Testament times, but not everyone accepted God's grace then—just like not everyone accepts it today when it comes to spiritual things. When it comes to material things, though, everyone in every age has received God's blessings through grace. All these blessings of life are God's unearned favors, designed to lead us to repentance (Mt. 5:44-48; Rom. 2:4-6; Acts 17:28; Jas. 1:17).\n\nPeople in Old Testament times became partakers of spiritual blessings—even the Holy Spirit Himself—by grace through faith. You can find many examples in Rom. 4:1-25; Gal. 2:15-19; 3:1-18; Heb. 11. These passages prove that people were justified by faith through grace, completely apart from works or law. If not through works, how else could they have received except by grace?\n\nThat Old Testament saints received the Holy Spirit by faith through grace is clear from Gen. 41:38; Exod. 28:3; 31:3; 35:31; Num. 11:17-29; 14:24; 24:2; 27:18; Deut. 34:9; Judg. 3:10; 6:34; 11:29; 14:6, 19; 15:14; 1 Sam. 10:6-10; 11:6; 16:13-23; 18:10; 19:20-23; 2 Sam. 23:2; 2 Ki. 2:15; 2 Chron. 15:1; 20:14; 24:20; Ps. 51:10-11; Isa. 11:2; 26:9; 42:1; 61:1-3; 63:10-14; Ezek. 2:2; 3:24; 11:5, 19; 36:27; 37:14; Dan. 4:8-9, 18; 5:11-14; 6:3; Mt. 10:1-21; Lk. 1:15, 41-46, 67, 70; 2:25-35; Acts 1:16; 3:18-21; 2 Tim. 3:15-17; Heb. 1:1-2; 2 Pet. 1:20-21.\n\nThese were permanent anointings and abidings of the Spirit—not just occasional visits, as some teach. John the Baptist was "filled" with the Holy Spirit from birth, and this was before the Spirit came in fullness at Pentecost!\n\nDid all these people receive the Holy Spirit by works? Of course not—they received by grace. And if they received by grace, then grace was clearly evident in Old Testament times.\n\nNow, these people didn't receive the baptism in the Spirit that all believers can have since Pentecost (Mt. 3:11; Jn. 1:31-34; 7:38-39; Acts 1:4-5; 2:1-21, 33, 38-39; 8:5-25; 9:1-7; 10:44-48; 11:15-16; 19:1-7), but they certainly had the Spirit in a measure.\n\nScripture makes clear there's a measure of the Spirit that all believers receive (Num. 11:16-17; 2 Ki. 2:9; Lk. 1:17; Jn. 3:34; 1 Cor. 12:13; 2 Cor. 1:22; 3:18; 5:5; Phil. 1:19; 1 Jn. 4:13). These passages speak of the Spirit "by measure" and "without measure."\n\nThis is the difference between receiving the Spirit at conversion—as all believers do (Jn. 3:3-8; Rom. 5:1-11; 8:1-17; 1 Cor. 3:16-17; 6:11-21; 12:3-13; 2 Cor. 3:18; Eph. 2:18; 4:4; Titus 3:5; 1 Jn. 4:13)—and the baptism in the Spirit that all believers may receive simply by asking their Heavenly Father (Lk. 11:13; 24:49; Acts 1:8-15; 8:1-21).\n\n### 4. The Fullness of Grace and Power Promised Modern Believers\n\nWe've seen that Old Testament saints experienced different measures of the Spirit and God's power. Christ was the first to receive the Spirit "without measure" (Jn. 3:34).\n\nJohn said, "of his fullness have we all received, and grace for grace," showing us that grace comes in measures (Jn. 1:16-17). The Weymouth translation reads "grace upon grace," and Moffatt's translation says "grace after grace"—plainly showing that we don't necessarily receive all of grace at once.\n\nWhy not? Often it's due to lack of knowledge and faith. As your knowledge grows, your faith grows. And as your faith grows, you receive more grace—more of God's favor—at every stage of your growth.\n\nThere's no limit to God's grace or what we can receive through it. But the benefits of grace don't come to everyone alike or all at once, despite what some modern teachers claim. If everyone received the same fullness of grace, everyone would have the same benefits from God. The fact that some receive more than others proves that these blessings come by faith, according to each person's faith. Faith governs what you receive through grace, and faith is based on knowledge.\n\nToday, we're promised "the fullness of God" and the same power to do the same works Jesus did (Jn. 14:12-15; Jn. 7:37-39; Acts 1:8; Eph. 3:16-20). Since believers have varying degrees of power and faith, this proves there are also varying degrees of grace received from God. (See Lesson Thirty for more on this.)\n\n### 5. False Theories About Grace\n\nThere's a school of Bible interpreters that magnifies grace above everything else in God's program. They ignore God's justice, His laws, and all the conditions that govern His dealings with us. They essentially cancel out thousands of Scriptures that reveal how God relates to free moral agents.\n\nThey say some good things about grace, but they go to an extreme, trying to make grace the sum total of everything about God and His plan.\n\nYes, it's true that from God's standpoint grace cannot be withheld because of our unworthiness, cannot be lessened by our failures, and cannot be mixed with earning salvation through works. But this doesn't erase the fact that there are conditions we must meet if we want certain benefits of grace.\n\nNot one statement in the entire Bible says God's grace is unconditional, or that we can receive grace while living however we please in disregard of God's laws and justice.\n\nWhen we say grace isn't withheld because of our unworthiness, we simply mean God's grace will cause Him to forgive all sin *when certain conditions are met*. When we say grace isn't lessened by our failures, we mean sin doesn't diminish God's grace toward us *when we meet the conditions laid out in His Word*. When we say grace can't be mixed with works, we mean no human effort can earn the blessings that come only by faith through grace—by meeting the plain conditions God has established for receiving these blessings.\n\nHere's how it works: When you realize you're a sinner, that God's grace is greater than your sins, that you have no merit of your own to earn God's favor, and that you come to God meeting the requirements for reconciliation—at that moment you become a recipient of God's grace.\n\nThink of it like a free gift that requires you to open your hands to receive it. If God withheld His grace from a penitent sinner simply because that person was a sinner, nobody could be saved. If sin lessened God's grace toward a penitent sinner, nobody could be blessed—sin would be greater than grace and would prevent grace from being shown. If blessings were earned by works, they wouldn't come by grace at all.\n\nIt's also true that God isn't obligated to save sinners because of anything we've done. But God, of His own free will and because of grace, obligated Himself to pay humanity's debt of sin.\n\nSince God has chosen to take on this task, He is now obligated to give every person the same freedom to accept the cancellation of their debt. God is now obligated to save everyone who accepts Christ's work for them. He cannot refuse to show His grace to any sinner who accepts Christ's work on their behalf.\n\nBut God is not obligated to bless anyone who refuses and rejects His offer and Christ's work on the cross. The choice is now up to each of us—not up to God. God has already made His choice. His work in paying humanity's debt is finished. He is obligated to give full benefits to all who accept.\n\nOf course we're saved by grace! But not without our free and voluntary choice to accept Christ's work, proper confession of sins to God, and faith in Christ's blood. All the grace in existence cannot save a single soul that refuses its benefits.\n\nSo in the final analysis, you govern your own salvation by your power of free choice. Yes, salvation is God's work for us—but God cannot save us without our free consent and cooperation, from the new birth to the grave. The idea that salvation depends *only* on God's grace and is *only* God's work is false.\n\nIf everything depended solely on God to save all sinners without any action on their part, then everyone would be saved—because that's God's desire (1 Tim. 2:4; 2 Pet. 3:9). If it were left entirely to God, He would be morally obligated to save everyone regardless of their consent, or He would be a respecter of persons and a breaker of His own law (Jas. 2:1, 9).\n\nYes, grace is pure kindness and unmerited favor from God. But since God voluntarily chose to pay our debt and save us from Hell on the condition that we accept this work, He is now fulfilling an obligation—one of His own free choice, not one we earned through merit. Our works didn't earn God's obligation to save us. This obligation is voluntary on God's part, for helpless humanity. This is what makes God's grace truly unmerited favor and love.\n\nThe school of thought we're discussing fails to recognize this human element in how grace works. They magnify grace as the only thing capable of saving people and insist that we can do nothing to receive its benefits.\n\nThey ignore our free moral agency. They ignore our responsibility for our own damnation if we're lost. They brush aside repentance, confession of sins, prayer, turning from sin—everything God requires of us to be saved.\n\nThey ignore the human element after salvation and dismiss all the conditions for continued salvation that Scripture plainly states we must meet to be saved in the end. They lay all blame and responsibility on God if anyone fails to do what God requires. They make God personally accountable for every lost soul.\n\nThis school holds some of the most foolish and unbiblical theories about God, grace, and salvation of any group claiming faith in Christ's gospel.\n\nWhen they argue that grace is pure kindness without obligation; that it's God's kindness regardless of how much or little we sin; that it's unrelated to human merit; that it's not treating a person as they deserve or better than they deserve; that it treats people graciously without any reference to what they deserve; that it never decreases or increases from God's standpoint; and that it offers the same unvarying blessing to everyone alike—their arguments are partly true.\n\nBut they've overlooked one crucial thing: the manifestation of grace in your life is governed by your free moral agency. God is limited in what He can and will do for you by your own will, faith, and obedience.\n\nTo teach that God doesn't forgive sinners out of big-heartedness, or that He doesn't show mercy but saves solely because of grace, demonstrates ignorance of the gospel and of what grace actually is.\n\nYes, Christ has already "taken away" the world's sin on the cross. Yes, He is our substitute who has already borne God's righteous judgment against sin. But this doesn't prove that God is without mercy, that He isn't big-hearted, or that grace is something separate from Christ's redemptive work.\n\nThe truth is: God shows grace to people who don't deserve it and can't earn it. And it's also true that God is love. He is big-hearted. He is merciful, kind, and compassionate toward those who have strayed from His family.\n\nWhen people get so technical that they separate grace from God's mercy, love, and kindness, they show the worst kind of ignorance. Their high-sounding phrases about grace as something distinct from all redemptive processes may impress people who love finding hidden mysteries in Scripture, but to simple believers, such talk is foolish.\n\nThese teachers may feel wonderful manufacturing technicalities and trying to magnify grace. But in the light of plain Scripture and common sense, their theories are foolish and false.\n\nLet's briefly study how "grace," "kindness," "love," and similar terms are actually used of God, people, and redemption, and see whether these theories hold up.\n\nA study of these words shows that "grace" is never used in either Testament as something separate from big-heartedness, love, compassion, and kindness between individuals—whether between people or between God and people. It's used repeatedly for the favor one person shows another, and this favor is governed by the recipient's disposition, life, service, faith, relationship, and attitude.\n\nNoah found grace in God's sight because he was righteous—God favored him because of this (Gen. 6:8; 7:1). God had mercy on him and his family. If Noah hadn't been righteous, God wouldn't have had mercy on him. He would have destroyed Noah with the rest of the ungodly. This is indisputable if we believe the record.\n\nWhen Lot found "grace" or "favor" in God's sight, it was because of God's mercy and because of Abraham, God's friend (Gen. 19:19, 29). When Moses and Israel found grace with God, it was because of God's mercy and choice (Exod. 33:12-17; 34:9). They were His chosen people because of Abraham, whom God saw would obey Him and command his children to serve the Lord (Gen. 18:17-19; 22:12).\n\nThose same people who found grace in God's sight were destroyed because of sin—because grace does not tolerate sin in those once blessed with grace (Exod. 32:30-35; Num. 14:22-35; Jude 5).\n\nWhen people in the early Church found grace from God, it was because they humbled themselves and freely accepted God's salvation. When they failed God, they were cursed just like the Israelites and the angels and people of past ages (Acts 1:25; 5:1-10; Rom. 11; 1 Tim. 1:19-20; 5:11-15; Heb. 6:4-9; 10:26-29; 2 Pet. 2:20-22; Rev. 2:5).\n\nIt's foolish to talk about being forgiven or saved without this being an act of grace and mercy. Everything God does for us is an act of grace and mercy!\n\nThe fact that God has already paid our debt doesn't mean that actual forgiveness of sins today isn't an immediate act of His grace. No sinner is personally saved until they accept Christ's work. Since we must do this in our own lives today, God's grace is manifested only when we accept Christ as personal Savior.\n\nThis doesn't mean God becomes "good enough" to excuse sins apart from Christ's work. It means God becomes personally gracious to each sinner the moment they accept Christ's work. Christ's work was done centuries ago, but it doesn't benefit you until you choose to accept it.\n\nGod blesses the sinner by His grace when that sinner surrenders. This can't happen until the sinner turns to God and permits grace to be manifested to them. No sinner will ever receive God's grace until they personally humble themselves and call on God for mercy. God is free to forgive the moment someone confesses because Christ has already paid the debt. It's only when we know the truth and accept it that we're set free (Job 33:23-24; Jn. 8:31-32).\n\nThe gospel of redemption is called "the word of his grace" (Acts 14:3; 20:24, 32). We're justified by grace (Rom. 3:24; Titus 3:7). All blessings come by grace (Jn. 1:16; Eph. 1:7; 2:7). Grace brings salvation (Titus 2:11-13). It's the source of answered prayer (Heb. 4:16). It can transform our lives (1 Cor. 15:10). It enables us to make great sacrifices (2 Cor. 9:8).\n\nGrace comes through faith (Eph. 2:8-9), the Holy Spirit (Zech. 12:10; Heb. 10:29), God's choice (Rom. 11:5-6; Gal. 1:15; Exod. 33:19), Jesus Christ (1 Cor. 1:4), and humility and choice on our part (Prov. 3:34; Jas. 4:6).\n\nGrace comes in various measures (Eph. 4:7; Rom. 12:3-6; 15:15) and is governed by individual faith (Rom. 4:16; 5:1-2), humility (Jas. 4:6), and the willpower and life of each believer (Heb. 12:15, 28; 13:9; Jas. 4:6; 1 Pet. 5:5).\n\nHere's further proof that grace in your life is governed by your own will and choices: You can receive God's grace in vain (2 Cor. 6:1). You can frustrate grace (Gal. 2:21). You can fall from it (Gal. 5:4). You can cause it to fail in your life (Heb. 12:15). You can turn it into license for sin (Jude 4). You can sin despite grace (Rom. 6:1). And you can continue or discontinue in it as you please (Acts 13:43).\n\nChristians can minister grace to others (Eph. 4:29; Phil. 1:7; 1 Pet. 4:10) and grow in it (2 Pet. 3:18). Grace is an attribute of God used alongside "mercy" and "compassion" when dealing with sinners (Exod. 34:6; 2 Chron. 30:9; Neh. 9:17, 31; Ps. 86:15; 103:8; 111:4; 112:4).\n\nThese facts might seem like blasphemy to some, but facts are facts. When they're stated in plain Scripture that anyone can read, it's foolish to reject them. Holding to some man-made theory that a church makes the sum total of its religion isn't worth the price.\n\nIt's better to be honest with all the Scriptures on a subject and believe them all, rather than twisting a few to say something they don't and flatly rejecting many others that speak to the same subject.\n\nSome may think we're making human will greater than God's grace. But this is true not only of grace but of many other attributes of God that cannot bless rebels who choose not to accept these blessings.\n\nIt's not so much that human will is greater than any attribute of God. It's that God cannot and has promised not to do certain things for us until we accept His grace and freely submit to His work in us. God simply cannot and will not break His own laws or act as a despot toward anyone. He will not force any free moral agent to conform to His will.\n\nTherefore, it's up to us to choose whether we want God's grace, love, and favor—and to what extent. If it were entirely up to God's will, all free moral agents would conform to Him, all would be blessed alike, and all would enjoy His grace and favor fully.\n\nAs it stands, no one can accuse God of not loving all people if they want to receive that love. The fact that not everyone is saved—and that even among the saved, not everyone partakes of God's love equally—proves that God's blessings through grace don't depend wholly on Him. Neither do they depend wholly on our free will.\n\nIt takes both God's will and our will in full cooperation to demonstrate the fullness of His love and grace. You can't work for the good of one master while serving another. So God's grace and love are naturally limited by our free moral agency.\n\nTo argue that forgiveness isn't an act of grace contradicts the many Scriptures cited above that say we're justified and saved by grace. To separate grace from God's mercy in forgiveness is also unbiblical—God cannot be gracious to anyone without showing mercy. Mercy and forgiveness go hand in hand (Deut. 5:9-10; Neh. 9:17; Ps. 26:11; Lk. 18:13-14).\n\nThroughout Scripture, people asked for mercy when seeking forgiveness and blessing (Ps. 51:1-13; Prov. 16:6; etc.). God is by nature gracious, merciful, loving, kind, good, and compassionate (Exod. 34:6; Neh. 9:17; Ps. 86:15; 111:4; 145:8).\n\nTo separate His graciousness from His other characteristics and magnify it above His justice and everything else about God just to uphold a church theory about grace—that's more than thinking people can do.\n\n### 6. Thirty Things Grace Cannot Do\n\n> (1) Grace cannot set aside all condemnation for future sins forever. It can only set aside condemnation as long as you stay free from sin. The modern idea that judicial forgiveness covers ALL sins—past, present, and future—that God never holds believers' sins against them, and that God charges all sins of believers to Christ rather than condemning them, is one of the most unbiblical and deceptive theories in any church.\n>\n> Those who hold this theory argue that no earthly court could punish someone twice for the same crime, and that since Christ bore all sins, He can't bear them twice. This argument is true as far as it goes—but it's only half the truth. Christ bore the sins of all, but nobody is saved until they repent and ask for mercy.\n>\n> Yes, a person can't be punished twice for the same crime. But if that same person commits the same crime again after being cleared of the first, they must be punished again. No court gives anyone a blanket pardon to keep committing the same crimes without punishment each time. Being pardoned for one crime doesn't excuse all future similar crimes.\n>\n> Think of it like a governor pardoning a murderer and restoring full citizenship. That murderer can't go out and keep murdering just because of the pardon. It would be ridiculous to argue that the pardon for one murder gives freedom to murder at will without punishment. No government could function if it dealt with criminals this way. No court could justly free someone to commit unlimited crimes just because they were punished or pardoned once. Is God the only Governor lacking the intelligence to not let His subjects be as lawless as they please?\n>\n> The same principle applies to salvation. God couldn't justly give anyone a blank check to commit any sin while being immune from punishment. That would be like giving a murderer a pardon that includes permission to commit unlimited murders—making the governor responsible for all future murders!\n>\n> Naturally, all sins have been paid for, and Christ bore all sins in His body on the tree. He will forgive all sins that are confessed. But this doesn't give the saved person a blank check to continue in sin without accountability. Salvation doesn't include freedom to live in sin. It doesn't guarantee immunity from Hell for those who return to sin and die in it.\n>\n> There's no Scripture teaching that God forgives past, present, and future sins all at once. Scripture tells us that all sins committed up to the time of repentance are forgiven and blotted out—so at the moment of forgiveness, God holds nothing against the person. God promised to keep us free from sin from that point forward if we meet certain conditions. God never promised more than this. No one ever received more than this. God only requires confession of sins already committed. Only sins that have been committed can be forgiven.\n>\n> This is clear from all Scripture (Exod. 32:30-34; Lev. 4:2-35; 5:1-17; 6:2-30; Num. 5:1-7; Ps. 32:1-5; 38:1-18; 51:1-13; Mt. 3:6; 18:21-35; Acts 2:38; 3:19; 5:31; 26:18; 2 Pet. 1:5; 1 Jn. 1:9; Rev. 1:5). Teaching that future sins are forgiven before they're committed is simply ridiculous.\n>\n> The fact that sins committed after the cross occurred after Christ died, or that Christ died for all future people's future sins, doesn't prove that all your future sins are forgiven before you commit them. Christ died for all people—but if everyone is forgiven simply because Christ died for them, and if people don't need to confess sins to be forgiven, then everyone is saved and there's no need for anyone to ever confess sins.\n>\n> The sins of Old Testament saints weren't committed after Christ died, yet Christ atoned for all those past sins (Rom. 3:24-25; Heb. 9:15). They all had to confess sins when and after they were committed, just as we must today, according to the passages cited above. We must conclude that no sin can be forgiven before it's committed. If this is true, there's no such thing as all future sins being blotted out by one act of faith in Christ, giving immunity from all future punishment.\n>\n> Statements like "taketh away the sin of the world" and "take away our sins" refer to Christ bearing the sins of all people in all ages—past, present, and future. But redemption benefits only those who personally confess their sins and accept Christ's work for them. If Christ saves everyone simply because He died for them, regardless of meeting any conditions, then everyone is saved from sin and will go to Heaven—and there's no need for further gospel preaching.\n>\n> God's will is that after forgiveness and becoming a new creature in Christ, we quit the sin business. Jesus told several people, "Sin no more, lest a worse thing come unto thee" (Jn. 5:14; 8:11). Jesus taught that "Whosoever committeth sin is the servant of sin" (Jn. 8:34). He taught that the Holy Spirit's work throughout this age would be to "reprove the world of sin" (Jn. 16:7-11).\n>\n> Paul stated that we shouldn't continue in sin just because God keeps forgiving (Rom. 6:1); that we "should not henceforth serve sin" after being born again (Rom. 6:1-6); that saved people are "freed from sin" (Rom. 6:7-23; 8:14); that we should walk in Christ as we received\n\n---\n\nThe Dispensation of Grace is not a relaxed version of God's standards — it's a completely new covenant with completely new power to meet those standards through the indwelling Spirit. You're not under Law. You're under grace. So here's the question: Are you actually living in that grace — experiencing the freedom and power it provides — or have you been living a performance-based Christianity that functionally treats the cross as an entry point but not a continuing reality?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk48i0012ewhjzjdr5n3u	cmnknk48e000xewhj49ao5az0	Lesson 22 — A Christian's Power of Attorney	READING	2365	4	\N	t	When someone gives you power of attorney, they're not lending you their influence or hoping you'll use it well. They're signing over legal authority to act on their behalf — to speak with their voice, enforce their rights, and conduct business in their name as if they were personally present. Jesus gave the Church something that functions exactly this way. Most Christians have never used it.\n\nLast time we examined who Jesus Christ actually is — God in the flesh, risen, seated at the right hand of the Father. Now we ask: what does that position of authority mean for you, specifically, in your daily life? What did He give you when He ascended?\n\n![Lesson 22 — A Christian's Power of Attorney](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsUEFDKI6edALvbYMWXfx7ZoQswlOpr26nuJ1t)\n\n## I. Definition of Agency and Power of Attorney\n\nThe Bible teaches a clear doctrine of divine agency. In the business world, agency describes a relationship where one person (the agent) represents another (the principal) in legitimate business transactions.\n\nThere are two types of agents. A General Agent has authority to represent the principal in all business matters or in a particular type of business. A Special Agent is authorized to handle specific tasks—like selling a farm, buying a house, or completing a particular transaction.\n\nAnyone capable of free and intelligent action can serve as either a principal or an agent. However, someone who lacks sound mind and understanding—such as a person who is mentally incapacitated—cannot act in either role.\n\nAn agent's authority can be established in three ways: by a sealed deed, by a written document, or simply by spoken word. When authority is given through a formal written document, it's called a power of attorney.\n\nThe principal is responsible for the agent's actions when the agent is conducting authorized business. If an agent hides who their principal is, the agent becomes personally responsible to third parties. An agent cannot appoint a substitute or pass their authority to someone else without the principal's consent.\n\nAuthority can be terminated in several ways:\n- When the principal revokes it\n- When the agent renounces the power\n- When either party dies\n- When the authorized time period expires\n- When the principal's business goes bankrupt or is sold\n- When either party becomes mentally incapacitated\n\nTermination takes effect the moment both the agent and any third parties receive notice. An agent cannot receive personal profit from business transactions beyond their agreed salary or commission.\n\nIf you're dealing with an agent, you should investigate their authority. Always ask for evidence of an agent's authority before doing business with them.\n\nWhen you need to delegate authority for signing notes, checks, or other legal documents, it's wise to create a written document and have it recorded in court. This document—the power of attorney—should be signed by the principal, witnessed, and acknowledged before an officer, just like a deed. It must clearly and explicitly state exactly what the agent has power to do.\n\nHere's a simple example of what a power of attorney looks like:\n\n> "KNOW ALL MEN BY THESE PRESENTS, That, I, John Doe, of Atlanta, County of Fulton, and State of Georgia, have made, constituted and appointed, and BY THESE PRESENTS do make, constitute and appoint, John Smith, true and lawful attorney for me and in my name, place and stead, (here state the purpose for which the power is given), giving and granting unto my said attorney full power of authority to do and perform all and every act and thing whatsoever, requisite and necessary to be done in and about the premises, as fully, to all intents and purposes, as I might or could do if personally present, with all power of substitution and revocation, hereby ratifying and confirming all that my said attorney or substitute shall lawfully do or cause to be done by virtue thereof.\n>\n> IN WITNESS WHEREOF, I have hereunto set my hand and seal the second day of January, one thousand nine hundred and forty-nine.\n>\n> Signed, Sealed and Delivered in the Presence of\n>\n> John Doe (Seal)\n\n## II. Biblical Proof for the Christian's Power of Attorney\n\nHere's the amazing truth: every believer has the legal and redemptive right to use the name of Jesus when asking and receiving from God. This puts prayer on a purely legal basis.\n\nJesus Himself said, "Where two or three are gathered together in my name, there am I in the midst of them" (Mt. 18:19-20). He also declared, "These signs shall follow them that believe; In my name shall they cast out devils; they shall speak with new tongues; They shall take up serpents (see Lesson Fourteen, V, II); and if they drink any deadly thing it shall not hurt them; they shall lay hands on the sick, and they shall recover" (Mk. 16:15-20).\n\nListen to these powerful promises: "Verily, verily, I say unto you, He that believeth on me, the works that I do shall he do also; and greater works than these shall he do; because I go unto my Father. And whatsoever ye shall ask in my name, that will I do, that the Father may be glorified in the Son. If ye shall ask any thing in my name, I will do it" (Jn. 14:12-15).\n\nAnd there's more: "Ye have not chosen me, but I have chosen you, and ordained you, that ye should go and bring forth fruit, and that your fruit should remain: that whatsoever ye shall ask of the Father in my name, he may give it you" (Jn. 15:16). Jesus also said, "Whatsoever ye shall ask the Father in my name, he will give it you. Hitherto have ye asked nothing in my name: ask and ye shall receive, that your joy may be full . . . At that day ye shall ask in my name" (Jn. 16:23-26).\n\nThese are Jesus's direct words—and they express your power of attorney as a Christian. He has given you unqualified use of His name to carry on God's work in this world. Everything God has invested in His name belongs to you.\n\nAs Paul put it, "All things are yours . . . And ye are Christ's; and Christ's is God's" (1 Cor. 3:21-23). Jesus assures us that all prayer prayed in His name receives special attention from both Himself and His Father. Think of it like this: it's as if Jesus is saying, "You ask the Father in My name; I'll endorse it, and the Father will give it."\n\nIf modern Christians could wake up and realize their New Testament rights—if we would exercise our full authority—there would be no failure, no sin, no bad habits, no unhappiness, no defeat, no sickness, and no want among us. As we've seen in Lesson Ten, God has promised not just our needs but all of our wants.\n\nWhen you pray in Jesus's name, you pray by His authority. It's as if Jesus Himself were praying. And God always hears Him—the Gospel records prove it. At Lazarus's grave, Jesus said, "Father, I thank thee that thou hast heard me. And I knew that thou hearest me always" (Jn. 11:41-42).\n\nIf we understood the power of praying by Christ's authority, we'd have so much more confidence in answered prayer. We'd realize that God always hears Jesus, and when we pray by His authority, our prayers will always be answered—just as Scripture promises.\n\nWhen you pray in Jesus's name, you're not just praying by His authority—your prayers pass into His hands, and He takes responsibility for the answer. He says, "Whatsoever ye shall ask in my name, that will I do" and "I will do it" (Jn. 14:12-15). Of the Father, He said, "Whatsoever ye shall ask in my name, he will give it you" (Jn. 16:23-26). Paul added, "He that spared not his own Son, but delivered him up for us all, how shall he not with him also freely give us all things" (Rom. 8:32).\n\nChrist isn't just our substitute in atonement—He also stands before God on our behalf, ever living to make intercession according to God's will (Rom. 8:34; Heb. 7:25). His work wasn't completed at the cross. He remains our High Priest before God (Heb. 4:14-16). He's our Advocate—our Defense Attorney—when Satan accuses us before God (1 Jn. 2:1-2; Rev. 12:10).\n\nWhere's the room for failure? We're commanded to come boldly to the throne of grace to obtain mercy and help in our time of need, leaving our case in His hands (Heb. 4:14-16; 10:19-23). There can be no failure when true, unwavering faith is exercised (Jas. 1:5-8; Heb. 11:6). Prayer is a business proposition and should be the chief business of our lives—because the chief business of our lives needs much prayer to succeed.\n\nThese Scriptures about praying in His name were among Jesus's last words before ascending to the Father. They're part of the Church's commission to evangelize the world and reconcile it to God. In these passages, we find the Christian's power of attorney over satanic forces that hinder the gospel. "In my name they shall cast out devils."\n\nThis was true during the disciples' preaching tours before the cross (Mt. 10:1-20; Lk. 9:1-9; 10:1-20; Mk. 6:7-13). It was true after the cross (Acts 2:43; 3:6-7; 5:12-16; 6:8; 8:1-21; 16:16-34; 19:11-20).\n\nThere is power in the name of Jesus. The demons said, "Jesus I know, and Paul I know"—meaning, we recognize Jesus and Paul as our masters whom we obey, but we know nothing about you seven men whom we've never encountered in battle (Acts 19:11-20).\n\nJesus declared, "All authority is given unto me in heaven and in earth" (Mt. 28:18). By His authority, believers were commissioned to go into all the world, preach the gospel to every creature, and watch God defeat demon powers in Jesus's name. This was to continue "even unto the end of the world" (Mt. 28:18-20). In other words, when we preach truth, it sets people free from demon powers—wherever and as long as truth is preached.\n\nJesus sent the disciples to make disciples in all nations, promising to back them up with confirming signs (Mk. 16:15-20; Heb. 2:3-4). Everyone who preaches has a perfect right to cast out devils and see all the gospel's signs following them. If this isn't happening, they're poor representatives of God, failing to fulfill their commission.\n\nThe name of Jesus means everything to the Father. He will always honor this name because it represents completed redemption and the world's salvation from Satan's eternal rule. It means the Father's eternal plan for a perfect, sinless society on Earth and throughout the universe is guaranteed. This means more to the Father than our finite minds can grasp—because we don't yet fully understand the scope of God's plan.\n\nThe Father recognizes everything Jesus's name implies. He knows we have a legal and family right to use this name in prayer. He knows its proper use delivers all people from sin, sickness, and failure. It's God's glory to recognize Jesus's name in prayer and answer according to our faith.\n\nTo Satan and demons, the name of Jesus means utter defeat, humiliation, and banishment to the lowest Hell. They're already defeated and must release anyone they control when Jesus's name is used against them. They obey that name. They fear and tremble when it's used in faith. They flee when resisted and rebuked by this name.\n\nTo the Church, the name of Jesus means that anything needed or wanted—now and forever—will be granted when used in unwavering faith. All of God's infinite resources belong to the Church. Nothing is impossible to believers who fully and freely use Jesus's name.\n\nGod's plan for the Church is to take and use the full power of attorney, conducting business for God and Christ by the Holy Spirit's power—just as Christ carried on God's work by the Spirit when He was on Earth. We saw this in Lesson Twenty.\n\nIf there's any failure to carry on this work and do even greater works than Christ did, it's not God's failure. It's not lack of power in Jesus's name. It's not that redemption is incomplete or our case has been lost in Heaven's High Court. It's not that the devil is stronger than God. It's not that the Holy Spirit has failed to empower people.\n\nIt's simply that the Church as a whole and individuals in particular have failed to exercise their God-given rights and privileges as taught in the gospel—and to become endued with power from on High.\n\nThe Church today largely doesn't understand the body of Christ's true purpose in the world. Most people don't understand the real issues between God and Satan. Satan takes advantage of this ignorance and unbelief, holding back the Church's usefulness on Earth. He's defeating Christians by the millions.\n\nHe convinces them it's not God's will for them to get what they want in life. He makes them afraid to use Jesus's name in prayer and daily conflict against him and his demons. As long as the Church lives in this ignorance, it will be defeated in doing God's works—works that should be manifest in every local church today as they were in early churches.\n\nMany thousands of religious people deny the essentials of gospel faith—more or less—things like the virgin birth, the incarnation, Jesus's deity and sinlessness, His redemptive work, His death, burial, resurrection, and ascension, the Holy Spirit's descent, the power of Jesus's name, the Christian's power of attorney, and many truths that would make the Church victorious. As long as this continues, New Testament Christianity will be rarely seen.\n\nIt's crucial that people gain true knowledge of Jesus Christ. That's why we've devoted most of these two lessons to the truth about Jesus. If He is divine, He is our Lord. If He is our Lord, He has the right to intervene in human affairs.\n\nHis gospel should be obeyed. His name should be honored and exercised with all authority over demons, sin, sickness, and life's troubles. Everything built around Him should be cherished and upheld. Men should follow His pure example of godliness and consecrate themselves to righteousness and true holiness. Women should pledge their lives to Him, preserving their purity for themselves and coming generations. Children should be raised to love, respect, and follow Him throughout life to the glorious future with Him.\n\nAll should obey His teachings and strive to reproduce New Testament Christianity in life and power—so multitudes can be saved, healed, and blessed with prosperity, health, and victory over all enemies of God and humanity.\n\n## III. Christ's Power of Attorney\n\nChrist obtained all authority in Heaven and Earth through several means—authority He gives to us to carry on His work throughout this age. If we're to be given power of attorney, we should understand that Christ has the right to give it and the extent to which we can exercise it.\n\nHere's the sevenfold way Christ obtained His great name and power of attorney:\n\n### 1. His Deity\n\nBy virtue of being God by nature and the creative agent in making and upholding all things, He has the right to this authority. As God, He alone could receive this honor (Col. 1:15-18; Heb. 1:1-3).\n\n### 2. By Birth\n\nNot only because of His deity but also His humanity, Christ obtained His place before God and man as Savior, Life-giver, and Power of God. "Thou shalt call his name JESUS: for he shall save his people from their sins" (Mt. 1:21). This person was "Emmanuel" or God with us (Mt. 1:23; Isa. 7:14; 9:6-7). Jesus was born to be King of the Jews and Savior of the world (Jn. 19:36-37; Lk. 2:11, 25-32).\n\n### 3. By His Sinlessness\n\nJesus was the only human being since the Fall who lived without sin. He alone could atone for humanity because the substitute had to be sinless, as we saw in Lesson Nineteen, Point IX.\n\nHe was made in the likeness of flesh that sin controlled, but His flesh was not sinful flesh or under sin's dominion like all other people (Rom. 8:1-3; 2 Cor. 5:21; 1 Pet. 2:21-25). Because of His sinless life, God blessed Him above all others: "Thou hast loved righteousness, and hated iniquity; therefore God, even thy God, hath anointed thee with the oil of gladness above thy fellows" (Heb. 2:9).\n\n### 4. By His Preparation\n\nFrom childhood, Christ prepared Himself for the work His Father sent Him to do. Certain passages make this clear (see Lesson Twenty-one, Point IX).\n\n### 5. By His Anointing\n\nBecause of His humanity and earthly limitations as a man—and because of His sinlessness and preparation—God gave Him the full anointing of the Holy Spirit to achieve greatness in three worlds: Heaven, Earth, and Hell.\n\nBy this anointing, as predicted in Isa. 11:1-2; 43:17; 61:1-2 and fulfilled in Mt. 3:16-17; Lk. 4:16-21; Acts 10:38; Jn. 3:34, He gained eternal fame throughout the universe.\n\n### 6. By His Achievements\n\nWhat He accomplished for God and humanity makes Him great. Death, Hell, and the grave were mastered by Him, along with sin and demons on Earth. Every enemy of God and humanity was fully conquered. Redemption was completed. God and humanity became reconciled, and God's eternal plan for us will be realized.\n\nHis fame reached the highest Heaven and lowest Hell. All creatures must bow their knees to Him (Phil. 2:5-11; Col. 1:16-21; 2:14-17; Eph. 1:20-23; 4:7-11; Heb. 2:14-15; 1 Pet. 3:22; Rev. 5:1-14; 7:11).\n\nHis achievements in life were so numerous that if they were all written, even the world couldn't contain the books (Jn. 21:25). His achievements in death were even greater. For His work in death, God highly exalted Him above every name, and every tongue must confess Him as Lord to God's glory (Phil. 2:5-11).\n\n### 7. By Inheritance\n\nPaul speaks of Christ as having been appointed "heir of all things . . . he hath by inheritance obtained a more excellent name than they" (the angels, Heb. 1:1-14).\n\nSome have achieved great names and fame through birth, education, preparation, and achievements. But no one has obtained such fame across all realms of the universe as Christ—and by all these means combined. Some achieve temporary, local fame. Christ, through all the above, has gained universal and eternal fame.\n\nThis super-exalted, eternal Person has authority in Heaven and Earth and wants to confer power of attorney upon all believers. Now all believers can do Christ's works—and greater works—because, as He said, "I go unto my Father" (Jn. 14:12-15).\n\nToday He's exalted at God's right hand, with angels, principalities, and powers subject to Him (Eph. 1:20-23; 1 Pet. 3:22). He upholds all things by His powerful Word. As Head of the Church and Custodian of the power of attorney, He bestows full legal authority upon all believers who learn the truth and tarry until endued with power from on High (Lk. 24:49; Acts 1:8).\n\nGod has on deposit in Heaven everything the Church needs now or ever will need. This is exclusively for the Church to carry on the work Jesus began—both to do and to teach. The Church has the right to draw to the limit of its faith.\n\nThink of it like a bank account with unlimited funds. There are no limits if our faith is unlimited. The only limitation is our unbelief and doubt about what we can receive from God—for ourselves and for the world in which we serve as God's agents in Christ's place.\n\nAll the power in Jesus's name is ours. If there's any limitation in God, then there's a limitation to the power in Jesus's name—and naturally there will be limits to what we receive. But since God cannot be limited, let's not limit what's in Christ's name or what we can get through it. We have the right to use His name against our enemies, in our petitions, in daily conflict, in our work, in worship, and in everything we do.\n\nThere's no question about Christ's authority to confer power of attorney on all Christians. There's no question about the legality of this transaction. He demonstrated His own power of attorney using His Father's name. He said, "I am come in my Father's name," and the works He did were "in my Father's name" (Jn. 5:43; 10:25; 17:6, 11, 26).\n\nBecause He faithfully exercised His power of attorney—acting in His Father's place on Earth and doing the works His Father would have done—God gave Him all the glory He had before coming into the world (Jn. 17:1-5). God gave Him all power in Heaven and Earth so He could confer this power on anyone worthy to represent Him as He represented God.\n\nChrist conferred this power on others while on Earth (Mt. 10; Lk. 10). How much more can He confer it now—since He has spoiled principalities and powers and finished redemption!\n\nIn actual combat with Hell's powers, when all demon hosts intended to overwhelm Him, He proved Himself more than their master. He shook them off and hurled back these evil forces, as pictured in Col. 2:14-17; Heb. 2:14-15.\n\nAt that moment, Satan was defeated and his power over humanity was broken forever. The devil is now a defeated foe, waiting only until Christ dispossesses him from Earth. Christ is the undisputed victor over all evil forces. He champions humanity's cause to regain original dominion.\n\nHe is Lord of all with a name above every name except His Father's. He holds all power to confer upon anyone who will fully cooperate with Him in the age-long struggle to defeat Satan in people's lives. The early believers after the cross fully exercised this power (Acts 1:8; 2:43; 3:6-7; 5:12-16; 19:11-20; etc.).\n\n## IV. Christian Exercise of the Power of Attorney\n\nAll Christians are commanded to use Jesus's name:\n\n- "in salvation" (Mt. 1:21; Acts 4:12)\n- "in baptism" (Mt. 28:19)\n- "in healing" (Acts 3:6-7, 16)\n- "in preaching" (Lk. 24:47; Acts 8:12)\n- "in miracles" (Acts 3:16; 4:30; Mk. 9:29)\n- "in judgment" (1 Cor. 5:1-5)\n- "in appeals to others" (1 Cor. 1:10)\n- "in prayer" (Jn. 14:12-15; 16:23-26)\n- "in faith and repentance" (Jn. 1:12; 3:18; Acts 10:43)\n- "in praise" (Heb. 13:15)\n- "in all gatherings" (Mt. 18:20)\n- "in consecration" (Mt. 19:29)\n- "in charity" (Mk. 9:41)\n- "in singing" (Rom. 15:9)\n- "in combat" (Mk. 16:17; Lk. 10:17; Eph. 6:10-18)\n- "in all things" (Col. 3:17; Eph. 5:20)\n\nThe name of Jesus and free use of it is the Church's deposit. But you must make definite contact with God, exercise faith in the name, and settle your authority as God's child to write checks on the Bank of Heaven before properly using Jesus's name.\n\nSinners have no right to use His name except in repentance (Acts 2:21; 10:43; Lk. 24:47). Sinners cannot perform miracles in His name (Acts 19:11-20). But any believer can use His name in all the ways listed above.\n\nNo special faith is required—just simple, ordinary, childlike faith in God and Christ's work is all that's needed. "Have faith in God . . . shall not doubt in his heart, but shall believe that those things which he saith shall come to pass; he shall have whatsoever he saith . . . What things soever ye desire, when ye pray, believe that ye receive them, and ye shall have them" (Mk. 11:22-24; Mt. 21:21-22).\n\nThe idea that faith isn't necessary to use Jesus's name isn't scriptural. Faith is necessary in everything we ask of God (Heb. 11:6; Jas. 1:5-8). Just because faith isn't emphasized in certain passages doesn't mean it's not required. Study and believe all Scripture on a subject, obeying it all, if you want results.\n\nLet me illustrate with a personal story. Years ago, a man gave me a blank check to buy the best overcoat I could find. He put no limit on the amount—I was to get the best coat in town, or whatever I wanted.\n\nBut I was timid and worried. What if I wrote too much and he didn't like it? What if he never gave me anything again? So I wrote the check for $18.50 and got the cheapest coat in town. It was a good coat, but not the best.\n\nIf I could do it over, I wouldn't hesitate to get what I wanted—what he really wanted me to have. But fear of what he might think kept me from receiving what was freely offered.\n\nThat's exactly how most Christians treat God.\n\nThere's no question about how much is deposited to your name in Heaven. There's plenty for all. But we're fearful we'll bankrupt God if we ask for what we really want. We receive according to our faith—according to the amount we write the check for—and no more. Heaven's divine cashier always honors checks signed in Jesus's name by any believer. Many times we go without because we don't write enough.\n\nHere's another story. Years ago in California, a woman prayed for $50,000 to build rest homes for missionaries. She prayed until she had God's assurance her prayer was answered.\n\nShe had a vacant lot. One day a man came asking to lease it for oil drilling. The Lord told her this was her answer. She leased the lot and kept royalty rights. A well was drilled and produced oil until she had exactly what she'd prayed for. Then it went dry.\n\nShe got exactly what she had faith for. This is always the case. Jesus said, "according to your faith be it unto you" (Mt. 9:29). You can sign a check on Heaven's Bank for what you want—or sign one for bare needs—and receive as you believe (Ps. 84:11; Mk. 11:22-24).\n\nChrist and the early Church demonstrated God's power and showed us how to ask, receive, and use the power of attorney. They performed miracles and signs, which opened doors to win souls and gave them authority in their calling. They believed God and manifested the supernatural, confirming what they preached.\n\nEvery born-again Christian has the right to use this name for what they want. The weakest child of God will be honored if they serve Jesus Christ and walk in God's Word's light. Healing, health, salvation from sin and habits, and material blessings of all kinds are available in abundance.\n\nAll that Jesus was, His name still is to believers. Very few are availing themselves of this power, but if Jesus tarries and this message spreads, Christians will see the truth and practice it. The time will come when multitudes understand the secret of faith and use their power of attorney for great and mighty things in Jesus's name.\n\nYou who read these lessons should be openhearted and believing. Seek God and build up faith in the Christian's power of attorney. Realize that Satan is a defeated foe, helpless before Jesus's name when invoked by faith. Sicknesses, sins, and all results of the Fall have been conquered by Jesus Christ. At His rebuke, they all must give way. May God open our eyes to the blessings for all who accept truth and exercise faith in Jesus's name and the New Testament gospel.\n\nThe Christian's power of attorney includes authority to do Christ's works and greater works (Jn. 14:12-14). It covers everything God has promised in hundreds of wonderful, unlimited promises. It covers every need in this life and the life to come (2 Cor. 1:20; 2 Pet. 1:3-4; 1 Tim. 4:8).\n\nIt's appalling how far the Church has backslidden from the New Testament program. True spiritual life and power with God is poorly understood even by today's best Christians. Listening to average preachers, you'd think the devil went out of business. The sooner we believe the heavenlies are filled with demons seeking to defeat believers and hinder prayers, the sooner we'll take up arms and cooperate with God against them.\n\nWhen dealing with demon spirits, we must be clean Christians, free from sin—or they'll mock our attempts to cast them out. We must know the power of Jesus's name and how to use it. We must take a definite stand against demons in His name and refuse defeat.\n\nWhatever the need, it can be supplied in Jesus's name. The enemy hindering us must be resisted, and he will flee. James said, "Resist the devil and he will flee from you" (Jas. 4:7).\n\nWe must understand that we're God's representatives, taking Christ's place in this world to cast out demons. Christ has taken our place before the Father to represent us and defeat Satan, who accuses saints before God day and night. We act in Christ's stead on Earth as He acts in our stead in Heaven. Our position in Christ gives us the standing before God that Christ had when here. We rebuke demons and heal the sick in His place, at His command. We are in Christ, Christ is in us, and we have the legal right to represent Him on Earth.\n\nChristianity began in miracles and is propagated by miracles. Its founder was a miraculous man and miracle worker. Every phase of His life and death was miraculous. His resurrection guarantees continued miracles.\n\nEvery new birth, every answered prayer, every Christian victory is a miracle. When reasoning and questioning replace faith and power, Christianity becomes fruitless and dead. Christianity is God's life in us through Christ—the Holy Spirit's power through faith in Christ's cross.\n\nPeople crave the miraculous, and those who produce divine works gain great followings. Many are deceived by demons and counterfeit miracles because of the natural human hunger for God's power. How sad! People turn from the miracle-working Christ to demon powers while the Church sleeps, unaware of what's happening. When anyone tries to restore Christianity's power to churches, they're often cast out as fanatics and deceivers. But if we remove God's power from Christianity, we have only a form of godliness (2 Tim. 3:5).\n\nThe Bible records miracles, and whoever produces them draws crowds—whether false or true. This love of the miraculous isn't ignorance; it's humanity's longing for spiritual power that comes from knowing and hungering for God.\n\nEvery revival in any generation has produced miracles. Education doesn't eliminate the desire for the miraculous—it intensifies it as education reveals our dependence on the unseen. Denying the miraculous isn't scholarship; it's foolishness. Any outpouring of the Spirit will produce miraculous revival. All normal people crave the supernatural. No one is satisfied until finding God's reality in their life.\n\nThink of it like this: the miracle element in humanity has driven us to invent, discover, investigate, and experiment with unseen laws around us. That's why we have so many wonderful modern things. The miracle realm is humanity's natural realm. We were created to experience the supernatural. People are again coming to know that faith and trust in God come as we simply and consciously live in God's realm through Jesus Christ.\n\nThe name of Jesus stands for justice, love, righteousness, holiness, peace, power, influence, happiness, liberty, life, light, joy, friendship, judgment, salvation, home, Heaven, and all that is good. It has been history's greatest name. Some have died for it; others have hated it enough to be damned because of it. It is the world's hope and all saints' joy.\n\nThe most wonderful truth for believers is the free, unreserved use of His name to carry on God's work on Earth and act in His stead among humanity, defeating sin and Satan.\n\nThink of it! An ordinary believing human being—given full power of attorney to act in God's place in the world! We are God's only visible agents on Earth. Why wouldn't God want us to fully represent Him with great power and authority, proving to people that God is great and powerful?\n\nFrom how most people represent God, you'd think Satan is greater and more powerful. Weak, failing representatives don't convince unbelievers of God's greatness.\n\nThe purpose of the power of attorney and its normal use is to confirm God's Word and prove to humanity that God is the Supreme Being of the universe.\n\nAll God's representatives should be like Paul, who told the Romans, "I will not dare to speak of any of those things which Christ hath not wrought by me, to make the Gentiles obedient by word and deed, through mighty signs and wonders, by the power of the Spirit of God . . . I have fully preached the gospel of Christ . . . And I am sure that, when I come unto you, I shall come in the fullness of the blessing of the gospel of Christ" (Rom. 15:18-19, 29).\n\nHe told the Corinthians, "I will come unto you shortly, if the Lord will, and will know, not the speech of them which are puffed up, but the power. For the kingdom of God is not in word, but in power" (1 Cor. 4:18-20).\n\nThe proper use of Jesus's name will heal the sick, cast out devils, make one immune to poisons, answer every prayer, give health and prosperity, perform miracles, and defeat all the devil's powers. God never intended any change in His methods of preaching or confirming His Word.\n\nBefore the cross, the disciples used His name to defeat Satan (Mt. 10:1-20;\n\n---\n\nYou have been given power of attorney in the name of Jesus Christ — the authority to act on His behalf in prayer, in ministry, and in spiritual warfare. Most believers never exercise this authority at full strength. So here's the question: Think of the most pressing situation in your life or someone else's life right now. Have you actually stood in the authority of Christ's name and addressed it — or have you only hoped that God might do something about it on His own?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk48i0014ewhjsvfy2c95	cmnknk48e000xewhj49ao5az0	Lesson 23 — The Bible Doctrine of Sin	READING	6338	6	\N	t	Sin is the most unpopular subject in modern Christianity — not because people disagree that bad things exist, but because the biblical definition of sin is far more comprehensive than most people are comfortable with. It's not just the crimes. It's the attitudes behind the crimes. It's not just what you do — it's the condition of the heart that produces what you do. And until you understand sin biblically, you can't understand salvation biblically.\n\nLast time we saw the authority Christ has placed in the hands of every believer. Now we turn to the problem that made that authority necessary: sin — what it is, where it came from, and what it costs.\n\n![Lesson 23 — The Bible Doctrine of Sin](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsQXmvWvhQWkP21SLEA3tZrcs70u6UjOq5zopY)\n\n### 1. LAW MEANS A RULE OF ACTION\n\nLaw is a rule of action established by a recognized authority to enforce justice and define what we owe to one another and to those in charge. Physical law governs the material universe—it keeps planets in orbit and seasons cycling according to God's design.\n\nMoral law, on the other hand, is the rule of action for beings like us—free moral agents—guiding how we use our choices and creative abilities according to God's plan. Think of it like this: physical law is the rails a train runs on automatically, but moral law is the road map for someone who can choose which way to drive.\n\n### 2. THE PURPOSE OF MORAL LAW\n\nMoral law exists to reveal and declare the right way for free wills to act regarding the highest good of everyone and everything in the universe. It governs our acts and intentions in our relationship with God and all creation.\n\nIt speaks to our reason, showing us what we *ought* to choose, will, and intend.\n\n### 3. THE IMMUTABILITY OF MORAL LAW\n\nMoral law can never change. Its demand remains the same: that all free wills devote themselves to the same goal God pursues—the highest good of the universe and everything in it.\n\nEven when free wills rebel, God's plan for creation's good doesn't budge. Because of this, the moral law stays constant forever.\n\n### 4. THE OBLIGATION OF MORAL LAW\n\nBecause moral law exists for the good of all beings, it's naturally binding on everyone. Whatever works against the good of beings is obviously wrong and must be prohibited.\n\nWe, as free moral agents, must work together to bring about the greatest public good and stand against whatever brings misery and hardship to society. It's a mutual plan benefiting each person and the whole universe—and we're all obligated to keep it according to the light we've received.\n\n### 5. THE REQUIREMENT OF MORAL GOVERNMENT\n\nFor moral law to work, there must be moral government to direct, guide, and control moral agents. Think of it like traffic laws: without enforcement—without penalties for running red lights and rewards for safe driving—roads would be chaos.\n\nThere must be a standard of living to which all must conform to be part of society, and there must be a way of dealing with rebels who disturb the peace. Law without penalties and rewards isn't really law at all—it's just advice people can take or leave without consequences.\n\nMoral government is obligated to execute the moral law faithfully and completely.\n\n### 6. REQUIREMENT OF A MORAL GOVERNOR\n\nSince we need moral law and moral government, we also need a moral governor—someone whose will and decisions are considered infallible by everyone under that government. This governor must be authoritative, not merely advisory.\n\nHe must maintain the respect of his subjects through faithful, unselfish service. He must be able to execute penalties and distribute rewards. Naturally, whoever is best qualified to rule and secure the highest good of all should be the one to rule—it's both his right and his duty.\n\nNo one but God can meet these requirements. His natural attributes, His perfect moral character, His relationship to the universe as Creator, and His history of absolute justice qualify Him alone to be the Supreme Moral Governor. Since our good and His glory depend on our mutual commitment to the same goal, He is obligated to require us to be holy and dedicated to helping Him secure the highest good of all.\n\n### 7. FREE MORAL AGENCY DEFINED\n\nFree moral agency consists of three things: intellect, sensibility, and free will. These form the foundation of our moral obligation to moral government.\n\nThe intellect includes reason and self-determination. Sensibility includes self-consciousness, all sensation, desire, emotion, passion, and feeling. Free will is the power to choose concerning moral law—it's your ability to choose good or evil without being forced.\n\nThis capacity was originally created in humanity, and we'll have it for all eternity.\n\n### 8. THE BASIS OF RIGHT CHOICE\n\nOur moral obligation to always act for the best good of all is the foundation of right moral action. Nothing happens in life except what someone chooses.\n\nIt's absolutely critical that we make right choices that bring about the greatest public and private good. This obligation extends to the deepest intentions of our will. We're obligated to choose holiness as the path to the greatest good and happiness.\n\n### 9. THE BASIS OF JUSTICE\n\nJudging based on intention is the right foundation for justice under moral government—and this is how civil governments operate too.\n\nIf you make a choice with good intentions but things don't turn out well, or if you make a choice with bad intentions and it happens to turn out okay, you should be held responsible for your *intention*, not just the results. The Bible values intention more than outward results (2 Cor. 8:12; Mk. 7:15-23; Jas. 1:13-15; 3:9-14).\n\nAll vice and virtue are considered as coming from the heart. Where the heart is right, all is considered right; where the heart is bad, all is considered bad (Mt. 7:15-21). Even sinners do some things outwardly that God requires, but if the heart isn't right—if the intention is selfish—the acts don't change the heart.\n\nVirtue means being devoted to the same goal God is devoted to. Vice means being devoted to what Satan pursues—self-gratification contrary to moral law and the highest good of the universe.\n\n### 10. THE EXTENT OF OBEDIENCE TO THE MORAL LAW\n\nSince the foundation of moral obligation is the highest good of all, complete devotion to that goal must constitute obedience to moral law. You can't have divided loyalties here.\n\nYou can't choose the good of others and self-gratification at the same time. God can't tolerate half-heartedness in choice and service (Rev. 3:15-16; Mt. 22:36-40). He can't justify someone who continually falls short of full obedience to known duty.\n\nIf you're always coming up short of what you know you should do, there's not a single moment when you're not under the curse of a broken law (Gal. 3:10-14; Jas. 2:10; 4:17). God can't set aside the penalty until repentance, forgiveness, and full obedience are realized (Rom. 8:1-13; Gal. 5:16-26; Rom. 6:16-23; Jn. 8:34; 1 Jn. 3:8-10).\n\n### 11. OF WHAT DOES DISOBEDIENCE TO THE MORAL LAW CONSIST?\n\nDisobedience consists in choosing self-gratification as your life's goal instead of the greatest good of the universe. It means committing your will and dedicating your life to serving sin, Satan, and your senses rather than the moral law and intelligence.\n\nIt's like choosing to be governed by your impulses and passions instead of by reason and wisdom. Self-gratification is the root of all sin, and human selfishness is closely allied to Satan's own self-interest.\n\nYour will is always free to resist desire and lust contrary to the law, but when it doesn't, sin happens. Your mind knows its obligation, so when you choose contrary to the law, it's not a choice made in ignorance—it's a free action that brings condemnation.\n\nSelfishness is always unreasonable. It denies true humanity and rational nature. It shows contempt for God's law in your own reason.\n\nNo sinner chooses the way of reason and common sense. They rarely consult reason for their actions—they usually just obey their lusts and stay in stubborn rebellion against moral law and reason. They're lustful at heart whether they can act on all those lusts or not. As long as they remain that way, they're condemned before God and need regeneration to bring them to obedience.\n\n### 12. THE BASIS OF DEGREES OF GUILT AND VIRTUE\n\nBoth reason and Scripture confirm that there are degrees of guilt and virtue. Some people are more guilty or more virtuous than others, and any individual may be more guilty or more virtuous at one time than another—whether saint or sinner.\n\nAll guilt and virtue depend on exercising moral obligation, and that depends on your light and knowledge concerning moral law. Think of it like this: a doctor who harms a patient through negligence is more culpable than someone with no medical training who makes the same mistake.\n\nThe sinner's guilt equals their knowledge of the value of the interests they reject. You're held more responsible today than people were in ancient times because you know more (Acts 17:30; Jas. 4:17). Your guilt or virtue matches the knowledge you have about a matter and your conformity to it (Rom. 2:12-16; Jn. 9:41; 15:22-24; Mt. 13:11-12; Lk. 12:47-48).\n\nSelfishness is the rejection of all moral obligation, regardless of how much light you have. Sin lies in the intention, and intention can only be measured by the knowledge under which it's formed and maintained.\n\nIf a selfish person preaches the gospel, it's because it happens to be the most gratifying thing they could do for themselves. They might even preach for the good of others while having personal benefits as their chief motive. Take away personal gain—financially or socially—and they'd stop preaching. If that same person became a robber, it would be for the same reason: not to do evil for its own sake, but to gratify themselves.\n\nIf a sinner abstains from some evil for the sake of loved ones, their reputation, fear of judgment or disgrace, or any other reason, it's not because they're good at heart or because they think the act is wicked. It's merely for selfish reasons and personal gain.\n\nThis is true for every area of life where the heart isn't right. Natural people look for gain or advantage in everything. It's only when sinners devote themselves to the highest good of being that they stop being sinners by nature and practice and begin acting from an unselfish standpoint instead of living for self-gratification.\n\n## II. The Bible Definition of "Sin" and "The Old Man"\n\nThe Hebrew and Greek words translated "transgression" mean "moral, religious and national rebellion," "revolt," "unrighteousness," "wickedness," "violation," and "breaking the law." Sin is twofold:\n\n### 1. The Bible Definition of Outward Sin\n\n> (1) "Sin is transgression of the law" (1 Jn. 3:4).\n\n> (2) "Where no law is, there is no transgression" (Rom. 4:15).\n\n> (3) "By the law is the knowledge of sin" (Rom. 3:20; 7:7).\n\n> (4) "Sin is not imputed when there is no law" (Rom. 5:13).\n\n> (5) "Without the law sin was dead" or dormant (Rom. 7:8).\n\n> (6) "The thought of foolishness is sin" (Prov. 24:9; 2 Cor. 10:4-5).\n\n> (7) "Every idle word that men shall speak" is sin (Mt. 12:36-37).\n\n> (8) "All unrighteousness is sin" (1 Jn. 5:17).\n\n> (9) "Whatsoever is not of faith is sin" (Rom. 14:23).\n\n> (10) "If ye have respect of persons, ye commit sin" (Jas. 2:9).\n\n> (11) "He that knoweth to do good, and doeth it not, to him it is sin" (Jas. 4:17).\n\n> (12) All "actions" contrary to the law are sin (1 Sam. 2:3; Jas. 1:13-15).\n\n> (13) "Rebellion is as the sin of witchcraft" (1 Sam. 15:23).\n\n> (14) Any "omission" of the law is sin (Mt. 23:23).\n\n> (15) All "desires" and "lusts" contrary to the law, inwardly or outwardly manifested, are sin (Mk. 7:19-21; Rom. 1:29-32; Gal. 5:16-21; Eph. 2:3; Col. 3:5-10; Jas. 1:13-15).\n\nThese passages describe outward acts of something far more sinister and powerful than appears on the surface, as we'll see in the next point.\n\n### 2. The Bible Definition of Inbred Sin\n\n> (1) "The prince of the power of the air, the spirit that now worketh in the children of disobedience" (Eph. 2:1-3; 1 Jn. 3:8; Jn. 8:41-44). This single statement defines "the old man" and inbred sin. It's the devil himself working in disobedient people—in contrast with "the new man," which is the Spirit and nature of God working in obedient sons and daughters.\n\n> (2) Other terms Scripture uses for "the old man" as the devil working in people include: "sin" (Jn. 1:29; 8:34; Rom. 6:4-22; 7:7-25; 8:2); "the body of sin" (Rom. 6:6); "darkness" (Acts 26:18); "the body of this death" (Rom. 7:24); "the old man" (Rom. 6:6; Eph. 4:22-24; Col. 3:9); "the lusts of the flesh" (Eph. 2:3); "the lust of the flesh" (1 Jn. 2:15-17; Gal. 5:16-21); "the lust of the eyes" (1 Jn. 2:15-17); "the pride of life" (1 Jn. 2:15-17); "all ungodliness and unrighteousness" (Rom. 1:18); "the world" (1 Jn. 2:15-17); "vile affections" (Rom. 1:26); "the lusts of the mind" (Eph. 2:3); "a reprobate mind" (Rom. 1:28); "the lusts of sin" (Rom. 6:11-12); "the motions of sins" (Rom. 7:5); "the law of sin and death" (Rom. 7:7-25; 8:2); "the carnal mind" (Rom. 8:1-13); "the works of the flesh" (Gal. 5:16-21, 24); "the flesh" (Rom. 8:1-13; Gal. 5:24); "the carnal man" (Rom. 7:14-25; 8:1-13); "the power of Satan" (Acts 26:18); "the god of this world" (2 Cor. 4:3-4); "principalities . . . powers . . . the rulers of darkness of this world . . . spiritual wickedness in high places" (Eph. 6:12); "the lusts of your father" the devil (Jn. 8:44); "sin that dwelleth in me" (Rom. 7:7-25); "the course of this world" (Eph. 2:2); and "the body of the sins of the flesh" (Col. 2:11).\n\nThese Scripture statements plainly tell us that sin is something more than "transgression of the law" or outward sin—it's something separate from the person themselves. Every one of these expressions proves there's a real law of sin and death, and behind this law, enforcing it, is a real power from a real nature and a real person who is stronger than we are.\n\nIt's the "old man," "the body of sin," and "the body of this death" that are to be destroyed—not your body, soul, and spirit (Rom. 6:1-22; 8:1-13; Gal. 5:24; Col. 2:11). When this "old man" is delivered from or annulled from operating in you, your body, soul, and spirit—as originally created—are still there. You're liberated from the devil for the first time in your life when this happens.\n\nIt's the "lusts," "desires," and "motions of sin" from the devil in your body, soul, and spirit that are destroyed, put to death, and "put off"—not any part of your created being. These "lusts" are plainly stated as being "of the devil" and "of the world" system, which will finally pass away and won't operate in people anymore (Jn. 8:44; Eph. 2:1-3; 1 Jn. 2:15-17; 3:8; Rom. 6:11-22).\n\nTheologians call this aspect of sin by various names: inherent sin, sin principle, infection of nature, moral disease, contagious corruption, incentive to sin, depraved nature, sinful propensities, evil tendencies, manifold infirmity, the carnal nature, the flesh, the carnal mind, the presence and pollution of sin in the heart, the remains of sin, inbred sin, and many other things. These are theological terms expressing the same scriptural truth: sin is something real in you, separate from your body, soul, and spirit as originally created.\n\n## III. Sin is Not Man's Body, Soul, or Spirit\n\nThe following points prove that sin is not only "transgression of the law," but that inbred sin is the very spirit and nature of the devil working in people through Satan's lusts. It's something real and living and entirely separate from our body, soul, and spirit as originally created by God.\n\n### 1. Statements That Prove Sin Is Separate From Man\n\nPhrases like "taketh away the sin of the world" (Jn. 1:29); "servant of sin" (Jn. 8:34); "under sin" (Rom. 3:9); "by one man sin entered the world" (Rom. 5:12); "sin abounded" (Rom. 5:20); "sin hath reigned" (Rom. 5:12); "dead to sin" (Rom. 6:1-12); "not serve sin" (Rom. 6:6); "freed from sin" (Rom. 6:7); "let not sin therefore reign" (Rom. 6:12); "sin shall not have dominion over you" (Rom. 6:14); "servants of sin" (Rom. 6:20); "free from sin" (Rom. 6:18, 22); "wages of sin" (Rom. 6:23); "sin revived" (Rom. 7:7-11); "sold under sin" (Rom. 7:14); "sin dwelleth in me" (Rom. 7:17, 20); "into captivity to the law of sin" (Rom. 7:23-25); "made me free from the law of sin and death" (Rom. 8:2); "condemned sin in the flesh" (Rom. 8:3); "deceitfulness of sin" (Heb. 3:13); "put off" and "put away sin" (Eph. 4:22; Heb. 9:26); "striving against sin" and "wrestle against principalities, against powers, against the rulers of darkness of this world, against spiritual wickedness in high places" (Eph. 6:12; Heb. 12:4); "against the wiles of the devil" and "his devices" (Eph. 6:11; 2 Cor. 2:11); and "He that committeth sin is of the devil" (1 Jn. 3:8)—all these prove that "sin" is something more than a person outwardly transgressing the law.\n\nAll these statements imply that sin is a slave master and a tyrant over people, causing death by forcing transgression (Rom. 7:7-25; 8:2). Sin is a real person apart from us, or it couldn't reign, dominate, enslave, deceive, slay, be present with, control, dwell in, or dictate to us.\n\nSin is also a law. If sin is a law, there must be someone who made that law and enforces it. There could be no law unless someone made it, and it would be worthless unless it could be enforced according to its purpose. The devil made and enforces the law of sin and death through demons and spirit forces, and it can only be annulled in free moral agents by a greater law enforced by a greater power (Heb. 2:14, 15).\n\nFree wills seek the annulment of this law by applying to the Court of Heaven according to the moral law (Rom. 8:1-13). Both laws conflict, as stated in Rom. 7:7-25, proving there are real persons and powers behind each law. This demonstrates that sin is a real spirit and nature in us, separate from our own body, soul, and spirit.\n\nWhen you're "freed from sin," you're cut loose from the body of sin and don't need to be chained to it again—if you walk and live in the Spirit (Gal. 5:16-26; Rom. 8:1-13).\n\nThe expressions "the body of sin" and "the body of this death" probably allude to a horrible ancient punishment where a condemned criminal was chained to the corpse of the person they murdered and left to die in the loathsome company of that decaying body. It's similar to being handcuffed to a rotting corpse—that's what being bound to sin is like.\n\n### 2. Being "Under Sin"\n\nPeople are described as being "under sin" (Rom. 3:9; Gal. 3:22); as being "sold under sin" which made them "carnal" (Rom. 7:14); and as being "under the elements of the world" (Gal. 4:3, 9).\n\nThe Greek word for "under" is *hupo*, meaning "underneath, in place or condition," "an inferior position"—it implies a power higher than us which has brought us under sin's control.\n\nLook at how the word is used elsewhere: "under authority" and "soldiers under me" (Mt. 8:9); "not under the law, but under grace" (Rom. 6:14-15); "bruise Satan under your feet" (Rom. 16:20); "I keep under my body" (1 Cor. 9:27); "under obedience" (1 Cor. 14:34); "put all enemies under his feet" (1 Cor. 15:24-28); "under tutors" (Gal. 4:2); and "under the mighty hand of God" (1 Pet. 5:6).\n\nAll these prove that a real power and real person are involved in executing these laws and accomplishments. If we're "under sin," then there must be a real person and power greater than us keeping us there and ruling over us.\n\nThe phrase "sold under sin" implies a slave master as well as a slave—and this is exactly what Paul experienced when he was helpless "under sin" (Rom. 7:7-25).\n\n### 3. Knowledge of Sin Came Later\n\nThe phrase "by the law is the knowledge of sin" proves that sin wasn't in humanity originally—it wasn't created in us. Sin came later, entering the world through the devil's malice.\n\nSin is so closely connected to the devil that Scripture says, "He that committeth sin is of the devil" (1 Jn. 3:4-10; Jn. 8:44; Eph. 2:1-3). Sin, then, is something separate from both humanity and God's law.\n\n### 4. Sin Has Its Own Lusts\n\nSin is "in the mortal body" but isn't the mortal body or any part of it—and this "sin" has its own lusts (Rom. 6:12, 16-17; 7:5; Jn. 8:44).\n\nWe're commanded not to yield to these lusts or to the spirit and nature to whom these lusts belong. Instead, we're to yield to God and to the desires of His Spirit and nature (Rom. 6:16-22).\n\nThis contrast proves that the sins and lusts in us are from the devil's spirit and nature, just as new desires in a born-again soul come from God's Spirit and nature (2 Cor. 5:17).\n\nBoth the devil and God have desires of their own, and we become partakers of them when we serve one or the other (Jn. 8:44; 1 Jn. 3:8; 2 Pet. 1:4). If the old lusts are from Satan and the new desires are from God, then sin is the devil's spirit and nature working in sons of disobedience, while righteousness is God's Spirit and nature working in sons of obedience.\n\n### 5. Dead to Sin, Not Dead to Your Original Being\n\nWhen believers are made "dead indeed unto sin" and "freed from sin" at the new birth, they don't become dead to or freed from their original body, soul, and spirit. Every original part of us was created sinless—sin couldn't be part of our own being (Rom. 5:12-21; 6:5-22; 8:1-13).\n\nDeath to and freedom from sin refer to being free from the person of the devil and his spirit that took control of humanity at the Fall.\n\n### 6. Christ's Death Dealt With Sin, Not Human Nature\n\nChrist "died unto sin once," to "take away sin" and to "put away sin"—but He didn't die to destroy any part of our bodily members, soul passions, or spirit faculties, since these were originally created sinless (Rom. 6:10; Jn. 1:29; Heb. 7:27; 9:12, 26).\n\nThe whole person becomes sinless when becoming a new creature in Christ (2 Cor. 5:17). If you stay one (and you can), you continue in a sinless life (Col. 2:6-7; 1 Jn. 2:1-2, 29; 3:8-10; 5:1-5, 18).\n\nThe Greek word for "put away" is *athetesis*, meaning "to annul" or "to cancel sin"—to cancel the devil's operation in a person. The word is translated "disannul" in Gal. 3:15 and Heb. 7:18, showing how and to what extent sin is "put away" in the believer.\n\nThe Greek word for "take away" is *airo*, meaning "to take out of the way," "do away with," "loose," "put away," or "remove." It means to remove sin from the believer by virtue of Christ taking it in His own body on the cross.\n\n### 7. Romans 6:1–8:13: Five Illustrations\n\nA study of Rom. 6:1–8:13 reveals that sin is the devil's spirit and nature working in sons of disobedience. The old relationship to the devil through obeying the law of sin and death, and the new relationship to God through obeying the law of the Spirit of life in Christ Jesus, are illustrated through five main ideas:\n\n> **(1) By union with Christ in His death, burial, resurrection, and new life:** "If we have been planted together in the likeness of his death, we shall also be in the likeness of his resurrection: Knowing this, that our old man was crucified with him, that the body of sin might be destroyed, that henceforth we should not serve sin. For he that is dead to sin is freed from sin . . . Knowing that Christ being raised from the dead dieth no more; death hath no more dominion over him. For in that he died, he died unto sin once: but in that he liveth, he liveth unto God" (Rom. 6:1-10).\n\n> **(2) By reckoning the "old man" dead and the "new man" alive:** "Likewise reckon ye also yourselves to be dead indeed unto sin, but alive unto God through our Lord Jesus Christ. Let not sin therefore reign in your mortal body, that ye should obey it in the lusts thereof. Neither yield ye your members as instruments of unrighteousness unto sin: but yield yourselves unto God, as those that are alive from the dead, and your members as instruments of righteousness unto God. For sin shall not have dominion over you: for ye are not under law, but under grace" (Rom. 6:11-14).\n>\n> How could anyone reckon themselves dead to something that wasn't constantly present to deal with? When believers become dead to sin and alive to God, they no longer live after the flesh, in sin, under Satan's power and dominion. But they still have to "reckon" themselves dead to sin—proving that the "old man," the devil, is still present to deal with. He doesn't have to be inside you to be present.\n\n> **(3) By the master and servant relationship (Rom. 6:15-23).** Paul illustrates freedom from sin and the new life of service to God through the imagery of a servant who can choose between two masters—sin or righteousness, Satan or God.\n\n> Paul asks: "Shall we sin, because we are not under the law, but under grace?"—meaning, shall we yield again to the "old man" or the devil after being made free? The answer: "God forbid" (Rom. 6:15).\n\n> **A. Acts of obedience indicate the master served:** "Know ye not, that to whom ye yield yourselves servants to obey, his servants ye are to whom ye obey; whether of sin unto death, or of obedience unto righteousness?" (Rom. 6:16). This applies to saints as well as sinners. If a saint yields to sin and Satan, they become a sinner and servant of sin again. Whoever you yield to is your master—and you're dead to the one you're not serving.\n\n> **B. Change in obedience indicates a change in masters:** "But God be thanked, that ye were the servants of sin, but ye have obeyed from the heart that form of doctrine which was delivered you. Being then made free from sin, ye become the servants of righteousness" (Rom. 6:17-18). When you change masters from Satan to God (or vice versa), your changed actions show it.\n\n> **C. The master served indicates the nature of obedience:** "For as ye have yielded your members servants to uncleanness and to iniquity; even so now yield your members servants to righteousness unto holiness. For when ye were the servants of sin, ye were free from righteousness" (Rom. 6:19-20). Under the devil's control, there's ever-increasing iniquity and transformation toward Satan's likeness (Jn. 8:44). Under God's mastery, there's ever-increasing holiness and transformation into Christ's image (2 Cor. 3:18).\n\n> **D. The nature of obedience indicates the wages received:** "What fruit had ye then in those things whereof ye are now ashamed? For the end of those things is death. But now being made free from sin, and become the servants of God, ye have your fruit unto holiness and the end everlasting life. For the wages of sin is death; but the gift of God is eternal life through Jesus Christ our Lord" (Rom. 6:21-23).\n\n> **(4) By marriage (Rom. 7:1-6).** Paul illustrates freedom from sin and new life with God through marriage. He starts by stating that "the law hath dominion over a man as long as he liveth," then continues: "The woman which hath an husband is bound by the law to her husband so long as he liveth; but if her husband be dead, she is loosed from the law of her husband" (Rom. 7:1-3).\n\n> He applies this to believers: "Wherefore, my brethren, ye also are become dead to the law by the body of Christ; that ye should be married to another, even to him who is raised from the dead, that we should bring forth fruit unto God" (Rom. 7:4). Sin is like the husband—there's no legal freedom until death. Sin was crucified with Christ so we can be free and married to God (Rom. 6:6-12). When sin was alive in us, it produced death; now that it's been killed, we can be free and produce life (Rom. 7:5-6). We become free from sin at the new birth (1 Jn. 2:29; 3:8-10; 5:1-5, 18).\n\n> **(5) By Paul's own experience (Rom. 7:7–8:13).** Paul shares his own story of being bound to the law of sin and death and his deliverance from it. The main points:\n\n> **A.** By the law is the knowledge of sin (Rom. 7:7).\n\n> **B.** Sin itself is what's sinful in me, working evil desires in me and refusing to let me obey God's law (Rom. 7:7-8).\n\n> **C.** Sin rebels against the law and makes me break it, so the death penalty can be carried out against me (Rom. 7:9).\n\n> **D.** The law was ordained to give life, but sin wouldn't let me obey it, cutting me off from that life and bringing the curse of the broken law (Rom. 7:10; Gal. 3:10).\n\n> **E.** Sin used the commandment as an opportunity to demonstrate its authority over me, deceived me, and through it killed me (Rom. 7:11). Sin betrayed me by taking advantage of my ignorance and helplessness.\n\n> **F.** I conclude that the law is holy, but I\n\n---\n\nThe doctrine of sin is not meant to crush you — it's meant to show you why you need what God has provided. You can't receive a cure you don't think you need. So here's the question to sit with: According to the biblical definition of sin — not cultural standards, not comparison to others, but conformity to the character of God — is there an area of your life you've been calling a weakness or a habit that Scripture would call sin? And what would change if you called it what it is?	\N	\N	\N
cmq76xgtz0003hks81q1gig09	cmq76xgtn0001hks8s3kqs5yq	Lesson 11 — The Watchers and the Nephilim: Genesis 6 and the Roots of Demonic Activity	READING	\N	0	\N	t	![Lesson 11 — The Watchers and the Nephilim: Genesis 6 and the Roots of Demonic Activity](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsGH4bfFQtkjPm8YJfVWKDcL3Bvzgo59AS2es6)\n\n## Lesson 11 — The Watchers and the Nephilim: Genesis 6 and the Roots of Demonic Activity\n\nThe text is six verses long and has generated more theological controversy than almost anything else in Genesis. Most pastors skip past it in three minutes. The ancient world treated it as the explanation for everything — the origin of giants, the reason for the flood, the source of the demonic hierarchy that still operates today.\n\n[Genesis 6:1-4](data-scripture="Genesis 6:1-4"): *"When men began to multiply on the face of the earth, and daughters were born to them, the sons of God saw that the daughters of men were fair; and they took to wife such of them as they chose... The Nephilim were on the earth in those days, and also afterward, when the sons of God came in to the daughters of men, and they bore children to them. These were the mighty men that were of old, the men of renown."*\n\n---\n\n## What It Is\n\nThe "sons of God" (*bene Elohim*) in Genesis 6 are the same term used in Job 1:6, 2:1, and 38:7 — where they clearly refer to angelic beings. The ancient Jewish interpreters (1 Enoch, Jubilees, the Dead Sea Scrolls community), the New Testament authors, and the early church fathers were virtually unanimous: Genesis 6 describes a transgression by a class of angelic beings called Watchers, who abandoned their assigned domain, took human women as wives, and produced a hybrid race — the Nephilim.\n\n[Jude 1:6](data-scripture="Jude 1:6"): *"The angels which kept not their first estate, but left their own habitation, he hath reserved in everlasting chains under darkness unto the judgment of the great day."*\n\n[2 Peter 2:4](data-scripture="2 Peter 2:4"): *"God spared not the angels that sinned, but cast them down to hell, and delivered them into chains of darkness, to be reserved unto judgment."*\n\nThese angels sinned in a specific and unprecedented way — they crossed the boundary between the divine and human realms, producing offspring and contaminating the human bloodline.\n\n## The Demonic Deception\n\nThe standard modern theological reading — that "sons of God" means the line of Seth and "daughters of men" means the line of Cain — collapses under the weight of the ancient textual evidence and the New Testament's own citations. It was proposed in the 4th century precisely because the Watcher interpretation was becoming a theological embarrassment.\n\nThe demonic significance of Genesis 6 is this: the Flood was not merely a judgment on human wickedness. It was a specific divine response to a specific supernatural transgression that threatened the integrity of the human bloodline through which the Messiah would eventually come. Satan's strategy in Genesis 6 was genetic — contaminate humanity so thoroughly that no pure human bloodline remained from which Christ could be born.\n\nThe Nephilim who survived the Flood (the text notes "and also afterward") explain the giant clans of Canaan that Israel was commanded to exterminate — the Anakim, Rephaim, Emim, Zamzummim. The command to kill every man, woman, and child in those cities was not genocide. It was the completion of the Flood's purpose against a specific supernatural contamination.\n\n## How It Opens the Door\n\n**The disembodied spirits of the Nephilim are one proposed origin of demons.** When the hybrid offspring of Watcher unions died — beings with no place in God's created order, neither fully angelic nor fully human — their spirits had no designated destination. The intertestamental literature and several early church fathers held that these disembodied spirits became the roving demons of the second temple period: the unclean spirits that Jesus cast out of people, who knew His name and feared the coming judgment.\n\n**The Watcher transgression established the template for demonic strategy.** The pattern of Genesis 6 — infiltrate, corrupt, hybridize, contaminate — is the template for every demonic strategy since. Every attempt to blur the boundary between the divine/supernatural and the human, every attempt to corrupt the human image through genetic or spiritual contamination, follows the Watcher playbook.\n\n**Understanding Genesis 6 explains Revelation 9.** The locust army released from the abyss in Revelation 9 has a king — Abaddon/Apollyon. The abyss (*tartarus* in 2 Peter 2:4) is specifically where the Watchers are imprisoned. The end-times scenario releases what the Flood imprisoned.\n\n## What This Means for You Right Now\n\nThe purpose of this lesson is not speculation — it is theological grounding. Understanding that demonic beings have operated against humanity since before the Flood, that their strategies are ancient and documented in Scripture, and that God has repeatedly and specifically intervened to shut down those strategies, gives you a framework for the warfare you are in.\n\n[Genesis 6:9](data-scripture="Genesis 6:9"): *"Noah was a just man and perfect in his generations."* The Hebrew *tamim* — "perfect" — in the context of this passage likely refers to genetic integrity: Noah's line had not been contaminated by the Watcher transgression. God preserved what was pure to restart what had been corrupted.\n\nYou are in the restored image — [2 Corinthians 5:17](data-scripture="2 Corinthians 5:17"). The demonic strategy of contamination has been answered by the new creation. The seed of the woman has crushed the head of the serpent. [Genesis 3:15](data-scripture="Genesis 3:15"). The warfare continues — but its outcome was determined at the cross.\n\n---\n\n*The Watchers were a specific class of rebel beings with a documented strategy. The next lesson examines the strongmen they left behind — the named principalities that the New Testament identifies as operating through specific patterns of deception.*	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk48i0015ewhj7cbhfj70	cmnknk48e000xewhj49ao5az0	Lesson 24 — How to Get Rid of Sin and Sickness	READING	1573	7	\N	t	If God provided healing in the atonement the same way He provided forgiveness, then the practical process for receiving healing should look similar to the practical process for receiving forgiveness. And it does. The problem is that most Christians know the steps for receiving forgiveness by heart but have never been taught the steps for receiving healing — even though both are in the same atonement, purchased by the same Christ.\n\nLast time we established what sin is — any deviation from the character of God — and where it leads. Now we get to the practical question everyone eventually asks: How do you actually get free from sin and sickness? What are the specific steps?\n\n![Lesson 24 — How to Get Rid of Sin and Sickness](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsmRcyZeTjs8MAGw2JgFBiIXKDThNCyqSVfZL3)\n\n## I. Recognition of Sin and Sickness\n\nThe first step to getting free from sin and sickness is recognizing that you're a sinner who stands guilty before God and deserving of eternal punishment. You experience sickness because of sin — not necessarily your own personal sin, but always because of the Fall and because we live in a broken, corrupted world.\n\nHere's the good news: Christ died to save you from your sins and sicknesses and to deliver you from all the works of the devil. Once you understand that, you can go to God in faith — trusting in what Christ accomplished on the cross and coming in Jesus' name — just like you'd go to your earthly father to receive something he'd already promised and provided for you.\n\nIf you don't believe you're a fallen creature, you naturally won't see any need for redemption from the Fall. So a true understanding of both the Fall and redemption is essential before you can receive any deliverance. In Lesson Nine, Points IV, VII, and VIII, we made the doctrine of the fall of man clear. In Lesson Twenty-three, we defined sin and showed the awful guilt that hangs over every person.\n\nIf you're not convinced by now that you're a sinner who needs to be saved, something is seriously wrong. The thing to do is lay aside all rebellion and unbelief toward God and His Word. Acknowledge your guilt and be genuinely sorry for your sin (2 Cor. 7:9-11). You must do this before you can be saved.\n\nIn order to see God, you must be saved from sin (Mt. 1:21; Heb. 12:14). You must be born-again and become a new creature (Jn. 3:1-8; 2 Cor. 5:17). You must be made alive from death in trespasses and sins to a new life (Eph. 2:1-10; Col. 2:13).\n\nYou must cooperate with God to be cleansed from all sin (Isa. 1:16-18; Jn. 1:12; Eph. 4:20-25; 5:13-14; Col. 3:9-17). You must confess all sin, repent of being a sinner, and turn from Satan to God — from darkness to light — from sin to a life of holiness in order to be born-again (1 Jn. 1:9; Lk. 13:1-5; Acts 26:18).\n\nYou must have faith in Christ and in the atonement (Rom. 3:24-25). You must identify yourself with Christ in His death, burial, resurrection, and new life (Rom. 6:1-13). These are "musts" for everyone who wants to be saved. There are many other things you'll need to do after you've been saved, but before that happens, don't worry about those duties yet.\n\n## II. Two Necessary Things to Do to Be Saved or Born-Again\n\nThere are only two things necessary for you to be saved from all sin — just two. Of course, this assumes you already recognize that you're a sinner and that Christ died to save you. With that understood, here's what must be done:\n\n### 1. Confession of Sin or True Repentance\n\nSome ultra-grace teachers claim they've discovered a "super-grace" that frees you from any responsibility in salvation — except maybe one single act of faith. They say once you believe one time, that's all that's ever needed. At that point, responsibility for your eternal salvation supposedly shifts from you to God, and if you're ever lost, it's God's fault.\n\nThey claim confession of sins isn't necessary for salvation; that people are incapable of repenting; that if you had to confess all your sins you'd never be saved; that prayer isn't necessary for salvation; that requiring repentance is an error in this age; that no Scripture after the cross requires confession as a condition of salvation; that salvation isn't conditioned on asking, seeking, or praying; that this was fine for Israel before the cross but isn't required now; that only Israel was commanded to seek the Lord, pray, and repent; and that all Gentiles need to do is believe and accept Christ.\n\nThe Bible doesn't teach any of this. Scripture teaches that people not only can repent but must repent to be saved. You must confess your sins before you can be forgiven. You must continue in faith after you're saved. You must walk in the light of God's Word to keep being cleansed from all sin. And there are many things you must continue doing if you want to please God and live as He demands.\n\nThe word "repent" actually appears more often in the New Testament than in the Old. There's no biblical authority for teaching that salvation before the cross was different from what it is now when it comes to seeking God, repenting, praying, and confessing sins. There's no authority for saying Jews and Gentiles are saved differently.\n\nScripture has always demanded — and always will — that all people be saved by repentance and faith in God. The Bible doesn't teach that repentance was only for Jews before the cross and not required since. That's a clear display of ignorance — willful ignorance, really — because no one who genuinely wants to teach what the Bible says could come away with these conclusions. There's no excuse for not knowing what Scripture says; it's plain to anyone who will investigate the truth.\n\nTrue repentance includes a sense of personal guilt (Ps. 51:1-7; 1 Tim. 1:13), godly sorrow for sin (2 Cor. 7:9-11), an understanding of God's mercy through Christ (Isa. 53:4-6; Lk. 1:76-78; Titus 3:5-6), and a turning away from sin to be free from further guilt (2 Chron. 7:14; Acts 26:18; Jer. 7:3; 18:11; Ezek. 18:30; 33:10-20). Scripture states clearly that if people don't repent, they will perish — so repentance is absolutely necessary (Mt. 11:20-22; Lk. 13:1-5; Rom. 2:5).\n\nIn the New Testament, repentance is mentioned thirty-seven times in passages after the cross and only twenty-five times before. The word "repent" with its various forms appears sixty-four times in the New Testament and only forty-six times in the Old Testament. So this is just as much a New Testament teaching as an Old Testament one. Not a single passage says people shouldn't repent, pray, confess, or seek God since the cross. That idea is purely a lie of the devil — a doctrine created by ultra-grace teachers to prop up their false theories.\n\nIn Mt. 28:19-20, the apostles were commanded to make disciples of all nations and to teach people throughout this entire age to observe everything Christ had commanded them. This proves that all the passages on repentance, praying, confessing sins, and meeting the requirements of salvation that Christ commanded before the cross were to be taught to all nations after the cross and throughout this age.\n\n**Before the Cross:** Christ and others commanded all people to "repent and believe the gospel" (Mk. 1:15; 6:12), to "bring forth therefore fruits meet for repentance" (Mt. 3:8; Lk. 3:8), and warned "except ye repent, ye shall all likewise perish" (Lk. 13:3-5). Christ repeatedly said He came to call "sinners to repentance" (Mt. 9:13; Mk. 2:17; Lk. 5:32), and that there was more rejoicing in Heaven over one sinner who repented than over ninety-nine just persons who needed no repentance (Lk. 15:1-7).\n\n**Since the Cross:** Christ again commanded the disciples "that repentance and remission of sins should be preached in his name among all nations, beginning at Jerusalem" (Lk. 24:46-48). At Pentecost, Peter commanded Jews and Gentile proselytes to "Repent, and be baptized every one of you [not just the Jews] in the name of Jesus Christ for [because of] the remission of sins, and ye shall receive the gift of the Holy Ghost. For [because] the promise is unto you [Jews and Gentiles of that generation], and to your children [Jews and Gentiles of the new generation], and to all that are afar off [Jews and Gentiles of all generations], even as many as the Lord our God shall call" (Acts 2:38-39).\n\nIn other sermons since the cross, Peter demanded that people repent (Acts 3:19; 5:31; 8:22). The entire group of apostles recognized that God required Gentiles to repent in order to be saved (Acts 11:18). Peter, many years later, wrote in his epistles that both Jews and Gentiles had to repent to be saved (2 Pet. 3:9).\n\nPaul taught that both Jews and Gentiles had to repent to be saved (Acts 17:30-31; 20:21; 26:20; Rom. 2:4; 2 Cor. 7:8-10; 2 Tim. 2:25; Heb. 6:1, 6). Jesus commanded people to repent as late as 96 A.D. when the last book of the Bible was written (Rev. 2:5, 16, 21-22; 3:3, 19; 9:20-21; 16:9-11).\n\nPeople in both Testaments were also commanded to confess their sins to be saved (Lev. 5:5; 16:21; 26:40; Prov. 28:13; Rom. 10:9-10; 14:11; 1 Jn. 1:9). People in both Testaments were saved by confessing sins (Ps. 32:5; Neh. 9:2-3; Mt. 2:6; Mk. 1:5; Acts 19:18).\n\nThe use of prayer in getting forgiveness is commanded (Lk. 11:4; Acts 8:22). Prayer brought salvation to people in both Testaments (2 Chron. 7:14; Ps. 51; Lk. 18:10-14; Acts 9:11; 22:16). People are promised salvation through prayer and by calling upon the name of the Lord (Acts 2:21; Rom. 10:9-14). We're also told to seek God in order to find Him (Lk. 12:31; 13:24; Acts 15:17; 17:27; Rom. 2:7; 3:11; Heb. 11:6; Mt. 6:33; 7:7-11; Gal. 2:17; Col. 3:1).\n\nNo one should try to get right with God while refusing to repent, confess, pray, seek, turn from sin, forsake all, and believe the gospel. When you truly turn to God, you do all these things and more. Imagine trying to accomplish all this through just one act of faith and nothing else! To teach that people don't have to do these things in this age — or that Jews must do them while Gentiles don't — is to demonstrate ignorance of the Bible.\n\nGod has the same standards for one person as for another. All people must meet the same conditions to be saved and to stay saved. "For there is no difference between the Jew and the Greek: for the same Lord over all is rich unto all that call upon him. For whosoever shall call upon the name of the Lord shall be saved" (Rom. 10:9-13).\n\nBefore the law, Abraham was saved by faith and grace. During the law, David and others were saved the same way (Rom. 4:1-8). Since the law, people are saved in the same way — by faith and grace (Eph. 2:1-10).\n\nConfession of sins and true repentance includes prayer, turning away from sin, forsaking sin, turning to God with godly sorrow, seeking God, asking God, believing God, and having faith in the atonement of Christ. All of this is necessary to be saved from sin.\n\n### 2. Believe That God Has Forgiven You\n\nThe second thing you must do to be saved — after you know you're a sinner and have confessed your sins to God — is to believe that God has forgiven you.\n\n"Without faith it is impossible to please him: for he that cometh to God must believe that he is, and that he is a rewarder of them that diligently seek him" (Heb. 11:6). "For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: not of works, lest any man should boast" (Eph. 2:8-9). "For God so loved the world that he gave his only begotten Son, that whosoever believeth in him should not perish, but have everlasting life" (Jn. 3:16).\n\nGod has promised to forgive if you confess. Regardless of your feelings, you must believe that you are forgiven (1 Jn. 1:9). It must be faith in the right thing — the blood of Jesus (Rom. 3:24-25).\n\nFaith in water baptism, church membership, or religious rituals will never save your soul. It must be faith in the sacrifice of Christ and in Jesus as your personal Saviour. Nothing else will bring peace to your soul or give you any assurance of eternal life.\n\nThink of it like this: a check is worthless unless it's drawn on a real account with actual funds. Your faith has to be "drawn on" the right account — the finished work of Christ — or it can't deliver what you need.\n\nThis faith must come from your heart, not just be mental agreement with a fact. It must be a faith of surrender to the whole will of God. Paul said, "That if thou shalt confess with thy mouth the Lord Jesus, and shalt believe in thine heart that God hath raised him from the dead, thou shalt be saved. For with the heart man believeth unto righteousness; and with the mouth confession is made unto salvation" (Rom. 10:9-13). This one passage states both necessary things you must do to be saved — confess sins (or repent) and believe the gospel.\n\nWhen you believe from the heart, you give up everything for Christ and wholeheartedly serve God and righteousness. You renounce sin and everything that would hinder your new life of faith.\n\nJesus said, "If any man come to me, and hate not his father, and mother, and wife, and children, and brethren, and sisters, yea, and his own life also he cannot be my disciple . . . So likewise, whosoever he be of you that forsaketh not all that he hath, he cannot be my disciple" (Lk. 14:26, 33).\n\nThe word "hate" here is a Greek idiom of preference. The idea is that unless you prefer Christ above everything else in life, you cannot be saved. This is the same as the first commandment — loving the Lord God with all your heart, all your soul, all your mind, and all your strength (Mt. 22:37).\n\n## III. How to Get Healed\n\nThe same two things necessary to save your soul are necessary to heal your body. You must recognize the sickness and its source, then pray and ask God to heal, and then believe that it's done.\n\nFaith is just as necessary for healing the body as for saving the soul. The law of faith is this: "According to your faith be it unto you" (Mt. 9:29). If you believe you're healed, you're healed. If you doubt or wonder whether you're healed, it won't be done. You cannot be healed without unwavering faith that you are healed.\n\nJames said, "And the prayer of faith shall save the sick, and the Lord shall raise him up; and if he have committed sins, they shall be forgiven him" (Jas. 5:14-16).\n\nIn other words, healing comes on the same basis as forgiveness of sins — the atonement (Mt. 8:17; 1 Pet. 2:24). Jesus had power on Earth to do both, and He did heal when prayer and faith were present on the part of people (Mt. 9:1-7).\n\nSince Christ has been given "All power in heaven and in earth," He certainly has as much power now as when He walked the Earth. If He has such power today, He will freely use it to deliver people from the works of the devil. Paul said, "Jesus Christ the same yesterday, and today, and for ever" (Heb. 13:8).\n\nThe only thing standing between any person's healing and God is that person's own failure to connect with God through Christ. If that's all that stands in the way, it seems like anyone in need would put forth the greatest possible effort to be healed.\n\nGod promises it. Jesus died for it. The Holy Spirit is here to enable you to receive it. Faith in the atonement will bring it. That's the truth in a nutshell.\n\nIf you want healing, first go to the Bible to see if these things are true. If they are — and you find them plainly written in Scripture — then deal with yourself and bring yourself to the terms plainly stated in the promises. There will be no question about the results.\n\nBy this method, you can get rid of both sin and sickness and stay free from them. The same means you use to get rid of these enemies will keep you free from them. Prayer, faith, and complete daily surrender of your life to God will bring the results you desire.\n\nEvery person should thank God daily for forgiveness and healing, and God will honor this daily faith and keep you saved and healed.\n\n## IV. Identification With Christ\n\nThe chief secret of all redemptive benefits is your identification with Christ in His work for fallen humanity. By identification with Christ, we mean your union with Him in all phases of His redemptive work. Everyone who believes in Christ must become identified with Him in every phase of His life, death, burial, resurrection, and manifestation.\n\nBelievers are spoken of as being identified with Christ in:\n\n> 1. His sufferings (Rom. 8:17; 1 Pet. 4:1-2; Jn. 15:20; 16:2-3; Mt. 10:25).\n\n> 2. His work (Mk. 16:20; Jn. 14:12-15; Heb. 2:3-4; Acts 1:1-2; Mt. 28:19-20).\n\n> 3. His crucifixion (Rom. 6:6; 8:12-13; Gal. 2:20; Col. 3:5-17).\n\n> 4. His death (Rom. 6:3-11; 8:17; 1 Pet. 4:1-2; Col. 3:3).\n\n> 5. His burial (Rom. 6:4; Col. 2:12).\n\n> 6. His resurrection (Rom. 6:5; Col. 2:12; 3:1; Eph. 2:5-6).\n\n> 7. His exaltation and glory (Rom. 8:17; Col. 3:4; Eph. 2:5-6).\n\n> 8. His security (Col. 3:1-4; 1 Thess. 4:16-17).\n\nChrist identified Himself with humanity and became sin for us so that we might become the righteousness of God in Christ (2 Cor. 5:21). He became our substitute and died in our place. He died to make us live, to make us well and healthy, to make us strong and victorious over all our enemies, to make us happy and prosperous, and to take us to Heaven to live forever with Himself and God.\n\nIt's similar to how a power of attorney works: when someone grants you power of attorney, you have the legal right to act on their behalf. Christ has granted us His authority — when we're united with Him, we share in everything He accomplished.\n\nUnion with Christ means we are crucified to the world and the world to us (Gal. 2:20; 6:14). There's nothing in us the world wants, and nothing in the world we desire. We are free from sin and Satan, from sickness and failure. We are sons of God with power, capable of representing God and exercising the full power of attorney according to the gospel.\n\nChrist didn't just become sin for us — He became sick for us so that we might be made well. The literal rendering of the Hebrew in Isa. 53:3-5 reads: "He was despised, and forsaken of men; a man of pains [or anguish], and acquainted with sickness: and as one from whom men hide their face he was despised [or scorned]; and we esteemed [or regarded] him not. Surely he hath borne our sicknesses, and carried our pains; yet we did esteem him violently beaten, slain of God, and degraded. But he was slain for our crimes, he was beat to pieces [or maimed] for our guilt; the corrections of our peace was upon him; and with his wounds [or bruises] we are healed."\n\nIn Isa. 53:10, the Hebrew text reads: "Yet it pleased Jehovah to beat him to pieces; he hath made him sick: when thou shalt make his soul an offering for sin."\n\nThese are hard statements to comprehend — it's difficult to understand how any human being could have suffered this much — but it's true. Isaiah further speaks of Christ as being so marred that He was hardly recognizable as a man (Isa. 52:14). The literal translation of this verse is: "Just as many were dumbfounded at Him, for deformed was His appearance so as not to be human, or His figure no more resembled man."\n\nWe have no true conception of how He suffered, taking in His own body the beatings and insults of the mob and the sins and sicknesses of the whole world. Who can be so hard-hearted and ungrateful for His sufferings that they can go on in sin and rebellion against Him?\n\nIt shocks us to know that all these sufferings were heaped upon Him and that it pleased God to cause Him such agonies so that all creation could be redeemed. He was so sinful and sick during this period, so forsaken by God who detested the sins and sicknesses of the whole world concentrated in His body, that He cried, "My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me?"\n\nIf every seeking sinner could see that they died with Christ on the cross — that their sins and sicknesses were borne away at that time — it would be easy to accept both forgiveness and healing from God. Such teaching isn't even a known doctrine to the average church member, much less a reality to their soul. Is it any wonder, then, that so few attain the full benefits for which Christ died?\n\nChrist not only bore sins, sicknesses, and pains, but they were laid upon Him so He could do away with them. His whole person — body, soul, and spirit — was involved in this sacrifice and suffered this agony. Every person was nailed to the cross with Christ, and every sin and sickness and pain that any person ever had or ever will have was put upon Him.\n\nWhen we as believers recognize this fact, believe it, and identify ourselves with Christ, we will receive the benefits made possible by this sacrifice. If He bore our sins, we're no longer required to have them or be bound by them. If He bore our sicknesses, we no longer must suffer them. Satan has no legal right to put any sin or sickness upon us, and we have redemptive rights to reject such works of the devil in our lives.\n\nThe Scriptures are clear that neither sin nor sickness shall have dominion over those who are delivered from sin and filled with God's divine life. Since Satan has been defeated, he cannot put sickness upon us unless we give consent and fail to resist him in faith.\n\nWe are new creations and the Spirit of God dwells in us. If this is true, and if we believe it, the Spirit will quicken our mortal bodies because He dwells in us (Rom. 8:9-13). If Christ put away sin and sickness, we don't have to tolerate them in our lives.\n\nWith all of our sins and sicknesses, we were nailed to the cross. If we cease all struggle, exercise true faith, and reckon that we have already died with Christ, that we are buried with Him, that we were resurrected with Him to walk in newness of life, and that we are raised together with Him and are now sitting together with Him in heavenly places — we will know in reality the power of His life in us.\n\nWe were freed from sin when He was freed from the sins He atoned for on the cross. We were healed with Him when He was healed after taking our sicknesses on the cross. As we identify ourselves with Him in His new freedom from sin and sickness, we are made free from them.\n\nLet's all take our place in Him and identify ourselves with Him in His freedom from both sin and sickness, and fully realize the benefits of Calvary.\n\nIf we have to suffer the sins, pains, and sicknesses that Christ bore in our place, His substitutionary work for us counts for nothing. Through the death of Christ we are made alive. It was our sin that caused Him to die. When death slew Him, it destroyed itself. When sin conquered Him, it was conquered by Him. When disease conquered Him, it was conquered by Him. When He put away our sins, He also put away our diseases. By His stripes we were healed.\n\nWe partake of His righteousness and healing when we accept His work for us. Just as our identification or union with sin, sickness, and Satan brought defeat, so our union with Christ brings victory over these things.\n\nWe were crucified with Christ, and if we believe, we live with Him by resurrected power. He is our perfect Saviour, Healer, Provider, and everything we need in this life and in the one to come.\n\nJust as Christ conquered sin, sickness, and Satan, we can conquer them in Him through proper identification with Him. His victory was our victory. He was victor in our place, and we are victors in Him. We were there when the devil was defeated. We were identified with Him when He rose from the dead as master over all Hell. We are masters of these same powers through Christ.\n\nWe are the ones who must carry on the work of God that Jesus started. There can be no defeat or failure. We are free now if we will only accept freedom in Christ. We have salvation and healing now if we will take it.\n\nWe must refuse to have either sin or sickness. We must take our rightful place in Christ and conquer in His name. We have the power of attorney — all we need to do is use it to get what we want and what is rightfully ours in Christ.\n\nWe have the power of God as sons of God. We have God's Word abiding in us. We have the name of Jesus. We have the Holy Spirit. We have faith, knowledge, and wisdom. We have the righteousness of God and the experience of a true new creature. We have everything we need to get what we want — so let's cooperate with God and the means of grace, and we cannot fail to succeed in what we undertake in Christ.\n\n---\n\n## Questions on Lesson Twenty-four\n\n- What is the first thing to do to get rid of sin and sickness? Why?\n\n- Name a few "musts" of the gospel if one wants its benefits.\n\n- What are the two necessary things to do to get saved and healed?\n\n- Name the things that ultra-grace people claim are not necessary to be saved?\n\n- Can these claims of ultra-grace people be proven by the Bible? What does the Bible teach on these points?\n\n- Is the doctrine of repentance only an Old Testament one? Prove.\n\n- Is there any authority in Scripture for the doctrine that men were required to get salvation in one way before the cross and another way after the cross?\n\n- Is there any authority for the doctrine that Jews get saved differently from Gentiles?\n\n- What does true repentance include? Are all men required to repent to get saved?\n\n- Is there any Scripture that says men are not to repent, pray, confess sins, or seek God in getting saved?\n\n- What were the apostles commanded to teach all nations? Does this include repentance, confession of sins, praying, and seeking God to be saved? Prove.\n\n- What was required of men before the cross in order to get saved?\n\n- What is required of men since the cross in order to get saved?\n\n- Prove that both Jews and Gentiles have to do the same things to be saved?\n\n- Prove from both Testaments that men have to repent, confess sins, pray, seek God, and meet other conditions to be saved.\n\n- Are men saved by feelings or by faith?\n\n- Will faith in anything save the soul? Why?\n\n- What does true heart repentance and faith include?\n\n- Explain the word "hate" in Lk. 14:26.\n\n- What two things are necessary in order to be healed?\n\n- What is the law of faith?\n\n- On what basis do men get healing from God?\n\n- What stands between man's healing and God?\n\n- What should one do who wants healing for the body?\n\n- Define identification with Christ.\n\n- In what aspects are men to identify themselves with Christ?\n\n- Prove from the Bible that Christ not only became sin but also became sick for us.\n\n- How does this give us a basis of claiming healing for the body?\n\n- Should sin or sickness have dominion over saved people? Why?\n\n- If we suffer the things for which Christ died to take from us, does His work prove a failure? Where is the failure? How can we correct this failure?\n\n---\n\nThe steps for getting free from sin and sickness are not complicated — they're just demanding. They require honesty about what you're dealing with, faith in what Christ has already done, and action that demonstrates that faith. So here's the question: Is there a sin or a physical condition you've been living with for months or years that you've never fully worked through these specific steps? What's stopping you from starting today?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk461000tewhjo29y3rda	cmnknk45y000gewhjn5pl0kw9	Lesson 17 — The Dispensation of Law	READING	3094	12	\N	t	The Mosaic Law is the most misunderstood institution in Scripture — misunderstood in two opposite directions simultaneously. Some people treat it as still fully binding on Christians today, picking and choosing which parts to apply. Others dismiss it entirely as irrelevant ancient history. Both get it wrong. The Law was given for a specific people, in a specific dispensation, for a specific purpose — and understanding that purpose is the key to understanding both the Old Testament and the New.\n\nLast time we saw how to access God's provision through prayer and faith. Now we take a major step in the dispensational timeline: Israel receives the Law at Sinai, and a new age of human history begins.\n\nWe call this period "the Dispensation of Law" because of the law God gave to Moses. This law became the guiding framework for faith and daily life throughout this entire era.\n\n![Lesson 17 — The Dispensation of Law](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsSKgmZ4J9p8Osi7f2L3jgeayVJxF6MbNCPdun)\n\n## II. The Length of the Dispensation of Law\n\nThis dispensation stretched from Moses all the way to Christ — or more specifically, from the exodus out of Egypt to John the Baptist's preaching about the kingdom of Heaven (Mt. 11:12-13; Lk. 16:16). It lasted more than 1,718 years, and here's how we can trace that out:\n\n### 1. FROM THE EXODUS FROM EGYPT TO THE ENTRANCE INTO CANAAN WAS OVER 41 YEARS\n\nIsrael camped at Sinai for one year and one month (Num. 10:11-12). After that, they wandered in the wilderness for forty years following their rebellion at Kadesh-barnea — giving us over 41 years total (Num. 14:33-34; 32:13; Deut. 2:7; 8:2-4; 29:5; Josh. 5:6; Acts 7:23-42; 13:18; Heb. 3:9, 17).\n\n### 2. FROM THE ENTRANCE INTO CANAAN TO THE REIGN OF SAUL WAS OVER 520 YEARS\n\nHere's the breakdown:\n\n| Period | Duration |\n|--------|----------|\n| (1) Joshua in Canaan (Josh. 14:7; 24:29) | 30 years |\n| (2) Elders that outlived Joshua (Judg. 2:7–3:7) | ? years |\n| (3) The first servitude (Judg. 3:7-8) | 8 years |\n| (4) Othniel, the first judge (Judg. 3:9-11) | 40 years |\n| (5) The second servitude (Judg. 3:12-14) | 18 years |\n| (6) Ehud, the second judge (Judg. 3:15-30) | 80 years |\n| (7) The third servitude (Judg. 3:31) | ? years |\n| (8) Shamgar, the third judge (Judg. 3:31) | ? years |\n| (9) The fourth servitude (Judg. 4:1-3) | 20 years |\n| (10) Deborah and Barak, the fourth and fifth judges (Judg. 4:4–5:31) | 40 years |\n| (11) The fifth servitude (Judg. 6:1-10) | 7 years |\n| (12) Gideon, the sixth judge (Judg. 6:11–8:32) | 40 years |\n| (13) Abimelech, the seventh judge (Judg. 8:33–9:57) | 3 years |\n| (14) Tola, the eighth judge (Judg. 10:1-2) | 23 years |\n| (15) Jair, the ninth judge (Judg. 10:3-5) | 22 years |\n| (16) The sixth servitude (Judg. 10:6-18) | 18 years |\n| (17) Jephthah, the tenth judge (Judg. 11:1–12:7) | 6 years |\n| (18) Ibzan, the eleventh judge (Judg. 12:8-10) | 7 years |\n| (19) Elon, the twelfth judge (Judg. 12:11-12) | 10 years |\n| (20) Abdon, the thirteenth judge (Judg. 12:13-15) | 8 years |\n| (21) The seventh servitude (Judg. 13:1) | 40 years |\n| (22) Samson, the fourteenth judge (Judg. 13:2–16:31) | 20 years |\n| (23) Civil war (Judg. 17:1–21:25) | ? years |\n| (24) Eli, the fifteenth judge (1 Sam. 4:1; 15-18) | 40 years |\n| (25) Samuel, the sixteenth judge, at least 40 years between Eli and Saul (1 Sam. 4:1, 15-18; 7:2-15; 8:1-5) | 40 years |\n| **TOTAL:** | **520 years** |\n\nYou'll notice that Scripture doesn't give us definite dates for points (2), (7), (8), and (23), so the actual period was likely longer than 520 years. The 450 years mentioned in Acts 13:19-21 "until Samuel the prophet" probably begins with Point (3) and ends with Point (24). This list brings us down to Samuel, leaving those undated periods unspecified.\n\n### 3. FROM SAUL TO THE BABYLONIAN CAPTIVITY WAS 513 YEARS\n\n| Period | Duration |\n|--------|----------|\n| (1) Saul's reign over all Israel (Acts 13:21) | 40 years |\n| (2) David's reign over Judah and all Israel (2 Sam. 5:4) | 40 years |\n| (3) Solomon's reign over all Israel (1 Ki. 11:42) | 40 years |\n| (4) Rehoboam's reign over Judah after the kingdom was divided 1009 b.c. (1 Ki. 14:21) | 17 years |\n| (5) Abijam's reign over Judah (1 Ki. 15:1-2) | 3 years |\n| (6) Asa's reign over Judah (1 Ki. 15:10) | 41 years |\n| (7) Jehoshaphat's reign over Judah (1 Ki. 22:41-42) | 25 years |\n| (8) Jehoram's reign over Judah (2 Ki. 8:16-17) | 8 years |\n| (9) Ahaziah's reign over Judah (2 Ki. 8:25-26) | 1 year |\n| (10) Athaliah's reign over Judah (2 Ki. 11:3) | 6 years |\n| (11) Jehoash's reign over Judah (2 Ki. 12:1) | 40 years |\n| (12) Amaziah's reign over Judah (2 Ki. 14:1-2) | 29 years |\n| (13) Azariah or Uzziah (2 Ki. 15:1-2) | 52 years |\n| (14) Jotham's reign over Judah (2 Ki. 15:32) | 16 years |\n| (15) Ahaz's reign over Judah (2 Ki. 16:1-2) | 16 years |\n| (16) Hezekiah's reign over Judah (2 Ki. 18:1-2, six years before and twenty-three years after the ten-tribe captivity, 749 b.c.) | 29 years |\n| (17) Manasseh's reign over Judah (2 Ki. 21:1) | 55 years |\n| (18) Amon's reign over Judah (2 Ki. 21:19) | 2 years |\n| (19) Josiah's reign over Judah (2 Ki. 22:1) | 31 years |\n| (20) Jehoahaz's reign over Judah (2 Ki. 23:31-33) | 3 months |\n| (21) Jehoiakim's reign over Judah (2 Ki. 23:36) | 11 years |\n| (22) Jehoiakin's reign over Judah (2 Ki. 24:6-16) | 3 months |\n| (23) Zedekiah's reign over Judah (2 Ki. 24:18) | 11 years |\n| **TOTAL:** | **513 years** |\n\nThe 480-year period ending with Solomon's fourth year (1 Ki. 6:1) runs from the entrance into Canaan to that point, but it excludes the 111 years of servitude and the three years of chaos under Abimelech. So the 480 years represents Israel's time as a nation free from foreign oppression and civil conflict.\n\n### 4. FROM THE BABYLONIAN CAPTIVITY TO THE RESTORATION WAS 164 YEARS\n\n| Period | Duration |\n|--------|----------|\n| (1) The Babylonian captivity (Jer. 25:11-12; 29:10) | 70 years |\n| (2) Darius the Mede (Dan. 5:31; 8:3, 20; 9:1-2) | 2 years |\n| (3) Cyrus the Persian (Ezra 1:1-4; 3:8; Is. 44:28; 45:1) | 9 years |\n| (4) Cambyses, the son of Cyrus (Ezra 5:1–6:22) | 7 years |\n| (5) Darius I, of profane history (Ezra 5:1–6:22) | 35 years |\n| (6) Xerxes, the fourth king of Dan. 11:1-3 | 21 years |\n| (7) Artaxerxes (Neh. 2:1) | 20 years |\n| **TOTAL:** | **164 years** |\n\n(The dates for the Medo-Persian kings were taken from the Encyclopedia Britannica.)\n\n### 5. FROM THE RESTORATION TO CHRIST WAS 483 YEARS\n\nIn Dan. 9:24-27, we find a prophecy about Seventy Weeks. Each "Week" represents seven years, as we can see from the last half of the Seventieth Week being 3½ years (Dan. 12:7; Rev. 12:6, 14; 13:5). This makes the entire Seventy Weeks equal to 490 years — or 483 years to the crucifixion of Christ. (We'll dive deeper into these Seventy Weeks in Lesson Forty.)\n\nThis 483-year period begins with the command to restore Jerusalem and ends with the Messiah's crucifixion. It includes the Seven Weeks and the 62 Weeks of Dan. 9:24-26, but not the one Week of Dan. 9:27.\n\nThe Seven Weeks (49 years) covered the restoration of the temple and city — starting with the command to rebuild and ending when the work was complete. The 62 Weeks (434 years) began immediately after the city's completion and ended with the Messiah's crucifixion. The remaining one Week awaits fulfillment in the future, when Israel will once again be a nation in full control of Palestine.\n\n### The Grand Total\n\nWhen we add up all five periods, we get 1,721 years or more. But since this age really ended with John the Baptist's ministry (Mt. 11:11-13; Lk. 16:16), we subtract Christ's three-year ministry, leaving us with 1,718 years.\n\nThis brings our total from Adam to Christ to 4,234 years or more. Add the 1,915 years since the crucifixion, and we reach 6,143 years from Adam to 1949. Factor in the years from 1949 to the Antichrist's revelation, the seven years from his revelation to the Millennium, and the Millennium itself — and we arrive at a grand total of 7,149 years or more from Adam to the end of the Millennium. What happens after the Millennium? We'll explore that in Lessons Forty-nine through Fifty-two.\n\n## III. The Favorable Beginning of this Dispensation\n\nNever did fallen humanity have a better starting point than Israel did at the dawn of this dispensation. Think about what they had witnessed! They saw God's mighty power through signs and wonders in Egypt, during the exodus, and throughout forty-plus years in the wilderness. No people in history had ever experienced such miracles or such displays of God's power.\n\nGod spoke the law to them in an audible voice (Deut. 5:22-24). He was visibly present with them — a pillar of fire by night and a cloud by day (Exod. 14:19-31). He supernaturally provided bread and kept their clothes from wearing out for forty years (Deut. 29:5; Exod. 16:35).\n\nHe gave them quail and water whenever they needed it (Exod. 16:12; 17:6). He removed sickness from among them — not a single weak person could be found in all their tribes (Exod. 15:26; 23:25; Ps. 105:37; 107:20). He gave them the riches of Egypt as payment for their long years of slavery (Ps. 105:37; Exod. 12:36).\n\nHe gave them revelations, made personal appearances, and led them to victory over every enemy (Exod. 24:9-11; Deut. 4:6-8; 5:1-6; Josh. 5:13-15). He brought them into a land flowing with milk and honey and promised prosperity and protection if they would serve Him (Num. 13:27; Exod. 23:20-33).\n\nHe gave them a complete legal code covering every aspect of human life. He made new eternal covenants with them and gave promises of blessing that addressed every human need — in this life and the next. They had the old covenants and promises about God's eternal purpose on Earth with them. They had rich spiritual experiences of the gospel, as we'll see in our next lesson.\n\nWhat more could they have asked for? What better beginning could there possibly have been?\n\n## IV. The Test for Man in This Dispensation\n\nThe test for people in this dispensation was straightforward: obey the Law of Moses in every detail. This law included three main categories:\n\n> 1. **THE COMMANDMENTS** (Exod. 20:1-17; Deut. 5:1-21). These express the heart of God's holy will for every area of human life. They summarize all of God's laws, judgments, and ordinances.\n\n> 2. **THE JUDGMENTS** (Exod. 21:1–23:33). These spell out how people should relate to one another in daily life. They're the detailed social regulations that flow from the Ten Commandments.\n\n> 3. **THE ORDINANCES** (Exod. 24:12–31:18). These outline the religious life and worship of God's people. They're the detailed religious regulations that expand on the Ten Commandments.\n\n## V. The Purpose of God in This Dispensation\n\nEvery previous dispensational test had proven humanity a failure — utterly incapable of doing God's will on their own. So in this new age, God purposed to give people His will in complete detail. Think of it like a parent who, after watching a child repeatedly make poor decisions, finally sits down and spells out exactly what they expect — leaving nothing to guesswork.\n\nGod planned to organize a commonwealth of nations with Israel at the head, governed by His laws and led by people He personally chose. He established a visible worship system and a place where His glory could shine continually — a place where He could maintain ongoing contact with humanity (Exod. 19:3-7; Deut. 28).\n\nHe also purposed to use Israel to destroy the second generation of giants — those offspring of the sons of God and daughters of men born after the flood — and to defeat the devil through the coming Messiah from Israel's line.\n\nGod intended to bless Israel so abundantly that they would become a living example to all nations. It's similar to how a successful business becomes a model for others: "Look what's possible when you do things right!" God wanted the nations to see Israel's prosperity and be drawn to turn from their idols to serve the true Living God. His purpose in other respects remained the same as in the Dispensation of Promise (See Lesson Fifteen, Point V).\n\n### God Speaking Through Prophets\n\nThis dispensation marked the beginning of God speaking to people through prophets more than ever before (Heb. 1:1-3). Before this time, God Himself would come down to talk and personally intervene in human affairs — especially at the ending of one age and the beginning of another.\n\nDuring the Dispensation of Innocence, God came and fellowshipped with humanity in the cool of the day. When humanity fell, God appeared personally to deal with them, driving them from the Garden and cursing the Earth. He started them under conscience with new conditions and responsibilities.\n\nHumanity continued in the Dispensation of Conscience until wickedness grew so extreme that God came down again to bring judgment. He personally dealt with and spoke to people at the beginning and end of both Innocence and Conscience (Gen. 2:15-25; 3:1-24; 4:1-16; 6:9-22; 7:1-5); of Human Government (Gen. 8:15–9:17; 11:1-9); and of Promise (Gen. 12:1-4; 13:14-18; 15:1-21; 17:1-22; 18:1-33; 22:1; 26:2-5, 24-25; 31:3; 35:1).\n\nThere was a long period during the Dispensation of Promise when God's voice wasn't heard. Then at that period's end, He revealed Himself again (Exod. 2:23–4:31). From that point through the Dispensation of Law, He sent scores of prophets to His chosen people — until 400 years before Christ. After those 400 silent years, He began speaking through a prophet-forerunner and through His own Son (Mt. 3:1-17; 17:1-5; Heb. 1:1-3).\n\nAfter the Son returned to Heaven, God spoke through His apostles and preachers, giving them gifts of the Holy Spirit according to His will (1 Cor. 1:18-31; 12:1-28; Eph. 4:11-16; Heb. 2:1-4). This remains God's program throughout the Age of Grace.\n\nAt the end of this Age of Grace, God will once again deal personally with the human race, sending His Son from heaven to establish an earthly kingdom, put down all rebellion, and rule the Earth in righteousness (Mt. 24:1–25:46; Lk. 21; 1 Cor. 15:24-28; 2 Thess. 1:7-10; 2:1-12; Jude 14; Rev. 19:11-21; 20:1-15). Grace began with the Son's coming and will end the same way (Mt. 1-2; Rev. 19:11-21). Similarly, the Millennium will begin with war and end with war (Rev. 19:11-21; 20:1-10).\n\nSo we see that God is the Great Overseer of all His creation, guiding it toward a desired end — until the universe is cleansed of all rebellion and righteousness reigns forever (1 Cor. 15:24-28; Eph. 1:10; Rev. 21-22).\n\n### God's Purpose in Giving the Law of Moses\n\n> 1. God's purpose was for the Law of Moses to govern Israel until the Messiah came. It "prophesied until John" (Mt. 11:11-13; Lk. 16:16-17).\n\n> 2. It stopped every mouth and made the whole world guilty before God, so He could have mercy on everyone alike: "Now we know that what things soever the law saith, it saith to them that are under the law: that every mouth may be stopped, and all the world become guilty before God" (Rom. 3:19-20).\n\n> 3. It brought people to a knowledge of sin: "Therefore by the deeds of the law there shall no flesh be justified in his sight: for by the law is the knowledge of sin . . . The law worketh wrath: for where no law is there is no transgression sin is not imputed where there is no law . . . Moreover the law entered that the offense might abound" (Rom. 3:19-20; 4:15; 5:13, 20; 7:5, 7-14).\n\n> 4. "It was added because of transgressions, till the seed should come . . . The law was our schoolmaster to bring us unto Christ, that we might be justified by faith" (Gal. 3:12-13, 19-24).\n\n> 5. The law brought bondage: "These are the two covenants; the one from Mount Sinai which gendereth to bondage" (Gal. 4:24-31; 5:1).\n\n> 6. The law was a "shadow of good things to come [imposed upon men] until the time of the reformation" (Col. 2:14-17; Heb. 8:5; 9:1-10, 24; 10:1).\n\n### The Fulfillment of the Law and Why it Was Done Away in Christ\n\nGiven God's purposes in giving the law, it makes perfect sense that the law would be set aside once Christ fulfilled it and it had served its purpose. Think of it like scaffolding on a building — essential during construction, but removed once the building is complete.\n\nThat there was a change in God's law to humanity after Christ came, and that He fulfilled the law, isn't just implied in the passages above — it's stated directly:\n\n"I am come to fulfill the law . . . one jot or tittle shall in no wise pass from the law, till all be fulfilled" (Mt. 5:17-18). "The law prophesied until John" (Lk. 16:16). "All things must be fulfilled, which was spoken in the law of Moses" (Lk. 24:44). "For the priesthood being changed, there is made of necessity a change also of the law" (Heb. 7:12). "The first covenant . . . was a figure for the time then present . . . imposed on them until the time of the reformation" (Heb. 9:1, 9, 10). "Cast out the bondwoman and her son" (an allegory teaching the law was to be done away, Gal. 4:24-31). "Christ is the end of the law for righteousness to everyone that believeth" (no one is made righteous by keeping it, Rom. 3:19-31; 8:3; 10:4; Gal. 2:16-21; 5:4; Eph. 2:15; Col. 2:14-17; Acts 13:39; 15:5-29). "He taketh away the first, that he may establish the second" (Heb. 10:1-9).\n\nThe word "fulfill" means "completed," "brought to an end," "expired," and "finished" — in the same sense that many prophecies were fulfilled in Christ. Because they're fulfilled, they're no longer in force. They've served their purpose. See how "fulfill" is used in Mt. 1:22; 2:15, 17, 23; 4:14; 8:17; 12:17; 13:35; 21:4; 26:54, 56; 27:9, 35; and elsewhere.\n\nThree times in 2 Cor. 3:6-18, Paul states that the Old Covenant (the Law of Moses) was done away. Once he says it was abolished, and that the New Covenant has taken its place. What specifically was done away and abolished? "The ministration of death written and engraven in stones" — the Ten Commandments, since they were the only part of the law written on stones.\n\nNow, we're not obligated to keep the Ten Commandments of the Old Covenant. We're obligated to keep only those commandments that were brought into and made part of the New Covenant. You can find nine of the ten in the New Covenant — all except the fourth commandment about the sabbath.\n\nWhy wasn't the sabbath included? Because it was a special day commemorating Israel's deliverance from Egyptian bondage (Deut. 5:15). It was a day of rest specifically for Israel and for Gentiles who became part of that nation (Exod. 31:12-18; Ezek. 20:12-20).\n\nSince Gentiles and Christians weren't delivered from Egyptian bondage, it doesn't make sense for us to commemorate that day as Israel was commanded to do. Christians are freed by the New Covenant from the Jewish sabbath and are free to observe any day (Rom. 14:5-6; Gal. 4:9-10; Col. 2:14-17). Early Christians observed the day of Christ's resurrection to celebrate their deliverance from slavery to sin and Satan (Jn. 20:1, 19, 26; Acts 20:7-11; 1 Cor. 16:1-2).\n\n### What Was Wrong With the Law of Moses That it Needed Changing?\n\n> 1. It had a sinful mediator (Gal. 3:10).\n\n> 2. It could not give eternal inheritance (Rom. 4:13-25; Gal. 3:18).\n\n> 3. It was dedicated by the blood of animals (Exod. 24:6-8; Heb. 9:16-22).\n\n> 4. It brought condemnation, not life (Rom. 3:19-20; 4:15; 7:1-25; 8:3-4; Gal. 3:21).\n\n> 5. It could not justify anyone (Acts 13:39; Gal. 2:16-21).\n\n> 6. It could only benefit when kept perfectly, but it was impossible for anyone to keep perfectly on their own (Rom. 2:25; 7:7-25; 8:7; Gal. 3:10-25).\n\n> 7. It brought everyone under a curse and executed them (Gal. 3:10-14).\n\n> 8. It was not based on faith or grace (Gal. 3:11; Jn. 1:17).\n\n> 9. It was a law of works and bondage (Rom. 3:26-31; Gal. 3:10-12; 4:21-31).\n\n> 10. It gave knowledge of sin and made people sinners, but it couldn't save them from their sins (Rom. 3:19-20; 4:15; 7:7-25; 8:3-4).\n\n> 11. It brought wrath instead of blessing (Rom. 4:15).\n\n> 12. It wasn't the same as the gospel given to Abraham (Rom. 4:3-16; Gal. 3:6-18).\n\n> 13. It was only a temporary law (Gal. 3:19-25; 2 Cor. 3:6-18; Eph. 2:15; Col. 2:14-17; Heb. 7:16).\n\n> 14. It could never make anyone perfect (Heb. 7:11-12, 18-19; 9:9-10; 10:1-18).\n\n> 15. It was set aside because of its weakness and lack of usefulness (Heb. 7:18).\n\n> 16. It could not redeem or make people dead to sin (Rom. 8:1-4; Gal. 2:16-21; 3:13-21), but it could bring death and condemnation (Rom. 7:7-25; 2 Cor. 3:6-18).\n\n> 17. It was not established by an oath, and neither were its priests — therefore both the law and priesthood could be changed (Heb. 7:12, 20-22).\n\n> 18. It wasn't as good as the New Covenant (Heb. 7:22).\n\n> 19. It had a constantly changing priesthood, while the New Covenant does not (Heb. 7:11-28).\n\n> 20. It had sinful priests, while the New Covenant does not (Heb. 5:1-4; 7:26-28; 9:7).\n\n> 21. It had a temporary, shadowy sanctuary (Heb. 8:1-5, 13; 9:9-25; 10:1-18).\n\n> 22. It had a flawed ministry and a weaker foundation than the New Covenant (Heb. 8:6; 7:11-28).\n\n> 23. It demanded righteousness but couldn't produce it (Rom. 4; 8:1-4; Gal. 3).\n\n> 24. It was faulty (Heb. 8:7, 13), while the New Covenant is not (Jas. 1:25).\n\n> 25. It kept people away from God (Heb. 9:7-8; 10:19-22).\n\n> 26. It was made necessary by sin and was added and imposed until Christ should come (Gal. 3:19-25; Heb. 9:9-10, 23-28; 10:5-18).\n\n> 27. It had animal sacrifices that had to be offered daily, while the New Covenant has Christ as a once-for-all sacrifice (Heb. 9:11-28; 10:3-4).\n\n> 28. It was merely a shadow of things to come (Heb. 8:5; 9:11; 10:1-18).\n\n> 29. It could not satisfy God's demands and could not impart the Holy Spirit or produce miracles (Gal. 2:21; 3:1-5; Rom. 8:1-4).\n\n> 30. It was designed to be changed when Christ came (Gal. 2:19-21; 3:19-25; Heb. 7:11-22; 8:5-6; 9:9-10; 10:1-18).\n\n## VI. The Means of God in Accomplishing His Purpose\n\nGod used several means to further His plan during this age: He completed Israel's organization and established them as a nation. He destroyed the giants in the land. He gave Israel complete victory over all their enemies. He manifested His glory and power. And He blessed Israel so abundantly that the surrounding nations could see that God is the true Living God.\n\n## VII. The Failure of Israel in This Dispensation\n\nThe history of Israel during this dispensation reads like one long record of failure: in the wilderness, under Joshua, under the judges, under the kings, during the captivities, in the restoration — and finally reaching its climax in the rejection and crucifixion of their Messiah and the persecution of His followers until God's wrath came upon them in full. Let's trace these failures:\n\n### 1. FAILURES IN THE WILDERNESS FOR FORTY-ONE YEARS\n\n> (1) Murmured for water at Marah (Exod. 15:22-26)\n\n> (2) Murmured for bread and meat (Exod. 16:1-22)\n\n> (3) Murmured for water at Rephidim (Exod. 17:1-7)\n\n> (4) Murmured for flesh the second time (Num. 11)\n\n> (5) Murmured because of the giants (Num. 13:31–14:5)\n\n> (6) Murmured for water the third time (Num. 20:1-12)\n\n> (7) Murmured because of the death of the princes (Num. 16:41)\n\n> (8) Murmured because of the hardships in travel (Num. 21:1-9)\n\n> (9) Leaned on the arm of flesh (Exod. 18:1-27)\n\n> (10) Sinned in worshiping the golden calf (Exod. 32)\n\n> (11) Miriam and Aaron's sin of self-exaltation (Num. 12)\n\n> (12) Refused to enter Canaan through unbelief (Num. 14)\n\n> (13) Gainsaying of Korah (Num. 16; Jude 11)\n\n> (14) Sin of adultery (Num. 25)\n\n> (15) Sin of Moses (Num. 20:1-12; 27:14)\n\n**NOTE:** Eleven different plagues and judgments fell upon Israel in the wilderness for these sins. Nine of them came because of the misuse of the tongue. This is still the cause of most trouble in the human race, as Scripture makes clear (Ps. 34:13; Prov. 6:17; 12:18; 21:23; Mk\n\n---\n\nThe Law did what it was designed to do: it revealed sin, contained a nation, and served as a schoolmaster to bring Israel to Christ. It was never designed to justify — only to expose the need for justification. So here's the honest question: Are you living under the Law or under grace? Not in your theology, but in your actual experience — do you approach God through merit and performance, or through the finished work of Christ? What would change in your daily prayer life if you genuinely believed the Law's demands had been fully met on your behalf?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk461000uewhj7jj31yl8	cmnknk45y000gewhjn5pl0kw9	Lesson 18 — The Old Testament Church	READING	3019	13	\N	t	The Church didn't begin on the Day of Pentecost. That's a widely held assumption — and it creates enormous confusion about what the Church actually is, why Israel matters, and how the Old Testament relates to the New. Scripture makes clear that God has always had a called-out people, a congregation, an *ekklesia* — long before Jesus walked the earth.\n\nLast time we examined the Dispensation of Law and why the Law's purpose was always to lead to Christ. Now we look at the people through whom that Law was given and preserved: the Old Testament assembly — the church that existed before the Church.\n\nBefore we dive into what the Old Testament Church actually is, let's clear away some common misconceptions. Understanding what it's *not* will help us grasp what it truly *is*.\n\n> 1. It is not the New Testament Church of this Age of Grace (Mt. 16:18).\n\n> 2. It is not the Gentiles of any or all past ages.\n\n> 3. It is not the Jews who will become heavenly, glorified saints (Heb. 11).\n\n> 4. It is not the kingdom of Heaven (Mt. 3:2; 4:17).\n\n> 5. It is not the kingdom of God (Mt. 21:43).\n\n![Lesson 18 — The Old Testament Church](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsL0R5q6ZpCzRqFa9lYkMo1NvGIjUrV6tcW2sX)\n\n### II. What the Old Testament Church Is\n\nThe Old Testament Church was made up of living Jews and proselytes during the Old Testament period. The nation of Israel is actually called "the Church in the wilderness" (Acts 7:38).\n\nNow, you might be surprised to see the word "church" used for Israel. The Greek word in this passage is *ekklesia* — the exact same word used for the New Testament Church.\n\nIt means "assembly," "a popular meeting," or "political gathering" — especially a religious congregation of people called out from the mass of humanity. Think of it like this: if you were called out of a crowded stadium to join a special group with a unique purpose, you'd be part of an *ekklesia*.\n\nThe word comes from two Greek roots: *ek* meaning "from" or "out of," and *kaleo* meaning "to call." In the Septuagint (the Greek Old Testament), it's used for the Hebrew *kahal*, from which we get our word "call."\n\nThis word appears 114 times in the New Testament — eighty times in the singular and thirty-four times in the plural. It's translated "assembly" (Acts 19:32, 39, 41); "church" (Mt. 16:18; 18:17; Acts 2:47; 5:11; 7:38; 8:1, 3; 11:22, 26; 12:1, 5; 13:1; 14:23, 27; 15:3, 4, 22; 18:22; 20:17, 28; Rom. 16:1, 5-23; 1 Cor. 1:2; 4:17; 6:4; 10:32; 11:18, 22; 12:28; 14:4, 5, 12, 19, 23, 28, 35; 15:9; 2 Cor. 1:1; Gal. 1:13; Eph. 1:22; 3:10, 21; 5:23-32; Phil. 3:6; 4:15; Col. 1:18, 24; 4:15-16; 1 Thess. 1:1; 2 Thess. 1:1; 1 Tim. 3:5, 15; 5:16; Phm. 2; Heb. 2:12; 12:23; Jas. 5:14; 1 Pet. 5:13; 3 Jn. 6, 9-10; Rev. 2:1, 8, 12, 18; 3:1, 7, 14); and "churches" (Acts 9:31; 15:41; 16:5; 19:37; Rom. 16:4, 16; 1 Cor. 7:17; 11:16; 14:33-34; 16:1, 19; 2 Cor. 8:1, 18-19, 23-24; 11:8, 28; 12:13; Gal. 1:2, 22; 1 Thess. 2:14; 2 Tim. 1:14; Rev. 1:4, 11, 20; 2:7, 11, 17, 23, 29; 3:6; 22:16).\n\nIsrael was a "called-out" nation in Old Testament times, just as Christians today are a "called-out" people from the world. Israel was supposed to be separate from the world and wholly given over to serving God and evangelizing the nations (Gen. 12:1-3).\n\nHere's an important distinction: their calling was *earthly*, but the present Church's calling is *heavenly*. Israel was governed by natural laws and ruled by earthly men who were accountable to God for their leadership. They were promised an everlasting possession of the land.\n\nThey had the covenants, the promises, the callings, and the service of God — and they were supposed to bring the knowledge of God to all nations (Rom. 2:17-29; 9:4-7; 11:25-29).\n\nSadly, Israel failed in every century from her calling to the present day. God sent prophets and teachers time after time, urging them to fulfill their calling, but they rebelled more and more. Eventually, they had to be scattered among the nations.\n\nThey won't be fully gathered and made a blessing to all nations until their rejected Messiah returns in glory (Mt. 24:29-31; Rom. 11:25-29; Isa. 11:10-15).\n\nThe Old Testament Church was — and will continue to be — the earthly nation of Israel with an earthly calling forever. It was made up of living Jews and proselytes in Old Testament days, and it will be again in the future.\n\nThis company has always been made up of living earthly people, and it always will be. Those who have died are no longer members by virtue of their death. They will be resurrected to become part of the heavenly family of God and the bride of Christ (Heb. 11; 13:14; Eph. 3:15; Rev. 21:9).\n\n### III. The Calling and Purpose of the Old Testament Church\n\nAs we noted, the Hebrew *kahal* carries the same meaning as the Greek *ekklesia*. It's used 123 times in the Old Testament and is translated "congregation" eighty-six times, "assembly" seventeen times, "company" seventeen times, and "multitude" three times.\n\nIsrael was called out of all nations to accomplish specific things in God's eternal earthly plan:\n\n> 1. To remain separate from all nations (Gen. 12:1-3; Exod. 19:5-6).\n\n> 2. To evangelize the nations (Gen. 12:1-3; Mt. 21:33-46; Rom. 9:4-5; 11:29).\n\n> 3. To write, preserve, and transmit the Holy Scriptures (Jn. 1:16-17; Rom. 9:4-5; 1 Pet. 1:10-12; 2 Pet. 1:19-21).\n\n> 4. To bring the Savior into the world (Gen. 49:10; Deut. 18:15-19; 2 Sam. 7; Isa. 9:6-7; Mic. 5:1-2; Lk. 1:32-35; Rom. 9:4-5; Heb. 2).\n\n> 5. To save the world from physical and moral decay (Gen. 12:1-3; Exod. 20:1-19; Rom. 9:4-5; 2 Pet. 2:9).\n\n> 6. To head the nations of the Earth in all ages under the government of their Messiah (Deut. 28:13; Isa. 9:6-7; Dan. 7:13-14; Lk. 1:32; Rom. 11:29). See Lesson Fifteen, Point V, for other reasons for Israel's calling.\n\n### IV. The History of the Old Testament Church\n\nThe history of the Jews began about 4,100 years ago with the call of Abraham. For all these years, they have remained distinct and separate from all peoples, maintaining their own laws and customs.\n\nThey have produced some of the greatest men in human history. Their faithfulness to their calling has been temporary and local, but in the Millennium and future ages, it will be permanent and worldwide.\n\nA brief history of Israel is recorded in Lesson Fifteen, Point VII, and Lesson Seventeen, Point VII. Practically all the important events of their history are in the Bible.\n\nYou can read about the origin of Israel in Genesis; the establishment of the theocracy in the other books of the Pentateuch; the entrance into Canaan in Joshua; the period of the judges in Judges; the monarchy and its development in 1 and 2 Samuel and 1 Kings; the division of the kingdom and the history of the two kingdoms down to the captivities in Kings and Chronicles; and the return from captivity in Ezra, Nehemiah, Haggai, Zechariah, and Malachi.\n\nFrom there until Christ, we have only scattered dates — except for the second century B.C., which is partially recorded in 1 and 2 Maccabees. The prophetic books provide much historical data from the reign of Amaziah (about 884 B.C.) to the return from captivity and the restoration (about 400 B.C.). Much of Israel's history during this period has been illuminated by monuments recently found in Palestine, Egypt, Babylon, Assyria, and other countries.\n\nThe history of Israel from Abraham to our day reveals continued oppression because of her sins. During this time, she has undergone numerous sieges, servitudes, and captivities.\n\nThe history of Jerusalem, her capital city, is especially striking. It has undergone twenty-eight sieges:\n\n> 1. By the tribe of Judah against the Jebusites about 700 years before the founding of Rome (Judg. 1:8).\n\n> 2. By David against the Jebusites (2 Sam. 5:6-10; 1 Chron. 11:4-7).\n\n> 3. By Shishak, king of Egypt, against Judah (1 Ki. 14:25; 2 Chron. 12).\n\n> 4. By the Philistines, Arabians, and Ethiopians against Judah (2 Chron. 21:16-17).\n\n> 5. By Israel against Judah (2 Ki. 18:13–19:37).\n\n> 6. By Syria and Israel against Judah (1 Chron. 28).\n\n> 7. By Assyria against Judah (2 Ki. 18:13–19:37).\n\n> 8. By Babylon against Judah (2 Chron. 36:6-7).\n\n> 9. By Babylon against Judah (2 Chron. 36:10).\n\n> 10. By Babylon against Judah (2 Chron. 36:17-20).\n\n> 11. By Egypt against Judah after the captivities, 320 B.C.\n\n> 12. By Scopus, a general of Alexander, about 299 B.C.\n\n> 13. By Syria against Judah after the captivities, 203 B.C.\n\n> 14. By Syria against Judah, 168 B.C.\n\n> 15. By Syria against Judah, 162 B.C.\n\n> 16. By Syria against Judah, 135 B.C.\n\n> 17. By Hyrcanus, the priest of Aristobulus, 63 B.C.\n\n> 18. By Pompey against Aristobulus, 63 B.C.\n\n> 19. By Herod with a Roman army, who besieged the city in 39 B.C.\n\n> 20. By Titus in 70 A.D. (Mt. 24:1-2; Lk. 19:42-44; 21:20-24).\n\n> 21. By the Romans again in 135 A.D. against the false messiah, Bar-Cochebas, who had acquired possession of the ruins. The city was obliterated, renamed Elia Capitolina, and a temple to Jupiter was erected.\n\n> 22. After 400 years of so-called Christian colonization, Chrosroes the Persian (about 559 A.D.) swept through the country; thousands were massacred, and the Church of the Holy Sepulchre was destroyed. The emperor Heraclius afterwards defeated him and restored the city and the church.\n\n> 23. The Caliph Omar, in 636-637 A.D., besieged the city against Heraclius. It passed into the hands of the Turks, in whose hands it remained until 1917 A.D.\n\n> 24. Afdal, the Vizier of the Caliph of Egypt, defeated the Muslims in 1098 A.D. and pillaged the city.\n\n> 25. In 1099 it was besieged by the army of the First Crusade.\n\n> 26. In 1187 it was besieged by Saladin for seven weeks.\n\n> 27. The wild Tartar hordes in 1244 captured and plundered the city, slaughtering the monks and priests.\n\n> 28. It was taken by General Allenby and the British in 1917 A.D. At this writing (1949), there is war between the Jews and Arabs over control of the city.\n\nThe history of Israel from the rejection of their Messiah to our day reveals terrible sufferings.\n\nBeginning about 50 A.D., 30,000 were killed by the Romans. In 66 A.D., Titus with a Roman army of 66,000 met Josephus and killed 40,000 Jews and subjugated Galilee.\n\nIn 70 A.D., Titus with 100,000 men captured Jerusalem after four months. One million were killed, 97,000 were captured, and the city was destroyed — fulfilling Dan. 9:26; Mt. 24:1-2; Lk. 21:20-24.\n\nIn 135 A.D., the Jews revolted again. After a war of three-and-a-half years, 580,000 were killed, the land was made utterly desolate, a plow was run over Zion (Mic. 3:12), and Jews were forbidden to enter the land. Since then, Jews have been few in the land until recent years.\n\nFrom 140 to 312 A.D., the Jews flourished in Rome with imperial favor and played a significant part in the persecutions of Christians. When Constantine became Emperor, they lost imperial favor and became a condemned sect.\n\nIn England in 1020 A.D., Canute banished them from the country. In 1096 A.D., war was waged against Jews who would not submit to baptism.\n\nHenry II made them pay $291,000.00 to help the crusades. In 1189 A.D., murderous riots broke out against the Jews, and every Jewish home in London was plundered.\n\nIn 1190 A.D., the chief rabbi of York and 500 Jews were besieged until they slew one another. In 1272 A.D., Edward I claimed to own the Jews and all they possessed, letting them get rich only to rob them. He finally drove them from England, and for four centuries no Jew was found there.\n\nIn France in 1306 A.D., 100,000 Jews were stripped, robbed, and banished. In ten years they were allowed to return, but fanatical shepherds killed thousands. In 1683 A.D., they were again ordered to leave France and her colonies and weren't permitted to own real estate there until 1723.\n\nIn 1348-1350 A.D., the black plague swept Europe but affected Jews much less because of their simple life and the hygienic requirements of the law. They were suspected of poisoning Europe's waters.\n\nIn Germany, thousands were killed by cauldron, fire, and many other means. They were banished from Prague and Constantinople.\n\nIn Spain during the Inquisition, thousands disappeared never to return. They were finally banished from Spain. Many were sold into slavery. Their sufferings were indescribable, and for 200 years they were not permitted to enter Spain.\n\n### V. The Present State of the Old Testament Church\n\nAt present, the Jews enjoy relative peace, except in many parts of Europe and Asia where they are again being persecuted, killed, and used as slaves. They are looking forward to a homeland of their own again.\n\nTheir ambition will be partially realized before they face another period of persecution in their land under the Antichrist. In fact, even now the Jews have a nation of their own again with about 700,000 population.\n\nThey have recently fought a war with all the Arab states — Egypt, Syria, TransJordan, Iraq, Iran, and others — and have won the respect of many nations. Various nations are now establishing diplomatic relations with the new state.\n\nMany thousands of Jews are returning to the land of their fathers. But their final restoration awaits the return of their Messiah in glory to deliver them from the Gentiles and set up an eternal kingdom over them, as predicted by the prophets.\n\n### VI. The Future of the Old Testament Church\n\nWhen it comes to the future of the Jews, God's Word speaks in remarkable detail. Nothing is clearer in the Bible than this subject. Let's look at the key facts:\n\n**1. THE FACT OF THEIR RESTORATION.** This is called the "second time to recover the remnant of his people."\n\nThe first time was the restoration of Israel after the Babylonian captivity, as in the days of Ezra and Nehemiah. Since that first restoration, Israel became a nation again, then rejected their Messiah and crucified Him. Because of this, they were led away captive into all nations, and Jerusalem has been "trodden down of the Gentiles," just as Jesus predicted in Lk. 21:20-24.\n\nThe Scriptures are so clear on Israel's future restoration that we'll simply quote a few of the many hundreds of passages revealing this truth.\n\nIsaiah said, "It shall come to pass in that day [the Millennium], that the Lord shall set his hand again the second time to recover the remnant of his people . . . he shall set up an ensign for the nations, and shall assemble the outcasts of Israel, and gather the dispersed of Judah from the four corners of the earth" (Isa. 11:10-16).\n\n"Fear not [Israel]: for I am with thee: I will bring thy seed from the east, and gather thee from the west, I will say to the north, Give up; and to the south, Keep not back: bring my sons from far, and my daughters from the ends of the earth" (Isa. 43:1-7).\n\nJeremiah foretold that the days will come "that it shall no more be said, The Lord liveth, that brought up the children of Israel out of the land of Egypt; But, The Lord liveth, that brought up the children of Israel from the north, and from all lands whither I have driven them: and I will bring them again into their land that I gave unto their fathers" (Jer. 16:14-15).\n\n"I will save thee from afar, and thy seed from the land of their captivity; and Jacob shall return, and shall be in rest, and be quiet, and none shall make him afraid . . . though I make an end of all nations whither I have scattered thee, yet will I not make a full end of thee: but I will correct thee in measure, and will not leave thee altogether unpunished . . . I will punish all that oppress them . . . Ye shall be my people, and I will be your God . . . In the latter days ye shall consider it. And at that time . . . I will build thee . . . I will bring them from the north country, and gather them from the coasts of the earth . . . He that scattered Israel will gather him, and keep him, as a shepherd doth a flock . . . they shall come and sing in the height of Zion" (Jer. 30:10-24; 31:1-40; 32:37-44; 33:7-26).\n\nEzekiel also prophesied of Israel's final gathering: "I will even gather you from the people, and assemble you out of the countries where you have been scattered, and I will give you the land of Israel" (Ezek. 11:17-21).\n\n"As I live, saith the Lord God, . . . I will bring you out from the people and will gather you out of the countries wherein ye are scattered . . . I will bring you into the land of Israel" (Ezek. 20:33-44).\n\n"When I shall have gathered the house of Israel from the people among whom they are scattered . . . then shall they dwell in their land" (Ezek. 28:25-26; 34:11-31; 36:16-38).\n\n"I will take the children of Israel from among the heathen . . . and bring them into their own land. And I will make them one nation in the land upon the mountains of Israel; and one king shall be king over them all: and they shall be no more two nations, neither shall they be divided into two kingdoms any more at all . . . my sanctuary shall be in the midst of them for evermore" (Ezek. 37:1-28; 47:13–48:35).\n\nOther Old Testament prophets speak of this final regathering:\n\n"The children of Israel shall abide many days without a king, and without a prince, and without a sacrifice, and without an image, and without an ephod, and without teraphim: Afterward shall the children of Israel return, and seek the Lord their God, and David their king; and shall fear the Lord and his goodness in the latter days" (Hos. 3:4-5).\n\n"For, behold, in those days, and in that time, when I shall bring again the captivity of Judah and Jerusalem . . . the Lord will be the hope of his people, and the strength of the children of Israel . . . Judah shall dwell for ever, and Jerusalem from generation to generation" (Joel 3. See also Amos 9:9-15; Obadiah 15-21; Mic. 4:1-13; 5:3-15; 7:7-20; Zeph. 3:8-20; Zech. 8:1-8, 20-23; 12:1-14; 13:1-9; 14:1-21; Mal. 4:5-6).\n\nJesus predicted that Israel will be finally gathered at His second advent (Mt. 24:29-31), and the apostles taught that God would build again the house of David after the Church age (Acts 15:13-18; Rom. 11:25-29).\n\nSo it's clear from both Testaments that Israel is yet to be regathered, converted, and ruled by their Messiah forever (Lk. 1:32-35). The reason they are now scattered is their rebellion against God (Lev. 26; Deut. 4:22-28; 28:1-68; Judg. 2:11-19; 1 Sam. 8:6; 2 Ki. 21:11; 25:3; Jer. 15:4; Mt. 23:37-39; 27:25).\n\n**2. THE TIME OF THEIR RESTORATION.** Israel's partial restoration will come before the Antichrist reigns, but their final restoration will occur at the close of "the times of the Gentiles" at Christ's second coming (Lk. 21:24; Mt. 23:38-39; 24:27-31; Zech. 12:10–14:21; Isa. 11:10-11).\n\n**3. THE MANNER OF THEIR RESTORATION.** They will be gathered back unconverted (Ezek. 36:24-27). Before their conversion, they will go through the greatest period of trouble they have ever experienced — the great tribulation — which will bring them back to their God.\n\nAfter their restoration and conversion to their Messiah, they will have a most glorious future. They will become the richest and most powerful nation on Earth.\n\nThe land of Palestine is already rich in natural resources and coveted by many nations. This partially explains why Palestine will become the center of many wars just before Christ's second coming.\n\nWho among the nations wouldn't want the mineral wealth of the Dead Sea alone? According to the British Government, it's estimated to be worth about $1.19 trillion — nearly three times the total wealth of the United States!\n\nHere's the fascinating breakdown: The Dead Sea covers 340 square miles, with its surface 1,292 feet below Mediterranean Sea level. It contains potash amounting to 1.3 billion tons, valued at $68.040 billion; bromide 835 million tons, valued at $252.72 billion; salt 11.9 billion tons, valued at $46.17 billion; gypsum 81 million tons, valued at $116.64 million; and magnesium chloride 22 billion tons, valued at $797.04 billion.\n\n### VII. Who Belong to the Old Testament Church\n\nThe earthly Jews — the natural seed of Abraham — who obeyed the covenant terms and met the conditions of the promises belong to the "called-out" people in the wilderness.\n\nThose who sinned were cut off from the covenant blessings, just as people in the present Church are cut off through sin (1 Cor. 10:1-13; Heb. 2:1-4; 3:7–4:11; Jude 5).\n\nThink of it like membership in a club that requires certain conduct. If you violate the rules, you lose the privileges — even if you were once a member in good standing.\n\nSaints who lived before Abraham's calling and the founding of Israel couldn't be considered part of the Jewish "called-out" body, simply because they lived before that time.\n\nIn a technical sense, those who died "in faith" and in a justified state before God during the whole period from Abraham to Christ don't belong to the Old Testament Church either. Why? Because it's an earthly Church with an earthly calling.\n\nThose who died will be resurrected to become immortal and will have rulership over the future Old Testament Church (the Jews) after Christ comes. In other words, God's promises and callings with Israel are irrevocable and earthly, connected with earthly living people forever (Rom. 11:29).\n\nThese promises will be fulfilled with the earthly Jews of the future when they are reconciled to God (Rom. 11). Jews who have died and become part of the heavenly, glorified family of God will have no part in the earthly fulfillment of Israel's promises. By their death and resurrection to immortality, they become severed from the natural people of Israel and its earthly blessings.\n\nNeither the New Testament Church nor future living Gentiles belong to the Old Testament Church. They will have no part in Israel's restoration or in any prophecies concerning the Jews in their land in the eternal future.\n\nSo we conclude that the Jews of today and the future make up the original Old Testament Church, called out of all nations to evangelize and reconcile them to God. The Jews were temporarily set aside from their calling because they failed to fulfill it, but they will be redeemed and will yet accomplish their calling among all nations in the Millennium.\n\nThe Old Testament Church is an eternal "called-out" body of people, merely temporarily inactive and set aside because of sin. But it will become the channel of blessings to all nations in the future, after the New Testament Church has fulfilled its earthly ministry (Isa. 52:7; 61:6; 66:18-21; Rom. 11; Acts 15:13-18).\n\n### VIII. The Marriage of the Old Testament Church\n\nYou may have heard that the Jews are the Bride of God the Father, while the present Church is the Bride of Christ. But this isn't quite accurate.\n\nIt's generally held that Israel is now God's wife and that the present Church isn't yet Christ's wife but will become so at the marriage of the Lamb after the rapture. But there's an inconsistency here: if Israel is God's wife now through covenant relationship, then the body of Christ is also already married to Him on the same basis.\n\n**Was Israel the Wife of God in Old Testament Times?**\n\nThe Bible definitely states that Israel was God's wife, that she is now the estranged or divorced wife of God, and that she will become His wife again in the future. Israel is called the "wife" of God in multiple passages (Isa. 54:1-6; Jer. 3:1-25; Hos. 1:1-11; 2:1-23; Mal. 2:14-16).\n\nIn what sense could Israel be called God's "wife"? It can only be by covenant relationship — not physical. And if by covenant relationship, then she became God's covenant wife when He made covenants with her through Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, Moses, and David.\n\nOnly those who entered covenant relationship with God can be considered married or united to Him by covenant. Abraham, as the father and source of the chosen family, was as much united to God as any of his descendants.\n\nThink of it like a marriage contract: the different covenants made between God and the chosen race were entered voluntarily and were binding on both parties from the moment the contract was finalized. That's why Israel can be called the covenant wife of God.\n\nIn Jeremiah, Hosea, and other books, Israel is pictured as divorced from God because she broke the covenant terms and refused to continue in covenant relationship. She will remain divorced until her final conversion and reconciliation to God.\n\nThen Israel will once again be considered God's wife, just as she was in Old Testament times, because of the New Covenant God will make with her (Jer. 31:31-40; Ezek. 36:16-38; 37:15-28; Hos. 2:14-23; Rom. 11:25-32; Heb. 8:6-13; 10:16-18).\n\nSo we conclude that Israel was God's wife in the past, is currently divorced, and will become His covenant wife again in the future.\n\n**Now, what about the idea that Israel is only the wife of God the Father?** This teaching doesn't harmonize with Scripture for several important reasons:\n\n1. Any person or group entering relationship with God — whether by covenant or by actual re-creation — does so equally with God the Father, God the Son, and God the Holy Spirit. Israel's God was "one [united] God" (Deut. 6:4), existing in three separate and distinct persons, as we saw in Lesson Four (1 Jn. 5:7-8). Therefore, Israel being the covenant people of the Triune God is related to each person in the "one" God through the same covenant terms.\n\n2. It's impossible for God the Father to have a wife with which the Son has nothing to do or isn't related to. The same is true of the Spirit. Likewise, the Son cannot have a wife unrelated to the Father or Spirit by the same covenant and redemptive work. Such teaching would make the Father, Son, and Spirit independent of each other regarding different groups of redeemed people — something the Bible never teaches, as proven in Lesson Four, Point III.\n\n3. Both Testaments speak of the Son as Israel's direct, personal, and visible king — meaning He must be related to the Jews as much as the Father or Spirit (Isa. 9:6, 7; Mic. 5:1-2; Lk. 1:32-35). Would the Father permit the Son to take His own wife and rule over her forever if the Son had no covenant relationship with Israel?\n\n4. The whole family of God in Heaven and Earth is one family (Eph. 3:14, 15). It includes all spiritual and material, glorified and natural, heavenly and earthly free moral agents who are in harmony with God. God is the Father of all, the Son is the Savior of all, and the Spirit is the Helper of all (Eph. 4:4-6). If God the Father is the Father of all, isn't He the Father of New Testament saints as well as Old Testament saints? If the Son is the Savior of all, isn't He the Savior of Old Testament saints as well as New Testament saints? If the Spirit is the Creator and Helper of all, hasn't He been the agent of regeneration for Old Testament saints as well as New Testament saints? The relationship of the Father, Son, and Spirit is the same with each redeemed person and must be the same with each redeemed company of saints. It's unscriptural to teach that the Father has a bride (Old Testament saints), the Son has a bride (New Testament saints), and the Holy Spirit has no bride. It's equally erroneous to teach that the Son won't marry the New Testament Church but will marry Israel, as some teach.\n\nWe conclude that Israel is simply one part of the great universal family of God, related to each person of the Triune God equally according to His respective relationships.\n\n### IX. The Blessings and Glory of the Old Testament Church\n\nThere's a widespread misconception among Christians that Old Testament saints didn't experience the New Testament spiritual blessings promised to the present Church.\n\nSome theories suggest that only a few special prophets were filled with the Spirit, that these were temporary anointings, and that Old Testament saints weren't born again, sanctified, made holy, or blessed with the many graces available today.\n\nEvery one of these ideas is mistaken, as the following facts from both Testaments demonstrate:\n\n**Old Testament Saints Experienced Many Spiritual and Physical Blessings Such As:**\n\n> 1. The Lord as their Savior and salvation (Exod.\n\n---\n\nThe Old Testament assembly of Israel was the original called-out people of God — the congregation through which every covenant and promise flowed toward their ultimate fulfillment in Christ. Understanding this continuity between the two testaments changes how you read the whole Bible. So here's the question: Are there passages in the Old Testament you've written off as 'Jewish' and therefore not for you — without ever asking whether they might contain promises, patterns, or principles that apply directly to your life as a member of God's called-out people?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk48j001bewhj1kw6sfsu	cmnknk48e000xewhj49ao5az0	Lesson 28 — Faith — How to Attain to All the Known Needs of Life	READING	2388	13	\N	t	Faith is the word Christianity uses more than almost any other — and means less by it than almost any other. Ask most believers what faith is and they'll say 'believing in God' or 'trusting things will work out.' But that's not the biblical definition. Biblical faith is not a mood, not an optimistic attitude, not a spiritual feel-good state. It's a specific faculty with a specific object and a specific mechanism — and Hebrews 11:1 defines it precisely.\n\nLast time we established the nature of the Trinity — one God in three distinct Persons. Now we turn to the instrument by which believers access everything God has provided: faith — what it actually is, how it works, and how to develop it.\n\nSo what do these words actually mean? They carry the idea of confiding in someone so completely that you feel secure without fear. They mean fleeing to someone for refuge, taking shelter in them, staying and resting on them, relying on them completely. It's about believing someone — taking them at their word — and putting absolute trust in a person without any questioning or doubts about their faithfulness.\n\nThe Bible gives us its own definition in Heb. 11:1: "The substance of things hoped for, the evidence of things not seen." Various translations help us see the richness of this verse:\n\n> "Now faith is a well-grounded assurance of that for which we hope, and a conviction of the reality of things which we do not see" (Weymouth)\n\n> "Now faith means we are confident of what we hope for, convinced of what we do not see" (Moffatt)\n\n> "Now faith is the title-deed of things hoped for; the putting to proof of things not seen" (Centenary Translation)\n\n> "Now faith is an assumption of what is being expected, a conviction concerning matters which are not being observed" (Concordant Version)\n\n> "Now faith is the persuasion of the things that are in hope, as if they were in act; and it is the manifestness of the things not seen" (The Syriac)\n\n> "Now faith is assurance of things hoped for, a conviction of things not seen" (Revised Version)\n\nPaul describes true faith as an attribute of God Himself in Rom. 4:17 — God "quickeneth the dead, and calleth those things which be not as though they were." Think of it like this: faith is a union of assurance and conviction, counting something done as if it were already accomplished.\n\nFaith doesn't need to see before it believes. It laughs at impossibilities! It laughs at circumstances that seem to contradict it! Faith counts the thing done the moment you ask God for it.\n\nHere's what's remarkable about faith: it's not swayed to believe God only when things seem possible. And it doesn't waver or question when things seem to go contrary to what you've asked. Faith doggedly keeps going — counting the impossible as possible, counting as done what isn't yet seen, and counting things that don't exist yet as though they already do.\n\n![Lesson 28 — Faith — How to Attain to All the Known Needs of Life](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8Os8v2lWgwQn65F0NSyXfv2WGAqVjL7icrDpPIo)\n\n## II. What Faith is Not\n\nFaith is not *feeling* that your prayer is answered. The average person who tries to exercise faith depends on what they can see, hear, or feel. Most testimonies about faith are tied up with feelings, emotions, or physical sensations. But sense-faith — faith based on physical evidence or your soul's emotions — will eventually deceive you.\n\nYour faith should be based on God's Word regardless of any sense-knowledge or feeling-evidence. Here's what happens to so many people: they constantly look to their feelings to determine whether prayer has been heard. If they feel good, or if something encouraging happens, they think believing is easy. But when setbacks come and those good feelings disappear? These same people plunge into despair.\n\nThey're quick to accuse God of being unfaithful to His Word. Or if they don't go that far, they quickly assume it wasn't God's will to grant the answer. They become satisfied to go without what God has plainly promised.\n\nLet me tell you something liberating: faith has absolutely no relationship with feelings and sense-evidence. At the very moments when you think you have all the faith in the world because of how you feel — that's when you have the least! And when you think your faith is nothing — that's often when you have more than at any other time in your life.\n\nMany people are surprised to get answers to prayer because when they prayed, they thought their faith was worthless. But here's the key: you shouldn't believe you're healed because the pain is gone, or that you're saved because you feel forgiven, or that your prayers are answered because things are working out. Instead, always maintain faith because of what the Word of God says!\n\nGod's Word should have first place in your life — not your senses. All basing of faith upon what you've done, how well you live, or what experiences you've had will lead to failure in answered prayer. God doesn't answer on those grounds. He answers solely on the grounds of grace and faith in Him and His Word.\n\nTrue faith is not trusting in another person's goodness or faith. It must be *personal* faith in God and His Word. People constantly go around seeking someone who has faith, someone who can get answers for them. This might work temporarily while you're learning about God and His Word, but if you don't develop personal faith that refuses to be denied, you'll eventually revert to failure and unbelief. You'll have to settle for the modern theory that answered prayer isn't for everyone.\n\nThe program that actually pays off is having personal faith in God, faith in Jesus Christ, faith in the Holy Spirit, faith in the Word, faith in the atonement, and total personal consecration to believe God regardless of anything that might hinder prayer. The right approach isn't talking about faith or the need for it — it's the actual exercise of it!\n\nSimple faith in the Word — regardless of feelings and circumstances — is never possible for someone who lives only in the realm of their senses. Such a person believes only what they can see, feel, hear, or understand to be possible. This was Thomas's kind of faith when he declared he wouldn't believe until he had seen. It was Martha's kind when she could see nothing but the natural fact that Lazarus had been dead four days and "by this time he stinketh." This is the kind of faith modern religious leaders teach and encourage, but it's not what the New Testament requires.\n\nMental faith — mere assent to truth combined with dependence on feelings — isn't enough either. You must come to life and action before you'll realize the benefits of active, living faith that refuses to know defeat and failure.\n\nYou must learn to fight the fight of faith and lay hold of God and His Word. You must understand that you're surrounded by an unbelieving world and an atmosphere of doubt. Demons, fallen angels, and wicked men have lived for centuries in unbelief, creating subtle currents of doubt and mistrust. The effects of the Fall have left deep wounds of doubt and wavering in fallen humanity that must be healed.\n\nYou must learn that you have to wrestle with powers of darkness and currents of mistrust and unbelief that make it a struggle to exercise active, living faith for things not yet seen. And you must not only learn how to do this — you must actually do it to get results.\n\n## III. Kinds of Faith\n\nScripture mentions several kinds of faith. Understanding these will help you identify the kind of faith you should have:\n\n### 1. Common Faith (Titus 1:4)\n\nThis is also called "the mutual faith" (Rom. 1:12). It's the faith common to all who claim to be Christians — belief in Christ as Savior and in eternal life, along with a few other commonly accepted truths held more or less alike by all Christians.\n\nThe *uncommon* kind is that rare faith in apostolic power and in the fullness of God in believers' lives. Faith in these truths brings controversy because the average church member hasn't been taught that they can have all the benefits of the gospel that early Christians experienced. That all men can have this uncommon faith and power has been made clear in Lessons Twenty and Twenty-two.\n\n### 2. Weak Faith (Rom. 4:19; 14:1-23; 15:1-4; 1 Cor. 8:1-13)\n\nThis kind of faith constantly limits personal benefits and privileges in the gospel due to wrong teachings and personal scruples about non-essentials of life. Literally thousands of people spend much of their time arguing and condemning each other over small details that Scripture doesn't even specifically mention — what one may or may not eat, drink, wear, or do and still be a Christian.\n\nThe Scriptures above make clear that the kingdom of God doesn't consist of meat, drink, and personal details not specifically forbidden in Scripture. Instead, it is "righteousness, and peace, and joy in the Holy Ghost" (Rom. 14:17).\n\nIf the devil can't get you to commit great sins forbidden in Scripture, he'll urge honest-hearted people to go to the extreme and condemn this or that in personal liberties that God hasn't strictly condemned. Multitudes today go to the utter extreme and condemn almost every phase of human living in some degree.\n\nThe law of Scripture concerning anything not definitely forbidden by God is this: "He that doubteth is damned if he eat, because he eateth not in faith: for whatsoever is not of faith is sin" (Rom. 14:23).\n\nThe Christian law of love to others is:\n\n> "Let us therefore follow after the things which make for peace, and things wherewith one may edify another. For meat destroy not the work of God. All things are pure; but it is evil for that man who eateth with offence. It is good neither to eat flesh, nor to drink wine, nor any thing whereby thy brother stumbleth, or is offended, or is made weak. Hast thou faith? have it to thyself before God. Happy is the man that condemneth not himself in the things which he alloweth . . . We then that are strong ought to bear the infirmities of the weak, and not to please ourselves. Let everyone of us please his neighbor for his good to edification" (Rom. 14:19-22; 15:1-2).\n\n### 3. Strong Faith (Rom. 4:20)\n\nThis is the faith that refuses to be defeated. It refuses to take no for an answer! It laughs at circumstances, symptoms, all outward appearances, and what is seen, heard, or felt. It doggedly holds to the fact that what you've asked of God is granted.\n\nAbraham had this kind of faith:\n\n> "Who against hope believed in hope, that he should be the father of many nations, according to that which was spoken, So shall thy seed be. And being not weak in faith, he considered not his own body now dead, when he was about an hundred years old, neither yet the deadness of Sarah's womb [who was about ninety years old]: He staggered not at the promise of God through unbelief; but was strong in faith, giving glory to God; and being fully persuaded that, what he had promised, he was able to perform. And it was counted to him for righteousness" (Rom. 4:18-22).\n\nThis is the kind of faith all of us should have — and could have — if we would simply persuade ourselves that God is true to His Word. Most people claim they have faith in God to this extent, and no one wants to say boldly that He's a liar who won't keep His word. But when it comes to actually exercising faith that won't doubt or waver in prayer? Very few will maintain such strong faith and thank God for the answer even before they see it.\n\nVery few will simply refuse to question in the slightest when things seem to go contrary to what they've asked. And that's exactly why they don't get definite answers from God.\n\nThere is no person who exercises faith as strong as Abraham's who will go very long without an answer from God. The idea that God would refuse someone who came to Him in such unwavering faith is literally impossible and unheard of (Jas. 1:5-8; Heb. 11:16). All of us are definitely assured of getting from God what He has promised if we will "walk in the steps of that faith of our father Abraham" (Rom. 4:12, 23-25).\n\n### 4. Little Faith (Mt. 6:30; 8:26; 14:31; Lk. 12:28)\n\nThis is essentially the same as no faith because it's the wavering kind (Heb. 10:23; Jas. 1:5-8).\n\n### 5. Great Faith (Mt. 8:10; 15:28)\n\nThis is true, unwavering faith that will always move God to answer according to His abundant promises (Heb. 11:6).\n\n### 6. Unfeigned Faith (1 Tim. 1:5; 2 Tim. 1:5)\n\nThis faith knows no hypocrisy, sham, or counterfeit. It doesn't brag or put on an outward show to prove it's real. It's simply genuine, real, and sincere in every aspect. It proceeds out of a pure heart and from a good conscience, as these Scriptures state.\n\nThis faith isn't hereditary, although in 2 Tim. 1:5 Paul speaks of it being in three successive generations. It's the kind of faith all honest, pure, and sincere people have. It's unselfish, holy, and godly — the kind that naturally increases in the life of every true child of God as they grow in grace and knowledge (2 Pet. 1:4-9; 3:18).\n\n### 7. Temporary Faith (Lk. 8:13)\n\nThis faith believes for a while and springs up like a mushroom, but because of its shallowness and lack of root, it fails in times of temptation and testing.\n\nPeople through the ages have been this way. They receive God's Word with great joy, and it seems they're going to outstrip everyone else in faith. But after a few days or weeks, you don't hear from them again — or if you do, it's in another revival meeting where their emotions get temporarily stirred and they make another start in faith, soon to fall again.\n\nThey never fully come clean with God or make the full surrender of their lives except for the moment. They don't prepare the soil so the seed can take root and produce fruit.\n\nSome teach this can't happen, but it does — despite the so-called impossibility of falling away from faith and being renewed in repentance. Some teach that one can't fall from the faith, but Jesus certainly said that some would "receive the word with joy; and these have no root, which for a while believe, and in time of temptation fall away" (Mt. 13:20-21; Lk. 8:13).\n\nPaul speaks of these as those who "concerning faith have made shipwreck" (1 Tim. 1:19), as "having cast off their first faith" by going "aside after Satan" (1 Tim. 5:12-15), and as having "erred concerning the faith" (1 Tim. 6:21).\n\nMany Scriptures say that men can depart from and fall from the faith (Acts 14:22; 1 Tim. 2:15; 4:1; 5:8-15; 6:10, 21; 2 Tim. 2:18; Heb. 3:6, 12-14; 6:11-12; 10:22-28). Men are repeatedly told to continue in the faith (Acts 14:22; Col. 1:23; 2:6-7). Anybody with common intelligence knows they can disbelieve anything they choose to lose faith in — especially things that aren't seen.\n\n### 8. Historical Faith (1 Jn. 5:10-13)\n\nThis is faith in the history or record of God concerning the past and His work for men. You can believe God's record to the letter and still not be saved. It's simply believing in the record of anything from the past. No consecration to God is necessary to believe history.\n\n### 9. Mental Faith (Jas. 2:14-26)\n\nMental faith is similar to historical faith in that it believes history, but goes further and believes every part of the Bible — past, present, and future — as well as all the truths about God's blessings. However, it doesn't act upon God's Word. Faith without works is dead, being alone. It's passive faith or mere mental assent to truth.\n\nThousands of sinners have mental faith in God and the Bible, but they keep neglecting to definitely obey truth.\n\nTrue faith is not mental assent to truth as a fact. Most people have such assent. They'll believe God is able, that He has promised to do certain things, and that He would act if true faith were exercised. But there's little effort to cooperate with God or get what He's promised. It's merely mental assent to truth without active faith in it.\n\nIt's like having faith that will turn to every source of help *except* God, while claiming to believe in God and His Word. It's similar to believing food is good without ever eating to get its benefits. Mental assent — passive faith — is one of the most dangerous enemies of true faith in God. It claims all the faith in the world and is easily satisfied with anything or nothing from God.\n\nIf it doesn't get what it wants, it doesn't care. It would accept something from God if He came personally, laid the answer in your lap, and begged you to keep it. But apart from that, there's little effort to act in faith concerning God's promises.\n\nMental faith may go as far as believing every truth and every part of God's program. It may even claim to be contending for certain benefits — yet it dares not act on God's promises.\n\n### 10. Active Faith (Jas. 2:14-26; Heb. 10:19-38)\n\nThis is the kind that acts upon God's Word as it becomes clear. James put it this way: "shew me thy faith without thy works, and I will shew thee my faith by my works." All of us must have this faith to get anything from God.\n\nAs long as faith is purely mental and passive, you won't take action to obey truth. You must get beyond the stage of hearing and into actually *doing* what God says before you get results.\n\nActive, living faith moves to obey every truth of God to the letter and to appropriate what God has promised. It acts as if unseen things are reality. It's quick to provide works to prove it's sincere and obedient.\n\nThink of having a real, active, living faith of your own! Imagine the thrill of having your own prayers answered!\n\nAs I write, I have a large stack of letters on my desk marked "Testimony." These letters, received in recent months, tell how God has answered prayer. People hear the radio programs and are inspired to take God at His Word. As they act, they receive answers.\n\nOne lady said she had been sick for ten long years and hadn't done any housework. She heard me say on the air that if you believe God and refuse to be sick and defeated, you can have whatever you want. She said, "I acted upon the Word and got out of bed, and God met my faith. I have been doing my work ever since."\n\nAnother is thrilled that her prayers have been answered three times from laying her hands on the radio and praying with us. Many more are deeply stirred to new life and deeper consecration because they're learning to act upon the Word.\n\nMultitudes have never had an answer to prayer, and they're missing life's greatest blessings by such neglect. Most people depend on others to get answers for them. They pray — as they think — but they want someone else to do the believing. In reality, they're not praying at all. They're merely saying words and going through a form of prayer.\n\nTrue New Testament praying is always heard by God, and the answer is always sure. God has planned for all of us to have this kind of faith and results. "These signs shall follow them that believe," and "all things are possible to him that believeth" (Mk. 9:23; 11:22-24; 16:15-20; Jn. 14:12-15).\n\nFaith can be developed into a mighty living force that knows no defeat! You can know that you are master of all demon powers and circumstances through Jesus Christ. You no longer need to be a slave to fear, timidity, weakness, failure, sickness, poverty, helplessness, and despair. You can be a conqueror in the very areas where you've suffered defeat.\n\n> "If you abide in me, and my words abide in you, ye shall ask what ye will, and it shall be done unto you" (Jn. 15:7, 16).\n\n> "He that abideth in me, and I in him, the same bringeth forth much fruit: for without me ye can do nothing" (Jn. 15:5).\n\nYou must awake to the consciousness of your new life in Christ! As a branch, you have His life, His health, His power, His love, and His nature flowing through you. You are the fruit-bearing part of Christ in the Earth. You are in Him and He is in you.\n\nYou have His words abiding in you, giving you faith for whatever you ask. You have as much right in Him to ask and receive from God as He had. You have a right to expect the same answers from God that He got. You represent God here as He did — so don't be satisfied to go without the benefits He died to give you. (See Lessons Sixteen and Twenty-two).\n\nYou are commanded to be a doer of the Word — and that means ask and receive! You're not merely to be a hearer. You're not deceiving yourself when you act on the Word. It will be confirmed because it is truth. It must be a living, active faith, not a mere mental passive something that even sinners can have.\n\nYou are in Christ, and as God's child and heir, you have the authority to act on the Word. You are a fruit-bearer, a producer for God — so get busy and produce for Him according to His Word!\n\n### 11. Wavering Faith (Jas. 1:5-8)\n\nThis is faith that doubts God and refuses to believe. To truly believe and have faith is to act on the Word. It means taking what is already yours.\n\nTo believe on Jesus means taking Him for all that the Bible declares Him to be. It means taking salvation from sin, healing for the body, answers to your prayers, and all that He died to bring you.\n\nBelieving is an act of the will. When you really believe, you have acted. You've taken the necessary step to get what you want from God. Faith is action — counting things that don't exist as though they do (Rom. 4:17; Mk. 11:22-24).\n\nDoubting is refusing to act on the Word. Unbelief is either refusing to act according to the knowledge you have, or it's a manifestation of ignorance of God's Word. If you don't know, you can't act because you don't understand. If you don't understand, you're afraid to act because you don't know how. The cure for all unbelief is a thorough knowledge of the Word and consecration to obey it to the letter, regardless of how impossible it may seem at the moment.\n\nWavering faith is called double-mindedness — a constant change of mind about what you want or whether you even want it. It's an attitude of yes—no, yes—no, yes—no, until God Himself can't tell whether it's yes or no!\n\nOne minute you decide something is wanted, maybe even with real desperation, but the next minute it's not so important. People with wavering faith don't really make up their minds to see the fight of faith through to an answer. They'd accept the answer if it came without any hesitation or effort on their part — but taking the necessary steps to get an answer? Fighting even a little for what they've asked? That's another question entirely.\n\n### 12. Unwavering Faith (Heb. 10:23; 11:6)\n\nThis is faith taking God at His Word without any question. God commands us:\n\n> "Ask in faith, nothing wavering. For he that wavereth is like a wave of the sea driven with the wind and tossed. For let not that man think that he shall receive any thing of the Lord. A doubleminded man is unstable in all his ways" (Jas. 1:5-8).\n\nWe're told:\n\n> "Hold fast the profession of our faith without wavering; (for he is faithful that promised)" (Heb. 10:23).\n\nAnd again:\n\n> "But without faith it is impossible to please him: for he that cometh to God must believe that he is, and that he is a rewarder of them that diligently seek him" (Heb. 11:6).\n\nThis is faith refusing to doubt, wonder, question, or reason about any detail of what you've asked God for. It has absolute confidence that what was asked is according to God's Word, and therefore it *knows* prayer is answered.\n\nIt rests upon the promises as the basis of the answer instead of upon feelings and things seen. It refuses to act contrary to what it has asked or to question any delay in the answer. It counts the thing done regardless of all outward evidence to the contrary.\n\nIt laughs at impossibilities and goes on in utmost confidence that what God has promised, He is able to perform. It gives thanks for the answer from the moment it asks and looks forward with childlike expectation of receiving it.\n\n### 13. Human Faith (Mk. 11:22-24)\n\nThis is simply the exercise of your human faculties in having confidence and conviction that you can believe God and that God is true to all He has promised. All of us are capable of faith in ourselves and faith in anyone else who makes a promise to us.\n\nFaith is an attribute of your created being. It was natural for man to believe God before the Fall. There was no such thing as doubt, questioning, or unbelieving reasoning before that time.\n\nIt was the devil who injected doubt and unbelief into mankind's moral and spiritual makeup. And since humanity's Fall — because of the near-total depravity of our being — it has become one of the greatest struggles of our redemptive journey to have that same simple, unwavering faith that was natural before the Fall.\n\nIt was doubt and unbelief that caused the Fall, and it's of the greatest importance to get rid of all such in redemption.\n\n### 14. Divine Faith (Heb. 11:3; Gal. 2:20; 1 Cor. 13:14)\n\nFaith isn't only a natural attribute of man — it's also an attribute of God. It's God's absolute confidence and conviction in His own Being and Word.\n\nHeb. 11:3 teaches that the ages were planned by faith through God's Word, and things were brought into existence that never existed before. God had faith in Himself when He planned the worlds! He believed He could do what He commanded, and He counted things that weren't as though they were (Rom. 4:17).\n\nGal. 2:20 speaks of "the faith of the Son of God," proving that Christ also has faith as the Father does. In fact, He is the author and finisher of faith (Heb. 12:1-2). And 1 Cor. 13:14 speaks of "faith" abiding along with hope and love — meaning these three qualities are eternal attributes of God.\n\nWe will have to exercise faith in the infinite throughout eternity, for there will always be the need for confidence in God and His eternal plan concerning matters the finite hasn't yet comprehended of the infinite (Isa. 32:17). If faith will be necessary in eternity, how much more is it needed now when we don't know as we're also known!\n\nThis life is the probationary period of our eternal existence, and it's necessary to learn faith and obedience to carry out God's plan for us both now and forever. Faith is acting on God's Word, and this will be necessary throughout eternity because God will eternally give His Word to the saved and make known His will concerning all things to come.\n\nSo it's clear: God has faith, Christ has faith, the Holy Spirit has faith, angels have faith, demons have faith (Jas. 2:11), and we must have faith to please God (Heb. 11:6).\n\nFaith is absolutely necessary in carrying on moral government. If moral agents can't have faith in the Moral Governor of the universe, they won't trust Him. Lack of faith on the part of subjects in human government tends to create anarchy and rebellion. When doubt, questioning, unbelief, dissatisfaction, and lack of confidence show up in subjects of any government, it cannot long endure.\n\nFaith is an absolute necessity. Childlike faith and confidence in God — with utter abandonment of self-interest, trusting Him to leave everything in His hands for the common good of all — is not only demanded by God but is the only reasonable attitude for us to take.\n\nThe history of mankind, angels, and demons proves that God has been good, merciful, loving, kind, and consecrated to the best good of all creation. Not one person in all of moral creation can point a finger of accusation that God has dealt with them unjustly. No one today can testify that God has been unfaithful if they've done His will and conformed to His Word in faith.\n\nNot one free moral agent can accuse God of the least degree of unfaithfulness in any dealing. Not one will be able throughout eternity to accuse Him of being a respecter of persons. God has been and always will be good to all.\n\nHe longs to bless everyone today! He will heal every disease, save from every sin and bad habit, deliver from failure and poverty, and answer every prayer prayed in faith in the name of Jesus Christ. He longs to do what He has promised. He desires to supply all our needs.\n\nBut He will not break His own law by blessing those who don't have faith. He has laid down plain laws of faith whereby anyone can get what they want in life, and He will hold us to obedience to these laws before granting our desires.\n\nIf you want the benefits, then intelligently obey God's laws to the letter — and by doing so, you'll get what He has promised. If the benefits aren't worth the price, then don't complain that you're not supplied. If they *are* worth the simple effort to get them as God has made clear, then don't doubt or waver one moment about the outcome!\n\nIt's already settled that the benefits are guaranteed and sure if true, simple, childlike faith is exercised. There's no longer any question about this if we believe the Bible — so let it be settled forever.\n\n## IV. The Importance of Faith\n\nThe Bible definitely declares that faith is all-important:\n\n> "But without faith it is impossible to please him: for he that cometh to God must believe that he is, and that he is a rewarder of them that diligently seek him" (Heb. 11:6)\n\n> "Now faith is the substance of things hoped for, the evidence of things not seen" (Heb. 11:1)\n\n> "But let him ask in faith, nothing wavering. For he that wavereth is like a wave of the sea driven with the wind and tossed. For let not that man think that he shall receive any thing of the Lord" (Jas. 1:5-8)\n\n> "Whatsoever is not of faith is sin" (Rom. 14:23)\n\n> "Above all, taking the shield of faith, wherewith ye may be able to quench all the fiery darts of the wicked" (Eph. 6:16)\n\n> "Cast not away therefore your confidence, which hath great recompense of reward . . . Now the just shall live by faith: but if any man draw back, my soul shall have no pleasure in him" (Heb. 10:35-39)\n\n> "And all things, whatsoever ye shall ask in prayer, believing ye shall receive" (Mt. 21:21)\n\n> "If thou canst believe, all things are possible to him that believeth" (Mk. 9:23)\n\n> "Have faith in God. For verily I say unto you, That whosoever shall say unto this mountain, Be thou removed, and be thou cast into the sea, and shall not doubt in his heart, but shall believe that those things which he saith shall come to pass; he shall have whatsoever he saith. Therefore, I say unto you, What things soever ye desire, when ye pray, believe that ye receive them, and ye shall have them" (Mk. 11:22-24)\n\nThese scriptures need no interpretation. They plainly promise all of us that we can get whatever we have faith for. There are no limitations or qualifications concerning known needs of this life or the life to come — so don't limit them!\n\nThey're clear that faith is absolutely necessary to get what you desire in life. No one should expect to get anything from God if they refuse to have faith. There's no such thing as the impossibility of having faith, so if you don't have faith, it's your own choice and responsibility. Jesus *commanded* us to "Have faith in God" — and such is possible, or it wouldn't be mandatory.\n\nIt's all-important to have faith because:\n\n- No prayer will be answered without it\n- God cannot be pleased without it\n- You won't get anything from God without it\n- You'll be disobedient to God without it\n\nIf faith is commanded, then not having faith is breaking God's law and committing sin. If we believers realized this, we'd become desperate about this law-breaking just as we are about breaking other laws of God!\n\nIf we could only realize that unbelief and doubting God is *sin*, we'd at least be moved to action against such law-breaking. The trouble has been that most people consider unbelief and doubt as part of human nature — something to be expected, not changed. The attitude has been indifference. When it's easy to believe, there's no problem. But when believing becomes a struggle, it's passed off as something that can't be helped.\n\nThis is where the devil has gained the upper hand. He makes people think that not everyone can believe God — and that if you can't, it's perfectly fine to live in unbelief and doubt all you please.\n\nIf prayer isn't answered, most people take for granted that it's not God's will to grant an answer. They're quickly turned aside from the purpose of getting an answer and are satisfied to be defeated. Blame for such defeat is laid on God and considered His will without any question.\n\nBut the truth is that this attitude is surrender to the devil and evil spirit-forces who oppose answered prayer! It's so habitual for the average Christian to be defeated that they give it little thought. It's taken for granted when offering prayer that not getting an answer will be all right, and that this "reveals" God's will not to answer.\n\nThis condition has been created by wrong teachings in the churches for\n\n---\n\nFaith is not a spiritual personality trait some people have and others don't — it's a faculty that grows through specific, identifiable means: hearing the Word of God (Rom. 10:17), exercising the faith you already have, and acting on what you believe before you see the result. So here's the question: Name one specific promise of God that you believe in your head but have never acted on with your body. What would it look like to step out on that promise this week?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk48j001dewhjslx1in6y	cmnknk48e000xewhj49ao5az0	Lesson 29 — The New Testament Church	READING	7102	15	\N	t	What is the Church? Not the building, not the organization, not the denomination — but the thing Jesus said He would build when He told Peter 'upon this rock I will build my church' (Mt. 16:18). That declaration was made two thousand years ago. The institution that carries the name today looks dramatically different from what He described. When did it change? And does it matter?\n\nLast time we examined faith — the instrument that accesses everything God has provided. Now we examine the community God built to carry the gospel forward: the New Testament Church — its nature, its structure, its authority, and its mission.\n\nThe words "church" and "churches" appear 108 times in the New Testament. (See Lesson Eighteen, Point II.) You won't find them once in the Old Testament. All the truths about the Church — its organization, government, officers, discipline, ordinances, and other important questions — we'll cover in the studies that follow.\n\n![Lesson 29 — The New Testament Church](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8Oss0Df2WnIFYWHUNhmRXnATM2qSZzVGaodwQ5D)\n\n## I. What the New Testament Church is Not\n\n> 1. It is not any one or all the denominations and independent parts of Christendom.\n\n> 2. It is not the continuation of the Old Testament Church of Lesson Eighteen.\n\n> 3. It is not the beginning of the eternal kingdom of Christ and of God.\n\n> 4. It is not the visible part of this kingdom in the world, through which God is trying to rule the nations, as taught by some.\n\n> 5. It is not the kingdom of Heaven and the kingdom of God, and it is never confused with them in Scripture.\n\nWe are "heirs" of the kingdom (Rom. 8:17; Jas. 2:5), but not of the Church. Think of it this way: you belong to the Church — you can't inherit yourself! But you will inherit the kingdom.\n\nWe are "children of the kingdom" (Mt. 8:11-12), but not of the Church. The kingdom is yet to come (Mt. 6:10; Rev. 11:15), but the Church is here now. We will receive the kingdom (Heb. 12:28), but not the Church.\n\nAlso, the Church is a new mystery revealed (Eph. 3), while the kingdom has been the subject of the prophets for ages past (Dan. 2:44-45; 7:13-14; Isa. 9:6-7; etc.). These and many other points of contrast between the Church and the kingdom prove that the Church is not the same as the kingdom.\n\n## II. What the New Testament Church Is\n\nThe New Testament Church is a "called out" body of people from all nations who have been born-again, lived a true Christian life, and died in the faith. It also includes all those who are alive in Christ today. This present Church is the *ekklesia* of the New Testament times — called out of the world to evangelize the world in this age. (See the definition and usage of *ekklesia* in Lesson Eighteen.)\n\nThe Church, in its largest sense, is the whole company of the redeemed in all ages, in Heaven and on Earth (Eph. 3:1-11; Heb. 12:23). It's the spiritual people who have been made members of the universal family of God.\n\nThe Church in this sense is the body of Christ, and it doesn't consist of saved and unsaved mixed together. It's not merely a group of individuals associated for social and benevolent purposes, as seems to be the idea on every hand. It's the body of people indwelt by God through the Holy Spirit — and through whom God works in the world.\n\nThe Bible teaches one universal Church and many local churches in which the universal Church takes local and temporal form. It also teaches a spiritual and invisible Church and many visible and material churches through which God is made known to the world.\n\nA local church may be defined as "a company of redeemed people in any locality or community, who unite themselves voluntarily together as a part of the universal Church for the purpose of establishing themselves in the faith of the gospel and to evangelize their community and to propagate the gospel to the ends of the Earth."\n\nLocal churches are mentioned many times in Scripture (Mt. 18:17; Acts 14:23; Rom. 16:5; 1 Cor. 1:2; 4:17; 16:19; Phil. 4:15; Col. 4:15; 1 Pet. 5:13; Rev. 1:11; 2:1, 8, 12, 18; 3:1, 7). A local church may consist of as few as two or three people (Mt. 18:15-20).\n\nMany human organizations that claim to be Christian are not churches. Any organization that is instituted and managed solely by man, that permits unsaved men in it, that has no definite form of doctrine according to the Scriptures, that does not have the Church laws and ordinances, and that does not follow the whole Bible as the rule of faith and practice — cannot be a Church. Such organizations may be good in supplementing the work of the Church, but they cannot be classified as churches.\n\nThe laws of the Bible through which believers unite themselves into churches may be summarized as follows:\n\n> 1. The sole authority of the entire Bible, rightly divided, as the rule of faith and practice in life and conduct.\n\n> 2. The new birth and genuine Christian living as necessary to church membership.\n\n> 3. Willful obedience to the Bible in all of its ordinances, doctrines, and laws.\n\n> 4. Consecration to the will of God and to the end to which He is consecrated.\n\n> 5. The right of each member of the church to a voice in government and discipline (Mt. 18:15-20; 1 Cor. 5:1-7; Rev. 2-3).\n\n> 6. The freedom of the conscience and personal responsibility to God.\n\n> 7. Total independence of the church and state from each other.\n\nThe Church is not an organization based on the common brotherhood of man and resting upon humanitarian principles only. It's a supernatural body of believers filled with the presence and power of God as individuals conform to the gospel of Christ.\n\nThe Church is like a family in its social life, yet it's unlike a family in its formation. Each local church is a part of the universal Church in the broad sense. However, there are many in local churches that don't belong to the body of Christ, and therefore they are not a true part of the universal Church of the New Testament.\n\nThe following terms used of the Church in the New Testament reveal in more detail what the Church really is:\n\n## III. Names and Appellations of the New Testament Church\n\nUnder this heading we'll list the 105 names of the Church under three main points, and deal with each group in its relation to God, to the saved, and to the world.\n\n### 1. THE CHURCH AS "THE BODY OF CHRIST."\n\nThe Church is referred to by this term several times in various ways (1 Cor. 10:16; 12:27; Eph. 4:12). This truth is otherwise expressed in Scripture by the following terms: "one body" (Rom. 12:4-5; 1 Cor. 10:17; 12:12-13, 20; Eph. 2:16; 4:4; Col. 3:15); "the body" (1 Cor. 12:14-16, 18, 19, 22-25; Eph. 4:16; 5:23); "the whole body" (1 Cor. 12:17; Eph. 4:16); "his body" (Eph. 1:23; 5:30); "same body" (Eph. 3:6); "the body, the church" (Col. 1:18); "his body's sake, which is the church" (Col. 1:24); and "all the body" (Col. 2:19).\n\nAll these passages clearly show that all saved men are members of the body of Christ, which is the Church of the New Testament. In this sense — with all the saved being members of Christ — the Church is called "the Christ." Picture it this way: the Church as the body of Christ, and Christ as the head of the body, together make the complete Christ (1 Cor. 12:12).\n\nThis relationship speaks of the union of the saved with Christ. They are in Him and He is in them (2 Cor. 5:17; Col. 1:27; 2:10).\n\nThe Church is also called "one new man" (Eph. 2:15) and "a perfect man" (Eph. 4:13), made up of both Jews and Gentiles (Eph. 2:10-22; 3:1-6; 1 Cor. 12:12-13). These two terms beautifully convey the idea of Christ as the head of the body, the Church. The Church and Christ are so united as to be one body of people. Paul expressed it this way: "But he that is joined unto the Lord is one Spirit" (1 Cor. 6:17).\n\nThe "one new man" and the one "perfect man" is made up of only new and perfect men in Christ (2 Cor. 5:17; Phil. 3:15). It's true that not everyone in Christ is as mature and perfect in development as they will be if they continue to grow in grace and knowledge. But everyone in Christ is a new creature and is perfect as far as being cleansed and free from committing the sins that will damn the soul — and they will always be that way if they walk in Christ as they have received Him (Col. 2:6-7; Gal. 5:16-26; Rom. 8:1-13; 2 Cor. 5:17; 1 Jn. 2:29; 2:5-10; 5:1-4, 18).\n\nIt's generally held by Bible students that the Church is called a woman, a virgin, a lady, and a bride, and that feminine pronouns are always used in referring to the Church — but just the opposite is taught in Scripture. In no Scripture is the Church referred to by a feminine pronoun and as a virgin or a woman, married or unmarried.\n\nWhy should the Church be called a woman when it is the body of Christ? Was Christ a woman or a man? If He is a man, then why shouldn't the Church be called a "man" instead of a woman? This would be the only natural and scriptural analogy. (See Supplement Eight, Questions 1 and 2. We'll deal fully with the bride of Christ in Lesson Fifty-two.)\n\nThe members of the body of Christ have a threefold relationship to each other. They are "organically related" (1 Cor. 12:12-27; Rom. 12; Eph. 4); "co-operatively related" (1 Cor. 12:21-23; Eph. 4); and "sympathetically related" (1 Cor. 12:24-26; Rom. 12). They are related to Christ as being His body and members of His flesh and of His bones (Eph. 5:30). Christ is the head of the body in all things (Eph. 1:20-22; 5:23-27; Col. 1:18, 24; Eph. 4:7-11).\n\nThe members of the body of Christ are called "babes in Christ" (1 Cor. 3:1-3). All saved men are like this in the initial stage of the Christian experience, until they learn to discern between both good and evil and live according to that knowledge (Heb. 5:11-14; 1 Pet. 2:2).\n\nThey are called "the branches" (Jn. 15:1-10). All men in Christ are branches in Him who is the true vine and the root and fatness of the olive tree (Rom. 11:13-25). They are called "my friends" because of the close relationship of Christ and His "branches" (Jn. 15:15). As "branches" in the vine they are called "members of Christ" (1 Cor. 6:15; Eph. 5:30). They are also called "the ambassadors of Christ" and His "witnesses" (Acts 1:8). They are also spoken of as "the epistle of Christ" (2 Cor. 3:15).\n\nThe body of Christ is called "The church of God" (Acts 20:28; 1 Cor. 1:2; 10:32; 11:22; 15:9; 2 Cor. 1:1; Gal. 1:13; 1 Tim. 3:15) and "the church of the living God" (1 Tim. 3:15). It's called this because it belongs to God. Even as Christ belongs to God, so His Church would also be God's (1 Cor. 3:21-23; 11:3).\n\nIt is called "the pillar and stay of truth'' because it upholds the truth of God in the world (1 Tim. 3:15). The one great truth that the Church upholds in the universe is the headship of God (1 Cor. 11:3; Eph. 1:10; Phil. 2:11; Col. 1:15-19). This is the truth that Lucifer did not abide in (Jn. 8:44; Isa. 14:12-14; Ezek. 28:11-17). He is now denying it (1 Jn. 2:18-23).\n\nFailure to uphold the headship of God is what caused the Fall; so the redeemed are now given the responsibility to uphold it before men by an actual demonstration of it (Eph. 3:10; 4:11-16; 5:24; Heb. 13:7, 17, 24; Phil. 2:1-4; 1 Pet. 5:1-6).\n\nThe history of the devil and fallen angels and men reveals one effort after another to destroy the sovereignty of God and subjection to proper authority. The great struggle in the world today is that of rebelling against law and order. Rebellion is manifest among angels and men, including husbands and wives, parents and children, masters and servants, pastors and people, and rulers and subjects. The Church is called to be a stabilizing influence among all classes of people and even to demonstrate to the angels the manifold wisdom of God by an example of submission to God and to His law (Eph. 3:8-11).\n\nThe body of Christ is called by Jesus "my church" (Mt. 16:18). It's made up of people who belong to Jesus as well as to God. Those who are truly saved recognize both the Father and the Son. Those who deny the Son cannot have the Father, for He is the way to God (Jn. 14:1-14; 1 Jn. 2:22-25). The Church belongs to Jesus because He purchased it with His own blood and redeemed them to God (Acts 20:28; 1 Cor. 6:20; 1 Pet. 1:18-23).\n\nThe body of Christ is called "the church" in many passages (Mt. 18:17; Acts 2:47; 5:11; 8:1-5; 12:1-5; Eph. 1:22; 5:23-32; Col. 1:18, 24). Local churches are referred to as "the church" in a certain locality (Acts 11:22-26; 13:1; 14:27; Jas. 5:14).\n\nMany local churches are mentioned in Scripture: "the church that is in their house" (Rom. 16:5; 1 Cor. 16:19; Col. 4:15; Phlm. 2), "the church of the Thessalonians" (1 Thess. 1:1; 2 Thess. 1:1), "the church that is at Babylon" (1 Pet. 5:13) "the church of Ephesus" (Rev. 2:1), "the church in Smyrna" (Rev. 2:8), "the church in Pergamos" (Rev. 2:12), "the church in Thyatira" (Rev. 2:18), "the church in Sardis" (Rev. 3:1), "the church in Philadelphia" (Rev. 3:7), and "the church of the Laodiceans" (Col. 4:16; Rev. 3:14).\n\nTerms like the following are used in Scripture of the universal Church and of local churches: "every church" (Acts 14:23; 1 Cor. 4:17; Phil. 4:15), "the whole church" (Acts 15:22; Rom. 16:23; 1 Cor. 14:23), and "assembly" (Jas. 2:2).\n\nAll local churches on Earth make the visible and universal Church, and all truly saved men in all local churches make the invisible body of Christ. Universal terms are used of all local churches such as "the churches of God" (1 Cor. 11:16; 1 Thess. 2:14; 2 Thess. 1:4), "the churches of the Gentiles" (Rom. 16:16), "the churches of Christ" (Rom. 16:16), "the churches" (Acts 9:31; 15:41; 16:5; 19:37; 1 Cor. 14:34; 2 Cor. 8:19-24; Rev. 2:7, 11, 17, 29; 3:6, 13, 22; 22:16), "all churches" (1 Cor. 7:17; 2 Cor. 8:18; 11:28), and "all churches of the saints" (1 Cor. 14:33).\n\nSectional churches are referred to such as "the churches of Galatia" (1 Cor. 16:1; Gal. 1:2), "the churches of Asia" (1 Cor. 16:19), "the churches of Macedonia" (2 Cor. 8:1), "other churches" (2 Cor. 11:8; 12:13), "the churches of Judea" (Gal. 1:22), and "the seven churches which are in Asia" (Rev. 1:4, 11, 20).\n\nThe body of Christ is also called "the church of the firstborn" (Heb. 12:23), because it belongs to Christ, Who is "the firstborn of every creature . . . the first-begotten of the dead . . . the firstborn among many brethren" (Col. 1:15-18; Rom. 8:29; Rev. 1:5). It is also called "the general assembly" (Heb. 12:23). This term refers to the redeemed of all ages who will inhabit the Holy City, the New Jerusalem, which is called "the bride, the Lamb's wife" (Rev. 21:9-10).\n\n### 2. THE BODY OF CHRIST, THE CHURCH, AS THE DWELLING PLACE OF GOD AMONG MEN.\n\nThe New Testament speaks of the Church as follows: "the house of God" (1 Tim. 3:15), "the house" (Mt. 10:25; 2 Tim. 2:20-24; Heb. 3:6) "an habitation of God" (Eph. 2:22), "an holy temple" (Eph. 2:21), "a spiritual house" (1 Pet. 2:5), "the building" (Eph. 2:21; 1 Cor. 3:9), "God's building" (1 Cor. 3:9), "lively stones" (1 Pet. 2:5), "the temple of God" (1 Cor. 3:16), "the temple of the Living God" (2 Cor. 6:16), and the "house" of Christ (Heb. 3:6).\n\nAll these passages teach that God dwells in men and that they are the outward manifestations of divinity on Earth. It's similar to how an embassy represents a foreign nation on another country's soil — saved men are demonstrators of God and His power among the unsaved. They are to be God's representatives to bring the knowledge of God to all nations.\n\nScriptures speak of God dwelling in men (Jn. 14:23; 2 Cor. 6:14-18; Rom. 8:9; 1 Cor. 3:16-20), but this must be understood in the sense of union with men to a common end, as defined in Lesson Four, 1, 4.\n\n### 3. THE BODY OF CHRIST, THE CHURCH, AS "THE HOUSEHOLD OF GOD."\n\nThe New Testament speaks of the Church as "the household of God" (Eph. 2:19); "the household of faith" (Gal. 6:10); "children of God" (Mt. 5:9); "the children of the kingdom" (Mt. 13:38); "firstfruits of his creatures" (Jas. 1:18); "sons of God" and part of "the whole family" of God (Rom. 8:14; 1 Jn. 3:1; Eph. 3:15); "the elect of God" (Rom. 8:33; Titus 1:1); "the called of God" (Rom. 8:28; 1 Cor. 1:9, 24-26; Eph. 4:1-4; Col. 3:15); "a chosen generation" (1 Pet. 2:9); "the flock of God" (1 Pet. 5:2-3); "the flock" (Mt. 26:31; Lk. 12:32; Acts 20:28-29); "one fold" (Jn. 10:16); "the sheep" (Mt. 26:31; Jn. 10:1-18, 26-27; Heb. 13:20; 1 Pet. 2:25); and "my lambs" (Jn. 21:15).\n\nAs God's people, the members of the body of Christ are called "the sect of the Nazarenes" (Acts 24:5; Mt. 2:23; Jn. 1:45-46); "Christians" (Acts 11:26; 26:28; 1 Pet. 4:16); "saints" (60 times, Acts 9:13, 32, 41; 26:10; Rom. 1:7; 8:27; 12:13); "believers" (Acts 5:14; 1 Tim. 4:12); "beloved of God" (Rom. 1:7; 2 Thess. 2:13); "holy brethren" (Heb. 3:1); the true "circumcision" (Phil. 3:3; Rom. 2:29; Col. 2:11-17); "fellow-citizens" (Eph. 2:19); "disciples" (Mt. 5:1; 10:1; Jn. 20:19); "the faithful" (Eph. 1:1; Col. 1:2); "followers of God" (Eph. 5:1); "heirs of God" (Rom. 8:17); "God's husbandry" (1 Cor. 3:9); "God's heritage" (1 Pet. 5:3); "kings and priests" (Rev. 1:6; 5:10); "a royal priesthood" (1 Pet. 2:9); "a peculiar people" (1 Pet. 2:9); "an holy priesthood" (1 Pet. 2:5); "vessels of mercy" (Rom. 9:23); "vessels of honor" (2 Tim. 2:21); "wise men" (1 Cor. 10:15); "worshippers" (Heb. 10:2); "workers" (2 Cor. 6:1); "a sweet savour of Christ" (2 Cor. 2:15); "the righteousness of God" (2 Cor. 5:21); "ministers of God" (2 Cor. 6:4); "the salt of the earth" (Mt. 5:13); "one bread" (1 Cor. 10:17); and "the light of the world" (Mt. 5:14).\n\nThese many passages not only reveal what saved men are called, but they also make clear the true character of believers. They are all Christians, meaning "Christ-like" — not only in word but in deed and in truth. They are all holy, faithful to the truth, and followers of God according to the gospel, and when they cease to be this they no longer are part of the family of God. Just plain common sense proves this as well as all Scripture, as we have seen in Lesson Nineteen.\n\nWhen they cease being like Christ they are no longer Christians. When they cease being holy they are unholy (1 Jn. 3:8-10). When they cease to keep God's laws they break them and incur the death penalty (Ezek. 18:4; Rom. 8:1-13). When they cease to be followers of God they become followers of the devil (1 Jn. 3:8). When they cease being a peculiar people they are like the world, and the world loves them (Jn. 15:18-20). And when they transgress the law of God they are plain sinners like all others who break the law (Jas. 5:19-20; Gal. 5:19-21).\n\nBelievers are compared to a human body, a man, a house, a building, a temple, a household, a pillar, a flock, a sheep-fold, sheep, lambs, babes, branches, vessels, salt, and lights, and other symbols. How little do modern men realize that they are called to manifest certain characteristics of all these symbols! The many lessons that can be given to men by these figures are too numerous to mention in this short study of the body of Christ.\n\nAnyone who is open to truth can see from these expressions found in Scripture that any attempt to make any one local church or denomination the New Testament Church is unscriptural. Each of these expressions has a different phase of truth to express about the Church on Earth. Some of these names, titles, and designations apply to the body of Christ in general and some to only one part of it in particular, as represented in the local churches.\n\nLocal churches in the world today have a right to call themselves by any of these names if the name applies to that particular body of believers, or any group of churches can go by any one of these names if the churches are qualified to live up to the name they choose to go by.\n\n## IV. The Calling and Purpose of the New Testament Church\n\nThe present Church is called out of all nations to do the same work of evangelizing the world that Israel and others were called for in other ages. Its calling is heavenly, while Israel's calling is earthly; its blessings are mainly spiritual, while Israel's are mainly earthly.\n\nThe Church is to become a heavenly people while Israel will always be an earthly people, except those who die in Christ before the final and eternal restoration of Israel to her original calling.\n\nThe mission of the Church during its earthly pilgrimage is to bring men to the knowledge of the truth, to learn the art of submission, and to prepare for eternal service as rulers over earthly nations forever. It is not to be an outward organization only, but a living organism. It is not to be a social club, a place of amusement, or a house of merchandise.\n\nIts members must be "lights" to shine (Mt. 5:14; Phil. 2:15); "salt" to preserve (Mt. 5:13); "evangels" to preach (Mk. 16:15-20); "examples" to live like Christ (Col. 1:26-27); "ambassadors" to represent the kingdom of God (2 Cor. 5:20); "pillars" to uphold the truth of God (1 Tim. 3:15); "members" of the invisible body of Christ to form a habitation for God (Eph. 2:19-22); and men who seek to meet all the above-stated qualifications of true members of the body of Christ and who do His whole will.\n\n## V. The Formation of the New Testament Church\n\nThe body of Christ is being formed or built by the Spirit upon the foundation of the apostles and prophets, Jesus Christ Himself being the chief cornerstone (Eph. 2:19-22). Of course, Christ is the builder, but the Spirit is the main agent in the making of the body (Mt. 16:18; 1 Cor. 12:13).\n\nThe Spirit uses other agencies, such as the different ministries, to form the body of Christ (1 Cor. 12:1-28; Eph. 4:7-11). Each person who repents is re-created by the Spirit and made a member of that body (1 Cor. 12:12-13).\n\nWhen a person is born-again he is saved and redeemed from all sin and made a new creature in Christ (1 Jn. 2:29; 3:5-10; 5:1-4, 18). He is baptized into Christ and into His body by the agency of the Holy Spirit (1 Cor. 12:12-13; Gal. 3:26-29), as we shall see more fully in Lesson Thirty-four.\n\n## VI. The Origin of the New Testament Church\n\nThe Church had its origin in the mind of God "before the foundation of the world" (Eph. 1:3-5). It did not become a reality until the earthly ministry of Christ. Since then it has been in the building and will not be complete until it is presented to Christ (Eph. 5:25-27; 1 Thess. 4:13-18; 1 Cor. 15:51-58).\n\nThe Church then is older in election than the kingdom prepared "since the foundation of the world" (Mt. 25:34). Christ first announced the building of the Church. It is now being built upon the foundation of the apostles and prophets, Jesus Christ Himself being the chief cornerstone (Eph. 2:19-22; 1 Cor. 3:11).\n\n## VII. Who Belongs to the New Testament Church?\n\nThe people saved from the ministry of Christ to the rapture of the Church belong to the present Church. The Old Testament saints could not belong to the present, called-out body of people for the reason that they did not live during this time. For this same reason the Church now could not have belonged to any company of saints in the Old Testament times.\n\nThe 144,000 Jews who are to be sealed after the rapture of the Church could not belong to the present Church, for they are saved and sealed after the complete company of this period is caught out of the world (Rev. 7:1-8; 14:1-5). The future tribulation-saints do not belong to the present company of saints because they are saved and martyred after the rapture of the Church (Rev. 7:9-17; 15:2-4; 20:4).\n\nAll these different companies of redeemed will have their respective places in God's future plan as each individual is rewarded at the Judgment Seat of Christ according to the deeds done in the body, whether they be good or bad. All of them will finally be united in one family living in the New Jerusalem, the bride of Christ, as will be seen in Lesson Fifty-two. (See Chapter Nine, Point 3, and Chapter Forty-five of my book *Revelation Expounded*.)\n\n## VIII. Was the Church Founded on the Day of Pentecost?\n\nThis is the general conception, but it's not true. The Church really began with the ministry of Christ, for He is the one who founded the Church (Mt. 16:18). He is the head of it (Eph. 1:21-23).\n\nThe law and the prophets were until John; since then the kingdom of God is preached and all men press into it (Lk. 16:16). God hath "set some in the Church, first apostles" (1 Cor. 12:28; Eph. 2:19; 4:11). When did He set apostles in the Church? Certainly before Pentecost (Mt. 10; Acts 1:15-26).\n\nWhat is the Church? It is a "called-out" body of people from the world. Weren't believers before Pentecost "called-out" of the world, and weren't they just as much followers of Christ before as after Pentecost? (Jn. 17).\n\nThe apostles were the first chosen and "called out" (Mt. 10), then seventy others (Lk. 10) and later many others were chosen (Acts 1:12-15; 1 Cor. 15:6). All of this selecting was done before Pentecost.\n\nWhat took place at Pentecost was the enduement of power upon the newly formed Church (Jn. 14:16, 17, 26; 15:26; 16:7-15; Lk. 24:49; Acts 1:4-8; 2:1-47). Think of it like this: the Church already existed, but at Pentecost it received its power source — like a building that's already constructed but then gets its electricity turned on.\n\nAt Pentecost 3,000 more were added to the called-out people, the Church (Acts 2:40-46). A few days later 5,000 more were added (Acts 4:4). These additions still continue and will continue until Christ comes in the air to take away the New Testament Church, together with the redeemed of all ages past. Then this particular called-out body of people will\n\n---\n\nThe New Testament Church was designed to be a living organism, not a managed institution — the actual body of Christ on earth, carrying His authority, expressing His character, and finishing His mission. So here's the question to take with you: Are you a functioning member of a local body of believers — genuinely connected, contributing, and accountable? Or are you attending church as a consumer, sitting in the audience of someone else's ministry while the body tries to function without the part you were made to be?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk48j001eewhjmlcfccvq	cmnknk48e000xewhj49ao5az0	Lesson 30 — The Truth About the Baptism of the Holy Spirit	READING	1858	16	\N	t	Few doctrines have divided the modern church more sharply than the baptism in the Holy Spirit. Entire denominations have split over it. Believers have been told it's essential for salvation — wrong. Others have been told it ceased with the apostles — also wrong. Both extremes are reading the same Bible and reaching opposite conclusions. The reason isn't a lack of intelligence. It's a failure to distinguish what Scripture actually says from what various traditions have added to it.\n\nLast time we examined the Church as Christ's body — designed to carry His authority and mission. Now we turn to a pivotal question for every member of that body: What is the baptism of the Holy Spirit, who receives it, and what is it for?\n\nLet's walk through what the Bible actually teaches about this important subject.\n\n![Lesson 30 — The Truth About the Baptism of the Holy Spirit](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsgZ00yEax52U6tuomWf38j1QMHRBV4griYbAZ)\n\n## I. General Facts About the Spirit Baptism\n\n> **1.** Most people who've grown up in mainline Protestant or Catholic traditions have been taught, implicitly or explicitly, that the baptism of the Holy Spirit is either the same as conversion or was a temporary apostolic phenomenon that no longer occurs. But look at what Scripture actually presents.\n\nThere really is a baptism in the Holy Spirit — this isn't something people made up (Mt. 3:11; 20:22-23; Mk. 1:8; Lk. 3:16; Jn. 1:31-34; Acts 1:4-8; 2:1-39; 8:14-20; 9:17; 10:44-48; 11:14-18; 15:7-11; 19:1-7; Gal. 3:14; Heb. 6:2).\n\n> **2.** Jesus Himself was the first person to be baptized in the Holy Spirit (Mt. 3:16-17; 20:22-23; Lk. 3:21-22; 4:16-21; Jn. 1:31-34; 3:34; Acts 10:38).\n\n> **3.** This baptism fulfilled Old Testament prophecy (Isa. 11:2; 42:1-7; 61:1-2; Mt. 11:2-6; 12:18; Lk. 4:16-21).\n\n> **4.** Regular people weren't baptized in the Spirit until Pentecost — and that couldn't happen until after Jesus was glorified (Jn. 7:37-39; 14:16-17, 26; 16:13-15; Acts 1:4-8; 2:1-21, 33-39).\n\n> **5.** Now here's something important to understand: even though people weren't baptized in the Spirit until Pentecost, they absolutely did receive the Holy Spirit in their lives in various ways throughout all of history up to that point.\n>\n> Old Testament believers — including Jesus's own disciples before Pentecost — experienced all kinds of spiritual blessings. They had physical and material blessings from God. They bore the fruit of the Spirit. They operated in the gifts of the Spirit (except tongues and interpretation). They experienced different measures of the Spirit, the new birth, sanctification, justification, and countless other benefits of the gospel, including power to witness.\n>\n> They were "filled" with the Spirit. They had the Spirit "in" and "upon" them. They were anointed with the Spirit and given power in varying degrees. And yet — they were never baptized in the Spirit until Pentecost came. For proof of all this, see Lesson Eighteen, Point IX.\n>\n> Think about John the Baptist. He was "filled" with the Spirit from birth, but he was never baptized in the Spirit (Lk. 1:15-17). Mary was "filled" with the Spirit before Jesus was even born, but she wasn't baptized in the Spirit until thirty-three years later at Pentecost (Lk. 1:41-56; Acts 1:12–2:4). Others were "filled" with the Spirit before Pentecost too, but that wasn't the same as being baptized in the Spirit (Lk. 1:41-67; 2:25-38).\n>\n> Even Jesus Himself was "filled" with the Spirit from birth (Isa. 50:4-5; Lk. 2:40, 52), but He wasn't baptized in the Spirit until He was about thirty years old (Mt. 3:16-17; 20:22-23; Jn. 3:34; Lk. 4:16-21; Acts 10:38; Isa. 11:1-2; 42:1-5; 61:1-2).\n>\n> The disciples were "filled" with the Spirit before Pentecost (Mt. 10:1-8, 16-20; 16:17; Mk. 6:7-13; Lk. 10:1-24; Jn. 17:14, 16, 22; 20:22), but they weren't baptized in the Spirit until three years later at Pentecost (Acts 1:4-8; 2:1-4, 16, 33-34; 10:44-48; 11:14-18; 15:7-11).\n>\n> Let's be clear about the disciples' spiritual condition before Pentecost. They were saved, born-again, converted — their names were written in Heaven (Mt. 4:20; 10:1-8, 16-20; Lk. 10:20; Jn. 17). They were "clean" (the Greek word means "pure," as in Mt. 5:8; 1 Tim. 1:5; 3:9; 2 Tim. 1:3; 2:22; Rev. 15:6; 19:8, 14) and therefore sanctified (Jn. 13:10-11; 15:3; 17:6, 14). They were "not of the world even as Christ was not of the world" (Jn. 14:16-17; 15:19; 17:14, 16). They were fully trained in God's ways by Jesus Himself (Jn. 15:15; 17:6, 14). They were called "sheep" (Mt. 10:16). They were "of one accord and one mind" (Acts 1:12–2:1). And they were even commissioned to evangelize the world before they were baptized in the Spirit (Mt. 28:19-20; Mk. 16:15-20; Acts 1:4-8).\n>\n> The disciples had the Spirit in them and were anointed to preach, teach, heal, and cast out demons (Mt. 10:1-8, 20; Mk. 6:7-13; Lk. 10:1-19). They had been baptized in water and had baptized others (Jn. 1:35-46; 3:22; 4:1-2). They had kept God's Word (Jn. 17:6). They had eternal life (Jn. 17:2-3; 10:27-29). They had experienced spiritual revelations (Mt. 11:25; 16:16-17; Jn. 15:15). They had received the glory of God (Jn. 17:22). They had learned to pray (Mt. 6:1-15; 7:7-11; 18; 19; Lk. 11; 18). They had seen thousands healed and had healed many people themselves (Mt. 8:1-17; 9:35; 10:1-7; Mk. 6:7-13; Lk. 10:1-19). They had confessed Jesus as the Son of God (Mt. 16:16-17; Jn. 6:67-69). They had watched many backslide while they stayed true (Jn. 6:66-71). They had been branches in the vine for years (Jn. 15:1-17). They had so many spiritual blessings — all before they were baptized in the Spirit.\n>\n> People in the Old Testament received different measures of the Spirit and did work according to whatever measure of Spirit and power they received. This is clear from both Testaments.\n>\n> In Num. 11:16-25, the one portion of the Spirit that Moses had was divided into seventy-one parts. Elisha received a "double portion" of Elijah's Spirit, and the record shows he did twice as many miracles that were twice as great (2 Ki. 2:9). Elijah did fifteen miracles; Elisha did thirty. Elijah shut the heavens for 3½ years; Elisha shut them for seven (1 Ki. 17:1–2 Ki. 13:21; Jas. 5:17).\n>\n> John the Baptist had the same measure of the Spirit that Elijah had — not the "double portion" of Elisha or the seventy-one portions of Moses (Lk. 1:15-17). The disciples had a great measure of the Spirit and power of Christ (Mt. 10:1-8, 20; Mk. 6:7-13; Lk. 10:1-20), but they didn't have the fullness of the Spirit until Pentecost (Lk. 24:49; Jn. 7:37-39; 14:12-17, 26; 15:26; 16:13-15; Acts 1:4-8; 2:1-39). Jesus had the Spirit "without measure" (Jn. 3:34).\n>\n> Paul speaks of "the supply of the Spirit" (Phil. 1:19), "the earnest of the Spirit" (2 Cor. 1:22; 5:5), and "the fulness of God" (Eph. 3:19). John speaks of men receiving "of his Spirit" and "of his fulness" — implying only a part of the fulness of God (Jn. 1:16; 1 Jn. 4:13).\n>\n> Today we all recognize that people have different measures of the Spirit, power, faith, and other spiritual blessings. So it shouldn't be hard to understand that these same experiences existed both before and after Pentecost. If everyone today had the exact same gifts and blessings, then everyone could do the same works and have the same success.\n\n> **6.** Every believer today receives the Spirit of adoption and is filled with the Spirit in a measure when they're born again: "Ye are not in the flesh, but in the Spirit, if so be that the Spirit of God dwell in you. Now if any man have not the Spirit of Christ, he is none of his... ye have received the Spirit of adoption, whereby we cry, Abba, Father. The Spirit itself beareth witness with our spirit, that we are the children of God" (Rom. 8:9-16; 1 Cor. 3:16; 12:13). You simply cannot be a child of God without receiving the Spirit in some measure.\n>\n> It's by the Spirit that people are "convicted" (Jn. 16:7-11), "born-again" (Jn. 3:1-8), able to "call Jesus 'Lord'" (1 Cor. 12:3) and "God 'Father'" (Rom. 8:15; Gal. 4:6), "baptized into the body of Christ" (1 Cor. 12:13) and "into Christ" (Gal. 3:27-29), "changed into the image of Christ" (2 Cor. 3:18), "made new creatures" (2 Cor. 5:17), "freed from sin" (Rom. 8:1-13), "kept free from sin" (Gal. 5:16-24; Rom. 6:3-23), "led and receive witness of sonship" (Rom. 8:14-16; 1 Jn. 5:6), given "access to God" (Eph. 2:18), given "gifts" (1 Cor. 12:14), given "life" (2 Cor. 3:6), given "liberty" (2 Cor. 3:17), given "love, joy, peace, etc." (Gal. 5:21-22), "washed, sanctified and justified" (1 Cor. 6:11; 2 Thess. 2:12; 1 Pet. 1:2), enabled to "worship God" (Phil. 3:3), given "grace" (Heb. 10:29), "invited to Christ" (Rev. 22:17), and given "many other spiritual blessings" (Rom. 5:5; 14:17; Eph. 1:3).\n>\n> Here's the key: you can have all of these blessings and still not be baptized in the Spirit, because people in all ages before Pentecost had these same experiences.\n\n## II. When the Baptism in the Spirit Was First Given\n\nThe following facts prove that no one before Pentecost — besides Christ — ever received the Spirit-baptism:\n\n> **1.** Several prophets foretold a great outpouring of the Holy Spirit upon all flesh (Isa. 28:9-12; 32:15; 44:3; Ezek. 39:29; Joel 2:28-29). This outpouring had to be something the Old Testament saints didn't experience, or it wouldn't have been prophesied as something still to come. When you study these passages, you'll notice they have a Millennial setting, but both Peter and Paul taught that these passages were also being fulfilled in this present age. Compare Isa. 28:9-12 with 1 Cor. 14:21-22 and Joel 2:28-29 with Acts 2:15-21.\n\n> **2.** Jesus was the first ever to receive the baptism in the Spirit — the Spirit in all fullness — "for God giveth not the Spirit by measure unto him" (Jn. 3:34). This implies that God did give the Spirit by measure to everyone else before Christ. We've already proven this in Point I, 5 above. That this Spirit "without measure" was the baptism in the Spirit is clear from Mt. 3:16-17; 20:22-23. It was the fullness of the Spirit and power foretold for the Messiah (Isa. 11:1-2; 42:1-7; 61:1-2), fulfilled in Mt. 3:16-17; 11:2-6; 12:18; Lk. 4:16-21; Acts 10:38.\n\n> **3.** John wrote that "the Holy Ghost was not yet given; because that Jesus was not yet glorified" (Jn. 7:37-39). This couldn't be referring to the different measures, fillings, and blessings given to people before Pentecost (as we discussed in Point I, 5 above and in Lesson Eighteen, Point IX), because those experiences had been given before Jesus was glorified. This could only refer to the baptism in the Holy Spirit — the Spirit in all fullness — that people wouldn't receive until Jesus was glorified. Peter taught at Pentecost that the baptism in the Spirit was given precisely because Jesus was glorified (Acts 2:33).\n\n> **4.** John the Baptist taught that Christ would baptize His followers in the Spirit (Mt. 3:11; Jn. 1:31-34). This couldn't happen until the Messiah came. John himself was "filled" with the Spirit from birth, but when he saw Jesus, he desired the baptism in the Spirit that Christ was going to baptize people with (Mt. 3:14).\n\n> **5.** It wasn't until Jesus came that He promised the Spirit-baptism to people (Jn. 7:37-39; 14:16-17, 26; 15:26; 16:13-15; Acts 1:4-8). Peter then promised it to people after that (Acts 2:38-39; 5:32).\n\n> **6.** The Spirit-baptism was first experienced by people at Pentecost (Jn. 7:39; Acts 1:4-8; 2:1-4, 33). After that, it was given to the Samaritans (Acts 8:5-20), to Paul (Acts 9:17), to the Gentiles (Acts 10:44-48; 11:14-18; 15:7-11), and to the followers of John (Acts 18:24-28; 19:1-7).\n\n## III. What the Baptism in the Spirit is Not\n\nSpirit-baptism is not a "filling" or a "measure" of the Spirit. It's not receiving the gifts or fruit of the Spirit. It's not salvation, sanctification, justification, or any one — or even all combined — of the spiritual blessings we listed in Point I above and in Lesson Eighteen, Point IX.\n\nNone of these experiences should be mistaken for the baptism in the Holy Spirit, nor should they be used as proof of such baptism. Here's why: God's people in every Old Testament age before Pentecost had all these experiences, while the baptism in the Spirit wasn't given to anyone until Pentecost.\n\nJesus plainly said that the Holy Spirit in baptismal measure was not given because He was not yet glorified (Jn. 7:37-39). Peter confirmed this on the day of Pentecost (Acts 2:33).\n\nIf the Spirit baptism wasn't given before Pentecost, but people did have all the blessings we mentioned before Pentecost, then there's a world of difference between the baptism in the Spirit and all these other blessings. These blessings will naturally be present in the lives of those baptized in the Spirit, but they don't constitute that baptism, and they shouldn't be taken as evidence of it.\n\nThe baptism in the Spirit is the Spirit without measure (Jn. 3:34; Mt. 20:20-23; Lk. 4:18-21; Acts 10:38). All these other blessings are simply different measures of the Spirit — only a part of the fullness of God.\n\n## IV. What the Baptism in the Spirit Is\n\nThe Spirit-baptism is the immersion or burial of the believer in the Spirit. At this moment, you receive the Spirit in your life in all "fullness" and "without measure," and you're "endued with power from on high" to do the works of Christ (Lk. 24:49; Jn. 7:37-39; 14:12-17; Acts 1:4-8; 3:6; 5:12-16; 19:11; Mk. 16:16-20; Heb. 2:3-4; etc.).\n\nIt's the same full anointing of the Spirit that Christ received (Isa. 11:1-2; 42:1-7; 61:1; Mt. 11:4-6; 12:18; 20:22-23; Lk. 4:16-21; Jn. 3:34; 14:12; Acts 10:38).\n\nIt's the Spirit coming "in, upon, filling, overwhelming, infusing, anointing, and enduing with full power" — not just a "measure" like in Old Testament days. It's the Spirit taking full possession of the believer to live, speak, and work through them in the same degree that was manifested through Christ and the apostles. It's the fullness of what people had only in part before Pentecost.\n\n## V. A Filling and a Baptism Illustrated\n\nThink of it like a glass and a pitcher of water. To whatever extent you pour water into a glass, to that extent it's filled. You can fill the glass by pouring different amounts into it at different times until it's full, or you can fill it all at once. You can even keep pouring until the glass is overflowing — but it's still not baptized. It's just full and running over.\n\nBut if you take that glass and immerse it — bury it completely in the water — then it's both filled and baptized. This explains how the disciples were all "filled" (Acts 2:4) as well as "baptized" (Acts 1:4-5). A filling always accompanies a baptism, but a baptism doesn't accompany a mere filling.\n\nNow here's the thing: once you're "filled" and "baptized," you have to stay that way. The minute you step out of the "fullness" of the Holy Spirit and start living in self, you're no longer baptized — just like the glass wouldn't be baptized anymore if you pulled it out of the water.\n\nLike that glass, you can get out of the baptism or the fullness of the Spirit and still retain a "measure" or be "filled" with the Spirit like you were before being baptized.\n\nChrist stayed baptized in the Spirit by yielding to God and by praying daily for more virtue and power to bless everyone who came to Him (Mt. 14:23; 17:21; Mk. 1:35; 6:46; Lk. 5:16; 6:12, 16; 9:18, 28-29; 22:32; Jn. 17; Heb. 5:7). The disciples also lived in prayer and received new fillings and anointings from time to time (Acts 4:31; 6:4).\n\nThere's no such thing as "once baptized in the Spirit, always baptized in the Spirit" — any more than there's "once saved, always saved," "once in grace, always in grace," "once healed, always healed," "once alive, always alive," "once in sin, always in sin," "once a child of the devil, always a child of the devil," or — picture it this way — "once full of chicken, always full of chicken."\n\nNo such principle exists! Common sense alone tells us that certain conditions must be met continuously to maintain any position in life or in God. If it takes complete surrender and yieldedness to God to be wholly possessed with the Spirit, it will take that same consecration daily to maintain a life in all the fullness of God.\n\nIf you want to always be in grace and be saved, stay in grace and stay saved. If you want to always be a child of the devil, keep sinning. And if you want to always be full of chicken — well, we all know what you'd have to do! If you ever get baptized in the Spirit, you must constantly maintain that place in God, or you'll lose the spiritual anointing.\n\n## VI. The Purpose of the Baptism in the Spirit\n\nThe purpose of the Spirit-baptism is not to save your soul, sanctify you, make you a child of God, or qualify you for the rapture. These ideas are unscriptural, and here's the proof:\n\n> **1.** All the Old Testament saints and the disciples before Pentecost were saved, sanctified, justified, and free from all sin — as we proved in Lesson Eighteen, Point IX and Point I above. Since they were never baptized in the Spirit, the purpose of Spirit-baptism clearly wasn't to give them these blessings.\n\n> **2.** Jesus was always sinless, yet He needed the Spirit-baptism to have power for His mighty works. So in His case, the purpose of Spirit-baptism wasn't to sanctify Him, save His soul, or make Him a child of God.\n\n> **3.** In Point I above, we showed many Scriptures proving that people receive the Spirit in a measure at the new birth — and these blessings are not the Spirit-baptism.\n\n> **4.** The Spirit-baptism was promised only to saved people after they had repented, been baptized in water, were obedient to God, and had asked for it as a child of God (Mt. 3:11; Jn. 1:31-34; 14:12-17, 26; 15:26; 16:13-15; Acts 1:4-8; 2:38-39; 5:32; Lk. 11:7-13).\n\n> **5.** It was given only to saved people. The disciples were saved and sanctified before receiving the baptism in the Spirit at Pentecost, as proven in Lesson Eighteen, Point IX and in Point I above. The Samaritans were saved, sanctified, baptized in water, healed, and had great joy before Peter and John laid hands on them so they could receive the Spirit-baptism (Acts 8:5-20). The disciples of John were saved, sanctified, and baptized in water years before Paul laid hands on them so they could receive the Spirit-baptism (Acts 19:1-7).\n>\n> The only two recorded cases of people receiving the baptism in the Spirit at the same time they were saved are Paul and the Gentiles (Acts 9:17; 22:16; 10:43-48; 11:14-18; 15:7-11). In both cases, they were "granted repentance unto life" and their hearts were "purified by faith" — or they wouldn't have received the Spirit-baptism. So even here, the purpose of this baptism wasn't to give them those blessings.\n\n> **6.** The baptism in the Spirit is not given to qualify saints for the rapture. Not a single passage mentions this subject in connection with the rapture. Old Testament saints will go in the rapture, and they were never baptized in the Spirit. Multitudes of people throughout all ages will go in the rapture who have never even heard of the baptism in the Spirit.\n>\n> Here are the actual qualifications to go in the rapture:\n>\n> - **(1)** Be "in Christ" (1 Thess. 4:16). Being "in Christ" means you must be a "new creature" (2 Cor. 5:17; Eph. 4:22-24).\n>\n> - **(2)** Be "Christ's at His coming" (1 Cor. 15:23). Being "Christ's" means the flesh with its affections and lusts have been crucified (Gal. 5:24).\n>\n> - **(3)** Be "blessed and holy" (Rev. 20:4-6; Heb. 12:14).\n>\n> - **(4)** Be "good" (Jn. 5:28-29; Gal. 5:16-24; 2 Cor. 7:1).\n>\n> - **(5)** Be in "the body of Christ," the Church which He is coming for (Eph. 5:25-27; 1 Cor. 13; Eph. 1:22-23; Col. 1:18, 24).\n>\n> - **(6)** Be "pure" (1 Jn. 3:1-3; Mt. 5:8; Eph. 5:27).\n>\n> - **(7)** Be walking "in the light" (1 Jn. 1:7; 2:6-11; 3:9-10).\n>\n> - **(8)** Be "worthy" (Lk. 21:34-36).\n>\n> The sole purpose of the Spirit-baptism is to clothe you with power from on High to do the same works that Christ did. Christ plainly taught that such an enduement of power was necessary to confirm the Word of God (Mk. 16:20; Heb. 2:3-4), to continue the things "that Jesus began both to do and teach" (Acts 1:1-2; Mt. 28:20), to make people successful witnesses (Acts 1:8), and to prove that believers are genuine and divinely sent to represent God (Mk. 16:15-20; Jn. 14:12; Acts 1:8).\n>\n> Christ reminded people that if they wouldn't believe in Him for what He said, they should believe in Him because of His works (Jn. 5:20, 36; 9:4; 10:25, 32, 37-39; 14:11; 15:24). It was by such works that people would recognize the Messiah (Isa. 11:1-2; 42:1-7; 61:1; Mt. 11:2-6). It was by the good works of the disciples that God would be glorified (Mt. 5:16; 2 Tim. 2:21; Titus 3:1; Heb. 13:21).\n>\n> Paul used his power to do miracles as proof that he was sent by God (2 Cor. 6:4-7; 10:3-11; 12:12; 13:10; Gal. 3:3-5; 1 Thess. 1:5; Heb. 2:3-4); to defeat and punish those who opposed the gospel and didn't live right (Acts 13:6-11; 1 Cor. 4:18-21; 5:1-5); to make people obedient (Rom. 15:18-19, 29; 1 Cor. 4:18-21); and to establish people's faith in God (Rom. 1:11; 1 Cor. 2:1-5).\n>\n> Here's the bottom line: if you're not able to do the same things Christ and the apostles did, it stands to reason you don't have the same enduement of power they had. The same enduement of power today would accomplish the same things it did in the early Church.\n>\n> Think about how this principle worked in Scripture: the same portion of the Spirit that Moses had, when divided among the seventy elders, accomplished the same work it had through Moses before its division (Numbers 11:16-25). The "double portion" of Elijah's Spirit caused Elisha to do exactly twice as much and perform miracles twice as great. Elijah did sixteen miracles; Elisha did thirty-two. Elijah shut the heavens for 3½ years; Elisha shut them for seven years — proving he had a double portion of the Spirit that rested on Elijah. Read all of 1 Ki. 18:1–2 Ki. 13:21 for a record of these miracles. If the same portion of Samson's Spirit came upon anyone else, it would accomplish similar results.\n>\n> The law of nature and the law of mechanics prove that two persons or things of equal power can do exactly the same things. It's also true that the stronger of the two will be able to do more than the weaker one, and each one can do exactly according to the degree of power they possess.\n>\n> To whatever extent you're endued with power from on High, you'll be able to work. Christ received the Spirit "without measure" and was unlimited in His power (Jn. 3:34). The Old Testament saints were able to do works according to their power. The disciples did works according to the power given them. The same principle holds true today.\n>\n> You're promised unlimited power, and it can be attained in this life if you meet the conditions (Lk. 24:49; Jn. 14:12; Mk. 9:23; 11:22-24). We'll prove this from Scripture in Point VIII below.\n>\n> So we conclude: the real purpose of the Spirit-baptism is for the enduement of power for service — not to save or sanctify you, and not to qualify you for the rapture.\n\n## VII. Importance of the Spirit-Baptism\n\nIf the chief purpose of the Spirit-baptism is to give you power to accomplish what we've described above, then this is the all-important experience for believers today who want to be successful in gospel work.\n\nJesus commanded His disciples to teach all nations throughout this age "to observe all things whatsoever I have commanded you" (Mt. 28:20). That being baptized in the Spirit was a definite command to them is clear from Lk. 24:49; Acts 1:4-5. If it was a command for them, it's still commanded for us, because we're still in this age.\n\nThis enduement of power for service is promised to every believer in the simplest language possible (Mt. 3:11; Lk. 11:13; 24:49; Jn. 1:31-34; 7:37-39; 14:12-17, 26; 15:26; 16:13-15; Mk. 16:15-20; Acts 1:4-8; 2:16-21, 38-39; 5:32; 19:17). In fact, it's one of the two things Christ died for (Gal. 3:14).\n\nJesus made it absolutely clear to the disciples that they had to be baptized in the Spirit before going out to evangelize the world (Lk. 24:40; Jn. 14:12, 17; 16:7-15; Acts 1:4-8). Only one hundred and twenty of many hundreds of followers (1 Cor. 15:6) felt it was necessary to obey (Acts 1:4-5; 12-15), and these are the only ones who received the enduement of power at Pentecost (Acts 2:1-33).\n\nFor you to receive this power today is even more needful! There are infinitely more demon-possessed people, more sick and diseased people, more false doctrines, more false teachers, and more divisions among Christians than at any other time in history.\n\nThe Word of God needs confirmation today just as before to convince people of the truth. This is the greatest need of the modern Church. If God could get people to believe in these things again and "tarry until" as they did of old, we would again see mighty works of power and a new type of Christianity.\n\nLet no one complain about lacking power to win souls, heal the sick, and do the works of Christ. Instead, let everyone quit their laziness and unbelief and seek God again "till he come and rain righteousness" on us (Hos. 10:12). Then God will show His mighty works again (\n\n---\n\nThe baptism of the Holy Spirit is a genuine, distinct experience available to every believer — not for salvation, but for power in ministry and deeper intimacy with God. Scripture never tells believers to stop seeking it or to settle for less than what the early Church received. So here's the honest question: Have you received the baptism of the Holy Spirit? Not 'do you believe in it theologically' — but have you actually, personally, experienced the empowerment Jesus promised in Acts 1:8? If not, what's standing between you and asking?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk48j001gewhjhkjftqum	cmnknk48e000xewhj49ao5az0	Lesson 31 — The Kingdom of Heaven and the Kingdom of God	READING	3541	18	\N	t	Two phrases appear all over the Gospels — 'Kingdom of Heaven' and 'Kingdom of God' — and most readers treat them as interchangeable synonyms. They're not. Failing to distinguish them is one of the primary reasons so many people misapply Jesus's teaching, confuse Israel's promises with the Church's promises, and end up with a distorted picture of both the present age and the one to come.\n\nLast time we examined the baptism of the Holy Spirit — distinct from salvation, available to all believers. Now we turn to two terms that Jesus used constantly and that most Christians have never been taught to distinguish: the Kingdom of Heaven and the Kingdom of God.\n\n![Lesson 31 — The Kingdom of Heaven and the Kingdom of God](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OssHEPxknIFYWHUNhmRXnATM2qSZzVGaodwQ5D)\n\n## I. Definitions of the Kingdom of Heaven and Kingdom of God\n\nThe Greek word for "kingdom" is *basileia*, meaning "a realm" or "a region governed by a king." Think about it this way: in recent centuries, some countries that were once kingdoms became republics when they no longer had a king. Without a king, there's no kingdom.\n\nThe country itself stays the same—same people, same cities, same mountains and rivers—but it's no longer a kingdom. You simply cannot have a kingdom without a king.\n\n### 1. THE KINGDOM OF HEAVEN\n\nHere are all the passages in the Bible where "the kingdom of Heaven" appears: Mt. 3:2; 4:17; 5:3, 10, 19, 20; 7:21; 8:11; 10:7; 11:11-12; 13:11, 24, 31, 33, 44, 45, 47, 52; 16:19; 18:1, 3, 4, 23; 19:12, 14, 23; 20:1; 22:2; 23:13-14; 25:1.\n\nNotice something interesting? Every single one of these references appears in Matthew's Gospel. That's because Matthew presents Christ as Jehovah's King. Mark shows Christ as Jehovah's Servant, Luke presents Him as Jehovah's Man, and John reveals Him as Jehovah's Anointed One—God manifested in the flesh.\n\nThe word "Heaven" in all these passages is actually plural in the Greek. The phrase "kingdom of Heaven" literally means "the kingdom from the heavens." It's a dispensational term referring to the Messiah's kingdom on Earth—not originating from this world. The sovereignty comes from Heaven because that's where the King is from (Jn. 18:36).\n\nThis is why Jesus was born and what His ministry was all about from the very beginning (Mt. 3:2; 4:17).\n\nThe Gospels cover four main subjects:\n- **First**, the proclamation of the kingdom (306 verses: Mt. 4:12–7:29; Mk. 1:14-20; Lk. 4:14–5:11; Jn. 1:35–4:54)\n- **Second**, the proclamation of the king (964 verses: Mt. 8:1–16:20; Mk. 1:21–8:30; Lk. 5:12–9:21; Jn. 5:1–6:71)\n- **Third**, the rejection of the king (901 verses: Mt. 16:21–20:34; Mk. 8:31–10:52; Lk. 9:22–18:43; Jn. 7:1–11:53)\n- **Fourth**, the rejection of the kingdom (782 verses: Mt. 21:1–26:35; Mk. 11:1–14:25; Lk. 19:1–22:38; Jn. 11:54–17:26)\n\nNot only was the kingdom rejected, but people even rejected hearing any more about it (Mt. 22:2-7; Acts 1:6-7; 3:19-26). After that, the earthly realization of the kingdom was postponed. It's on hold until the King returns from glory (Acts 3:20; Rev. 19:11-21).\n\nThe parables of the kingdom of Heaven we'll explore reveal this postponement and what happens during this time when both the King and His kingdom are rejected. Regardless of where you land on other doctrines, we can all agree that the literal kingdom hasn't been set up yet—and won't be until Christ's second coming.\n\nSatan is still the ruler of this world system and will continue to be until Christ returns to eject him from Earth and restore dominion to man (Rev. 19:11-21; 20:1-10; Zech. 14:1-5; Mt. 24:29-31; 25:31-46; 2 Thess. 1:7-10; 2:7-12; Jude 14; Isa. 63:1-5; Dan. 2:44-45; 7:9-14, 18, 27; Lk. 1:32).\n\nRight now, the kingdom of Heaven isn't the literal reign of Heaven over Earth. Instead, it's the "sphere of profession"—the professing Christian world. This becomes crystal clear in each parable we'll study below. During this Dispensation of Grace, this sphere of profession covers what we call Christendom, taking in both the good and the bad—anyone who claims to be a child of the future kingdom (Mt. 13).\n\nIn the next dispensation, the Millennium, everything changes. The kingdom of Heaven stops being just the sphere of profession and becomes the real, literal kingdom of the Son of Man—the very kingdom that was rejected at the beginning of this age. Christ will rule with a rod of iron, putting all enemies under His feet, whether they genuinely profess to be sons of the kingdom or not (1 Cor. 15:24-28).\n\nThe kingdom of Heaven is what the prophets foretold—designed to re-establish the kingdom of God over the rebellious part of the universe so God can be all-in-all, just as He was in the beginning.\n\n### 2. THE KINGDOM OF GOD\n\nHere are the references for "the kingdom of God": Mt. 6:33; 12:28; 19:24; 21:31, 43; Mk. 1:14, 15; 4:11, 22, 30; 9:1, 47; 10:14, 15, 23, 24, 25; 12:34; 14:25; 15:43; Lk. 4:43; 6:20; 7:28; 8:1, 10; 9:2, 11, 27, 60, 62; 10:9, 11; 11:20; 12:31; 13:18, 20, 28, 29; 14:15; 16:16; 17:20, 21; 18:16, 17, 24, 25, 29; 19:11; 21:31; 22:31; 22:16, 18; 23:51; Jn. 3:3, 5; Acts 1:3; 8:12; 14:22; 19:8; 20:25; 28:23, 31; Rom. 14:17; 1 Cor. 6:20; 6:9, 10; 15:24, 50; Gal. 5:21; 1 Cor. 4:11; 2 Thess. 1:5; Rev. 12:10.\n\nThe "kingdom of God" refers to God's sovereignty over the entire universe. It includes and embraces the kingdom of Heaven along with every other realm in existence. It's moral and universal, existing from the beginning with no end in sight.\n\nHere's a key insight: the kingdom of God existed even before Earth was created. When God laid the foundations of the Earth, angels and other spirit-beings were already in His kingdom (Job 38:4-7). The kingdom of Heaven couldn't have existed then because there was no Earth for the kingdom from the heavens to rule over.\n\nThe term "kingdom of God" appears 72 times in Scripture; "kingdom of Heaven" appears 33 times. (See also Mt. 13:43; 16:28; Col. 1:13; 2 Pet. 1:11; Rev. 11:15.)\n\n## II. The Usage of "the Kingdom of Heaven" and "the Kingdom of God"\n\nThere's been quite a bit of controversy over whether these two kingdoms are different or the same. A study here will help clear things up. The controversy exists because both expressions sometimes appear in parallel passages. For example, compare Mt. 11:11 with Lk. 7:28, and Mt. 13 with Mk. 8; Lk. 8 and 13.\n\nHere's how we can understand these parallel usages: The kingdom of Heaven is a more specific term than the kingdom of God. Think of it like this: the kingdom of Heaven is the earthly sphere within the universal kingdom of God. It's similar to how a state exists within a nation—the state (kingdom of Heaven) shares many things in common with the nation (kingdom of God), but the nation is the larger reality.\n\nBecause of this relationship, almost everything you can say about the kingdom of Heaven you can also say about the kingdom of God—since the kingdom of Heaven is contained within the kingdom of God. However, some things said about the kingdom of God in this age could never be said about the kingdom of Heaven.\n\nWhen the two terms appear in parallel passages, they're referring to this present age. That's because during this age, the kingdom of Heaven has shifted from being a literal kingdom to being the sphere of profession—all because the King who will rule the earthly kingdom was rejected. When He's finally established in the next age, things will be different.\n\n## III. General Contrasts Between the Two Terms\n\n## IV. Entrance into the Two Kingdoms\n\nLet's clarify the technical difference in how you enter these two kingdoms.\n\nThe kingdom of God on Earth right now is primarily spiritual. In one sense, it includes only those who willingly submit to God's will. Of course, like any kingdom, it also includes rebels—but God's purpose in establishing the kingdom of Heaven and sending His Son with an "expeditionary force" from Heaven was to put down that rebellion in this earthly part of the universal kingdom.\n\nPicture it this way: imagine a king whose territory includes some areas in active rebellion. Until those rebels are either reconciled or subdued, they can't really be considered loyal subjects—even though they're technically within the kingdom's borders.\n\nOnce the Messiah puts down all rebellion and every enemy is destroyed, the kingdom of Heaven will be absorbed back into the kingdom of God. God will become supreme over all, just as He was before rebellion started in the universe. The Son will become subject to the Father but continues to reign with the Father forever and ever (Isa. 9:6, 7; Dan. 7:13, 14; Lk. 1:32-35; Rev. 11:15; 1 Cor. 15:24-28).\n\nHere's a crucial distinction: in this age, both professed sons and true sons of God are in the kingdom of Heaven. And all of them, along with the rest of the universe, are technically in the universal kingdom of God. But God only recognizes the *true* sons as being genuinely part of His kingdom.\n\nThis is why you must be born again to become a willing subject of God and truly belong to His Kingdom (Jn. 3:1-8; Rom. 8:1-13). You don't have to be born again to be recognized as part of the kingdom of Heaven in this age—since it's merely the sphere of profession (Mt. 13). But you *must* be born again to be a true subject of God and share in the kingdom of Heaven in the next age (Mt. 5:3, 5, 10; 18:3).\n\nTo wrap up this section: the kingdom of Heaven is what God gives to His Son and the glorified saints. It's the kingdom promised to David that will become universal over all earthly kingdoms. Jesus called it "my kingdom" (Lk. 22:50; Jn. 18:36). The disciples recognized it as such (Mt. 20:21; Lk. 23:42). It's what God has prepared for the righteous since the foundation of the world (Mt. 25:34).\n\nThis kingdom was first announced by John the Baptist (Mt. 3:2-3), then by Christ and the apostles (Mt. 4:17; 10:7) as being "at hand." But it was rejected and is now on hold until the King returns. Then it will be established as a separate kingdom from Heaven with the purpose of re-establishing the kingdom of God on Earth forever.\n\n---\n\n# The Kingdom of Heaven Parables\n\nUnderstanding parables is one of the most important skills you can develop for studying the Bible. Getting them right matters tremendously because so many false teachings come from misinterpreting them.\n\nHere's a problem we need to address: the average person in Christendom thinks parables don't really mean anything specific. Thousands of people dismiss clear biblical truth by saying, "Oh, that's just a parable"—as if parables cancel out truth rather than teach it! Let's fix that misunderstanding right now.\n\n## I. The Definition of Parable\n\nA parable comes from the Greek word *parobollo*, which means "to throw beside for comparison." It's a story that compares some moral, spiritual, or literal truth with a human experience or natural fact. Parables use real-life situations to illustrate truth in a way that's different from just stating it directly. Sometimes parables also serve to conceal truth from uninterested listeners while revealing it to those who genuinely want to learn.\n\nHere's a helpful tip: the most common word for "parable" today would be "illustration." If you substitute "illustration" every time you see "parable" in your Bible, a lot of confusion will clear up immediately.\n\nYou understand how illustrations work, right? But the enemy has made the word "parable" seem so mystical that many people think parables have some supernatural, magical meaning beyond human understanding. Some actually believe that because "parable" appears in the Bible, it must mean something completely different from what it means anywhere else.\n\nThis is one of the devil's greatest tricks to sidetrack people from truth. He gets us to think that words in the Bible automatically have different meanings than those same words outside the Bible. We need to wake up to this fallacy if we want simple, correct knowledge of Scripture.\n\nWords in the Bible mean exactly what they mean in other books. We shouldn't spiritualize, reinterpret, or change their plain, common meanings just because they appear in Scripture. Why would anyone start changing word meanings when they see them in the Bible? Why spiritualize and make symbolic what's expressed in plain human language when they'd never do that with the same expressions elsewhere?\n\nThis foolish practice alone should alert us that demonic spirits are real and actively working to make the Bible seem so mystical and confusing that people give up trying to understand it. If the word "parable" intimidates you, just substitute "illustration" and use plain common sense. A parable is simply an example or comparison that helps explain truth.\n\nAll New Testament parables are straightforward illustrations—understand them that way. The ones that need interpretation are interpreted for us. These interpretations give us the right foundation for understanding all the others. In fact, every parable is either interpreted for us, or the illustrated points are so clearly stated within the parable itself that there's no room for misunderstanding—if we're honest enough to accept what the Bible actually says.\n\nThe Old Testament parables are prophetic sayings of plain truth that explain themselves. In Num. 23:7-12, 16-26; 24:1-10, 15-25, we have several parables that are clear prophecy and blessing concerning Israel. Even the pagan king Balak understood them! They're so clear that no interpretation is needed.\n\nIn Job 27:1 and 29:1, Job's complaints are called parables—again, clear in themselves. In Ps. 49:4, a song for the sons of Korah is called a parable. Ps. 78:2 prophesies that the Messiah would open His mouth to speak parables—truths that had been hidden since ancient times.\n\nThe phrase "dark sayings" in that passage doesn't mean parables are hard to understand. It means they *were* hidden previously and *are now* made clear, as Mt. 13:35 explains. Things kept secret from the world's foundation naturally couldn't be understood until revealed. But once they're revealed, they're no longer "dark"—they're clearly explained by the greatest Teacher who ever lived.\n\nA few parables appear in the prophets. In Ezek. 17:1-10, the prophet calls his message a riddle and parable. The king of Babylon is compared to a great eagle taking the best branches of Lebanon's cedars—referring to Nebuchadnezzar capturing Israel's best people. The king of Egypt is compared to another eagle whom Israel trusted for help, but God says it won't work. This parable is interpreted in Ezek. 17:11-24, so there's no room for misunderstanding.\n\nEzek. 24 contains another prophetic parable, also explained in the same chapter. In most Old Testament passages, "parable" could have been translated "proverb" since it comes from the Hebrew *mashal*, meaning "a pithy saying," usually metaphorical—"an adage," "byword," or "proverb." But they're all explained and just as clear as New Testament parables. The idea of riddle or proverb is evident in Prov. 26:7, 9; Ezek. 20:49; Mic. 2:4; Hab. 2:6.\n\nCertain New Testament passages carry this same sense, as shown in Mt. 15:11-20; Mk. 3:23-27; 7:14-21; Lk. 5:26-39; 6:39. These are all entirely clear—either self-explanatory or explained by context. The parables we'll study below mainly concern the course of this present age and Christian living—and they're frequently misused to teach false doctrines.\n\n## II. The Nature of Parables\n\nParables are extended similes that stay within the realm of reality. The stories relate true facts and experiences from human life and nature, making them genuine illustrations and comparisons of the ideas being conveyed.\n\nHere's a crucial distinction: the parables themselves are *not* the truths being communicated—they're merely *illustrations* of those truths. The parable is one thing; the truth it illustrates is something entirely different. Once a parable has done its job of clarifying the truth, it has no further use in teaching doctrine—as we'll see in Point IV below.\n\n## III. The Purpose of Parables\n\n> 1. To present moral, spiritual, and eternal truth in an attractive form.\n\n> 2. To capture and stimulate the interest of hearers (Mt. 13:10-17, 35; 2 Sam. 12:1-14).\n\n> 3. To deliver correction without creating resentment (2 Sam. 12:1-14; Lk. 7:36-50).\n\n> 4. To convey truth to sincere listeners while concealing it from others as a test of their hearts (Mt. 13:10-17, 34-35).\n\n## IV. Rules Governing the Interpretation of Parables\n\n> 1. Note when and why the parable was given.\n\n> 2. Identify the main point being conveyed or illustrated.\n\n> 3. Always distinguish between the illustration and the point being illustrated.\n\n> 4. Consider the historical background of the parable.\n\n> 5. Examine the illustration itself and make sure you understand the meaning of its words.\n\n> 6. Guard against fanciful interpretations.\n\n> 7. Don't spiritualize the details of illustrations.\n\n> 8. Don't base doctrines on the details of illustrations. Those details are necessary to construct the story that illustrates the intended truth. Take the story as a literal illustration. Once it clarifies the truth, the illustration has served its purpose and carries no further meaning—no more than details of illustrations outside the Bible would.\n\n> 9. The point made by a biblical illustration is always clear within the parable itself and in parallel passages, being evident:\n>\n> (1) At the beginning (Lk. 18:9; 11:11).\n>\n> (2) At the close (Mt. 25:13; Lk. 16:9).\n>\n> (3) In both places (Mt. 18:21-35; 19:27–20:16).\n>\n> (4) In parallel passages (Lk. 15:3-7 with Mt. 18:11-14).\n>\n> (5) Through the general scope (Lk. 13:6-9; 15:11-32).\n>\n> (6) Through the doctrine illustrated. (Kingdom of Heaven parables should be understood as illustrating different aspects of truth about the kingdom of Heaven, while others illustrate individual experience, etc.)\n\n> 10. Understand parables in light of their context. Any interpretation inconsistent with the illustrated truth must be rejected.\n\n## V. Summary of the Kingdom of Heaven Parables\n\nThe following kingdom of Heaven parables reveal the course of the Dispensation of Grace discussed in Lesson Nineteen. Each parable highlights a distinct aspect of truth about this age—always clear within the parable itself. Many of these parables have been wrongly interpreted, as we'll see. Here are the parables and the central truths they illustrate:\n\n> 1. **The Sower**—Classes of hearers in the kingdom of Heaven (Mt. 13:3-8, 18-23).\n\n> 2. **The Tares and Wheat**—Mixture of good and bad in the kingdom of Heaven (Mt. 13:24-30, 36-43).\n\n> 3. **The Mustard Seed**—Abnormal growth of the kingdom of Heaven (Mt. 13:31-32).\n\n> 4. **The Leaven**—Truth about the kingdom of Heaven being corrupted (Mt. 13:33).\n\n> 5. **The Hidden Treasure**—Israel in the kingdom of Heaven (Mt. 13:44).\n\n> 6. **The Pearl of Great Price**—The Church in the kingdom of Heaven (Mt. 13:45-46).\n\n> 7. **The Dragnet**—Separation of good and bad in the kingdom of Heaven at the end of this age (Mt. 13:47-50).\n\n> 8. **The Scribe**—Mixture of old and new truths by members of the kingdom of Heaven (Mt. 13:52).\n\n> 9. **The Unmerciful Servant**—Mercy and forgiveness between members of the kingdom of Heaven (Mt. 18:21-35).\n\n> 10. **The Laborers in the Vineyard**—Selfishness rebuked; humility in service required of kingdom members (Mt. 19:27–20:16).\n\n> 11. **The Householder**—Transfer of the kingdom of God from Israel to the Gentiles (Mt. 21:23-46).\n\n> 12. **The Marriage Feast**—Individual preparation for eternity and the necessity of humility (Mt. 22:1-14).\n\n> 13. **The Pounds**—Postponement of the kingdom of God (Lk. 19:11-27).\n\n> 14. **The Fig Tree**—Nearness of the second coming (Mt. 24:32-33).\n\n> 15. **The Goodman of the House**—Readiness for the second coming (Mt. 24:43-44; Lk. 12:39-40).\n\n> 16. **The Faithful and Evil Servants**—Faithfulness in view of the second coming (Mt. 24:45-51; Lk. 12:42-48).\n\n> 17. **The Ten Virgins**—Watchfulness in view of the second coming (Mt. 25:13).\n\n> 18. **The Talents**—Diligence in view of the second coming (Mt. 25:14-30).\n\n## VI. The Kingdom of Heaven Parables Interpreted\n\n### 1. The Parable of the Sower (Mt. 13:3-8, 18-23)\n\nBoth the parable of the sower and the parable of the tares and wheat are explained by our Lord Himself. He gives us the true foundation for interpreting parables. Remember: keep the illustration separate from the truth being illustrated. Do this consistently with each parable, and you'll be protected from the many errors others have fallen into.\n\n> **(1)** The kingdom of Heaven is "like."\n\n> **(2)** The sower illustrates the Son of Man (Mt. 13:24, 37).\n\n> **(3)** The seed illustrates the Word of the kingdom (Mt. 13:19).\n\n> **(4)** The four kinds of ground illustrate four classes of people who hear the Word of the kingdom (Mt. 13:19-23). These four classes are determined by the attitude people take toward the preached Word. This does *not* divide humanity into four equal parts, nor does it teach that only one-fourth will be saved, as some claim.\n>\n> **A.** The seed sown on wayside ground, eaten by birds, illustrates people who hear the Word but refuse to obey due to indifference and lack of understanding. The devil immediately snatches away the Word sown in their hearts before it can take root and bear fruit (2 Cor. 4:4).\n>\n> **B.** The seed sown on stony ground, which sprang up quickly but had no depth, illustrates people who receive the Word with joy but are shallow in their experience. The seed can't go deep enough to take root and withstand trouble. When persecution comes, they stumble and reject the Word as quickly as they received it (Mt. 18:6; 2 Tim. 3:13).\n>\n> **C.** The seed sown among thorns, which got choked and became unfruitful, illustrates people who hear the Word but let it get choked by worldly loves, life's anxieties, and the deceitfulness of riches (Rom. 12:1-3; 2 Tim. 4:10; Jas. 4:1-7; 1 Jn. 2:15-17).\n>\n> **D.** The seed sown on good ground, producing fruit, illustrates people who hear the Word and let it work freely, producing fruit—some hundredfold, some sixtyfold, some thirtyfold. This group makes an effort to understand the Word and remove from their lives the obstacles symbolized by hard, stony, and thorny ground. The varying amounts of fruit don't represent degrees of Christians but degrees of fruit-bearing, which depends on soil quality and the care of cultivation (Jn. 15:1-16). There's only one kind of Christian—the "hundred percent" kind represented by wheat in the next parable. While the ground produces varying amounts of fruit, the wheat itself cannot be part wheat and part tare (Mt. 6:24; 7:13-29; Heb. 12:14; 1 Jn. 3:8-10).\n\n### 2. The Parable of the Tares and Wheat (Mt. 13:24-30, 36-43)\n\n> **(1)** The kingdom of Heaven is "like" (Mt. 13:24).\n\n> **(2)** The sower illustrates the Son of Man (Mt. 13:24, 37).\n\n> **(3)** The "good seed" illustrates the true children of the kingdom of Heaven. The tares illustrate those who *profess* to be children of the kingdom but are actually sons of the devil, who sowed them among the wheat (Mt. 13:25, 38-39).\n\nThe wheat being planted first shows God's original plan—only good should be in the kingdom, and everyone professing to be a child of God should actually be one. But an enemy sowed tares among the wheat, creating a mixture of good and bad in the same field. This gives the kingdom of Heaven a new dimension: it becomes "the sphere of profession," "Christendom," or "the kingdom of Heaven in mystery."\n\nThe word "tares" (Greek *zizania*, found only in Mt. 13:25, 36) isn't the same as the naturalists' darnel but what's known today in Palestine as *zewan*. While growing, it looks like wheat. But when mature, the ears are long and the grain is black. Each grain must be removed before grinding wheat, or the bread becomes bitter and poisonous. Tares reveal their true color as they ripen.\n\nVerse 26 shows that tares couldn't be detected until fruit began appearing (Mt. 7:15-20). The enemy "went his way," confident the seed would grow according to its nature. Shouldn't we have the same confidence about good seed?\n\n> **(4)** The field illustrates the world of humanity (Mt. 13:38).\n\n> **(5)** Both tares and wheat grow side by side in the same field—according to the householder's will—until harvest (Mt. 13:37-40). Both saved and unsaved who profess to be kingdom children will mingle together in this world until the age ends. For a real Christian to escape sinners and hypocrites, they'd have to leave the world entirely (1 Cor. 5:9-11).\n\n> **(6)** The harvest illustrates the separation of the wicked from the righteous at the end of this age, when the Messiah comes to reign (Mt. 13:39-43; 25:31-46; 1 Thess. 5:1-11; 2 Thess. 1:7-10; Jude 14; Zech. 14:1-21). It does *not* illustrate the rapture of the Church before His coming to rule this world.\n\n> **(7)** The reapers illustrate angels who will separate the wicked from the good when Christ comes (Mt. 13:39-43; 24:31, 51; 25:31-46; 2 Thess. 1:7-10).\n\nThe expression "the end of the world" means "the end of the age." At that time, the kingdom of Heaven stops being merely the sphere of profession and becomes the literal kingdom of Heaven—the Messiah's reign over all the kingdoms of this world (Rev. 11:15; Dan. 7:13-14, 18).\n\n> **(8)** "Then shall the righteous shine forth in the kingdom of their Father" (Mt. 19:28; 25:31-46; Rev. 5:10; 20:1-10).\n\n### 3. The Parable of the Mustard Seed (Mt. 13:31, 32; Mk. 4:31)\n\nThe popular interpretation says the Church starts small and grows large enough to shelter all nations. But this *cannot* be the true meaning.\n\n> **(1)** The kingdom of Heaven (not the Church) is "like" (Mt. 13:31).\n\n> **(2)** The kingdom of Heaven, like a mustard seed, has a tiny beginning but grows rapidly to an abnormal size—becoming a tree that shelters things it was never intended to shelter.\n\nThe mustard seed is mentioned in Mt. 13:31; 17:20; Mk. 4:31; Lk. 13:19; 17:6. In all these passages, the seed's minuteness is emphasized, while the first three also mention the large size of the plant it produces. Several varieties of mustard have notably tiny seeds but grow under favorable conditions into tall herbs—ten to twelve feet high in just a few months. This rapid growth is always striking. Among the rabbis, "a grain of mustard" was a common expression for anything minute, which explains our Lord's phrase "faith as a grain of mustard seed" (Mt. 17:20; Lk. 17:6).\n\n> **(3)** The "birds" are the same ones that devoured seeds by the wayside in the parable of the sower. They represent demonic powers headed by the devil. This parable illustrates the rapid, abnormal growth of the kingdom of Heaven—from an insignificant beginning to a great presence on Earth. It went from being poor, little-known, and separate from the world to becoming a great worldly institution that serves as a dwelling place for wicked people and demonic powers (Mt. 13:4, 19, 32; Rev. 17:1-18; 18:2).\n\nThis parable reinforces the truth from the tares and wheat parable: God's plan was to have only wheat in the field and only good people in the kingdom of Heaven. But an enemy sowed tares and transformed the kingdom's true nature into something abnormal—a mixture of good and bad.\n\n### 4. The Parable of Leaven (Mt. 13:33; Lk. 13:20-21)\n\nThe popular interpretation says the Church (the woman) takes the gospel (leaven) and introduces it into the world (three measures of meal) until the whole world is converted. This *cannot* be the true meaning, as we'll see.\n\n> **(1)** The kingdom of Heaven (not the Church) is "like" (Mt. 13:33).\n\n> **(2)** Leaven (sour dough) is *always* used negatively in Scripture, while meal is used positively. Therefore, the common interpretation—leaven converting the world—is the exact *opposite* of the true meaning: leaven corrupting the meal.\n\nLet's look at how leaven is used in Scripture:\n\nIn the **Old Testament**, leaven portrays the sinner's evil nature (Exodus 12:8, 15-20; 34:25; Lev. 2:11; 6:17; 10:12). These last three passages forbid leaven in certain sacrifices. Only one sacrifice permitted it (Lev. 7:12-13), and there it signifies that although the believer has made peace with God through another's work (Christ), human traits and selfishness remain and must be continually purged through growth in grace.\n\nIn the **New Testament**, leaven is used four ways:\n- **First**, the leaven of the Pharisees—external religion,\n\n---\n\nUnderstanding the distinction between these two kingdoms isn't academic hair-splitting — it determines which passages apply to you now, which will apply in the Millennium, and how you understand the relationship between Israel and the Church. So here's the question to take into your study: Think of a teaching of Jesus from the Sermon on the Mount or the parables that you've always applied to yourself as a Christian. Now ask: Is this addressed to the universal Kingdom of God, to the Church specifically, or to Israel in the context of the coming earthly Kingdom? Does your answer change how you apply it?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk4bq001sewhj4sy54u76	cmnknk4bp001qewhjk5bcqnfj	Lesson 38 — The Seven Judgments of Scripture	READING	1111	1	\N	t	Most people have one vague picture of 'the judgment' — a day when everyone stands before God and receives their eternal verdict. But Scripture describes not one judgment, but seven distinct ones, each with a different subject, a different judge, a different time, and different outcomes. Confusing them is one of the main reasons people end up terrified by passages that don't apply to them or complacent about passages that do.\n\nLast time we established where the dead are — that death is not the end of consciousness but a transition. Now we examine what awaits: the seven distinct judgments Scripture describes for both the saved and the lost.\n\nThe Bible reveals that different groups will face judgment at different times and places. Angels and various categories of people will each have their own separate judgment. In all, there are seven distinct judgments revealed in Scripture.\n\nLet's walk through each one, looking at who's being judged, when and where it happens, what it's based on, and what the outcome is.\n\n![Lesson 38 — The Seven Judgments of Scripture](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8Os76UaHERlSEtNozZx4rmO6UfQcdhyY2pDqwX3)\n\n## I. The Judgment of the Believer's Sin (Jn. 12:31-32)\n\n> 1. **Subject:** The believer as to sin (Rom. 6:10; Heb. 9:25-28).\n\n> 2. **Time:** On the cross (Jn. 3:14; 1 Pet. 2:24; Jn. 17:4).\n\n> 3. **Place:** Calvary (Lk. 23:33; Jn. 19:17-24).\n\n> 4. **Basis:** Christ's perfect work (Jn. 3:16; 5:24; Rom. 6:8).\n\n> 5. **Result:** Death to Christ, justification to believers (Rom. 4:1-25; 5:1-11, 15-21; 8:1-16). This judgment deals with the believer as a sinner.\n\nHere's the incredible news: if you're a believer, the judgment for your sin has already happened! It took place two thousand years ago at Calvary when Jesus hung on the cross.\n\nThink of it like someone paying off your entire debt before you even knew you owed it. Christ took the full penalty that your sin deserved. The result? Death came to Him, but justification—being declared righteous—came to you.\n\nThis judgment deals with you as a sinner, addressing the fundamental problem of your guilt before God.\n\n## II. The Believer's Judgment of Himself (1 Cor. 11:31, 32)\n\n> 1. **Subjects:** Believers walking in the light (1 Jn. 1:7).\n\n> 2. **Time:** During this life (Eph. 4:22-32; Col. 3:5-17).\n\n> 3. **Place:** In his own life (Rom. 8:1-16; Heb. 12:5-11).\n\n> 4. **Basis:** Obedience to God and His Word (Jas. 1:22-25).\n\n> 5. **Result:** Chastisement or approval from God (Heb. 12:5-11). This judgment deals with the believer as a son during his earthly pilgrimage.\n\nThis one is happening right now, every day of your life. As a believer, you're called to examine yourself—to walk in the light and honestly evaluate your thoughts, words, and actions against God's Word.\n\nIt's similar to how a good athlete reviews their own performance after every game. You don't wait for the coach to point out every mistake; you look at the film yourself and make corrections.\n\nWhen you judge yourself honestly and deal with sin, God doesn't have to discipline you. But when you refuse to examine yourself, your loving Father will step in with correction. This judgment deals with you as a child of God during your journey through this life.\n\n## III. The Judgment of the Believer's Works (Rom. 14:10; 2 Cor. 5:10)\n\n> 1. **Subjects:** Believers (Gal. 6:8; Eph. 6:8; Col. 3:24; Rom. 14; 2 Cor. 5:10).\n\n> 2. **Time:** Between the rapture and the second advent (Lk. 14:14).\n\n> 3. **Place:** In Heaven (1 Cor. 9:24-27; Rom. 14:10; 2 Cor. 5:10).\n\n> 4. **Basis:** Works, both good and bad (1 Cor. 3:11-15; 2 Cor. 5:10; Rom. 14; Col. 3; Mt. 16:27; Rom. 2:6; 2 Tim. 4:14; Rev. 2:23; 22:12). Believers will be judged concerning:\n\n> (1) Doctrines (Rom. 2:14-16; Rom. 14).\n\n> (2) Conduct to others (Mt. 18; Rom. 14).\n\n> (3) Carnal traits (Col. 3; Rom. 1-2; 8:1-13; 14:1-23).\n\n> (4) Words (Mt. 12:32-37; Rom. 14).\n\n> (5) Things that affect others: slander, quarrels, idle words, foolishness, joking, debts, broken promises, wrong dealings, etc. (Rom. 1:29-32; 1 Cor. 6:9-11; Gal. 5:19-21; Col. 3; Eph. 4:1-32; 5:1-33; Rom. 12:1-21; 14:1-23).\n\n> (6) Things that affect themselves: neglected opportunities, talents wasted, loose living, lack of spirituality, etc. (Rom. 2:14-16; Heb. 2:1-4; Gal. 5:1-26; 6:1-10; Col. 3).\n\n> (7) Things that affect God: refusal to walk in the light, disobedience, rejection, failure to cooperate and yield to the Spirit, etc. (1 Cor. 12; Rom. 12; Eph. 4:1-32; 5:1-33).\n\n> 5. **Result:** Reward or loss of reward, but never the loss of the soul for those wrong doings providing they have been forgiven (1 Cor. 3:11-15). This judgment deals with the believer as a servant (Rom. 14; 2 Cor. 5:10, 11; 1 Cor. 3:11-15).\n\nThis is the judgment seat of Christ, and it's coming for every believer. After the rapture but before Christ returns to earth, you'll stand before Him in heaven to have your life's work evaluated.\n\nHere's what's crucial to understand: this isn't about whether you get into heaven. That's already settled! This is about rewards—it deals with you as a servant.\n\nYour works will be tested like metal going through fire. What was done with pure motives and in obedience to God will survive and bring reward. What was done from selfishness, pride, or wrong motives will burn away—you'll lose the reward, but not your soul.\n\n> 6. **Illustration:** Analysis of the zeal of a candidate for a crown in glory.\n\nThe following analysis was taken from the experience of a prominent Christian worker who was successful above the ordinary minister. His church was crowded, souls were being saved and everything was going along fine.\n\nOne day he went home after a successful Sunday at church and fell asleep in a parlor chair. An angel appeared to him and asked him, "How is your zeal?" He was proud of his zeal and answered, "Fine!"\n\nThe angel then asked him for his zeal and he pulled it out of his bosom and gave it to him. The angel put it in a crucible and put a fire under it until it melted. He then poured it out on the hearth and it formed strata. He then broke it in pieces and put each piece on scales and weighed it and gave the following analysis to the minister:\n\n**TOTAL WEIGHT 100 POUNDS.** Of this analysis there proves to be:\n\n- (1) Bigotry — 11 parts\n- (2) Personal ambition — 22 parts\n- (3) Love of praise — 19 parts\n- (4) Pride of denomination — 15 parts\n- (5) Love of authority — 12 parts\n- (6) Pride of talents — 14 parts\n- (7) Love of God — 4 parts\n- (8) Love of man — 3 parts\n\nThis Christian worker said his anger arose and he began to deny the truth of this analysis, but the angel walked toward the door, stopped and looked back with pity in his eyes and said, "May God save you," and walked out the door.\n\nWhen the minister awoke the conviction seized his heart and he got down and prayed more to be saved from himself than he had ever prayed to be saved from Hell. He prayed until the refining fire of the Spirit purged him of self and thereafter he was a greater success than ever.\n\nThis refining process is no doubt the greatest need of the modern church (Rom. 6; 12; Gal. 5; Col. 3; Eph. 4 and 5).\n\nWhat a sobering illustration! Can you imagine your zeal being weighed and finding that only 7% of it was actually love for God and people? This story reminds us that outward success doesn't equal inward purity—and God sees everything.\n\n## IV. The Judgment of Israel (Ezek. 20:33-44)\n\n> 1. **Subjects:** The living Jews (Zech. 12:10-13).\n\n> 2. **Time:** During the Tribulation (Jer. 30:3-11; Mt. 24:15-22; Dan. 9:27).\n\n> 3. **Place:** Palestine, Edom and Moab (Zech. 12:4-13; 13:1; 14:1-21; Dan.11:40-45; Isa.16:1-5; Ezek. 20:33-44; Hos. 2:14-17; Mt. 24:15-31; Rev. 12).\n\n> 4. **Basis:** Obedience to God and His Word (Jas. 1:22-25).\n\n> 5. **Result:** Conversion of Israel as a nation at the second coming of Christ (Rom. 11:25-29; Zech. 12:10–13:1; Ezek. 22:19-22; Jer. 30:3-11; Isa. 66:7, 8; Mt. 23:37-39).\n\nGod has a special plan for His chosen people Israel. During the Tribulation period, the Jewish people who are alive will go through an intense time of judgment in the land of Palestine and surrounding regions.\n\nBut here's the beautiful outcome: this judgment leads to the national conversion of Israel! When Christ returns, they will look upon Him whom they pierced and finally recognize their Messiah. What looks like devastating judgment becomes the pathway to restoration.\n\n## V. The Judgment of the Nations (Mt. 25:31-46)\n\n> 1. **Subjects:** Gentile nations (Mt. 25:32).\n\n> 2. **Time:** At the revelation of Christ before the Millennium (Mt. 25:31-46).\n\n> 3. **Place:** Palestine (Zech.14; Mt. 25:31; Rev. 19:11-21).\n\n> 4. **Basis:** Their treatment of Christ's brethren—the Jews (Mt. 25:31-46).\n\n> 5. **Result:** Some saved to go into the Millennium and some destroyed (Mt. 25:34, 41, 46; Zech. 14; Mt. 13:41-50; 24:51; Isa. 53; Joel 3).\n\nWhen Christ returns to earth, He will gather all the Gentile nations before Him for judgment. The basis is fascinating: how did they treat "the least of these my brethren"—the Jewish people—especially during the Tribulation?\n\nPicture it this way: during the darkest time in human history, some will risk their lives to help persecuted Jews. Others will join in the persecution. Christ will separate them like a shepherd separates sheep from goats.\n\nThose who showed compassion will enter the Millennial Kingdom. Those who didn't will face destruction.\n\nNow, some people confuse this judgment with the Great White Throne Judgment we'll discuss in Point VII. But they're completely different events. Here's a comparison:\n\n| Judgment of the Nations | Great White Throne Judgment |\n|---|---|\n| 1. Living nations (Mt. 25:31-46). | 1. Wicked dead resurrected (Rev. 20:11-15). |\n| 2. Before the Millennium (Mt. 25:41-46). | 2. After the Millennium (Rev. 20:1-15). |\n| 3. Christ the judge (Mt. 25:31). | 3. God the judge (Rev. 20:11-15). |\n| 4. On Earth (Mt. 25:31-46; Zech. 14). | 4. In Heaven (Rev. 20:11-15). |\n| 5. Two classes (Mt. 25:31-46). | 5. One class (Rev. 20:11-15). |\n| 6. Some saved (Mt. 25:34; Zech. 14:16). | 6. None saved (Rev. 20:4-15). |\n| 7. Some destroyed (Mt. 25:41, 46). | 7. All destroyed (Rev. 20:4-15). |\n| 8. No resurrection (Mt. 25:32; Zech. 14). | 8. A resurrection (Rev. 20:4-15). |\n| 9. No books opened (Mt. 25:31-46). | 9. Books opened (Rev. 20:11-15). |\n| 10. Basis: sins, especially one sin—persecution of Israel (Mt. 25:31-46). | 10. Basis: sins of all the ages recorded in the books (Rev. 20:11-15). |\n| 11. One generation (Mt. 25:31-46). | 11. All generations (Rev. 20:11-15; Acts 17:31). |\n| 12. Gentiles only (Mt. 25:32). | 12. Jews and Gentiles (Rev. 20:11-15; Acts 17:31). |\n| 13. Angels help in this judgment (Mt. 13:42-50; 24:31; 25:31). | 13. No angels mentioned at this judgment. |\n\nAs you can see, these are two distinct events separated by a thousand years!\n\n## VI. The Judgment of Angels (2 Pet. 2:4; Jude 6-7)\n\n> 1. **Subjects:** Fallen angels (2 Pet. 2:4; Jude 6-7).\n\n> 2. **Time:** After the Millennium (2 Pet. 2:4; Jude 6-7; Rev. 20:7-15).\n\n> 3. **Place:** In Heaven (Rev. 20:12).\n\n> 4. **Basis:** Sins (2 Pet. 2:4; Jude 6-7).\n\n> 5. **Result:** Eternal damnation in the Lake of Fire (Rev. 20:10; Mt. 25:41).\n\nYou might not think about it often, but angels face judgment too! The fallen angels—those who rebelled with Satan—are being held for their day in court. After the Millennium, they'll stand before God's throne.\n\nThe outcome is certain: eternal damnation in the Lake of Fire. Satan and demons will likely be judged at this same time (Eph. 6:12; Mk. 8:28; Lk. 8:31).\n\n## VII. The Great White Throne Judgment (Rev. 20:11-15)\n\n"And I saw a great white throne, and him that sat on it, from whose face the earth and the heaven fled away; and there was found no place for them. And I saw the dead, small and great, stand before God; and the books were opened: and another book was opened, which is the book of life: and the dead were judged out of those things which were written in the books, according to their works. And the sea gave up the dead which were in it; and death and hell delivered up the dead which were in them: and they were judged every man according to their works. And death and hell were cast into the Lake of Fire. This is the second death. And whosoever was not found written in the book of life was cast into the Lake of Fire" (Rev. 20:11-15).\n\nThis is the final judgment—the most solemn event in all of Scripture. It happens after the Millennium and after Satan's final revolt. John sees a great white throne occupied by God, and the scene is so overwhelming that Earth and Heaven flee from His face.\n\nThe Greek word for "face" is *prosopon*, meaning the countenance, appearance, or outward form. It appears nine times in Revelation and shows that God has a real body and visible appearance (Rev. 4:7; 6:16; 7:11; 9:7; 10:1; 11:16; 12:14; 22:4). This isn't symbolic language—God truly has a face.\n\nNow, you might notice that Christ isn't specifically mentioned here. Does that mean He's absent? Not at all. Both the Father and the Son will participate in this final judgment. Let's look at the various aspects:\n\n### 1. The Judges\n\n> (1) God the Father is spoken of as the judge of all men (Heb. 12:23-24; 13:4; Rev. 6:10; Acts 17:30, 31; Rom. 2:12-16).\n\n> (2) God the Son is also spoken of as the judge of the world (Jn. 5:19-27; Acts 10:42; 17:30, 31; 2 Tim. 4:8; Rev.19:11).\n\n> (3) Both the Father and the Son will be judges (Acts 17:31; Rom. 2:16).\n\n> (4) God the Father will judge by His Son (Acts 17:31; Rom. 2:16).\n\n> (5) God the Father will decree; the Son will execute (Jn. 5:22-27; Rom.1:32; 2:5).\n\n> (6) The saints will have part in this judgment (1 Cor. 6:2-3; Rev. 3:21).\n\nHere's something amazing: you, as a believer, will actually participate in this judgment! Scripture tells us that the saints will judge angels and the world alongside Christ.\n\n### 2. The Subjects Judged\n\n> (1) Wicked men of the whole human race except the beast, false prophet, the goat nations, tares, etc., will be the subjects judged (Acts 17:31; Rom.3:6; Rev. 20:11-15). That those judged at the judgment of the nations will not be judged at the final judgment seems clear from Mt. 13:30, 39-43, 49, 50; 24:51; 25:30, 41, 46; Rev. 14:9-11; 19:20, 21; 20:10. These already will have had sentence pronounced upon them a thousand years before the final judgment, so it would not be necessary to judge them again. The ones judged at this judgment will be the wicked dead of all ages, whereas those judged at the judgment of the nations will be the living nations on Earth at the return of Christ to set up His eternal Kingdom.\n\n> (2) The angels "that sinned" and "are now bound" in Tartarus will also be loosed from their long confinement in chains and be judged at this judgment (2 Pet. 2:4; Jude 6, 7).\n\n### 3. The Time of the Judgment\n\nThe time of this judgment will be after the Millennium and after the doom of Satan in the Lake of Fire is realized (Rev. 20:7-15). It is called "the day of judgment" and so there must be a definite time set for it (Mt. 10:15; 11:24; 12:36; Acts 17:31; 2 Pet. 2:4; Jude 6, 7).\n\n### 4. The Place of the Judgment\n\nThe judgment is to be before the great white throne of God, which will still be in Heaven, for it is not to come down to the Earth until after the renovation of the Earth by fire and after the New Heaven and the New Earth are completed (Rev. 21:1-5). This seems to be proven further by 2 Pet. 3:7 where it states that the renovation of the Earth takes place during the final judgment. It seems to be pictured also in Rev. 20:11 at the same time of the final judgment.\n\n### 5. The Purpose of the Judgment\n\n> (1) To give every man a fair trial before his condemnation and punishment (Acts 17:31).\n\n> (2) To judge the "secrets of men" (Rom. 2:16).\n\n> (3) To judge all idle words (Mt. 12:36).\n\n> (4) To judge all the works, thoughts, actions and sins of man (1 Tim. 5:24; 1 Pet. 1:17; Rev. 20:12, 13; 22:12; Rom. 2:5-6).\n\n### 6. The Basis of Judgment\n\n> (1) The law of conscience (Rom. 2:12-16; Heb. 10:27).\n\n> (2) The law of memory (Lk. 16:25).\n\n> (3) The Law of Moses (Rom. 2:12-16; Rev. 20:11-15).\n\n> (4) The law of character (Heb. 3:8-10, 15; Eph. 4:19).\n\n> (5) The gospel (Rom. 2:12-16; Jn. 12:47, 48; Rev. 20:11-15).\n\n> (6) All acts and words (Mt. 12:36; Lk. 12:2-9; Jn. 3:18).\n\n> (7) The Book of Life (Rev. 20:11-15).\n\nHere's something profound: a person who goes through this judgment will have no excuse—no legitimate complaint about the sentence. In a sense, each person becomes their own judge.\n\nWhy? Because their own conscience will testify against them. Their own memory will recall every sin. The law they knew will condemn them. Their own hardened character will witness to their choices. And most importantly, the absence of their name in the Book of Life will seal their fate.\n\nThe "books" mentioned in Rev. 20:12 aren't records kept by some recording angel. They contain God's Word—the standard by which every person will be judged. The Book of Life is mentioned throughout Scripture (Exod. 32:32-33; Ps. 69:25-29; Dan. 12:1; Lk. 10:20; Phil. 4:3; Rev. 3:5; 13:8; 17:8; 20:12, 15; 21:27; 22:19) and contains the names of everyone who will receive eternal life. This book alone is sufficient to condemn—it will show even those who claimed to serve Christ (Mt. 7:21-23) that their names were never written in it.\n\nScripture doesn't specifically tell us what basis the angels in Tartarus will be judged on, but they probably know. What's clear is that "the dead are judged out of those things which are written in the books" and "every man" will be judged according to his works.\n\nThe dead mentioned here don't include angels—they're the occupants of "death and hell." This proves that Hades (the present, temporary place of the wicked dead) is different from the Lake of Fire (eternal Hell). Think of it like the difference between a county jail and a federal prison. The sinner waits in Hades before being formally sentenced to the Lake of Fire—already guilty, already suffering (Lk. 16:19-31), but awaiting final judgment.\n\n### 7. The Nature of the Judgment\n\nIt will be one of justice and righteousness to every man (Ps. 9:8; Mt.7:2; 2 Tim. 1:5; 1 Pet. 2:23). This won't be some invisible, ongoing spiritual process. It will be a visible, actual trial happening at a specific time and place (Heb. 9:27; 2 Pet. 2:4-9; 3:7-10; Rev. 20:11-15).\n\n### 8. The Result of the Judgment\n\n> (1) If a person's name is not written in the Book of Life, he will be cast into the Lake of Fire. Hades is the present abiding place for the souls of the wicked dead. It never means the grave where the body of man goes. It is a place of consciousness, where men are in torment until the resurrection, as we have seen in Lesson Thirty-seven. All human beings in Hades and in death, the angels in Tartarus, the demons in the Abyss and every rebel against God will be cast into the Lake of Fire to be punished forever and forever (Rev. 2:11; 14:9-11; 20:10-15; 21:8; Mk. 9:42-48; Mt. 18:9; 24:51; 25:41, 46; Isa. 66:22-24).\n\n> (2) The degrees of punishment will be the result of this judgment, as degrees of reward will be the result of the judgment of the saints at the Judgment Seat of Christ (Mt. 7:2; 10:15; 11:22-24; 12:41-45; 23:12-14; Mk. 6:11; Lk. 10:14; 11:31, 32; Rev. 20:11-15).\n\nHell, as far as the torment of fire is concerned, will be alike for all the lost—just as Heaven's bliss and comfort will be alike for all the redeemed. But the degrees of punishment will come through the torment of conscience. The inward anguish over sins committed will eat more deeply into a person's being as the ages roll on into eternity.\n\nThis is the exact opposite of rewards for the saints, which will increase in glory and splendor throughout all eternity.\n\n### 9. The Length of the Judgment\n\nThe judgment passed upon each individual will be eternal. The same words used to describe God's eternity are used to describe the eternity of Hell—if one is eternal, so is the other (Isa. 66:22-24; Mt. 5:22, 29, 30; 10:28; 13:42, 50; 18:9; 23:15, 33; 24:51; 25:30, 41-46; Mk. 9:42-48; Lk. 12:5; Heb. 6:2; 10:26-31; Rev. 14:9-11; 19:20; 20:10-15; 21:8). See Lesson Nine, Point VIII, 4 and Lesson Thirty-seven for arguments why man's punishment must be eternal.\n\n---\n\n## Questions on Lesson Thirty-eight\n\n- Do the Scriptures teach a general resurrection and judgment?\n\n- Name the seven judgments and give a brief study of each.\n\n- Discuss the judgment of the believer's sin.\n\n- Discuss the believer's judgment of himself.\n\n- Discuss the judgment of the believer's works.\n\n- Give an illustration of the result of a believer's judgment of his works.\n\n- Discuss the judgment of Israel.\n\n- Discuss the judgment of the nations.\n\n- Give a number of contrasts between the judgment of the nations and the Great White Throne Judgment.\n\n- Discuss the judgment of the angels.\n\n- Discuss fully the Great White Throne Judgment.\n\n- Prove from Scripture that God has a real face like other persons.\n\n- How can God the Father and the Son both be judges?\n\n- Who else will help in this judgment? Prove.\n\n- Who will be judged at this judgment? Prove.\n\n- Prove from Scripture the time and place of this judgment.\n\n- Discuss the purpose and basis of this judgment.\n\n- State the nature of this judgment.\n\n- Explain fully the result and length of this judgment.\n\n- Prove from Scripture that there will be degrees of punishment for the wicked.\n\n---\n\nThe seven judgments of Scripture leave no room for confusion about who is judged when, by whom, and for what. For the believer, every sin has already been judged at the cross — what remains is a judgment of works, not of guilt, at the Bema Seat. For the unbeliever, the Great White Throne awaits. So here's the question: Which of these judgments most directly applies to you — and are you living in light of it? Specifically, if you will stand before the Bema Seat to give account of your works, what are you building right now that will survive the fire?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk48i0010ewhj96p593lr	cmnknk48e000xewhj49ao5az0	Supplement 10 — For Lessons 19 & 20	SUPPLEMENT	1047	2	\N	t	The Dispensation of Grace is the age you're in right now — and the New Testament program for the modern church is the assignment you've been given within it. Most Christians know the doctrine of grace without experiencing its full power. Most know the church's program without actually running it. This supplement is where doctrine becomes deployment.\n\nWe've worked through many misconceptions—as well as truths—about God's grace and His plan for this present age. We've also explored the New Testament program for the modern church and discovered something exciting: there are no limitations on believers getting and using God's power in this life. We can operate just as Jesus Christ and the apostles did.\n\nIn this supplement, I want to show you how you can receive God's power in your life—and what to do once you have it.\n\n![Supplement 10 — For Lessons 19 & 20](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsP0BsrJo536itGQmsLn2KwlakR9vFryYAfXZB)\n\n## Commands to Get the Power of God\n\nHere's something important: it's a definite command for all believers to receive God's power so they're equipped to witness for Christ.\n\nIn Acts 1:4-5, the disciples—not just the apostles—were commanded not to leave Jerusalem until they received the baptism in the Spirit: "And being assembled together with them, commanded them that they should not depart from Jerusalem, but wait for the promise of the Father, which, saith he, ye have heard of me. For John truly baptized with water; but ye shall be baptized with the Holy Ghost not many days hence."\n\nBeing baptized with the Holy Ghost and receiving power are the same thing. When you're baptized in the Spirit in all fullness, you receive power: "But ye shall receive power, after that the Holy Ghost is come upon you: and ye shall be witnesses unto me . . . unto the uttermost part of the earth" (Acts 1:8).\n\nIn Lk. 24:49, Christ commanded them: "Behold, I send the promise of my Father upon you: but tarry ye in the city of Jerusalem until ye be endued with power from on high."\n\nNow, are all believers throughout this age also commanded to tarry until they're endued with power from on High? Absolutely—and this passage makes it clear: "Go ye therefore, and teach all nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Ghost: Teaching them to observe all things whatsoever I have commanded you: and, lo, I am with you alway, even unto the end of the world" (Mt. 28:19-20).\n\nJesus also taught that we should ask the Father for the Holy Spirit: "If ye, then, being evil, know how to give good gifts unto your children: how much more shall your heavenly Father give the Holy Spirit to them that ask him" (Lk. 11:11-13).\n\n## How to Get the Power of God\n\nTo receive the Holy Spirit baptism, you must thirst, drink, and believe. Jesus made this clear: "In the last day, that great day of the feast, Jesus stood and cried, saying, If any man thirst, let him come unto me and drink. He that believeth on me as the scripture hath said, out of his belly shall flow rivers of living water. (But this spake he of the Spirit which they that believe on him should receive: for the Holy Ghost was not yet given; because Jesus was not yet glorified.)"\n\nThis passage (Jn. 7:37-39) can only refer to the fullness of the Holy Spirit received on the day of Pentecost. In Acts 2:33, we read about Jesus being glorified and pouring out the Holy Spirit upon the Church in baptismal measure. Up to that point, people had received the Spirit in a measure, as we saw in Lesson Nineteen, I, 3.\n\nYou must "hunger and thirst after righteousness" to be filled (Mt. 5:6).\n\nSo let's be clear: this enduement of power is for people who are already children of God—not for sinners (Jn. 14:17). If you're a believer, born-again and part of God's family, then you need to tarry, wait, hunger, thirst, drink, believe, and receive the Spirit.\n\n## Men Must Still Tarry for the Spirit Baptism\n\nYou might have heard the theory that believers don't need to wait for the Holy Spirit today because He's already come—that all you have to do is receive Him. That's only part of the truth.\n\nYes, you must receive Him. But until He's given to you personally, you don't have Him. Until He's received and fills your innermost being, you have to wait until it's done.\n\nThink of it like this: you can't just claim a gift that hasn't been opened yet. You can believe it's coming, and that faith is necessary—but to be satisfied without actually having Him would leave you with an empty life. It's impossible to receive such a mighty enduement of power, with rivers of living water flowing out of you, without knowing it!\n\nAll the terms we've used—hunger, thirst, tarry, wait—prove there's a time element involved, even if it's just a few moments. If the Spirit were given before you hungered or thirsted, you couldn't have those passions. If He were given before you received, there would be no faith to hope for what we don't yet see—and no expectancy at all.\n\nConsider this: 120 disciples were in the temple continually praising and blessing God for ten days before the Spirit came (Acts 1:3, 12-14; 2:1; Lk. 24:49-53). Now that the Spirit has come into the world to bless people in all fullness, there's no need to wait for Him to arrive in the world. But you still need to go through whatever period of preparation is necessary before He personally takes control of your life.\n\nSo approach it this way: the Spirit is already here. He wants personal control of your life. It takes yieldedness, asking God, and hungering and thirsting until you're literally filled with the fullness of God. As soon as you learn to yield to the Spirit and receive Him into your life, that's when the Spirit manifests Himself.\n\n## How to Work the Works of God\n\nHere's something crucial to understand: as a Christian, you always remain a free moral agent. You can help or hinder God's work in various ways.\n\nCertainly, no one would knowingly want to hinder God's work. But sometimes people do things ignorantly, even with the best intentions. Studying the Bible is one of the best ways to help God's work—then you'll know what to do in every circumstance. You won't be doing things wrong when you act.\n\nWhatever measure of the Spirit you receive, use it to the glory of God!\n\nYou might ask, "What is the work of God?" Jesus answered that question: "This is the work of God, that ye believe on him whom he hath sent" (Jn. 6:28-29).\n\nTo believe on Christ as Scripture describes is the greatest work of God that anyone can do. Jesus said if you do this—and do it right—you'll have rivers of living water flowing from your innermost being. You'll be able to do even greater works than He did (Jn. 7:37-39; 14:12-15).\n\n## What Does it Mean to Fully Believe?\n\n> 1. To believe that all the promises of God are yea and amen to all (2 Cor. 1:20).\n\n> 2. To believe that all things are possible to the believer (Mk. 9:23).\n\n> 3. To believe that all things we ask in prayer we receive (Mt. 7:7-11; 21:22).\n\n> 4. To believe that even all of our desires will be granted (Mk. 11:22-24).\n\n> 5. To believe that we can ask what we will, and it will be given (Jn. 15:7).\n\n> 6. To believe in those things that be not as though they were (Rom. 4:17).\n\n> 7. To believe that God is a rewarder of them that diligently seek Him (Heb. 11:6).\n\n> 8. To believe that God gives liberally and that he does not upbraid or rebuke when we come to him for anything (Jas. 1:5-8).\n\n> 9. To believe without ever a question or a waver that what is asked is done (Jas. 1:5-8).\n\n> 10. To believe that it is the will of God to give what He has promised (1 Jn. 3:22).\n\n> 11. To believe that Christ took all sickness and sin in His own body on the cross and that we do not have to bear them one minute after we accept His vicarious work (Mt. 8:16-17; 1 Pet. 2:24; Isa. 53:4-5).\n\n> 12. To believe that every believer can do the same works of God that Christ did and that God's power in the baptism of the Spirit is the same today as when Christ and the early believers received it (Jn. 14:12-15; Lk. 24:49; Acts 1:8; 2:38-39; 5:32).\n\nTo believe all this—and every other part of Bible doctrine and practice—really means something! If someone says believing isn't work, let them try to believe all of this and fully experience it without any effort whatsoever.\n\nThe very fact that we're spending so much time writing about these things from every angle, and that we have to answer every unbelieving argument from people who try to deny these truths, is proof enough that believing takes work from all of us.\n\nHere's the simple truth: if you can truly and wholeheartedly believe these things, God will abundantly meet you, and the Holy Spirit will work through you without limitations.\n\nWe cannot overemphasize this: you must believe these things before you see the results you want to see. That's why we're going into repeated detail—so this fact can finally dawn on all of us: God means what He says and says what He means about power from God, just as He does about forgiveness of sins (which we've all been taught to believe).\n\nStudy these lessons and supplements over and over, especially the Scriptures that naturally build up faith. Never doubt or waver about having all things possible. It doesn't matter what church, pastor, or religious zealot says about any theory to the contrary—if God has said these things, His Word is all-sufficient.\n\nPeople may argue that these powers aren't for us today, or that we're not seeing them, or that it's impossible to see them. But by now, you know better than to believe such arguments.\n\nWe can all get into the New Testament program if we follow the example of the early Church. We know what they did—the record is clear. We know we don't have this program in full operation in our churches today. We also know why: we're not putting into practice the things that made the early Church such a dynamic force.\n\nSo why not change our methods? Let's do what the early Church did and see what can be accomplished! If we want what they got, let's go to God to get it. Let's be honest and quit claiming we have what they had until we actually do.\n\nIt's similar to claiming you've run a marathon when you've only trained for it—you haven't actually crossed the finish line yet. Let's use plain common sense and realize that anything short of what they received isn't exactly what they received.\n\nIf we desire what they got, let's go to God for it in all fullness—the way they received it—and not be satisfied without it. If we don't want what they got, then let's not complain when we don't get it.\n\nWhen we finally receive what they received, it will produce the same works it did for them—and we'll know it just as surely as they knew it. We'll have the same power and manifestation of God that we read about in the New Testament, and then we'll know the New Testament program is in full operation again.\n\n---\n\n## Ten Questions For Supplement Ten. Do You Know:\n\n- That the Antichrist will never bring universal peace and prosperity so that the world will worship him and take his mark? All Scriptures teach contrary to this common theory. He will make a seven-year covenant of peace with Israel and by peace destroy many of them (Dan. 8:25; 9:27; Mt. 24:15-26). But among other nations, he goes forth conquering and is at war from his rise to power until Christ defeats him at Armageddon (Dan. 7:8, 24; 8:20-25; 9:27; 11:40-45; Rev. 13:4-7; 17:9-17; 19:11-21; Zech. 14:1-21). He will cause craft (cunning, or deceit) to prosper in his hand, but he cannot bring universal material prosperity—he doesn't rule the world, as we saw in Supplement Nine, Question 8. He personally prospers through war like any other conqueror, but even that is limited to his territory, as in any other war (Dan. 8:24; 11:35-45).\n\n- That multitudes of people will not know Jesus Christ has come to Earth until after His second advent and the resurrected saints begin taking over world governments in the Millennium? This is clear from the facts in Supplement Nine, Question 8, and because Scriptures definitely say many people will not see His glory or person the day He comes to Earth (Isa. 66:19). Only when Christ is reigning and missionaries and rulers go from Jerusalem to tell of Christ reigning in Zion will many nations say, "Let us go up to the mountain of the Lord" (Isa. 2:2-4; 52:7; Zech. 8:23). Only in the immediate vicinity of Jerusalem will every eye see Him at the second advent, which is pictured "as lightning" shining under only one part of Heaven (Mt. 24:27-31; Rev. 1:7).\n\n- That the Bible does not teach a general judgment for both the righteous and the wicked? Scripture teaches that the righteous will be resurrected and reign 1,000 years before the wicked are resurrected and judged (Rev. 20:1-15). The righteous will be judged and rewarded at the Judgment Seat of Christ in Heaven before returning with Christ to reign (2 Cor. 10:9-11; Rom. 14:10-11). Saints will already know their rewards and positions in the eternal kingdom before coming back to Earth with Christ, for when they come, they'll receive those positions (Mt. 16:27). Anyone can see there are 1,000 years between the end of this age when Christ comes (Mt. 16:27; 24:29-31; 25:31-46) and the end of the Millennium (Rev. 20)—so there's at least that much time between the two judgments.\n\n- That saints will be judged for sins committed in this life? "For we must all appear before the judgment seat of Christ; that every one may receive the things done in his body, according to that he hath done, whether it be good or bad" (2 Cor. 5:10-11; Rom. 14:10-11; Mt. 12:36; 16:27).\n\n- That there will be degrees of punishment in Hell and rewards in Heaven? Degrees of punishment in Hell are clear from Mt. 10:15; 11:22; 12:41; 23:14; Mk. 6:11; 12:40; Lk. 10:14; 11:31-32; 20:47. Degrees of reward in Heaven are clear from Mt. 16:27; 1 Cor. 3:11-15; Rom. 2:6; 14:10; 2 Cor. 5:9-11; 2 Tim. 4:14; Rev. 2:23; 22:12.\n\n- That there are five horsemen of the first four seals of Revelation instead of four as generally taught? There are four horses mentioned—white, red, black, and pale (green)—and then the account says "Hell followed with him," following the green horse. If there are four with Hell following the fourth, there must be five. Both Death and Hell are referred to as separate entities, so they must be symbolized by two separate horsemen (Rev. 6:8).\n\n- That the ten virgins do not represent any class of Christians today? These were ten young ladies who lived in Christ's day—this is clear from the parable itself. Christ used this actual wedding event to illustrate "watchfulness" in the kingdom of Heaven at His second advent after the Tribulation. That's the point He was making, as Mt. 25:13 shows: "Watch therefore, for ye know neither the day nor the hour wherein the Son of man cometh." In other words, you people in the kingdom of Heaven or realm of profession—where tares and wheat, good and bad, exist together as taught in Mt. 13—should learn from those ten virgins and not be slumbering and sleeping as they were. Every Christian living today will be either dead or alive at the rapture, and at that time every Christian will go to Heaven before the Tribulation. They won't be on Earth after the Tribulation but will return with Christ to set up an eternal kingdom (Zech. 14:5; Jude 14). The first word in the parable is "Then," proving the connection is to the second coming after the Tribulation, not to the rapture before it—so this parable couldn't refer to any class of Christians today who will go before the Tribulation.\n\n- That the seals, trumpets, and vials of Revelation are yet future? This is clear because all events of Rev. 4-22 must be fulfilled after the rapture of the Church, as proven in Supplement Six, Question 6. If true, then none of the seals, trumpets, vials, or other events of Rev. 4-22 will happen before the rapture. Since the rapture is future and these events must occur afterward, they're also future.\n\n- That the sun-clothed woman of Rev. 12 does not represent the Church? This is very clear, since every event from Rev. 4:1 onward must occur after the churches, as proven in Point 8 above and in Lesson Six, Question 6. Because the woman symbolizes something left on Earth after the rapture, she couldn't symbolize the Church—the Church is gone by then. She symbolizes national Israel, as we'll see in Lesson Forty-five.\n\n- That the manchild of Rev. 12:5 does not symbolize Christ or the Church? The simple reason is that everything from Rev. 4:1 onward must be fulfilled after the rapture of the Church. Rev. 1:1 says the entire book concerns things yet to come to pass from the time John saw them—so bringing the historical ascension of Christ into these prophetic future events is unscriptural. See Lesson Forty-five for further proof.\n\n---\n\nGrace isn't just the way you entered the Christian life — it's the operating system for all of it. And the church's program isn't optional for mature believers — it's the assignment. So here's the question: What would it look like for you to be actually running the New Testament program in your specific context — preaching and confirming the gospel — rather than observing others do it? And what's one concrete step you can take this week toward that?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk4bq0023ewhjpo63o7r0	cmnknk4bp001qewhjk5bcqnfj	Lesson 45 — Sun-Clothed Woman, Manchild, Dragon, Beast, and False Prophet	READING	4531	12	\N	t	Revelation 12 drops you into a cosmic drama with a pregnant woman, a dragon waiting to devour her child, and a war in heaven. If you read it as strange poetry, you'll miss what it actually is: a compressed summary of the entire conflict between God and Satan, from Lucifer's original rebellion to his final defeat. Once you know the symbols, the chapter reads like a news summary — of events that span all of history.\n\nLast time we worked through Jesus's prophetic discourse in Matthew 24-25. Now we dive into the symbolic heart of Revelation: the cosmic figures who appear in chapters 12-13 and what each one represents in God's unfolding plan.\n\nIn these passages, you'll find a complete picture of who this woman is — her identity, her motherhood, the persecution she faces, her flight, and her protection. Let's walk through these subjects together, connecting them with other scriptures as we go.\n\nNow, John saw the symbol of the sun-clothed woman in Heaven, but what she *represents* is actually on Earth. Here's how we know:\n\n> 1. The manchild will be caught up to Heaven at its deliverance — and that couldn't happen if the woman giving birth were already in Heaven (Rev. 12:5).\n\n> 2. The woman will flee into the wilderness, which is on Earth (Rev. 12:6).\n\n> 3. When the dragon is cast down to the Earth, he will persecute the woman (Rev. 12:13).\n\n> 4. The Earth will help the woman by swallowing the flood cast out of the dragon's mouth to devour her. This couldn't be said of her if she were in Heaven (Rev. 12:15, 16).\n\nHere's something important to remember: the woman is a *symbol*, and we need to treat her as such. We have to clearly distinguish between her and what she symbolizes.\n\nThink of it like a flag representing a nation — the cloth isn't the country itself, but it stands for something much bigger. There's always one central truth conveyed by a symbol, and we shouldn't stress the minor details. When details *are* given attention, they must harmonize with the main truth. This eliminates speculation about unexplained parts of the symbol.\n\nWe'll see shortly why this woman could not symbolize the Virgin Mary, the Church, or Christendom when we examine the four theories about the manchild in Point II below.\n\nWe firmly believe this woman symbolizes **National Israel**. By this term, we mean all the Israelites who will be in Palestine during the Seventieth Week and who will form the nation at that time (Joel 3; Zech. 9:10-16; 12:4-14; 13:8, 9; 14:1-21; Mt. 24:15-26; Dan. 9:27). The Jews in all other lands will remain where they are today and won't be gathered back to their land until the second advent (Isa. 11:11, 12; Ezek. 37:1-28; Mt. 24:31).\n\nHere's why the woman symbolizes National Israel:\n\n> 1. Israel is often mentioned in the Old Testament as a married woman (Isa. 54:1-6; Jer. 3:1-14). The entire book of Hosea shows the marriage of Israel and God, Israel's backsliding in playing the harlot and leaving God, her future humiliation and brokenness in the wilderness, and her return to her husband (Hos. 2:14-23; Rom. 11; Acts 15:13-18).\n\n> 2. The Bible recognizes only three classes of people in the world today: the Church, the Jews, and the Gentiles (1 Cor. 10:32). At the time this woman travails — in the middle of the Week — the Church will have been removed from the Earth, leaving only the last two classes, as proven in Lessons Forty-three and Forty-four.\n\n> 3. If the sun, moon, and twelve stars mean anything, they can only symbolize the same as seen in Joseph's dream in Gen. 37:9-11 — namely, the twelve tribal heads and their parents.\n\n> 4. Israel is the only one dealt with in Scripture that could fulfill what's said about the woman. Let's look at this more closely:\n\n![Lesson 45 — Sun-Clothed Woman, Manchild, Dragon, Beast, and False Prophet](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsajQk1NEhtesUMivpomO8x4WSPknRFTuAI75Q)\n\n### (1) The Travail of the Woman (Rev. 12:2-5)\n\nIn this passage, "travail" is used figuratively, since the woman is a symbol and therefore can't travail literally. Israel's travails have been numerous throughout her history — clearly revealed in her bondages and deliverances, beginning with Israel in Egypt and continuing through the period of the judges and kings, as listed in Lesson Seventeen, Point II.\n\nThese travails have continued more or less throughout history and will finally culminate in the worst period of travail she has ever known (Dan. 12:1; Mt. 24:15-26). Passages like Exod. 18:8; Jer. 4:31; 6:24; 13:21; 22:22, 24; and Mic. 4:9, 10 record some of Israel's past travails.\n\nThere is to be a future continued travail of Israel in the Tribulation Week with a two-fold result:\n\n> **A.** The deliverance of the manchild in the middle of the Week (Rev. 12:2-5; Jer. 30:6-9; Dan. 12:1; Isa. 66:7, 8). This will result from the terrible anguish and sorrow of Israel in the middle of the Week, because the Antichrist — with whom she has made a seven-years covenant as a guarantee of her protection from the great whore and the ten kings of Revised Rome — will have broken this covenant and determined to completely destroy her. At this time of travail, the manchild is to be delivered because that company of Jews symbolized by the manchild is not to go through the great tribulation, which will immediately follow.\n\n> **B.** The deliverance of the woman herself at the end of the Week when the Antichrist, along with many nations, tries to destroy Israel (Isa. 66:7, 8; Mic. 5:3; Zech. 12:10–14:21; Joel 3:21). The result of this travail will be the conversion of Israel as a nation in one day when Christ comes to defeat the Antichrist, exalt Israel, and set up His kingdom (Rom. 11:26, 27; Rev. 19:11–20:6; 2 Thess. 1:7-10).\n\n### (2) The Persecution, Flight, and Protection of the Woman (Rev. 12:6, 13-16)\n\nThe result of the war in Heaven will be the casting out of Satan and his angels to the Earth. "When the dragon saw [actually realized] that he was cast unto the earth, he persecuted the woman, which brought forth the manchild."\n\nThe dragon's first move will be to destroy the manchild "as soon as it was born" (Rev. 12:4). Having failed in this because of the catching up of the manchild, he will then turn on the woman who brought forth the manchild and will bitterly persecute her by causing the Antichrist to break his covenant with Israel.\n\nThe dragon will give his "power, throne, and great authority" to the Antichrist (Rev. 13:1, 2), and together they will make war on Israel. This persecution of Israel by the Antichrist is often referred to by the prophets (Isa. 10:20-27; 14:1-27; Jer. 30:3-9; Dan. 7:21-27; 8:23-25; 9:27; 11:40-45; Mic. 5:3-15, etc.).\n\nThe Jews will realize that the only way of escape is to flee into some country that is not under the control of the Antichrist (Mt. 24:15-24; Dan. 11:40-45). This persecution will therefore cause Israel to flee into the wilderness.\n\nShe will be aided by God in her flight, symbolized by the "two wings of an eagle," which will be given her "that she might fly into the wilderness into her place where she is nourished for a time, and times, and half a time from the face of the serpent" (Rev. 12:14. Compare Exod. 19:4; Deut. 32:11, 12).\n\nWhen the dragon learns that the woman is fleeing, he will cast out of his mouth water "as a flood" to swallow the woman, but the Earth will help her and open its mouth and swallow the flood, thus permitting the woman to escape the dragon.\n\nNow, a symbolic dragon cannot cast out a literal flood, nor can a symbolic flood be swallowed up by a literal Earth. The dragon is symbolic of Satan (Rev. 12:9), the woman of Israel, and the flood of the armies of the dragon and of the Antichrist, who will pursue after the fleeing Israelites to destroy them.\n\nThe Earth will open its mouth and swallow these armies as it did the company in Num. 16:29-35. Armies are often symbolized by floods (Jer. 46:7, 8; 47:2, 3; Dan. 9:26, 27; 11:21, 26).\n\nThere are many other prophecies in the Bible concerning the persecution, flight, and protection of the woman during the last 3½ years of the Week. Let's consider them briefly:\n\n> **A. In Isaiah.** "Send ye the lamb to the ruler of the land from Sela to the wilderness, unto the mount of the daughter of Zion . . . For it shall be, as a wandering bird cast out of the nest, so the daughters of Moab shall be at the fords of Arnon . . . hide the outcasts; bewray not him that wandereth . . . Let mine outcasts dwell with thee, Moab; be thou a covert to them from the face of the spoiler: for the extortioner is at an end, the spoiler ceaseth, the oppressors are consumed out of the land . . . And in mercy shall the throne be established; and he shall sit upon it in truth in the tabernacle of David, judging, and seeking judgment, and hasting righteousness" (Isa. 16:1-5).\n\n> This is the first prophecy of importance concerning the flight of the woman. It names the very place to which she will flee — the rock-hewn city of Sela, or Petra, as the Romans called it. It's located in Mount Seir, near Mount Hor, in the land of Edom and Moab.\n\n> This prophecy states that the daughters of Moab shall be at the fords of Arnon — a little stream at the north border of Moab — to welcome the Israelites as they flee into this wilderness city and country.\n\n> The city Petra is located in Edom proper, but the prophet speaks of Moab as also being Israel's protector at that time. This country forms part of the great wilderness mentioned so often in Scripture where Israel wandered for forty years.\n\n> Petra was a commercial center in the days of Solomon and was taken by Amaziah, king of Judah (2 Ki. 14:7; 2 Chron. 25:11, 12). It's mentioned several times as "the rock" because Sela means "rock" or "stronghold" (Judg. 1:36; Isa. 42:11; Jer. 48:28; 49:16; Obad. 3). In the first and last reference, the Revised Version translates it "Sela." It's also called "Bozrah" (Amos 1:12; Isa. 34:5, 6; 63:1-5; Jer. 48:24; 49:13, 22). The ancients gave it the name "Bozrah of the rock."\n\n> In 105 A.D. the Romans conquered this country and called it Arabia-Petra. When the Roman Empire's dominion waned, this territory fell back into the hands of the Arabians and was completely lost to the civilized world in the seventh century. Burckhardt rediscovered it in 1812.\n\n> The city of Petra lies in a large valley and is reached only by one narrow passageway, so narrow at places that two people cannot ride abreast. The sides at the top of this passage are so close as to shut out the sunlight.\n\n> The mountains forming a great protecting wall on all sides are from 200 to 1,000 feet high. The sides of this canyon are lined with temples, houses, and tombs, all hewn out of rock with great skill — they look as fresh as if they had been cut yesterday. The temple alone has accommodation for 3,000 people! Tradition says that Paul visited this city when he went into Arabia (Gal. 1:17). (See National Geographic magazine of Feb. 1935 for interesting pictures of this rock-hewn city.)\n\n> Isaiah 26:20, 21 also shows that this will be the place prepared by God to feed Israel for the 1,260 days: "Come, my people Israel, enter thou into thy chambers and shut thy doors about thee: hide thyself as it were for a moment [3½ years] until the indignation [great tribulation] be overpast." The setting of this passage is positively identified as being fulfilled in the last days, for the next verse states, "The Lord cometh out of his place to punish the inhabitants of the earth for their iniquity."\n\n> Isaiah again predicts that Israel will be hiding in Edom when Christ comes: "Who is this that cometh from Edom, with dyed garments from Bozrah? This that is glorious in his apparel, travelling in the greatness of his strength? I that speak in righteousness, mighty to save. . . Wherefore art thou red in thine apparel, and thy garments like him that treadeth in the winefat? . . . I have trodden the winepress alone; and of the people there was none with me: for I will tread them in mine anger, and trample them in my fury; and their blood shall be sprinkled upon my garments, and I will stain all my raiment . . . For the day of vengeance is in mine heart, and the year of my redeemed is come . . . And I looked, and there was none to help; and I wondered that there was none to uphold: therefore mine own arm brought salvation unto me; and my fury, it upheld me . . . And I will tread down the people in mine anger, and make them drunk in mine fury, and I will bring down their strength to the earth" (Isa. 63:1-6).\n\n> Here is a vivid picture of Christ coming to Earth to destroy the nations at Armageddon, which answers the questions asked in the first part of this passage. In His coming to Earth, Christ will pass over Petra, descending from the east (Mt. 24:27), and the Jewish fugitives hiding in Moab and Edom will see Him coming in clouds of Heaven with power and great glory (Mt. 24:15-31).\n\n> Then the words of Isa. 42:11-13 will be fulfilled: "Let the wilderness and the cities lift up their voice . . . let the inhabitants of the rock [Sela] sing, let them shout in the top of the mountains . . . The Lord shall go forth as a mighty man . . . He shall cry like a man of war . . . He shall prevail against His enemies." These passages, with Joel 3; Zech. 9:1–14:21; 2 Thess. 1:7-10; Rev. 14:14-20; 19:11-21, picture Armageddon when Christ delivers Israel from the Antichrist.\n\n> **B. In the Psalms.** "Moab is my washpot; over Edom will I cast out my shoe: Philistia triumph thou because of me . . . Who will bring me into the strong city? Who will lead me into Edom? . . . Wilt not thou, O God, which hadst cast us off? And thou, O God, which didst not go out with our armies? . . . Give us help from trouble: for vain is the help of man . . . Through God we shall do valiantly: for He it is that shall tread down our enemies" (Ps. 60:8-12).\n\n> This predicts the preservation of these countries from the Antichrist and God's protection of Israel in her flight from Judea into the wilderness when Antichrist breaks his covenant with her and seizes Palestine (Mt. 24:15-21; Dan. 11:40-45).\n\n> **C. In Ezekiel.** "As I live, saith the Lord God, surely with a mighty hand, and with a stretched out arm, and with fury poured out, will I rule over you: . . . And I will bring you out from the people, and will gather you out of the countries wherein ye are scattered, with a mighty hand, and with a stretched out arm, and with fury poured out . . . And I will bring you into the wilderness of the people, and there will I plead with you face to face. Like as I pleaded with your fathers in the wilderness of the land of Egypt, so will I plead with you, saith the Lord God . . . And I will cause you to pass under the rod, and I will bring you into the bond of the covenant: . . . And I will purge out from among you the rebels, and them that transgress against me: I will bring them forth out of the country where they sojourn, and they shall not enter into the land of Israel: and ye shall know that I am the Lord" (Ezek. 20:33-38).\n\n> This passage records another prophecy that God will bring Israel back from the nations in the last days and then bring her "into the wilderness of the people, and there I will plead with you face to face. Like as I pleaded with your fathers in the wilderness of the land of Egypt."\n\n> God will send the two witnesses with power to do miracles and to bring Israel back to God, literally fulfilling this passage in pleading with Israel like God pleaded with them through the first two prophets — Moses and Aaron — in their first wilderness experience (Rev. 11:3-13; Mal. 4:4-5). At the time of Israel's flight, the two witnesses will appear and do miracles in protecting them from the devil and Antichrist, and they will lead Israel into Petra and bring them back to their God.\n\n> Thus, when Christ appears over Edom in His descent to the Earth, Israel will look upon Him whom they have pierced and will say, "Blessed is he that cometh in the name of the Lord" (Mt. 23:37-39; Rev. 1:7).\n\n> **D. In Daniel.** "And at the time of the end shall the king of the south [Egypt] push at him [Syria] and the king of the north [Syria] shall come against him like a whirlwind with chariots, and with horsemen, and with many ships; and he shall enter into the countries, and shall overflow and pass over . . . He shall enter also into the glorious land, and many [not all] countries shall be overthrown: but these shall escape out of his hand, even Edom, and Moab, and the chiefs of the children of Ammon . . . He shall stretch forth his hand also upon the countries: and the land of Egypt shall not escape" (Dan. 11:36-45).\n\n> This reveals that at the time of Syria's conquests under the Antichrist, "many countries shall be overthrown: but these shall escape out of his hand, even Edom and Moab, and chief of the children of Ammon."\n\n> If Edom and Moab are to escape his hand, this seems to be the only nearby place where the Israelites in Judea can flee during the great tribulation. God will reserve these countries from Antichrist to use them as a place of refuge for His people, showing the "place prepared of God, that they [these countries] should feed her there" for 3½ years. This shows that the feeding is not necessarily supernatural — the inhabitants of the wilderness will no doubt feed Israel.\n\n> **E. In Hosea.** "Therefore, behold, I will allure her, and bring her into the wilderness, and speak comfortably unto her . . . And I will give her vineyards from thence, and the valley of Achor for a door of hope: and she shall sing there, as in the days of her youth, and as in the day when she came up out of the land of Egypt . . . And it shall be at that day, saith the Lord, that thou shalt call me Ishi; and shalt call me no more Baal" (Hos. 2:14-23).\n\n> This speaks of the wilderness to which Israel will flee for protection from the Antichrist, at which time God redeems Israel and marries her forever.\n\n> **F. In Matthew.** "When ye therefore shall see the abomination of desolation, spoken of by Daniel the prophet, stand in the holy place, (whoso readeth, let him understand:). . . Then let them which be in Judea flee into the mountains: . . . let him which is on the housetop not come down to take any thing out of his house: . . . Neither let him which is in the field return back to take his clothes . . . And woe unto them that are with child, and to them that give suck in those days! . . . But pray ye that your flight be not in the winter, neither on the sabbath day: . . . For then shall be great tribulation, such as was not since the beginning of the world to this time no, nor ever shall be . . . and except those days should be shortened, there should no flesh be saved: but for the elect's sake those days shall be shortened" (Mt. 24:15-22).\n\n> Jesus predicted that at the time the abomination of desolation is set up — when the Antichrist breaks his seven-years covenant with Israel, does away with their daily sacrifices to God in their temple, and sets himself and his image in the Holy of Holies to be worshipped during the last half of the Week — the Jews "in Judea" will flee into the mountains and "there shall be great tribulation, such as was not since the beginning of the world to this time, no, nor ever shall be" (Mt. 24:15-22; Dan. 9:27; 2 Thess. 2:1-3; Rev. 13:1-18).\n\nSo we have many passages speaking of the persecution, flight, and protection of the woman in the wilderness. They all refer to Israel — not to the Church, Christendom, or the Gentiles, as is clear in each passage.\n\nWhere in Scripture do we find even *one* passage that speaks of the Church or any tribe or nation other than Israel going into the wilderness? We don't. Therefore, we conclude that National Israel is the one symbolized by the sun-clothed woman of Rev. 12.\n\n---\n\n## II. The Manchild—Various Interpretations\n\nLet's briefly discuss the various interpretations of the manchild of Rev. 12:5. This is another symbol and must be treated as such. Here are four theories of the manchild and the reasons why they could not possibly be what the symbol means.\n\n### 1. The Manchild as Christ\n\nSome believe the manchild is Christ because He came from Israel according to the flesh (Rom. 9:4, 5), because He will rule the nations with a rod of iron (Rev. 19:15; Ps. 2), because He was translated to God (Eph. 1:20), and because He is called a man (2 Tim. 2:5).\n\nThis argument is too general and proves nothing, for *every* Jew came from Israel according to the flesh. On this basis, any son of Israel could be taken as the manchild!\n\nThe expression "rod of iron" means authority over the nations, and in this sense *every* raptured saint will have such authority with Christ. Scripture proves this for all classes of redeemed people:\n\n> (1) The Old Testament saints will reign with Christ (Ps. 149:6-9; Dan. 7:18, 27; Mt. 9:11, 12; Jer. 30:9; Ezek. 34:24; 37:24, 25; Hos. 3:5).\n\n> (2) The Church saints will reign with Christ (Mt. 19:28; 20:20-28; Rom. 8:17; 1 Cor. 4:8; 6:2; 2 Tim. 2:12; Rev. 1:5, 6; 2:26, 27).\n\n> (3) The 144,000 Jews will reign with Christ (Rev. 7:1-8; 12:5; 14:1-5; Ps. 149:6-9; Dan. 7:18, 27).\n\n> (4) The tribulation saints and all who have part in the first resurrection will reign with Christ (Rev. 20:4-6).\n\nSince all saints of all ages will reign with Christ, we conclude that the phrase "rod of iron" in Rev. 12:5 doesn't prove that Christ is the manchild any more than it could prove that any one of the above-mentioned four companies of the redeemed is the manchild.\n\nAll these saints will not have the same *degree* of rule, nor will it depend on being in a certain company. All will be given authority in proportion to their degree of faithfulness in service here on Earth, determined at the judgment seat of Christ (Lk. 19:11-27; Mt. 16:27; Rom. 14:10; 1 Cor. 3:11-16; 2 Cor. 5:10, 11).\n\nThe translation of Christ is no definite proof that Christ is the manchild either. The angel is not showing John the historical ascension of Christ, which he saw with his own eyes about sixty years previously, but a *prophecy* that will be fulfilled in the middle of the Week, as proven in Lesson Forty-one, Point II. All raptured saints will be caught up to God and His throne (1 Thess. 2:19; 3:13; 4:13-18; 5:23; Rev. 3:21; 4:4; 7:9-17; 14:1-5; 19:1-10).\n\nThat Christ is called a man doesn't prove He is the manchild, for others are also called men in Scripture. The Church is called a man (Eph. 2:15; 4:13). So all these arguments are too general and cannot be used as proof that Christ — or any other individual or group — is symbolized by the manchild.\n\nThis theory destroys the plain consecutive order of the book of Revelation, disregards its natural divisions, and inserts historical events in this plain prophecy. If this theory were true, it would require the fulfillment of all of Rev. 1:1–12:4 *before* the ascension of Christ.\n\nSurely it has been proven incontrovertibly that we are dealing with things in the middle of the Week and that all of Rev. 4-22 takes place after the Church Age. The plain statements concerning the woman and manchild could not possibly be harmonized with the events in the lives of Christ and the Virgin Mary.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that this theory is out of harmony with the intended thought in the "prophecy" and has no part in the "things which must shortly come to pass."\n\n### 2. The Manchild as the True Church\n\n> **(1)** Some believe the Church is the manchild, and professing Christians are the woman. They teach that the Church will be caught up out of the mass of professing Christians who will be left here to go into the wilderness, because the majority in Christendom won't be in the Church and therefore won't be ready to go up in the rapture.\n\n> This argument is no proof that the Church is symbolized by the manchild and the mass of professing Christians. There is no statement or indication to this effect in Scripture. Neither does such an argument prove that the Church is caught up in the middle of the Week. We have already proven in Lessons Forty-three and Forty-four that the Church will be raptured before the Week in Rev. 4:1. This being true, the Church cannot be caught up in the middle of the Week as the manchild will be. This theory, like the one about Christ, is out of harmony with plain revelation concerning the woman and manchild.\n\n> **(2)** It is further believed that the sun clothing the woman means the righteousness of Christ, that the moon symbolizes the Jewish ordinances superseded by Christ's teachings, and that the twelve stars in her crown represent the twelve apostles.\n\n> This too is without scriptural foundation and actually contradicts their own arguments in Point 2, (1) above. The woman cannot be the mass of professing Christians who are not ready to meet Christ or go up in the rapture *and still* be clothed with the righteousness of Christ.\n\n> If professing Christians were clothed with the righteousness of Christ, they would *be* Christ's, and if they were His, they would go in the rapture along with all other Christians (1 Cor. 15:20-23; 1 Thess. 4:16). Instead of clothing professing Christians with His righteousness, Christ condemns such to eternal damnation (Mt. 23; Rev. 3:14-17; Gal. 5:19-21; 6:7-8; Rom. 8:12-13).\n\n> On the same grounds, the twelve stars cannot represent the twelve apostles, for they are the foundation of the true Church and are not in the crowd of backslidden professing Christians (Eph. 2:19-22). Such a theory would have us believe that Christ and the apostles sanctioned hypocrisy and mere profession of the gospel — but they never did.\n\n> God will never protect a body of professing Christians who trample His Word under their feet any more than He will protect others who admit they are sinners. It is absurd to believe He would do this while allowing godly tribulation saints to suffer martyrdom without such protection and care.\n\n> However, in the case of Israel, God has sworn to protect her in the same manner that He is to protect this woman. God, to fulfill His covenants with their fathers, is obligated to protect Israel and bring her back to Himself. The primary purpose of the Tribulation is to accomplish this.\n\n> **(3)** It is argued that Christendom will travail in the middle of the Week because of the persecution of Christians by the Antichrist. This argument cannot be used of Christians, for in no passage do we find any statement that Christendom will travail in the middle of the Week or at any other time.\n\n> If the woman symbolizes the mass of professing Christians, she does not have real salvation, or she would be raptured. People must have real salvation if they undergo any serious persecution. This need not be the case with Israel, for the Jews are to be persecuted in fulfillment of prophecy. They cannot escape persecution by denying some faith as will be possible with Christians during the Seventieth Week — they will be persecuted because of *nationality* regardless of their religion.\n\n> **(4)** Some think the manchild is the Church because the seventh trumpet (Rev. 11:14–13:18), according to their belief, is the same as the "last trump" blown at the rapture of the Church (1 Cor. 15:51-58; 1 Thess. 4:13-18).\n\n> This association of the seventh trumpet with the "trump of God" does not prove the Church is the manchild. There are many points of contrast between these trumpets as has been proven in Lesson Forty-three, Point I, 2. Even after this seventh — or so-called "last trump" — takes place, there will be others sounded in the Tribulation (Isa. 27:12, 13; Zeph. 1:16-18; Zech. 9:14, 15; Mt. 24:31).\n\n> In conclusion, it is clear that the Church cannot be symbolized by the manchild, nor Christendom by the woman.\n\n### 3. The Bride Part of the Church as the Manchild\n\nThis theory teaches that the manchild represents the bride part of the Church, and that the bride of Christ is not the Church but merely a select company of believers — known as full overcomers — who are outside of the Church. This theory resolves itself into two questions:\n\n> **(1) Are the body of Christ and the Church the same?** According to this theory, they are not. However, three definite passages prove they *are* the same (Eph. 1:22, 23; Col. 1:18, 24). These passages state that the body of Christ and the Church are the same. There is no plain passage teaching a difference between the Church and a bride part of the Church. No Scripture ever mentions a difference between the body of Christ and the Church.\n\n> **(2) Are there two classes of people in the Church — partial overcomers as the Church and full overcomers as the bride part of the Church?** This theory teaches there are two classes and attempts to create within the Church two groups of believers — those who partially live in sin and those who do not, those who partially overcome and those who fully overcome.\n\n> This class of interpreters uses the seven promises made to the overcomer in Rev. 2 and 3, claiming that full overcomers form the bride who will receive rewards and reign with Christ, while partial overcomers are to be finally saved but have no part in Christ's reign.\n\n> Let's examine the New Testament books to determine whether there are two groups in the Church or not.\n\n> **A.** Paul, in writing to the Corinthians, leaves no doubt as to whether they form part of the body of Christ, saying, "For as the body is one [not two parts] and hath many members, and all the members of the one body, being many, are one body . . . by one Spirit are we all baptized into one body."\n\n> If the body is not one, then we might say that the Spirit is not one. If we can misconstrue such plain language and divide the one body, we can also divide the one Spirit on the same grounds, for the same terms are used of both.\n\n> Paul continues, "Now ye are\n\n---\n\nThe symbols of Revelation 12-13 are not puzzles to be solved but realities to be recognized — the same conflict that began with Lucifer's rebellion is still being played out on the world stage, and it is approaching its final act. So here's the question: The Dragon 'knows he has but a short time' (Rev. 12:12) and responds with great wrath. If Satan is operating with increased urgency as the end approaches, how does that change the seriousness with which you take your own spiritual warfare and the protection of those you love?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk4bq0024ewhjt3d3yuqv	cmnknk4bp001qewhjk5bcqnfj	Lesson 46 — The Beasts Out of the Sea and Earth	READING	2366	13	\N	t	The Antichrist is the most written-about future figure in popular prophecy — and the most misunderstood. Novels, films, and pulpit speculation have produced a caricature: a sinister villain obviously identifiable as evil. But the biblical portrait is more disturbing than that. He rises to power through peace, not war. He's embraced as a savior before he's revealed as a destroyer. He fools almost everyone — because he's designed to.\n\nLast time we identified the major symbolic figures of Revelation 12-13: the woman, the dragon, the manchild. Now we examine the two beasts specifically: the political Antichrist who rises from among the nations and the False Prophet who gives him religious legitimacy.\n\n![Lesson 46 — The Beasts Out of the Sea and Earth](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsJKBwb54ZDlMH1zAnxErj0PQYB2bIe8GdaVWL)\n\n## I. The Beast Out of the Sea (Rev. 13:1-10, 18)\n\nFirst, let's examine what this passage tells us about the beast out of the sea. This is symbolic language, and we need to treat it that way.\n\nThe sea represents peoples (Dan. 7:2, 3; Rev. 17:1, 15). The beast in Revelation points to the rise of a kingdom — and more specifically, to the Antichrist, the earthly head of that kingdom (Rev. 13:18). It also symbolizes a supernatural spirit out of the Abyss, as we'll see later.\n\nThroughout Scripture, beasts symbolize kingdoms and kings (Dan. 2:38, 39; 7:2-7 with 7:17, 23), as well as the supernatural powers controlling those kingdoms. This passage covers the personal Antichrist — his power, where that power comes from, his worship, characteristics, mouth, titles, wars, exaltation, reign, length of reign, and more. Let's walk through these briefly:\n\n### 1. Who is the Antichrist?\n\nAt the present time (1949), this question simply cannot be answered. It will remain unanswerable until the Antichrist personally makes the covenant with Israel for seven years (Dan. 9:27).\n\nMany people today, as always, speculate about whether the pope, Stalin, or others might be the Antichrist. This kind of speculation has done tremendous harm to the study of prophecy — many thinking people have become disgusted by it. Plenty of folks have turned their backs on prophetic Scripture because of unfounded guesses about Hitler, Mussolini, or other men who never became the Antichrist.\n\nHere's the bottom line: no man now prominent in world affairs could possibly be the Antichrist. The points below make this clear.\n\n### 2. From where does he come?\n\nThis question is fully answered in our study of Daniel (see Lesson Thirty-nine, Point IV, 2).\n\n### 3. When is he to be revealed?\n\nWhen will the Antichrist step into prominence in world affairs? Scripture answers this clearly:\n\n> **(1)** In Dan. 7:24, we have definite proof that the Antichrist cannot be revealed or prominent until after ten kingdoms are formed inside the Roman Empire. According to this verse, those ten kingdoms must first exist for some time as the seventh kingdom, or Revised Rome.\n>\n> The Antichrist will arise and gain control of all ten kingdoms during the first 3½ years of the Week. By the middle of the Week, he'll be seen as the beast of Rev. 13 coming up out of the sea of humanity — already possessing the seven heads and ten horns he conquered before the Week's midpoint.\n>\n> His emergence "out of the sea" at mid-Week simply represents the recognition of his power by the ten kingdoms and their acceptance of him, granted by the ten kings and the dragon (Rev. 13:2-4; 17:12-17). This verse also teaches that because he rises from within the ten kingdoms, he comes out of obscurity and his rise to power will be rapid. Daniel saw the "little horn" rising so suddenly among the ten that he was bewildered (Dan. 7:7-8, 19-24). So no one can identify the Antichrist until after those ten kingdoms are formed.\n\n> **(2)** The Antichrist also cannot be revealed until after the rapture, as proven in 2 Thess. 2:6-8 (see Lesson Forty-three, Point VII, 9).\n\n### 4. How long is he to reign?\n\nHe'll reign over one of the ten kingdoms of Revised Rome at the start of the Week. During the last 3½ years, he'll gain power over all ten kingdoms (Rev. 13:5; 7:25; 12:7).\n\nIt's in those final 3½ years that he'll exalt himself above every god and be worshipped by many of his subjects (Rev. 13:14-18; Dan. 8:25; 11:36-45; 2 Thess. 2:4).\n\n### 5. Where is he to reign?\n\nDuring part of the last 3½ years, the Antichrist will reign in Jerusalem "in the glorious holy mountain" where the temple will be rebuilt (Dan. 11:45). He'll sit "in the temple of God, showing himself that he is God" (2 Thess. 2:4).\n\nThis temple is where the abomination of desolation will be placed (Dan. 9:27; 12:7-13; Mt. 24:15-22; Rev. 11:1, 2; 13:12-18). Until then, Babylon — not Rome — will be his place of reign (as we saw in Lesson Thirty-nine, IV, 2, (6)).\n\nThe fact that there will be ten separate kingdoms with ten separate capitals and ten separate kings during the first 3½ years shows that up to mid-Week, the Antichrist doesn't have one central capital. The ten kingdoms won't be under him yet. Rome will be just one of those ten capitals, with her king ruling only over Italy and her possessions — not over all of Revised Rome.\n\nOnly when the Antichrist becomes head of the ten kingdoms at mid-Week will he establish one central throne in Jerusalem for the entire newly formed empire. Even then, the kings will continue as kings under him (Rev. 17:9-17).\n\n### 6. The power of the Antichrist\n\nThe Antichrist's power will come from Satan, the spirit of the Abyss, and the ten kings. God has already predicted this power, and it will be given to him at the proper time.\n\nThink of it like a CEO who receives authority from multiple sources — a board of directors, investors, and executive teams. In the Antichrist's case, God will permit Satan and his agents to give their power to the beast and inspire his evil designs (Dan. 8:24; 2 Thess. 2:8-12; Rev. 13:1, 2). God will put it into the hearts of the ten kings to give him their power for the purpose of destroying Mystical Babylon (Rev. 17:12-17). The satanic prince out of the Abyss (Rev. 11:7; 17:8) will be the executor of Satan's power to the beast, inspiring and backing the Antichrist in all his diabolical activities (more on this in the next two lessons).\n\nSatan will give the Antichrist the world-kingdoms he once offered Christ. The Antichrist will accept what Christ refused. But the Antichrist will still have to fight to possess them — just as Christ would have had to do and will yet do.\n\nThe Antichrist will succeed in this world conquest by conquering the Revised Roman Empire by mid-Week, and all the northern and eastern countries of Asia and Europe by the Week's end. He'll also gain cooperation from many other nations through the ministry of three unclean spirits, who will help him against the Jews and Christ at the second advent.\n\nAfter his defeat at Armageddon by Christ, the Antichrist will be cast into the Lake of Fire. The kingdom of God will succeed his kingdom and extend throughout all the Earth.\n\nHere's a summary of the beast's power:\n\n> **(1)** To blaspheme God (Dan. 7:8, 11, 20, 25; 11:36; Rev. 13:5, 6)\n>\n> **(2)** To overcome saints (Rev. 7:9-17; 14:13; 15:2-4)\n>\n> **(3)** To overcome the Jews (Dan. 7:21; 12:7; Rev. 13:7, 15)\n>\n> **(4)** To conquer many nations (Dan. 7:8, 20-24; 11:36-45; Ezek. 38; 39) and rule them as he wills (Rev. 13:7)\n>\n> **(5)** To destroy Mystery Babylon (Rev. 17:12-17)\n>\n> **(6)** To overcome and kill the two witnesses (Rev. 11:7)\n>\n> **(7)** To change times and laws (Dan. 7:25)\n>\n> **(8)** To understand mysteries (Dan. 8:23)\n>\n> **(9)** To protect the Jews as long as he desires, and also to succeed in destroying them for a period (Dan. 9:27; 2 Thess. 2:4; Rev. 11:1-2)\n>\n> **(10)** To work signs and wonders (Dan. 8:24; 2 Thess. 2:8-12; Rev. 13:1-18; 19:20)\n>\n> **(11)** To cause craft to prosper (Dan. 8:25)\n>\n> **(12)** To control money and riches in his own realm (Dan. 11:38-43)\n>\n> **(13)** To cause great deceptions (2 Thess. 2:10-12; Jn. 5:43; Dan. 8:25; Rev. 13:1-18)\n>\n> **(14)** To do according to his own will (Dan. 11:36)\n>\n> **(15)** To control religion and worship (Dan. 11:36; 2 Thess. 2:4; Rev. 13:1-18; 14:9-11; 16:21)\n>\n> **(16)** To control the lives of all men in his realm (Rev. 13:12-18)\n>\n> **(17)** To control kings as he wills (Rev. 17:12-17)\n>\n> **(18)** To make all other nations fear him (Rev. 13:4)\n>\n> **(19)** To fight against Christ (Rev. 19:11-21; Dan. 8:25)\n>\n> **(20)** To reign forty-two months (Rev. 13:5)\n\n### 7. The Titles of the Antichrist\n\n> **(1) "Antichrist."** This is the most common title we use when speaking of him, since he'll be the great opponent of Christ at the end of the age. The word appears only four times in the Bible (1 Jn. 2:18, 22; 4:3; 2 Jn. 7), but our studies show he's the one who bears that title more than any other and is expressly stated to come according to these passages.\n\n> **(2) "The Assyrian"** (Isa. 10:20-27; 30:18-33; 31:4–32:20; Mic. 5:3-15). The prophecies in these passages were recorded against the Assyrian king in the prophets' days, but a careful study reveals they have a latter-day fulfillment in the future Assyrian king who will oppress Israel just before her final restoration. (The Assyrian territory will be part of the Antichrist's kingdom, so in that sense he is the king of Assyria.)\n>\n> The first passage (Isa. 10:20-27) refers to the "remnant" of Rev. 12:17: "In that day the remnant of Israel . . . shall no more again stay [Hebrew, 'look for support'] upon him [Antichrist] that smote them; but shall stay upon the Lord. . . . O my people that dwellest in Zion, be not afraid of the Assyrian: he shall smite thee with a rod . . . yet a little while [1,260 days, Rev. 12:6, 14-17; 13:5], and the indignation [Hebrew, 'God's anger and wrath,' the day of vengeance in the Tribulation, as in Isa. 26:20; Dan. 8:19; 11:36] shall cease, and mine anger in their destruction . . . in that day his burden shall be taken away from off thy shoulder . . . the yoke shall be destroyed because of the anointing." The Hebrew root for "anointing" is *shawman*, "to shine," and no doubt refers to the brightness of Christ's coming in 2 Thess. 2:8, 9.\n>\n> The second passage (Isa. 30:18-33) clearly refers to Israel's final restoration under the Messiah: "Therefore will he [the Lord] be exalted . . . the people shall dwell in Zion at Jerusalem: thou shalt weep no more . . . in the day that the Lord bindeth the breach of his people . . . the name of the Lord cometh from far, burning with his anger . . . to sift the nations . . . with the flame of a burning fire, with scattering, and tempest, and hailstones. For through the voice of the Lord shall the Assyrian be beaten down." (See also Ezek. 38:17-21; 2 Thess. 1:7-10; 2:8-12.)\n>\n> The third passage (Isa. 31:4–32:20) speaks of the same truth: "Like as the lion roaring on his prey . . . shall the Lord of hosts come down to fight for mount Zion . . . As birds flying so will the Lord of hosts defend Jerusalem; defending also he will deliver it; and passing over he will preserve it . . . For in that day every man shall cast away his idols . . . then shall the Assyrian fall with the sword, not of a mighty man [but by Christ, 2 Thess. 2:8, 9] . . . a king shall reign in righteousness . . . And my people shall dwell in a peaceable habitation, and in sure dwellings, and in quiet resting places."\n>\n> The last passage (Mic. 5:3-15) definitely speaks of Israel being given up "until the time that she which travaileth hath brought forth [until Israel has brought forth the manchild, as we saw in the previous lesson]: then the remnant of his brethren shall return unto the children of Israel. And he [Christ, verses 1, 2] shall stand and feed in the strength of the Lord . . . now shall he be great unto the ends of the earth. And this man shall be the peace, when the Assyrian [Antichrist] shall come into our land: and shall tread in our palaces . . . thus shall he deliver us from the Assyrian . . . I will execute vengeance in anger and fury upon the heathen, such as they have not heard." (See also Dan. 9:27; 11:40-45; 2 Thess. 2:3-4; Rev. 13.)\n\n> **(3) "The king of Babylon"** (Isa. 14:4). This passage is in a prophecy of Babylon that had partial fulfillment in Babylon's overthrow by the Medes and Persians (Isa. 13:17). The complete fulfillment will come in the last days under the Antichrist, as proven by the mention of "the day of the Lord" and Israel's restoration, which will occur when Christ comes to Earth during the Antichrist's reign (Isa. 13:6-16, 19-22; 14:1-8, 18-27). All these prophecies have never been fulfilled as stated here. The Antichrist will be the king of Babylon because he'll be the king of Assyria, which will include Babylon (see Lesson Forty-one on Babylon).\n\n> **(4) "The spoiler" and "the extortioner"** (Isa. 16:1-5). That these terms refer to the Antichrist is clear from studying this passage in Lesson Forty-five, Point I.\n\n> **(5) "Gog, the chief prince of Meshech and Tubal"** (Ezek. 38; 39). These two chapters will be fulfilled at Armageddon (see Lesson Forty-nine).\n\n> **(6) The "little horn"** (Dan. 7:8, 24; 8:9, 23).\n\n> **(7) "A king of fierce countenance"** (Dan. 8:23).\n\n> **(8) "The prince that shall come"** (Dan. 9:26, 27). This title refers to the same man as the "little horn" coming from the ten kingdoms of Revised Rome who will make the seven-year covenant with Israel and then break it mid-Week, causing the abomination of desolation in the Jewish temple at Jerusalem (see Lesson Forty).\n\n> **(9) "The king of the north"** (Dan. 11:36-45). This is the king of the Syrian division of the old Grecian Empire (see our study of Daniel in Lesson Thirty-nine). He's called the king of the north because he'll come from the northern division of old Greece — that is, north of Palestine.\n>\n> Many Bible teachers say the Antichrist will come from Russia and use this term to prove it. But if "the king of the north" refers to Russia, then what countries are there north of Russia that could fight against Russia, as required in Dan. 11:44? There are none! So this title applies to the future king of Syria — the northern division of the four divisions of the old Grecian Empire from which the Antichrist must come (Dan. 8:8, 9, 20-25).\n\n> **(10) "The man of sin"** (2 Thess. 2:1-12).\n\n> **(11) "The son of perdition"** (2 Thess. 2:1-12).\n\n> **(12) "The wicked" and "that wicked"** (Isa. 11:4; 2 Thess. 2:1-12).\n\nThese last four titles picture the Antichrist in his role as the most sinful and wicked man of his time — perhaps of all time. He'll literally murder multitudes who won't conform to his every desire (Rev. 7:9-17; 13:16-18; 15:1-3; 20:4-6). For this wickedness, he's "the son of perdition" because he's destined for perdition — destruction and eternal Hell.\n\nNow, some teach that the Antichrist is the "mystery of iniquity," or Satan manifest in the flesh just as Jesus was the mystery of godliness (God manifest in the flesh). They claim the Antichrist will be "the son of perdition" — a literal son of Satan by a woman, just as Jesus was "the Son of God" by a woman. They argue the Antichrist is Christ's opposite in every detail.\n\nBut this is not taught in Scripture.\n\nThe Antichrist is a mysterious figure, and he'll accomplish astonishing things. But not one statement in Scripture about him is truly mysterious or teaches that he'll be supernatural, an immortal man from the Abyss, an incarnation of the devil, or a natural son of the devil. We'll see this clearly in the next two lessons.\n\nThe phrase "mystery of iniquity" literally means "the invisible spirit of lawlessness" or "the evil spirit forces that cause man to sin" (Jn. 8:44; 14:30; Eph. 2:1-3; 1 Jn. 3:8; Eph. 6:10-18; 2 Cor. 4:3, 4).\n\nSome who teach that the Antichrist is the mystery of iniquity also claim he's the beast now bound in the Abyss who will come out again as the Antichrist. They teach this spirit is Judas who will be reincarnated, with their main argument being that both Judas and the Antichrist are called "the son of perdition" (Jn. 17:12; 2 Thess. 2:14).\n\nBut as we'll see in the next two lessons, no human being has ever gone into the Abyss, nor will one ever go there — so Judas couldn't be in the pit to come out. The expression "the son of perdition" literally means "the son of destruction" because both Judas and the Antichrist are destined for destruction — not because they're natural sons of Satan.\n\nThey couldn't be sons of Satan and sons of their earthly fathers at the same time. And they couldn't be natural sons of Satan and incarnations of him too, as some teach.\n\nIn the Greek, it reads "the son of the destruction" just as it reads "the man of the sin." This last phrase doesn't limit the Antichrist to being the only man of sin, and the former phrase doesn't limit him to being the only son of destruction.\n\nIt's similar to how the Hebrews and Greeks called anyone subject to a particular trait the "son" or "child" of that trait. We see this throughout Scripture:\n\n- "sons of Belial" (1 Sam. 1:16; 2:12; 25:17, 25; 1 Ki. 21:10)\n- "child of the devil" (Acts 13:10)\n- "children of the wicked one" (Mt. 13:38)\n- "children of the devil" (1 Jn. 3:10)\n- "children of wisdom" (Lk. 7:35)\n- "children of the world" (Lk. 16:8)\n- "children of light" (Lk. 16:8; Jn. 12:36)\n- "children of disobedience" (Eph. 2:1-3; 5:6-8; Col. 3:6)\n\nAnyone destined for a particular fate was called the child of that destiny:\n\n- "children of the kingdom" (Mt. 8:12)\n- "children of wrath" (Eph. 2:1-3)\n- "children of the resurrection" (Lk. 20:36)\n\nGiven this common usage, it's only natural to call both Judas and the Antichrist "the son of perdition" or destruction — both are destined for destruction in Hell because of their sin.\n\nThe word "perdition" appears only eight times and comes from the Greek *apoleia*, meaning "ruin, loss, destruction, perdition, and perish." It's never used as a name for the devil. So calling Judas and the Antichrist "sons of the devil by a woman" is simply not biblical.\n\nScripture never states that Judas ever was or will be, nor that the future Antichrist ever will be, a direct and literal child of the devil by a woman. Such a statement is as far from truth as the devil himself!\n\nTry substituting "devil" for "perdition" everywhere else it's found, and see if it makes sense (Phil. 1:28; 1 Tim. 6:9; Heb. 10:39; 2 Pet. 3:7; Rev. 17:8, 11). The Greek word *apoleia* is translated "destruction" (Mt. 7:13; Rom. 9:22; Phil. 3:19; 2 Pet. 2:1; 3:16), "damnation" (2 Pet. 2:3), "die" (Acts 25:16), "perish" (Acts 8:20), and other ways — but never as "devil."\n\nWe can conclude that "son of perdition" does not mean "son of the devil."\n\nAll other statements about the Antichrist — "coming in his own name" (Jn. 5:43), "exalting himself" (2 Thess. 2:4), "being worshipped" (Rev. 13:8), "being cast into Hell" (Rev. 19:20), "doing his own will" (Dan. 11:36), "destroying men" (Dan. 8:24), "being wicked" (2 Thess. 2:3-8), and other facts — don't prove he's some super-mysterious being.\n\nAll these statements can be understood in connection with any natural, mortal man, as we'll see. If the Antichrist is the mystery of lawlessness, then he's been here all along and can't come from the pit — Paul said this mystery was already at work in his day (2 Thess. 2:7).\n\nToo many people spend their lives trying to make the Bible a mystery instead of seeking to understand the simple book it really is. All such hidden interpretations must be rejected for the sake of simple truth.\n\nThe devil is never going to have a natural son by a woman. Genesis 3:15 certainly doesn't teach this. The seed of the serpent should be understood as the natural offspring of snakes and the spiritual children of the devil (Mt. 13:38; 1 Jn. 3:8-10; Jn. 8:44).\n\nSome take this last passage to mean the Antichrist will be a natural seed of the devil: "Ye are of your father the devil . . . When he speaketh a lie, he speaketh of his own; for he is a liar, and the father of it." They claim "it" refers to one particular son of the devil — the Antichrist.\n\nBut this same passage speaks of all those men as being "of your father the devil." Plus, the "lie" refers to an actual lie, not a natural son of the devil by a woman!\n\nIf telling a lie can be turned into a natural son of the devil here, then we could make natural sons of Satan in Acts 5:3, Rom. 1:25, Ps. 78:36, and everywhere else in Scripture where lies were spoken. If the "lie" here means a natural son the devil will have by a woman, then he'll "speak" this son into existence — which means he couldn't be a natural son by a woman or by incarnation, as some teach.\n\nIf Satan could do this, he'd be speaking enough sons into existence to fill the Earth for a better chance to defeat God. Such teaching is plain foolishness.\n\nSome also argue that Judas was the only one ever called a "devil," proving he was the devil incarnate, the mystery of iniquity, and a son of perdition (Jn. 6:70, 71; 17:12). They point to the definite article "the" making Judas "the devil."\n\nBut the definite article isn't in the Greek at all — it reads "a devil." The Greek word for "devil" is *diabolos*, meaning "adversary" or "slanderer." It's used of other people who are called "false accusers" (2 Tim. 3:3; Titus 2:3) and "slanderers" (1 Tim. 3:11).\n\nSince the word is used of other people, Judas clearly isn't the only one it applies to. The word never implies incarnation. If it did, then these other men who are slanderers (devils) were also incarnations of the devil — which destroys the whole theory.\n\nThe devil never incarnates himself in the Antichrist any more than he did in Judas. The dragon is always seen as a separate person outside the beast.\n\nIf Judas were the devil incarnate, then the devil was the son of Simon, a human being — so he couldn't have been created by God and couldn't have existed until Christ's time (Jn. 6:70, 71).\n\nAccording to this reasoning, it was the devil who:\n\n- Betrayed Jesus (Mt. 10:4)\n- Jesus chose as one of His own trusted disciples and planned to give him a throne in the eternal kingdom (Mt. 10:1-8; Lk. 22:28-30)\n- Received power from the Holy Spirit through Jesus to cast out himself and his demons and destroy his own work (Mt. 6:7-13)\n- Followed Jesus and had fellowship with Him for over three years (Ps. 41:9; 55:12, 13)\n- Had a place in the bishopric and fell from it by transgression (Acts 1:15-25; 109:8)\n- Lost his name out of the Book of Life (Acts 1:20 with Ps. 69:25-28)\n- Entered into himself to betray Jesus (Lk. 22:3)\n- Carried the bag and was the trusted treasurer of the apostolic band (Jn. 13:29)\n- Repented himself for betraying Jesus (Mt. 27:3-10)\n- Hung himself (Mt. 27:5)\n- Had his bowels gush out and was buried in a potter's field (Acts 1:17-20)\n\nWho could believe these things happened to the devil?\n\nIf Judas were the devil incarnate, then it was Judas who:\n\n- Was a created being and angel that ruled Earth before Adam and invaded Heaven and was cast out (Isa. 14:12-14; Ezek. 28:11-17; Lk. 10:18)\n- Opposed Israel and smote Job with boils and has present access to Heaven (2 Chron. 21:1; Job 1:6–2:7)\n- Stood at his own right hand to betray Jesus and became childless ever afterward (Ps. 109:20)\n- Tempted Christ (Mt. 4:1-11)\n- Used Peter as a tool (Mt. 16:23)\n- Caused all the sickness in men (Acts 10:38; Lk. 13:16)\n- Was still alive and worked against early believers after committing suicide (Acts 5:3; 26:18)\n- Does all the things the devil does throughout Scripture\n\nCheck the words "Satan" and "devil" in a concordance and see all the things Judas (supposedly) did to oppose God from creation until now — if he's the devil.\n\nIf the Antichrist were the devil incarnate, we'd have to conclude:\n\n- That the devil hasn't come yet (1 Jn. 2:18; Jn. 5:43)\n- That he won't come until after ten kingdoms form inside the Roman Empire (Dan. 7:24) and after the Church is raptured (2 Thess. 2:7, 8)\n- That he'll only continue forty-two months when he does come (Rev. 13:5)\n- That the devil is a "man" (Rev. 13:18)\n- That this man is in Heaven now accusing the saints and will be cast out of Heaven mid-Week (Rev. 12:7-17; 13:1-8)\n- That the dragon isn't a separate person outside the beast as he's pictured in all relevant passages (Rev. 13:2-4; 16:13-16; 19:20; 20:10)\n- That the devil will be "slain" by Christ at His second advent (Dan. 7:11; Isa. 11:4; 2 Thess. 2:8, 9)\n- That he'll be put in two different places during the Millennium — the beast in the Lake of Fire and the dragon in the Abyss (Rev. 19:20; 20:1-3)\n- That the devil is still in the Lake of Fire while he's loosed at the Millennium's end and will be cast back where he already is and has been since Armageddon (Rev. 19:20; 20:1-10)\n- That the devil has died once and will die twice more if we believe these theories\n\nWe'd also have to believe that Judas became his own father when he incarnated himself in a woman and was born, that he died and will incarnate again in a woman in the latter days as a second incarnation of the devil — or of himself.\n\nHow could anyone become nothing but a seed in a woman twice and grow from nothing but a seed to a full-grown man twice? How could the devil as an immortal angel be a man and die three times? How could he be the devil outside both Judas and the Antichrist while being an incarnation of the devil in both?\n\nHow could the devil enter into Judas if he were Judas? How could he give his power to the Antichrist and be a dragon outside him while still being the Antichrist? How could he be the beast in the Lake of Fire and still be himself in the pit?\n\nThese and other ridiculous theories we'd have to believe if we accepted what some teach about the Antichrist being both Judas and the devil. These teachings aren't the truth about the devil, Judas, or the Antichrist — or about the head wounded to death, as we'll see in the next two lessons.\n\n> **(13) "The beast"** (Dan. 7:11; Rev. 13:1-18; 14:9-11; 15:2, 3; 16:2, 10; 17:1-18; 19:19-21; 20:2-4, 10). We'll explain this beast in the next two lessons.\n\n### 8. His Person\n\nAll our studies prove that the Antichrist will be a real person — not a religious system or the successive head of some system like the pope. He's yet to come and will literally fulfill all the prophecies concerning himself.\n\nHis character and characteristics are clearly implied in the points above. He'll be a man possessing the talent and leadership of all previously gifted conquerors and leaders. Beyond these natural gifts, he'll possess miraculous power\n\n---\n\nThe Antichrist's power comes not from obvious evil but from the world's desperate hunger for a human solution to human problems. He offers peace, economic stability, and unified global leadership — and the world accepts it. So here's the question that this lesson presses: Are you developing the discernment to recognize counterfeit solutions to real problems — in politics, in spirituality, in global leadership — that look like answers but move the world further from God? And is your trust in human institutions calibrated appropriately, or has it quietly displaced your trust in God?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk461000vewhjz15z22vp	cmnknk45y000gewhjn5pl0kw9	Supplement 9 — For Lessons 17 & 18	SUPPLEMENT	1982	14	\N	t	The Mosaic Law was given to one specific people for one specific era — and the Old Testament Church carried God's purposes through eighteen centuries of history. Both truths carry a practical question: How does the Law relate to you today? Not theoretically. Not dispensationally. In your actual experience of guilt, obligation, and grace.\n\nWe've walked through the Dispensations of Innocence, Conscience, Human Government, Promise, and Law. We've also studied God's plan for meeting human needs, why those needs often go unrealized, the Old Testament Church, divine healing and health, and asking and receiving from God.\n\nBy now, you should have a clear understanding of these subjects and how to access the material, physical, and spiritual blessings of the gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God. All these studies of the historical past will serve as a firm foundation for our future studies on God's present and future dealings with mankind.\n\nHere's the exciting part: the richest studies of your life are yet to come! Lesson after lesson will grow richer and clearer concerning God's plan for you. You'll be able to conform more and more to the total will of God until you become hidden with Christ in God — able to exercise full authority as a son of God with power.\n\n![Supplement 9 — For Lessons 17 & 18](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8Os7BtJ5dpRlSEtNozZx4rmO6UfQcdhyY2pDqwX)\n\n## Satanic Opposition Must Be Understood\n\nAs we continue our lessons, we need to do more than just reveal truths that may be new to you. We also need to warn you about old errors that have hindered Christians for centuries.\n\nOne of the greatest errors in the Christian world today is accepting any opposition to answered prayer — or any delay in answers — as the will of God. Think of it like this: if you asked your loving father for something he'd already promised you, and someone kept blocking the delivery, would you assume your father had changed his mind? Of course not! You'd realize someone else was causing the problem.\n\nLet me share an example. I recently received a letter from a woman saying she knew it wasn't God's will to heal her insane son because she had believed God and He didn't heal the boy. She said God's answer was simply "No." In other words, because her prayer wasn't granted, she concluded it must not be God's will to heal her son.\n\nThere's not one scriptural word to justify this kind of reasoning. This is a plain deception by the devil, making her believe it's not God's will to heal her boy and that she should just accept it. The devil has not only robbed God of the glory He would receive from such a healing, but he's kept his victim insane and deceived a mother into thinking this is how God answers prayer.\n\nThe truth is that it *is* God's will to heal this boy and deliver this mother from such slanderous concepts of God. This kind of thinking actually accuses God of being a liar — of not answering prayer as He promised, of being unmerciful and hard-hearted with no sympathy for mother or child, of sending Jesus to die to bring deliverance and then deliberately refusing to provide what Christ died for, and of not meeting His children's faith according to His promises.\n\nYou can believe these fallacies if you want, but we warn you — these are some of the very reasons prayer goes unanswered. As long as the devil can deceive people this way, he can hold them in bondage and rob them of the benefits for which Christ died. If we're ever going to receive answers to prayer, we need to learn what to do and intelligently proceed according to that knowledge.\n\nThe devil knows the individual disposition of each person. He wages spiritual war against each one according to what he thinks will be the best way to keep that person from getting what they want from God. Satan stops at nothing to achieve his goals. He has no righteous principle about him and no respect for God or any person.\n\nHe's out to defeat both God and man, and he has a distinct advantage in some ways. People are born in sin and spiritually dead — sons of the devil by choice and lifestyle. We're born with a natural tendency toward sin, selfishness, doubt, and unbelief.\n\nThe Bible declares that God's ways and thoughts are higher than ours as the heavens are higher than the Earth (Isa. 55:9). So naturally, those born in sin contrary to these ways will have some difficulty conforming wholly to them. The carnal mind is not subject to the law of God, neither indeed can be (Rom 8:7-8).\n\nThe new birth is necessary to enable us to conform by nature to God's ways (Jn. 3:3-5; 2 Cor. 5:17). This is why natural people, who are more in harmony with Satan and sin by birth than with God and His ways, experience conflict with spiritual things. It also explains why there's a natural conflict between God and Satan, and between God and the natural person.\n\nWe shouldn't expect anyone to learn God's ways by nature or get prayers answered without opposition from Satan. We shouldn't expect there will be no conflict with carnal desires, unbelief, and doubts in the process of getting prayers answered from God.\n\nPaul speaks of being "hindered" many times by Satan in his spiritual work (1 Thess. 2:18). Peter speaks of prayers being "hindered" even by wrong living on the part of those who pray (1 Pet. 3:7). If this is true, then it's even more true that Satan and supernatural evil spirits can and do hinder prayers.\n\nWe're instructed to give no "place to the devil" (Eph. 4:27), to "stand against the wiles of the devil" (Eph. 6:10-18), know "his devices" (2 Cor. 2:11), and "resist the devil" (Jas. 4:7; 1 Pet. 5:8). All these facts prove that people who pray to God should expect opposition and all kinds of hindrances to their prayers.\n\nHow foolish it is to accept such opposition as God's will or as an indication that God won't grant what's been asked according to His promises!\n\nHere's something important to understand: the reason many followers of false religions seem to get great benefits without opposition is that they're in league with the devil and deceived by him. Why would the devil oppose people he's already deceived, who are already safe in his snare and doomed to Hell with him? That wouldn't be to his advantage.\n\nIt's actually wise strategy on his part to help people get some benefits in order to keep them deceived. If he can get anyone to reject the true God and the essentials of salvation through Christ, he'll gladly cooperate by giving that person certain benefits or removing opposition to blessings that come to all by divine providence. He'll even heal or remove sickness from people to deceive them into thinking some false religion is the true one.\n\nIt's only when someone becomes aggressive against Satan and turns to God for help that demons wage war against that person. They do everything in their power to defeat anyone trying to receive the benefits of the gospel. They'll work on your feelings, hinder your faith in God, cause doubt and questioning, and obstruct in every conceivable way those who seek God by faith for the benefits promised in His Word.\n\nThis must be thoroughly understood. You must make up your mind to go ahead and get God's blessings in spite of satanic opposition.\n\n## Necessary Steps to Follow When Faith Seems Weak and Victory Seems Lost\n\n> 1. Recognize the source of the opposition as being of Satan.\n\n> 2. Be sure that the promises of God cover the thing asked for.\n\n> 3. Be sure that you are not living in open or secret sin.\n\n> 4. Be sure that no doubt or unbelief is permitted in the life concerning the promises.\n\n> 5. Sincerely desire the benefit that you ask of God.\n\n> 6. Ask of God in faith, nothing wavering, believing that what is asked is yours.\n\n> 7. Do not tolerate for one single moment a thought to the contrary.\n\n> 8. Count the thing done that you have asked.\n\n> 9. Give glory to God even before you receive what you have asked.\n\n> 10. Act as if you have received what you have asked.\n\nRemember this: the moment you doubt, or wonder why God hasn't answered, or look around for some reason why He didn't hear your prayer, or begin accepting the delay as God's will for you not to have what you asked — you're defeated. You've failed to hold firm in unwavering faith for the answer.\n\nNever under any circumstance question God's will in the matter of answered prayer. If He has promised you can have whatsoever you ask if you don't doubt, then don't for a moment think it may be God's will to withhold what He promised and what you asked for in faith.\n\nIt's always God's will for you to ask and receive what you will. Don't make the mistake multiplied thousands are making — constantly wondering and wavering concerning God's will in anything He has promised. If you faithfully follow the steps listed above, settle on what God's will is according to His Word, and hold to it through all opposition, you cannot fail.\n\nIn the final analysis, the answer to your prayer depends on you more than on God. Why? Because God doesn't fail or change — there's no possible failure on His part. The only possible failure is on your part. It's similar to a power outlet: the electricity is always there, always available, always ready. The outlet never fails. If something doesn't work, you check the connection on your end.\n\nWe're so used to living by feelings instead of faith and being swayed by circumstances instead of counting things that are not as though they were. We don't always doggedly hold to the necessary steps in answered prayer. Make sure you don't fail, and then there won't be even a faint possibility of failure.\n\n## Some Mistaken Ideas Among Christians\n\nThe average Christian not only misses the mark by failing to understand the laws of answered prayer — or knowing them but not obeying them faithfully — but also holds to various fallacies that hinder prayer:\n\n**1. That if a person does the best he can, God will not permit harm to befall him.**\n\nSometimes the so-called "best one can do" isn't enough to please God or ward off demon attacks. Our performance can't always be adequate because we lack the knowledge to conform intelligently to God's will. People frequently think they're doing their best when they're really putting forth all the effort possible to live as they please and be as much like the world as they can while maintaining a little religion.\n\nIn other words, the great effort is holding on to as much religion as possible while living a life of pleasure and selfishness. The best a person can do — if they really want to do their best — is forsake all sin, selfishness, worldliness, and everything displeasing to God, giving themselves wholly to God's total will according to the Scriptures. Until this happens, you haven't done and aren't doing your best. Using "doing your best" as an excuse actually covers up failure to please God according to the light you've received.\n\nEven if you're fully consecrated, you shouldn't become careless by assuming you're eternally safe from all evil and harm. If you truly want to be kept from harm, you must watch and pray and live as taught in the 91st Psalm. You must bring your body under subjection and crucify the lusts of the flesh (1 Cor. 9:27; Rom. 6:1-23; 8:1-13; Col. 3:5-7; Eph. 4:22-24).\n\nIt's folly to talk about doing your best until you've done this and continue doing it. In that state, you can claim the full protection of God's promises. But the minute you go back into sin and rebellion, you can't claim that no evil shall befall you. "Whatsoever a man soweth, that shall he also reap" applies to the saved as well as the unsaved.\n\nThere's no place in God where you're forced to continue in righteousness contrary to your will. God cannot and will not keep one saved who continues in sin. That's one of the most ungodly and unscriptural theories Satan has manufactured. God works according to certain fixed laws, and when they're broken, penalties must be paid. Protection is promised only to those who fear the Lord (Ps. 34:7-10; 91:1-6).\n\n**2. Another fallacy is that God will not allow an honest heart to be deceived.**\n\nThis is itself a deception, based on ignorance of the fall of man and a misconception of how God and the devil work. God doesn't work except by spiritual and natural laws that must be understood and obeyed to receive His help.\n\nEve was as honest as could be, yet she was deceived (1 Tim. 2:14). Other honest people have been deceived through the ages. If God wouldn't permit honest people to be deceived, we wouldn't have so many backslidings, divisions, and differences in doctrines today.\n\n**3. Another false idea is that if a person is wrong in doctrine or manifestation, God won't use or bless them.**\n\nThe fact is that God blesses anyone to the extent of their faith and uses anyone to the extent of their yieldedness to Him. God has used sinners to further His plan on Earth, and they were certainly wrong in both doctrine and life.\n\nGod used Pharaoh in Egypt (Rom. 9), Cyrus in Persia (Isa. 44:28–45:1), Nebuchadnezzar in Babylon (Dan. 2), and many other sinners. So the theory that God's using someone proves they're saved and in perfect harmony with Him is wrong. God even used a donkey once, and we know it wasn't saved from its sins or correct in doctrine (Num. 22:22-35).\n\nMany good ministers today are used by God in saving souls, yet they're ignorant concerning certain truths. Don't be deceived by this fallacy.\n\n**4. Another fallacy is that God won't permit the devil to afflict a child of God with sickness without giving special permission, and therefore God is the cause of sickness and His children shouldn't rebel against any sickness He sends.**\n\nThe argument used to prove this is Job's affliction. However, remember that God was in the process of making His written Word and used Job's experience to teach all people of all ages certain truths — such as the cause of sickness, patience when attacked by Satan, and how God delivers His children from the devil's power.\n\nScores of truths have been learned from Job's experience through the ages. Naturally God permitted Job to go through this test to purify, humble, and refine him — just as God teaches everyone lessons in times of trouble. But despite this, God cannot be charged with causing this trouble or any other troubles.\n\nJob didn't have the many promises or revelations we have today. He didn't have one book of either Testament showing him God's ways or giving assurance of help in trouble. We have not only Job's book but the entire Bible teaching us that Satan causes sickness and God delivers His people.\n\nThe book of Job teaches why people are in trouble. This is clear from Job 33:14-30: "God speaketh once, yea twice, yet man perceiveth it not. In a dream, in a vision of the night . . . Then he openeth the ears of men, and sealeth their instruction, that he may withdraw man from his purpose, and hide pride from man." This passage continues explaining that man is brought low with sickness because of waywardness, and when he finds "an interpreter" to show him that God doesn't desire man to be lost or in pain, then God is gracious and delivers him from Satan.\n\nThis fallacy is partly true — God does permit people to be afflicted with bodily pain by Satan to hide pride from them. But this permission doesn't mean God causes the sickness. The foolishness, sin, and pride of man in breaking natural or spiritual laws is the cause. The devil is the direct power executing the law of reaping upon people the ills they've sown.\n\nGod permits affliction simply because He doesn't directly break His own law of sowing and reaping. He permits it by not acting to keep Satan from his work in people. It's not God's will for anyone to get into these troubles. If sin had never entered, all would have remained sinless and healthy. If all would submit to God, they could be made free from sin and sickness — this is the very truth God teaches in Job's story and throughout Scripture.\n\nThe trouble with true Christians who get sick is failure to understand they're directly attacked by the devil and need faith in God for healing. Almost every one has no consciousness of definite sin committed or rebellion against God's will. Such sickness is plainly a direct attack of Satan, and all that's needed is a vigorous refusal to stay sick and tolerate the devil's work.\n\nIf such people would quit blaming God and cooperate with Him against Satan through the gospel, they could experience deliverance. God's permission for them to be sick shouldn't enter their minds. The trouble isn't the idea of His permission — it's their will to be sick.\n\nIf you want to be healed, do what God tells you to do for deliverance. Rebuke Satan and take a stand against the devil's work. Yield to God and believe the gospel so God's work can be manifest. Jesus said, "The thief [Satan] cometh not, but for to steal, and to kill, and to destroy: I am come that they might have life and have it more abundantly" (Jn. 10:10).\n\nThere's no excuse for defeat for any child of God since Jesus came to give the more abundant life. Review Lessons Ten, Twelve, and Fourteen which prove that God is the deliverer of the sick, sinful, poor, and needy — not their oppressor. With all Scripture's truths written since Job lived, we can all get deliverance from Satan's power and live victoriously over sin, sickness, poverty, and failure.\n\n**5. Other misconceptions are seen everywhere in Christendom.**\n\nThe whole Christian world is full of false concepts of God and His truth, as we'll see in future studies. People in error of any kind are hindered in spiritual growth and often become autocratic, proud, and fanatical before others — unable to act rightly until some calamity strikes to disillusion them and dispel their false ideas.\n\nFrom the beginning of your Christian experience, recognize that demons are real and can deceive honest believers. Understand the methods by which demons work. Know that no place should be given them in life or conduct and that no doctrine should be accepted from them in any form.\n\n## The Source of All Christian Opposition\n\nDemons constantly wage war on saints to instill false concepts about God and make them weak, nervous, discouraged, and ruined spiritually, physically, and materially. They do their utmost to keep people in poverty and failure to bring reproach upon God and His gospel.\n\nThey must be resisted and rejected once and forever if complete victory is to be gained (Jas. 4:7; Eph. 4:10-15; 1 Pet. 5:8-9; 2 Cor. 10:4-7).\n\n## Christian Duties in View of Satanic Opposition\n\nIn coming lessons, we'll try to make the entire truth of God clear to you on every subject. We want to enable you to rightly divide the Word of Truth, detect demon workings, and confute all error.\n\nThe days ahead are rich in blessings according to the knowledge of God. "Ye shall know the truth, and the truth shall make you free" (Jn. 8:31-32).\n\nConstantly affirm your faith in God and vigorously deny all fallacy and demon workings — everything contrary to the plain written Word of God. You have a great part in making yourself free. Your part is knowing the truth and cooperating with it in Christ by the power of the Holy Spirit living in you by faith.\n\nGive no place to the devil. If opposition comes when you try to attain the gospel's blessings, don't be confused. Recognize it as the devil's work and as proof you're on the right path to God and His wonderful benefits. Don't fail God, and He won't fail you. Draw near to Him, and He will draw near to you (Jas. 4:6-11).\n\nIntelligently follow truth as you learn it, regardless of opposition, and you'll be richly rewarded — not only here but in all eternity.\n\nLet's all constantly affirm that we believe in God and the gospel of His Son, Jesus Christ; that we know we're saved from sin and bad habits; that we're healed through faith in Jesus Christ; that we have what we've asked of God; that we're being blessed in material things of this life; that our faith is increasing according to the knowledge of God; and that we're getting definite results through obedience to Truth.\n\nIf there's any lack of surrender of our lives to God, or any sin in our lives, it must be judged and put under the blood of Jesus Christ. This alone will hinder prayers, as absolute faith cannot be exercised when there's known rebellion in the heart. See to it that this is corrected. Without any question, believe and affirm truth from the heart, and faith will bring results.\n\n## Ten Questions For Supplement Nine. Do You Know:\n\n- **That the Antichrist will never be assassinated?** Many Bible teachers say the head on the beast in Rev. 13:3 being wounded to death and healed symbolizes the assassination of the personal Antichrist. They believe he'll remain dead for three days and that Satan will resurrect him and incarnate himself in him, imitating Christ's death, burial, and resurrection. For some unknown reason without scriptural authority, they make the Antichrist opposite to Christ in almost every detail. This isn't necessary to believe in the coming Antichrist — the word simply means "against Christ." There's no need to manufacture theories. Sufficient prophecy exists concerning him without all this. The head wounded to death simply refers to an empire's destruction and its revival as the eighth and last empire to oppose Jesus Christ at His second advent. The seven heads on the beast are explained as seven kingdoms: five had fallen before John's day; one was in his day; the seventh was to come after his day, made up of ten kingdoms inside the Roman Empire — but that was not the beast itself, which is explained to be "the eighth" of the eight kingdoms symbolized by the beast and its seven heads (Rev. 17:8-17; Dan. 7:23-24). If the heads symbolize kingdoms, not personal kings, then the head wounded to death doesn't refer to the Antichrist's assassination but to the destruction of one kingdom and its revival as the eighth. According to Dan. 7:11; Isa. 11:4; 2 Thess. 2:8-9, the Antichrist will be a mortal man up to the very day Christ comes to Earth, when he'll be killed. This disproves the theory of the Antichrist being killed and resurrected as an immortal man during the last 3½ years before Christ comes, as would be required if he were the head wounded to death and healed.\n\n- **That Antichrist will never reign from Rome or any great capital of the present world?** According to Isa. 13:6, 9-13; 14:1-11; Zech. 5:5-11; Rev. 16:17-21; 18:1-24 and other passages, the city of Babylon will be rebuilt and be the Antichrist's capital during the first 3½ years of Daniel's Seventieth Week. In the middle of that week, he'll break his covenant with the Jews, enter Palestine, and make Jerusalem his capital for most of the last half of the Week (Dan. 9:27; 11:40-45; 2 Thess. 2:3-4; Rev. 13). We've already proven in Supplement Eight, Questions 9 and 10, that the Antichrist will come from Syria. Therefore, his capital will be Syria's capital in the days of these prophecies' fulfillment — which will be Babylon, as proven in the above passages.\n\n- **That Antichrist will never be an incarnation of Satan?** This isn't taught anywhere in Scripture. Satan is a separate person from the Antichrist and merely gives his power to him (2 Thess. 2:7-12; Dan. 11:35-39; Rev. 13:1-4). The dragon (Satan), the beast out of the sea (Antichrist), and the beast out of the Earth (false prophet) are always seen as three separate persons throughout Revelation from when they're mentioned (Rev. 12:1-17; 13:1-18; 19:19-21; 20:1-10). Satan is never incarnated in anyone and doesn't need to be to dominate a person. Teaching that Antichrist is an incarnation of Satan is unscriptural. It would be impossible, for Satan — an angel with a real spirit-body — cannot get inside any man bodily. If he did, he couldn't exist as a separate person outside that body. Because he's always a separate person from any man who has lived or will live, it's best to forget such a theory.\n\n- **That the personal Antichrist will never come from the bottomless pit?** This is clear because he'll be a mortal man up to Christ's second coming, as proven in Point I above. No mortal man could live in the bottomless pit. It isn't even the place for departed human spirits, as is clear wherever it's mentioned in Scripture. The beast that ascends out of the bottomless pit is an angelic being and supernatural spirit working with the human Antichrist, causing him to gain power over the ten kingdoms yet to form inside the Roman Empire (Dan. 7:23-24; Rev. 17:8-17). According to Dan. 10:11-21, there are angelic beings ruling every kingdom of this world. Three things are symbolized in the one symbol — the beast of Revelation: first, the spirit of the Antichrist out of the abyss (Rev. 11:7; 17:8); second, the human Antichrist, a man (Rev. 13:18); third, the kingdom of the human Antichrist and the spirit out of the pit, the eighth kingdom of Rev. 17:8-17. It's the angelic spirit backing the Antichrist's rise to power that comes out of the pit, not the personal, human Antichrist. See Lesson Forty-seven.\n\n- **That Antichrist will not reign over America or be a worldwide dictator?** This is proven by these facts:\n\n> (1) He will reign over only ten kingdoms yet to form inside the old Roman Empire (Dan. 7:23-24). Only these ten kingdoms will give their power and kingdom to him (Rev. 13:1; 17:12-17).\n\n> (2) Certain countries will escape his rule (Dan. 11:40-44).\n\n> (3) Certain countries will make war on him at the very time he's supposed to be ruling the whole world (Dan. 11:40-44).\n\n> (4) Because he'll reign only over ten countries inside Roman Empire territory, because America was never inside that territory and never will be, and because certain countries escape him, we can scripturally conclude he won't rule America or be a worldwide ruler. See Lesson Forty-eight, Point III.\n\n- **That multitudes of people of many nations will never take the mark of the beast and they will never be killed by the Antichrist for not doing so?** It's been proven in the point above that many nations won't be under the Antichrist. Naturally they won't have to take his mark, and they won't be killed because they won't be ruled by him. The Bible speaks of many peoples of all nations left after the Antichrist's reign, people who'll be alive in the Millennium and go up yearly to worship God (Zech. 14:16-21). This further limits the Antichrist's kingdom to part of the world and limits his ability to kill everyone who doesn't take the mark even in his own empire. According to Rev. 14:9-11, no one who has taken the mark will go into the Millennium but will be sent to eternal Hell. That multitudes go into the Millennium proves they haven't taken the mark and haven't been killed, contrary to what many Bible students teach. If Antichrist kills every person on Earth who doesn't take his mark and the Lord sends to eternal Hell everyone who does take it, no one would be left to enter the Millennium. This would contradict Zech. 14:16-21; Mt. 25:31-46; Isa. 2:1-4 and many other Scriptures.\n\n- **That many nations of the Earth will be able to buy and sell and yet not be killed for not taking the mark of the beast?** The old theory that every person in every nation won't be able to buy or sell unless they take the mark is false, as proven by facts and Scriptures in the last two points above. The law that people won't be able to buy or sell applies only to kindreds, tongues, and nations inside the ten kingdoms yet to form inside the old Roman Empire. If the Antichrist reigns only over these countries, then all nations outside his ten kingdoms will be able to buy and sell and won't be affected by any law he makes in his kingdom.\n\n- **That multitudes of people living on the Earth will never even know that Antichrist has been on the Earth until he has come and gone?** It's a known fact that over half the people on Earth today have never heard the name of Jesus Christ or His gospel. It's also well-known that multitudes in interior Africa, South America, Australia, India, Tibet, and many parts of the Earth don't know there have been two world wars in a generation. It will equally be true that these people out of touch with so-called civilization will never know there's been an Antichrist and great tribulation until Christ has come and resurrected saints of all ages extend Christ's kingdom from Jerusalem to the ends of the Earth in the Millennium. It's utter folly to teach that a human being called the Antichrist ruling only ten kingdoms inside Roman Empire territory can take over every part of the Earth and literally kill everybody who doesn't take his mark. This would be teaching that one human being is going to do more in forty-two months or 1,260 literal days in establishing his religion in every person on Earth than God, Jesus Christ, the Holy Spirit, and the whole Church have done in over nineteen centuries to spread the gospel. Are we going to believe the Antichrist as a man is greater than God and all other men who've ever lived? The sensible thing to believe is that Antichrist will be a mere man limited in his rule to only part of the Earth and that he can't make laws affecting anyone outside his kingdom.\n\n- **That the mark of the beast is not 666?** Three brands will be available for people to choose from in the days of the Antichrist inside his kingdom: his name, his mark, and the number of his name (Rev. 13:16-18). The name and mark of this man are not given in any Scripture, so no one will know what they are until he comes and people learn his name and what mark he chooses for his followers. The only one of these three brands given is the number of his name — stated as 666 in Rev. 13:18. So 666 is not the name or the mark of the beast. It is the number of the beast.\n\n- **That at this time no man knows who the Antichrist will be?** This is true because he's coming "after" the ten kingdoms form inside the old Roman Empire territory (Dan. 7:23-24) and "after" the rapture of the Church, or "after" the one who hinders lawlessness has been removed from the Earth (2 Thess. 2:7-8).\n\n---\n\nYou are not under the Law — but you are under something: the law of Christ, the New Covenant, the indwelling Spirit who writes God's requirements on your heart. So here's the question: Are you relating to God through the framework of the Old Covenant — performance, guilt, and conditional acceptance — or through the New Covenant, where God says 'I will' rather than 'you must'? And what specific area of your spiritual life most clearly reveals which covenant you're actually living under?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk48j001cewhjbg5s6wc1	cmnknk48e000xewhj49ao5az0	Supplement 14 — For Lessons 27 & 28	SUPPLEMENT	1180	14	\N	t	The Trinity is three Persons in one God — Father, Son, and Spirit, each distinct, each fully divine. Faith is the instrument that accesses everything God has provided. This supplement brings those two truths together: you don't pray to an abstraction, you pray to a triune Person — and you approach Him in faith, not in vague hope.\n\nBased on everything we've studied so far, one truth stands out crystal clear: all of God's blessings come through faith. If you want to receive what you need in this life—and in the life to come—you must have faith and put it to work.\n\nNothing in Scripture is more obvious than this: faith is absolutely essential to receive the benefits of the gospel. And here's the flip side—you receive according to how much faith you have.\n\nThink of it like a faucet: the wider you open it, the more water flows. The law of faith works the same way: "nothing doubting . . . according to your faith, so be it done unto you . . . If thou canst believe, all things are possible" (Mt. 9:28-29; Mk. 1:15; 9:23; 11:22-24; 16:17; Jas. 1:5-8; Heb. 11:6; Rom. 12:6).\n\nIf you receive little, it's because you have little faith. If you receive much, you have much faith. If you waver, you get nothing.\n\nIf you believe for one thing but not another, you only get the one thing. You can always tell how much faith you have by what you receive.\n\nIf a person gives up in the battle and quits believing for what they've asked, it's not God's fault they don't receive. It's their own.\n\n![Supplement 14 — For Lessons 27 & 28](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsEUXLugtdLWqJHyu8Q5MfmIRAjonSBKDvC0V2)\n\n### II. God Never Rebukes Faith\n\nPeople have been rebuked for lack of faith, but never for having faith or for asking great things from God (Mt. 6:30; 14:31; 16:8; Lk. 24:25; Jas. 1:5-8).\n\nThere should never be any doubt about receiving from God if you'll simply see to it that you have faith without doubting or questioning. The answering part is God's job—so why should you, a small human being, worry so much about that?\n\nLeave all that worry to God. He's responsible for the answer, and He will answer if you exercise true faith.\n\nYour part is to ask, simply believe, and refuse to doubt after you've prayed. If you would take care of your part of the program, God would take care of His—and there would be no failure.\n\nWhen you start meddling with God's business—the answering part—that's when God lets you sweat. He lets you alone until you take care of your own part, and then the answer comes.\n\nIf the law of faith says that no answer is granted until all doubting, questioning, unbelief, and worrying cease, then let's settle this once and for all: God is not going to break His own law to please someone who spends more time criticizing God for not answering than praising Him for the answer.\n\nIf you want answers, stop worrying about how and when God is going to carry out His part. Let God run His own business in His own way and time.\n\nBe patient and faithful until He answers, and prayer will become a simple, natural part of your life.\n\n### III. How to Get Faith\n\nThere are several ways to increase your faith. If you truly want to believe God, take these steps:\n\n#### 1. Get into Christ\n\nThis is the first step of the Christian experience. When you're in Christ, you're a new creature—old things, including unbelief, have passed away, and all things have become new and are of God (2 Cor. 5:17).\n\nAnyone who has had a real, personal experience with God will remember that the beginning of the new life was marked by utter simplicity and childlikeness. In the matter of faith, the newly born-again believer was so confident and simple that they would believe God for anything.\n\nThere weren't all those arguments of unbelief or endless questioning about God's will. It was taken for granted that anything God promised as good was His will for every believer. Getting answers to prayer was easy—believing God for anything you wanted was natural.\n\nIt was only when questioning and reasoning about truth crept in that prayer started failing. That's when you began hearing things like: "God doesn't answer every prayer," "It's not His will to give everything He's promised," "He knows best what to give and what not to give," and "You shouldn't expect all prayers to be answered."\n\nAs long as you stayed simple and childlike—believing God was good enough to give His children anything and everything good—prayers were answered in such supernatural ways that you truly felt like a child of God.\n\nA simple faith is naturally born into every new child of God by the Spirit. Faith takes root immediately, and God is taken at His Word. There's no doubt at first that God will answer prayer.\n\nIf this confidence in God were nourished and developed normally as we grew in grace and knowledge, we would never have so many failures in prayer. But by listening to older believers who sometimes live in doubt and unbelief, new believers begin following their example—wavering and questioning God about everything.\n\nChrist is the author and finisher of faith in everyone, and a life of faith naturally begins when Christ is received (Heb. 12:1-2). As you receive Christ, you're to walk in Him (Col. 2:6-7). If you continued as you started, faith and zeal would never be dampened, and you'd never revert to a life of failure and constant questioning about God's will regarding His promises.\n\n#### 2. Know the Word of God\n\nThe second step to getting faith is to become thoroughly familiar with the Word of God. "Faith cometh by hearing, and hearing by the word of God" (Rom. 10:17; Acts 10:44; 15:7; 1 Cor. 1:18-21; Gal. 3:11; 2 Thess. 2:13).\n\nAfter getting into Christ, the next thing to do is start reading God's Word and meditating on it day and night. God promises that if you do this, you'll be like a tree planted by rivers of water, bringing forth fruit in its season—and whatever you do shall prosper (Ps. 1; Josh. 1).\n\nSo many people pray without ever thinking about what God says concerning what they're asking. If you asked the average person what promise of God they were depending on as the basis of their faith, they wouldn't have any particular Scripture in mind.\n\nNo wonder such people are seldom heard by God! Jesus said, "If ye abide in me, and my words abide in you, ye shall ask what ye will, and it shall be done unto you" (Jn. 15:7).\n\nThe Word of God and prayer must go together if you expect definite results. There is no ground for answered prayer outside of God's Word.\n\nThe Bible reveals God's will in all things and promises certain benefits through Christ and faith. No one can normally get prayers answered who doesn't know the truth.\n\n#### 3. Do Not Pray for Faith in God and His Word\n\nHere's something that might surprise you: praying for faith is actually a prayer of unbelief. Consequently, there can't be an answer to it.\n\nPraying for faith is simply an attempt to evade trusting God's Word and get things from God contrary to the way He's set things up. Only the Word can give you the basis for answered prayer.\n\nIt's similar to having a bank account but praying for the bank to give you money instead of simply writing a check. The provision is already there—you just need to use it!\n\nSimple faith in God's Word may not seem sufficient or even sensible to the natural person who lives by their senses and always wants to see and feel everything. But this kind of confidence is necessary if you want what God's promises offer.\n\nWe're not told to pray for faith—we're told to "have faith in God" (Mk. 11:22-24).\n\n#### 4. Have Faith in God\n\nIf people would learn to put confidence in God the way they do in other people—if they would have unwavering faith in His promises the way they trust human words—answered prayer would be a great success.\n\nPrayers would be answered without so much effort. Prayer would be a simple, everyday transaction between God and man.\n\nIt's sad to say, but people will trust other humans and put their whole confidence in them more readily than they trust God and His Word. At the same time, they claim they believe God more than any man!\n\nThey would never boldly declare that they don't have confidence in God or that they don't believe He's faithful to His Word—but their actions prove otherwise.\n\nIf somebody tells them, "Maybe it's not God's will to answer," or "If He doesn't answer, He knows best," or some other excuse for unbelief, it's generally accepted as true. Seldom do such people go to the Bible to see exactly what it says and intelligently follow it.\n\nEven if they find that they're definitely promised the answer, the average person questions God's will with some unreasonable argument of unbelief.\n\nSuch people would never be turned aside so easily if another human had made those promises! They would go to court or fight to their last breath to hold someone to their obligations.\n\nBut they're so full of unbelief that they don't want to "bother" God with the same problems. They won't put forth the least fight against demonic powers and unbelief to get what God has promised or to hold God to His obligations.\n\nIs it any wonder God ignores such unbelieving, disinterested requests for things He's promised? We would do the same!\n\nIn fact, we wouldn't be as tolerant of people who reject us and our word and constantly fail to believe us as God is toward failing, unbelieving humanity. We would be so disgusted with such half-heartedness that we'd withdraw all offers of help.\n\nBut despite our failures, God goes on year after year, seeking to prove that He means what He says and says what He means. He's constantly trying to show us that He's faithful to His Word and won't go contrary to it for anyone.\n\nHe will meet His own obligations—but on the grounds of His own revealed terms: faith, nothing wavering. We are to blame for all failure; so the quicker we realize the trouble and correct it, the sooner we'll get what we want from God.\n\n#### 5. Listen to Good Preaching\n\nWhen we constantly hear stories of the triumphs of faith in the Bible—when we read of miracles like God dividing the waters, raining manna from Heaven, sending quails, giving water from rocks, multiplying food, quenching fire, raising the dead, healing the sick, defeating His enemies, and causing hundreds of other miraculous events written for our instruction and to build up our faith—our faith runs high!\n\nWe fully realize that what God has done, He can and will do again in meeting His obligations to give us what He's promised. Our faith naturally becomes powerful and expectant.\n\nPersonal experiences of believers in all ages are also a great source for building faith. Accept and believe any record of God's dealing with anyone if it's in harmony with His Word.\n\n#### 6. Reject All Bad Preaching\n\nWhen we go to churches where ministers question these miracles and make fun of them as mere fairy tales—trying to explain away such facts with high-sounding, clever, pseudo-scientific generalizations—our faith is hindered.\n\nWe must be consecrated to renounce all such foolish theories as from the devil and cleave to the plain Word of God in all things. No one can expect to build up faith and get to the place where benefits can be received while spending time in places where God's Word is criticized.\n\nIf you want simple, unwavering faith, you cannot listen to this kind of preaching and come out without questioning some things.\n\nYes, all faith-killing, Spirit-quenching, truth-destroying, man-made traditions, sects, and creeds—however clever they may be in doing away with simple gospel truths—must be rejected and renounced once and for all.\n\nDon't tolerate any person or church that slanders God or His Word, which is the source of your faith and eternal life.\n\n#### 7. Obey the Word of God\n\nObey God's Word to the letter, regardless of how foolish it may appear. God's ways are not our ways, and His thoughts are not our thoughts. His ways are as high above ours as Heaven is above Earth.\n\nAccept His Word as the right thing on every question and obey it to the letter—and it will be proven right. It will be proven true in every respect.\n\nThe test of this age is "the obedience to the faith among all nations," as we've seen in Lessons Nineteen, Point IV. Those who fail in obedience to the faith will be rejected by God in the end and consigned to eternal Hell for their failure.\n\nThus it's critically important that we conform to the Word and obey to the letter what is plainly written.\n\n#### 8. Exercise Your Faith\n\nBy exercising your faith, you'll cause it to grow (Rom. 1:17). One of the best secrets to increasing faith is simply to begin using what faith you have.\n\nPicture it this way: faith is like a muscle—what you don't use, you lose. It's a divine law: "Whoever hath, to him shall be given; and whosoever hath not, from him shall be taken even that which he seemeth to have" (Lk. 8:18).\n\nJust as any muscle that remains unused becomes weak and lifeless, so it is with faith. We must use what we have if we want it to become strong and dynamic.\n\nIt's not only a natural law that faith increases when used in all life's problems, but God will supernaturally see to it that it's increased. He will impart more faith—the divine kind—to those who properly exercise human faith.\n\nOur faith must work by love; that is, we must exercise our faith for the good of all people, whether friend or foe (Gal. 5:6). Unless we have fervent love for all, our faith can never reach its highest peak of efficiency. Where there is love, there is faith, for both are fruit of the Spirit (Gal. 5:22-23).\n\nWhere love, unity, peace, and true Christian fellowship prevail in a church, there will be little sickness and defeat, and few failures in prayer. Where malice, jealousy, gossip, and constant division and strife exist, there will be much more sickness and failure in prayer. Experience, observation, and Scripture all establish this fact.\n\nThe Fall would not have been possible without doubt and unbelief in God and His Word. God demands that we learn He is faithful to His Word and means exactly what He says—before He redeems us and before He answers prayer.\n\nGod must demand faith and freedom from doubt in order to redeem us from the Fall. (See Lesson Nine for the steps leading to the fall of man.) These steps must be retraced in our redemption and complete reconciliation to God.\n\nWe must learn the lesson of faith and absolute confidence in God sooner or later if we expect to live with God forever. There's no better time than while we're on probation to learn this lesson.\n\nThis is why God requires faith in all that He says before He obligates Himself to answer us definitely. At first, there's naturally a struggle to trust God whom we cannot see. But after we learn to do this, the hard part of faith is over, and we enter into a place with God where we get things easily—by simply asking and receiving through faith in God and His Word.\n\n#### 9. Yield to the Holy Spirit\n\nFaith is not only a natural ability or exercise of a created faculty—it's also a fruit of the Spirit and a gift from God (Gal. 5:22-23; 1 Cor. 12:4-11).\n\nWhen we use our own powers of faith in God and His Word properly, we make contact with God in a supernatural way and become partakers of the divine nature (2 Pet. 1:3-4).\n\nWe're then free to walk and live in the Spirit, and the fruit of the Spirit will naturally be manifest in our lives (Gal. 5:16-26). We should constantly add to our faith the virtues of God by yielding to Him in daily life (2 Pet. 1:4-10).\n\nThis kind of faith becomes a natural fruit of the Spirit and of holy living. It's not an effort to have this faith—it grows in the life of one who yields to God and loves God enough to draw near to Him in daily conflict.\n\nIt is a fruit, not a work. It is a gift, not something we earn (Rom. 12:3; 2:4-5; 12:4-11; Gal. 5:22-23).\n\nHuman faith in union with the divine will make all things possible to the believer. Let us all have faith in God, in Christ, in the Holy Spirit, in the Word of God, in the atonement, and in all the gospel truths.\n\nHave faith in God and look to Him for needed daily grace for body, soul, and spirit. The more we love God and conform to His Word, the more faith will grow.\n\nThis faith will grow until it rests solely in the Infinite. In this place with God, we can reach up and pull down the unlimited blessings of God for ourselves and others.\n\nRemember this: the secret of faith is faith in God.\n\n---\n\n## Ten Questions For Supplement Fourteen. Do You Know:\n\n- That Christ was not the only one that multiplied food for men? Elisha multiplied food and fed one hundred men miraculously (2 Ki. 4:42-44). Elijah also multiplied the widow's meal daily for "many days" (1 Ki. 17:8-16).\n\n- That Russia will not be the head of the nations at Armageddon? Instead, Russia will be conquered by the Antichrist and will be under him at Armageddon. Antichrist will come from Syria and not Russia, as we have already seen in Questions 9 and 10, Supplement Eight. According to Dan. 11:44 he conquers all the northern and eastern nations outside of the Roman Empire Territory, and he will then be ruler of these countries. Ezekiel 38-39 pictures his coming down from the north at Armageddon after having conquered these countries. Zechariah 14; Rev. 19:11-21 predicts his defeat at the second coming of Christ. So Syria, not Russia, will be the head of the nations at Armageddon.\n\n- That Russia will be defeated three times between our day and the Millennium? This is clear from the fact that ten kingdoms will be formed inside the Roman Empire before Antichrist comes (Dan. 7:23-24; Supplement Eight, Question 5). Because Russia dominates Romania, Bulgaria, Hungary, Austria, Albania, and other Roman Empire states, they must be liberated from Russia before the ten kingdoms can be formed. This will require one defeat of Russia. Another defeat will occur when Antichrist heads these ten kingdoms and conquers Russia during the last 3½ years of this age, as seen in the question above. The third defeat of Russia will be by Christ at Armageddon (Ezek. 38; 39; Zech. 14:1-5; Rev. 19:11-21).\n\n- That men will not spend eternity in Heaven? According to Rev. 21-22 the New Jerusalem, the home of the saints, will be moved from Heaven to the Earth after the Millennium. This proves men will spend eternity on Earth instead of Heaven.\n\n- That God eats and wears clothes as we do? This is clear from Gen. 18; Exod. 24:11; Dan. 7:9-14; Isa. 6; Rev. 4-5.\n\n- That angels also eat food and wear clothes? This is clear from Gen. 18-19; Ps. 78:25; Dan. 10:5-21.\n\n- That Babylon as a literal city will be rebuilt before the second coming of Christ? Babylon will be destroyed as a city under the seventh vial (Rev. 16:17-21; 18:1-24) and in the day of the Lord (Isa. 13:6, 9; 14:3-4); so it must be a city in that day (See Chapter Thirty-seven of Revelation Expounded).\n\n- That there will be sin and death on Earth during the Millennium? This is plainly stated in Isa. 65:20-25; 1 Cor. 15:24-28.\n\n- That God has already sent some of His sons to Hell? Angels are called sons of God in Job 38:7. Many of them are in Hell now (2 Pet. 2:4; Jude 6-7).\n\n- That the devil never enters bodily into anyone? The word "in" means "in union with" and not "bodily entrance into," as we have seen in Lesson Four, Point 1, 4. This is the only sense in which he enters into anyone because he has a body of his own and he cannot get bodily into any other body (1 Chron. 21:1; Job 1:6-12; 2:1-7; Rev. 12:7-12; Zech. 3:1-3; Mt. 4:1-11; Ezek. 28:11-17; Rev. 20:1-10).\n\n---\n\nFaith directed at the triune God is the most powerful force available to a human being — because it connects your need to the infinite resources of the One who created all things. So here's the question: In your prayer life, do you actually engage with all three Persons of the Trinity, or do you relate primarily to one while the others remain theoretical? And is your prayer characterized by genuine faith — the 'substance of things hoped for' — or by a hoping-it-might-work attitude?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk4bq0026ewhj6qlo86gp	cmnknk4bp001qewhjk5bcqnfj	Lesson 47 — The Beast with Seven Heads and Ten Horns	READING	4321	15	\N	t	Revelation 17 introduces a beast with seven heads and ten horns — one of the most discussed images in all of prophetic literature. Commentators across centuries have identified the seven heads as Roman emperors, medieval popes, modern political leaders, and world empires. Many of these identifications have come and gone. But the angel who interprets the vision tells John exactly what each symbol means — and those definitions don't depend on the interpreter's historical moment.\n\nLast time we examined the two beasts of Revelation 13 — the Antichrist and the False Prophet. Now we go deeper into the political and religious system that supports them: the beast with seven heads and ten horns, and what it reveals about the final world empire.\n\nThe Greek word *therion*, translated "beast," appears thirty-seven times in Revelation in connection with this symbol. It refers to a wild, untamed, dangerous animal. Think of it like this: if you were choosing an image to represent the hostile powers that will stand against Christ in the last days, a ferocious predator would be the perfect choice.\n\n![Lesson 47 — The Beast with Seven Heads and Ten Horns](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsFMAtSG0bM1hRYQ2o4Z38BzgHNSP5KdLckVeF)\n\n## I. Sixty Fundamental and Prophetic Facts\n\nRevelation gives us sixty historical and prophetic facts about the beast. These serve as our foundation — without them, we can't truly understand the future beast with its seven heads and ten horns.\n\nAny interpretation of this subject must line up with these facts, or it's built on shaky ground. The historical details help us grasp the prophetic ones, showing how the last Gentile kingdom will be reconstructed during "the times of the Gentiles" under the Antichrist's direction.\n\nHere's the key purpose of this vision: it's not meant to teach us history for history's sake. It's meant to help us identify the eighth and final kingdom that will form after the rapture.\n\nWe shouldn't interpret anything in Rev. 4-22 as already fulfilled in past history — all these events are still future, coming after the rapture, as we established in Lesson Forty-one. But here's what's perfectly legitimate: prophecy can point to a future event while also giving us a look back at what led up to it.\n\nSo to help us properly identify the eighth kingdom of the future, Scripture mentions the seven kingdoms that precede it. This shows us that the final kingdom will reign over all the peoples and territories of the ones that came before.\n\nHere are the sixty factual statements about the beast found in Rev. 11:7–20:10:\n\n> 1. The beast is to come out of the bottomless pit (Rev. 11:7; 17:8).\n\n> 2. He will make war on the two witnesses during the last part of the Week (Rev. 11:7).\n\n> 3. He will finally overcome and kill the two witnesses (Rev. 11:7).\n\n> 4. He will come from the sea of humanity (Rev. 13:1; 17:1, 3, 11, 15).\n\n> 5. He has seven heads and names of blasphemy written on them (Rev. 13:1; 17:3, 7-11).\n\n> 6. He has ten horns and on them ten crowns (Rev. 13:1. Compare Rev. 12:3; 17:12-17; Dan. 7:7, 8, 19-25).\n\n> 7. He is like a leopard, a symbol of Greece (Rev. 13:2; 17:3. Compare Dan. 7:6).\n\n> 8. He has the feet of a bear, a symbol of Medo-Persia (Rev. 13:2. Compare Dan. 7:5).\n\n> 9. He has the mouth of a lion, a symbol of Babylon (Rev. 13:2. Compare Dan. 7:4).\n\n> 10. The dragon gives him his power, his seat (throne), and great authority (Rev. 13:2. Compare Dan. 8:24; 2 Thess. 2:7-12).\n\n> 11. One of the beast's heads was wounded to death and it will be healed (Rev. 13:3, 12, 14).\n\n> 12. All the world will wonder after him (Rev. 13:3; 17:8).\n\n> 13. Many will worship the dragon, which will give power to the beast. Also they will worship the beast, saying, "Who is like unto the beast? Who is able to make war with him?" (Rev. 13:4, 8, 16-18; 14:9-11; 15:2-4; 20:4-6).\n\n> 14. The beast will be given a mouth to speak great things and blasphemies (Rev. 13:5. Compare this passage with Dan. 7:8, 11, 20; 11:36).\n\n> 15. He will be given power for forty-two months (Rev. 13:5).\n\n> 16. He will blaspheme God, His name, His tabernacle, and those that dwell in Heaven (Rev. 13:6. Compare this passage with Dan. 7:8, 11, 20; 11:36).\n\n> 17. He will make war on the saints (Rev. 13:7. Compare this passage with Dan. 7:19-22).\n\n> 18. He will overcome the saints and kill them (Rev. 13:7; 14:9-11; 15:2-4; 20:4-6).\n\n> 19. He will be given power over all the people of the ten kingdoms (Rev. 13:1, 7; 17:12-17. Compare these passages with Dan. 7:23, 24).\n\n> 20. Saints are commanded to have patience and faith and not to repay him evil for evil (Rev. 13:10).\n\n> 21. He will have a co-worker and religious leader in the false prophet who will exercise his power and cause men to worship him (Rev. 13:11-18; 16:13-16; 19:20; 20:10).\n\n> 22. Miracles will be done in his sight to get the world to worship him (Rev. 13:12-14).\n\n> 23. An image will be made of him to be worshiped (Rev. 13:14, 15).\n\n> 24. His image will be given life to speak and cause men to be killed if they refuse to worship the beast and his image (Rev. 13:15).\n\n> 25. He will have a name, which at present is not known (Rev. 13:17, 18; 15:2).\n\n> 26. He will have a mark, which at present is not known (Rev. 13:17; 14:9; 15:2; 16:2; 20:4-6).\n\n> 27. His number, not his mark or his name, is 666 (Rev. 13:16-18; 15:2).\n\n> 28. These three things — his mark, his name, or the number of his name — will be forced upon men who must take either one of three brands in the right hand or forehead, or they will be boycotted and killed (Rev. 13:16-18; 14:9-11; 15:2-4; 20:4-6).\n\n> 29. The beast is a man (Rev. 13:18; 16:13-16; 19:19-21; 20:10. Compare these passages with Dan. 7:7, 8, 19-27; 8:9, 20-25; 9:26, 27; 11:36-45; Isa. 11:4; 2 Thess. 2:1-12; Jn. 5:43).\n\n> 30. Men will be warned by literal angels flying in mid-heaven not to take his mark, his name, or his number, or they will suffer eternal Hell (Rev. 14:9-11).\n\n> 31. His worship will be so apostate in nature that all who worship him or take any one of his three brands will be damned in Hell forever (Rev. 14:9-11).\n\n> 32. Many will win the victory over his worship and brands (Rev. 15:2-4).\n\n> 33. His followers will partake of the vial plagues (Rev. 16:2, 10).\n\n> 34. He has a throne and a kingdom (Rev. 16:10).\n\n> 35. He will be in league with, and be possessed by demon powers, to gather the nations at Armageddon who are not under him (Rev. 16:13-16).\n\n> 36. He carries the woman or great whore (Rev. 17:3, 7).\n\n> 37. He "was" or had been on the Earth before John's day (Rev. 17:8).\n\n> 38. He "is not." That is, he was not on the Earth, but was in the bottomless pit during John's day (Rev. 17:8).\n\n> 39. He "shall ascend out of the bottomless pit" and live on Earth again to revive the Grecian Empire and rule for forty-two months (Rev. 17:8).\n\n> 40. He will then "go into perdition," or be destroyed at the second coming (Rev. 17:8).\n\n> 41. "The seven heads" are seven mountains on which the woman sitteth. And "there are seven kings" or kingdoms (Rev. 17:9).\n\n> 42. "Five are fallen." That is, the first five of these seven kingdoms had passed away before John saw the Revelation (Rev. 17:10). They are Egypt, Assyria, Babylon, Medo-Persia, and Greece.\n\n> 43. "One is." That is, the sixth of these seven kingdoms, or the old Roman Empire, was in existence in John's day (Rev. 17:10).\n\n> 44. "The other is not yet come; and when he cometh he must continue a short space." That is, the seventh of these seven kingdoms which will be made up of the ten kingdoms inside the old Roman Empire has not yet come. When such ten kingdoms are formed they will continue a short time before the "little horn" will arise from among them to get power over them (Rev. 17:10. Compare this passage with Dan. 7:7, 8, 19-27).\n\n> 45. "The beast that was, and is not, even he is the eighth." That is, when Antichrist comes out of the ten kingdoms he will get power over them by the middle of the Week and he will form the eighth kingdom, which continues forty-two months (Rev. 17:11; 13:5).\n\n> 46. He "is of the seven and goeth into perdition." That is, he is one of the seven, not necessarily the seventh (Rev. 17:11). He must be one of the first five that had fallen before John, either of Egypt, Assyria, Babylon, Medo-Persia, or Greece, for the beast "was," or had been before the sixth that was in existence in John's day, and he becomes the eighth kingdom following the seventh, which was to come between the sixth and the eighth. It is the Grecian Empire revived as the eighth kingdom that will fight against Christ at Armageddon and be destroyed (Zech. 9:13; Dan. 8:23-25; Rev. 19:19-21).\n\n> 47. The ten horns "are ten kings" inside the Roman Empire (Rev. 17:12. Compare this passage with Dan. 7:23, 24).\n\n> 48. They "have received no kingdom as yet" (Rev. 17:12. Compare this passage with Dan. 7:23, 24).\n\n> 49. They "receive power as kings one hour (Greek, *hora*, 'hour' or 'period') with the beast" (Rev. 17:12. Compare this passage with Rev. 13:5).\n\n> 50. "These have one mind, and shall give their power and strength unto the beast" (Rev. 17:13, 16, 17. See Lesson Forty-seven, Point III).\n\n> 51. "These shall make war on the Lamb, and the Lamb shall overcome them" at the second advent, so the ten kings must all be in existence at that time (Rev. 17:14; 19:19-21. Compare these passages with Dan. 2:44; 7:23-24; 8:20-25; 11:45–12:7).\n\n> 52. "These shall hate the whore, and shall make her desolate and naked, and shall eat her flesh, and burn her with fire" (Rev. 17:16).\n\n> 53. "For God hath put in their hearts to fulfill his will, and to agree, and give their kingdom unto the beast, until the words of God shall be fulfilled" (Rev. 17:17).\n\n> 54. The beast is the commander-in-chief of all armies of the kings of the Earth at Armageddon (Rev. 19:19. Compare this passage with Ezek. 38; 39).\n\n> 55. He mobilizes the armies of the nations to fight against Christ and the armies of Heaven at the second coming (Rev. 19:19. Compare this passage with Zech. 14:1-5; Rev. 16:13-16; Ezek. 38; 39).\n\n> 56. The beast will be slain by Christ at His coming, thus proving him to be a mortal man at that time (Rev. 19:20. See also Dan. 7:11; Isa. 11:4; 2 Thess. 2:8, 9).\n\n> 57. He, and the false prophet, will both be cast bodily into the Lake of Fire (Rev. 19:20).\n\n> 58. The beast's armies will be destroyed by Christ and His armies (Rev. 19:20, 21; 17:14. Compare these passages with Ezek. 38:17-21; 2 Thess. 1:7-10; Joel 3).\n\n> 59. Those whom he kills for not worshipping him will be resurrected in the first resurrection and will reign with Christ (Rev. 20:4-6).\n\n> 60. The beast and the false prophet will be in the Lake of Fire for 1,000 years before the devil is cast into that lake, and all will "be tormented day and night for ever and ever" (Rev. 20:10; 14:9-11; Isa. 66:22-24; Mt. 25:41, 46).\n\nNow here's what all these statements make clear: the beast isn't just one thing — it's a symbol representing several different realities. The heads, horns, feet, body, mouth, the wounding of one head — each of these symbolizes different truths.\n\nThe beast itself represents something entirely different from the seven heads and ten horns that sit on it. If we fail to make this distinction, we'll end up with a confused understanding.\n\nHere's the key: what the seven heads and ten horns represent will already have come to pass when the beast appears. They're already on the beast when he rises. They show us the succession of seven world kingdoms throughout Israel's history before the eighth kingdom arrives.\n\n## II. The Beast Symbolizes Three Things\n\n> 1. **A human being** — the Antichrist, or beast out of the sea, which we covered in Lesson Forty-six. Facts like Points 4, 10, 12-35, and 49-60 above prove that a human being is part of this symbol. He's explicitly called "a man," so we can't understand the beast without recognizing this. We studied the human aspect in the previous lesson.\n\n> 2. **A supernatural being** — the beast out of the Abyss, which we'll explore in the following sections. Points 1-3, 35, and 37-40 prove that a satanic prince is also part of this symbol.\n\n> 3. **An empire** — the eighth kingdom, which immediately follows the seven heads or kingdoms. Points 5-9, 11, 36-38, and 41-46 prove that a kingdom is also symbolized by the beast.\n\nHere's where some interpreters have gone wrong. Some see only a kingdom and miss the human or supernatural person. Others see only a human being. Still others recognize an earthly king and kingdom but miss the satanic powers controlling it.\n\nAll these errors have led people to ignore some of the plain facts we just listed. But when you see all three things in the one symbol — and distinguish which passages clearly refer to which — everything becomes clear.\n\nThe key is to take each passage and apply it to whichever aspect makes the reading most sensible. Some passages apply to all three things; others apply to just one or two.\n\nSo the beast out of the Abyss, the beast out of the sea, and the beast as the eighth kingdom are all different things, but all three are present in the one symbol. We've already studied the beast out of the sea (the human aspect), so now let's turn to the beast out of the Abyss — the satanic aspect of the symbol.\n\n## III. The Beast Out of the Abyss (Rev. 11:7; 17:8)\n\nThere have been many theories about who or what this beast from the Abyss might be. Some believe it's a human spirit currently confined there who will come out and exist again as the Antichrist. Others think it represents the revival of a kingdom that once existed and will exist again.\n\nBut Scripture suggests something more than a mere kingdom revival. Think about it: how could a kingdom be confined to the Abyss for centuries, come out again as a kingdom, and then go into destruction a second time? That would require all the original subjects of that kingdom to live again on Earth — and Scripture doesn't teach that (Heb. 9:27).\n\nOnly a person can be described as "coming out of the Abyss." If that's true, then the beast's identity must be either a human or a supernatural spirit who once existed on Earth, is currently confined in the Abyss, and will exist again on Earth in the last days.\n\nYou can find about as many candidates for the future Antichrist as there are interpreters! Among them are Nimrod, various Egyptian, Assyrian, and Babylonian kings, Alexander the Great, Antiochus Epiphanes, Julius Caesar, Nero, Judas Iscariot, Mohammed, various popes of Rome, Napoleon, and more.\n\nHere's what this school of interpreters believes: when this human spirit (whoever he is) gets reincarnated as the Antichrist, he'll grow to maturity. In the middle of the Week, an assassin will kill him. His body will lie in state for three days, then be raised from the dead. At that point, Satan will incarnate himself in this man, who becomes an immortal being. They claim this will be Satan's imitation of Christ's resurrection and proves that Antichrist won't be an ordinary mortal during the last 3½ years — which would explain why he gets cast "alive" into the Lake of Fire.\n\nHowever, here's the problem: none of this can be proven from Scripture. We can't prove that any of these historical figures will be the beast out of the Abyss, nor can we prove that any of them will be the Antichrist.\n\nNeither the beast out of the Abyss nor the Antichrist will be a reincarnated human being — or any other kind of reincarnated being. Let me show you why the beast of the Abyss and the Antichrist are actually two different persons. If we can establish this, then all the reincarnation arguments collapse, and there's no basis for believing the Antichrist will be assassinated and resurrected as an immortal being.\n\nHere's the evidence:\n\n> 1. We've already established from the sixty scriptural statements that the beast symbol represents three things: first, a human being (the beast out of the sea of humanity); second, a supernatural being (the beast out of the Abyss); third, an empire (the eighth kingdom composed of many peoples, Rev. 17:1, 3, 7, 9-11, 15). According to Scripture, then, the beast out of the Abyss and the Antichrist are not the same person.\n\n> 2. The Antichrist will be an ordinary mortal throughout his life until the Battle of Armageddon, when Christ will kill him at His coming. Look at these passages: "He [Christ] shall smite the earth with the rod of His mouth, and with the breath of His lips shall He slay the wicked" (Isa. 11:4). "I beheld even until the beast was slain, and his body destroyed, and given to the burning flame" (Dan. 7:11; Rev. 19:20). "He [Antichrist] shall be broken [slain, as in Dan. 8:8, 22; 11:4, 22] without hand" (Dan. 8:25). "And then shall that wicked [Antichrist] be revealed, whom the Lord shall consume [Greek, *analisko*, 'to slay, remove'] with the spirit of his mouth, and shall destroy [Greek, *katargeo*, 'to be useless, destroy'] with the brightness of his coming" (2 Thess. 2:8). Antichrist must be mortal to fulfill these statements. He's expressly called "a man" in Rev. 13:14-18 and 2 Thess. 2:3, 4. He can't be mortal and also come out of the Abyss without being reincarnated or resurrected to immortality.\n\n> 3. Reincarnation isn't taught in Scripture. You certainly can't build such a doctrine on the mere fact that a spirit comes out of the Abyss. To be reincarnated, you'd have to go through rebirth and growth to maturity all over again. This would make Antichrist a baby at least twice! And if he's also killed by an assassin in the middle of the Week and then slain by Christ, he'd have to die at least three times. This contradicts Scripture: "it is appointed unto men once to die, but after this the judgment" (Heb. 9:27). This verse doesn't teach that after death there can be reincarnation and another death. The only exceptions to dying once will be those translated without seeing death at the rapture and at the catching up of the manchild (1 Cor. 15:51-58; 1 Thess. 4:16-17; Rev. 12:5). The Bible does teach resurrection after death, at which time each person will be raised full-grown and have an immortal body that cannot die (1 Cor. 15:20-23, 34-51; Jn. 5:28, 29). There's simply no room for a doctrine where a mature human spirit becomes a baby again, is born in flesh, grows to maturity a second time, and dies again. And if Antichrist became a baby and grew to maturity in a second life, what would be the purpose? Any baby could grow up and fulfill the same things. If this one person can be reincarnated, why not others? And if we accept reincarnation, we shouldn't criticize Hindus who believe the same thing! The logical conclusion is that Antichrist is simply a man born on Earth in the last days for the first time, and he won't be the beast out of the Abyss — who, as we'll see, is a supernatural spirit.\n\n> 4. Scripture makes clear that the Abyss is not where departed human spirits go — therefore, a human spirit cannot come out of it. The Abyss is the prison for demon spirits (Lk. 8:31; Rev. 9:1-21). It's also where certain satanic princes are currently confined (Rev. 9:11; 11:7; 17:8). We don't know how many other angels are there. It will be Satan's prison for a thousand years (Rev. 20:1-10). Based on these Scriptures, the Abyss is reserved for Satan and his demon and angelic followers — never for human spirits. Human spirits went into Paradise and Hell before Christ's resurrection (Lk. 16:19-31; Ps. 16:10; Lk. 23:43; Eph. 4:7-11; Mt. 12:40; Rev. 20:11-15). When Christ rose, He led all righteous souls out of Paradise under the Earth and took them to Heaven with Him. So now when a Christian dies, they go to be "with Christ" until the first resurrection (Eph. 4:7-11; Heb. 2:14; Phil. 1:21; 2 Cor. 5:8; Rev. 6:9-11). Wicked human spirits still go to Hell as before — Judas included. No human spirit is in the Abyss to come out as the beast, so we must identify the beast of the Abyss as a supernatural spirit. This proves the human Antichrist is not the same as the beast of the Abyss. (See the Chart and Lesson Thirty-seven for more on the Abyss and the place of the dead.)\n\n> 5. There's no Scripture proving that Antichrist will be different from any other natural man regarding his being, birth, or death. He'll simply be used by satanic powers in the last days. Neither God nor Satan needs to bring back a dead man to fulfill prophecy. If some historical man could fulfill the requirements, why couldn't a future man do so just as well? Reincarnation has never been necessary for supernatural powers to operate in the world, and it won't be in the future either. The only requirement for receiving God's or Satan's power is complete submission to the operator's will.\n\n> 6. The idea that Antichrist will be assassinated in the middle of the Week and rise from the dead as an immortal, Satan-incarnate being is unscriptural. The wounding of one of the heads and its healing doesn't prove this. Here's the crucial detail some interpreters have missed: it was one of the seven heads on the beast that was wounded to death — not the beast itself. The angel explained to John that the seven heads were seven kingdoms, and all of them precede the beast itself, which is the eighth kingdom ruled by Antichrist and the satanic prince of the Abyss (Rev. 17:9-11). So the Antichrist, king of the eighth kingdom, won't be assassinated at all, and therefore can't be resurrected in the middle of the Week. And Antichrist will never be an incarnation of Satan — we see the dragon (Satan) as a separate being from Antichrist throughout the entire period he's supposedly incarnated in him (2 Thess. 2:8, 9; Rev. 13:1-4; 16:13-16; 20:10). Satan can't be incarnate in Antichrist while also existing as a separate being outside of him.\n\n## IV. Identification of the Beast Out of the Abyss\n\nWe firmly believe the beast out of the Abyss represents a mighty supernatural satanic prince — not a human spirit. We don't believe this spirit will be incarnated in any human body to become the Antichrist. Instead, he'll be the ruling prince under Satan's guidance, dominating the Antichrist and exalting him as the earthly king over the eighth and last kingdom.\n\nHere's what we believe about this being's history and future:\n\n- He ruled one of the first kingdoms that had fallen before John's day (represented by the first five heads on the beast)\n- When his kingdom fell, he was cast into the Abyss and confined there during the sixth kingdom (the Roman Empire in John's day)\n- He'll remain confined until the formation of the ten kingdoms (the seventh kingdom)\n- He'll be released from the Abyss during the seventh, cause Antichrist's rise from the ten kingdoms, and revive the Grecian kingdom he ruled before\n- This kingdom will become the eighth and last, headed by Antichrist, who will fight Christ at Armageddon\n\nThis is what the revealing angel meant when he said the beast "was [had existed on Earth before John], and is not [on the Earth in John's day], and shall ascend out of the bottomless pit [in the last days to revive the kingdom he ruled before being confined] and go into perdition" (Rev. 17:8).\n\nThis angelic spirit will control Antichrist through demons, making him the embodiment of wickedness and the manifestation of satanic power. The angel is represented by the beast out of the Abyss, which will cause Antichrist's rise. Antichrist in turn is symbolized by the beast out of the sea of humanity, reviving and forming the eighth kingdom. That kingdom is also symbolized by the beast. All three things make up the powers represented by the one symbol, as we've seen.\n\nIt's the beast out of the Abyss that will make war on the two witnesses through Antichrist. Here we see the cruel operations of satanic powers through Antichrist and his kingdom — a combination of human and supernatural forces. But only when the witnesses' testimony is finished will God remove His protecting hand from them.\n\nIt's similar to how kingdoms have always worked throughout history: supernatural powers control the kingdoms of this world, and earthly events are really the result of battles won or lost between the good and evil spirits of God and Satan.\n\nThe devil controls the kingdoms of this world. He offered them to Christ, who didn't deny Satan's claim (Lk. 4:5, 6). Throughout this present age, God intervenes in Satan's plans to make His prophetic Word possible — while Satan and evil forces constantly try to make it fail. That's why there are battles in the heavenlies.\n\nThis battle between Satan and God can be traced throughout the Bible from Genesis to Revelation. God is working to bring humanity back to where we were before the Fall. To do that, He must continually counteract Satan's powers, as the enemy tries every conceivable way to hinder God's plan.\n\nIn the Old Testament, the battle was mainly about the coming of the woman's seed and God's purpose for Israel. Satan's great weapon was inspiring the world's kingdoms to destroy Israel so Christ would never come to pronounce doom on him. (See Lesson Eleven, Point VII, 6, (9), F and G.)\n\nFrom Israel's beginning in Gen. 12 through the Old Testament, five great kingdoms served Satan's purpose. But God turned Satan's efforts around and used these same nations to discipline Israel when she needed it. When one nation overstepped its role in God's plan, He overthrew it through another — and that required dealing with Satan in the heavenlies.\n\nThese five kingdoms were Egypt, Assyria, Babylon, Medo-Persia, and Greece with its divisions — the five that had fallen before John. The sixth was the old Roman Empire in John's day, which scattered Israel to the four winds. The seventh will be that same empire in the form of ten kingdoms, which will also persecute Israel. The beast will establish and rule the eighth, immediately following the seventh, becoming the greatest and bitterest persecutor of Israel of all eight kingdoms. It will try to carry out Satan's original plan to destroy Israel and thwart God's eternal purpose.\n\nIn every crisis in Israel's history with these kingdoms, God has intervened at just the right moment. That's how Israel has been preserved in the past, and how she'll be preserved in the future as God promised.\n\nBesides using kingdoms to oppress Israel, Satan has also caused Israel to sin so that God Himself has cursed them at times. But during those times, God has used the very tool Satan devised to destroy Israel as the means to chasten them and bring them to repentance. This will happen again in Israel's final repentance during the Tribulation.\n\nYou can see these patterns throughout Israel's history in the Pentateuch and historical books, especially in the prophets, where God's servants try to bring Israel to repentance by warning of foreign enemies.\n\nThe Bible gives us numerous passages showing satanic powers operating over different kingdoms in attempts to thwart God's purpose for Israel. We also see God's spirit forces opposing these satanic powers to carry out His plan (Isa. 14:4-21; 24:21-23; 25:7; Ezek. 28:11-17; Dan. 2:19-23, 28, 31-45; 4:25-37; 5:18-31; 7:1-28; 8:1-26; 9:24-27; 10:1-12, 13; Joel 3; Zech. 14; Lk. 4:5, 6; Jn. 12:31; 2 Cor. 4:4; Eph. 2:2; 6:11-17; 2 Thess. 2; Rev. 11:15; 13:1-18; etc.).\n\nThese references clearly illustrate this truth, and the beast out of the Abyss is plainly identified in Daniel alongside Revelation. In Daniel, we see empires rise and fall — each used to carry out God's purpose in disciplining or delivering Israel — from Daniel's time to the Messiah's coming.\n\nWhen one kingdom fails to carry out God's purpose, He chooses another, as illustrated in 2 Ki. 23:29–24:10. In Dan. 2, 7, 8, 9, and 10-12, we see six world kingdoms that oppressed Israel:\n\n- Babylon as the head of gold and the lion\n- Medo-Persia as the silver, the bear, and the ram\n- Greece as the brass, the leopard, and the he-goat\n- Rome as the iron and the terrible beast\n- Revised Rome as the ten toes and ten horns\n- Revived Grecia as the kingdom of the little horn out of the ten horns of Dan. 7, out of the four horns of Dan. 8, and as the king of the north (Syria) in Dan. 11, as we studied in Lesson Thirty-nine\n\nNow here's the key passage: Dan. 10:1–11:1 is the main Scripture in the entire Bible concerning the supernatural princes under Satan who rule different kingdoms. It tells us how God overrules these rulers to fulfill prophecy.\n\nPicture it this way: just as earthly kings have their territories, so do these spiritual princes. And just as earthly wars determine who rules which land, spiritual warfare determines which supernatural power controls which kingdom.\n\n---\n\nThe beast with seven heads and ten horns represents the final form of Gentile world power — a global political and economic system that will align under the Antichrist in the last days. It's not a distant fantasy. It's a trajectory that current global trends are already building toward. So here's the question: Do you have a biblical framework for understanding the globalization of power, economics, and religion that you see happening today? And does that framework produce in you a coherent response — one rooted in Scripture — rather than either panic or disengagement?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk48j001fewhj5s17d3v9	cmnknk48e000xewhj49ao5az0	Supplement 15 — For Lessons 29 & 30	SUPPLEMENT	692	17	\N	t	The New Testament Church is the body of Christ — not a building or an institution but a living organism. The baptism of the Holy Spirit is the empowerment that makes that body function at full capacity. This supplement bridges those two truths: what the Church is, and what it needs to operate as designed.\n\n![Supplement 15 — For Lessons 29 & 30](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsYWg1axuPZk7DNnrmEFXgTARstSavVPji1eMG)\n\n## All Spiritual Opposition is Satanic\n\nHere's something worth thinking about: if God is the author of all the Scriptures we've studied proving these blessings are for us today, then who do you think is behind the criticism of these doctrines? It has to be His opponent — the devil.\n\nAnyone who condemns a spiritual experience that's plainly stated in Scripture is being used as a tool of the enemy. The devil's goal is simple: rob people of the purposes and benefits God intends for them.\n\n## Christians Should Unite to Fight Satan\n\nInstead of fighting each other, Christians should be joining forces against our real enemies — sin, sickness, and the devil. Think of it like this: if an army spent all its energy fighting within its own ranks, the actual enemy would have free rein to do whatever he wanted.\n\nThat's exactly what Satan accomplishes when he gets believers squabbling over church creeds, rituals, minor theological theories, and even spiritual experiences that should never cause division. His long-range strategy is to keep Christians from gaining power with God against him and his works.\n\nAs long as he can prevent aggressive spiritual warfare and keep believers from walking in victory, he's free to continue his work of bringing sin, sickness, defeat, and poverty into people's lives.\n\n## The Goal of Every Christian\n\nYour goal as a believer should be to attain all the fullness of God until the full evidences of Spirit-baptism (as we discussed in Lesson Thirty) are manifested in your life. Don't be satisfied with the measure of the Spirit you currently have.\n\nSadly, the least little blessing from God seems to satisfy most people. But that's not how it should be.\n\n## Do Not Ridicule Experiences of Others\n\nMost of us have heard people make slanderous remarks about those who claim divine healing power, who say they've been healed by God, who testify to the baptism in the Spirit, or who share some other biblical experience. Some folks have ridiculed supernatural manifestations so thoroughly that hundreds of thousands of people in our biggest churches know nothing of the real truth.\n\nChurch members get taught to avoid any mention of these biblical doctrines and to write off all spiritual manifestations as either demonic or fanatical. Meanwhile, those same members smoke, chew tobacco, play cards, drink, and commit all kinds of sins — and their teachers never rebuke them or warn them away from these evils. What a sad condition!\n\n## Spiritual Gifts are of God\n\nThis situation should never be tolerated by any church. When it is, that church becomes spiritually dead and stops being a true Christian church — it's just a center for entertainment and social activity.\n\nWe've looked at plenty of Scripture proving there's a real baptism in the Spirit, real power from God for believers, and real gifts of the Spirit that should be operating in every church and every member. Instead of shunning these experiences and the people who teach them, church members should welcome any good news about these blessings. They should be eager to learn everything they can.\n\nAsk yourself: Would God give us hundreds of Scriptures making these things clear if He didn't intend for us to have them?\n\n## No Excuse for Ignorance or Controversy\n\nThere's no reason to be ignorant of these spiritual blessings if you'll just read your Bible and see for yourself what God says. And there's no excuse for controversy about spiritual experiences if people will be even half honest and open to the truths plainly stated in Scripture.\n\nA popular excuse among those who fight these doctrines is pointing to failures in Christian living — people who don't demonstrate the power such experiences are supposed to bring. But these critics seem to forget that some people in *all* churches fail to live the Christian life. Should we call all those churches "of the devil" because some members fail? Should we condemn their doctrines because a few members don't live right?\n\nRegardless of human failure, we must accept plain truths of Scripture as being from God.\n\n## All Spiritual Manifestations are Strange\n\nPaul told us that the things of God's Spirit would seem like foolishness to the natural man (1 Cor. 2:14) and that God would use the foolish things of the world to confound the wise (1 Cor. 1:26-31). It's similar to how someone who's never seen a smartphone might think the whole concept is absurd — until they actually use one and discover what it can do.\n\nNaturally, any manifestation of the nine gifts mentioned in 1 Cor. 12 would seem foolish and strange to someone who's never seen them in operation. But why reject or criticize something you know nothing about — either from Scripture or from experience?\n\nThe natural man isn't used to spiritual experiences, and every one of them will seem like foolishness until he decides to be open enough to investigate what's written about them. When something is clearly written about any spiritual blessing, that should settle it as biblical and from God. We should then bring our natural thinking under control and refuse to reject the truth.\n\n## Christians Cannot Get a Wrong Spirit\n\nMany people are afraid that God might let them get a wrong spirit if they seek spiritual experiences. But this fear has no foundation, as Lk. 11:7-13 proves. God will not permit any honest person to be deceived and receive a wrong spirit if they walk uprightly according to His Word and ask only for things He has promised.\n\nOnly dishonest people who try to evade truth and live unclean lives need to fear, because they've opened themselves to demonic deception. As a true child of God, you should walk in simple faith and obedience to all known truth, praying daily, staying under the blood of Christ — and you won't need to give a single thought to getting a wrong spirit.\n\n## The One Great Need of the Modern Church\n\nThe greatest need in any church is to forget petty differences and seek God for the New Testament enduement of power for service — the kind of power that does the works of Christ in delivering people from sin, sickness, failure, and poverty.\n\nThe Church as a whole may not be willing to do this. But as individuals, we must pursue this if we want to experience the full benefits of the gospel personally.\n\nThe world today is dying for apostolic power and genuine Christian living. One of the last letters of the New Testament commands us to contend for the faith once delivered to the saints (Jude 3). Instead of doing this, the modern Church as a whole is making war on the few who are earnestly contending for this faith.\n\nNo preacher can lead his people into a revival of faith and power in God if he's fighting against the very experiences that would bring these benefits. Every preacher should encourage anyone in his congregation who hungers and thirsts for righteousness, anyone who has even the least desire for deeper experiences in God than what's typical in the average church today. This is the minimum he can do to bring about a New Testament revival.\n\nBut when Christian leaders begin to oppose and resist the Holy Spirit like Israel of old — when even the smallest manifestation of God's Spirit is fought and rejected as demonic — it becomes very hard for others to attain these experiences. God doesn't require anyone to accept these experiences if they don't want them. But every person should at least encourage others to pursue them, even if they don't want them personally.\n\n---\n\n## Ten Questions For Supplement Fifteen. Did You Know:\n\n- **That Melchisedec was an ordinary man?** He is called a "man" in Heb. 7:4. He had a "descent" or genealogy but it was not counted (Heb. 7:6). He was an ordinary man who lived in the days of Abraham, as plainly taught in Gen. 14:17-24; Heb. 7:1-11. He was without recorded father or mother, beginning of days and end of life, so that he could be a type of Christ, who really had no beginning or end of days and who was to be a priest after his order forever (Heb. 6:20).\n\n- **That people have appeared to men on Earth after death?** Although this is not true of men in general, as proven in Lk. 16:19-31, it has been true of special cases when God willed it, as is clear in the cases of Moses (Mt. 17:3) and of the saints resurrected after Christ was resurrected (Mt. 27:52). Moses could not have been resurrected because Christ had not yet become the first-fruits of all that slept (1 Cor. 15:23). He appeared in his soulish form, as explained in Question 8 below.\n\n- **That there will be male and female in Heaven?** This is clear from the fact that every person will receive his own body in the resurrection (1 Cor. 15:35-58). The Scripture in Gal. 3:28 simply means that male and female have the same privileges in the gospel and in Christ. No Scripture teaches that any person will be de-sexed by the resurrection.\n\n- **That there may be baby bodies in Heaven?** The fact that every person will receive his own body in the resurrection proves this (1 Cor. 15:35-58). Paul illustrates the resurrection by small and large grains reproducing their own size and kind, so also is the resurrection of the dead. Nothing in nature continues to grow after death. This is one reason we shall know as we are known (1 Cor. 13:12). On the other hand, all may be resurrected fully mature like any normal seed when it is reproduced and fully grown.\n\n- **That the Bible plainly says that the Earth is round?** See Isa. 40:22. When "four corners" are mentioned it means four quarters or directions (Isa. 11:11-12; Mt. 24:31; Rev. 7:1-3).\n\n- **That the devil still has access to Heaven?** He will have access to Heaven until the middle of Daniel's Seventieth Week or the last seven years of this age, as proven in Rev. 12:7-12. Then he will be cast out to the Earth and his place will be found no more in Heaven as the accuser of the brethren. (See Supplement Six, Question 6, for proof that all of Rev. 4–22 must be fulfilled after the rapture and during the last seven years of this age.)\n\n- **That Jesus Christ Himself went to Hell?** See Ps. 16:10; Acts 2:27-31; Mt. 12:40; Eph. 4:7-11, which proves that He did go to Hell.\n\n- **That souls of men after death wear clothes?** Moses wore clothes when he appeared with Christ (Mt. 17:3) and the souls under the altar were given clothes to put on (Rev. 6:9-11). See also the last paragraph of Lesson Four, Point 7.\n\n- **That Judas was one time a saved man?** He had the Holy Spirit and power like the other apostles and was a successful preacher and healer like them (Mt. 10:1-20; Mk. 6:7-13). He had his name written in Heaven, as proven by Acts 1:20 with Ps. 69:25-29. He fell from the apostleship by transgression (Acts 1:25). These and other facts that will be given in detail in Lesson Thirty-five prove he was one time a saved man.\n\n- **That Judas was not a devil from the beginning?** There is no such statement in the Bible. He was "a devil" or an adversary against Christ toward the latter part of His ministry and this is all the Bible does say about the question (Jn. 6:70). He was not always an adversary for he was once a saved man, as proven above.\n\n---\n\nThe Church was never designed to function at half-power — and the baptism of the Holy Spirit was given so it wouldn't have to. So here's the question: Are you a genuinely connected, contributing member of a local church body — not attending, but belonging? And are you operating in the full empowerment of the Spirit that Christ promised His Church — or are you functioning on natural ability alone?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk48j001hewhj4sb0ue2f	cmnknk48e000xewhj49ao5az0	Lesson 32 — The Old and New Covenants	READING	5513	19	\N	t	The word 'covenant' appears hundreds of times in Scripture — and most Christians couldn't tell you the difference between the Old Covenant and the New one if asked to explain it in plain language. This is a problem, because every promise, every obligation, and every relationship with God is defined by which covenant applies to you.\n\nLast time we distinguished the Kingdom of Heaven from the Kingdom of God. Now we examine the legal framework that governs how God relates to humanity in each age: the Old Covenant made at Sinai and the New Covenant ratified in Christ's blood.\n\nHere's something that might surprise you: the entire law of God and Moses—including the Ten Commandments and all the civil and ceremonial laws built on them—was abolished in Christ on the cross. The New Covenant has completely taken the place of the old one.\n\nPaul makes this crystal clear in 2 Cor. 3:6-15: "Who hath made us able ministers of the new testament . . . if the ministration of death, written and engraven in stones (the Ten Commandments); was glorious, so that the children of Israel could not steadfastly behold the face of Moses for the glory of his countenance which was to be done away. For if the ministration of condemnation be glory, much more doth the ministration of righteousness exceed in glory. For even that (the Old Covenant) which was made glorious had no glory in this respect, by reason of the glory that excelleth. For if that (the Old Covenant) which is done away was glorious, much more that (the New Covenant) which remaineth is glorious . . . not as Moses, which put a veil over his face, that the children of Israel could not steadfastly look to the end of that (the Old Covenant) which is abolished: But their minds were blinded: for until this day remaineth the same vail untaken away in the reading of the old testament; which is done away in Christ" (see also Col. 2:14-17; Eph. 2:15).\n\nNow, here's something important to notice: the word "vail" in verse 14 isn't actually in the original Greek. So it's not just the veil that's "done away"—it's the Old Covenant itself that's done away in Christ on the cross.\n\nYou might think you need a scholar to explain this passage, but honestly, it's pretty straightforward for any reader. The Old Covenant has been "done away" and "abolished" entirely, and the New Covenant has taken its place. Remember, the Ten Commandments were the only part of the law written and engraved on stone tablets, so they were done away with on the cross (Exod. 24:12; 31:18; 32:15; 34:1-4, 27-35; Deut. 4:13; 5:22; 10:4).\n\nSome folks argue that only the *glory* of the Old Covenant was done away. But think about it—what good is a covenant that's lost its glory? Why would we cling to it when we have a better, more glorious New Covenant that's still in force?\n\nPaul's logic is this: the Old Covenant was given in glory, so if its glory is done away, the covenant itself is also done away. When you leave out the second "glory" in verse 7 and the second "vail" in verse 14 (which aren't in the original Greek), you see plainly that it's "the old testament; which is done away in Christ." And notice in verse 15 that the Old Covenant is called "Moses"—proving that Moses gave the Ten Commandments.\n\nLet me show you the contrasts Paul draws between the Old and New Covenants in 2 Cor. 3:\n\n- The first is "old"; the second is "new"\n- The first brought "death"; the second brings "life"\n- The first was "glorious"; the second is "much more glorious"\n- The first brought "condemnation"; the second brings "righteousness" that frees from condemnation (Rom. 8:1-4)\n- The first was of "the letter"; the second is of "the Spirit"\n- The first had "no glory" compared to the second "by reason of the glory that excelleth"\n- The first was "done away" and "abolished"; the second "remaineth"\n- The first came by "Moses" and was "done away in Christ"\n- The first required a veil to hide the glory because it was "the ministration of death," but the exceeding glory of the second can be looked upon with "open face" because it is "the spirit that giveth life"\n- The first brought blindness and bondage; the second brings enlightenment, liberty, and transforms character by the Spirit of the Lord\n\nThink of it like this: a mere change in how the Old Covenant was administered, or a failure by either party to keep it, or even where it was written—none of that changes one covenant into another or affects its glory. Some may argue and try to get around this plain Scripture that says three times the Old Covenant was "done away" and once that it was "abolished" in Christ.\n\nIt's similar to a contract or a will: if you just change how a contract is administered, that doesn't change the contract itself. The contract or will itself would have to be changed—or a new one would have to take the place of the old one—for it to be "done away" and "abolished" as Scripture says of the Old Covenant.\n\nWhy any group, if they had the slightest honesty and faith that the Bible is God's Word, would twist Scripture to fit some human theory is hard to understand. It would be much easier to accept God's plain words and, if human theories don't harmonize with them, throw those theories away and stick with God and His Word on all questions.\n\n![Lesson 32 — The Old and New Covenants](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsIng0y6DFm5fHMlhCe2Nngyo6VzaWuJEO4Dj7)\n\n### 2. Christ Fulfilled the Law Before Abolishing It\n\nChrist came to "fulfill" the law before He "abolished" it on the cross (Mt. 5:17, 18). The Greek word for "fulfill" is *plero*, meaning "to satisfy," "execute," "finish," "end," "make complete," and "cause to expire."\n\nThis same word is used for many prophecies that came to an end when they were fulfilled (Mt. 1:22; 2:15, 17, 23; 4:14; 8:17; 12:17; 13:35; 26:54-56; 27:9, 35; etc.). That the law also ceased to be in force when it was "fulfilled" is clear from Mt. 11:13; Lk. 16:16; 24:27, 44; Gal. 3:19-25.\n\nThe law was only a shadow of things to come, and when those realities arrived, the mere type, picture, or shadow was no longer needed (Col. 2:14-17; Heb. 4:1-11; 8:1-6; 9:1-10; 10:1-18).\n\n### 3. Grace and Truth Replaced the Law\n\n"The law was given by Moses, but grace and truth came by Jesus Christ" (Jn. 1:17). This not only proves that the law was not made known before Moses, but that it was replaced by the New Covenant of grace and truth when Christ came.\n\n### 4. The Apostles Did Not Require Law Keeping\n\nThe early church faced this very question head-on. In Acts 15:5-29, we read: "But there rose up certain of the sect of the Pharisees which believed, saying, That it was needful to circumcise them [Gentile Christians], and to command them to keep the law of Moses. And the apostles and elders came together for to consider of this matter. And when there had been much disputing, Peter rose up, and said . . . Now therefore why tempt ye God, to put a yoke upon the neck of the disciples, which neither our fathers nor we were able to bear . . . Simeon hath declared how God at the first did visit the Gentiles, to take out of them a people for his name. And to this agree the words of the prophets; as it is written, after this [the Church Age] I will return, and will build again the tabernacle of David, which is fallen down, and I will build the ruins thereof, and I will set it up . . . Wherefore my sentence is, that we trouble not them, which from among the Gentiles are turned to God: But that we write unto them, that they abstain from fornication, and from things strangled, and from blood. For Moses of old time hath in every city them that preach him [his law, the Old Covenant], being read in the synagogues every sabbath. Then pleased it the apostles and elders and the whole Church to send men . . . they wrote letters by them after this manner; The apostles and elders and brethren send greeting unto the brethren which are of the Gentiles . . . Forasmuch as we have heard, that certain which went out from us [Jews in the early Church who have been under the law from birth] have troubled you with words, subverting your souls, saying, ye must be circumcised, and keep the law: to whom we gave no such commandment . . . For it seemed good to the Holy Ghost, and to us, to lay upon you no greater burden than these necessary things; that ye abstain from meats offered to idols, and from blood, and from things strangled, and from fornication [all fleshly lusts as in Gal. 5:19-21]; from which if ye keep yourselves, ye shall do well."\n\nThe apostles did away with all law keeping and circumcision. They stated in no uncertain terms that Gentiles were not to keep the Law of Moses, which included the old Jewish sabbath. This is the Christian position throughout this age. However, if this decision had been made by some modern teachers, it might have been law and sabbath-keeping as the sum total of salvation!\n\nIncluded in this apostolic decree was the very sign of law-keeping—circumcision—which was abolished. Interestingly, even those who insist on keeping the Jewish sabbath don't practice circumcision, proving their inconsistency in requiring people to keep a law they themselves don't fully keep (Exod. 12:44-48; Lev. 12:3; Jn. 7:22-23).\n\nCircumcision was required as a sign of the covenant God made with Abraham (Gen. 17:9-14). The New Covenant doesn't require it (Rom. 4:11; 1 Cor. 7:18; Gal. 2:3; 5:1-5). This change proves that the New Covenant is *not* the same as the one made with Abraham, as some teach today. If it were the same covenant, circumcision would still be required.\n\n### 5. Freedom Regarding Days and Meats\n\nPaul writes about meats and sabbath days: "One man esteemeth one day above another: another esteemeth every day alike. Let every man be fully persuaded in his own mind. He that regardeth the day, regardeth it unto the Lord; and he that regardeth not the day, to the Lord he doth not regard it . . . But why dost thou judge thy brother? or why dost thou set at nought thy brother? for we shall all stand before the judgment seat of Christ . . . So then every one of us shall give account of himself to God. Let us not therefore judge one another any more" (Rom. 14:1-13; see also Gal. 4:9-11; Col. 2:14-17).\n\nIf the Law of Moses, including the fourth commandment, were still in force under the New Covenant, Paul never would have written these statements. Each Christian can do as they please concerning the sabbath and shouldn't be judged by their brother "any more."\n\n### 6. Christian Experiences Don't Come by the Law\n\nPaul asks pointedly: "Received ye the Spirit by the works of the law, or by the hearing of faith? . . . the law is not of faith: but, the man that doeth them [things of the law] shall live in them . . . how turn ye again to the weak and beggarly elements, whereunto ye desire to be in bondage?" (Gal. 1:6-9; 2:15-21; 3:1-12, 19-25; 4:1-3, 19-31; 5:1-9, 11-21).\n\nHe continues: "Ye observe days, and months, and times, and years [that the law required]. I am afraid of you, lest I have bestowed labour upon you in vain" (Gal. 3:1-12; 4:9-11).\n\nGoing back to observe sabbaths according to the law is described here as returning to bondage. If someone does this, "he is a debtor to do the whole law" (Gal. 5:3).\n\n### 7. The Law Was a Temporary Schoolmaster\n\nThe law "was added because of transgressions, till the seed [Christ] should come to whom the promise was made . . . But before faith came [that Christ brought in the gospel and the New Covenant, Heb. 12:1, 2], we were kept under the law, shut up unto that faith which should afterwards be revealed . . . Wherefore the law was our schoolmaster to bring us to Christ that we might be justified by faith. But after that faith is come, we are no longer under a schoolmaster" (Gal. 3:19-25).\n\nPicture it this way: not being "under" the law is like graduating from school—you're no longer under your teacher's authority. The law is not in force and has no claim or authority over someone under the New Covenant. You're now obligated to keep New Covenant laws and commandments, which include new laws that were never part of the Old Covenant, as well as those old laws that God chose to bring into the New Covenant.\n\n### 8. The Law Has Been "Cast Out"\n\nPaul uses a powerful illustration: "Tell me, ye that desire to be under the law, do ye not hear the law. For it is written, that Abraham had two sons . . . he who was of the bondwoman was born after the flesh; but he of the freewoman was by promise. which things are an allegory: for these are the two covenants; the one from mount Sinai, which gendereth to bondage, which is Agar (Hagar) . . . and answereth to Jerusalem which now is, and is in bondage with her children. But Jerusalem which is above is free, which is the mother of us all . . . Now we, brethren, as Isaac was, are the children of promise. But as then he that was born after the flesh persecuted him that is born after the Spirit, even so it is now. Nevertheless what saith the Scripture? Cast out the bondwoman [the first or Old Covenant from Mount Sinai] and her son [those under the law]: for the son of the bondwoman shall not be heir with the son of the freewoman. So then, brethren, we are not children of the bondwoman [the old "ten commandment" covenant and its many laws], but of the free [the New Covenant]. Stand fast therefore in the liberty wherewith Christ hath made us free, and be not entangled again with the yoke of bondage [the Old Covenant] . . . For I testify again to every man that is circumcised [no man had any part in the Old Covenant unless he was circumcised], that he is a debtor to do the whole law" (Gal. 4:21-31; 5:1-5, 18).\n\nThose who advocate law-keeping today rarely mention this passage—and for good reason! There's simply no way to explain away such clear language: the Old Covenant "from Sinai" has been "cast out" along with all its sons who are under bondage and death.\n\nThe Old Covenant that brought death has no more relationship to people under the New Covenant than Hagar and Ishmael had with Sarah and Isaac. Abraham fathered both Ishmael and Isaac, but Ishmael had no part in Isaac's inheritance (Gen. 21:9-21). God was the author of both covenants, but one was designed to bring death and guilt of sin, while the other was designed to bring life and freedom from sin. You can't keep both, for reasons we'll explore further below.\n\n### 9. The Law Was Shadows—Now Abolished\n\nThe law was a covenant of types and shadows, and it was abolished when the realities of those shadows appeared.\n\n"Having abolished in his flesh the enmity [the law of bondage and death], even the law of commandments contained in ordinances . . . blotting out [making void] the handwriting of ordinances that was against us, which was contrary to us, and took it out of the way, nailing it to his cross . . . let no man therefore judge you in meat, or in drink, or in respect of an holyday, or of the new moon, or of the sabbath days: which are a shadow of things to come; but the body [or reality of which those things were mere shadows] is of Christ" (Eph. 2:15; Col. 2:14-17).\n\nNow, followers of Moses today naturally try to explain away these passages that say the old law or covenant has been "abolished." But the more they attempt this impossible job, the more ridiculous their position becomes. It's like trying to sink a battleship with a blow gun—it simply can't be done!\n\nIn trying to explain away these passages, they say "the law of commandments" refers only to the ceremonial law, and "the sabbath days" of Col. 2:14-17 were the ceremonial sabbaths, not the weekly sabbath.\n\nHere's the inconsistency: wherever else "commandments" and "sabbath days" appear in Scripture, they argue those refer to the Ten Commandments and the weekly sabbath! This only magnifies how inconsistent their doctrine is.\n\nRegardless of what Old Covenant disciples say about these passages, Paul certainly wasn't one of them—he never once required people to keep any certain day, especially the old fourth commandment sabbath. The Greek word for "sabbath" is plural, and "days" isn't in the original language, so it should read "sabbaths." It's the same Greek word used for weekly sabbaths in 59 other places, so it must include the weekly sabbath as well as any other sabbath of the Old Covenant.\n\nThe weekly sabbath, along with the other sabbaths, is part of the ordinances God gave to Moses. The weekly sabbath was actually the first of eight feasts of the Lord in Lev. 23: "Concerning the feasts of the Lord, which ye shall proclaim to be holy convocations, even these are my feasts. Six days shall work be done: but on the seventh day is the sabbath of rest, an holy convocation; ye shall do no work therein: it is the sabbath of the Lord in all your dwellings." After commanding this weekly "feast" and "holy convocation," the Lord next commanded the seasonal "feasts" and "holy convocations," to be kept "in their seasons" (Lev. 23:2-4). So we have a "weekly feast" and "seasonal feasts," all meant to be "holy convocations" and times of rest and holy worship.\n\nIn Lev. 23:38, after giving the seven seasonal feasts (which came after the weekly feast), the Lord said, "These are the feasts of the Lord . . . Besides the sabbaths of the Lord," referring to the weekly sabbaths. This proves that "sabbaths" includes the weekly sabbath as much as any other sabbath. See also Exod. 31:13-17; Ezek. 20:12, 20, or any place where "sabbaths" appears—you'll have to acknowledge that the weekly sabbath is included in "sabbaths" in Col. 2:14-17.\n\nAll these "feasts" were to be observed "for ever" (Lev. 23:14, 31, 41), so the theory that the weekly sabbath is the only eternal feast is unscriptural. All eight feasts were part of one eternal law that would have continued eternally if man could have kept his part of the contract. Since man couldn't keep it, and there was no provision in it to make a person righteous and capable of keeping it, it had to be set aside for the New Covenant—which doesn't have faults like the old one.\n\nWhen the old law was "abolished," the weekly sabbath (the sign of the Old Covenant commemorating deliverance from Egypt) was done away, along with all other sabbaths and parts of the old law. Interestingly, eternal terms are used profusely in the so-called ceremonial part of the Law of Moses, but not once in connection with the Ten Commandments of Exod. 20:1-17 or Deut. 5:1-21. So if anything, it can be argued more strongly that the ceremonial part was eternal and the Ten Commandments were temporary—if we're using eternal terms as proof!\n\nThe Greek word for "ordinances" is *dogma*, meaning "a civil or religious law" (Lk. 2:1; Acts 16:4; 17:7; Eph. 2:15; Col. 2:14). Since the Ten Commandments were part of God's law to Israel, and the whole law has been abolished, there's no basis to argue that "the law of commandments" in Eph. 2:15 refers to all laws *except* the Ten Commandments. If those commandments were the foundation of the whole law (as everyone agrees), then they must be included in the law of commandments abolished in Christ on the cross.\n\nAs we've already seen, it's specifically the Ten Commandments that are singled out as having been "done away" and "abolished." And since the weekly sabbath is part of the ceremonial law, when people admit that "sabbaths" in Col. 2:14-17 refers to ceremonial sabbaths, they're telling the truth—but this doesn't mean the weekly sabbath is excluded from all those old law ceremonial sabbaths.\n\n### 10. The Old Law Was Imperfect and Had to Be Changed\n\nChrist came "after the order of Melchisedec" and not after the Levitical priesthood, which couldn't bring perfection—and under which "the people received the law . . . For the priesthood being changed, there is made of necessity a change [abolishment] also of the law . . . For there is verily a disannulling of the commandment going before for the weakness and unprofitableness thereof. For the law made nothing perfect, but the bringing in of a better hope did . . . By so much was Jesus made a surety of a better testament. . . . For the law maketh men priests which have infirmity; but the word of the oath, which is since the law, maketh the Son, who is consecrated for evermore. . . . But now hath he obtained a more excellent ministry [than Old Covenant priests], by how much also he is the mediator of a better covenant, which is established upon better promises. For if that first covenant [from Sinai, Gal. 4:21-31] had been faultless, then should no place have been sought for the second. But finding fault with them, he saith . . . a new covenant, he hath made the first old [obsolete]. Now that [Old Covenant] which decayeth [is obsolete, discarded] and waxeth old is ready to vanish away [to be abrogated]. Then verily the first covenant had also ordinances of divine service, and a worldly sanctuary . . . Which was a figure for the time then present. . . . Which stood only in meats and drinks, and divers washings, and carnal [human] ordinances, imposed on them until the time of the reformation [setting things right of the new order] . . . for this cause he is the mediator of the new covenant, that by means of death, for the redemption of the transgressions that were under the first testament, they which are called might receive the promise of eternal inheritance. For where a testament is, there must also of necessity be the death of the testator. For a testament is of force after men are dead: otherwise, it is of no strength at all while the testator liveth [the New Covenant was not in force until Christ died]. Whereupon neither the first testament was dedicated without blood. For when Moses had spoken every precept [including the Ten Commandments] to all the people according to all the law, he took the blood of calves and of goats . . . and sprinkled both the book and all the people, Saying, this is the blood of the testament which God hath enjoined unto you . . . For the law having a shadow of good things to come, and not the very image of the things can never . . . make the comers thereunto perfect . . . Then said he, Lo, I come to do thy will, O God. He taketh away [abolishes] the first [Old Covenant], that he may establish the second [New Covenant]" (Heb. 7:11-12, 22, 28; 8:6-13; 9:1, 9, 10, 15-22; 10:1-18).\n\nThe word "establish" here means to enact the laws of the New Covenant, while the same word in Rom. 3:31 means the righteousness of the Old Covenant is upheld in the New Covenant by fulfilling that righteousness in people—not by keeping the old law, but by faith in Christ provided through the New Covenant (Rom. 8:1-4; Gal. 2:15-20; 3:1-14; 5:1-26).\n\nThese many passages from Hebrews and the other points above make it clear: the Old Covenant with the Ten Commandments and all the laws given to Moses was abolished, abrogated, and annulled.\n\n## II. Contrasts Between the Old and New Covenants\n\nThe contrasts between the Old and New Covenants prove they were two distinct covenants made for different purposes and to be in force at different times:\n\n> 1. One is called "the first covenant"; the other "the second covenant" (Heb. 8:7; 9:1-18; 10:1-9).\n\n> 2. The first is called "the old covenant"; the second is called "the new covenant" (Mt. 26:28; 2 Cor. 3; Heb. 8:13).\n\n> 3. The first covenant was given by Moses; the second by Jesus Christ (Jn. 1:17; Gal. 3:19; Heb. 9:15; Mt. 26:28).\n\n> 4. One is "the law of Moses"; the other "the law of Christ" (Acts 13:39; Gal. 6:2).\n\n> 5. One is "the law of sin"; the other is "the law of righteousness" (Rom. 7:7-25; 8:1-4; 9:31).\n\n> 6. One is the law of "the flesh"; the other is "the law of the Spirit" (Rom. 7:5, 6; 8:1-4; Gal. 5:16-26).\n\n> 7. One is "not of faith"; the other is "the law of faith" (Gal. 3:12; Rom. 3:27).\n\n> 8. One is the "yoke of bondage"; the other is "the law of liberty" (Gal. 5:1; Jas. 1:18-25).\n\n> 9. One is brought to an end by Christ (Rom. 10:4; 2 Cor. 3:14; Heb. 10:9); the other is established by Christ (Heb. 8:6; 9:15; 10:9; 2 Cor. 3:6-18; Mt. 26:28).\n\n> 10. One brought death; the other brought life (2 Cor. 3:6-18; Rom. 8:1-4; Gal. 3:21; Heb. 9:15; 10:1-18).\n\n> 11. One makes guilty (Rom. 3:19-20); the other justifies or makes not guilty (Acts 13:39; Rom. 3:21-31; 5:1-11).\n\n> 12. One is "a shadow of things to come"; the other is the reality (Col. 2:14-17; Heb. 10:1-18).\n\n> 13. One is "fulfilled" or completed; the other is still in force (Mt. 5:17, 18; 2 Cor. 3:6-18; Heb. 10:9).\n\n> 14. One demanded righteousness; the other gave righteousness (Lk. 10:28; Rom. 8:1-4; Gal. 3:1-29; 5:1-26).\n\n> 15. One made nothing perfect; the other made perfect (Heb. 7:19).\n\n> 16. One was glorious; the other was more glorious (2 Cor. 3).\n\n> 17. One was powerless to save from sin; the other saves to the uttermost (Heb. 7:11-28; 8:7-13; 9:9-28; 10:1-18).\n\n> 18. One had many sacrifices; the other had only one (Heb. 9:9-14; 10:14; Rom. 6:6-13).\n\n> 19. One had a changeable priesthood; the other did not (Heb. 7:23-28; 4:14-16; Rom. 8:34).\n\n> 20. One remembered sins; the other forgets sin (Heb. 10:3; 8:12; etc.).\n\n> 21. One had a representative and a seasonal access to God (Heb. 9:7-10); the other had a personal and daily access to God (Heb. 4:14-16; 7:25; 10:19, 20; Eph. 2:18).\n\n> 22. One had a sinful ministry (Heb. 5:1-4); the other had a sinless ministry (Heb. 7:26-28; 2 Cor. 3; 1 Jn. 3:9).\n\n> 23. One was given under the Levitical priesthood (Heb. 7:11, 12); the other under the present Melchisedec priesthood (Heb. 6:20; 7:11-21, 24).\n\n> 24. One had an earthly tabernacle service of animal sacrifices (Heb. 9:1-10); the other had a heavenly tabernacle service of spiritual salvation (Heb. 4:14-16; 7:25; 8:1-5; 9:11-15).\n\n> 25. One had a sinful mediator (Gal. 3:19); the other had a sinless mediator (1 Tim. 2:5; Heb. 7:26; 1 Pet. 2:22).\n\n> 26. One had no eternal inheritance (Rom. 4:13); the other had an eternal inheritance (Rom. 8:17; Heb. 9:15; 1 Pet. 1).\n\n> 27. One was ratified by animal blood (Exod. 29:1-8; Heb. 9:16-22); the other by the blood of Christ (Mt. 26:28).\n\n> 28. One was a law of works; the other a law of grace (Jn. 1:17; Rom. 3:24-31; Gal. 3:10-12; Eph. 2:8, 9).\n\n> 29. One brought wrath (Rom. 4:15); the other brought salvation from wrath (Rom. 5:9; Gal. 3:13, 14).\n\n> 30. One could not redeem; the other could (Gal. 3:10-14; Rom. 8:1-4; Eph. 1:7; Col. 1:14; Heb. 7:25).\n\n> 31. One could not satisfy God's demands; the other one did (Gal. 2:21; Heb. 7:22; 8:6; 10:5-18).\n\n> 32. One made no provision for doing miracles; the other one provided for them (Gal. 3:1-5; 1 Jn. 5:8; Jn. 14:12; Lk. 24:49).\n\n> 33. Prophecy foretold the abolishing of one and the establishing of the other (Isa. 51:4; Jer. 31:33; Acts 3:22; Heb. 8:7-13; 10:4-18; Rom. 11:25-29).\n\n> 34. One had a fleshly sign of obedience (Acts 7:8; Rom. 2:25); the other did not (Rom. 4:11; 1 Cor. 7:18).\n\n> 35. One was too weak to overcome sin; the other gave victory over sin (Rom. 6:1-23; 8:1-4; Eph. 2:8, 9; 1 Jn. 5:1-18).\n\n> 36. One was made to be changed (Heb. 7:11-22; 8:5, 6; 9:9, 10; 10:1-18; Gal. 3:19-24); the other was made to be unchanged (Heb. 7:22; 8:6; 10:9; 13:20).\n\n## III. The Old and New Covenants Illustrated\n\nThe scriptures we've studied prove that Christians are not under the law in any sense. No Christian is obligated to keep the Ten Commandments—not one of them—because they're part of the Old Covenant that was "abolished."\n\nHowever, those commandments and laws of the Old Covenant that were brought into the New Covenant, you're\n\n---\n\nYou are living under the New Covenant — the better covenant, established on better promises, ratified in better blood. Nothing in the Old Covenant can bind or obligate you apart from what the New Covenant affirms. So here's the question: Are there obligations, guilt structures, or religious rules you've been living under that come from the Old Covenant rather than the New? And what would your relationship with God look like if you actually lived in the freedom the New Covenant was designed to produce?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk48j001iewhj282cie25	cmnknk48e000xewhj49ao5az0	Supplement 16 — For Lessons 31 & 32	SUPPLEMENT	935	20	\N	t	The Kingdom of God and the Kingdom of Heaven are distinct — and the Old and New Covenants operate on entirely different foundations. Getting these distinctions right changes how you read almost every passage in the Gospels and Epistles. This supplement helps you apply those distinctions practically.\n\nThroughout our lessons and supplements, we've been showing you that God's plan for humanity is remarkably simple to understand. By now, you can see just how crystal clear the Bible really is on every subject it addresses.\n\nYou've probably marveled at how straightforward God's Word becomes when you read it literally, the same way you'd read any other book. For too long, this Book has been held up as some great mystery—supposedly so spiritual, so heavenly, and so supernatural that only a select few "specially anointed" teachers could possibly unlock its secrets.\n\nBut despite all the ways men have reinterpreted and changed it over the centuries, it remains a very simple, sensible, logical, and understandable book. Our eyes are opening now to the deceptions—both human and demonic—that have worked so hard to mystify and confuse sincere believers about what God plainly says regarding life now and in eternity.\n\n![Supplement 16 — For Lessons 31 & 32](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsZmI5yzdWQAzP5eOGYXjM0itdfBSUE9IN6u8o)\n\n## The Will of God Made Plain\n\nWe've seen clearly that God's will for His children is that they receive from Him—and His abundant supply—everything they need in this life and the one to come. We've pointed out that God loves His own children infinitely more than we could ever love ours.\n\nHe gladly gives His children all the good things of life. His promises cover every known need—body, soul, and spirit—forever. His work in providing these benefits is already finished.\n\nThe only thing standing between any person and full enjoyment of these blessings is their own failure to take the revealed steps necessary to receive them.\n\n## No Excuse for Ignorance Possible\n\nAt this point, there's simply no excuse for not knowing. You now understand God's will and plan for your spiritual, physical, and material needs.\n\nWe've provided plenty of Scripture to prove and clarify what you need to know—if you genuinely desire to understand. You've been shown what God's will is and what your responsibility is in obtaining what you need for life.\n\n## No Excuse for Failure to Get What You Want\n\nLikewise, there's no excuse for failing to receive what you want in life. If you understand God's simple plan for you and believe it, where is there room for failure?\n\nHere are some honest questions to consider: Have you earnestly applied yourself? Are you truly convinced that God is faithful to His Word and won't fail to do what He promised?\n\nHave you given your heart fully to God? Are you free from sin and destructive habits that create separation between you and Him?\n\nHave you intelligently taken the steps we've clearly outlined for meeting your needs? Do you accept God's promises as true just because they're in the Bible, or do you believe them from the heart as personal words from God to you?\n\nHave you set your face like flint, determined to receive these benefits regardless of opposition from people or demons? Have you fully resolved that you won't be denied—that nothing will hinder you from seeing these promises fulfilled?\n\nThis is your family right and gospel privilege as a child of God. So be bold to obtain grace and find help in your time of need (Heb. 4:14-16; 10:19-23).\n\n## You Have a Part to Do\n\nRemember—you have a part to play in receiving the benefits Christ died to provide. The plan is simple. God's will has been revealed and made plain. You're no longer ignorant about what to do.\n\nMentally, you should recognize there's no excuse for failure if you'll obey the truth. But that's only the starting point. There must be definite action on your part.\n\nYou can't just give mental agreement to truth—you must act on it with all your heart, soul, and strength. You must take the necessary steps before you'll experience what you're seeking.\n\n## Every Problem of Your Life Can Be Solved\n\nLet us state this again clearly: Every financial problem can be solved. Every burden on your heart can be rolled away. Every physical sickness, disease, and plague you're currently facing can be rebuked—and you can be made completely whole.\n\nEvery mental struggle and worry can be cleared up. Every spiritual lack can be supplied. Every degree of moral decay can be repaired. Every sin can be forgiven. Every destructive habit can be broken. Every sorrow can be healed.\n\nEvery need and want in your life can be met through faithful obedience to God's Word. No problem can remain with you for long if you'll fully seek God and turn your situation over to Him.\n\n## Nothing Impossible With God\n\nYou probably already believe this—but you need to become fully conscious of it in your heart. Think of it this way: God made the sun, moon, stars, planets, and everything in the vast universe.\n\nGod also created all the moral beings—angels and humans—throughout the universe. He planned for them to conform to His will, look to Him, and depend on Him for the grace and power needed for daily life. He's made an abundance available for everyone.\n\nWe proved this thoroughly in Lesson Ten. We also saw in Lesson Twelve why people's needs often go unmet, so review those two lessons.\n\nCheck up on your personal situation. Where are you weak, failing, or sinful? Correct those conditions immediately, and you'll be amazed at what happens.\n\nYou must confess and magnify God's greatness. Really settle the fact that He is capable of meeting every need in your life. Once this is firmly established, take the next step—ask, receive, and all things will be yours.\n\n## All Things Are Possible to Him That Believeth\n\nThis is a direct quotation from God's eternal, unchanging Word (Mk. 9:23). When you fully realize that God is capable and that all things are possible with Him, you should equally settle that all things are possible for you as you cooperate with Him.\n\nConsider the man with the demon-possessed boy in Mk. 9. He had undoubtedly tried every remedy available. He had watched his son suffer unbearable agony countless times—and no parent can witness that without becoming desperate for help from any source.\n\nHe had heard about Jesus and brought his son to Him. First he sought help from Jesus' disciples, but they couldn't deliver the boy. When Jesus returned from the mountain, the father brought his son forward and described the desperate situation, including the disciples' failure.\n\nJesus said, "O faithless generation, how long shall I be with you? how long shall I suffer you? bring him to me." The man replied, "If thou canst do any thing, have compassion on us, and help us." Jesus answered, "If thou canst believe, all things are possible to him that believeth." The man cried out with tears, "Lord, I believe; help thou mine unbelief."\n\nThis kind of desperation and faith will always be met by God. Jesus healed the boy and presented him to his father.\n\nWe have the same Jesus today—He is alive forevermore and holds the keys of Hell and death (Rev. 1:18; Heb. 13:8). If you want benefits from God, you must have faith—simple, unwavering faith that refuses to be denied.\n\nIf you don't want something enough to ensure you don't doubt God, if you're willing to accept "no" for an answer, then honestly—you're not worthy of a favorable response. This is all God requires of anyone, but He does require it of everyone equally without exception before He answers (Heb. 11:6; Jas. 1:5-8).\n\nSince this is God's absolute requirement—and He won't bend from it in the slightest—let it be settled in your heart that unwavering faith must be exercised. Get down to business. Make certain you don't waver, and the answer will be yours.\n\n## Don't Lay the Blame on God for Any Failure\n\nSome people constantly insist they have all the faith in the world and never waver—yet their prayers go unanswered. It's sometimes difficult to get such people to stop blaming God and seriously consider that they themselves might be wavering.\n\nThe first step in any such case is for the person to clear God of all blame and take responsibility themselves. This shows both honesty and intelligence by first identifying where the failure actually lies.\n\nIf God has promised that He will hear without exception when unwavering faith is exercised, then the blame simply cannot rest on Him.\n\n## The New Testament Terms of Blessings Are Clear\n\nPeople can argue all they want that God's promises are unconditional—that there's nothing anyone must do to enter grace or receive any blessing. Despite such claims, there are definite conditions people must meet to receive God's promised benefits.\n\nNot a single statement in Scripture says that God's grace and promised blessings on any point are unconditional. If benefits were truly unconditional, everyone would be blessed equally—regardless of whether they're saints or sinners, regardless of whether they pursue holiness and truth or not.\n\nBut obviously, not everyone is blessed equally in all things. This proves that certain things must be done to receive what God has promised.\n\nWe've shown you lesson after lesson what steps are necessary and what benefits you can receive from God. The best thing for you to do is forget the theories of men that can't be supported by clear Scripture.\n\nYou can see for yourself, by reading the promises, what you must do. Don't deceive yourself by casually dismissing these conditions.\n\nIt might be something as minor as beginning to question God's will, His goodness, or whether an answer would really be best for you. There are countless arguments of unbelief we've all heard over the years, as we saw in Lesson Twelve.\n\nWe must throw aside all these questionings, unsound reasonings, and unbelieving theories. We must stubbornly persist in simple, unwavering faith until the answer comes.\n\n---\n\n## Ten Questions For Supplement Sixteen. Do You Know:\n\n- **That we have plain revelations in Scripture of eternal conditions on Earth?** Many passages speak of eternal conditions on Earth. These will be fully explained in Lessons Forty-nine through Fifty-two, so we'll simply list a few references here. There will be an eternal kingdom (Dan. 2:44-45; 7:13, 14, 18; Isa. 9:6-7; Lk. 1:32, 33; Rev. 11:15); eternal generations of natural people (See Supplement Two, Question 2); and eternal natural conditions as we know them now—minus the curse and its effects. In other words, the natural conditions that would have continued if humanity had never fallen will continue in the New Earth forever (Gen. 8:22; Isa. 65:17; 66:22-24; 2 Pet. 3:13; Rev. 21-22).\n\n- **That ordinary angels do not have wings?** They've appeared to humans many times without wings—otherwise they would have been immediately recognized as angels (Heb. 13:2; Gen. 18; 19; Judg. 13:6-20; Mt. 28:2-5; Acts 1:11). Only Seraphim, Cherubim, and special classes of angels have wings, as explained in Lesson Six.\n\n- **That the theory of evolution cannot be reconciled with the biblical story of creation?** The "missing link" between humans and beasts has never been found—and never will be—because there is no link missing between them. God created humanity as a fully-grown, intelligent person in one act. In one day He made both humans and land animals as separate creatures and gave them the law of reproduction: everything after its own kind. That's how it has been ever since (Gen. 1:26-31; 2:7-10; 7:14). Throughout Scripture, humanity has always been treated as lord over all animals—the one responsible for any curse upon other creatures. There's simply no way to believe both the Bible and the nonsense of evolution at the same time. Evolution is not a proven fact in any realm—it's really "devilution," a deception designed to discount God and His wonderful works. If anyone can conceive of the vast universe evolving from nothing into the most intelligent human being, they have a seriously warped understanding. The world's greatest scholars have never believed in evolution, declaring it isn't even science—just a theory. So let's forget it once and for all and remain faithful to God's inspired revelation, the Bible. The following ode to a fly by Rev. Thomas H. Nelson, L. L. B., illustrates how foolish such a theory is: "Don't be discouraged, poor little fly, You'll be a chipmunk by and by. Ages later I can see, You'll be a full-grown chimpanzee. Next I see with a prophet's ken, You'll take a place in the ranks of men. And then in the great sweet by and by, We'll be angels—you and I. Why should I swat you, dear little fly? Prospective chum of my home on high. This is what Darwin says—not I."\n\n- **That the ten tribes of Israel were never lost any more than the two tribes?** All the tribes of Israel were scattered among the nations during the time that ten of them were supposedly "lost" (Lk. 21:24; Rom. 11). Paul, James, and others recognized this fact (Acts 26:7; Jas. 1:1; Rev. 7:1-8; Ezek. 37). All of them were "lost" spiritually in the same sense that Gentiles are lost (Mt. 10:5-6; 15:24). This theory will be fully discussed in Lesson Thirty-six.\n\n- **That Christ was crucified on Wednesday instead of Friday?** This is clear from the fact that He was fully three days and three nights in Hell while His body lay in the tomb (Mt. 12:40; Eph. 4:7-11; Ps. 16:10), and that He rose early the first day of the week—after sunset Saturday (Mt. 28:1; Mk. 16:1-2; Lk. 24:1-6; Jn. 20:1-10). If He had been buried on Friday before sunset, He would have been in the grave only one night and one day—making Jesus Himself a liar, since He said He would be there three days and three nights. This proves He was crucified on Wednesday and placed in the tomb before sunset that day. He remained dead Wednesday night, Thursday, Thursday night, Friday, Friday night, and Saturday. He was resurrected soon after sunset Saturday, for He had already risen before Sunday morning, as the passages above explain. Therefore, we should observe a "Good Wednesday" instead of a "Good Friday" festival.\n\n- **That capital punishment is scriptural?** Anyone can see this by reading Gen. 9:6; Exodus 20:13; 21:12, 23; Rom. 13:1-7. (See Lesson Thirteen.)\n\n- **That God the Father is coming visibly to the Earth at the second coming of Christ?** This is plainly stated in Dan. 7:9-14; Zech. 14:5; Titus 2:13. (See Supplement Seven, Question 10.)\n\n- **That there will be planting and harvesting on Earth forever?** This is clearly stated in Gen. 8:22 as continuing as long as the Earth exists—and that is forever (Eccl. 1:4; Ps. 104:5).\n\n- **That Syria and Israel will soon make a military alliance?** This is clear from the fact that Antichrist will come from Syria (See Supplement Eight, Question 10) and will make a seven-year covenant with Israel (Dan. 9:27). We'll explore this fully in Lesson Forty.\n\n- **That the Bible tells why the 144,000 Jews and the great multitude of Rev. 7 will not go up in the rapture of the Church before the future tribulation?** The reason is that not one person in these two groups will be saved at the time of the rapture—only the saved will go at that time (1 Thess. 4:16). See Supplement Six, Questions 6 and 10, and Supplement Seven, Question 2.\n\n---\n\nKnowing which kingdom and which covenant applies to a given passage isn't a scholarly luxury — it determines whether you're applying God's Word correctly or misapplying it. So here's the question: Think of a passage you've applied to yourself regularly from the Gospels. Have you ever checked whether it's addressed to the universal Kingdom of God, to Israel, or to the Church specifically? And does your answer change how you apply it?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk48j001jewhj564tv9cf	cmnknk48e000xewhj49ao5az0	Lesson 33 — The Biblical Doctrine of Salvation	READING	5529	21	\N	t	If you asked ten people on the street how a person gets saved, you'd get ten different answers — and most of them would have some basis in Scripture. That's not a sign that salvation is ambiguous. It's a sign that most people have only ever studied one piece of a multifaceted biblical teaching. Salvation in Scripture isn't one event. It's a comprehensive rescue operation involving the past, the present, and the future.\n\nLast time we examined the Old and New Covenants — and how the New Covenant transforms the basis of our relationship with God. Now we get to what that covenant provides: the full biblical doctrine of salvation.\n\nSeven different Hebrew and Greek words get translated as "salvation," appearing 388 times total. These same words are also translated using 23 other English words that mean "salvation" or some aspect of it. Here's the full list: "deliverance," "deliver," "delivered," "deliverers," "save," "saved," "savest," "saveth," "saving," "health," "help," "welfare," "safety," "victory," "safe," "Savior," "Saviors," "defend," "avenged," "avenging," "rescue," "preserved," and "preservest."\n\n![Lesson 33 — The Biblical Doctrine of Salvation](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsJIiU5p4ZDlMH1zAnxErj0PQYB2bIe8GdaVWL)\n\n## I. "Salvation" the All-Inclusive Gospel Word\n\nSalvation is the great, all-encompassing word of the gospel. It gathers together all the redemptive acts and processes—everything expressed in the doctrines we'll explore below.\n\nWhen you look at these words and the Scriptures that follow, you'll see that salvation teaches full deliverance from dangers, troubles, sin, disease, and poverty. Think of it like an umbrella that covers absolutely everything that threatens your wellbeing—nothing is left out in the rain.\n\nSalvation includes deliverance from every phase of sin, including inbred sin (or the old man) and the devil himself, as we'll see in Lessons Thirty-three and Thirty-four. It wouldn't be true deliverance from all sin if you were still bound by sin and Satan in any form. The following is a summary of the different words that express the different phases of salvation:\n\n1. **REPENTANCE**—A change of mind, a new mind about God (Mt. 3:2; 4:17; Lk. 13:1-5; 24:47; Acts 2:38; 3:19; 10:43; 2 Cor. 7:9, 10; 1 Jn. 1:9).\n\n2. **CONVERSION**—A change of direction, a new walk with God (Ps. 19:7; 51:13; Mt. 18:3; Lk. 22:32; Acts 3:19; Jas. 5:19).\n\n3. **REGENERATION**—A change of nature, a new nature before God (Jn. 3:3-5; Rom. 3:24, 25; 2 Cor. 5:17; Titus 3:5; 2 Pet. 1:4-14).\n\n4. **ADOPTION**—A change of family, a new relationship with God (Rom. 8:14-23; Gal. 4:5; Eph. 1:4; 3:15; Jn. 1:12; 3:16; 1 Jn. 3:8-10; 5:1-18).\n\n5. **WASHING**—A change of morals, a new creature before God (1 Cor. 6:9-11; Eph. 4:24; 5:26; Titus 3:5; Rev. 1:5; 7:14; Jn. 15:3; Isa. 1:16-18).\n\n6. **REDEMPTION**—A change of owners, a new master in God (Rom. 3:24, 25; 6:16-23; 1 Cor 1:30; 3:16; 6:19-20; Gal. 3:13; Eph. 1:7; Col. 1:14, 22; 1 Pet. 1:18-23).\n\n7. **PROPITIATION**—A change of clothing, a new covering of sins before God (Rom. 3:24, 25; 4:7; 1 Jn. 2:1, 2; Ps. 32:1; 85:2; 1 Pet. 4:8).\n\n8. **GRACE**—A change of favor, a new standing before God (Jn. 1:16, 17; Rom. 5:1, 2; Eph. 2:1-9; Titus 2:11-14; 2 Pet. 3:18).\n\n9. **IMPUTATION**—A change of accounts, a new debt to God (Rom. 1:14; 4:1-11, 22-24; Gal. 3:6-14; Jas. 2:23; 2 Cor. 5:17-19).\n\n10. **FORGIVENESS**—A change of judgment, a new pardon by God (Ps. 103:3; Eph. 1:7; Col. 2:13; 1 Jn. 1:7-9; Lk. 24:47; Acts 10:43).\n\n11. **DELIVERANCE**—A change of bondage, a new liberty before God (Lk. 4:18; Gal. 5:1, 13; Heb. 10:19; 2 Cor. 3:6-18; Jas. 1:18-25).\n\n12. **FAITH**—A change of confidence, a new trust in God (Mk. 9:23; 11:22-24; Rom. 1:16-17; 4:1-25; 10:9-17; Heb. 11; 12:1, 2; 2 Pet. 1:5).\n\n13. **BLOOD**—A change in life, a new life from God (Lev. 17:11; Mt. 26:28; Rom. 5:1-11; 6:1-23; 8:1-13; Eph. 1:7; 1 Jn. 1:7; Rev. 1:5; 5:8-10).\n\n14. **FORBEARANCE**—A change of attitude, a new patience in God (Eph. 4:2; 2 Tim. 2:24; Rom. 3:24, 25; 5:1-4; 15:4, 5; Col. 3:13; Jas. 1:3, 4).\n\n15. **RECONCILIATION**—A change of friendship, a new union with God (Rom. 5:10; Eph. 2:14-16; Col. 1:20, 21; 2 Cor. 5:17-20; Heb. 2:17; 1 Cor. 6:17).\n\n16. **SUBSTITUTION**—A change of victims, a new sacrifice to God (Gen. 22:8, 13; Isa. 53; Heb. 10:5-18; 1 Cor. 5:7; Rom. 12:1, 2; Heb. 13:15).\n\n17. **REPRESENTATION**—A change of agency, a new representative before God (Rom. 8:26, 27, 34; 1 Tim. 2:5; Heb. 2:18; 4:14-16; 5:5-10; 7:24-28; 9:1-28).\n\n18. **NEW BIRTH AND SONSHIP**—A change of parents, a new Father in God (Mt. 6:8, 9; Jn. 1:12; 3:1-18; Gal. 4:5, 6; Heb. 12:7; 1 Jn. 3:1-3; 5:1-5, 10-18).\n\n19. **HEIRSHIP**—A change of fortune, a new inheritance in God (Rom. 8:17; Gal. 3:29; Heb. 1:4; 9:15; Jas. 2:5; 1 Pet. 1:4; Eph. 1:11-18).\n\n20. **PRAYER**—A change of communication, a new approach to God (Mt. 6:9; 7:7-11; 21:22; Mk. 11:22-24; Jn. 14:12-15; 15:7, 16; 16:23-26; Heb. 10:19-23; 11:6; 1 Jn. 3:22; 5:14-16).\n\n21. **ELECTION**—A change of office, a new position in God (Mt. 20:16; Eph. 1:4; Rom. 8:33; 1 Pet. 1:2; 1 Thess. 1:4; 2 Pet. 1:10; Jn. 15:16).\n\n22. **RIGHTEOUSNESS**—A change of acts, a new obedience to God (Rom. 4:1-25; 6:11-22; 8:3; Mt. 6:33; 1 Jn. 2:29; 3:7, 10; 1 Cor. 1:30; Eph. 4:24).\n\n23. **CRUCIFIXION**—A change of death, a new subjection to God (Rom. 6:3-22; 8:13; Gal. 2:20; 5:24; 6:14; 2 Cor. 5:17; Col. 3:5-10).\n\n24. **RESTITUTION**—A change of dealings, a new honesty before God (Exod. 22:1-12; Lev. 6:4, 5; 24:21; Prov. 6:31; Lk. 3:8; 19:8).\n\n25. **RETRIBUTION**—A change of vengeance, a new attitude before God (Mt. 5:39; 7:12; Lk. 6:27-46; 9:51-55; Rom. 12:17-21; Heb. 10:30; 1 Cor. 6).\n\n26. **RANSOM**—A change from slavery, a new release by God (Job 33:24; Mt. 20:28; 1 Tim. 2:6; Gal. 5:1; 6:1; Rom. 6:3-6, 11-23; 8:1-13).\n\n27. **MORTIFICATION**—A change of practice, a new conduct before God (Rom. 8:13; Col. 3:1-17; Mk. 11:24; Mt. 5:6; 1 Jn. 4:19).\n\n28. **CONSECRATION**—A change of devotion, a new offering to God (Rom. 12:1; 2 Cor. 8:5; Acts 6:4; 1 Tim. 4:13-15; Heb. 13:15).\n\n29. **ASSURANCE**—A change of security, a New Covenant with God (Col. 2:2; 1 Thess. 1:5; Heb. 6:11; 10:19-23; 2 Tim. 3:14; 2 Pet. 1:4-14).\n\n30. **HUMILITY**—A change of spirit, a new temperament in God (Mt. 18:1-4; Acts 20:19; Col. 3:5-17; 1 Pet. 5:5, 6; Jas. 4:6-11).\n\n31. **LOVE**—A change of affection, a new devotion to God (Deut. 6:5; Mt. 22:37; Rom. 5:5-8; Jn. 13:34, 35; 1 Cor. 13; 1 Jn. 3:11-18; 4:7-21; Gal. 5:21, 22).\n\n32. **JOY**—A change in emotion, a new delight in God (Neh. 8:10; Ps. 16:11; 51:12; Lk. 19:37; 24:52; Jn. 15:11; 1 Pet. 1:8, 9; Gal. 5:21, 22).\n\n33. **PEACE**—A change in mind, a new tranquillity in God (Acts 10:36; Rom. 5:1; 14:17; 15:13; Eph. 2:14-18; Col. 1:20; Gal. 5:21, 22).\n\n34. **LONGSUFFERING**—A change in forbearance, a new endurance before God (Gal. 5:21, 22; Mk. 4:17; 2 Tim. 2:3; 4:5; Jas. 5:11; Rom. 5:1-8).\n\n35. **GENTLENESS**—A change in manner, a new behavior before God (Gal. 5:21, 22; 1 Thess. 2:7; 2 Tim. 2:24; Titus 3:2; Jas. 3:17).\n\n36. **GOODNESS**—A change in virtue, a new holiness in God (Gal. 5:21, 22; Rom. 15:14; Eph. 5:9; Phil. 4:8; 2 Pet. 1:3-14).\n\n37. **MEEKNESS**—A change of disposition, a new humility before God (Gal. 5:21, 22; Num. 12:3; Ps. 76:9; 149:4; Eph. 4:2; Col. 3:12; 1 Tim. 6:11).\n\n38. **TEMPERANCE**—A change to moderation, a new sobriety before God (Gal. 5:21, 22; Acts 24:25; 2 Pet. 1:3-14; 1 Cor. 9:25-27; Titus 1:8; 2:2).\n\n39. **REVELATION**—A change of knowledge, a new doctrine from God (Mt. 11:25; 13:35; 16:17; Deut. 29:29; Rom. 1:17; 1 Cor. 2:10; Eph. 1:17; 3:1-11).\n\n40. **CONVICTION**—A change in the sense of guilt, a new awakening before God (Jn. 8:9; 16:7-12; Acts 2:37; 18:28; 1 Cor. 14:24; Jas. 2:9).\n\n41. **REMISSION**—A change of sentence, a new justification by God (Mt. 26:28; Lk. 24:47; Acts 2:38; 10:43; Heb. 9:22).\n\n42. **HEALING**—A change in health, a new health in God (Ps. 91; 103:3; Mt. 8:17; Isa. 53; Rom. 8:11; 1 Pet. 2:24; 2 Jn. 2; Jas. 5:14-16).\n\n43. **SOUNDNESS**—A change of condition, a new perfection in God (Prov. 8:14; 14:30; Ps. 91; Col. 2:10; 2 Tim. 1:7; 1 Tim. 1:10; Titus 1:9, 13; 2:1-8).\n\n44. **CREATION AND TRANSFORMATION**—A change of likeness, a new image before God (2 Cor. 3:18; 5:17; Rom. 12:1, 2; 6:3-22; 8:1-13; 2 Pet. 1:3-14; Col. 3:1-17; Gal. 5:16-26).\n\n45. **SANCTIFICATION**—A change of service, a new separation unto God (Jn. 10:36; 1 Cor. 6:11; 2 Tim. 2:21; Exod. 13; 30:39; 40:10, 11; Lev. 27:14-27; Jer. 1:5).\n\n46. **JUSTIFICATION**—A change of state, a new righteousness in God (Rom. 3:20-30; 5:1, 9; 1 Cor. 6:11; Gal. 3:6-14; Acts 13:38, 39).\n\n47. **PRESERVATION**—A change of assurance, a new security in God (Ps. 37:28; 145:20; 2 Tim. 4:18; Phil. 1:6; 1 Thess. 5:23; Jude 20-24; 1 Pet. 1:5).\n\n48. **PROVIDENCE**—A change of supply, a new provision in God (Mt. 6:33; Phil 4:18; Mk. 11:22-24; Jn. 15:7, 16; Ps. 84:11).\n\n49. **BAPTISM INTO CHRIST**—A change of elements, a new burial in God (Eph. 2:1-9; Rom. 6:3-6; 1 Cor. 12:13; Gal. 3:27; 5:24; Col. 2:11-17).\n\n50. **RESURRECTION**—A change in substance, a new body given by God (1 Cor. 15:21-58; Phil. 3:20, 21; 1 Thess. 4:13-17; 1 Jn. 3:1-3; Jn. 5:28, 29).\n\n51. **PREDESTINATION**—A change of decree, a new destiny in God (Rom. 8:28-30; Eph. 1:5, 11; 3:10, 11; 1 Pet. 1:2-13; 2 Pet. 1:4-14; 2 Cor. 3:18).\n\n52. **FOREKNOWLEDGE**—A change in revelation, a new knowledge of God (Rom. 8:29; 11:2; Acts 2:23; 1 Pet. 1:2; Eph. 1:1-17; Mt. 11:25; Deut. 29:29).\n\n53. **FOREORDINATION**—A change in appointment, a new future in God (Rom. 3:25; Jn. 15:16; 1 Thess. 5:9; 1 Pet. 1:20; Eph. 1:10; 2:7; 3:10, 11).\n\n54. **POWER**—A change in authority, a new power in God (Lk. 24:49; Jn. 14:12-14; Mk. 16:15-20; Acts 1:8; 1 Cor. 12-14; Eph. 1:19; 3:19, 20; Heb. 2:3, 4; Mk. 11:22-24).\n\n55. **GLORIFICATION**—A change of realm, a new dominion with God (Rom. 8:17, 30; Acts 3:13; Phil. 3:21; 1 Cor. 15:21-58; Rev. 5:10; 20:1-7).\n\n56. **DESTINATION**—A change of dwelling, a new home with God (Jn. 14:1-3; Heb. 11:10, 13-16; 13:14; Rev. 3:12; 21:1–22:5).\n\n57. **HOLINESS**—A change in living, a new conformity to God (Rom. 6:16-23; 8:1-13; 12:1, 2; 11:16; 1 Cor. 3:16-17; 6:19, 20; 2 Cor. 5:17; 7:1; Heb. 12:14; Rev. 20:4-6).\n\n58. **LIFE**—a change of existence, a new eternal life with God (Jn. 3:16-20, 36; 5:24; 17:2, 3; 1 Jn. 2:24, 25; 5:11, 12; 1 Thess. 4:13-17).\n\n59. **MERCY**—A change of heart, a new disposition in God (Mt. 5:7; Rom. 12:8-19; Jas. 2:13; Lk. 6:36; Phil. 2:1; Col. 3:12).\n\n60. **INSPIRATION**—A change of thoughts, a new influence from God (1 Cor. 2:9-14; 2 Tim. 3:15-17; Jn. 14:16, 17, 26; 15:26; 16:7-15; 1 Jn. 2:27).\n\nNow here's something really important to understand: not one of these phases of salvation should be thought of as a separate work from the salvation they're part of. You couldn't possibly receive salvation without having every single one of these blessings started in you that has to do with sin.\n\nThey all have both an initial, instantaneous aspect and a progressive aspect until they're completed in the eternal future.\n\nAlthough salvation and all its various phases that deal with sin are received in their initial stages by faith when you truly repent and believe the gospel, the different phases continue operating in you from the moment of salvation until you receive and enter into the final stage of salvation at the rapture and resurrection (1 Pet. 1:5-9, 13; Rom. 13:11; Heb. 1:14; Phil. 3:20, 21; 1 Thess. 4:13-17; 5:23; 1 Cor. 15:51-58; 1 Jn. 3:1-3).\n\nSalvation, in its general sense, even includes the redemption of all creation at the end of the Millennium (Rom. 8:17-25; Heb. 1:10-12; 12:25-28; 2 Pet. 3:10-13; Rev. 21-22; Isa. 66:22-24). If salvation takes in all creation affected by the Fall, then it's easy to see how it encompasses every phase of deliverance for mankind in the process of redemption from beginning to completion.\n\n## II. Righteousness of God (Rom. 3:21-23)\n\nThe word "righteousness" (Greek, *dikaiosune*) occurs 92 times in the New Testament and 36 times in Romans alone. The Greek word *dikaioma* means "a righteous ordinance, a decree of acquittal," and is translated "righteousness" (Rom. 2:26; 5:18; 8:4; Rev. 19:8); "ordinance" (Lk. 1:6; Heb. 9:1, 10); "judgment" (Rom. 1:32; Rev. 15:4); and "justification" (Rom. 5:16).\n\nRighteousness in the Bible means:\n\n> 1. The perfection of the divine nature, whereby God is most just and holy in Himself, and in all His dealings with His creatures (Jn. 17:25).\n\n> 2. The clemency, mercy, and goodness of God (Deut. 2:25; Ps. 51:14).\n\n> 3. The faithfulness of God to His promises (Ps. 31:1; Isa. 42:6; 1 Cor. 10:12-13).\n\n> 4. "The righteousness of Christ" is the active and passive obedience of Christ, whereby He perfectly fulfilled the law, and propitiated the justice of God so that man could obey God and receive the righteousness which is by faith, and their persons be accepted of God and received to eternal glory (Dan. 9:24; Jer. 23:6; 1 Cor. 1:30).\n\n> 5. Uprightness and justice in dealing between men (Lk. 1:75).\n\n> 6. Holiness of life and conversation (1 Cor. 15:34).\n\n> 7. "The righteousness of God" is that righteousness which has been fully met and maintained in the atoning work of Christ on the cross, by which God is now able to save sinners and still be righteous in doing so (Rom. 1:17; 3:5, 21-22; 10:3). Under the law God required righteousness from man, but the law, instead of giving it to many, only demonstrated that he was destitute of it and needed God's righteousness, which He now freely gives under Grace.\n\nThe following points are different phases of the righteousness of God and help us understand it:\n\n> (1) His righteousness is revealed in the gospel (Rom. 1:17).\n\n> (2) It is not commended by unrighteousness (Rom. 3:5).\n\n> (3) It is manifested wholly apart from the law (Rom. 3:21).\n\n> (4) It is witnessed by the law and the prophets (Rom. 3:21).\n\n> (5) It is imputed by faith apart from works (Rom. 3:22; 4:3, 22).\n\n> (6) It is for all who believe (Rom. 3:22).\n\n> (7) It is freely given to us through redemption (Rom. 3:24-26).\n\n> (8) It is the righteousness of faith because it is apprehended and applied by faith in Christ and His work (Rom. 4:5, 11, 13; 9:30; 10:6).\n\n> (9) It is the righteousness of faith because all the righteousness of God, which He required in order to redeem man, was fully met by Christ and now God is righteous in justifying all who believe (Rom. 5:18).\n\n> (10) It is the medium of grace unto eternal life by Christ (Rom. 5:21).\n\n> (11) It is the controlling factor in the lives of saints (Rom. 6:13, 16, 18-20).\n\n> (12) It brings eternal life to all who believe (Rom. 8:10).\n\n> (13) It is the righteousness which God required under law but gives under grace (Rom. 8:4; 9:31-32; 10:5-6).\n\n> (14) Conditions for receiving righteousness (Rom. 3:22; 4:1-5, 11; 6:13-19; 8:1-10; 10:10). You become righteous in God by faith in Christ.\n\nOther than the righteousness of God, there is self-righteousness which prides itself in being righteous through self-effort (Rom. 10:3; Phil. 3:3-9; Isa. 64:6; Lk. 18:9-14; Mt. 23).\n\n## III. Atonement or At-one-ment (Lev. 17:11)\n\nThe words "atonement" and "blood" are used 523 times in the Bible. The following points reveal the blessings of the blood of Jesus, which come through faith:\n\n> 1. Remission of sins (Rom. 3:24, 25; Heb. 9:22; Eph. 1:7; 1 Jn. 1:7).\n\n> 2. Salvation from wrath (Exod. 12:13, 23; Rom. 5:9-11; 1 Thess. 5:9-10).\n\n> 3. Deliverance from Hell (Zech. 9:11; Rom. 5:1-11; Rev. 5:8-10).\n\n> 4. Sanctification (Heb. 2:11; 10:14, 29; 13:12).\n\n> 5. Justification (Rom. 3:24-30; 5:9-11; Acts 13:38-39; Gal. 2:16-17; Titus 3:4-7).\n\n> 6. Citizenship (Eph. 2:12, 13; Phil. 3:20-21; Gal. 3:27-29).\n\n> 7. Peace with God (Rom. 5:1; Col. 1:20).\n\n> 8. Purging of the conscience (Heb. 9:14).\n\n> 9. Redemption (Rom. 3:24, 25; Eph. 1:7; Heb. 9:15; 1 Pet. 1:18-23; Rev. 5:8-10).\n\n> 10. High priestly benefits (Heb. 4:14-16; 9:21; 10:1-18).\n\n> 11. Access to God (Heb. 10:19; Eph. 2:14-18).\n\n> 12. Power to become the elect of God (1 Pet. 1:2).\n\n> 13. Washing from sins (Rev. 1:5; 7:14; 1 Jn. 1:7-9; Eph. 1:7).\n\n> 14. Atonement for the soul (Lev. 17:11; Rom. 5:11).\n\n> 15. Life eternal, abundant life (Rom. 5:1-11; Col. 1:20).\n\n> 16. A New Covenant (Mt. 26:28).\n\n> 17. The new birth (1 Pet. 1:18-23).\n\n> 18. Righteousness (Rev. 7:14; 19:8).\n\n> 19. Overcoming power (Rev. 12:11).\n\n> 20. Membership in the body of Christ (Acts 20:28).\n\n> 21. Salvation (Ps. 51; Acts 4:12; Eph. 1:7).\n\n### Many Scriptures Proving Unlimited Atonement\n\nThe Bible does not teach a limited atonement. What we mean by this is that salvation is provided for all human beings alike, and on the same basis. This is clear from many statements in the Bible itself:\n\n1. God told Cain that if he did well and would offer the right sacrifice, he also would be accepted, the same as his brother, Abel (Gen. 4:7). There was no difference between these two or any others when it came to God's requirements for atonement.\n\n2. The sprinkling of blood was required of every Israelite in Egypt who wanted protection—and this was on a voluntary basis (Exod. 12).\n\n3. Every man in Israel, without exception, was under contract with God to offer blood atonement, and this also was on a voluntary basis—from Moses to Christ (Exod. 24:5-8; Lev. 1:2-4; Heb. 9:22). The same was true of any Gentile who became a Jewish proselyte (Exod. 12:49; Lev. 24:22; Num. 15:15-16, 29).\n\n4. Atonement was made for every person and for every sin, disease, and corruption among men, under the law (Lev. 12:6-8; 14:53-57). Proper atonement was always required of any and every man and woman, Jew or Gentile, who chose to follow God. Not once was any person of the human race turned down by God or told that atonement was not for him. In the thirty-nine books of the Old Testament there is not a single statement that even hints of a limited atonement or of an atonement for a special few of the human race, instead of all men.\n\n5. In prophecy and in promise, atonement has been referred to as being for all men alike, Jews and Gentiles (Isa. 1:16-20; 11:10; 42:1, 6; 49:6, 22; 60:3-16; 61:9; 62:2; Mal. 1:11).\n\n6. The God who accused the whole race of going astray from Him said that He had laid on Christ the Messiah "the iniquity of us all" (Isa. 53:6).\n\n7. This applies to all men alike: "Thou shalt call his name Jesus; for he shall save his people from their sins" (Mt. 1:21). It's clear from God's promises that "whosoever\n\n---\n\nSalvation is past (justification — you have been saved from sin's penalty), present (sanctification — you are being saved from sin's power), and future (glorification — you will be saved from sin's presence). Most Christians are clear on the first and vague on the second and third. So here's the question: Which of the three tenses of salvation is most real in your daily experience right now? And which one are you treating as theoretical rather than something God is actively working in your life today?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk48j001kewhjzf8lfyyb	cmnknk48e000xewhj49ao5az0	Lesson 34 — The Truth About Sanctification and Justification	READING	6726	22	\N	t	Two of the most important words in the Christian vocabulary are used constantly and understood rarely. Justification and sanctification — ask most believers to define either one in plain English and you'll get a blank stare or a confident answer that's half-right. Getting these wrong doesn't just produce theological confusion. It produces people who don't know whether they're accepted by God, and people who don't know whether they're expected to grow.\n\nLast time we laid out the full scope of salvation — past, present, and future. Now we go deeper into two of the most foundational components: what justification accomplishes for your standing before God and what sanctification accomplishes in your day-to-day character.\n\nLet's be honest — sanctification is one of those Bible topics that gets people tangled up! The Christian world is pretty divided on it, and that's exactly why every thoughtful believer should dig into Scripture with an open mind. So here's my invitation to you: set aside any preconceptions you might have and let's honestly examine what the Bible actually says. What follows comes from looking at over 25,000 separate statements in Scripture.\n\n![Lesson 34 — The Truth About Sanctification and Justification](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OskNyTETX8rZcMaXYe27SivEmH1t8Ad0OozjUK)\n\n### 1. The Fact and Usage of the Doctrine of Sanctification\n\nSanctification is definitely taught in Scripture — there's no question about that. The word "sanctification" itself only appears five times, but "sanctified" shows up 62 times, "sanctify" appears 70 times, and "sanctifieth" is used four times. That's 141 occurrences total — 110 in the Old Testament and 31 in the New Testament.\n\nHere's something interesting: this doctrine is mentioned about four times more often in the Old Testament than in the New. The same pattern holds for related words like "holy," "holiness," "clean," and "pure."\n\n### 2. The Different Material or Inanimate Things Sanctified\n\nHere's where things get really interesting. When you look at everything the Bible calls "sanctified," you quickly realize this can't just be about removing sin from people. Think about it — how do you remove sin from a building or a piece of bread?\n\nScripture tells us these things were sanctified:\n- "The seventh day" (Gen. 2:3)\n- "The tabernacle and its furniture" (Exod. 29:43–30:29; Lev. 8:10-15)\n- "The priest's garments" (Lev. 8:30)\n- "Houses, fields and other property" (Lev. 27:9-29)\n- "Bread" (1 Sam. 21:5)\n- "The temple of Solomon and its furniture" (2 Chron. 7:16-29; 29:17-19)\n- "The gates of Jerusalem" (Neh. 3:1)\n- "Mt. Sinai" (Exod. 19:23)\n- "The offerings" (Exod. 29:27)\n- "God's name" (Isa. 29:23; Ezek. 36:23)\n- "Daily food" (1 Tim. 4:5)\n- "Herod's temple" (Mt. 23:17)\n\nIn every one of these passages, the idea isn't about removing some "old man" or dealing with sin. It's about setting something apart for sacred purposes — taking it from ordinary use and dedicating it to God.\n\n### 3. Examples of Persons Sanctified\n\nThe different people who were sanctified in both Testaments make the same point even clearer. Sanctification wasn't primarily about extracting an "old man" or sin from someone. It was about "setting apart to a sacred purpose or work," "hallowing," "holding as sacred," and "consecrating to accomplish the divine will." If cleansing from sin was necessary for any person being sanctified, that was just a secondary part of the main purpose.\n\nLook at this list:\n- "Israel as a nation" (Exod. 19:14-15)\n- "Individuals and families" (1 Sam. 16:5)\n- "Heathen soldiers" (Isa. 13:3, 17)\n- "Prophets" (Jer. 1:5)\n- "Priests" (Lev. 8:30)\n- "The first-born of Israel" (Exod. 13:2; Num. 8:17; Deut. 15:19)\n- "Unsaved companions" (1 Cor. 7:14)\n- "Christ and the disciples" (Jn. 10:36; 17:17-19)\n- "God the Father" (Lev. 10:3; Num. 20:13; Isa. 5:16; Ezek. 20:41; 28:22, 25; 36:23; 38:16; 39:27)\n\nNow think about this: those heathen soldiers were never cleansed from sin as far as we know — they were simply set apart as God's instrument for disciplining Babylon. Jeremiah was sanctified before he was even born in sin. Both Christ and God the Father were sanctified, yet neither of them had any sin in them!\n\nThis shows us clearly that people can be sanctified without sin being part of the picture at all. John the Baptist was sanctified before birth because he was filled with the Spirit from that moment (Lk. 1:15). And here's an important principle: one must be sanctified before being filled with the Spirit (Jn. 14:17).\n\n### 4. Various Ways Sanctification is Used in Scripture\n\nThe different ways sanctification is used throughout both Testaments prove that the meaning isn't about cleansing from sin or removing the "old man" from someone.\n\n**Sanctification is Associated With God the Father**\n\n> (1) It is used of God in setting apart inanimate things from a profane or secular to a sacred and divine use, as seen in Point 3, above.\n\n> (2) It is used of God in setting apart men to execute His vengeance upon Babylon, and this was done without any reference to cleansing from sin (Isa. 13:3, 17).\n\n> (3) It is used of God in setting apart men from a sinful life to a life of holiness and divine service. (See Point II, below.)\n\n> (4) It is used of God to manifest His glory and vindicate Himself (Ezek. 36:23).\n\n> (5) It is used of God making food holy for natural use (1 Tim. 4:4, 5; Rom. 14:1-23; Col. 2:14-17).\n\n> (6) It is used of God in consecrating His Son to fulfill a mission in the world and to die for all men in order to redeem them (Jn. 10:36).\n\n> (7) It is used of God in manifesting His will concerning sin (1 Thess. 4:3, 4).\n\n> (8) It is used of God in manifesting His choice (2 Thess. 2:13; 1 Pet. 1:2).\n\n> (9) It is used of God as an agent in sanctification (Jude 1).\n\n> (10) It is used of God as the source of sanctification (Jn. 10:36; 17:17-19; 1 Thess. 5:23; 1 Cor. 1:30).\n\n**Sanctification is Associated With Jesus Christ**\n\n> (1) Christ sanctified Himself (Jn. 17:17-19).\n\n> (2) Christ, and not some experience, is our sanctification (1 Cor. 1:30).\n\n> (3) Christ sanctifies the Church with the Word (Eph. 5:25, 26).\n\n> (4) Christ sanctifies sinners by His own blood (Heb. 9:13-15; 10:10-14; 13:12).\n\n> (5) Christ was sanctified by God (Jn. 10:36).\n\n**Sanctification is Associated With the Holy Spirit**\n\n> (1) He sanctifies sinners by the blood (1 Pet. 1:2, 18-23; Heb. 10:29).\n\n> (2) He sanctifies sinners by the Word (Jn. 3:5; 15:3; 1 Pet. 1:23; Jas. 1:18-25; 2 Thess. 2:13).\n\n> (3) He sanctifies men by the name of Jesus (1 Cor. 6:9-11).\n\n> (4) He sanctifies by His own power (Rom. 8:1-13; Jn. 3:5).\n\n**Sanctification is Associated With Man**\n\n> (1) Man can sanctify himself; that is, set himself apart from sin to God (Lev. 11:44; 20:7, 8; 2 Cor. 7:1; Jas. 4:8-10).\n\n> (2) Man can set himself apart to see God's works (Exod. 19:9-22; Josh. 3:5).\n\n> (3) Man can sanctify God and His name; that is, hold them in reverence and as sacred (Isa. 29:23; Ezek. 36:23; Mt. 6:9; 22:37; 1 Pet. 3:15).\n\n> (4) Man can keep himself sanctified by divine means (1 Thess. 4:3, 4).\n\n### 5. The Two-Fold Meaning of Sanctification\n\nThe Hebrew and Greek words for "sanctification" carry rich meaning: "to make or pronounce clean or holy, morally, physically, and ceremonially," "to consecrate, dedicate, hallow," "to purify," "to set apart from a profane or secular use to a sacred or holy use," "to separate from carnal and natural to spiritual purposes," and "to venerate or reverence."\n\n> (1) Separation from an evil, profane or secular purpose to a sacred purpose; to make or to be holy, reverent, sacred, separated, pure, hallowed; to prepare for spiritual and sacred use, to cleanse from sin and uncleanness and make free from manifestations and uses of the natural and carnal life; to make holy anything consecrated to God; the state of being holy and consecrated to God; and the devotion of a person or thing to a particular, spiritual exercise and use.\n\n> (2) Separation, dedication or consecration unto God. This includes any person or material thing that can be given entirely over to God for sacred and spiritual use, anything set apart for service unto God. Thus, the two-fold meaning of sanctification in one statement is any person or thing separated from a profane or secular use, and consecrated unto God to be used wholly for divine and spiritual use, whether sin is involved or not.\n\n### 6. The Threefold Moral Sanctification of Man\n\n> (1) **Past or instantaneous sanctification.** This refers to the initial, definite act of making a person holy. Both God and man have parts in this act. Man must first consecrate or sanctify himself to God, and then God makes him holy by the various means of the gospel, as we shall see.\n\n> (2) **Present or progressive sanctification.** This refers to the state of being sanctified, of growth to maturity, and of conformity to the image of Christ in all the principles of holiness that have been imparted in the initial act of making holy. The work of sanctification by God or man could not end with one act of being made holy, for if the person is made holy and does not continue in this state, he could not be considered as sanctified. There must be the state of holiness as well as the act of making holy. If there is a definite act of making holy, there must also be a definite process to keep holy.\n\n> Think of it like breeding prize-winning birds — if you neglect them after developing them to a fine breed, they'll revert back to their original state. Or picture a garden: you can plant it and pull every weed, but neglect it and it'll become a desolate mess again. It's the same with our spiritual lives. If you neglect keeping holy a man who has been made holy and given the principles of holiness, he'll slide back into a sinful life. After being sanctified, we must keep ourselves sanctified — hence the need for a daily process to maintain what has been established. Both God and man have roles in this, as we'll see.\n\n> (3) **Future or complete sanctification.** This refers to the final act and process of being made holy forever in body, soul and spirit, and preserved blameless forever. It's plainly evident that no one made holy here is made absolutely and eternally holy by one act. There must be the process of keeping holy until one is made "whole" in body, soul, and spirit at the rapture, the resurrection, and entrance into the eternal state.\n\n---\n\nThese six points make clear the meaning, time, place, extent, process, standing, purpose, and use of a person or thing sanctified or separated from a profane to a sacred use. Every scripture in both Testaments fits perfectly with these facts.\n\nSo here's our conclusion: sanctification cannot be limited to dealing with sin in a person. A holy person can sanctify himself more and more, and anything he possesses can also be sanctified to God for His use. The main idea of sanctification is setting apart any person or thing from profane and secular use to spiritual and holy use — and sin may or may not be involved.\n\nIf the person to be sanctified is sinful, then and only then would sin be involved. But when we're talking about houses, fields, material things, a holy person already saved from sin, or Christ and God themselves — there's no sin involved and no "old man" taken out. When God the Father is spoken of as being sanctified, it simply means "to reverence." When Christ is sanctified, it means "to set apart for God's service."\n\n## II. When Were Men in the Old Testament Sanctified?\n\n1. **When they were separated from the womb:** "Sanctify unto me all the firstborn . . . both man and beast: it is mine . . . thou shalt set apart [sanctify] unto the Lord all that openeth the matrix" (Exod. 13:2, 12).\n\n2. **When they were chosen before birth:** "Before I formed thee in the belly I knew thee; and before thou camest forth out of the womb I sanctified thee, and I ordained thee to be a prophet unto the nations" (Jer. 1:5).\n\n3. **When they prepared to worship God:** "Go unto the people, and sanctify them today and tomorrow . . . be ready against the third day for the third day the Lord will come down in the midst of the people" (Exod. 19:10, 11, 14, 15, 22).\n\n4. **When they were set apart for ministry** (they were sanctified before, as in Point 3 above): "Thou shalt anoint them [Aaron and sons by Moses], and consecrate them, and sanctify them, that they may minister unto me in the priests' office" (Exod. 28:41).\n\n5. **When they ate the atonement flesh and bread:** "Aaron and his sons shall eat those things wherewith the atonement was made, to consecrate and to sanctify them" (thus they were sanctified daily, Exod. 29:32, 33).\n\n6. **When they were "anointed" and had "the blood" applied:** "Moses took the anointing oil, and of the blood which was upon the altar, and sprinkled it upon Aaron . . . and upon his sons . . . and sanctified them" (Lev. 8:30; Heb. 9:21, 22). Notice: there was never a time gap between two applications of the blood upon any person or thing being sanctified.\n\n7. **When they were made holy:** "Sanctify yourselves, and ye shall be holy; for I am holy" (Lev. 11:44; 20:7, 8). This proves that Old Testament saints were morally sanctified and holy like God (See Lesson Eighteen, Point IX).\n\n8. **When they were to see miracles:** "Sanctify yourselves: for tomorrow the Lord will do wonders among you" (Josh. 3:5).\n\n9. **When they had sinned:** "Sanctify yourselves against tomorrow . . . there is an accursed thing in the midst of thee" (Josh. 7:13). Sanctification had to happen every time men sinned, so there's no such thing as "once sanctified, always sanctified."\n\n10. **When a new priest was consecrated:** "The men . . . sanctified Eleazar his son to keep the ark of the Lord" (1 Sam. 7:1).\n\n11. **When sacrifices were offered:** "I am come to sacrifice to the Lord . . . sanctify yourselves and come with me to the sacrifice. And he [Samuel] sanctified Jesse and his sons, and called them to the sacrifice" (1 Sam. 16:2, 5).\n\n12. **When the ark was brought back to Jerusalem:** "The priests and the Levites sanctified themselves to bring up the ark of the Lord" (1 Chron. 15:12-15).\n\n13. **When revival was needed:** "And they gathered their brethren and sanctified themselves" (2 Chron. 29:5, 15-19, 34; 30:3, 15-17, 24; 31:18; 35:6).\n\n14. **When there was fear of sin:** "Job sent and sanctified them. . . . for Job said, It may be that my sons have sinned, and cursed God in their hearts. Thus did Job continually" (Job 1:5).\n\nIt's crystal clear from all these statements that sanctification isn't just one or two acts — it's a constant separation from sinful and profane uses to holy uses.\n\n## III. When Does Sanctification Begin According to the New Testament?\n\nSanctification began in God's plan in eternity past (Eph. 1:4). But in each person's actual experience, if sanctification means separation from a life of sin to a life of holiness, then it naturally begins — from our viewpoint — when we turn to God to be made holy, and from God's viewpoint when He makes us holy. You have to be made holy before you can continue in holiness throughout your life. Sanctification begins with these experiences:\n\n1. **When you get salvation:** "God hath from the beginning chosen you to salvation through sanctification of the Spirit and belief of the truth" (2 Thess. 2:13). What could be clearer about when you're sanctified? You can't be saved or get salvation except "through sanctification of the Spirit" — and this happens when you believe the truth. Zacharias, speaking by the Holy Ghost, said people would serve God "in holiness and righteousness" from the time they're "redeemed," get "salvation," have "remission of sins," and are "saved" (Lk. 1:67-77).\n\nThe word "salvation" is the all-inclusive word of the gospel, as we've seen in Lesson Thirty-three, Point I, especially the last three paragraphs after Point 60. Sanctification is part of salvation and should never be understood as a separate work of grace apart from it. Christ is our salvation (Lk. 2:30) and sanctification (1 Cor. 1:30), and you can't have Christ without having both. You'd have just as much right to take any other phase of salvation and make it a separate work of grace from salvation as you would to do this with sanctification.\n\n2. **When you receive Christ who is your sanctification** (1 Cor. 1:30; Jn. 1:12; 1 Jn. 2:29; 3:5-10; 5:1-5, 18). Sanctification comes from receiving a person more than getting an experience separate from that person.\n\n3. **When you are born-again.** "Every one that doeth righteousness is born of him . . . he that doeth righteousness is righteous, even as he is righteous . . . Whosoever is born of God doth not commit sin . . . he cannot sin, because he is born of God . . . everyone that loveth is born of God, and knoweth God . . . Whosoever believeth that Jesus is the Christ is born of God . . . Whatsoever is born of God overcometh the world . . . he sinneth not; but he that is begotten of God keepeth himself, and that wicked one [the old man, the devil] toucheth him not" (1 Jn. 2:29; 3:7-10; 4:7, 17; 5:1-5, 18).\n\nIf these benefits start at the new birth, they couldn't begin months or years afterward. If you had to wait until some indefinite time after the new birth to start acting in righteousness, cease from sin, love, know God, overcome the world, and keep yourself from that wicked one (so the old man can't touch you), then you'd be a sinner until that point after being born-again — and the new birth would mean nothing to you.\n\nPeter also taught that everyone born-again is "holy": "Be ye holy; for I am holy. . . . Forasmuch as ye know ye were not redeemed with corruptible things . . . but with the precious blood of Christ . . . Seeing ye have purified your souls in obeying the truth . . . see that ye love one another with a pure heart fervently: Being born again . . . by the word of God" (1 Pet. 1:15-23). You're made holy, redeemed, purified, and love with a pure heart when born-again, or this Scripture isn't true. Heart-purity comes at the new birth and redemption, so it couldn't come later. We're born-again by the blood and by the Word of God — and that's exactly how we're sanctified (Eph. 5:26; Heb. 13:12). Sanctification and the new birth happen at the same time by the same means.\n\nSome holiness groups believe people are made holy at the new birth, yet they constantly contradict themselves by saying people aren't made holy until they're sanctified sometime after the new birth. If the Scriptures above are true and you're made holy in the new birth (to the extent that you don't commit sin), then you're holy to the fullest extent of living free from the old man, the devil — and this is all that ever needs to be done. This is all God required of Adam before he fell.\n\nThe reason the born-again person doesn't commit sin is because he's not "of the devil" (1 Jn. 3:8), and he "keepeth himself, and that wicked one toucheth him not." Therefore, he couldn't have an old man (1 Jn. 5:18). We've seen in Lesson Twenty-three that the old man is the spirit and nature of the devil in people, so if the "born-again one" is free from the devil to the extent that he's not touched by him, then he's as free from the old man as he'll ever be. All he has to do is stay free from the old man as Paul commanded in Col. 2:6-7 and walk in the light as John taught in 1 Jn. 1:7; 2:1, 3-6, 9-11, 29; 3:3-10, 20-24; 4:17; 5:1-5, 18.\n\nEvery person in Christ is a new creature (2 Cor. 5:17), holy and free from sin — and they must walk in the Spirit to remain free (Rom. 6:14-23; 8:1-13; Gal. 5:16-26; Col. 3:5-17). If they stay free (which they may do at all times), they never yield to the old man any more than anyone else who claims freedom from sin through other experiences.\n\nJohn Wesley himself, the founder of "the second-work theory," taught that sanctification begins at the new birth. Here's what he wrote in Page 109, in "Sermons," Vol. 1 of The Works of Rev. John Wesley:\n\n> "We allow that the state of the justified person is inexpressibly great and glorious. He is born again . . . he is a child of God, a member of Christ, an heir of the kingdom . . . peace keepeth his heart and mind in Christ Jesus. His very body is the temple of the Holy Ghost . . . He is created anew in Christ Jesus; he is washed, he is sanctified. His heart is purified by faith. He is cleansed from all corruption that is in the world; the love of God is shed abroad in his heart by the Holy Ghost which is given him. As long as he walketh in love (which he may always do), he worships God in spirit and in truth. He keepeth the commandments of God, and doeth those things that are pleasing in his sight; so exercising himself as to have a conscience void of offence towards God and towards man; and he has power both over outward and inward sin, even from the moment he is justified."\n\nWhat could be clearer about when sanctification begins? Any other idea is entirely out of harmony with the Bible. Whatever Wesley taught after outlining these early convictions, he never came any closer to biblical truth than in these statements.\n\nWe're not arguing that sanctification ends at the new birth, but we must be honest with God and His Word and teach that it begins there. People will constantly need deeper works of grace as they conform to truth and walk in the light. This is just as true for those who claim a special second work of grace as for those who don't. To limit sanctification to one, two, or even three works of grace misses the mark and shows ignorance of the whole truth — it limits a person to a shallow, stationary experience in God.\n\nAfter all, even those who limit sanctification this way are constantly deepening in grace and power if they're normal Christians. Limiting the constant work of sanctification to some human theory is simply an error of the mortal mind. The Christian development actually happening in these people's lives is a constant rebuke and testimony against such a theory.\n\n4. **When you become God's "elect":** "Elect . . . through sanctification of the Spirit [the new birth by the Spirit], unto obedience and sprinkling of the blood of Jesus Christ . . . which according to his abundant mercy hath begotten us again unto a lively hope" (1 Pet. 1:2-4; Eph. 1:7; Rom. 8:33; Col. 3:12).\n\n5. **When you are made "righteous, even as he is righteous"** — at the new birth (1 Jn. 2:29; 3:7-10; 5:1-5, 18; 2 Cor. 5:17; Eph. 4:24; Gal. 5:24; Rom. 8).\n\n6. **When you "are created in righteousness and true holiness"** — when you get in Christ (2 Cor. 5:17; Eph. 4:24; Gal. 5:24).\n\n7. **When you "put off" the old man and "put on" the new man**, "which after God is created in righteousness and true holiness" (Eph. 2:8-10; 4:22-24; Rom. 6:3-23; 8:1-16; 2 Cor. 5:17; Gal. 5:24).\n\n8. **When you become "dead to sin"** and are "raised up from the dead" to start walking "in newness of life" (Rom. 6:2-22; Eph. 2:1-10). This happens when you're "saved" and "created in Christ" (Eph. 4:22-24; 2 Cor. 5:17-18).\n\n9. **When you are "baptized into Jesus Christ"** and into his body (Rom. 6:2-7; 1 Cor. 12:13; Gal. 3:27-29). This happens when we become the "children of God by faith in Jesus Christ" (Gal. 3:26-29. See the Three Baptisms on the chart, in the Age of Grace).\n\n10. **When you cease to live longer in sin** (Rom. 6:2-7; 8:1-13). This happens at the new birth (1 Jn. 2:29; 3:7-10; 5:1-5, 18; 2 Cor. 5:1).\n\n11. **When you truly reckon the "old man" dead and the "new man" alive** through Jesus Christ (Rom. 6:6-22). This happens when you're created in Christ (Eph. 4:22-24; 2 Cor. 5:17; 1 Jn. 5:1-5, 18).\n\n12. **When you refuse to let "sin reign in the mortal body"** (Rom. 6:12-22). You do this at the new birth (1 Jn. 3:5-10; 5:1-5, 18; Eph. 4:24; 2 Cor. 5:17).\n\n13. **When you quit yielding your members as instruments of unrighteousness unto sin** — this is at the new birth (Rom. 6:11-22; 1 Jn. 3:8-10; 5:1-18).\n\n14. **When you recognize you're free from one master** (the old man, the devil) and are a servant to righteousness and the new master — God (Rom. 6:14-22). Paul here states that acts of obedience indicate what master is being served (Rom. 6:16), that a change in acts of obedience indicates a change in masters (Rom. 6:17-18), that the one served indicates the nature of obedience (Rom. 6:19-20), and that the nature of obedience indicates the wages received (Rom. 6:21-23). You can't serve God and Satan at the same time, but you can serve both, one at a time (Mt. 6:24). Hence the old man or the devil is not in a saved person (1 Jn. 5:18).\n\n15. **When you are made free from the law of sin and death** (Rom. 8:1-4, 12-13; Gal. 5:16-26). Paul illustrates freedom from sin by the marriage of a man and woman who are bound to each other until one dies. Only on the death of one is the other free to marry again (Rom. 7:1-6).\n\nIt's similar to how a widow becomes legally free to remarry only after her husband dies. By the same token, when you become free from sin by the crucifixion and death of the old man, you're then free to marry Christ and enter the new relationship with God under the New Covenant. To claim that the old man isn't destroyed until sometime after the new birth is to compare both Christ and us to adulterers — which would mean an illegal marriage before the old man is dead.\n\nWe're freed from sin at the new birth, so that's when we're sanctified and free to marry Christ. If the old man isn't destroyed at the new birth when you marry Christ, then Christ marries someone still married to the devil, who has the devil in them, who is still unclean and lives in sin. This cannot be, for "the temple of God is holy" and "he that is joined to the Lord is one spirit" (1 Cor. 3:16-17; 6:17-20; Rom. 11:16).\n\n16. **When you receive the spirit of adoption** (Rom. 8:1-17). This is at redemption and forgiveness of sins (Gal. 4:4-7; Eph. 1:7). Every adopted son is holy according to Eph. 1:4-5; Heb. 12:5-15; Rom. 11:16.\n\n17. **When you become an heir to the kingdom of God** (1 Cor. 6:9-11). This is so when you're made righteous according to this Scripture and Rom. 8:1-17; 1 Jn. 2:29; 3:7-10; 5:1-18.\n\n18. **When you turn "from darkness to light, and from the power of Satan unto God, that they might receive forgiveness of sins, and an inheritance among them that are sanctified by faith that is in me"** (Acts 26:18). Paul here is telling Agrippa what Christ said to him. If Christ can be depended upon as knowing what He says, then it's clear that people are sanctified by faith in Christ at the time they turn to God, have their sins remitted, and become heirs of God. You'd have just as much right to argue that "forgiveness of sins" is a separate experience from the new birth occurring sometime after it, as to argue that being "sanctified by faith" is after the new birth, because "forgiveness of sins," "an inheritance" and "sanctified by faith" are all spoken of in this same verse as results of turning to God.\n\n19. **When you are accepted of God** (Rom. 15:16). Do people have to wait months or years after the new birth before being accepted by God?\n\n20. **When you get into Christ** (1 Cor. 1:2). This is when you become a new creature in Christ (2 Cor. 5:17; Eph. 4:24; Gal. 5:24; Rom. 8:1-7).\n\n21. **When you become Christ's:** "they that are Christ's have crucified the flesh with the affections and lusts" (Gal. 5:24). You can't belong to Christ until you've done this. But you no longer have the old man when you've done this (1 Jn. 3:7-10; 5:1-5, 18).\n\n22. **When you are washed from all sin before justification** (1 Cor. 6:9-11). Here Paul gives the true order of these phases of salvation: "but ye are washed, but ye are sanctified, but ye are justified" (For proof of this see Points IV, V and VIII, below).\n\n---\n\nJustification declares you righteous before God — a verdict that cannot be appealed. Sanctification makes you righteous in practice — a process that continues until you see Him face to face. Both are gifts. Neither can be earned. So here's the question: Do you live with the settled confidence of someone who has been declared righteous by God? Or do you approach God with ongoing uncertainty about whether He accepts you — still presenting your record instead of Christ's?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk48j001lewhj6vbemp65	cmnknk48e000xewhj49ao5az0	Supplement 17 — For Lessons 33 & 34	SUPPLEMENT	867	23	\N	t	Salvation has three tenses. Justification declares you righteous; sanctification makes you righteous in practice. These two truths form the backbone of what it means to be a Christian — but most believers are much clearer on one than the other. This supplement helps you inhabit both.\n\nIt's essential that you understand salvation correctly and learn how to obtain full deliverance from sin, sickness, poverty, want, and failure through the means of grace God has provided.\n\n![Supplement 17 — For Lessons 33 & 34](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8Os7BrAblDRlSEtNozZx4rmO6UfQcdhyY2pDqwX)\n\n## 1. Salvation From Sin\n\nScripture makes it abundantly clear that God wants every Christian to be free from sin. Let's walk through some key passages together.\n\n"Thou shalt call his name JESUS: for he shall save his people from their sins" (Mt. 1:21). Jesus also told people to "sin no more, lest a worse thing come upon thee" (Jn. 5:14; 8:11).\n\nHe explained, "Whosoever committeth sin is the servant of sin. And the servant abideth not in the house forever" (Jn. 8:34-36).\n\nPaul addressed this powerfully in Romans: "Shall we continue in sin, that grace may abound? God forbid. How shall we that are dead to sin, live any longer therein? . . . Knowing this, that our old man was crucified with him, that the body of sin might be destroyed, that henceforth we should not serve sin . . . Likewise reckon ye also yourselves to be dead unto sin . . . Let not sin reign in your mortal body, that ye should obey it in the lusts thereof. Neither yield ye your members as instruments of unrighteousness unto sin: but yield yourselves unto God, as those that are alive from the dead, and your members as instruments of righteousness unto God. For sin shall not have dominion over you . . . Know ye not, that to whom ye yield yourselves servants to obey, his servants ye are to whom ye obey; whether of sin unto death, or of obedience unto righteousness? . . . Being then made free from sin, ye became servants of righteousness . . . When ye were the servants of sin ye were free from righteousness . . . But now being made free from sin, and become servants of righteousness, and become servants to God, ye have your fruit unto holiness, and the end everlasting life" (Rom. 6:1-23).\n\n"For the law of the Spirit of life in Christ Jesus hath made me free from the law of sin and death" (Rom. 8:1-3). Paul also wrote, "And they that are Christ's have crucified the flesh with the affections and lusts" (Gal. 5:24).\n\nJohn assures us, "The blood of Jesus Christ his Son cleanseth us from all sin" (1 Jn. 1:7, 9).\n\n## 2. Salvation From Fleshly Lusts and Bad Habits\n\nMany teach that the body sins every day while the soul remains free from sin. However, according to the passages above, the body must be holy just as much as the soul and spirit.\n\nLook at these Scriptures that specifically mention the body being free from sin:\n\n"That ye present your bodies a living sacrifice, holy, acceptable unto God, which is your reasonable service. And be not conformed to this world: but be ye transformed by the renewing of your mind" (Rom. 12:1-2).\n\n"Let not sin therefore reign in your mortal body" (Rom. 6:11-23).\n\n"Therefore, brethren, we are debtors, not to the flesh, to live after the flesh. For if ye live after the flesh ye shall die: but if ye through the Spirit do mortify the deeds of the body, ye shall live" (Rom. 8:12-13).\n\n"Know ye not that ye are the temple of God, and that the Spirit of God dwelleth in you? If any man defile the temple of God, him shall God destroy; for the temple of God is holy, which temple ye are" (1 Cor. 3:16-17; 6:19-20).\n\n"Let us cleanse ourselves from all filthiness of the flesh and spirit, perfecting holiness in the fear of God" (2 Cor. 7:1).\n\n"Putting off the body of the sins of the flesh by the circumcision of Christ" (Col. 2:11-12; 3:5-17).\n\n"That every one of you should know how to possess his vessel [body] in sanctification and honour" (1 Thess. 4:3-7; 2 Tim. 2:19-22).\n\n## 3. Salvation From Sickness and Disease\n\nHealing of the body and perfect health is part of salvation. You can see this clearly in Lesson Thirty-three, Point 1, 42, and Lesson Fourteen.\n\nJesus died on the cross so that all people might be set free from sin and disease. There's no reason why anyone should go without these benefits (Mt. 8:16-17; 1 Pet. 2:24; Jas. 1:5-8).\n\n## 4. Salvation From Poverty and Want\n\nWithin salvation, we find doctrines like grace, faith, prayer, providence, and power that guarantee freedom from poverty and want. Review Lesson Ten and note in Points III, 1, 2, 3, and 4 how God has provided every benefit for your body, soul, and spirit.\n\nWhatever you need from God that is good for you has been promised. If you believe these promises and ask God in faith without doubting, it will be yours.\n\nGod does not and cannot lie. When He makes clear and abundant promises that "all things ye shall ask in prayer believing ye shall receive," He means exactly that.\n\nThink of it like this: if you doubt Him, that's your sin and your loss. He won't grant you the answer when you doubt, so you'll have to go without the things you need and want if you refuse to believe from the heart that your prayers are answered.\n\nWouldn't it be better to wait and not pray until you firmly believe that God is true, that He means what He says, and that you will receive what He has promised? Then when you pray, settle it in your heart that you already have by faith what you've asked for.\n\nIf you maintain this kind of faith and don't doubt in your heart, it shall be done according to Mt. 21:21-22; Mk. 9:23; 11:22-24; Jn. 15:7; Jas. 1:5-8; Heb. 11:6.\n\n## 5. Salvation From Failure in Life\n\nThere is no possible failure in life concerning answers to prayer and receiving what God has promised — that is, if you make up your mind that you're not going to fail.\n\nThis means you're willing to follow the laws of faith, healing, and prosperity to the letter without any doubt about the outcome. It means getting your eyes off the benefits and onto God in Christ, simply having unwavering faith.\n\nIt means that regardless of what happens to make you think God hasn't heard your prayers, you still believe and faithfully obey the laws of these benefits. It's similar to an athlete training for a marathon — you doggedly take every step necessary to reach the finish line you're seeking.\n\nYou can have salvation from failure just as surely as deliverance from sin and sickness. Review all the supplements and concentrate on eliminating any sins or deficiencies that may hinder your progress in getting what you want from God.\n\nMany churches understand salvation from sin fairly well. Salvation from sickness is understood less, and salvation from poverty and failure even less so. That's why we constantly emphasize that God has provided for every known need of the human race, both now and for all eternity.\n\nIn Lesson Twelve, we showed why people's needs aren't fully realized. If you want all the benefits that others fail to receive from God, learn from their failures and don't make the same mistakes.\n\nIn Supplement Two, we gave you the key that unlocks God's promises. But you must obtain this key and use it correctly. After all, certain benefits from God can only be received by those who are truly born-again and obedient to the gospel.\n\n## 6. Hindrances to Personal Salvation\n\nAs we saw in Lesson Six, the hindrances to your personal attainments in God are the devil, his demons, and fallen angels. These powers must be recognized and dealt with intelligently — but not as mere "errors of the mortal mind."\n\nPicture it this way: as God's child, you must overcome these powers and become a master of yourself through Jesus Christ, the Holy Spirit, the Word of God, and the precious blood of Christ. These powers are real and must be treated as such.\n\nNot only should you recognize the devil and his demons as real spirit-beings, but you must fully understand their power, purpose, and tactics of warfare before you can wage successful conflict against them.\n\nThese demon powers will wage unceasing war against believers to defeat them in every way possible. They must be cast out to gain victory, and they must be resisted daily to keep that victory (Rom. 6:11-12; Eph. 4:27; 6:10-18; Jas. 4:7; 1 Pet. 5:8-9).\n\nSee Supplements Three, Four, Six, Seven, and Nine for the secrets of how to gain victory over sin, sickness, and poverty.\n\n## 7. Take All Problems to God\n\nYou must learn to take all your problems to God, regardless of their nature. He doesn't just save from sin, deliver from sickness, and help with a few problems — He's interested in every problem you face.\n\nTake each one to God in simple faith, believing that He hears you, and you'll be amazed at how He helps.\n\nThe only requirements are:\n\n1. Believe God is a personal being, not some abstract, impersonal force.\n2. Believe in Jesus Christ as the Son of God, accept Him as your Saviour, and become born-again by the Holy Spirit.\n3. Take the promises of God that cover your needs, go to Him in prayer, and ask in the name of Jesus Christ for your answer based on the authority of what He has promised.\n4. Ask in faith, nothing wavering (Jn. 14:12-15; 15:7, 16; 16:23-26; Jas. 1:5-8; Heb. 11:6).\n\n---\n\n## Ten Questions For Supplement Seventeen. Do You Know:\n\n- **That the oil of Mt. 25:1-13 does not symbolize the Holy Spirit?** As we learned in Lesson Thirty-one under "The Kingdom of Heaven Parables," a parable is an illustration, and the details of Bible illustrations shouldn't be given separate meanings from the plain literal statements. No doctrine should be based upon the details of parables. Details are necessary to make a story illustrate a point, but once the truth is made clear, the details and the whole story have served their purpose. The oil in the parable of the ten virgins simply means literal oil in lamps for giving light. There are plenty of Scriptures about the Holy Spirit that we should use as the basis for doctrine, so we don't need to take literal lamp oil as proof of anything regarding this question. (The ten virgins will be dealt with fully in Lesson Forty-four.)\n\n- **That there will be day and night in the New Earth forever?** The Bible plainly says that day and night will continue as long as the Earth remains (Gen. 8:22), and that is forever (Eccl. 1:4; Ps. 104:5). When the Bible says there shall be no night there, it refers to the Holy City, which has no need of the sun or moon to shine in it (Rev. 21:23-26). The sun, moon, and stars will shine forever on the New Earth outside the city (Ps. 72:5, 17-19; 89:4, 29-37; Gen. 1:14-18; 8:22).\n\n- **That the serpent in the Garden of Eden was not the personal devil?** The serpent was one of the beasts of the field which the Lord God had made and was merely a tool of the devil to cause the fall of man (Gen. 3:1). The serpent was cursed to go upon its belly all the days of its life, but Satan does not do this. The ordinary snake does go on its belly now and will continue to do so even in the Millennium (Gen. 3:14; Isa. 65:25). There is no statement in Scripture that says the serpent was the personal devil. (See Lesson Nine, Point VIII, 1.)\n\n- **That the present Earth is eternal?** This is plainly stated in Eccl. 1:4; Ps. 104:5. When Peter said the Earth shall be burned up, he referred to its renovation by fire at the end of the Millennium, as we'll see in Lesson Fifty.\n\n- **That every man does not have a set time to die?** Many statements in Scripture disprove this theory. Paul said many Corinthians had died because they hadn't rightly discerned the Lord's Supper (1 Cor. 11:27-31). Children are promised long lives if they keep the Fifth Commandment (Exod. 20:12; Eph. 6:2). People are promised long lives if they obey God (Ps. 91). God made it a law that those who break the law should die for it — not because that was God's set time or way for them to die, but because of disobedience (Gen. 9:6; Exod. 21:12-36; Rom. 13:1-8). People who commit suicide take their own lives before they would die naturally. Many have died in plagues when they could have lived if they had obeyed God (1 Cor. 10:1-18). People die in many other ways that aren't according to God's plan and before the time they should, as we see every day.\n\n- **That the New Testament does not command men to keep any particular sabbath?** On the contrary, it teaches that you can choose which day, if any, you want to keep unto the Lord (Rom. 14:5-6; Gal. 4:9-10; Col. 2:14-17). (See Lesson Thirty-two.)\n\n- **That there will be the greatest spiritual revival in the last days that this Earth has ever seen?** This will occur during the Tribulation after the rapture, as proven in Acts 2:16-21; Rev. 7:1-14; 12:17; 15:2-4. This will be discussed in Lesson Forty.\n\n- **That the soul of no man will ever be resurrected from the dead or out of the grave?** This is because souls don't go into the graves at death. Only bodies go into the graves at physical death, and they are the only things that will be resurrected out of the graves at the resurrection (Jn. 5:28-29; 1 Cor. 15:35-58). Only bodies die at physical death (Jas. 2:26), so they are the only parts of people that will be resurrected. Souls are immortal and cannot be resurrected from physical death (1 Pet. 3:4). However, they can be resurrected from death in trespasses and sins (Eph. 2:1-9). This will be discussed fully in Lesson Thirty-seven.\n\n- **That there are animals in Heaven just like we have on Earth?** This is clear from the fact that Paul said the invisible things are clearly seen by the visible (Rom. 1:20) and from many references stating that there are horses in Heaven (2 Ki. 2:11-12; 6:13-17; Zech. 1:8-11; 6:1-8; Rev. 19:11-21). If some animals are definitely mentioned as being in Heaven and if other invisible things are clearly seen by visible things on Earth, then there must be other kinds of animals in Heaven. We'll list many of the heavenly things in Lesson Forty-two.\n\n- **That there will not be one sick person on Earth after the second coming of Christ?** Everyone will be healed in the Millennium as proven in Isa. 30:26; 32:1-5; 33:24; 35:1-10.\n\n---\n\nYou have been declared righteous by God — that verdict is permanent and irrevocable. And God is actively working to make you righteous in practice — that process is ongoing and cooperative. So here's the question: Which of these two realities is more real to you on a daily basis? And which one are you currently neglecting — the security of justification, or the serious work of sanctification?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk48j001mewhjkkrwuhn8	cmnknk48e000xewhj49ao5az0	Lesson 35 — The Truth About Eternal Security	READING	12351	24	\N	t	Can a genuine believer lose their salvation? This question has divided Christians for centuries and produced two camps that read the same Bible and reach opposite conclusions. Both sides have proof texts. Both sides have serious theologians. Both sides think the other is missing something obvious. What the debate usually misses is the most important question: What does Scripture actually say — not as proof texts pulled from their context, but as a coherent whole?\n\nLast time we established what justification and sanctification actually are and how they differ. Now we face one of the most personally significant questions in all of Christianity: once God declares you righteous, can that verdict be reversed?\n\nSadly, for too many people, this teaching of "eternal security" actually creates *eternal insecurity*. It lulls them into a "sinning-saint" religion that will bring real disappointment on Judgment Day. Some accept these arguments simply because they don't know the full scope of what Scripture teaches on the subject. Others, being willfully blind, embrace it because they want an excuse to keep sinning.\n\nNow, let me be clear: there *is* a true sense in which every believer can have "everlasting life" and be "eternally secure." These blessings are absolutely real—whenever God's conditions are met. That's what we'll explore in this lesson:\n\n> I. What is the Question Involved in the Teaching of Unconditional Security?\n\n> II. Is Man a Free Moral Agent?\n\n> III. What Is Eternal Life?\n\n> IV. Can Eternal Life Be Lost?\n\n> V. Examples in the Bible of Eternal Life Being Lost.\n\n> VI. Is Eternal Life an Unconditional Possession Now?\n\n> VII. What Are the Conditions for Keeping Eternal Life?\n\n> VIII. Real Bible Assurance and True Eternal Security.\n\n![Lesson 35 — The Truth About Eternal Security](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsJZTHxpJ4ZDlMH1zAnxErj0PQYB2bIe8GdaVW)\n\n## I. What is the Question Involved in the Teaching of Unconditional Security?\n\nThe main question in this whole debate is this: Who bears the responsibility for bringing about this coveted state of eternal security? And we might add: Under what conditions, if any, can it be obtained? Are there "any strings attached," as we commonly say? Could it be completely unconditional—or might we find somewhere in the Bible that conditions belong to this doctrine just as much as the blessings do?\n\nThe Bible *does* teach conditional eternal security. This is the only kind of security a sensible person can expect and a just God can offer. But to teach *unconditional* eternal security—and to maintain that position in the face of overwhelming scriptural evidence to the contrary—is to make God the most unjust tyrant in the universe.\n\nTo hold this view, you have to manufacture and maintain many other false doctrines to prop up the sinking-sand foundation of the main false doctrine. You have to twist, contort, misinterpret, misapply, take from, and add to many Scriptures to support such teaching.\n\nEvery Bible doctrine should be measured by plain facts of Scripture and by true principles and examples from life—not by misinterpreting a few texts while ignoring everything else Scripture says on the subject. It's vital to get God's complete thought on a topic as expressed across *all* Scripture.\n\nThe devil has always used Scripture to cloak his teachings. He can twist it until whatever he teaches *appears* quite scriptural. Just because a theory has a few passages that seem to support it doesn't prove it's true. Any doctrine that contradicts the true principles of life—or even *one* plain Scripture rightly understood—is bound to be wrong.\n\nTeachers of unconditional security, like teachers of other false doctrines, tend to explain away all Scriptures that don't fit their theory. Their usual approach is to claim that contradicting passages apply only to Jews, not Christians—or to some past age, not ours—and to quickly dismiss other Scriptures written to Christians as irrelevant to the question.\n\nMeanwhile, passages written to both Jews and Gentiles that *can* be twisted to fit their theory get emphasized beyond all true meaning, under the guise of "just accepting what the Bible says."\n\nGod designed the Bible so that no single passage contains all the truth on any subject, and no passage contradicts another (Isa. 28:9-13). All Scriptures on a topic must be gathered and harmonized with each other—and with all Scriptures on other subjects—before you can fully understand the whole truth.\n\nNo "Scripture is of any private interpretation" (2 Pet. 1:19). That means no Scripture should be isolated from all others and treated as the complete truth on a subject while ignoring everything else written about it. Two or three plain passages are required to establish any single truth, and if there are more than that, they must all testify to the same thing without contradicting each other or any other passages on any other subject (Num. 35:30; Deut. 17:6; 19:15; Mt. 18:16; 2 Cor. 13:1).\n\nWe're told to "rightly divide the word of truth" (2 Tim. 2:15) and to compare "spiritual things with spiritual" (1 Cor. 2:13). When you do this with the very few passages used to teach unconditional security, you'll find they're in perfect harmony with the many *hundreds* of passages that plainly teach conditional security.\n\nOn the other hand, those hundreds of passages simply cannot be harmonized with the wrong interpretations placed on those few verses. Teaching unconditional security leaves an irreparable gap between two sets of Scriptures, but teaching conditional security brings perfect harmony to all of them.\n\nIf every Christian would be willing to believe *all* Scriptures on this subject—and be just as eager to defend all truth—there would be no division over the question of true Bible security.\n\nThe truth is this: the Bible teaches a true, just, and lasting security for all believers who put forth an honest effort to remain secure in Christ. Any person can have true biblical security if they'll meet a few simple conditions God has clearly stated—conditions anyone can meet through the means of grace provided in the gospel.\n\n## II. Is Man a Free Moral Agent?\n\nThis is the first question we need to settle. If man is *not* a free moral agent, then God can be held entirely responsible for all sin, rebellion, sickness, and all the effects of sin—as well as for the damnation of men and angels. That's what unconditional security teachers end up implying.\n\nOne of these teachers says, "No man is an absolutely free moral agent, for he is either led away by the devil against his will or he is a servant of Christ. I do not simply choose to keep myself in a place where I am secure. God has chosen me and I am secure in His choice."\n\nAnother says, "Ownership means lordship. That which is owned has no right of will contrary to the will of the owner... Adam was the only true free moral agent. When he sinned he lost free moral agency. No man ever wills to be born in the human race, and equally powerless is he to will to separate himself from the human race... Yet it is argued that man can separate himself from God... It was not his own will to be born-again, but the will of God (Jn. 1:13)... Man has as little to do with that as he had to do with his physical birth. As it is impossible for man to separate himself from the human race, so it is equally impossible for him, by a free act, to separate himself from God's kingdom. There is no such thing as free moral agency in the kingdom of God... To say that a man can will to go away from God and be lost is to make the sovereign grace of God subject to the will of man."\n\nLet me unpack what these statements really mean. They're saying:\n\n- Man has no power to choose to serve God\n- Man has no responsibility in serving God or Satan, or in being saved or lost\n- It's entirely up to God to choose each person—or not\n- Those God chooses are secure by His choice alone, with man having nothing to do with it\n- God is a respecter of persons because He doesn't choose everyone equally\n- God is responsible for the sins and damnation of the lost, as well as the security of the few He supposedly "delights in choosing"\n- Some people can never be saved because God hasn't chosen them\n- God is responsible for all the sins, sufferings, and curses in the universe\n\nBut think about Adam. If he was responsible for his fall from grace, if he *chose* to fall from his former state, if grace and divine nature didn't automatically keep him saved and holy, and if God left it up to his free will in the beginning—then it's the same with all people today.\n\nThere's no passage proving that man is *not* a free moral agent all the days of his life, whether saved or unsaved.\n\nMan is absolutely a free moral agent and serves the devil or God as he freely chooses. If someone wants to turn to God from the devil, he's always free to do so. If he wants to turn from God to the devil, he's always free to do that too. Neither master can force obedience if the person doesn't freely choose to give it. Until death, everyone has the free choice of whom to serve.\n\nMan is ethically obligated by his moral nature to serve righteousness, live holy, and consecrate himself to the same end God is consecrated to—the highest good of all. But man isn't *forced* to live holy. He's free to consecrate himself to the end the devil pursues—a life of self-gratification. He's free to choose either end and to use the means of attaining whatever end he chooses. No one forces him to choose either path.\n\nThink of it like a GPS navigation system. The GPS can tell you the best route, warn you of dangers, and recalculate when you make a wrong turn—but it can't grab the steering wheel. *You* decide whether to follow it or ignore it. That's what free moral agency is like.\n\nA saved person has the same power of choice he had before salvation. His *desires* are changed in salvation, but he can permit the old desires to take hold again. The flesh can gain control over him once more, and he can choose to live in his old sins again.\n\nIf he chooses to sin again, he incurs the same death penalty and will be damned as surely as if he'd never been saved (Ezek. 18:4; Rom. 6:14-23; 8:1-13; Gal. 6:7-8; Jas. 5:19-20).\n\nNow, in matters outside the moral realm where man has no choice—like not choosing to be born—that has nothing to do with free moral agency. That faculty kicks in after birth. As he grows up, he's free to choose his own destiny by the life he lives in serving God or Satan. It's only when someone *becomes* a free moral agent that he's held responsible.\n\nTo say a person has no willpower when he chooses to go back into sin and becomes overpowered by it—that's false. This doesn't prove he's not a free moral agent. It actually *proves* he is one, because he couldn't have chosen to sin again without free will.\n\nWhen someone yields to sin, Satan can make him a captive and dominate his life to varying degrees, depending on how much the person submits. A person can completely submit to demons and become their total slave. But at any moment, he can turn to God by his own free will and defeat those same spirit-rebels with God's help.\n\nIf he had no power to choose to live right and turn to God, *then* we might argue he's not a free agent in the moral realm. It's similar to how winning a war doesn't prove the losing side had no willpower—the Axis powers chose to make war and chose to surrender, which proves they had will.\n\nIf a person can resist sin *at all*, it proves willpower. If he's exercising will *in the least degree* concerning moral things, it proves he's a free moral agent.\n\nBeing a servant of Christ doesn't eliminate willpower—it *demonstrates* it! The person couldn't have turned from Satan and sin without exercising that power. To believe otherwise would make God an unjust tyrant holding slaves in worse bondage than Satan ever did.\n\nIt's actually Satan who tries to enslave people and control their choices. God always gives them freedom to serve Him. When they voluntarily turn to God, He delivers them from Satan and sin—but He still permits perfect freedom of action regarding whether they'll continue serving Him or go back into sin. Yes, people are called "servants" of God, but it's a voluntary service. There's nothing arbitrary about it on God's part.\n\nTrue, we have no freedom to choose whether to come into existence, and no freedom to choose to become nothing again—we're eternal creatures and can't will ourselves out of existence. But this doesn't disprove free moral agency. We're always free to choose the highest good for ourselves and others, or to choose selfish ends contrary to that highest good.\n\nIf we admit that actions have certain effects, we should also admit these are free moral actions. If a person had no free actions concerning moral living and God alone controlled everything, then *God* would be responsible for all evil and its effects. God would be responsible for sin and couldn't justly judge anyone. He'd also be responsible for death—the penalty for sin—and couldn't rightly execute the sentence of broken law.\n\nAdam never lost the power of choice in the moral realm as long as he remained sane—and neither does anyone else. To lose the power to *be* what we were before the Fall doesn't take away our power of *choice*. That's simply the result of the Fall, of a wrong choice.\n\nWe're still free to choose God and righteousness and return to our original state where the effects of the moral Fall are erased. We can be redeemed from those effects and do right again from free choice. Being overpowered by sin was a willing action to begin with—otherwise Adam never would have fallen, unless we want to blame God for it.\n\nSince the Fall, it's been a willing cooperation with Satan toward the same end: self-gratification. No one has to serve sin and Satan even one day if they don't choose to.\n\nIf a person never wills to be saved, he never will be saved. This is why some are saved and some aren't. It's God's will that all be saved and come to the knowledge of the truth (1 Tim. 2:4; 2 Pet. 3:9). If God's will were automatically done, everyone would be saved. But God will not and cannot save anyone until that person chooses to be saved and calls upon Him.\n\nGod's plan is "whosoever will," and God won't operate contrary to His own Word. However, you're not saved by your own choice alone—there's no saving power in a mere choice. And salvation can't be given by God without your choice (Mk. 16:15; Jn. 3:15-20; 4:13-22; 14:6; Rom. 1:16; Heb. 7:25; 1 Jn. 1:9; 5:1; Rev. 22:17).\n\nNeither can you continue to be saved apart from your free choice (Isa. 59:2; Rom. 8:1-13; 2 Tim. 2:12; 1 Cor. 15:2; 1 Thess. 3:8; Heb. 10:26-29; 2 Pet. 2:20-21; etc.).\n\nThis power of choice to get saved and stay saved doesn't make human willpower more sovereign than God's power or grace. It simply means that God, as an intelligent being, made His plan—which includes free wills for people, granting them sovereignty over their own wills regarding whether they want to be saved or stay saved by grace.\n\nGod won't break His own law and save or keep people saved contrary to their choice. If this is God's plan, then for God to arbitrarily force people to be saved or stay saved against their will would be completely unjust—it would make God break His own laws.\n\nTo teach that people have nothing to do with the new birth and that it's entirely up to God's choice is to make God false according to His own Word. You must make a choice in your spiritual birth because you *exist* with power to make a free choice. In natural birth, you weren't in existence with power to decide whether you wanted to be born.\n\nGod has told us that anyone who is a respecter of persons is a sinner (Jas. 2:9). Therefore, if it were left up to God alone to save some and damn others as He sees fit, He would be a sinner Himself. But God is no respecter of persons (Rom. 2:6-16). If a person can't exercise willpower in God's kingdom, and can't sin after being saved, then many Scriptures are false—as we'll see in Point IV below.\n\nYou are a subject of moral law and moral government. You're under moral obligation to obey the moral Governor of all free wills according to prescribed law. You're subject to penalties for disobedience and rewards for obedience.\n\nIf you have no free moral choice to render obedience or disobedience and meet your own moral obligation, your very existence is an act of injustice on the Creator's part. That you have intellect, reason, sensibility, and powers of knowledge and choice cannot be doubted as long as you're sane.\n\nThe freedom of human will in moral action has been denied in theory by those who believe in unconditional security, but it has never been disproved and never will be. The very people who deny free will actually admit it in their arguments while trying to disprove it! The fact that they *freely choose* to deny such a plain fact as free action proves they have it and are exercising it.\n\nThe fact that others who are more logical choose *not* to believe their empty arguments—and are honest enough to admit plain everyday facts—also proves that all people have free wills to do as they please.\n\nNo one can deny the fact of free action in daily life without appearing insane. In your own daily life, you *know* you have freedom to do right or wrong, to be saved or lost according to God's law. By the necessity of your own nature, you know you're a free agent in the moral realm.\n\nYou may try to argue yourself out of this fact, but you might as well try to argue yourself out of your very existence. Both truths remain: you *are*, and you *are free* to choose your own destiny and moral actions at every stage of life until eternity.\n\nYou not only have the power of choice, but you have a natural created faculty—your conscience—that tells you what choice you *should* make concerning every moral action. To deny the will is to deny the conscience. By doing this, you can soon make yourself believe you're not responsible for sin and its effects, even though you may sin every day. But it takes more than a false doctrine of unconditional security to make you immune from sin and its effects.\n\nHaving will and conscience makes you subject to the moral obligation to keep moral law and obey moral government. Every normal person recognizes these powers in themselves and in everyone else. This assumption is irresistible and universal and cannot logically be questioned—and wouldn't be, unless it's for some selfish purpose to prove a point or excuse living in sin contrary to Scripture.\n\nRegardless of why it's questioned, human free will remains an established fact of our constitutional makeup. The affirmation by millions of people of sound mind that they're responsible for their acts among others and before God shows beyond contradiction that everyone regards themselves—and others—as subjects of moral obligation.\n\nMoral obligation, made possible by free will and conscience, extends to the ultimate acts of the will concerning moral law. You're free to choose obedience to moral law, although you may be overpowered by sin and Satan in some acts because you chose not to serve God and live spiritually alive enough to resist the effects of a moral Fall.\n\nNevertheless, in these matters you're always free to reject Satan and turn to God to be free from the effects and responsibility of these sinful acts—or you're free to continue submitting to such bondage by your own choice. You have a Helper in God and deliverance in salvation if you choose it. But as long as you choose to remain in rebellion against God, you have no claim on such help.\n\nYou're absolutely free to choose the end God is consecrated to—the highest good of all. Or you're free to choose the end Satan and all rebels are consecrated to—self-gratification contrary to the highest good of the universe.\n\nYou can choose your own end and the means of securing it. You can relinquish your choice at any moment. You can refuse known necessary conditions and means to an end, or accept them. You can choose holiness as an end along with the means of attaining it, or choose sin and the means to secure that end.\n\nYou have absolute preference and choice in all matters pertaining to moral law and obligation. You can choose the means to obey moral law and meet moral obligations, or reject them. No choice can be made without the mind having a reason for it, and in this sense free will extends to the ultimate intention of the mind.\n\nSuch freedom of will and conscience is recognized in young children who justify themselves in their actions. Grown people also exercise such freedom and make excuses to justify many acts. In some actions, people are honest enough to admit wrong, while in others they're justified by right and voluntary decisions.\n\nCourts of justice have always assumed that people are free to choose, and they judge on the basis of ultimate intention. Only those considered mentally incompetent are regarded as not responsible for their acts—proving the universal belief in free moral agency as a first truth of reason, as well as of revelation.\n\nThe Bible throughout recognizes freedom of will and conscience. Moral obligation extends to everything about us over which the will has direct or indirect control. Our muscles, intellect, senses, and appetites are all controlled by the will. If you can't directly do as you will concerning moral law, you can choose the means of grace that will enable you to obey. Hence, you're a free moral agent in the highest sense of the term.\n\nMoral action is voluntary action, and moral obligation respects the ultimate intention only. Involuntary acts aren't moral actions. Choices concerning the well-being of God and the universe constitute moral obligation, and this is what God demands in His moral law. He can't excuse breaking this law because He's provided all necessary means to obey it, if you're willing to use them.\n\nThis is why you're just as responsible for your sins after being saved as before. God requires both in the law and in the gospel that free moral agents choose the same end He's consecrated to. All obligation consists of choosing that end. Virtue is consecration to this end; vice is consecration to the wrong end—self-gratification.\n\nSo-called "sovereign grace" and the will of God aren't the basis of moral obligation and free action. Moral law governs voluntary action only. It's your moral obligation to will the best good for all regardless of God's will or grace, because it's the law of your creative makeup.\n\nWhether God commanded it or not, you would by nature be obligated to will, choose, and secure the highest good for yourself and others. God's will merely reveals the rule of action and choice—it never *forces* the choice. This must be your own free choice.\n\nThe will of God as expressed in His law is the rule for all free wills. This law defines the rule of moral action, and the reason to conform to it is the highest good of the universe. If God commanded something your reason told you was unwise and not for the good of all, it would be impossible for you to feel obligated to obey Him. Thus reason doesn't regard His law as the foundation of moral obligation, but only as proof that what He commands is wise, best for all, and commanded for that reason.\n\nMoral law makes known what's right and wrong to free wills, but the whole thing is a farce if you have no choice in whether to do right or wrong. Any theory about moral action that destroys human free moral agency is completely illogical, impractical, unnatural, and unscriptural—and must be rejected.\n\nAll this fantasy of self-indulgence with dreams of Heaven and irresponsibility here and hereafter is a religion of Satan designed to damn souls, and it should be treated as such. To teach it means making God the one committing sins in both saints and sinners, and the one responsible for some being lost and others being saved.\n\nThe Bible never teaches that God saves and keeps anyone contrary to their will. Any action for which you're responsible through exercising your own created willpower—should you make the wrong choice—doesn't make God responsible. It doesn't lessen God's power, will, purpose, or change His attitude or plan to help you, should you change to the right choice and accept God's way and help.\n\nGod is limited in blessing you should you rebel against Him. If He weren't, He'd be obligated to force everyone to be saved alike. If anyone is lost, it only proves failure on that person's part, not God's.\n\nIt's God's will that everyone be saved, but who *will* be saved depends on the individual meeting the conditions of salvation and of staying saved (1 Tim. 2:4; 2 Pet. 3:9; Jn. 3:15-20, 36; Rev. 22:17). A saved person no longer belongs to God if they rebel and live and die in sin, as we'll see in Point IV below.\n\nThe following statements prove that both saints and sinners have the power of free choice, and that God holds them responsible for wrong use of this faculty:\n\n- "Choose you this day whom ye shall serve" (Josh. 24:15)\n- "did not choose the fear of the Lord" (Prov. 1:29-33)\n- "I have chosen the way of truth" (Ps. 119:30, 173)\n- "They have chosen their own ways" (Isa. 66:3)\n- "he will hold to the one, and despise the other" (Mt. 6:24)\n- "I will follow thee" (Mt. 8:19)\n- "If any man will come after me" (Mt. 16:24, 25)\n- "Whosoever will do the will of God" (Mk. 3:35; 16:16)\n- "ye shall not come to me, that ye might have life" (Jn. 5:40)\n- "If any man will do his will, he shall know of the doctrine" (Jn. 7:17; 9:31)\n- "If any man serve me, him will my Father honour" (Jn. 12:26)\n- "hath power over his own will" (1 Cor. 7:37)\n- "If I do this thing willingly" (1 Cor. 9:17)\n- "not by constraint, but willingly" (1 Pet. 5:2)\n- "whosoever will" (Rev. 22:17)\n\nThese and literally hundreds of similar passages prove free moral agency. The words "choice," "will," and similar terms are used over 4,000 times in Scripture. Other words expressing power of choice in all phases of life are used thousands of times. To deny free moral agency for all people—saved or unsaved—is the rankest ignorance and willful blindness to plain facts.\n\n## III. What is Eternal Life?\n\nEternal life is not the physical life of the body. We have physical life only as long as the soul and spirit remain in the body. Death in Scripture means *separation* from the purpose for which a creature was created.\n\nPhysical death is the separation of the inner person from the body. When this happens, you lose physical life (Jas. 2:26).\n\nEternal life is not the natural immortality or consciousness of the soul and spirit. Both saved and unsaved exist forever and are fully conscious after physical death (Lk. 16:19-31; Mt. 10:28; 17:3; Lk. 20:38; 23:43; Phil. 1:21-23; 2 Cor. 5:8; Rev. 6:9-11; Heb. 12:23; 1 Pet. 3:4; Jn. 11:24-25; 14:19).\n\nThe soul exists and is conscious even though it's "dead" (Eph. 2:1-10; Mt. 8:22; 1 Tim. 5:6). Spiritual death is the separation of the inner person from God because of sin (Isa. 59:2; Eph. 2).\n\nSo eternal life can't be the natural eternal life or consciousness of the inner person either in or out of the body. Eternal life can't be the eternal existence or consciousness of souls in eternal Hell, because only the righteous have eternal life by abiding in Christ (Jn. 3:15-18, 36; 5:24; 15:1-7; 1 Jn. 2:24, 25; 5:11).\n\nEternal death is eternal *separation* from God (Mt. 10:28; 25:41, 46; Rev. 2:11; 14:9-11; 20:11-15; 21:8; Isa. 66:22-24). Eternal life is the opposite—eternal *union* with God (Jn. 17:3; 1 Jn. 2:24-25; 5:11-12; 1 Cor. 6:17).\n\nIt was Adam in his sinless spiritual state who was told, "in the day that thou eatest thereof thou shalt surely die" (Gen. 2:17; 3:1-19). The Greek word for "life" is *zoe*, used thirty times with "eternal" and fourteen times with "everlasting."\n\nOne writer tries to prove that believers receive *zoe* life and that this isn't the kind of life Adam had. But 1 Cor. 15:45 states that Adam had *zoe* ("living") soul.\n\nOthers argue that Adam's life was natural and forfeitable, not spiritual and eternal like what believers have today. But the word translated "living" *is* zoe, used of Adam in that passage. It's also translated "living" in connection with God:\n\n- "the living [zoe] God" (Mt. 16:16; 22:32; 26:63)\n- "living [zoe] water... springing up into everlasting life" (Jn. 4:10, 14)\n- "living [zoe] bread" that gives eternal life (Jn. 6:51, 54)\n- "living [zoe] waters" referring to the Holy Spirit (Jn. 7:37-39)\n- "living [zoe] way" that leads to life (Heb. 10:20)\n\nDo God and all these things have "natural and forfeitable" life? If so, then that's what Adam had.\n\nEternal life was given to Adam on condition that he remain in union with God and not sin. God said if he sinned, he would die—be separated from God and lose that life (Gen. 2:17). Sin was the only thing that could and did take his eternal life away (Rom. 5:12-21; 1 Cor. 15:21, 22).\n\nSince Adam could have lived forever if he hadn't sinned, he had eternal life. If he had eternal life, he lost it by sin. If he could lose it by sin, then all others who regain it can also lose it again by sin.\n\nWhat kind of life did he lose the day he sinned, if not eternal life? According to Lesson Nine, Point VIII, 4, the penalty for sin was eternal death. So he had to lose the opposite of eternal death—eternal life.\n\nIt couldn't have been merely natural life he lost that day, because he lived 248,652 literal days after that (Gen. 5:5). If Adam's body didn't die the day he sinned, then it must have been his soul that died and was cut off from living forever.\n\nAdam's soul lost eternal life the day he sinned. Since it couldn't be natural life he lost, it must be spiritual and eternal life, canceled by eternal death.\n\nAdam's soul didn't lose its own life or consciousness, or it would have stopped functioning. The soul is the life of the body—the body can't function without it. Adam certainly didn't lose physical life since his body kept functioning for 930 years. Therefore, it must have been spiritual and eternal life that he lost.\n\nA person's natural life exists of itself, separate from God. Sinners continue to exist separate from God (Isa. 59:2; Eph. 2). Spiritual and eternal life can't be given to anyone until they're resurrected from sins and united to God's Spirit (1 Cor. 6:17; 12:12, 23; Rom. 8:9; Eph. 2:1-9). This proves that spiritual and eternal life are something apart from natural life.\n\nThink of it like electricity in a house. The house exists whether it's connected to the power grid or not—but it only has *electricity* when it's connected. If the power is cut off, the house still stands, but the lights are dead. Eternal life is like that connection to God's power; sin cuts off that connection.\n\nIf eternal life were a *part* of you, it couldn't be lost without losing yourself. But as long as something isn't part of you, it *can* be lost. The fact that eternal life is God's life—given to you as something separate from yourself—proves it can be lost. Since it's only *in God* that you have eternal life, to be separated from Him is to lose that life.\n\nSince only sin separates from God (Isa. 59:2) and causes death (Gen. 2:17; Ezek. 18:4), only sin can cause you to lose this life (Rom. 8:12, 13).\n\nEternal life is the opposite of eternal death. Since eternal death is eternal separation from God (as proven in Lesson Nine, Point VIII, 4), eternal life is eternal union with God by the cancellation of the eternal death penalty.\n\nIt's God's life, and it's never *your* life if you're not dwelling in God. It's yours only when you get into Christ, and only as long as you abide in Him (1 Jn. 2:24; 5:11, 12). It's therefore something separate from you, imparted only when you're saved and as long as you're saved.\n\nChrist is our life, and we have it as long as we have Him (Col. 2:6, 7; 3:4; Jn. 15:1-7). We're free from the death penalty as long as we live holy and free from sin. When we go back into sin, we incur the death penalty, we die again, and we lose the eternal life that's only in Christ. This will be proven by many Scriptures in Point IV below.\n\nIf this separation from sin is only temporary, the life with God will be only temporary. But\n\n---\n\nEternal security is not permission to live carelessly — it's the foundation that makes genuine growth possible. You can't grow in love toward someone you're afraid might reject you. Security is the soil that produces holiness, not its enemy. So here's the question to sit with honestly: Do you live with the settled assurance that God's grip on you is stronger than your failures — or are you still privately uncertain whether you're truly saved? And what would change in how you serve and love if you knew beyond doubt that nothing could separate you from Him?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk48k001newhjqcocahww	cmnknk48e000xewhj49ao5az0	Lesson 36 — Fifteen Great Covenants of Scripture	READING	2206	25	\N	t	God is a covenant-making God. Everything He does is structured around binding promises — not vague intentions or flexible plans, but specific covenants with specific parties, specific terms, and specific outcomes. There are fifteen of them woven through Scripture, and together they form the skeleton of biblical history. Knowing them is like finding the architectural plan for a building you've been exploring for years — suddenly every room makes sense.\n\nLast time we established the truth about eternal security — that God's grip on believers is permanent. Now we zoom out to the full landscape of God's covenantal history: all fifteen of the major biblical covenants and what each one reveals about God's character and purposes.\n\nLet's walk through each of these fifteen covenants together.\n\n![Lesson 36 — Fifteen Great Covenants of Scripture](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsVjsK7k31DSEv9r4BQbmhHPx2ALYz3ynujqdo)\n\n## I. The Solaric Covenant (Gen. 1:14-18)\n\nThis first covenant was made between God and humanity, promising eternal seasons of fruitfulness and the continuation of the natural world. These blessings would last as long as the solar system itself endures.\n\nThink of it like a landlord promising a tenant that the building's utilities will keep running as long as the building stands. God connected this promise to Noah, David, and others as we see in Gen. 8:22; Jer. 31:35-37; 33:19-26; Ps. 89:34-37.\n\n## II. The Edenic Covenant (Gen. 1:26–3:24)\n\nGod made this covenant with Adam and Eve before the Fall, and it was conditional upon their remaining faithful to Him (Gen. 2:17). The terms of this agreement required humanity to:\n\n> 1. Replenish the Earth (Gen. 1:28).\n\n> 2. Subdue the Earth and rule over God's creation (Gen. 1:28; Ps. 8).\n\n> 3. Work the ground and care for the Garden (Gen. 2:15).\n\n> 4. Stay away from the Tree of Knowledge (Gen. 2:17).\n\n> 5. Understand that disobedience meant death (Gen. 2:17).\n\n## III. The Adamic Covenant (Gen. 3:14-19)\n\nThis agreement came after the Fall but before Adam and Eve were expelled from the Garden. It marked the beginning of what we call the Age of Conscience. The covenant had two main parts:\n\n> 1. The five-fold curse:\n\n> (1) On the serpent (Gen. 3:14, 15; Isa. 65:25).\n\n> (2) On Satan (Gen. 3:15; Jn. 12:31; Col. 2:14-17; Heb. 2:14-18).\n\n> (3) On the woman (Gen. 3:16; 1 Cor. 11:3; 14:34; 1 Tim. 2:11-15).\n\n> (4) On the man (Gen. 3:17-19; Ps. 90:9, 10; Rom. 5:12-21).\n\n> (5) On the ground itself (Gen. 3:17-19; Rom. 8:18-23).\n\n> 2. The Promise (Gen. 3:15). This included complete redemption and the eventual removal of the curse (Rev. 22:3; Rom. 8:18-23).\n\n## IV. The Cainic Covenant (Gen. 4:11-15)\n\nGod made this contract with Cain, promising that he would be protected. Anyone who found and killed him would face sevenfold vengeance.\n\n## V. The Noahic Covenant (Gen. 8:20–9:29)\n\nAfter the flood, God established this covenant with Noah and even the animals of the field. It ushered in the Age of Human Government. Here are the terms:\n\n> 1. God would never again curse the ground and living creatures "while the earth remaineth" (Gen. 8:22; 9:12, 16).\n\n> 2. Humanity should replenish the Earth forever (Gen. 9:1, 12, 16).\n\n> 3. Humanity should rule the Earth (Gen. 9:2, 3).\n\n> 4. Animals could be eaten, but not their blood (Gen. 9:3, 4).\n\n> 5. Capital punishment was established for murderers (Gen. 9:5, 6; Num. 35).\n\n> 6. The rainbow would serve as the sign of this covenant (Gen. 9:12-17).\n\n> 7. This covenant would last forever (Gen. 9:12, 16).\n\n## VI. The Abrahamic Covenant (Gen. 12:1-13)\n\nWhen God saw that dealing with the whole human race at once wasn't working after the confusion of tongues, He made this covenant with Abraham. It marked the beginning of the Age of Promise (Gen. 13:14-18; 15:1-21; 17:4-8; 22:15-18; 26:1-5; 28:3, 10-15). This covenant had two parts:\n\n> 1. The sevenfold promise:\n\n> (1) "I will make of thee a great nation" (Gen. 12:1-3; 13:16; 17:18-20; 24:34, 35; Gal. 3).\n\n> (2) "Make thy name great" (Gen. 12:1-3; Exod. 2:24, 25; 6:3-8).\n\n> (3) "Thou shalt be a blessing" (Gen. 12:1-3; Gal. 3:13, 14).\n\n> (4) "I will bless them that bless thee" (Gen. 12:1-3; Mt. 25:31-46).\n\n> (5) "I will bless thee" (Gen. 13:14-18; 15:18-21; Gal. 3).\n\n> (6) "I will curse them that curse thee" (Zech. 14; Mt. 25:31-46).\n\n> (7) "In thee shall all the nations of the world be blessed" (Deut. 28:8-14; Isa. 60:3-5; 66:18-21; Gal. 3:16; Jn. 8:56-58).\n\n> 2. The sign of the covenant: circumcision (Gen. 17:1-21; Isa. 24:5).\n\n## VII. The Hagaric Covenant (Gen. 16:7-14)\n\nGod made this covenant with Hagar and her son Ishmael, extending for many generations. It contained three parts:\n\n> 1. Commands:\n\n> (1) Return and submit to Sarah (Gen. 16:9).\n\n> (2) Name her son Ishmael, meaning "God shall hear" (Gen. 16:11).\n\n> 2. Promises:\n\n> (1) Multiply her descendants beyond counting (Gen. 16:10; 17:20).\n\n> (2) Blessings on Ishmael to become a great nation (Gen. 17:20; 21:17-18).\n\n> (3) Ishmael would father twelve princes, just like Jacob would (Gen. 17:20; 25:12-18).\n\n> 3. Prophetic revelation:\n\n> (1) Ishmael would be a wild man (Gen. 16:12).\n\n> (2) His hand would be against every man (Gen. 16:12).\n\n> (3) Every man's hand would be against him (Gen. 16:12).\n\n> (4) He would dwell in the presence of his brethren (Gen. 16:12).\n\n## VIII. The Sarahic Covenant (Gen. 17:15-19; 18:9-15)\n\nGod made this covenant with Sarah, promising blessings to her and her offspring, Isaac, for many generations. The covenant had two parts:\n\n> 1. Commands:\n\n> (1) Change her name from Sarai to Sarah, meaning "princess" (Gen. 17:15).\n\n> (2) Name her son Isaac (Gen. 17:19).\n\n> 2. Promises:\n\n> (1) Bless Sarah with a son (Gen. 17:16-19; 19:10-15).\n\n> (2) Make her a mother of nations (Gen. 17:16).\n\n> (3) Make her a mother of many kings (Gen. 17:16).\n\n> (4) Continue the Abrahamic Covenant with Isaac (Gen. 17:19).\n\n> (5) Continue the Abrahamic Covenant with Isaac's descendants forever (Gen. 17:19).\n\n## IX. The Healing Covenant (Exod. 15:26; 23:25)\n\nGod made this covenant with Israel and anyone who chose to come under Moses' covenant. It had two parts:\n\n> 1. Commands:\n\n> (1) Diligently listen to God's voice (Exod. 15:26; Lev. 26:14-15).\n\n> (2) Do what is right in His sight (Exod. 15:26).\n\n> (3) Pay attention to His commandments (Exod. 15:26).\n\n> (4) Keep all His statutes (Exod. 15:26; Lev. 26:3; 14-15).\n\n> (5) Serve the Lord (Exod. 23:25).\n\n> 2. Promises:\n\n> (1) "I will put none of the diseases upon thee" (Exod. 15:26).\n\n> (2) "I am the Lord that healeth thee" (Exod. 15:26).\n\n> (3) "I will take away sickness from the midst of thee" (Exod. 23:25).\n\n> (4) This covenant became part of the New Covenant, for Christ "took our infirmities and bare our sicknesses" (Isa. 53; Mt. 8:17; 1 Pet. 2:24).\n\n## X. The Palestinian Covenant (Deut. 27:1–30:20)\n\nGod made this covenant with Israel through Moses, and it was conditional upon Israel's obedience (Deut. 11:8-32; 27:1–30:20; Lev. 26:1-46). It has seven parts:\n\n> 1. Dispersion for disobedience (Deut. 30:1; 28:63-68).\n\n> 2. Repentance while scattered among the nations (Deut. 30:2; Zech. 12:10-14).\n\n> 3. The return of the Lord (Deut. 30:3; Acts 15:14-17; Zech. 14).\n\n> 4. Restoration to the land (Deut. 30:5; Isa. 11:1-12; Mt. 24:31; Ezek. 37).\n\n> 5. National conversion (Deut. 30:6; Rom. 11:26, 27; Zech. 12:10-13; Isa. 66).\n\n> 6. Judgment on Israel's oppressors (Deut. 30:7; Mt. 25:31-46; Zech. 14).\n\n> 7. National prosperity (Deut. 30:9-10; Rom. 11).\n\n## XI. The Covenant of Levi (Num. 25:10-14)\n\nGod gave this covenant through Moses to Phinehas, the son of Levi, who was zealous for the Lord and executed judgment upon rebels (Num. 25:1-9). It contained two great promises:\n\n> 1. A covenant of peace and blessing to Levi's house (Num. 25:12).\n\n> 2. An everlasting priesthood (Num. 25:13).\n\n## XII. The Covenant of Salt (Lev. 2:13; Num. 18:19)\n\nGod made this covenant with Israel concerning the sacrifices they were to offer forever. You can see how salt is used symbolically in Mk. 9:49, 50; Col. 4:6.\n\nHere's something interesting: In Palestine and the surrounding regions, salt was used when making covenants. If two people — even the worst of enemies — ate food with salt together, they became friends. It's similar to how we might seal a business deal with a handshake today.\n\nThe Arab expression is "There is salt between us" or "He has eaten of my salt," meaning they've shared hospitality that creates a lasting bond. Since covenants were typically confirmed at sacrificial meals and salt was always present, this covenant of salt represented the everlasting friendship between God and His people (Num. 18:19).\n\n## XIII. The Davidic Covenant (2 Sam. 7:1-17)\n\nGod made this covenant with David and his descendants through Nathan the prophet. Like the other covenants, it was conditioned upon obedience. This everlasting covenant contains seven blessings:\n\n> 1. A Davidic house forever (2 Sam. 7:13-16; Ps. 89:20-37; Lk. 1:32-35).\n\n> 2. A Davidic throne forever (2 Sam. 7:12-16; Isa. 9:6, 7; Lk. 1:32-35).\n\n> 3. A Davidic kingdom forever (2 Sam. 7:12-16; Isa. 9:6, 7; Lk. 1:32-35).\n\n> 4. A secure land for Israel forever (2 Sam. 7:10; Gen. 17).\n\n> 5. Freedom from affliction by other nations forever (2 Sam. 7:10; Deut. 28:1–30:10).\n\n> 6. The fatherly care of God forever (2 Sam. 7:14; 2 Cor. 6:15-18).\n\n> 7. An eternal covenant (2 Sam. 7:10-16; Isa. 9:6, 7; Lk. 1:32, 33).\n\n## XIV. and XV. The Old and New Covenants\n\nThe Old and New Covenants, along with the relationship between law and grace, were covered in Lesson Thirty-two.\n\n## XVI. Examining British-Israelism\n\nNow let's turn our attention to this subject with a sincere desire to help you see what the Bible actually teaches about the Anglo-Saxon theory. Here's what this teaching claims:\n\n> 1. The ten tribes of Israel from the Northern Kingdom lost their identity for centuries, and the British and Anglo-Saxons are actually those lost tribes.\n\n> 2. The king of England is the true descendant and legal heir to David's throne over the ten lost tribes — but not over the Jews.\n\n> 3. All the promises made to Israel are being or will be fulfilled in the English-speaking peoples, not in the Jews.\n\n> 4. There's a vital distinction between Israel and the Jews, and each has a different destiny.\n\n> 5. The terms "Israel," "Judah," "Jews," "house of Israel," and "house of Judah" are never used interchangeably in Scripture.\n\n> 6. Because Anglo-Saxons are so great and powerful, they must be the ten lost tribes of the house of Israel.\n\n> 7. The Israel mentioned in Isaiah, Jeremiah, all the prophets, the Gospels, Acts, and the Epistles had no tribe of Judah in it.\n\n> 8. The Jews descended only from the tribe of Judah.\n\n> 9. Jesus Christ and none of the apostles were Jewish.\n\n### The Following Points Prove Anglo-Saxonism to Be Unscriptural:\n\n> 1. Judah was one of the twelve sons of Abraham through Isaac and Jacob. He was of the same stock and race as all Israelites (Gen. 29:28-35; 30:1-24).\n\n> 2. Judah was chosen as the ruling tribe among the twelve (Gen. 49:8-10; 2 Sam. 7; 1 Ki. 12:16-19; Mic. 5:1, 2; etc.).\n\n> 3. Balaam, who was hired by Balak to curse the twelve tribes of Israel as they came out of Egypt, said of all Israel, "The people shall dwell alone, and shall not be reckoned among the nations" (Num. 23:7-12). Think about this: Anglo-Saxons throughout history have never dwelled alone or separate from other nations. They've always been counted among the nations of the world.\n\n> 4. Israel outside the Promised Land was never supposed to receive the covenant blessings (Gen. 28:15; Deut. 5:16; 11:9; 25:19; 28:8, 11; 31:13; etc.). The tribes of Israel are scattered among the nations today because of a curse (Lev. 26:32-39; Deut. 28:63-65; Jer. 4:23-27). Prophecy tells us that this same promised land will be given back to them in the Millennium, when all twelve tribes will become one nation again in that land (Isa. 11:10-15; Ezek. 20:33-35; 37:1-28; 43:7; 47:13–48:35; Mt. 24:31; Rom. 11:25-29; Rev. 7:1-7). If Anglo-Saxons were Israel, this would require the people of England, America, Ireland, Scotland, Sweden, and other countries to leave their lands and all move back to Palestine!\n\n> 5. Israel outside the land was to be "few in number" (Deut. 4:26-27; 28:63-68). Weren't these promises spoken to all twelve tribes? This has been and continues to be fulfilled with the Jews, so why wouldn't the same apply to the ten tribes who also disobeyed and were cursed? If the Jews fulfill this prophecy and Anglo-Saxons don't, then which group is actually the Israel upon whom this curse was pronounced?\n\n> 6. The penalty for being uncircumcised was being "cut off" (Gen. 17:10–34:15, 24; Exod. 12:48). Every uncircumcised man was to be excluded from the covenants and promises. Circumcision was a physical sign of an everlasting covenant. So here's the question: If Anglo-Saxons are enjoying the benefits of the Old Covenant, why aren't they circumcised? If the penalty was being "cut off," then uncircumcised Anglo-Saxons have been cut off — no matter how much they claim to be the rightful heirs. And if they're under the New Covenant and don't need circumcision, they can't simultaneously be under the Old Covenant receiving its blessings. Under the New Covenant, they have no more privileges than any other nation, since all people from all nations share equally in the New Covenant blessings (Jn. 3:3-5, 14-18; 3:36; 5:24; Acts 10:11-24, 34, 35; 11:5-18; 13:44-52; 15:7-11; Rom. 1:16; 2:9-11; 10:9-13; 1 Cor. 12:13; Gal. 3:28; Col. 3:11).\n\n> 7. The godly people from the ten tribes stayed loyal to David when the kingdom divided (1 Ki. 12:17-23; 2 Chron. 11:5-17; 10:17; 15:9; 16:1-6; 30:1-11; etc.). The ungodly were cut off and lost their place in the covenant fulfillment (2 Ki. 17:7-23) until they repent at Christ's second coming (Rom. 11:25-29; Acts 15:13-18; Mt. 23:37-39).\n\n> 8. The ten tribes continually returned to Judah and the worship of the true God, becoming part of the Southern Kingdom. That's why they could be called Jews just as much as the Benjamites and others (1 Chron. 9:3; 2 Chron. 10:17; 11:3-17; 15:9; 16:1; 19:8; 23:2; 29:24; 30:1-25; 31:6; 35:17, 18). These passages prove that Israelites from all ten tribes returned to Judah when Jeroboam set up idols, and all of these from the twelve tribes were called Jews. Remember, the ten tribes left the Northern Kingdom "in abundance" and settled in Judah's cities. They weren't taken captive to Assyria with the Northern Kingdom but were later taken to Babylon with the Southern Kingdom. So we have all twelve tribes still represented in the kingdom of Judah.\n\n> 9. The captives returning from Babylon are called "all Israel" (Ezra 2:2, 59, 70; 3:1; 6:21; 7:10-13; 8:25; 9:1; 10:1-5, 10, 25; Neh. 2:10; 7:7, 61, 73; 8:17; 10:33; 11:3; 12:47). This shows that the Jews after the captivities still represented "all Israel."\n\n> 10. "All Israel" was permitted to return from Babylon because they were taken captive together, as these passages explain (Ezra 1:3-5; 1 Chron. 9:1-3; Ezra 2:2, 59, 70; 3:1; 6:16, 17, 21; 7:7-10).\n\n> 11. The Southern Kingdom after the ten tribes' captivity is called "Israel" (2 Chron. 31:1-6, 8, 20; 34:6, 7, 9, 33; 35:3, 17, 18, 25).\n\n> 12. Tribes other than Judah were called "Jews" during the captivities (Esther 2:5; 3:4-6; 5:13; 6:10; 8:7-17; 9:1-32; 10:1-3). Were these multitudes of Jews in all 120 provinces of Persia from the tribe of Judah only? Of course not!\n\n> 13. People from other nations became "Jews" during the captivities (Esther 8:17). The word "Jews" doesn't refer only to nationality or the tribe of Judah, as British-Israelism teaches. If other nations could become Jews, why couldn't other tribes of Israel be called Jews, even though they weren't born into the tribe of Judah?\n\n> 14. Men from tribes other than Judah are called Jews. Jesus was a Jew from the tribe of Judah (Jn. 4:9; Rev. 5:6; Heb. 7:14). The apostles were Jews (Acts 10:28; Gal. 2:11-15). Paul was a Benjamite and was called a Jew (Acts 21:39; 22:3; Phil. 3). Men from all the tribes of Israel, as well as Gentile converts, are called Jews in Acts 2:5. This proves that "Jew" doesn't mean only those born into the tribe of Judah. It also proves that Christ and the apostles were Jews.\n\n> 15. In the Palestinian Covenant, God said it would apply to "all the men of Israel" (Deut. 29:9-12). It also included Gentiles living within their borders. This proves that Judah is just as much God's chosen people as the ten tribes. All the curses applied to the ten tribes as well as the two tribes (Deut. 29:18-30). This directly contradicts Anglo-Israel teaching, which claims the curses were fulfilled only with the Jews. All twelve tribes were to be scattered among the nations the same way, and all were to remember the broken covenant while among the nations and return together. "Then the Lord thy God will turn thy captivity, and have compassion on thee, and will return thee from all nations, whither the Lord thy God hath scattered thee . . . bring thee into the land which thy fathers possessed . . . and multiply thee above thy fathers" (Deut. 30:1-20; Isa. 11:10-12; Jer. 31-32; Ezek. 37).\n\n> 16. The word "Israel" is used 160 times for all twelve tribes before 1 Ki. 12, when the kingdom divided. This was the "Israel" that David ruled and that was promised an everlasting kingdom. Judah has just as much right to this fulfillment as the ten tribes — in fact, more, since Judah was the ruling tribe and would always be (Gen. 49:8-10).\n\n> 17. The following terms are used for the same captives from Babylon who represented all Israel: "fathers of Judah and Benjamin" (Ezra 1:5); "fathers of Israel" (Ezra 4:3; 8:29); "elders of the Jews" (Ezra 5:5; 6:7, 8, 14); and "children of Israel" (Ezra 6:21; 7:7, 10, 13, 28; 9:1; 10:5). After the captivities, tribal distinctions broke down because there weren't enough people from each tribe to return and possess all twelve original plots of land. They kept their tribal identity by race but not so much by location. In Ezra alone, the Bible refers to the returned captives as "Jews" eight times, "Israel" forty times, and "Judah" fifteen times. In Nehemiah, "Jews" appears ten times, "Israel" twenty-two times, and "Judah" twenty-eight times.\n\n> 18. Anglo-Saxonism teaches that the Israel of the prophets and New Testament had no tribe of Judah in it. The following points from Isaiah to Malachi prove that the terms "house of Israel," "house of David," "house of Judah," "house of Jacob," "Jews," "Judah," and "Israel" are used interchangeably throughout. If this is true even once, the entire foundation of the Anglo-Saxon theory crumbles.\n\n> In the prophetic books, the terms "Jews" and "Judah" appear 528 times, while "Israel" appears only 487 times. The phrase "God of Israel" is used about 104 times, and in nearly every case it refers to Judah. In many of these 487 uses of "Israel," the word actually refers to Judah, as we'll see.\n\n> Nearly all the prophets from Isaiah to Malachi were written to Judah and Jerusalem. This is true of Isaiah (1:1; 2:1; 3:1); Jeremiah (1:1-3, 15-19); Lamentations (1:3-8, 17); Ezekiel (1:1-2; 4:1); Joel (2:1, 23; 3:1); Zephaniah (1:1, 12; 2:7); Haggai (1:1; 2:2); Zechariah (1:1, 12-21; 2:2); and Malachi (1:1; 2:11; 3:4). Daniel was a prophet of Judah living in Babylonian captivity, and his prophecies primarily concern his own people in the latter days (1:1; 2:25-28; 7:13-14, 18-27; 8:23-25; 9:2, 20-27; 11:40-45; 12:1-13). Hosea and Amos were prophets in the Northern Kingdom of Israel, Micah was a prophet in Judah at the same period, and all three prophesied concerning the whole house of Israel, which was then divided into two kingdoms (Hos. 1:1-11; 2:1; 3:4-5; 4:15; 6:4; 11:12; Amos 1:1-2; 3:1; 6:1; 9:11-15; Mic. 1:1, 5-16; 3:8-12; 5:1-2). Obadiah, Nahum, and Jonah were Jewish prophets to surrounding heathen nations. Obadiah prophesied about Edom and Judah. Jonah and Nahum prophesied about Nineveh's fall. Habakkuk was a prophet in Judah who foretold the Jewish captivities and how God would do a great spiritual work among the heathen during the Jews' dispersion (Hab. 1:5; Acts 13:41).\n\n> 19. In Isaiah, Judah is called "Israel" about 93 times (1:1, 3; 4:2-3; etc.). Zion, or the Jews, are called God's chosen people (Isa. 51:16; 65:9). They will be blessed forever (Isa. 11:10-12; 65:18-19; 66:7-24; Ezek. 37; Rom. 11:25-29). The kingdom of Judah and David was to be cut off because of sin until the Messiah comes, when it will be restored forever (Isa. 9:6-7; Jer. 21:24-30; 32:37-44; 35:7-26; 60:4-5; Ezek. 11:17-35; Rom. 11:25-29; Lk. 1:32-33; Rev. 11:15; 19:11-21; Zech. 14).\n\n> 20. Jeremiah was written to Judah 133 years after the ten tribes were taken captive. He refers to the Jews in Jerusalem before and during the Babylonian exile as Israel 120 times (Jer. 1:1-3; 2:2-4; 4:1-3; 5:1, 15, 20; 6:1-9; 10:1, 22; 18:6, 11-16; 19:1-13; 29:22-23; etc.). Judah is called Israel because it was the only kingdom of Israel in existence at that time. Both the children of Israel and the children of Judah are referred to as being one nation of Jews who would be punished by Babylon, proving that the Jews represented all Israel (Jer. 32:26-36). After the captivity, Jeremiah said all Israel would be gathered again at Jerusalem (Jer. 31:31-40; 32:37-44; 33:14-26). So the Jews who returned from Babylon were from all Israel. Those returning today are also from all Israel (Ezek. 36:16-38; 37:1-28; Rom. 11:25-29; Acts 15:13-18; Heb. 8:10-13). Jeremiah speaks of Israelites from all tribes under Zedekiah as "Jews" and "Hebrews" (Jer. 34:8-15). The term "Hebrews" is used for all Israel before the kingdom divided about 25 times (Gen. 40:15; Exod. 1:15-19; 1 Sam. 4:6-9; etc.) and about 15 times for the same people after the Babylonian captivity. Jews and Hebrews are the same people in the New Testament (Lk. 23:38; Acts 6:1; 2 Cor. 11:22; Phil. 3:5). Both Israel and Judah are described as being oppressed at the same time in Babylon, so they must be the same people (Jer. 50:17-20). Both were to return from Babylon together (Jer. 50:33-34; 51:5-6). The captives who returned under Ezra and Nehemiah weren't the only ones who came back, because Israel was a great nation in 70 A.D. when Jerusalem was destroyed. At that time, in Jerusalem alone, one million Jews were killed and 97,000 taken captive by the Romans.\n\n> 21. In Ezekiel, the Jews under Zedekiah are called the children of Israel, a rebellious nation, and the House of Israel about 82 times (Ezek. 2:3; 3:1-17; 4:13-17; 5:4-10; 6:2-11; 7:2-27; 8:1-18; 9:4-9; 11:1-16; 12:6-28; 13:2-9; 14:1-21; 17:2-24; 18:2-32; 19:1-14; 20:1-44; 21:1-32; 22:1-22; 33:1-20; 34:1-10; etc.). In Ezek. 37, we're told that the two nations of Israel will become one nation again. Since modern Jews are the only ones fulfilling this prophecy by returning to Palestine, they must be the Israel referred to. Not one Anglo-Saxon is fulfilling this and many other Scriptures by going back to Palestine.\n\n> In Ezek. 40–48, we find nine chapters predicting in detail the final restoration of the Jews, or all Israel, to the promised land. The borders run from Ha\n\n---\n\nThese fifteen covenants are not isolated agreements — they build on each other, each one advancing God's comprehensive plan from eternity past to eternity future. Every covenant you've studied is a chapter in a single story. So here's the question: Which of these fifteen covenants most directly applies to your life right now? And are you actually living in the benefits that covenant provides — or treating it as historical background rather than present reality?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk48k001oewhjlui56t9w	cmnknk48e000xewhj49ao5az0	Supplement 18 — For Lessons 35 & 36	SUPPLEMENT	1163	26	\N	t	Eternal security rests on God's faithfulness, not yours. And God's fifteen great covenants show that He keeps every promise He makes — across centuries, across generations, regardless of the failures of the people involved. This supplement is where those two truths produce something practical: confidence that doesn't depend on your performance.\n\nNow, some people object to what we're teaching about these blessings. They wonder why God would want to give us these blessings today more than He has in the past. Others argue that since God knows what we need, if it's His will to bless us, He'll do so without us putting forth any effort. Still others reason that if God wanted to give them these blessings, He would have already—so since He hasn't, it must not be His will.\n\nThese arguments are really just excuses rooted in unbelief. They prove nothing about what God actually wants for us.\n\n![Supplement 18 — For Lessons 35 & 36](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OstWtBlASSw8OsqyXxGpeBMuoLIEnWg5fdi9R1)\n\n## God Promised to Pour Out His Spirit in the Last Days\n\nGod always keeps His Word, and this is one of the greatest reasons for the present revival of faith and power among Christians. On the day of Pentecost, Peter declared:\n\n> "But this is that which was spoken by the prophet Joel; and it shall come to pass in the last days, saith God, I will pour out my Spirit upon all flesh: and your sons and daughters shall prophesy, and your young men shall see visions, and your old men shall dream dreams: and on my servants and on my handmaidens I will pour out in those days of my Spirit; and they shall prophesy. And I will show wonders in heaven above, and signs in the earth beneath; blood, and fire, and vapour of smoke: the sun shall be turned into darkness, and the moon into blood, before that great and notable day of the Lord come: And it shall come to pass, that whosoever shall call on the name of the Lord shall be saved" (Acts 2:14-21).\n\n## God and Satan in a Final Contest\n\nGod didn't just inspire Peter to predict revival in the last days. He also inspired others to warn that satanic powers would become increasingly active in this time (Mt. 24:24; 1 Tim. 4; 2 Tim. 3; 2 Thess. 2:1-12; Rev. 13:1-18; 16:13-16).\n\nBut here's the encouraging news: as these dark powers increase, God's power will also increase to counteract them and prove to humanity that He is the true God (Dan. 12:3; Jas. 5:7; Rev. 11:3-7; 12:11; Acts 2:14-21). The gifts of the Holy Spirit will be exercised in all their fullness, and God will once again demonstrate He is more powerful than Satan (1 Cor 12:1-11).\n\n## God Knows What We Have Need Of\n\nNobody's arguing this point—but using this fact as an excuse for unbelief is inexcusable. Just because God knows what you need doesn't mean He'll automatically meet those needs without your participation.\n\nThink of it like a scholarship fund. The money might be there, set aside for students, but you still have to apply for it. There must be faith and conformity to God's conditions before you can receive these benefits.\n\nThe requirement is clear: "But seek ye first the kingdom of God, and his righteousness; and all these things shall be added unto you" (Mt. 6:33).\n\nIf you want something badly enough, you'll put forth the necessary effort to get it. And honestly, the person who refuses to obey God and do what He requires isn't worthy of answered prayer.\n\nIf you want salvation from sin and bad habits, you'll repent and refuse to live in sin. You'll turn to God and serve Him with your whole heart. Then—and only then—will God deliver you.\n\nIf you want healing, you'll believe that healing is in the atonement, accept Christ as your healer, and have faith that you are healed when you pray.\n\nIf you need prosperity or help with material concerns or business problems, you'll take your need to God in prayer, commit it all to Him, and believe He will bring you through.\n\nWhatever you want from God that's promised in the Bible—if you want it badly enough—you'll get down to business and conform to God's laws that enable you to receive it.\n\n## Don't Play With Religion\n\nToo many people play around with religion. We'll use good sense in every other area of life to succeed, but when it comes to spiritual matters? We throw common sense out the window.\n\nHere's a sobering thought: if most people made a living the same way they handle their spiritual lives, they'd starve to death.\n\nSome folks will faithfully carry out the most intricate details in their careers or hobbies to succeed. But those same people won't take time to learn how to succeed spiritually, and they won't obey the simplest laws of answered prayer. Then they wonder why their prayers aren't answered!\n\nThey seem to think any hit-or-miss approach is fine when it comes to religion. They're quick to accept unanswered prayer—in fact, they're resigned to failure before they even pray.\n\nIf they're seeking deliverance from sin or sickness, they're satisfied with the outcome whether they get what they asked for or not. They take the same attitude with business problems—ignoring divine laws and trying every means of success except God.\n\nFailure in answered prayer is expected from the start. It's taken for granted that things can't be any different. Let someone suggest it might not be God's will for them to get what they desire, and immediately they accept this as settled, unalterable fact. The smallest hindrance causes them to surrender any hope of getting an answer.\n\nThis is simply playing with religion. The devil himself stands by laughing at this helplessness, highly pleased at how easily he keeps people from putting forth enough effort to get what they want from God.\n\nHe sidetracks people so easily—he knows exactly what to do and how long it takes to turn them from their purpose. He even gets people to blame God for their failures, or convinces them to abandon their purpose if the answer doesn't come the instant they pray.\n\nSome are foolish enough to listen to him. They cooperate with satanic powers and are more satisfied living in defeat than being bold and determined to get what is rightfully theirs in Christ.\n\n## God is Not Responsible for Suffering and Failure\n\nAs we've seen before, Satan, demons, and sin cause the troubles in this fallen world. We would have had no failure, no sickness, and no suffering if humanity hadn't sinned and given dominion over to the devil.\n\nThis alone proves God isn't responsible for such conditions in the human race. Yet the average Christian will blame God for their troubles.\n\nPreachers write books and deliver sermons on how God wills and causes suffering. On my desk as I write is a book by a preacher titled "Why Do Christians Suffer?" The author spends all his time trying to show that God authors suffering and sends it upon people.\n\nThis is only part of the truth. Yes, God made laws, and when people break those laws, they pay the penalties. God is responsible for making the laws and penalties, but He's not responsible for people breaking them, and He's not the one executing judgment. Satan, fallen angels, and demons execute the penalties for sin in the human race, as we saw in Lesson Fourteen, Point VI.\n\nHere's the truth: if God's will were obeyed by everyone, there would be no reaping for wrong sowing. There would be no sin, sickness, or death—no suffering of any kind.\n\nThis will be Earth's condition when God rules here (Rev. 21:1-7). This would be the condition now if God ruled here. Clearly, the rule of others on Earth who rebel against God has caused the sin and suffering we see everywhere.\n\nThe preacher I mentioned gives many Scriptures to prove God sends suffering, but he doesn't make clear in what sense God sends it. It's because people sin and disobey that certain sufferings are permitted to come upon them.\n\nThey have to reap what they sow. Therefore, God isn't directly causing this suffering—people and demons are. All suffering that doesn't result from personal sin is either the work of the devil (Acts 10:38; Lk. 13:14-16; Jn. 10:10) or the work of wicked people and demons who make war on Christians.\n\nNeither of these sources is from God. Just because God tolerates them due to the present curse doesn't prove He wills or sends such sufferings.\n\nJust because Paul said he was appointed to suffer for the gospel doesn't prove God sends or wills suffering. God knew Paul would face persecution for the gospel because it was new and contrary to existing beliefs. Naturally, this new faith would face conflicts, and those who preached it would suffer persecution.\n\nToday, in Christian lands, Paul wouldn't face the same sufferings because Christianity is the prevailing religion. So no Christian today should use Paul's experience to excuse unbelief or give in to the devil's attacks.\n\nEven Paul's suffering mentioned in 1 Thess. 3:3 wasn't caused by physical disease or sin—things Christ bore on the cross to remove. There's no comparison between Paul's situation and modern people suffering needlessly with pain and sickness because they refuse to accept the benefits Jesus died to provide.\n\nWhen Peter said we were called to suffer (1 Pet. 2:21), he couldn't mean we're called to bear the sins and sicknesses Christ bore on the cross (1 Pet. 2:24). The suffering he means is what Christ endured for righteousness' sake—revilings and persecutions for doing well (1 Pet. 2:20-23).\n\nChristians aren't called to live in sin, suffer sicknesses, and fail in life. Freedom from these things is exactly what Christ died to give us, as we've seen before.\n\nYes, tribulations, persecutions, trials, and hardships may come to Christians during certain periods due to wars against Christianity, and all believers are called to endure during such times. This caused the sufferings in the early Church.\n\nLocal persecutions for doing good may happen anywhere. Temptations and spiritual warfare with demons occur wherever saved people are, and these are things believers go through.\n\nBut to use these kinds of suffering as an excuse to tolerate sin, sickness, bad habits, financial failure, and poverty in America and other Christian nations? That's one of the biggest blunders in Christianity today.\n\nIt would only be if atheism gained the upper hand and Christians faced persecution again that believers would experience the tribulation mentioned in Jn. 16:33; Acts 14:22; 1 Thess. 3:4; Phil. 1:29.\n\nScriptures like Isa. 54:7-8; Num. 13:33–14:5; Jer. 29:10; Deut. 28:1-64; 33:39 and other passages about Israel's sufferings can't be applied to Christians who obey God. These sufferings wouldn't have happened even to Israel if they had obeyed God.\n\nGod permitted Israel's sufferings because of sin. He predicted they would suffer if they disobeyed, because then He couldn't protect them from the devil.\n\nFor Christians to use such passages to prove God wants them defeated, sick, sinful, poor, and plagued with calamities shows either ignorance or rebellion against God's Word. There's really no excuse for such slander against God or such ignorance of truth—if only people would be sensible and study the facts.\n\nIt wasn't God's will for Israel to suffer those defeats. He showed them the way out if they would have listened. The same is true today—it's not God's will for Christians to suffer such defeats if they'll take His plan and cooperate with Him.\n\nMay God save us from this modern fallacy that God gets glory from seeing His children in trouble. He doesn't like seeing them suffer defeat, live in sin, die from diseases, be full of despair and unbelief, or be given over to fallen angels and demons.\n\nThe Scriptures mentioned above specifically state why God permitted people to suffer defeats and plagues. They can't be used to prove that God directly wills for people to suffer things Christ died to deliver them from.\n\nGod always tells people it's because of sin that they're punished, and this will always be the case (Ezra 9:13; Ps. 103:10; Deut. 28; Lev. 26; Gal. 6:7-8; Heb. 10:26-29; 2 Pet. 2:20-22).\n\nIf you'll obey the Lord and follow what we're teaching, there will be no failure to get from God what He has promised. You can get anything you want in life. We've proven this in Lesson Ten and other lessons on prayer and faith.\n\nDon't let modern theories of unbelief rob you of these wonderful blessings in Christ. In future supplements, we'll go more fully into proof along these lines. Until then, we'll continue praying for you and with you for the fullness of Christ's gospel in your experience and in the lives of your fellow students.\n\n---\n\n## Ten Questions For Supplement Eighteen: Do You Know...\n\n1. **That it is possible for every Christian on Earth today to experience the power of God to do the works of Christ?** We've abundantly proven this in Lessons Twenty, Twenty-two, Twenty-six, Twenty-eight, and Thirty. You can't believe the many plain Scriptures proving this and still hold the unbelieving theories of men. These two positions can't be reconciled. To be honest, we have to stand with the Bible: all things Christ did by the anointing of the Spirit, believers can also do, as stated in Jn. 14:12-15; 15:7, 16; 16:23-26; Mt. 28:20; Mk. 16:15-20; 1 Cor. 12:1-11; etc.\n\n2. **That the great American desert will soon be like the Garden of Eden?** Many Scriptures teach that the desert shall blossom like a rose and waters shall spring forth in the desert during the Millennium (Isa. 35:1-8; 43:19). After that, the whole Earth will be made new, like a beautiful garden (Isa. 65:17; 66:22-24; 2 Pet. 3:13; Rev. 21-22).\n\n3. **That more men will be called to the ministry during the last seven years of this age than in any seven years in human history?** During the future tribulation, there will be the greatest revival ever witnessed. When the Church is raptured, people will realize they're in the final years of this age, and multitudes will turn to God and start preaching the gospel. This is predicted in Acts 2:14-21; Rev. 6:9-11; 7:1-17; 12:11, 17; 15:2-4; 20:4-6. There will be 144,000 Jews who get saved and become servants of God (Rev. 7:1-8; 14:1-5). It's doubtful this many Jews have become preachers in all of this age so far. Then a multitude of Gentiles from all nations will be destroyed because of their preaching (Rev. 7:9-17; 15:2-4; 20:4-6). None of these Jews or Gentiles will be saved at the rapture, or they would have been caught up to meet the Lord (1 Thess. 4:16). So they not only become witnesses after the rapture—they become saved after it.\n\n4. **That all future saints will not be changed from mortality to immortality by being resurrected?** All future saints will never be resurrected because God's plan permits earthly saints in the Millennium and New Earth to continue eternally as natural earthly people—just as Adam and the whole race would have lived if man hadn't fallen. After the resurrection is past, people will continue being born forever, as we proved in Supplement Two, Question Two. These people will never participate in the resurrection because they'll never die. They'll eat from the Tree of Life and live forever, just as Adam could have done (Rev. 22:1-5).\n\n5. **That no man can be saved and not know it?** Paul said if anyone is in Christ, he is a new creature—old things pass away and all things become new (2 Cor. 5:17). He also said everyone belonging to Christ has crucified the flesh with its affections and lusts (Gal. 5:24). Can this happen to someone without their knowledge? Such a transformation couldn't possibly occur without the person being aware of it. If this is true, those who don't know they've become new creatures aren't in Christ—regardless of what they profess.\n\n6. **That the devil never enters bodily into anyone?** He's an angel with a spirit-body, as proven in Lesson Six, Point X, so it would be impossible for him to fit his body inside a human body. If he left his body, he would be dead (Jas. 2:26), and the Bible doesn't teach that about angels. The only sense in which Satan enters anyone is by being in union with them, as explained in Lesson Four, Point I, 4.\n\n7. **That it is not God's direct will for any man to be a sinner or die of disease?** If God's perfect will were done on Earth as it is in Heaven, there would have been no sin or sickness at all. When God's will is finally done on Earth as in Heaven, there will again be no death or sin (Rev. 21:1-7). Since this is true, it cannot be God's highest will for people to suffer sickness and die in sin today (1 Pet. 2:24; 3 Jn. 2).\n\n8. **That Noah did not preach 120 years while building the ark?** The fact is, he didn't take that long to build the ark, as proven in Supplement One, Question 4, so he certainly couldn't have preached only during that time. Scripture doesn't state how long he preached. No doubt he preached for several hundred years, since he was righteous in his generation—600 years before the flood and 350 years after, with the exception of getting drunk one time.\n\n9. **That the devil, the beast, and the false prophet are three separate and distinct persons?** The idea that the beast is the devil incarnate and there are only two persons referred to by these terms is unscriptural. This is clear because the dragon (a symbol of Satan) is always seen as separate from the beast and the false prophet (Rev. 12:7-17; 13:1-18; 16:13-16; 19:19-21; 20:10). If all three are seen as separate persons in these passages, then Satan is never incarnate in the beast, and there must be three distinct persons.\n\n10. **That angels will be judged by human beings?** Paul plainly states this in 1 Cor. 6:3. This no doubt refers to the judgment of angels (2 Pet. 2:4; Jude 6-7) and to the position of glorified saints in the eternal kingdom as heirs of God and joint-heirs with Christ over the vast creations (Rom. 8:17-18; Rev. 5:9-10; 21:7). This doesn't mean saints will take positions held by faithful angels from ages past, but that humans will rule over fallen angels. Saints may even hold higher positions than good angels—that remains to be seen.\n\n---\n\nGod has kept every covenant He ever made — including the ones His people broke repeatedly. Your security in Christ is not dependent on your grip but on His. So here's the question: Are you living with the settled confidence of someone held by a faithful God — or are you still privately uncertain about your standing with Him? And what specific promise from one of Scripture's fifteen covenants can you stand on today?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk4bq001uewhjaem8jtdw	cmnknk4bp001qewhjk5bcqnfj	Lesson 39 — The Book of Daniel	READING	5292	3	\N	t	The Book of Daniel contains some of the most precise prophetic predictions in all of Scripture — including an exact timeline for the Messiah's arrival that Daniel wrote six centuries before Jesus was born. It's also one of the most attacked books in the Bible. Critics have argued for two centuries that it must have been written after the events it describes, because no one could actually know the future. But that argument proves too much — it proves exactly what Daniel claims: that the God of Israel knows the end from the beginning.\n\nLast time we mapped the seven judgments of Scripture — from the cross to the Great White Throne. Now we turn to the prophet whose visions form the backbone of all end-times prophecy: Daniel. Understanding Daniel is essential for understanding Revelation — and for understanding the age we're living in.\n\nNow, we're not claiming to be infallible ourselves. But we do claim that every simple statement and event in prophecy that God has given us is absolutely infallible.\n\nWe claim—for God and by His authority—that the Bible gives predictions of things to come that are 100 percent correct. Not 80 percent correct, as some modern prophets of current events suggest. The following 110 future prophetic wonders are plainly stated, and we'll deal with them in their proper order of fulfillment as nearly as possible.\n\n![Lesson 39 — The Book of Daniel](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsPNv5Wso536itGQmsLn2KwlakR9vFryYAfXZB)\n\n## 110 Prophetic Wonders From 1949 Into Eternity\n\n1. **Israel firmly established as a nation in Palestine.** This is now in process of fulfillment, as we've already explained in Lesson Eighteen, Points V and VI.\n\n2. **The revision of the Roman Empire into ten separate kingdoms ruled by ten kings**—the result of the first tri-continent war (Dan. 7:7, 8, 19-25). This will mean the defeat of Communism and Russia. In the formation of these ten kingdoms, Russia must give up her control of Austria, Hungary, Romania, Bulgaria, Albania, and all parts of the old Roman Empire. So we can say with definite authority that Russia and Communism will never rule the world.\n\n3. **The rapture of the Church—the end of the Church Age** (1 Thess. 4:13-17; 5:9; 2 Thess. 2:7, 8; 1 Cor. 15:23, 51-58; Phil. 3:20, 21; Rev. 4:1; etc.). It's not clear whether the rapture will come before or after the formation of the ten kingdoms inside the old Roman Empire. The rapture could take place today without any prophecy being fulfilled and without any sign that the end of the age is coming to pass. Both the revision of Rome and the rapture could happen at any time. When each will be fulfilled, no one knows yet. The rapture will take place in a moment, whereas it will take longer for Rome to be revised following the war that causes the ten kingdoms to form.\n\n4. **A world revival or spiritual awakening** (Acts 2:16-21; Jas. 5:7; Rev. 7:9-17; 12:12, 17; 15:2-4). There may be a great spiritual awakening before the rapture, but this isn't definite—nothing needs to happen before the rapture for it to be fulfilled. If there's no religious revival before the rapture, we know from these passages there will be one after the rapture, during the future tribulation days when God shows wonders in Heaven and on Earth, right up to the great and dreadful day of the Lord itself (Acts 2:16-21). We hope and pray there will be a revival in our day before the rapture, but to definitely teach that it must happen is to teach that the rapture cannot come any day before such a revival—and no one has authority to say this. True believers are always ready for the rapture, so a religious revival doesn't have to come to get the Church ready, as some men teach. If we don't have definite Scripture to prove an event will happen, we're not going to make such a prediction.\n\n5. **The rise of the Antichrist** (Dan. 7:7-8, 19-25; 8:8-9, 20-25; 9:27; 11:35-45; 2 Thess. 2:1-12; Rev. 6:1-2). By this we mean the Antichrist coming out of one of the ten kingdoms at the beginning of Daniel's Seventieth Week (Dan. 9:27). This Week, as well as the rise of the Antichrist, must take place after the rapture and after the ten kingdoms are formed (Dan. 7:23-24; 2 Thess. 2:7-8).\n\n6. **The seven-years covenant between Antichrist and the new nation of Israel** (Dan. 9:27). These seven years of the covenant will be the last seven years of this Age of Grace in which we now live. This seven years will be the Tribulation period, as we'll see in Lesson Forty.\n\n7. **The building of the Jewish temple at Jerusalem** (Dan. 9:27; Mt. 24:15; Rev. 11:1-2). We'll discuss this event in Lesson Forty-One. Whether the temple will be built before the seven-years covenant is not clear, but it must be built by the middle of the Week. That's when the Antichrist breaks his seven-years covenant with Israel and does away with their worship in the temple. He then sets himself up in the temple, declaring himself to be God (Dan. 9:27; 11:45; Mt. 24:15; 2 Thess. 2:3-4; Rev. 11:1-2; 13:1-18).\n\n8. **The Revival of the Grecian Empire—the second tri-continent war** (Dan. 7:23-24; 8:8-9, 23-24; 11:35-45; Rev. 13:1-7; 17:8-17). After the ten kingdoms form inside the old Roman Empire from the first tri-continent war, and after these ten kingdoms (the seventh kingdom of Rev. 17:9-12) exist "a short space," the Antichrist comes out of one of these ten kingdoms. He overthrows three others of the ten and forms the eighth and last kingdom, symbolized by the leopard of Rev. 13 and 17. It will take Antichrist the first 3½ years of his seven-years covenant with Israel to overthrow these three kingdoms and get power over the other six, forming the eighth kingdom which will rule for the last 3½ years of this age. We'll explain this more fully later in our study of Daniel.\n\n9. **The rise of Syria to be a world power** (Dan. 8:8-9, 20-25; 11:35-45). We'll see in our study of Daniel below that Antichrist will come from Syria and will make it a world power.\n\n10. **Military alliance between Russia, Germany, and many other countries of Europe and Asia outside of the Roman Empire territory** (Dan. 11:44; Ezek. 38; 39). While the Antichrist rises to power over the ten kingdoms of the old Roman Empire, the countries to the north and east of these ten kingdoms will be preparing for a death struggle with Antichrist and the ten kingdoms.\n\n11. **The fall of Russia, Germany, etc.—the third tri-continent war** (Dan. 11:44; Ezek. 38; 39). In the middle of Daniel's Seventieth Week, when the Antichrist comes to power over the ten kingdoms, war will be declared against him by the countries to the north and east of the old Roman Empire. This war will continue through most of the last 3½ years of the Week. Antichrist will conquer Russia and her allies in the north and east, and he'll then become the chief prince of Meshech and Tubal (Ezek. 38; 39). He'll then lead these newly conquered countries, together with the ten kingdoms and many other nations who cooperate with him through the ministry of the three unclean spirits of Rev. 16:13-16, and make war against Jerusalem to totally destroy the Jews and stop Christ from setting up His kingdom (Ezek. 38; 39; Zech. 14; Joel 3; Rev. 19:11-21). This will result in the second defeat of Russia and Communism at the end of this age. After this, Russia will be defeated by Jesus Christ at Armageddon, for she'll be one of the major powers under the Antichrist in that battle. We can say with scriptural authority that Russia will be defeated in wars three times between now and the Millennium: once in the formation of the ten kingdoms, once by the Antichrist, and once by Jesus Christ at His second advent.\n\n12. **The Judgment Seat of Christ—the judgment of the raptured saints in Heaven during Daniel's Seventieth Week** (Rom. 14:10-11; 2 Cor. 5:9-10; 1 Cor. 3:11-15). (See Lesson Thirty-Eight, Point III).\n\n13. **The heavenly tabernacle—scenes in Heaven after the rapture** (Rev. 4:1–5:14). In this passage we have different scenes in Heaven that will happen just as John saw them centuries ago. We'll discuss these in Lesson Forty-One.\n\n14. **The first seal—the rise of the Antichrist** (Rev. 6:1-2). This event begins the fulfillment of the prophecies of Rev. 6:1–19:21, which are to be fulfilled during Daniel's Seventieth Week. The following points reveal the order of events as to the time of fulfillment from the beginning of the Week to its end. We'll discuss these events in future lessons and in my book *Revelation Expounded*.\n\n15. **The second seal—war** (Rev. 6:3-4). These last two events are the same as those in Points 5 and 8 above.\n\n16. **The third seal—famine** (Rev. 6:5-6). This will be the natural result of the wars caused by the Antichrist's rise to power over the ten kingdoms.\n\n17. **The fourth seal—death and Hell** (Rev. 6:7-8). This will be the natural result of the wars, famines, and pestilences of the previous seals.\n\n18. **The fifth seal—the lesser tribulation martyrs** (Rev. 6:9-11). These will be the first martyrs of the Tribulation period after the rapture of the Church.\n\n19. **The sixth seal—the wrath of God begins** (Rev. 6:12-17). God's wrath begins at this point in the first 3½ years of the Week and continues through the Week, ending when the seven vials are poured out and Christ comes at the end of the Tribulation (Mt. 24:29-31; Rev. 19:11-21).\n\n20. **The sealing of the 144,000 Jews** (Rev. 7:1-8). They're sealed for their own protection from the trumpet judgments which follow immediately up to the middle of the Week (Rev. 7:1-3; 9:4).\n\n21. **The vision of the great multitude of tribulation saints** (Rev. 7:9-17). The martyrs of the fifth seal and all their brethren throughout the whole tribulation period who are killed are included in this company (Rev. 14:13; 15:2-4; 20:4-6).\n\n22. **The seventh seal—silence in Heaven for half an hour** (Rev. 8:1).\n\n23. **The seven trumpet angels and the priestly angel** (Rev. 8:2-6).\n\n24. **The first trumpet—hail, fire, and blood** (Rev. 8:7).\n\n25. **The second trumpet—one-third of the sea turned to blood** (Rev. 8:8-9).\n\n26. **The third trumpet—drinking waters poisoned** (Rev. 8:10-11).\n\n27. **The fourth trumpet—one-third of the planets affected** (Rev. 8:13).\n\n28. **The messenger angel—three woes upon men announced** (Rev. 8:13).\n\n29. **The fifth trumpet—the first woe, men tormented five months** (Rev. 9:1-12).\n\n30. **The sixth trumpet—the second woe, one-third of men killed** (Rev. 9:13-21).\n\n31. **The mighty angel—announcement of no delay in completion of the mystery of God and John's further commission and receiving of the rest of the Revelation** (Rev. 10:1-11).\n\n32. **Seven-years covenant between Antichrist and Israel broken** (Dan. 9:27; Mt. 24:15; Rev. 11:1-2). In the order of future prophetic wonders, this brings us down to the middle of Daniel's Seventieth Week when Antichrist breaks his covenant with Israel and enters Palestine (Dan. 9:27; 11:40-45). The following events come immediately thereafter.\n\n33. **Jerusalem falls and the Jewish temple will be seized as the capital building of the Antichrist** (Rev. 11:1-2; Dan. 9:27; 11:40-45; 2 Thess. 2:3-4; Mt. 24:15).\n\n34. **The two witnesses will appear on Earth** (Rev. 11:3-13). They'll protect Israel, turn her to God, and withstand the Antichrist during the last 3½ years of this age.\n\n35. **The great tribulation begins** (Dan. 9:27; 12:1-7; Mt. 24:15-31; Rev. 7:9-17; 12:1-17; 13:1-18; 14:9-11; 15:2-4; 20:4-6).\n\n36. **The seventh trumpet—the third woe or the casting out of Satan** (Rev. 11:15-19; 12:7-12).\n\n37. **The 144,000 Jews are translated** (Rev. 12:5; 14:1-5; Dan. 12:1; Isa. 66:7-8). These Jews will be protected through the trumpet judgments until the middle of the Week. They'll be caught up to Heaven as the manchild in Rev. 12:5, and they'll be in Heaven in Rev. 14:1-5, as we'll prove in Lesson Forty-Five.\n\n38. **War in Heaven** (Rev. 12:7-12). Satan and his angels will be cast down to the Earth to be among men for the last 3½ years, causing the third woe of Rev. 8:13; 11:15-19; 12:12.\n\n39. **Israel flees to the countries of Edom and Moab for the last 3½ years** (Rev. 12:6, 13-17; Isa. 16:1-5; Ezek. 20:33-39; Dan. 11:40-45; Hos. 2:14-16; Mt. 24:15-21). These Scriptures name the very place to which Israel will flee for protection, where the Arabs will protect Israel from the Antichrist. We'll discuss this fully in Lesson Forty-Five.\n\n40. **The Earth opens and swallows the armies of the Antichrist who follow fleeing Israel** (Rev. 12:13-16).\n\n41. **The remnant of Israel is protected from the Antichrist** (Rev. 12:17). This protection will come through the two witnesses (Rev. 11:3-13) and the new wars of the north and east (Dan. 11:44). Antichrist will be too busy in his new wars to concentrate on destroying Israel. After he conquers the countries of the north and east, he'll seek to destroy Israel, but the Lord Jesus Christ appears in Heaven and delivers them when they're about to be destroyed (Zech. 14; Joel 3; Rev. 19:11-21).\n\n42. **The rise of the beast out of the sea** (Rev. 13:1-10). This symbolizes the Antichrist being given power over the ten kingdoms of the old Roman Empire to continue for the last 3½ years (Rev. 13:1-7, 18; 17:8-17).\n\n43. **The rise of the beast out of the Earth** (Rev. 13:11-18). This represents the rise of another man, the false prophet, who will exercise the power of the Antichrist who precedes him (Rev. 16:13-16; 19:20; 20:10).\n\n44. **The setting up of the abomination of desolation spoken of by Daniel the prophet** (Rev. 13:11-18; Dan. 9:27; 12:7; Mt. 24:15). This simply means that the Antichrist will do away with Jewish worship in their temple and establish his own worship. He'll be worshipped as God in the temple (Dan. 11:45; 12:7; Rev. 13:11-18; 2 Thess. 2:3-4).\n\n45. **The making of a living image of the Antichrist** (Rev. 13:11-18).\n\n46. **The increase of satanic powers** (Rev. 13:1-18; Mt. 24:24; 2 Thess. 2:8-12).\n\n47. **The branding of the beast worshippers** (Rev. 13:16-18; 14:9-11; 15:2-4; 20:4-6).\n\n48. **The destruction of Mystical Babylon** (Rev. 17:16-18. See Lesson Forty-One).\n\n49. **The Jewish choir in Heaven—the 144,000 Jews in Heaven** (Rev. 14:1-5).\n\n50. **The first messenger angel—preaching the everlasting gospel** (Rev. 14:6-7).\n\n51. **The second messenger angel—the Fall of Literal Babylon announced** (Rev. 14:8; 16:17-21; 18:1-24; Isa. 13-14; Jer. 50-51).\n\n52. **The third messenger angel—the doom of beast worshippers** (Rev. 14:9-11).\n\n53. **The announcement—the blessed dead, those killed by Antichrist** (Rev. 14:13).\n\n54. **The harvest and the vintage—Armageddon symbolized** (Rev. 14:14-20).\n\n55. **The seven angels, the sea of glass, and the temple in Heaven** (Rev. 15:1–16:1).\n\n56. **The first vial—boils upon men** (Rev. 16:2).\n\n57. **The second vial—the sea to blood** (Rev. 16:3).\n\n58. **The third vial—rivers to blood** (Rev. 16:4-7).\n\n59. **The fourth vial—the great heat that scorches men** (Rev. 16:8-9).\n\n60. **The fifth vial—the dense darkness upon the kingdom of the beast** (Rev. 16:10-11).\n\n61. **The sixth vial—the river Euphrates dried up** (Rev. 16:12).\n\n62. **The three unclean spirits—the gathering of nations to Armageddon** (Rev. 16:13-16).\n\n63. **The seventh vial—a great earthquake and hail** (Rev. 16:17-21).\n\n64. **The destruction of Literal Babylon** (Rev. 16:17-21; 18:1-24).\n\n65. **Translation of the Tribulation saints** (Rev. 7:9-17; 15:2-4; 20:4-6).\n\n66. **The defeat, death, and translation of the two witnesses** (Rev. 11:7-13).\n\n67. **Worship in Heaven by all the redeemed and holy angels** (Rev. 19:1-6).\n\n68. **The marriage of the Lamb and the Marriage Supper** (Rev. 19:7-10).\n\n69. **The first coming of God the Father to the Earth** (Dan. 7:9-14; Titus 2:13; Zech. 14:5). God the Father will come to the Earth at the second coming of Jesus Christ. He will give the Earth kingdoms to the Son to rule and to put down all rebellion (1 Cor. 15:24-28; Dan. 7:13-14).\n\n70. **The second advent of Jesus Christ to the Earth** (Rev. 19:11-21; Jude 14; 2 Thess. 1:7-10; Mt. 24:29-31; Zech. 14; Isa. 63:1-7; Dan. 7:13-14).\n\n71. **The Mount of Olives splits into two parts.** It will then pass away, making a very great valley and raising the Dead Sea (Zech. 14:1-5; Ezek. 47).\n\n72. **The Battle of Armageddon—the defeat of Antichrist** (Rev. 19:11-21; Joel 3; Zech. 14; Ezek. 38; 39; 2 Thess. 1:7-10; Mal. 4:1-4).\n\n73. **Judgment of the beast and the false prophet** (Rev. 19:20; 20:10).\n\n74. **Expulsion of Satan from the Earth** (Rev. 20:1-7).\n\n75. **Salvation of a nation in a day** (Isa. 66:7-8; Rom. 11:25-28).\n\n76. **The end of "the times of the Gentiles"** (Lk. 21:24; Rom. 11:25-29; Zech. 14).\n\n77. **The judgment of the living nations at the second coming of Christ** (Mt. 25:31-46).\n\n78. **The final and complete regathering of Israel as an eternal nation under the Messiah** (Isa. 11:10-12; Jer. 31; 32; Mt. 24:29-31; Rom. 11:26-29; Lk. 1:32-33).\n\n79. **Jerusalem rebuilt as the world capital of the kingdom of Heaven** (Ezek. 48; Isa. 2:1-4; Mic. 4; Zech. 14:1-21).\n\n80. **The Millennial and eternal temple built** (Ezek. 43:7; Zech. 6:12-15).\n\n81. **Millennial and eternal worship established** (Isa. 2:1-4; Ezek. 40-48; Zech. 14).\n\n82. **The establishment of the kingdom of Heaven** (Dan. 12:7-13; Zech. 14; Rev. 20:1-10; Isa. 2:1-4; Mic. 4; Ezek. 43:7; Mt. 25:31-46).\n\n83. **Restoration of normal light as before the Fall** (Isa. 24:21-23; 25:7; 30:26; 60:18-22). The daylight will be increased seven-fold, and the night will be as light as our present day.\n\n84. **Restoration of fertility to barren lands** (Isa. 35; 55:12-13; Ezek. 36:8-12).\n\n85. **The nature of the animals changed** (Isa. 11:6-8; 65:20-25).\n\n86. **The whole Earth evangelized** (Isa. 2:2-4; 11:9; Zech. 8:22-23).\n\n87. **Great outpouring of the Holy Spirit** (Joel 2:28-32; Isa. 32:5; Ezek. 36:25-27).\n\n88. **Universal physical healing** (Isa. 32:1-5; 33:24; 35:3-6).\n\n89. **New form of government—a theocracy** (Isa. 9:6-7; Dan. 7:13-14; Zech. 14; Mt. 25:31-46; Lk. 1:32-33; Rev. 1:5-6; 5:10; 11:15; 20:1-10).\n\n90. **New eternal laws** (Isa. 2:2-4; Mic. 4:2; Ezek. 40:1–48:35).\n\n91. **Proper division of the Earth** (Ezek. 47:13-23; 48:1-35; Isa. 60:21).\n\n92. **The Millennial river created and the Dead Sea healed** (Ezek. 47; Zech. 14:8).\n\n93. **New and universal religion** (Mal. 1:11; Zech. 14:16-21; Isa. 2:1-4; Joel 2:28-32; Jer. 31:31-36; Ezek. 40:1–48:35).\n\n94. **Universal peace** (Isa. 2:1-4; 9:6-7; Mic. 4:3-4).\n\n95. **Universal prosperity** (Isa. 2:1-4; 65:24; Mic. 4:1-5).\n\n96. **Human life lengthened** (Isa. 65:20-25; Zech. 8:4; Lk. 1:33).\n\n97. **The glory of God continually manifest** (Isa. 4:4-6; Ezek. 43:1-7).\n\n98. **New financial system for the universal government and for world religion** (Gen. 14:20; Mal. 3:7-12; Prov. 3:9-10; Heb. 6:20; 7:1-11; 1 Cor. 9).\n\n99. **Universal justice for all** (Isa. 9:6-7; 11:3-5; 57:15; 65:20; 66:1-2).\n\n100. **The loosing of Satan and the last rebellion** (Rev. 20:7-10).\n\n101. **The second resurrection and the Great White Throne Judgment** (Rev. 20:11-15).\n\n102. **The renovation of the atmospheric heavens and of the Earth** (2 Pet. 3:10-13).\n\n103. **The new Heaven and the New Earth** (Isa. 65:17; 66:22-24; 2 Pet. 3:10-13; Rev. 21-22). These will be the old ones renovated and changed, not annihilated (Heb. 1:10-12; 12:25-28; Rom. 8:21-23; Eccl. 1:4; Ps. 104:5).\n\n104. **The second coming of God the Father to the Earth** (Rev. 21:1-7). The New Jerusalem, God's capital city of the universe, and the Bride, the Lamb's wife, will be moved from Heaven to the New Earth to be among men forever (Rev. 21:1-27; 22:1-5).\n\n105. **Eternal generations of natural peoples** (Gen. 8:22; 9:12; 2 Sam. 7; Isa. 9:6-7; 51:8; 59:21; Ezek. 37:24-28; Dan. 2:44-45; 7:13-14, 18; Lk. 1:31-35; Deut. 7:9; 1 Chron. 16:15; Ps. 105:8; Jer. 31:35-36; 32:38-40; Rev. 11:15).\n\n106. **No more curse** (Rev. 21:1-8; 22:1-7). Everything on Earth will be as it would have been if man had not fallen. There will be on Earth perfect eternal natural conditions, peoples, animals, planting and harvesting, marriages, childbirth, worship, natural life, covenants with God, laws, government, feasts, pleasures, food, raiment, and innumerable eternal natural blessings, as we'll see in Lesson Fifty-One.\n\n107. **The river of life for all peoples** (Rev. 22:1-5).\n\n108. **Trees of life for all peoples** (Rev. 21:1-5).\n\n109. **An eternal kingdom—the kingdom of God firmly established forever** (Heb. 12:25-28; Dan. 2:44-45; 7:13-14, 18; Isa. 9:6-7; Lk. 1:32-35; Rev. 11:15; 22:4-6).\n\n110. **God all-in-all as before rebellion started in the universe by Satan and Adam** (1 Cor. 15:24-28; Rev. 21:1-8; 22:1-7; Isa. 66:22-24).\n\n## How to Interpret Prophecy\n\nBefore we take up a study of prophecy, let's first learn how to interpret it. Thousands of preachers and Bible teachers constantly say that prophecy is hard to understand. And it's true—it can be hard if you're trying to harmonize all the foolish speculations and interpretations men have come up with on the subject.\n\nBut thank God, it's not hard to understand if you follow a few common-sense rules. Think of it like learning to read a map: once you know the basic symbols and rules, the whole thing becomes clear.\n\n### Rule 1: Give Words Their Normal Meaning\n\nGive the same meaning to the words of prophecy that you'd give to words of history. In other words, give the same meaning to words in the Bible that you'd give to the same words outside the Bible.\n\nThere's a common theory that just because a word is found in prophecy or in the Bible, it automatically has some mystical meaning and can't be taken literally. This theory is entirely wrong. For example, the word "year" is often taken to mean a day, and a day to mean a year, just because it's prophecy. This is entirely wrong, as we've seen in Lesson Fifteen, Point VIII.\n\n### Rule 2: Don't Change the Literal to the Symbolic\n\nDon't change the literal meaning to a spiritual or symbolic meaning. One modern writer in his book of lectures on Revelation is a fair example of this trend—changing words and statements from their literal meaning to whatever suits one's fancy.\n\nHe interprets the "earthquake" of the sixth seal (Rev. 6:12-17) as the breaking up of society instead of a literal earthquake. He says the sun darkened represents Christ rejected and God dethroned. The moon turned to blood? That's the destruction of derived authority. Stars falling? The downfall and apostasy of religious leaders in "the ecclesiastical heavens" (whatever those are). And the heavens departing as a scroll means all organized Christianity will be destroyed.\n\nThis same writer says the trumpet judgments aren't literal either. The grass of the first trumpet (Rev. 8:7) means the common people, and the trees mean the dignity of man. So instead of grass and one-third of trees being literally burned as it plainly states, all common men and one-third of man's dignity will be burned.\n\nInstead of a third of the sea turning to blood, a third of sea creatures dying, and a third of ships being destroyed in the second trumpet (Rev. 8:8, 9), he says the burning mountain means spiritual Babylon cast into the sea of nations and destroyed by the people.\n\nInstead of drinking waters being made bitter by a star falling from Heaven causing many deaths in the third trumpet (Rev. 8:10, 11), he says the star means the pope of Rome or some religious dignitary. But how could the pope fall from Heaven into the waters and poison them? How will he ever get to Heaven to fall from Heaven in the Tribulation? How could he poison the drinking waters if he did fall in them?\n\nThe darkening of a third of the sun, moon, and stars in the fourth trumpet (Rev. 8:12), he says, means spiritual darkness instead of the literal darkening of part of the Earth.\n\nThis writer explains the fifth trumpet (Rev. 9:1-12) like this: The star that falls from Heaven with the key to the Abyss is the pope or the apostate religious leader of the third trumpet. (This would make the pope fall from Heaven twice—once under the third trumpet and once again under the fifth trumpet.) The key is the system that opens the Abyss. The smoke of the pit is the blotting out of true light in man's spiritual sky by demon powers when false religions are dominant after the Holy Spirit is taken out of the world. The locusts aren't literal but symbolize false religions spreading like locusts. The torment of their stings is the torment these religions will bring. The faces of men these creatures have signify intelligence and reason (but since they're not real creatures, how are they to exercise these faculties?). Their hair like women represents an unholy life, and the iron breastplates mean the conscience is destroyed. The grass and trees aren't symbolical in this trumpet as they were in the first. The five months these creatures torment men aren't literal, and he doesn't explain what they mean. So nothing in Revelation is literal if we believe this method!\n\nThe sixth trumpet (Rev. 9:13-21), he says, isn't literal either—the 200 million creatures refer to Asiatic hordes that overran Europe and Palestine through many centuries. This writer says the two witnesses aren't two men but symbolize the witnessing Jewish remnant. He also says the manchild is Christ; the woman is Israel; the sun is the glory of the New Testament; the moon the glory of the Old Testament; the twelve stars are the twelve tribes; the 1,260 days of Rev. 12:6 mean the first part of Daniel's Seventieth Week\n\n---\n\nDaniel wrote from Babylon, in captivity, about events hundreds and thousands of years in his future — and the historical record confirms his accuracy with extraordinary precision. If he was right about the first 483 years of his 490-year prophecy, there's no rational reason to doubt the final seven. So here's the question: As you read the news today, do you have a biblical framework for interpreting what you see — a prophetic map that helps you understand where history is going? And if not, does that absence affect how you live and how you respond to the events around you?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk4bq001vewhjpw4a1pd3	cmnknk4bp001qewhjk5bcqnfj	Lesson 40 — Daniel's Seventieth Week and the Tribulation	READING	889	4	\N	t	There are seven years in the future that will be unlike any period in human history — a time of global tribulation so severe that Jesus described it as greater than anything that has come before or will come after (Mt. 24:21). Daniel predicted it six centuries before Christ. Jesus confirmed it. Revelation describes it in chapter-by-chapter detail. And yet most Christians alive today have given these seven years less thought than they've given their retirement plan.\n\nLast time we established Daniel as the prophetic backbone of all end-times understanding. Now we zoom into his most precise prophecy: the seventieth week — the final seven-year period that completes God's plan for Israel and the world.\n\nWhen Daniel received this vision, the phrase "seventy weeks" literally means "seventy sevens" of years. If days were meant, Scripture would say so directly, as it does in Dan. 10:3.\n\nDaniel's prayer that prompted this vision wasn't about days — it was about years (Dan. 9:2). We also know from Scripture that the last week (Dan. 9:27) divides into two parts of 3½ years each (Dan. 7:25; 12:7; Rev. 11:2, 3; 12:5, 14; 13:5).\n\nSo the whole period of "seventy sevens" equals 490 years. These years are marked off from all other years and concern only "thy people [Israel] and thy holy city [Jerusalem]" — the very things Daniel was praying for (Dan. 6:10; 9:1-23).\n\nSix prophetic events will take place during these 490 years, all relating to Israel and Jerusalem:\n\n**1. "To finish the transgression."** The Hebrew word "pasha" means "to revolt," "rebel," or "sin against lawful authority." It's often translated "transgression" (Ps. 51:13; Isa. 43:27; etc.). This refers to Israel's rebellion against God, and this prophecy foretells when that rebellion will finally reach its culmination.\n\nThe law was added because of transgression until the Seed should come, serving as a schoolmaster to lead Israel to Christ (Gal. 3:17-25). But Israel failed to receive the Messiah and was broken off in unbelief from God's favor as a nation. She won't be fully received again until Christ's second coming, when He will turn ungodliness from Jacob and cause a nation to be born at once (Rom. 11:25-29; Isa. 66:7-10; Ezek. 36:24-30).\n\n**2. "To make an end of sins."** If you could gather all of Israel's sins into concrete matter, they would fill the whole earth. She has been in rebellion against God from her beginning and will continue until this prophecy is fulfilled at Christ's return.\n\nThis "end of sins" won't happen until after the Tribulation. But from that time forward, Israel will obey God forever (Ezek. 36:24-30; 37:24-27; 43:7; Zech. 4:1-21).\n\n**3. "To make reconciliation [atonement] for iniquity."** The Hebrew word "avon" means "perverseness," "to be crooked," or "wrung out of course" (1 Sam. 20:30; 2 Sam. 19:19). Atonement was made on the cross for the whole world, but Israel as a nation hasn't yet appropriated its benefits. They won't until Christ returns (Zech. 13:1-7; Rom. 11:25-27).\n\n**4. "To bring in everlasting righteousness."** When the transgression has been finished, the end of sins made, and Israel fully realizes the benefits of atonement, then everlasting righteousness will be ushered in (Isa. 9:6, 7; 12:1-6; Dan. 7:13, 14, 18, 27; Mt. 25:31-46; Ezek. 43:7; Rom. 11:25-29).\n\n**5. "To seal up the vision and prophecy."** This means prophecies will end because they'll all be fulfilled regarding Israel and Jerusalem. The word "prophecy" should actually be translated "prophet." There will be no need for inspired men to rebuke Israel and lead them toward righteousness "for all shall know the Lord from the least unto the greatest" (Jer. 31:31-40; Isa. 11:9).\n\n**6. "To anoint the most Holy."** This refers to cleansing the Holy of Holies, the temple, and Jerusalem from the abomination of desolation and Gentile sacrilege. It also involves establishing and anointing the Millennial temple described in Ezek. 40-43; Zech. 6:12, 13.\n\nThe "most Holy" is never used for a person, nor would Jews ever associate this term with their Messiah. The Messiah is actually distinguished from this term in this passage by His own title. This vision needs no interpretation beyond what the angel gave Daniel — we simply need to understand that explanation, connect it with other Scriptures on the same subject, and determine when it will be fulfilled.\n\n![Lesson 40 — Daniel's Seventieth Week and the Tribulation](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsEJLyd23tdLWqJHyu8Q5MfmIRAjonSBKDvC0V)\n\n### The 490 Years Are Divided Into Three Periods:\n\n**1. THE FIRST PERIOD** consisted of seven sevens, or forty-nine years. During this time, the Holy City, its street, and wall were to be built "even in troublous times" (Dan. 9:25). This tells us the 490 years began with "the commandment to restore and build Jerusalem unto the Messiah."\n\nThere were three decrees for restoring the city. The first came during Cyrus's first year as king of Persia (Ezra 1:1-4; 3:8; Isa. 44:28; 45:1-4; 46:11). Cyrus reigned nine years, then his son Cambyses reigned seven years. During Cambyses's reign, work on the temple and city stopped (Ezra 4:1-24).\n\nDarius I reigned thirty-five years. In his second year, he reconfirmed Cyrus's decree and work resumed. The temple was finished in his sixth year, but the city still wasn't fully restored — even though fifty-seven years had passed since Cyrus's first decree (Ezra 6:1-15).\n\nXerxes reigned twenty-one years (Dan. 11:1-3), and the city still wasn't completed. Then Artaxerxes reigned, and in his twentieth year, he gave the third decree to Nehemiah to restore "Jerusalem unto the Messiah" (Neh. 2:1–6:19; Dan. 9:25, 26).\n\nNehemiah rebuilt the walls in just fifty-two days after reaching Jerusalem, but this wasn't the full restoration. That took the complete seven sevens — forty-nine years after the third decree, which was given around 452 B.C.\n\n**2. THE SECOND PERIOD** consists of sixty-two sevens, or 434 years. It began immediately after the first period and continued without interruption until the Messiah was "cut off" — crucified (Dan. 9:26). This phrase "cut off" comes from the Hebrew "karath," meaning "to cut off in death" (Gen. 9:11; Deut. 20:20; Jer. 11:19; Ps. 37:9).\n\nThese forty-nine years plus 434 years equal 483 years from the third decree to the Messiah's crucifixion. That accounts for sixty-nine of the seventy sevens, leaving one final seven-year period concerning Israel and Jerusalem to be fulfilled after the crucifixion.\n\n**3. THE THIRD PERIOD** will be one seven-year period, better known as Daniel's Seventieth Week. The Messiah's crucifixion ended the sixty-ninth week, and God ceased dealing with Israel as a nation.\n\nThey were broken off in unbelief, and their city was destroyed — just as this same vision foretold (Dan. 9:26) and as Jesus predicted (Mt. 21:43; 23:37-39; 24:2; Lk. 21:20-24). See also Acts 13:45-49 and Rom. 11.\n\nThis Seventieth Week will be fulfilled when Israel, partially gathered, exists as a nation possessing Jerusalem. That Jerusalem will be in their hands again is proven by the fact that it will be given to the Gentiles in the middle of the Week (Rev. 11:1, 2).\n\nNot one of those six events has been fulfilled yet. They must happen in the future.\n\nThe Seventieth Week will be the last seven years of this dispensation and will run parallel to the seven-year covenant between Antichrist and Israel (Dan. 9:27). This is when all the events of Rev. 6:1–19:21 will be fulfilled, and the entire tribulation will run its course.\n\nDaniel didn't receive the details of what would happen during this Week — but John did. This Week of years will begin after the Church is raptured, and at Christ's second advent, all six prophetic events concerning Israel will be fulfilled.\n\nThe present Church Age fits between the sixty-ninth and seventieth weeks — during the time of Israel's rejection. It will be completed before the Seventieth Week begins, which primarily concerns Israel, just as the first sixty-nine weeks did.\n\n## II. The Tribulation (Rev. 6:1–19:21)\n\n### 1. The Time and Length of the Tribulation\n\nThe Tribulation will begin affecting Israel before the Seventieth Week officially starts. How long before? We can't be certain.\n\nBut when Antichrist rises at the Week's beginning, Israel will already be undergoing persecution from the whore and the ten kings of Revised Rome. These kings are dominated by the whore until the middle of the Week.\n\nAntichrist will emerge from one of those ten kingdoms (see Lesson Thirty-nine) and make a seven-year covenant with Israel, promising to protect them as they continue establishing their nation (Dan. 9:27).\n\nThe Jews won't accept Catholicism when it dominates the old world nations again and starts murdering heretics as it did in the past. Because they won't submit, widespread persecution will break out — "they shall be hated of all nations" during "the beginning of sorrows" while Antichrist tries to conquer these nations (Mt. 24:4-12).\n\nAntichrist will need Jewish moral and financial support to help him rise over these nations, so he'll make an alliance with them for seven years. Think of it like a political deal where both sides need something from the other — but one side has hidden, sinister motives.\n\nTherefore, the Tribulation spans the entire Seventieth Week (Dan. 9:27). It will end at Christ's second advent (Mt. 24:29-31).\n\n### 2. The Divisions of the Tribulation\n\n**(1) THE FIRST DIVISION** covers the first 3½ years of the Seventieth Week. It's called "the lesser tribulation" because it's not as severe as the last 3½ years. Why? Because Antichrist is protecting Israel during this time.\n\nIsrael's persecution in this period comes from a completely different source than in the second half. During these earlier years, she'll be persecuted by the whore and the ten kings, as mentioned above.\n\nThis division includes the fulfillment of Rev. 6:1–9:21. The judgments of the sixth seal and first six trumpets occur in this period, proving there is real tribulation during this time.\n\n**(2) THE LAST DIVISION** covers the final 3½ years of the Week. It's called "the great tribulation" because persecution against Israel will be far more severe than the first 3½ years.\n\nHere's what happens: Antichrist, who protected Israel in the first half, breaks his covenant with her at the midpoint and becomes her most bitter enemy. It's similar to someone who pretends to be your closest ally, then suddenly turns and tries to destroy you — except this betrayal will be on a national scale.\n\nHe'll try to annihilate Israel, which triggers the judgments of the seven vials in the last 3½ years. This part of the Tribulation includes the fulfillment of Rev. 10:1–19:21.\n\nJesus, Daniel, Jeremiah, and many others describe this time of Israel's trouble as worse than anything that has ever been on Earth or ever will be (Dan. 12:1; Jer. 30:4-11; Mt. 24:21, 22; Rev. 11:1, 2; 12:14-17; 13:5-7).\n\n### 3. The Purpose of the Tribulation\n\n> (1) To purify Israel and bring them back to a place where God can fulfill the everlasting covenants made with their fathers (Isa. 2:6; 3:26; 16:1-5; 24:1-25; 26:20, 21; Ezek. 20:33, 34; 22:17-22; Rom. 11:25-29).\n\n> (2) To purify Israel of all rebels (Ezek. 20:33, 34; 22:17-22; Zech. 13:8, 9; Mal. 3:3, 4).\n\n> (3) To plead with and bring Israel into the bond of the New Covenant (Ezek. 20:33, 34; 36:24-28; Jer. 30:3-11; Zech. 12:10–13:9; Mal. 4:3, 4).\n\n> (4) To judge Israel and punish them for their rejection of the Messiah and make them willing to accept Him when He comes the second time (Ezek. 20:33, 34; Zech. 12:10–13:9; 14:1-15; Mt. 24:15-31).\n\n> (5) To judge the nations for their persecution of Israel (Isa. 63:1-5; Joel 3; Rev. 6:1–19:21).\n\n> (6) To bring Israel to complete repentance (Zech. 12:10–13:9; Rom. 11:26-29; Mt. 23:39).\n\n> (7) To fulfill the prophecies of Dan. 9:24-27; Rev. 6:1–19:21; Mt. 24:15, 29, etc.\n\n> (8) To cause Israel to flee into the wilderness of Edom and Moab and to be so persecuted by the nations that Israel will have to turn to God for help (Isa. 16:1-5; Ezek. 20:33-35; Dan. 11:40–12:7; Hos. 2:14-17; Mt. 24:15-31; Rev. 12).\n\n### 4. The Character of the Tribulation\n\nYou can understand the Tribulation's character when you consider that God's wrath will be poured out on mankind for wickedness and corruption exceeding even the days of Noah and Lot (Gen. 6; Mt. 24:37-39; Lk. 17:22-37; 2 Tim. 3:1-12).\n\nPeople will reject the truth until God turns them over to the "strong delusion" of the Antichrist. He'll cause them to believe a lie and be damned (2 Thess. 2:8-12; 2 Pet. 3:1-9).\n\nEven after God pours out His judgments, they'll still defy Him (Rev. 9:20, 21; 6:2-11; 17:1-18; 18:1-24). Words can't adequately describe the utter rebellion and wickedness of humanity during this final struggle between God and the devil over possession of the Earth (Rev. 11:15; 12:7-12; 19:11-21; 20:1-3).\n\n### 5. Will the Tribulation Be Worldwide?\n\nThe old theory that the Tribulation will be worldwide isn't stated in even one Scripture. On the contrary, the Bible is clear that Antichrist won't reign over the whole world — only over the ten kingdoms formed inside the old Roman Empire (see Lessons Forty-six and Forty-seven).\n\nMost trumpet and vial judgments are stated as affecting only a third or fourth part of the Earth (Rev. 8:7-12), falling specifically upon "the men which had the mark of the beast" and "upon the seat [throne] of the beast: and his kingdom" (Rev. 16:2, 10, 12).\n\nHowever, nothing limits the extent of the demon-locusts or the devil's wrath when he's cast out (Rev. 9:1-11; 12:7-12). The sixth trumpet kills only a third part of men (Rev. 9:12-21).\n\nWhen we talk about the Tribulation, we mean the troubles God's people — especially the Jewish people — will endure. Daniel's Seventieth Week concerns only Israel and Jerusalem. The last half of the Week will be "the time of Jacob's trouble," and these troubles primarily concern Israel, as Point 3 above shows.\n\nThe Tribulation won't be worldwide, even if it covers all lands where Christians live after the rapture. Unknown tribes in the interior of heathen lands won't even know about the Tribulation caused by Antichrist, as we'll see in Lesson Forty-six.\n\n---\n\n## Questions on Lesson Forty\n\n- Explain fully the vision of the seventy weeks.\n\n- What does the term "weeks" really mean? Prove.\n\n- What six main events will be fulfilled during the seventy weeks?\n\n- Have these events been fulfilled yet? When will they be?\n\n- How would you divide the seventy weeks into three periods?\n\n- When did the seventy weeks begin?\n\n- What was done concerning Israel during the first period?\n\n- What event marked the end of the second period?\n\n- What event will end the third period?\n\n- What takes place between the Sixty-ninth and the Seventieth Week?\n\n- Divide Daniel's Seventieth Week as to its relation with the Antichrist?\n\n- When is the time of the Tribulation and what will be its length?\n\n- How many divisions of the Tribulation are there?\n\n- What makes these divisions of the Tribulation and what are they called?\n\n- What is the purpose of the Tribulation?\n\n- What will be the character of the Tribulation?\n\n- Will the Tribulation be worldwide? Prove.\n\n- What part of the book of Revelation will be fulfilled in the Tribulation?\n\n---\n\nDaniel's seventieth week is not a theological abstraction — it's a specific, dated period of world history that may be closer than any generation before us has realized. It concerns Israel, the Antichrist, the temple, and the return of Christ. So here's the question: Are you living with any sense of urgency given the nearness of these events — urgency in prayer, in evangelism, in making peace with God and with people? Or has end-times teaching remained an interesting subject you study without it touching how you actually live?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk48i0013ewhj5exoz312	cmnknk48e000xewhj49ao5az0	Supplement 11 — For Lessons 21 & 22	SUPPLEMENT	676	5	\N	t	Jesus Christ is fully God and fully man — and every believer has been given power of attorney to act in His name. That's not a metaphor. It's a legal and spiritual reality that most Christians have never tested. This supplement is the practical training for using the authority that Lessons Twenty-One and Twenty-Two established.\n\nWithout truly grasping who Jesus is and how to use the power He gives every believer, we simply can't experience the full manifestation of Christ's power in our individual lives or in the Church as a whole.\n\n![Supplement 11 — For Lessons 21 & 22](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsukZ45PVZIJS2tQ1kPsY7EcO3rv8DqhnMN9ly)\n\n## The Gospel is the Same Today as Ever\n\nLet's be clear about something important: the name of Jesus has not lost any of its power. There's a foolish doctrine floating around that claims the power of Jesus' name was only for the apostolic period or for some future kingdom when Christ reigns.\n\nWe need to reject that idea once and for all.\n\nWhen you read the New Testament passages about the name of Jesus, you won't find a single statement limiting His power to any certain period or particular group. Christ promised to be with us "even to the end of the world" (Mt. 28:19-20), and believers throughout this age were to "observe all things" that Christ commanded the apostles.\n\n## Gospel Benefits are Personal\n\nIf certain churches don't want these benefits — if they want to cooperate with the devil and become tools to destroy faith in God and His Word — that's their responsibility. They are the losers, both now and for eternity.\n\nBut here's the beautiful truth: even if every other person in the world rejects the gospel, you can still receive every promised benefit. Why? Because every promise is personal, not national.\n\nEach person can and does receive exactly what they have faith for — no more, no less.\n\n## All Men Can Have God's Power Now\n\nThe name of Jesus and all the power behind it is for us right now! It's our legal right to use this power today.\n\nThat name belongs to everyone who is baptized into Christ (Gal. 3:27-29; Rom. 6:1-8). This works now. All things can be ours for the asking if we have faith and don't doubt.\n\nThink of it like this: every church could automatically become a channel for God's power if they would simply teach what the gospel says about this subject. But sadly, in the average church, men and women are groping in darkness, pleading with God for faith, bemoaning their weakness, and confessing their inability to meet the crisis of the hour.\n\nIf they only knew the truth about New Testament power in the name of Jesus, they would immediately become powerful in Christ.\n\n## The Sevenfold Right to Use the Name of Jesus\n\n> 1. **IT IS OUR GOSPEL RIGHT.** The gospel is still the power of God unto salvation to everyone that believes (Rom. 1:16). The word "salvation" here means full deliverance by God's power from sin, sickness, failure, powerlessness, and every known need of human life.\n\n> 2. **IT IS OUR REDEMPTIVE RIGHT.** "Redemption" means God has bought us — we belong to Him and we are not our own (1 Cor. 6:19-20).\n\n> 3. **IT IS OUR NEEDFUL RIGHT.** From the standpoint of need as God's children, we have a right to claim as full a supply as God has promised (Phil. 4:19).\n\n> 4. **IT IS OUR PROMISE RIGHT.** All the promises of God are for all who believe, but not one thing is promised to the unbeliever (2 Cor. 1:20; 2 Pet. 1:3-4).\n\n> 5. **IT IS OUR FAMILY RIGHT.** As sons of God, we naturally have power by virtue of our relationship with God (Jn. 1:12; 2 Tim. 1:7). It's similar to how a child has access to family resources simply because of who their father is.\n\n> 6. **IT IS OUR LEGAL RIGHT.** Legal right to use Christ's name is promised to every believer, as we've seen in Lesson Twenty-two.\n\n> 7. **IT IS OUR COMMISSION RIGHT.** Every child of God has been commissioned to use the name of Jesus (Mt. 28:18-20; Jn. 14:12-15; 15:16; 16:23-26).\n\n## How to Get the Christian's Power of Attorney\n\n> 1. Believe the gospel — that everything it teaches is for you (2 Tim. 3:16-17).\n\n> 2. Repent and turn to God, renouncing all sin and wrongdoing (Acts 26:18).\n\n> 3. Obey the gospel and seek the enduement of power from on High (Lk. 11:13; 24:49; Acts 1:4-15; 2:38-39; 5:32).\n\n> 4. Yield to God and walk and live in the Spirit (Rom. 8:1-16; Gal. 5:16-26).\n\n> 5. Learn to pray in faith, nothing wavering (Jas. 1:5-8; Heb. 11:6).\n\n> 6. Have faith in your mission in life to fully represent God (Acts 1:8).\n\n> 7. Learn to use the name of Jesus according to the Word of God (Acts 3:16; 4:12; Jn. 14:12-15; 16:23-26; Col. 3:17; Jn. 15:7, 16).\n\n## Using the Name of Jesus\n\nWhen you start using the name of Jesus, you must have a genuine desire to be a true and unselfish representative of God. God's glory and the good of others must be the driving force in all your praying and working for God.\n\nStart using His name in every area of life, and don't accept "no" when there's resistance to the answer. If you let Satan defeat you at the start, the battle will only get harder next time — so don't be defeated on any point.\n\nAs victories come, your faith and confidence will grow until you have a normal, steady faith in the name of Jesus where no defeat is possible.\n\nPray always to God in the name of Jesus. Rebuke demons in His name. Constantly use His name in daily conflict, pleading the merits of the blood of Jesus in His name by the power of the Holy Spirit. Challenge every opposing force in the name of Jesus.\n\nBe fearless and unwavering. It must move. All must yield to the name of Jesus.\n\nUse His name even if you tremble while doing it. It must be done. You don't have to feel any particular power when you do it.\n\nYou have faith in His name. You know the power of His name. You know that everything is made subject to His name. You ask — He will do it.\n\nThis is the truth of Jn. 14:12-15; 15:16; 16:23-26. This is power and prayer simplified. His power is unlimited, and you are to do unlimited asking and receiving.\n\nNever question God's will in anything you ask according to the promises. It's already His will for you to ask what you will (Jn. 15:7; Mk. 11:22-24).\n\n## Faith Based on God's Word\n\nIn all this kind of praying, your faith should be based on the Word of God itself — not on what you hear, feel, or see.\n\nPicture it this way: you're not healed because the pain is gone, saved because you feel well, or delivered because you see deliverance. You're blessed by believing what the Word of God says.\n\nIf God says a certain thing is yours when you ask, then count it done on that basis alone.\n\nMany people are living in failure today because they look to feelings and outward evidence for their answer. But the Word of God should be given first place in our lives — not the senses of our body and soul.\n\nNever say "I am healed because I feel no pain" or "my prayer is answered because I feel it is answered." Say it because the Word of God says you have what you ask for.\n\n## Ten Questions For Supplement Eleven. Do You Know:\n\n- That the mark of the beast cannot be given today? This is true because the beast or the Antichrist cannot come until after the kingdoms are formed inside the old Roman Empire (Dan. 7:23-24) and until after the rapture of the Church (2 Thess. 2:7-8). Since both events are still future and since the mark of the beast will only be given during the last 3½ years of this age, it cannot be given today (Rev. 13:5-18). See Supplement Six, Question 10 and Supplement Eight, Question 5.\n\n- That the first war ever fought happened long before Adam's days? It was a battle between God and Satan when Lucifer invaded Heaven to cast God out. Satan was defeated and cast down to Earth, as proven in Isa. 14:12-14; Lk. 10:18. This had to happen before Adam, because Lucifer was already a fallen creature when he came into Adam's Eden, as we saw in Lesson Seven.\n\n- That the greatest wars ever fought have been in Heaven? Not only was the original war between God and Satan fought in Heaven, but all wars fought on Earth have actually been won or lost in the heavenlies between good and evil spirits. This is clear from Dan. 10:11-21; 11:1; 12:1, where good and evil angelic rulers of various earthly kingdoms are pictured waging war in the heavenlies. Wars on Earth result from wars between these spirit rulers of kingdoms.\n\n- That Methuselah was not the oldest man who ever lived? He was the oldest man who ever lived on Earth, but his father Enoch is still alive, residing in Heaven. Enoch will return to Earth and die his appointed death at the hands of the Antichrist at the end of this age. He is over 5,551 years old, whereas Methuselah was only 969 years old when he died.\n\n- That Noah was a woman? The Noah who built the boat was a man, but the Noah mentioned in Num. 27:1 was the daughter of Zelophehad.\n\n- That the Bible records the day and month of Moses' birth and death? This was on the first day of the eleventh month, which was February (Deut. 1:3). This was the day of his birth and death as proven in Deut. 31:2, which says, "I am an hundred and twenty years old this day." In Deut. 32:48-52, God speaks to Moses on "that selfsame day" telling him to go up into the mountain and die. Between Deut. 1:3 and 31:2, we see that all of Deuteronomy was spoken by Moses on that one day, for throughout we read "this day" (Deut. 1:3, 10; 2:18, 22, 25, 30; 3:14; 4:4, 8, 20, 26, 38-40; 5:1, 3, 24; 6:6, 24; 7:11; 8:1, 11, 18, 19; 9:1, 3; 10:8, 13, 15; 11:2, 4, 8, 13, 26-28, 32; 12:8; 13:18; 15:5, 15; 19:9; 20:3; 26:3, 16-18; 27:1, 4, 9-11; 28:1, 13-15; 29:4, 10-15, 18, 28; 30:2, 8, 15, 16, 18, 19; 31:2, 27; 32:46-48).\n\n- That Solomon was the first recorded billionaire? The temple he built for God cost over $87 billion — or twice that much according to 1949 prices, which are more than double pre-war figures (See Lesson Seventeen, Point VII). Before David died, he collected $4,848,500,000 in gold and silver to build the temple, along with brass, iron, and other materials "without weight" (1 Chron. 22:14-16). Solomon inherited all of this plus the billions he accumulated after David's death. At one time Solomon received $17,451,000.00 from Ophir, and the Queen of Sheba and Hiram, King of Tyre, each gave him $3,490,200.00 (1 Ki. 9:14, 28; 10:10). David's personal gift to the temple above what was already mentioned was $100,835,000.00 (1 Chron. 28:2-4). In another year, $19,370,610.00 in gold was brought to Solomon besides gold from year to year and gifts from all surrounding nations (2 Chron. 9:13-28).\n\n- That only eight books of the Bible out of sixty-six have no prophecies in them? They are Song of Solomon, Ecclesiastes, Esther, Ezra, and Ruth in the Old Testament, and 2 John, 3 John, and Philemon in the New Testament.\n\n- That Moses fasted forty days on three different occasions? The first time was when he went into the mount for forty days to get the tables of stone and receive the law (Deut. 9:9). The second time was when he went back for the second tables of stone (Deut. 9:17-18). The third time was when Israel rebelled at Kadesh-barnea (Deut. 9:23-25).\n\n- That there are four men in the Bible who fasted forty days? Moses and Joshua fasted forty days (Exod. 24:12-18; 32:15-19); Elijah went on the strength of the angel's food for forty days and nights (1 Ki. 19:4-8); and Jesus fasted forty days and forty nights (Mt. 4:1-11).\n\n---\n\nYou have been given the name of Jesus Christ to act in, the authority of the Risen Lord to wield, and the same Spirit that raised Him from the dead living inside you. So here's the question: Name one situation in your life or someone else's life where you've been worried, anxious, or defeated — without ever standing in the authority of Christ's name and addressing it. What's stopping you from doing that today?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk460000mewhju5cckalc	cmnknk45y000gewhjn5pl0kw9	Supplement 6 — For Lessons 11 & 12	SUPPLEMENT	1907	5	\N	t	Conscience alone couldn't stop humanity from filling the earth with violence — that's the lesson of the Dispensation of Conscience. And God's plan for needs goes unrealized when specific barriers get in the way. This supplement is where those two lessons get personal: What does your conscience know that you've been ignoring? And what's blocking the provision God has already made available to you?\n\nHere's what we discovered about Satan's strategy: his goal was to prevent the seed of the woman — Christ — from ever entering the world. Why? Because if he could stop Christ from coming, he could avoid his own doom and keep control of the Earth indefinitely.\n\nBut here's the wonderful news: Satan was defeated in his plan for that age and every age that followed! The Messiah did come, and He made complete provision for every person to have power over all satanic forces. Because of Christ, you can claim God's benefits for this life and for eternity.\n\nIn Lesson Twelve, we tackled some tough questions: Why aren't God's plans for human flourishing fully realized? Why do we see such widespread failure, poverty, sickness, and unhappiness on Earth — and why shouldn't these conditions exist?\n\nHere's what we discovered: It is not — and could never be — God's will for you to suffer misery and want just so you'll stay humble enough to be saved. That doesn't have to be your lot in life! The gospel enables you to overcome every obstacle and live saved, prosperous, and happy in Christ.\n\nWe also saw that many doctrines being taught in modern churches are simply false and unscriptural. You are under a moral obligation to have faith in God and never doubt anything you ask of Him.\n\nThink about it this way: You can't secure the best for yourself or your fellow man while living in defeat and failure. You don't endure these things for God's sake or Christ's sake — you endure them because of your own failures and because of false teachings.\n\nIt's almost unpardonable to be satisfied with defeat when you have the power to do otherwise! That's why we need to understand the laws of prosperity — the principles through which all people can have guaranteed success if they'll obey them.\n\n![Supplement 6 — For Lessons 11 & 12](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsXbrbcux3hkKszq9AHmfWrCLc6tDpSgVyF1n0)\n\n## LAWS OF PROSPERITY\n\n### 1. Believe It's God's Will for You to Prosper\n\n**THE FIRST LAW OF PROSPERITY IS TO BELIEVE THAT IT IS GOD'S WILL FOR YOU TO PROSPER AND THAT YOU ARE IN HIS WILL.**\n\nContrary to what many people think, God wants you to be prosperous! If that's true — and it is — then God will see to it that you prosper if you'll learn these laws and cooperate with Him in all things.\n\nAsk yourself: Why would God want His own children to lack what they need while giving good things to those who rebel against Him? That simply doesn't make sense.\n\nUntil Christians wake up, believe the whole Bible, and demand that their leaders teach the whole truth, we won't see the prosperity, happiness, and success that should mark every believer's life.\n\nGod has created an abundance of everything people need — there's enough for everyone. He laid down certain laws of life and prosperity, and all He asks is that we obey them. These laws are plainly revealed in Scripture, given for all to follow, and those who follow them will surely reap the promised benefits.\n\nLook back at the promises we studied in Lesson Ten, Point III. You can't read all those promises without being convinced that it's God's highest will to bless His children with prosperity and everything they need and want right here and now.\n\nLet this be firmly settled in your heart: you are in harmony with God's will when you desire to prosper, be healthy, and have victory over sin and failure.\n\n### 2. Make God Your Partner\n\n**THE SECOND LAW TO FOLLOW IS TO MAKE GOD YOUR PARTNER IN LIFE.**\n\nWork with Him and obey His laws of prosperity. Make Him the Senior Partner in your business. Give Him the seat at the head of the table in all your conferences.\n\nRecognize Him in everything you do. Stay in touch with Him about His guidance in every business deal. Be aware of His presence with you always.\n\nTalk to Him about your problems as you would talk to your best friend. Turn all your problems over to Him. Don't make a move without feeling sure it's God's will and for everyone's best interest.\n\nThink of it like this: If you have a brilliant, successful business partner who knows everything and can handle any problem, wouldn't you want their input on every decision? That's what God offers to be for you.\n\nIf you give all your hard problems to God; if you simply stop fretting and worrying and trust Him with all your heart to work things out for His glory and your good — He will do it.\n\nBut here's the key: After turning things over to God, don't interfere and spoil things! Stay out of them until God works them out. Don't be impatient or unbelieving.\n\nIf God is your Senior Partner and He's running your business, trust Him — no one could operate it better. Since you believe God can handle your problems better than you can, let Him do it. He will remove obstacles and work for your good.\n\nBelieve His Word: "all things work together for good to them that love the Lord, to them who are called according to his purpose" (Rom. 8:28). Since God cannot fail, you cannot fail if you keep all things committed to Him.\n\nGod never meant for His children to become failures. Think about it: a failing, sinful, rebellious child brings disgrace upon his parents. Do you think God is any different? Do you think He'd be glorified by His children's failures or derive any pleasure from them? Would He want the worst for His children instead of the best?\n\nNo! Jesus taught us that God loves us more than we love our own children (Mt. 7:7-11). We are the only ones holding back the floodgates of God's abundant blessings.\n\nAs long as we believe God is a hard, unloving Father, we can't hope to receive these benefits. The truth is that nothing makes God happier than to see His people blessed, happy, and successful in everything they do.\n\nRemember: His children are not rebels against Him — rebels are children of the devil (Jn. 8:44; Eph. 2:1-3; 1 Jn. 3:8-10). Real children of God won't get into sinful business, so any legitimate, worthwhile business is something God will prosper.\n\nMaking God your partner means full surrender of your life to Him, obedience to His Word in all things, and constant dependence on God to know His will and ways. Without God's approval and help, you're already headed for failure. But with Him guiding your every step, you cannot fail.\n\nMake sure your plans, ambitions, and undertakings all harmonize with God's highest will, then go ahead fearing nothing. If you want to overcome what's been holding you back, start right now by taking God into your life and obeying Him. He will lead you into prosperity.\n\nIt's only when you stop trying to do everything on your own and quit leaving God out of your affairs that you'll begin to experience Him. That's when His life, wisdom, and power will begin showing up in your life.\n\nChrist came to give life and give it more abundantly (Jn. 10:10). There's no limitation in God unless you limit Him by unbelief and failure to claim the promised benefits.\n\n### 3. Get Into Your Right Lifework\n\n**THE THIRD LAW OF PROSPERITY IS TO GET INTO THE LIFEWORK THAT YOU FEEL GOD WOULD HAVE YOU IN OR FOR WHICH YOU ARE BEST SUITED.**\n\nGod has a lifework for you, and you can be extremely happy and successful if you find that work and make God your lifelong partner.\n\nIf your lifework isn't clear to you, get this settled once and forever. At minimum, you can know what your present work is at all times. If there comes a time to change, you'll move in God's will as you've been doing. As you go along, God will guide you and open doors of opportunity.\n\nHere's something to remember: You are capable of filling your own place in life and doing your work better than anyone else can. Put your whole life into your work, realizing you labor for God and others.\n\nWhat matters to God is whether you work honestly and faithfully at your calling. That's all He asks, and He'll reward you accordingly.\n\nReal happiness lies in liking what you're called to do, not in doing something solely because you think you'd like it. You can become very unhappy if you let yourself think you're in the wrong lifework. Thousands are actually in the right work but are unhappy because they yearn for something else.\n\nWhen plans don't materialize or doors close in one occupation, look to God — He will open doors in another.\n\nBecome content with your lot and make a success of what you're currently doing. Then you'll be capable of success in other areas too. But if you never succeed at anything and are always dissatisfied with every kind of work, it's not the work that's wrong — it's you.\n\nYou must get hold of yourself and conquer that restless, shiftless spirit. God will help you become stable, contented, successful, and happy in His will in any honest work.\n\nAsk God to guide you into your lifework and His best plan for you. Talk things over with Him and wait for His guidance, which may come in ways you don't at first understand.\n\nIf you're in your right work, seek God to open up even greater fields of service. If you're in the wrong work, much prayer will open the right door. Stay constantly in tune with God and praise Him for His help. Thank Him for the work you already have.\n\nAct as if God is guiding you. Be happy and contented in your work, and you will be a success.\n\n### 4. Have Faith in God\n\n**THE FOURTH LAW OF PROSPERITY IS FAITH IN GOD.**\n\nYou must believe that God loves you and that He is with you, giving you strength to overcome all hindrances to your happiness and success.\n\nEvery person who has made a success in life has had to overcome opposition to their progress. The stories of successful people are full of how they overcame obstacles and finally succeeded. These challenges continue through life, even in success and happiness.\n\nIt's in life's struggles that you need faith in God. All your obstacles are small — they shrink to nothingness when compared to God's promises and the faith they create.\n\nWhen your troubles seem like giants, steal away in secret and pray. Get a good night's rest. Read God's promises, have faith in Him, and watch those troubles become very small.\n\nIf problems persist, get your mind off them. Turn them over to God, forget them for the time being, and you'll find He takes care of them better than you ever could.\n\nWe're told: "Commit thy way unto the Lord; trust also in him; and he shall bring it to pass" (Ps. 37:5). This law of success works without fail because God's Word cannot fail. The more you do this and the less you think about hindrances, the more you'll overcome them.\n\nGod is your Father if you've surrendered to Him. He can be trusted. He'll keep His promises better than any earthly friend.\n\nGet His promises in you and abide in Him, then "ye shall ask what ye will, and it shall be done unto you" (Jn. 15:7). What a blessing to know that God is with you and by faith you can do all things and get what you want!\n\nYour enemies cannot triumph. Your problems cannot defeat you. God cannot fail.\n\nThe minute you're tempted to worry about some problem, turn it over to God. He sees further than you can and works out things invisible to you.\n\nThrow your worries away! Become reckless in faith and confidence in God. Accept what comes with peace and thankfulness. "This is the victory that overcometh the world, even our faith" (1 Jn. 5:1-4).\n\n### 5. Be Unafraid to Launch Out\n\n**THE FIFTH LAW OF PROSPERITY IS TO BE UNAFRAID IN LAUNCHING OUT INTO NEW VENTURES AND TO MAKE THE BEST OF YOUR OPPORTUNITIES THAT COME.**\n\nMany people could have been prosperous if they hadn't been afraid to seize opportunities that came to them.\n\nHave no fear! If you've been praying for things that would bring prosperity, God is sure to open many doors. You must not be afraid to enter them under His guidance.\n\nHave confidence in yourself and in God that He's leading you and you cannot fail.\n\nWhen going into new ventures, be led by God as to how far to go and how much to invest. Don't go into debt beyond your ability to pay. Don't become reckless or act unwisely.\n\nBe led by God and go as far as you feel He wants you to go — as far as common sense dictates. Don't get your eyes on sudden riches and overstep yourself.\n\nIt's similar to climbing a mountain: you can always climb higher, and a steady, healthy ascent with secure footing is better than racing up recklessly and tumbling down.\n\nYou can always grow, and steady, healthy growth in business with security is better than blowing a bubble beyond the bursting point. Act sensibly and wisely and you will succeed.\n\nLet the law of increase work for you normally. Stay within your ability to take care of business and build on a solid foundation that will last.\n\n### 6. Follow Biblical Business Principles\n\n**THE SIXTH LAW OF PROSPERITY IS TO FOLLOW CERTAIN BUSINESS PRINCIPLES TAUGHT IN SCRIPTURE.**\n\nPaul laid down some powerful business principles: "Let love be without dissimulation. Abhor that which is evil; cleave to that which is good. Be kindly affectioned one to another with brotherly love; in honour preferring one another; not slothful in business; fervent in spirit; serving the Lord; rejoicing in hope; patient in tribulation; continuing instant in prayer; distributing to the necessity of saints; given to hospitality; bless them which persecute you; bless, and curse not; rejoice with them that do rejoice, and weep with them that weep . . . Be not wise in your own conceits. Recompense to no man evil for evil. Provide things honest in the sight of all men . . . Be not overcome of evil, but overcome evil with good" (Rom. 12:9-21).\n\nIf you obey these principles in business, you cannot help becoming happy, prosperous, and successful in all you undertake.\n\nPaul also taught: "That ye study to be quiet, and to do your own business, and work with your own hands, as we commanded you: that ye may walk honestly toward them that are without, and that ye may have lack of nothing" (1 Thess. 4:11-12).\n\nAnd Solomon said: "Seest thou a man diligent in business? He shall stand before kings; he shall not stand before mean [obscure, or unknown] men" (Prov. 22:29).\n\nThese passages teach that you should constantly work at making a success. Don't let grass grow under your feet! Use every legitimate means and don't neglect one opportunity to reach your goal.\n\nThis may mean self-sacrifice at first. It will mean many hours of hard work, much prayer for guidance, being honest with all people, and living to serve others through your business.\n\nSome people don't care to succeed enough to obey these laws. They're naturally shiftless, careless, indifferent, and unconcerned about their responsibilities. They won't put forth the least effort beyond what they feel like doing.\n\nThey're like sinners who know they're lost and doomed to Hell but don't care. Or like sick people who know how to get well but won't make the effort to meet the conditions.\n\nIf sinners really cared about going to Hell, not one would neglect salvation for a minute. Likewise, some would succeed if they cared enough to put forth their best effort.\n\nIf you don't put forth proper effort to prosper, don't expect God to bless you with prosperity.\n\n### 7. Obey the Golden Rule\n\n**THE SEVENTH LAW OF SUCCESS IS TO OBEY THE GOLDEN RULE.**\n\nThis means you'll always do unto others as you would have them do unto you.\n\nIf you do this, you'll never take advantage of anyone to prosper. You'll always be fair and square in all business dealings, giving customers value for money received.\n\nYou'll never skimp in making products or cheat in selling them. You'll never misrepresent any item and never sell a product you wouldn't use yourself.\n\nYou'll never be dishonest but will always tell the truth. You'll never be selfish. You'll always be friendly, courteous, helpful, happy, and committed to serving your fellow man and pleasing God.\n\nYou'll always let customers know you appreciate their business and show them how you seek to serve them.\n\nYou must be a real human being and make people feel they'd like to be around you. You can be that kind of person!\n\nNo matter your age, appearance, education, or position — you can be real and attractive in personality. If you let Christ live in and through you, He will free you from dullness and fill your life with charm and influence.\n\nPower and radiance come from a change of heart and from Christ living in you. Regardless of age, you can have charm, attractiveness, and influence. These qualities can't be put on in a beauty parlor — they must come from the inward person.\n\nIt's fine to be as attractive as you can be outwardly, and this will help. But let the greatest attraction be "the hidden man of the heart, in that which is not corruptible, even the ornament of a meek and quiet spirit, which is in the sight of God of great price" (1 Pet. 3:1-4).\n\n### 8. Seek First the Kingdom of God\n\n**ONE UNFAILING LAW OF PROSPERITY IS TO "SEEK YE FIRST THE KINGDOM OF GOD, AND HIS RIGHTEOUSNESS."**\n\nIf we do this, we're promised: "and all these things [blessings of life] shall be added unto you" (Mt. 6:33).\n\nThis means putting the interests of God's kingdom and others before our own. We must recognize that a person's life doesn't consist of the abundance of things possessed (Lk. 12:15).\n\nOnce we learn this lesson, we can easily be free from covetousness, which makes us live only for self.\n\nJesus said: "He that findeth his life shall lose it: and he that loseth his life for my sake shall find it" (Mt. 10:39).\n\nIn other words, the self-centered person who holds onto life's things only to please himself will lose the reality of true living. He'll be banished from eternal society, where everyone — like God — consecrates themselves to what's best for all.\n\nBut if we give up selfish living and seek first God's kingdom and righteousness, we'll receive "an hundredfold" in this life and in the life to come, everlasting life. Christ Himself assures us of this in Mt. 19:27-30.\n\nGiving up self-interests for God's kingdom means consecrating yourself to live for the good of all people and the betterment of society. If everyone lived for everyone's best good, we'd have heaven on Earth!\n\nPaul said: "For none of us liveth to himself, and no man dieth to himself . . . For whether we live, we live unto the Lord; and whether we die, we die unto the Lord: whether we live therefore, or die, we are the Lord's" (Rom. 14:7-11).\n\n### 9. Give to Others\n\n**ONE LAW OF PROSPERITY IS THAT OF GIVING TO OTHERS.**\n\nGenerally, the person who always wants to know what they'll get out of everything makes a failure and loses what they already have. They lose friends and become an outcast from society. No one wants to be around them, and they go through life alone.\n\nBut if you start with the idea of giving instead of getting, you'll succeed.\n\nGive service to people in your business and you'll prosper. Bless others and seek God and His righteousness, and all the blessings you need will be added to you.\n\nIf there's one thing you can succeed at in life, it's giving. You can always give, and if you will, you'll have more to give. This is a law that never fails.\n\nJesus said: "Give, and it shall be given you; good measure, pressed down, and shaken together, and running over, shall men give into your bosom" (Lk. 6:38).\n\nPeople may temporarily hinder you from making money, but they can't hinder you from giving. No one wants to stop you from being generous — you become a hero when you give! You're wonderful to everybody as long as you're generous.\n\nSomething wonderful happens when you give. Begin by giving yourself to God and to others in service.\n\nGive your good will, your wishes, your time, your talents, your life, your all — and you'll begin receiving from both God and the people you bless.\n\nOccasionally you may find someone who won't give back, but for each one who doesn't, many others will give to you in appreciation. You should give your all to serving God and others not because you expect something in return, but because it's right and because it's your nature. The giving back will happen naturally.\n\nYou'll have many friends pulling for you to succeed in anything you undertake. They'll put forth every effort to help you. One will show you how to make more money. Another will open a door of blessing here, another there. Others will give you things that help in times of need.\n\nAs you're prospered materially and spiritually, give out to help others, and your supply will never be exhausted. "Give and it shall be given you" is both a divine law and a divine promise.\n\nThe Christian will prosper, and as you do, give yourself to hospitality, remembering that "Love worketh no ill to his neighbor: therefore, love is the fulfilling of the law" (Rom. 12:13; 13:10).\n\nPaul said: "He that soweth sparingly shall reap also sparingly; and he that soweth bountifully shall reap also bountifully . . . God is able to make all grace abound toward you; that ye, always having all sufficiency in all things, may abound to every good work: (As it is written, He hath dispersed abroad; he hath given to the poor; his righteousness remaineth forever. Now he that ministereth seed to the sower both minister bread for your food, and multiply your seed sown, and increase the fruits of your righteousness;) Being enriched in everything to all bountifulness, which causeth through us thanksgiving to God. For the ministration of this service not only supplieth the want of the saints, but is abundant also by many thanksgivings unto God" (2 Cor. 9:6-12).\n\n### 10. Honor the Lord with Your Substance\n\n**THE LAST LAW OF PROSPERITY WE WILL MENTION IS TO "HONOUR THE LORD WITH THY SUBSTANCE, AND WITH THE FIRSTFRUITS OF ALL THINE INCREASE"** (Prov. 3:6-12).\n\nIf we do this, we have the promise: "So shall thy barns be filled with plenty, and thy presses shall burst out with new wine."\n\nWe also have the promise in Mal. 3:10-11 that if we honor God and pay our tithes, He will open the windows of Heaven, pour out blessings we won't be able to receive, rebuke the devourer, and bless the works of our hands.\n\nIn this Malachi passage, we have an accusation by God that He was being robbed; a command to bring all the tithes into the storehouse; and a challenge by God to prove Him and see if He wouldn't cause prosperity.\n\nIf you pay your tithes — ten percent of all your income, wages, increase, or profit — to the Lord, and keep accurate records so there are no mistakes, God is under obligation to meet His challenge.\n\nMr. Kerr of the Kerr-Mason fruit jar fame started tithing when he was in debt. He had a mortgage on his home and was worried about his finances. Within three months of starting to tithe, unexpected blessings came in the form of a fruit jar patent.\n\nAs he put it: "God opened my eyes to see His love and faithfulness to His promises." That same year, with $100 and strong faith in God's tithing promises from Mal. 3:10, he organized a corporation that has grown beyond bounds.\n\nI've never seen or heard of anyone who hasn't been blessed when they've faithfully tithed with faith in God's promises. There are no testimonies I know of about rich people starting to tithe, but there are many testimonies of people who began tithing when poor and are now rich.\n\nHere are a few examples:\n\n> Mr. Crowell, founder of the Quaker Oats Company.\n\n> Mr. Colgate, founder of Colgate soaps, perfumes and powders.\n\n> Mr. Proctor of the Ivory Soap Company.\n\n> A. A. Hyde of "Mentholatum" fame.\n\n> Henry Delaney of Resinol Ointment renown.\n\n> Mr. Matthias Baldwin, founder of the Baldwin Locomotive Industry.\n\n> R. G. LeTourneau, maker of large earth-moving equipment.\n\nNo one can practice these laws of prosperity without becoming prosperous. You can't truly give yourself to God and others to be a blessing in business without reaping the rich rewards promised in these laws.\n\nIf you want to prosper, set your own house in order and faithfully follow these laws. Soon God will lead you into blessings you never thought possible.\n\nApply yourself strictly to these laws and principles, and watch things happen that will bless you more than you're blessing others.\n\n---\n\n## Ten Questions For Supplement Six\n\n### Do You Know:\n\n- That the so-called appearance of Samuel from the dead in 1 Sam. 28 was a demonic spirit imitating Samuel to deceive Saul? This is plainly stated in 1 Chron. 10:13-14.\n\n- That the Battle of Armageddon is not to be an ordinary battle between two sets of nations? This battle will be one between the armies of Heaven under Christ and the armies of the Earth under Antichrist as proven in Zech. 14; Joel 3; Rev. 19:11-21.\n\n- That all saints, dead or alive, will soon be taken to Heaven bodily to be forever with the Lord? This is expressly stated in 1 Thess. 4:13-16; 1 Cor. 15:23, 51-58; Phil. 3:20-21; Jn. 14:1-3; Col. 3:4.\n\n- That Russia will not fulfill prophecies Bible students say she will? Bible students in general teach that Russia is the king of the north of Dan. 11 and that Russia will be the head of the nations of Ezek. 38; 39 who will come against Christ at Armageddon. Neither one of these doctrines is true. The king of the north of Dan. 11 is the Antichrist from Syria, and the king of the south of this same chapter is Egypt, two of the four divisions of the Grecian Empire of Dan. 8:20-23, who will fight against each other "at the time of the end." The Antichrist will come from Syria and will overthrow Egypt and many other countries, fulfilling Dan. 11:35-45. In Dan. 11:44, after Antichrist conquers these many countries and gets power over the ten kingdoms that will yet be formed within the old Roman Empire of Dan. 7:23-24; Rev. 17:8-17, we read of tidings out of the north and out of the east that will trouble him, and of his going forth with great fury to "utterly make away many." In other words, when Antichrist gets power over these ten kingdoms, the countries to the north and east of these kingdoms will make war on him and he will become the victor over these new enemies during the last 3½ years of this age. Then he will become the ruler of Russia, Germany, and other countries to the north and east of the Roman Empire territory and will thus fulfill Ezek. 38; 39 by leading all these countries to war against the Jews and Jesus Christ at Armageddon. Thus, instead of Russia being the leading nation and the head of all nations at Armageddon, she is a conquered nation along with others under the Antichrist who will be the leader of all nations against Christ at Armageddon.\n\n- That the Bible plainly shows how the nations of the world will be lined up against each other in the last days and who will be the victor? As seen in the last point above, the Antichrist will come from Syria and will get power over ten kingdoms that will be formed inside the territory of the Roman Empire in the last days. These ten will be lined up against Russia, Germany, Japan, China, and other northern and eastern nations outside the ten kingdoms. The ten kingdoms under Antichrist will be the victor in a war lasting most of the last 3½ years of this age. Then the Antichrist will lead the nations against Jerusalem to destroy the Jews and to stop Christ from setting up His kingdom, but he will be defeated by Christ before all of Jerusalem is conquered (Zech. 14:1-21; Rev. 19:11-21). Thus Antichrist will be defeated before he conquers the whole world.\n\n- That the Bible gives you a simple key to the book of Revelation that will make the whole book simple to all alike? This key is found in Rev. 1:19 and 4:1. The book is in three clearly defined divisions: first, "the things which thou hast seen," that is, the vision of Christ, as in Rev. 1; second, "the things which are," that is, the things concerning the churches, as in Rev. 2-3; third, "the things which shall be hereafter," that is, after the churches, as in Rev. 4–22. To prove that everything of Revelation, from the fourth chapter on, must be after the churches, in Rev. 4:1 after he had written the vision of Christ in chapter one and the things concerning the churches in chapters two and three, John was caught up to Heaven and was told that he was to see the things which must be hereafter, that is, after the churches. If the things of Rev. 4:1 through the rest of the book must be after the churches, then they must be fulfilled after the churches and not during the time of the churches. This is proof that the rapture of the Church must take place before the fulfillment of everything in Rev. 4-22. If we will take literally and consecutively the events of these chapters and believe that they will happen after the rapture of the Church, nothing in the book will be hard to understand.\n\n- That prophecy is as simple as history to understand? This is true because prophecy is simply history written beforehand in the same literal language as history which is a record of things that have happened. If you take all prophecy as a simple record of what is going to happen — the same way you take history as a record of what has happened — it will be impossible to misunderstand prophecy.\n\n- That Christ is literally coming back to the world soon to set up a literal earthly kingdom and to reign over all nations forever? That He is literally coming back is clear from Zech. 14:1-9; Mt. 24:29-31; Acts 1:11; Rev. 19:11-21. That He will rule all nations forever after this is clear from Dan. 2:44-45; 7:13-14, 18; Isa. 9:6-7; Lk. 1:32-33; Rev. 11:15.\n\n- That at the second coming of Christ many sinners will be left to go into the Millennium? That sinners will be here is very clearly stated in Isa. 2:2-4; 65:20; 66:18-19; 1 Cor. 15:24-28; Rev. 20:1-10; Zech. 14:16-21.\n\n- That the rapture of the Church will be before the Tribulation and the coming of the Antichrist? We have already proven in Point 6, above, that the rapture must take place before the fulfillment of Rev. 4–22, which describes the Tribulation period and the Millennium and the New Earth after the Millennium. According to 2 Thess. 2:7-8 it is stated that the hinderer of lawlessness will be taken out of the way "And then shall that wicked be revealed" and since the wicked is here for the last seven years of this age, according to Dan. 9:27, the hinderer must be removed before Antichrist comes and before the Tribulation that he will cause when he comes. That the hinderer refers to the Church will be proven in Lesson Forty-three.\n\n---\n\nThe conscience is not a perfect guide, but it's a real one — and ignoring it has real consequences. The same is true of the conditions for receiving God's provision: they're not arbitrary, and bypassing them has a cost. So here's the question: Is there something your conscience has been telling you for weeks or months that you've been suppressing? And is there a condition for answered prayer — faith, obedience, forgiveness — that you've been avoiding? What would change if you dealt with both today?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk4bq001xewhjtj0r2lf8	cmnknk4bp001qewhjk5bcqnfj	Lesson 41 — A Gist of the Book of Revelation	READING	6459	6	\N	t	Revelation is the most avoided book in the New Testament — and the only book that promises a specific blessing to everyone who reads it: 'Blessed is he that readeth, and they that hear the words of this prophecy' (Rev. 1:3). Most people stay away from it because it seems impossibly strange: dragons, bowls of wrath, a beast with seven heads. But Revelation is not meant to confuse — it's meant to prepare. And once you have the framework, the strangeness resolves into one of the most coherent books in the Bible.\n\nLast time we walked through Daniel's seventieth week — the seven-year Tribulation period. Now we survey the book that describes that period and everything surrounding it in the most detail: Revelation. This lesson is the map. The following lessons are the territory.\n\n![Lesson 41 — A Gist of the Book of Revelation](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OstirIrkSSw8OsqyXxGpeBMuoLIEnWg5fdi9R1)\n\n## I. Literalness of the Book\n\nThe book of Revelation contains both literal and figurative language. Here's a helpful guideline: take statements literally wherever possible.\n\nIn other words, when you read something in Revelation, understand it to mean exactly what it says — unless doing so would be highly improbable, violate basic principles of language, contradict spiritually sound reasoning, or conflict with other Scriptures on the same topic. Only when a passage truly won't work as a literal statement should you look for a symbolic interpretation.\n\nThis approach is the only sound way to read Revelation. Why? Because the book calls itself a revelation — something meant to be understood, not a mystery wrapped in confusion. To spiritualize everything is to deny what the book claims to be.\n\nEvery scene and truth in Revelation is explained within the book itself. Before you go hunting through other parts of the Bible for answers, first discover what Revelation itself says about its own truths. Earlier prophecies from other books will certainly shed light on many passages and help you dig deeper, but Revelation stands in perfect harmony with all preceding prophecies — it's the logical and beautiful completion of them all.\n\n## II. The Key to the Interpretation\n\n"Write the things which thou hast seen, and the things which are, and the things which shall be hereafter" (Rev. 1:19).\n\n> 1. PART I. "The things which thou hast seen"; that is, Christ in the midst of the seven candlesticks (Rev. 1:12-18, 20), as seen by John before he began to write.\n\n> 2. PART II. "The things which are"; that is, the things concerning the churches then existent and those which should exist throughout the Church Age to the rapture. This division takes in only Rev. 2-3.\n\n> 3. PART III. "The things which shall be hereafter"; that is, the things which shall come to pass after the rapture of the Church. This division includes all of the events of Rev. 4-22. One has only to believe this threefold natural division as given by Christ to understand the book fully, especially as to the time of the fulfillment of the things of each division. The moment these divisions are forgotten and the reader begins to disarrange them and insert certain things into the one or the other that are not part of the division, he will become confused as to the divine order of these "things" which are so clearly given in consecutive order, and he will miss the true intent of the "things" written therein. That we refrain from confusing these "things" is absolutely imperative if a true understanding of them is to be gained.\n\nHere's something important to understand: everything in Rev. 4–22 must take place after the churches, and therefore after the rapture. Rev. 4:1 confirms this clearly.\n\nAfter John recorded the vision of Christ in Rev. 1 (completing Part I) and everything Christ told him to write to the churches in Rev. 2-3 (completing Part II), he was told in Rev. 4:1 that he would now see "things which must be hereafter" — that is, after the things of the churches. So if everything from Rev. 4:1 onward must be after the churches, then all the events of Rev. 4-22 must be after the churches.\n\nIf they must be after the churches, they cannot happen during the time of the churches. And if they cannot happen during the time of the churches, then the Church is no longer on Earth during the fulfillment of these events. Think of it like chapters in a book — you can't read chapter three while you're still in chapter two.\n\nRevelation 4:1 literally reads in the Greek, "After these things [the things concerning the churches of the previous division] I saw, and behold a door opened in heaven, and the first voice [of Rev. 1:10] which I heard was as a trumpet speaking with me, saying, Come up hither, and I will show to thee what things must take place after these things," that is, after the churches which he wrote about in the previous chapters.\n\nThese three divisions don't overlap or run simultaneously. One division must be completely fulfilled before the next begins.\n\nSo if you're fair with the text and recognize that every event from Rev. 4:1 through the end of the book must be fulfilled after the rapture — and if you don't pull any of these events back and place them among the churches as happening before the rapture — everything in the book will automatically clear up.\n\nFully 95 percent of the false theories about Revelation exist because interpreters have failed to recognize this natural threefold division. Many teachers emphasize these divisions at the start of their study, but when it comes to actually keeping all the events of Rev. 4-22 after the churches, they fail to follow through.\n\nThey'll place some events from the seals, trumpets, and vials back in the Church Age, explaining them as fulfilled before the rapture. They'll interpret the woman and the man child in connection with the Church of this age, when Jesus told John these were part of the "things" that must come after the churches. They'll explain the dragon, the beast, the false prophet, or other events of Rev. 4-22 as being fulfilled alongside the churches, when the truth is these are part of what must come after the churches.\n\nWatch for this pattern, and you'll be sensible enough to place the events of Rev. 4-22 where they belong — after the churches, and therefore after the rapture.\n\n## III. The Division of the Book By Classes (1 Cor. 10:32)\n\n> 1. Rev. 1–3 deals primarily with the Church on Earth.\n\n> 2. Rev. 4–5 pictures the Church and the Old Testament saints with God in Heaven after the rapture, represented by the twenty-four elders.\n\n> 3. Rev. 6–19 deals primarily with Israel under the last oppression by the Gentiles in fulfillment of Daniel's Seventieth Week after the rapture of the Church.\n\n> 4. Rev. 20–22 refers to all three classes, the Church, the Jews, and the Gentiles. The earthly Jews will be the head of all earthly Gentiles and Christ with the Church will reign over both forever and ever.\n\n## IV. The Consecutive Order of Revelation\n\nThe book follows a succession of consecutive events from beginning to end — it's not unsystematic or confused. The events should be understood in the order God gave them, not rearranged according to our own finite ideas.\n\nIt's almost universally recognized that Rev. 1-5 and Rev. 19-22 form a consecutive story. We can therefore logically conclude that the events of Rev. 6-18 are also consecutive.\n\nIf Revelation's events aren't meant to be taken consecutively, on what grounds would we attempt to fix another arrangement? Where would we find any other authorized standard than what's plainly evident in the book itself?\n\nWhy would we hopelessly confuse a plain prophecy given by the Lord concerning "things which must shortly come to pass" and change the order of events from what He gave? Surely He set forth the events in their proper order of fulfillment so He wouldn't confuse His servants.\n\n## V. The Outline of the Book of Revelation\n\nINTRODUCTORY REMARKS (Rev. 1:1-11, 19).\n\n> 1. The Introduction (Rev. 1:1-3).\n\n> 2. The Salutation—From God the Father, Jesus Christ, and the Holy Spirit (Rev. 1:4-5a).\n\n> 3. The Exaltation—"Unto him that loved us . . . washed us . . . made us kings" (Rev. 1:5b-6).\n\n> 4. The Chief Theme of Revelation—"Behold he cometh with clouds" (Rev. 1:7).\n\n> 5. The Eternity of the Son of God—"I am Alpha and Omega" (Rev. 1:8).\n\n> 6. The Prophet—John and His Commission to Write (Rev. 1:9-11, 19).\n\n> 7. The Key to the Interpretation (Rev. 1:19).\n\nI. "THE THINGS WHICH THOU HAST SEEN" (Rev. 1:12-18, 20).\n\n> 1. The Vision of Christ (Rev. 1:12-18).\n\n> 2. The Symbols of the Vision Interpreted (Rev. 1:20).\n\nII. "THE THINGS WHICH ARE" (Rev. 2:1–3:22).\n\n> 1. The Things Concerning the Local Church at Ephesus (Rev. 2:1-7).\n\n> 2. The Things Concerning the Local Church at Smyrna (Rev. 2:8-11).\n\n> 3. The Things Concerning the Local Church at Pergamos (Rev. 2:12-17).\n\n> 4. The Things Concerning the Local Church at Thyatira (Rev. 2:18-29).\n\n> 5. The Things Concerning the Local Church at Sardis (Rev. 3:1-6).\n\n> 6. The Things Concerning the Local Church at Philadelphia (Rev. 3:7-13).\n\n> 7. The Things Concerning the Local Church at Laodicea (Rev. 3:14-22).\n\nIII. "THE THINGS WHICH SHALL BE HEREAFTER" (Rev. 4:1–22:5).\n\n> 1. The Heavenly Tabernacle—the raptured saints with God (Rev. 4:1–5:14).\n\n> (1) The Heavenly Door (Rev. 4:1).\n\n> (2) The Heavenly Throne (Rev. 4:2, 3, 5).\n\n> (3) The Heavenly Elders (Rev. 4:4).\n\n> (4) The Heavenly Sea of Glass (Rev. 4:6b-8).\n\n> (5) The Heavenly Living Creatures (Rev. 4:6b-8).\n\n> (6) The Heavenly Worship because of Creation (Rev. 4:9-11).\n\n> (7) The Heavenly Book (Rev. 5:1-4).\n\n> (8) The Heavenly Lamb (Rev. 5:5-7).\n\n> (9) The Heavenly Worship because of Worthiness to the Lamb (Rev. 5:8-14).\n\n> 2. Daniel's Seventieth Week (Rev. 6:1–19:21; Dan. 9:27).\n\n> (1) The First Six Seals (Rev. 6:1-17). (Parenthetical, Rev. 7:1-17.)\n\n> (2) The Seventh Seal and First Six Trumpets (Rev. 8:1–9:21). (Parenthetical, Rev. 8:2-6, 13; Rev. 10:1–11:13.)\n\n> (3) The Seventh Trumpet (Rev. 11:14–13:18). (Parenthetical, Rev. 14:1-20.)\n\n> (4) The First Six Vials (Rev. 15:1–16:12). (Parenthetical, Rev. 15:2-4; Rev. 16:13-16.)\n\n> (5) The Seventh Vial (Rev. 16:17–18:24). (Parenthetical, Rev. 17:1-18.)\n\n> (6) The Marriage of the Lamb, the Second Advent, and Armageddon (Rev. 19:1-21). (Parenthetical, Rev. 19:1-10.)\n\n> 3. The Thousand Years and After (Rev. 20:1-15).\n\n> (1) The Expulsion of Satan from the Earth (Rev. 20:1-3).\n\n> (2) The Millennial Reign of Christ and His Saints (Rev. 20:4-6).\n\n> (3) Satan's Post-Millennial Career and Doom (Rev. 20:7-10).\n\n> (4) The Final Judgment (Rev. 20:11-15).\n\n> 4. The Eternal Perfect State—the Ages of the Ages (Rev. 21:1–22:5).\n\n> (1) The New Heaven (Rev. 21:1).\n\n> (2) The New Earth (Rev. 21:1).\n\n> (3) The New Jerusalem (Rev. 21:2, 9-21).\n\n> (4) The New Peoples (Rev. 21:3).\n\n> (5) The New Conditions (Rev. 21:4-8).\n\n> (6) The New Temple (Rev. 21:22).\n\n> (7) The New Light (Rev. 21:23-27).\n\n> (8) The New Paradise (Rev. 22:1-5).\n\nTHE CONCLUSION (Rev. 22:6-21).\n\n> 1. The Confirmation of Revelation (Rev. 22:6-7).\n\n> 2. The Mistake in the Object of Worship (Rev. 22:8-9).\n\n> 3. The Last Instructions (Rev. 22:10-19).\n\n> 4. The Last Promise and Last Prayer (Rev. 22:20-21).\n\n## VI. Part I. "The Things Which Thou Hast Seen" (Rev. 1:12-20)\n\nThis section concerns John's vision of Christ on the island of Patmos. You'll see Christ's body, head, eyes, feet, voice, right hand, mouth, and countenance described in vivid detail.\n\nJohn also records the powerful effect this vision had on him, along with the interpretation of the first symbols he saw.\n\n## VII. Part II. "The Things Which Are" (Rev. 2:1–3:22)\n\nThe messages to the seven churches appear immediately after the vision of Christ standing in the midst of the churches. Chapters 2 and 3 contain only "the things which are" — that is, the things concerning the Church on Earth down to the rapture.\n\n### The Threefold Application of the Letters\n\n**1. A LOCAL APPLICATION** of the letters to the churches in John's day. These seven epistles portray actual conditions in seven local churches in Asia. This is clear from the messages themselves and from Rev. 1:4, 11, 20.\n\n**2. A PROPHETIC APPLICATION** of the letters to churches throughout this dispensation until the rapture. They reveal the spiritual conditions found in local churches and individuals throughout the Church Age. This makes sense because the book is prophecy, and these letters are as applicable to believers throughout the Church Age as other New Testament epistles and Scriptures (2 Tim. 3:16, 17).\n\n**3. AN INDIVIDUAL APPLICATION** of the letters, so that individuals may be warned by the failures revealed, profit from the warning, and find encouragement from the promises to the overcomer.\n\nNow, here's something important to understand: the so-called "dispensational application" of these church letters — the idea that they portray seven church periods or phases of church history — is really based on human theory alone. You won't find Scripture to prove this theory in any aspect.\n\nSimilarity to certain phases of church history proves nothing. You can find similarities between the history of the Church and Israel, and there's no end to similarities you could find between almost any two events you choose to compare. It's similar to finding shapes in clouds — you can see what you're looking for, but that doesn't mean it's actually there.\n\nAny conclusions based on human theory alone are not dependable and can be misleading and unscriptural. Much confusion and many false teachings have grown from this method of interpretation. If there truly was such an application of these letters, surely we'd have two or three passages to support each letter and its application to some definite period in history.\n\nSome schools say we're in the Philadelphian or Laodicean periods, that the rapture takes place in one of these two periods, that the Church Age ends with Philadelphia, that Laodicea deals with things after the rapture, that the Ephesian period ended with the apostles, and many other unscriptural theories.\n\nThe truth is, we still have the kind of conditions described in Ephesus and all seven churches in local churches today. These conditions existed in John's day and will continue as long as the Church is on Earth. We can see Scriptures that prove local, prophetic, and individual applications of the letters, but anything said about a dispensational application of seven distinct periods must rest on human theory and causes endless differences in interpretation — so it's best to set this application aside entirely.\n\nWith the seven letters to the seven churches in Asia (Rev. 2:1–3:22), we end everything in Revelation concerning the Church on Earth. From Rev. 4:1 onward, the Revelation deals with prophecies that will happen after the rapture.\n\nWe won't attempt to discuss each application of the letters or explore all their details here. The applications are clear — they apply to anyone in any local church throughout this dispensation where these conditions exist.\n\nTo overcome such conditions and be rewarded is the message to every believer in any local church in this entire age. All these conditions have existed more or less in local churches since John's time and will always exist as long as churches remain on Earth. The duty of each person reading these letters is clear, so further comment isn't necessary here. This saves space for discussing more important truths in the book. (See Point II in the outline under Point V, above, for a list of the seven churches.)\n\n## VIII. Part III. "The Things Which Shall Be Hereafter" (Rev. 4:1–22:5)\n\n### 1. THE HEAVENLY TABERNACLE (Rev. 4:1–5:14)\n\nIn chapters four and five, we're given scenes in Heaven. We see almost a complete picture of the heavenly tabernacle — the pattern from which the earthly tabernacle was made (Heb. 8:1-5; 9:1-10, 22-24; 10:1). See and compare the illustrations of the earthly and heavenly tabernacles on your chart. Here are nine key truths:\n\n> (1) **The heavenly door** (Rev. 4:1). This is a literal opening to Heaven, as is plainly evident here and in Rev. 11:19; 14:15-18; 15:5-8; 16:1, 17.\n\n> (2) **The heavenly throne** (Rev. 4:2-5). This is the throne seen throughout the book where God the Father sits (Rev. 1:4; 3:21; 4:10; 5:1, 7, 11-13; 6:16; 7:9-17; 8:3; 12:5; 14:1-5; 16:17; 19:4-5; 21:5; 22:1-3). The throne is white (Rev. 20:11-15). It's a real throne because God as a real person sits on it (Dan. 7:9-14; Isa. 6; Heb. 8:1–12:1-2).\n\n> (3) **The heavenly elders** (Rev. 4:4-11; 5:8-10; 7:13; 11:16-19; 14:3; 19:4). These elders have thrones placed around God's throne — so if one throne is literal, the others must be too. The elders are redeemed persons, as the word "elders" is never used in Scripture of angels, only of human beings. Their white garments are used in Revelation only of the righteousness of saints (Rev. 3:5, 18; 6:11; 7:9, 13; 15:6; 19:9, 14). (See Revelation Expounded for a detailed study of all the truths of Rev. 4-5.)\n\n> (4) **The heavenly sea of glass** (Rev. 4:6). This is another part of the heavenly tabernacle or capital building of God. It's located before God's throne, and on it the redeemed and other heavenly creatures gather to worship on certain occasions (Rev. 7:9-17; 15:2-4).\n\n> (5) **The heavenly living creatures** (Rev. 4:6-8). These are four angelic beings that call attention to God's holiness. They appear in Rev. 4:4-8; 5:8-10; 15:7. (See Lesson Six for a study of spirit-beings.)\n\n> (6) **The heavenly worship** (Rev. 4:9-11; 5:8-14). How worship is done is made plain in these passages, so no additional comment is necessary.\n\n> (7) **The heavenly book** (Rev. 5:1-4; 10:1-11). This book contains the events of Revelation as recorded by John. It's not a book of redemption or of title deeds to the Earth, as some teach — there's no reference to such books anywhere in Scripture. The seals that bind the book clearly contain certain events (Rev. 6:1–8:1). The things "written within" are revelations that John wrote in his book, as is clear in Rev. 10:1-11 where the angel gives the book to John to reveal its contents. The things in the book are "concerning many peoples and nations and tongues and kings" which John wrote about after the seals were broken.\n\n> (8) **The heavenly Lamb** (Rev. 5:5-7). This symbolizes Christ, who is the root of David in this passage and in Gen. 49:10; Mic. 5:1-2; 2 Sam. 7:8-17; Ps. 89:35-37; Isa. 9:6-7; 11:1-9; Jer. 23:5-6. He is the Lamb of God (Isa. 53; Jn. 1:29; 1 Pet. 18-23).\n\n---\n\n### The First One-Half of Daniel's Seventieth Week or the Tribulation (Rev. 6:1–9:21)\n\n### 2. THE FIRST SIX SEALS (Rev. 6:1-17)\n\nThe seven seals and the first six trumpets take place in succession during the first 3½ years of the Tribulation Week — from the beginning to the middle of the Week. The seventh trumpet blows in the middle of the Week, as we'll see.\n\n> (1) **The first seal** (Rev. 6:1-2). The white horse rider represents the rise of the Antichrist at the beginning of Daniel's Seventieth Week. He'll make a seven-year covenant with Israel and go forth conquering and to conquer other nations, as we've studied in Lessons Thirty-nine and Forty.\n\n> This rider couldn't be a symbol of the United States, as some teach, because this is a revelation showing a man being crowned and then warring during events that must take place after the churches. There's no clear reference in prophecy concerning America, as we've seen in Supplement Thirteen, Question 7.\n\n> Neither could this be a symbol of Christ, for He's the one opening the seals and revealing events that happen on Earth after the rapture. Christ doesn't come to conquer until after the Tribulation (Mt. 24:29-31; Rev. 19:11-21). This seal reveals the conquests of Antichrist, fulfilling Dan. 7:8-9, 23-25; 8:8-10, 20-23; 35-45.\n\n> (2) **The second seal** (Rev. 6:3-4). This is a clear picture of war following the rise of an ambitious conqueror, the Antichrist (Dan. 7:24; 11:40-45).\n\n> (3) **The third seal** (Rev. 6:5-6). This symbolizes famine, which always follows wars. Bread by measure signifies scarcity of food (Ezek. 4:10-17).\n\n> (4) **The fourth seal** (Rev. 6:7-8). Here death and Hell are symbolized by riders on horses. Death and Hell are always turned loose on Earth during wars and famines, so this is the natural result of the first three seals (Mt. 24:6-7). The word "pale" means "green" in the Greek, as translated in Rev. 8:7; 9:4; Mk. 6:39. This is why the grave line on the chart is colored green from the Garden of Eden to the Great White Throne Judgment.\n\n> (5) **The fifth seal** (Rev. 6:9-11). This is a picture of the first martyrs of the Tribulation. They weren't ready to go in the rapture or they would have been translated before the Tribulation. They get saved after the rapture and are those killed between the rapture and the fifth seal. They're told to rest until the remaining tribulation saints are killed, and then vengeance will be taken on those who killed them.\n\n> (6) **The sixth seal** (Rev. 6:12-17). Beginning with this seal in the first part of the Week, God's wrath begins. The first five seals show the wrath of man, but with the sixth seal God begins pouring out His judgments because of the persecution of His people on Earth. Seven great events happen under this seal: an earthquake, darkening of the sun, turning the moon to blood, falling of meteors, rolling back of the heavens, moving of mountains and islands, and the beginning of God's wrath.\n\n---\n\n### (Parenthetical: the Two Companies of Redeemed, Rev. 7:1-17)\n\nRevelation 7 is the first parenthetical passage of the book. Picture it this way: the main story pauses to zoom in on important details happening alongside the main events. These events aren't the contents of the seals — they're inserted between the sixth and seventh seals to explain other things happening alongside the main order of events after the sixth seal.\n\nThis revelation of two companies of the redeemed breaks the order of the seal events to reveal that both Jews and Gentiles will be saved during the Tribulation.\n\n> (1) **The sealing of 144,000 Jews** (Rev. 7:1-8). That they're all Jews and not Gentiles is clear from the fact that they're from the tribes of Israel. They get saved after the rapture, for if they had been saved at the time of the rapture they wouldn't have been left on Earth (1 Thess. 4:16-17; 1 Cor. 15:23).\n\n> They're sealed for the purpose of going through the trumpet judgments — from when they begin after the seals are finished in the first part of the Week to the middle of the Week. The first four trumpet angels were told not to hurt the earth, trees, grass, and seas until the 144,000 were sealed. Special direction is given to the fifth and sixth trumpet angels to hurt only those who were not sealed (Rev. 9:4). The 144,000 will be caught up to Heaven under the seventh trumpet, as we'll prove in Lesson Forty-five.\n\n> The seal is the name of the Father written on their foreheads — it doesn't mean keeping Saturday as the sabbath (Rev. 7:3; 14:1). The promise of being written upon is given to all believers as a reward in the next life (Rev. 3:12; 22:4), but these will be written upon in this life to protect them from the trumpet judgments.\n\n> (2) **The tribulation saints** (Rev. 7:9-17). This company won't escape the Tribulation because they weren't ready to go in the rapture when it happened. They get saved after the rapture (Acts 2:16-21) and become martyrs for Christ (Rev. 6:9-11; 7:9-17; 15:2-4; 20:4-6). The majority will be slain by Antichrist (Rev. 13:7-18; 15:2-4; 20:4-6).\n\n---\n\n### 3. THE SEVENTH SEAL AND THE FIRST SIX TRUMPETS (Rev. 8:1–9:21)\n\n> (1) **The seventh seal** (Rev. 8:1). This simply means silence in Heaven for half an hour, just as it plainly states — no interpretation necessary.\n\n---\n\n### (Parenthetical: Seven Trumpet Angels and the Priestly Angel, Rev. 8:2-6)\n\nRevelation 8:2-6 is parenthetical because it explains the work of the priestly angel and the preparation of the seven trumpet judgments. This passage is fulfilled before the seven trumpets begin.\n\nAfter Christ's ministry at the altar, the seven angels prepare to sound their trumpets. The judgments are as literal as stated and are simple to understand if you believe what's written about what happens when each trumpet sounds. They're as literal as the plagues in Egypt and serve the same purpose — to protect Israel during the Tribulation of the first 3½ years.\n\n> (2) **The first trumpet** — hail, fire and blood burning up grass and trees (Rev. 8:7).\n\n> (3) **The second trumpet** — one-third of the sea changing to blood (Rev. 8:8-9).\n\n> (4) **The third trumpet** — one-third of the rivers poisoned (Rev. 8:10-11).\n\n> (5) **The fourth trumpet** — one-third of the planets darkened (Rev. 8:12).\n\n### (Parenthetical: Three Woes Announced, Rev. 8:13)\n\n> (6) **The fifth trumpet — the first woe** (Rev. 9:1-12). Demon creatures will be loosed from the pit to torment men for five months. Men will desire to die but cannot until this period is over. The language of this passage should be understood as literally as any other descriptive matter in human literature.\n\n> (7) **The sixth trumpet — the second woe** (Rev. 9:13-21). Two hundred million demon horsemen will be liberated from the same abyss to slay one-third of all men at a certain hour. This passage is also to be taken as literally as it reads — no special interpretation necessary.\n\n---\n\n### 4. THE MIDDLE OF THE TRIBULATION WEEK (Rev. 10:1–13:18)\n\n### (Parenthetical: the Angel, Temple of God, and the Two Witnesses, Rev. 10:1–11:13)\n\nBetween the sixth and seventh trumpets, this parenthetical passage explains certain events not revealed as the contents of the trumpets and vials, but which will happen during the same time.\n\n#### (1) The Mighty Angel (Rev. 10:1-11)\n\nThis angel is Christ, who has broken the seals on the little book of Rev. 5 and now holds it open to swear that delay in fulfilling the mystery of God will be no longer. Christ now gives John the little book to eat, signifying the reception of the rest of the Revelation to record for people.\n\nCompare this with Ezek. 2:3–3:14; Jer. 15:16; 20:7-9; Ps. 119:103. See also Point VIII, 1, (7), above, and my book, Revelation Expounded. The expression "time no longer" means "delay no longer" and refers to the casting out of Satan under the seventh trumpet, as we'll see below.\n\n#### (2) The Temple of God (Rev. 11:1-2)\n\nThis temple in Rev. 11:1-2 isn't Herod's temple — that one was destroyed in 70 A.D., about twenty-five years before Revelation was written. It also can't be the Millennial temple described in Ezek. 40-48, because that one won't be built until Christ comes to Earth (Zech. 6:12, 13).\n\nThis temple is one that will be rebuilt by the Jews before Daniel's Seventieth Week. It will be destroyed at the end of the Tribulation, either by the earthquake under the seventh vial (Rev. 16:18, 19), or by the armies of Antichrist when taking Jerusalem (Zech. 14:1-5).\n\nBoth Testaments are clear that there will be such a temple where sacrifices will be offered for at least 3½ years. Then this temple will be made desolate for 3½ years and will be polluted by the Antichrist and the Gentiles (Dan. 9:27; 12:7-12; Mt. 24:15; 2 Thess. 2:2-4).\n\nThis treading down of Jerusalem by the Gentiles for forty-two months proves that "the times of the Gentiles" won't end until Christ returns at the end of the forty-two months, as we've already proven in Lesson Fifteen.\n\n#### (3) The Two Witnesses (Rev. 11:3-13)\n\nLet's first determine what Rev. 11:3-13 says about the two witnesses before examining other Scriptures. Any theory about their identity must harmonize with the statements about them in Rev. 11.\n\nThe two witnesses are two men — not two covenants, two dispensations, or anything symbolic. This is clear from the plain description in Rev. 11:3-13 and elsewhere. Here's what we learn:\n\n> 1. They are my [Christ's] two witnesses (Rev. 11:3).\n\n> 2. They will be given power by Christ in the future (Rev. 11:3). This excludes the argument that they must be two men who have already exercised this power in past history.\n\n> 3. They will prophesy the last 3½ years. All passages referring to forty-two months, 1,260 days, and 3½ years in Daniel and Revelation always refer to the last half of\n\n---\n\nRevelation is not a book of fear — it's a book of victory. It shows the final defeat of Satan, the return of Christ, the resurrection of the dead, and the establishment of a kingdom that will never end. The chaos described in the middle is real — but it's the chaos of labor pains, not of meaningless destruction. So here's the question: When you read the news or observe the direction of the world, does Revelation function as a source of orientation for you — helping you understand where history is going — or does it remain an anxiety-producing mystery you've chosen to ignore?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk4bq001yewhjnll8ahuc	cmnknk4bp001qewhjk5bcqnfj	Lesson 42 — Heaven and the Resurrections	READING	998	7	\N	t	Most Christians, when pressed to describe heaven, produce an image built almost entirely from poetry, hymnody, and imagination — not Scripture. Floating on clouds. Endless worship services. A kind of beautiful boredom. If that's heaven, it's hard to blame people for not being especially excited about going there. But that's not the biblical description. Scripture describes a real place, a real city, real bodies, real activity — and a resurrection that is, in Paul's words, 'the hope of Israel' and the centerpiece of the entire gospel.\n\nLast time we surveyed the Book of Revelation — the final chapter of God's plan. Now we look at one of the most personally relevant truths in all of prophecy: the resurrection of the dead and the actual, physical nature of the heaven believers will inhabit.\n\n**Spiritual resurrection** is what happens when your spirit is brought to life after being dead in sin. This kind of resurrection renews you in the image and likeness of God (1 Cor. 11:7; Eph. 4:21-24; Col. 3:10; Jas. 3:9). You can see this taught in Eph. 2:1-6; 5:14; Rom. 6:11.\n\nHere's the crucial thing: spiritual resurrection only happens in this life. Once physical death comes, any chance for spiritual resurrection is gone (Eccl. 11:1; Heb. 9:27; Rev. 22:11).\n\n**Material resurrection** is the resurrection of the body—and that's what we'll focus on for the rest of this study.\n\n![Lesson 42 — Heaven and the Resurrections](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8Os7LBdYqRlSEtNozZx4rmO6UfQcdhyY2pDqwX3)\n\n## The Kinds of Material Resurrection\n\nThere are two kinds of physical resurrection. The righteous will rise to life *before* the Millennium, and the wicked will rise to damnation *after* the Millennium (Dan. 12:3; Jn. 5:28-29; Rev. 20:4-6, 11-15).\n\nThink of it like two separate court dates on a divine calendar—there will be 1,000 years between these two resurrections (Rev. 20:4-6).\n\n### 1. The Fact of a Resurrection\n\nThe following Bible characters taught it: Job (19:25-27); Isaiah (26:14-19); Daniel (12:3); David (Ps. 16; 17:15; Acts 2:31); Old Testament saints (Heb. 11:35); New Testament saints (Mt. 28; Mk. 16; Lk. 24; Jn. 20-21; Acts 2; 4; etc.); Jesus (Mt. 16:21; 22:23-31; Lk. 14:14; 20:27-36; 24:13-35); Matthew (28:1-20); Mark (16:1-18); Luke (24:1-49); John (19:21; Rev. 20:4-6); Mary (Jn. 11:24); Herod (Mt. 14:2); Peter (1 Pet. 1:3; 3:21); Paul (Acts 17:18, 32; 23:6; 24:15, 21; Rom. 1:4; 6:5; 8:34; 1 Cor. 15; Phil. 3:10-11; Heb. 6:2; 11:35).\n\n### 2. The Resurrections Foretold\n\nScripture predicted these resurrections in many places (Isa. 26:14-19; Dan. 12:3; Ps. 16:10; Mt. 12:41-42; 20:19; 27:63; 1 Thess. 4:14-17; 1 Cor. 15; Rev. 20:4-6; Jn. 5:28-29).\n\n### 3. Examples of Resurrection—Temporary and Permanent\n\n> **(1) Temporary**—raised to mortality to die again: "a widow's son" (1 Ki. 17:17-24); "the Shunammite's son" (2 Ki. 4:18-37); "a Moabite invader" (2 Ki. 13:20-21); "Jonah" (2:1-10; Mt. 12:40); "Jairus' daughter" (Mt. 9:18-26); "a widow's son of Nain" (Lk. 7:11-18); "Lazarus" (Jn. 11); "Tabitha" (Acts 9:36-43); "Eutychus" (Acts 20:6-12).\n\n> **(2) Permanent**—raised to immortality to live forever in their bodies. Christ "the firstfruits" was the first of all permanent resurrections (1 Cor. 15:1-23), then there were many Old Testament saints (Mt. 27:53).\n\n### 4. The Order of the Resurrection\n\nHere's the sequence laid out in 1 Cor. 15:20-34:\n\nChrist "the firstfruits" was the guarantee of the resurrection of all people (1 Cor. 15:20-23). The "many" Old Testament saints arose immediately after Christ did (Mt. 27:53).\n\nNext in order will be those who are "in Christ" and "are Christ's" at the rapture (1 Cor. 15:23, 51-58; 1 Thess. 4:14-17). After that will be the 144,000 Jews as the manchild of Rev. 12:5.\n\nThen comes the "great multitude" of tribulation saints and the two witnesses at the end of the Tribulation—they'll be there in time for the Marriage Supper and will return with Christ to establish His kingdom (Rev. 6:9-11; 7:9-17; 11:2-13; 15:2-4; 20:4-6).\n\nAll these groups—because they are "holy," "blessed," and "just"—will be part of the first resurrection and will help complete it (Rev. 20:4-6). The wicked dead won't be raised until 1,000 years later (Rev. 20:4-15).\n\n### 5. The Method of the Resurrection\n\nPaul illustrates the method of resurrection by pointing to the death and resurrection of a grain of wheat or some other grain (1 Cor. 15:35-50). The process grain goes through is something we can all understand, so if you apply this process to the resurrection, it becomes clear.\n\nHere are the main points:\n\n> **(1)** The fact that there is death and resurrection (1 Cor. 15:1-21, 36).\n\n> **(2)** Death must come before resurrection (Jn. 12:24; 1 Cor. 15:36).\n\n> **(3)** What is sown is not the body that will be reproduced (1 Cor. 15:37).\n\n> **(4)** In the resurrection, a body is produced just like the one sown in the ground. If the original body (if perfect) and the reproduced body were placed side by side, you couldn't detect one particle of difference between them (1 Cor. 13:12; 15:38; Lk. 24:39; Acts 1:11).\n\n> **(5)** A grain of corn won't reproduce as a grain of wheat. All flesh is not the same flesh, so in the resurrection you'll be raised with your own flesh—not that of fish or birds or anything else (1 Cor. 15:38-39). You'll be resurrected with your own size, shape, bodily appearance, and features. You won't look unlike you did in your natural body (1 Cor. 13:12).\n\n> **(6)** People will be raised to share more or less glory, or more or less damnation, according to what they've done in life. It's similar to how some grains that are poisonous and useless are reproduced only to be destroyed, while good grains are reproduced for good purposes (1 Cor. 15:40-50).\n\n> **(7)** A grain of wheat may be marred, shriveled, and parts of its body scattered here and there. Yet in reproduction, it will be reproduced as a perfect grain (under normal conditions)—regardless of where the particles were or what shape it was in at germination. The only thing necessary is that the life germ be in the grain. So in the resurrection, each body will be reproduced as a perfect body (1 Cor. 15:35-58).\n\n> **(8)** The only difference between grain and resurrected human bodies will be in the nature of existence. People will be raised to immortality—some to everlasting life and some to everlasting shame and contempt (Dan. 12:3; Jn. 5:28-29; Mt. 10:28). Both the righteous and the wicked will live again in their resurrected bodies, but only the righteous will enter into various glories and have different rewards and responsibilities of rulership in God's eternal affairs (1 Cor. 3:11-15; 15:21-23, 39-50; Rom. 14; 2 Cor. 5:10; Rev. 20:4-6). See Lesson Thirty-seven for a study of the immortality of the soul and body.\n\n### The First Resurrection\n\nThis is the resurrection of all the just, the blessed, and holy from Adam to the Millennium (Rev. 20:4-6; Dan. 12:3; Jn. 5:28-29). It covers the period from Christ's resurrection to the resurrection of the Tribulation saints and the two witnesses, including all the groups listed under Point 4 above.\n\nSeveral raptures are included in the first resurrection. All who have part in this resurrection will have some role in ruling with Christ—not just a select few, as many teach (Rev. 20:4-6).\n\nThis resurrection may be called the "out resurrection"—that is, a resurrection *out of* or *from among* the dead (Phil. 3:11-14; 1 Thess. 4:14-17; 1 Cor. 15:51-58; Lk. 14:14; 20:35-36; Heb. 11:35; Rev. 20:4-6). Picture it this way: just as Christ came out from among the dead as "the firstfruits," so at the rapture, the "dead in Christ" will come forth from among the wicked dead. Even the Tribulation saints will come out from the wicked dead (Rev. 20:4-6). The wicked dead don't live again until the thousand years are finished (Rev. 20:4-6).\n\nThe Greek expression *ek nekron*, "out of the dead," is used 49 times and teaches a select resurrection from the dead—the righteous selected from among the wicked. But it doesn't teach a select resurrection of some righteous from among the righteous (Mt. 17:9; Mk. 9:10; Lk. 24:46; Acts 3:15; 4:10; 10:41; 13:30; 17:31; Rom. 1:4; 4:24; 6:4-9; 8:11; 1 Cor. 15:12, 20-23; etc.).\n\nThe theory that only a few of the righteous will be selected from among the righteous is a mere human invention based on a few Old Testament stories that were never intended as types at all. It's not even worthy of consideration given the lack of one plain Scripture on the subject.\n\nAll the dead and living "in Christ" will be in the first resurrection—not just a few. All those who are outside of Christ will be raised together after the Millennium to be judged and cast into the Lake of Fire (Rev. 20:4-6, 11-15).\n\nThe "first resurrection" may also be called "the resurrection of the just," "resurrection of life," "a better resurrection," and the "resurrection of [from among] the dead" (Lk. 14:14; Jn. 5:28-29; 11:25; Heb. 11:35; Phil. 3:10-15; Rev. 20:4-6).\n\n### The Second Resurrection\n\nThis is the resurrection of all the wicked from Adam to the end of the Millennium (Dan. 12:3; Jn. 5:28-29; Rev. 20:4-6). It will take place after the Millennium and will also include those wicked who die during the Millennium (Rev. 20:4-6, 11-15).\n\nThey will be raised with immortal bodies to be tormented in Hell forever (Mt. 10:28; Jn. 5:28-29; 12:24; Dan. 12:3; Acts 24:15; 1 Cor. 15:21, 34-50; Rev. 14:9-12; 19:20; 20:4-6, 11-15).\n\nThe theory that only the righteous will be raised to immortality is false and isn't founded on one Scripture. The method of resurrection for the just and unjust is plainly taught and illustrated by a grain of wheat, as explained above.\n\nSo what makes the difference between the two resurrections? Doesn't a bad or poisonous seed reproduce in the same way that a good seed does? Don't both have to go through the same process?\n\nThe resurrection of people follows this same process. The only difference will be in the glory of one person over another. However, saved and unsaved will both be immortal, as Scripture plainly teaches. Not one statement was ever made to the contrary.\n\n## The Truth About Heaven\n\nWe won't take the time and space for a lengthy study about Heaven, but the following points will give you the right biblical understanding of the subject.\n\n### 1. Heaven Is a Created Place\n\nGod created Heaven (Gen. 1:1; Isa. 42:5; 45:18; Col. 1:16-18; Neh. 9:6; Ps. 102:25; Prov. 8:27).\n\n### 2. It Is a Real Planet Like the Earth\n\nHeaven is as real as the Earth (Gen. 1:1; 11:8-16). In this last passage, it's called a "better country" than the Earth.\n\nHeaven, therefore, is not an invisible nothing or a cloud floating around in space where saints sit and play a harp forever.\n\n### 3. There Are Three Heavens\n\nBesides the planet called Heaven where God dwells (Gen. 21:17; 22:11, 15; 2 Cor. 12:1-3; Deut. 26:15; 1 Ki. 8:30, 38, 43, 49), there are two other spheres called Heaven.\n\nPaul speaks of three heavens (2 Cor. 12:1-4). Here the third Heaven is called Paradise where God lives.\n\nThe other two heavens are: the clouds (Gen. 1:8; 7:23; 8:2; Job 38:9, 34; Isa. 14:14; Jer. 51:16) and the starry space between the Earth and the planet Heaven (Gen. 1:15-20; 22:17; Isa. 14:12-14; Ps. 8:3).\n\n### 4. Heaven Is the Capital of the Universe\n\nGod has His capital city—the New Jerusalem—His capitol building (the Heavenly Temple or Tabernacle), and His throne in the Temple in Heaven. John describes this city in Rev. 21-22, as we'll discuss more fully in Lessons Fifty-one and Fifty-two.\n\nThis capital city will continue to be in Heaven until the end of the Millennium, and then it will be moved from the planet Heaven to the planet Earth (Rev. 21:2, 9-21).\n\nMany Scriptures speak of God's throne being in Heaven (Ps. 103:19; Rev. 4:1-2). That God has a real temple in Heaven is clear from Rev. 4-5; 11:19; 14:17; 16:17.\n\n### 5. God Owns and Rules in the Heaven of Heavens\n\nGod owns and rules the highest Heaven (Gen. 14:19, 22; Ps. 11:4; 89:11; Lk. 10:21; 11:2). He also owns the Earth and all things in the universe, but at present this planet is in rebellion against Him.\n\nThis is why Jesus will be sent from Heaven with the armies of Heaven to seize this rebellious part of the universe and restore God's absolute rule on Earth as it was before the rebellion started (1 Cor. 15:24-28; Rev. 19:11-21; 20:1-10; Zech. 14; Jude 14; 2 Thess. 1:7-10; Joel 3; Mt. 24:29-31; 25:31-46).\n\n### 6. The Location of the Planet Heaven Is in the Northern Part of the Universe\n\nIn Isa. 14:12-14, we're told that Lucifer led a rebellion into Heaven "into the sides of the north." In Ps. 75:6-7, we're told that promotion comes not from the south, east, or west, but from the Lord—and therefore from the north.\n\n### 7. Heaven Is Inhabited\n\nHeaven is full of inhabitants (1 Ki. 22:19; 2 Chron. 18:18; Mt. 18:10; 22:30; 24:36; Rev. 12:12; 13:6). Armies and hosts of Heaven are mentioned a number of times in these passages and in Dan. 4:35; Lk. 2:13; Rev. 19:14.\n\nThese inhabitants include common angels, archangels, seraphim, cherubim, and other spirit-beings, as listed in Lesson Six. Heavenly bodies are mentioned in 1 Cor. 15:48-49. Rejoicing in Heaven by its inhabitants is referred to in Ps. 96:11; 148:1, 4; Isa. 44:23; Rev. 12:12.\n\n### 8. Paul Speaks of "The Invisible Things" in Heaven as Being Like the Visible Things on Earth\n\nThat is, the things in Heaven are just like things on Earth (Rom. 1:20). The word "things" is used in many passages referring to realities in Heaven (Phil. 2:10; Col. 1:16-20; Heb. 8:12; 9:23).\n\nWe know from Scripture that in Heaven there are:\n\n- "cities" (Rev. 21)\n- "mansions" (Jn. 14:1-3)\n- "trees" (Rev. 22:1-3)\n- "rivers" (Rev. 22:1-3)\n- "fountains of water" (Rev. 7:17)\n- "food" (Exod. 16:4; Ps. 78:25; 105:40; Jn. 6:31-51; Lk. 22:16, 18, 30; Rev. 2:7, 17; 19:1-10; 22:1-3)\n- "animals" (2 Ki. 2:11-12; 6:13-17; Zech. 1:8-11; 6:1-8; Rev. 19:11-14, 21; Rom. 1:20)\n- "furniture" (Isa. 6:1; Dan. 7:9; Heb. 8:5; 9:23; Rev. 4:2, 4; 6:17)\n- "altars" (Isa. 6:6; 6:9-11; 8:5)\n- "tongs" (Isa. 6:6)\n- "fire" and "coals" (Isa. 6:6; Rev. 8:5)\n- "censer" and "incense" (Rev. 8:4-6)\n- "smoke" (Rev. 8:4; 15:8)\n- "musical instruments" (Rev. 5:8; 14:1-5; 15:2-4)\n- "clothes" (Dan. 7:9; Rev. 1:13; 6:9-11)\n- "stones" (Rev. 2:17)\n- "books" (Rev. 3:5; 5:1-7; 10:1-11; 20:11-15)\n- "vials" or "bowls" (Rev. 5:8; 8:3; 15:7; 16:1)\n- "crowns" (Rev. 4:4; 14:14; 19:12)\n- "stones" (Josh. 10:11)\n- "treasures" (Mt. 6:20; 19:21)\n- "rainbows," "thunderings," "lightnings," "clouds" (Rev. 3-8; 10:1-7)\n- "lamps" (Rev. 4:5)\n- "a sea of glass" (Rev. 4:6; 15:2-4)\n- "singing" (Rev. 5:9; 14:1-5; 15:2-4)\n- "worship" (Rev. 4:9-11; 5:8-14; 7:9-17; 15:2-4; 19:1-10)\n- "palm leaves," so there must be palm trees (Rev. 7:9)\n- "a temple" (Rev. 7:15; 11:19; 14:17; 16:17)\n- "silence," so there must be noise at other times (Rev. 8:1)\n- "trumpets" (Rev. 8:2-6; Mt. 24:29-31; 1 Thess. 4:16)\n- "hail" (Rev. 8:7; 16:17-21; Ezek. 38:17-21)\n- "mountains" (Zech. 6:1; 8:8; 14:1; Heb. 12:23)\n- "keys" and "chains" (Rev. 9:1; 20:3; Jude 6-7)\n- "measuring sticks" (Rev. 11:1; 21:15)\n- "olive trees" and "candlesticks" (Rev. 11:4; Zech. 4)\n- "the ark of the testament" (Rev. 11:19)\n- "doors" and "posts" (Rev. 4:1; 11:19; Isa. 6:4)\n- "girdles" (Rev. 1:13; 15:6)\n- "banquets" (Rev. 19:7-10; Lk. 22:30)\n- "walls," "gates," "pearls," "diamonds," and other precious stones, as well as "gold" in abundance (Rev. 4:3, 4; 8:3; 15:7; 21:12-21)\n- "streets" (Rev. 21:21; 22:1-3)\n- "fruits" (Rev. 22:1-3)\n- and "innumerable things" (Rom. 1:20; 1 Cor. 2:9; 2 Cor. 12:4)\n\nIf the invisible things in the heavenlies are clearly seen by the things on Earth, as Paul taught in Rom. 1:20, then we have a right to believe there are in Heaven the same kind of things we have on Earth. We have no right to make an exception of any good thing that God created and intended for people to have before the Fall.\n\nSeveral natural men have gone to Heaven and have come back or will later come back. They are "Enoch" (Gen. 5:22; Heb. 11:5); "Elijah" (2 Ki. 2); "Paul" (2 Cor. 12:1-4); and "John" (Rev. 4:1).\n\nThe first two of these men are still in Heaven living a natural life and will come back as the two witnesses of Zech. 4:11-14; Rev. 11:3-13. Enoch has been in Heaven over 5,142 years and Elijah has been there over 3,500 years—and they're still eating, drinking, and enjoying life on the planet Heaven.\n\nIf we understand statements like these as literal, then the doctrine of the heavenly world will no longer be a mystery.\n\n---\n\n## Questions on Lesson Forty-two\n\n- What two main kinds of resurrections are there? Define both.\n\n- How many kinds of material resurrection are there? Name them.\n\n- Prove from Scripture the fact of a resurrection.\n\n- Give some Scriptures predicting the resurrection of the dead.\n\n- Give some examples of temporary and permanent resurrections.\n\n- Discuss the order of the resurrections.\n\n- Explain and illustrate the method of the resurrections.\n\n- Who will be included in the first resurrection?\n\n- How long does the first resurrection continue?\n\n- How many raptures are there in the first resurrection? Prove.\n\n- What is meant by the "out resurrection" of the dead?\n\n- Does the Bible teach a resurrection of some righteous from the righteous dead?\n\n- Discuss the second resurrection.\n\n- Prove that Heaven is a created planet.\n\n- How many heavens are there? Prove.\n\n- Where is God's capital located? Will it ever be moved? When?\n\n- Who owns and rules Heaven? The Earth?\n\n- Where is Heaven located? Is it inhabited? Prove.\n\n- Name as many things in Heaven as you can.\n\n- Can natural men go to Heaven? Prove.\n\n---\n\nYou will be resurrected. Your body will be raised, transformed, and glorified. You will live forever in a real place, in a real body, in unbroken relationship with God and with His people. Death is not the end — it's not even the worst thing that can happen to you. So here's the question: Does the reality of bodily resurrection and eternal life change how you hold the things of this life — your health, your possessions, your reputation, your fears? And if it doesn't, what would need to change for it to?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk461000pewhj1hbnx19r	cmnknk45y000gewhjn5pl0kw9	Supplement 7 — For Lessons 13 & 14	SUPPLEMENT	1381	8	\N	t	The Tower of Babel failed because humanity used its God-given authority to build monuments to itself. Divine healing is available because Christ bore our sicknesses in the atonement. Both truths carry the same implication: authority entrusted to you is meant to serve God's purposes, not your own ambition. That applies to the authority you exercise over your own body as much as anywhere else.\n\n![Supplement 7 — For Lessons 13 & 14](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsPrjkqQo536itGQmsLn2KwlakR9vFryYAfXZB)\n\n### I. Become Converted and Born-again\n\nThe gospel teaches that forgiveness of sins and healing of the body go hand in hand. If ministers would teach the whole gospel, people would have faith for both healing and forgiveness—and they'd receive both at the moment they get saved from sin.\n\nThe following Scriptures prove that both benefits were provided through the sacrifice at Calvary: "Himself took our infirmities, and bare our sicknesses" (Mt. 8:16-17); "Who his own self bare our sins in his own body on the tree, that we, being dead to sins, should live unto righteousness: by whose stripes ye were healed" (1 Pet. 2:24).\n\nAnd these passages show that forgiveness and healing should be received at the same time: "Who forgiveth all thine iniquities; who healeth all thy diseases" (Ps. 103:3); "For whether is easier to say, Thy sins be forgiven thee; or to say, Arise, and walk?" (Mt. 9:5); "For this people's heart is waxed gross, and their ears are dull of hearing, and their eyes they have closed; lest at any time they should see with their eyes, and hear with their ears, and should understand with their heart, and should be converted, and I should heal them" (Mt. 13:15); "Is any sick among you? Let him call for the elders of the church; and let them pray over him, anointing him with oil in the name of the Lord: And the prayer of faith shall save the sick, and the Lord shall raise him up; and if he have committed sins, they shall be forgiven him" (Jas. 5:14-16).\n\nSo you can see clearly—healing is just as easy to receive from God as forgiveness of sins. Both can be received by the same simple faith in God, asking in the name of Jesus. Healing is part of your salvation, so don't let yourself be cheated out of it any more than you'd let yourself be cheated out of forgiveness.\n\n### II. Have Faith in God for Healing\n\nIf you ask God for healing and believe it's done—the same way you're taught to ask for forgiveness—it will be done. Don't doubt for one moment that God will heal you, just as you wouldn't doubt that He forgives you.\n\nResist every doubt that you're healed, the same way you'd resist any temptation to doubt your forgiveness. Think of it like this: when you first got saved, you were taught to accept salvation by faith and count it done whether you felt it or not. You believed God forgave you regardless of your feelings—and because you believed, you were saved.\n\nIt's the same with healing. After you've prayed and asked for healing, count it done. Believe it regardless of feelings, symptoms, or anything your body seems to be telling you.\n\nIf you'll hold firm that you're healed—just as you know you're forgiven—God will confirm the healing just as He confirms forgiveness. This cannot be overemphasized: healing depends so much on standing firm that your prayer has been heard and answered.\n\nIf you want the benefits of the gospel, do what's required to get them, and you won't fail. If you're not saved or haven't followed the instructions in the previous supplements, you'll need to do so if you want results.\n\nWe can't guarantee the gospel's benefits until you meet these conditions. Are the benefits worth the effort? If they are, do as you were instructed. If they're not worth the effort to believe God without wavering, then don't be frustrated when you don't get what you want.\n\nYou can't expect God to show favoritism and give you these benefits when you fail to do what He requires. God isn't that kind of God—He won't break His laws for anyone. He knows every person can choose to meet the conditions of unwavering faith.\n\nStop babying yourself or complaining that it's hard to believe. That very attitude is sinful—you should ask God to forgive you for believing lies and Satan instead of truth and God. As long as you cooperate with Satan through unbelief, you can't cooperate with God through true faith. You can't serve two masters or hold faith and unbelief at the same time.\n\nIf you've repented of your sins and your unbelief, you have a right—a perfect right according to the gospel—to receive what the gospel promises and what Christ died to give you. So it's all-important to get rid of unbelief and have simple, child-like faith in God for healing, for material prosperity, and for anything you ask of God.\n\nFaith is required according to Mt. 21:21-22; Mk. 9:23; 11:22-24; Heb. 11:6; Jas. 1:5-8. (See Lesson Ten, Point III.) Everyone who has faith will be healed without exception.\n\n### III. Wholly Trust God in Sickness and Adversity\n\nThe Bible says, "Cursed be the man that trusteth in man, and maketh flesh his arm, and whose heart departeth from the Lord . . . he shall be like the heath in the desert . . . Blessed is the man that trusteth in the Lord, and whose hope the Lord is . . . he shall be as a tree planted by the waters . . . and shall not see when heat cometh, but her leaf shall be green [shall have health] . . . neither shall cease from yielding fruit" (Jer. 17:5-10).\n\nSee also Prov. 3:5-8; 2 Chron. 16:12-14; 2 Ki. 1:1-17; Mk. 5:25-34.\n\n### IV. Obey God and His Word\n\n"My son, forget not my law; but let thine heart keep my commandments: for length of days, and long life, and peace, shall they add to thee" (Prov. 3:1-8); "Attend to my words; incline thine ear unto my sayings . . . for they are life unto those that find them; and health to all their flesh" (Prov. 2:1-5; 3:13-24; 4:20-23); "If ye abide in me, and my words abide in you, ye shall ask what ye will, and it shall be done unto you" (Jn. 15:7; 1 Jn. 3:2-22; 5:14-15).\n\nSee also Exod. 15:26; Deut 4:40; 7:12-15; 28:1-62; 30:1-20; Ps. 105:37; 107:20.\n\n### V. Make Peace with Fellow Men\n\n"When ye stand praying, forgive, if ye have ought against any: that your Father also which is in heaven may forgive you your trespasses" (Mk. 11:22-26); "Confess your faults one to another, and pray one for another, that ye may be healed" (Jas. 5:14-16).\n\n### VI. Call Church Elders and Believe in Their Prayers\n\n"Is any sick among you? let him call for the elders of the church; and let them pray over him, anointing him with oil in the name of the Lord: and the prayer of faith shall save the sick" (Jas. 5:14-16); "They shall lay hands on the sick, and they shall recover" (Mk. 16:17-20).\n\nThousands of ministers today can testify to miraculous healings in their ministry—in the lives of people who obeyed James and called for prayer. Multiplied thousands are being healed every day by following this divine prescription. In almost every church, you'll find witnesses to the power of God to heal the body by faith in the name of Jesus Christ.\n\n### VII. Control the Tongue\n\n"The mouth of a righteous man is a well of life . . . There is that speaketh like the piercings of a sword; but the tongue of the wise is health . . . He that keepeth his mouth keepeth his life; but he that openeth wide his lips shall have destruction . . . A wholesome tongue is a tree of life . . . pleasant words are as an honeycomb, sweet to the soul, and health to the bones . . . Death and life are in the power of the tongue" (Prov. 10:11; 12:18; 13:3; 15:4; 16:24; 18:21).\n\n"What man is he that desireth life and loveth many days, that he may see good. Keep thy tongue from evil, and thy lips from speaking guile" (Ps. 34:13-14; 1 Pet. 3:10-11; Jas. 3).\n\nIt's interesting to note that nine times out of eleven plagues upon Israel, as recorded in Numbers alone, were caused by the misuse of the tongue.\n\n### VIII. Live in God and under His Protection\n\n"He that dwelleth [remains, settles down, or takes up a homestead] in the secret place of the Most High shall abide under the shadow [defense, protection] of the Almighty . . . I will say of the Lord, He is my refuge and my fortress: my God; in him will I trust. Surely he shall deliver thee from the snare of the fowler, and from the noisome pestilence. He shall cover thee with his feathers . . . his truth shall be thy shield and buckler. Thou shalt not be afraid for the terror by night; nor for the arrow that flieth by day; nor for the pestilence that walketh in darkness; nor for the destruction that wasteth at noonday. A thousand shall Fall at thy side, and ten thousand at thy right hand, but it shall not come nigh thee . . . There shall no evil befall thee, neither shall any plague come nigh thy dwelling. For he shall give his angels charge over thee, to keep thee in all thy ways. They shall bear thee up in their hands, lest thou dash thy foot against a stone. Thou shalt tread upon the lion and adder; the young lion and the dragon shalt thou trample under feet . . . He shall call upon me, and I will answer him: I will be with him in trouble; I will deliver him and honour him. With long life will I satisfy him, and shew him my salvation" (Ps. 91).\n\n### IX. Praise God in Faith for Healing\n\nDavid said, "Bless the Lord, O my soul: and all that is within me . . . forget not all his benefits: who forgiveth all thine iniquities; who healeth all thy diseases" (Ps. 103:1-5).\n\nIt's written of Abraham, "He staggered not at the promise of God through unbelief; but was strong in faith, giving glory to God; and being fully persuaded that, what he had promised, he was able also to perform. And therefore it was imputed to him for righteousness" (Rom. 4:17-22; Heb. 11:11-12).\n\nPaul said, "Be careful [anxious] for nothing; but in everything by prayer and supplication with thanksgiving let your requests be made known unto God" (Phil. 4:5-6; Heb. 13:15-16).\n\n### X. Serve God in Holiness as Earnestly as You Sought Him\n\n"As ye have therefore received Christ Jesus the Lord, so walk ye in him: rooted and built up in him, and stablished in the faith, as ye have been taught, abounding therein with thanksgiving" (Col. 2:6-7); "Sin no more, lest a worse thing come unto thee . . . Neither do I condemn thee: go, and sin no more" (Jn. 5:14; 8:11); "If our heart condemn us not, then have we confidence toward God. And whatsoever we ask, we receive of him, because we keep his commandments, and do those things that are pleasing in his sight" (1 Jn. 3:21-22; 5:14-15).\n\n### XI. Appropriate the Benefits of Calvary\n\n"Whosoever shall eat this bread, and drink this cup of the Lord, unworthily, shall be guilty of the body and blood of the Lord . . . he that eateth and drinketh unworthily, eateth and drinketh damnation to himself, not discerning [comprehending the benefits by faith of] the Lord's body. For this cause many are weak and sickly among you and many sleep" (1 Cor. 11:27-30); "What things soever ye desire, when ye pray, believe that ye receive them, and ye shall have them" (Mk. 11:24).\n\n### XII. Resist the Devil—Refuse to be Denied\n\n"Resist the devil, and he will flee from you. Draw nigh to God and he will draw nigh to you" (Jas. 4:7-8); "The devil, as a roaring lion, walketh about, seeking whom he may devour: whom resist steadfast in the faith" (1 Pet. 5:8-10).\n\nSee also Eph. 6:10-18; 2 Cor. 10:4-7.\n\n### XIII. Observe the Laws of God and of Nature\n\nYour body is God's house as well as your own. It belongs to God, not to you: "Know ye not that your body is the temple of the Holy Ghost which is in you, which ye have of God, and ye are not your own? For ye are bought with a price: therefore, glorify God in your body, and in your spirit, which are God's" (1 Cor. 6:19-20). Since it's God's house, you're forbidden to mar it, defile it, or abuse it (1 Cor. 3:16-17).\n\nCommon sense tells us that God can get more glory from a body that's well and strong—because a healthy person has complete use of all their faculties. A rugged constitution, a keen and alert mind, and unimpaired moral and spiritual faculties are necessary if you're going to fully glorify God in body and spirit.\n\nWe glorify God best by bearing much fruit (Jn. 15:8). To bear much good fruit, a tree or vine must be healthy and free from disease and things that cause faulty fruit (Mt. 3:10; 7:16-20). It's similar to how an orchard keeper knows the best fruit comes from the healthiest trees. The same is true for Christians.\n\nIf God can get some glory from lives hindered by physical handicaps, think how much more glory He could get from those same people without such handicaps! Naturally, someone with a physical limitation can still do many things—but without that limitation, they could do even more.\n\nAll governments and organizations require physical fitness. They set certain standards for their employees and make sure fitness is maintained at peak efficiency. They require that any accident or sickness be reported immediately, and every sore, illness, or cold be taken care of right away to prevent further layoff or spread to others.\n\nIn every factory and public place we see signs like "Be careful," "Watch that cold," "Don't expose your body," "Take exercise." Many companies provide their employees places for recreation and exercise. Laws in every city and state require physical fitness for marriage and certain public positions. Free clinics, medicine, and advice are provided for those who can't afford what they need to preserve their health.\n\nParents will spend everything and sacrifice all they have to make sure their children get the best physical care and protection. They'd heal their own children in a second if they could. Practically every human being would make some sacrifice to see their fellow people healed and enjoying good health. People can fight and hate each other, but when sickness or death comes, those same enemies would help each other get well or have a decent burial. Even most animals take care of their own and do everything possible to protect the life and welfare of their young.\n\nSo is God the only Being in the universe who loves to see His own children—His own creation, His own workers, those He professes to love—be sickly, puny, tired, worn out, and physically good-for-nothing? Is He the only one who loves to see pain and disease in His own creation? Is He the only one who hasn't provided for the physical welfare of His own?\n\nIs He the only one who gloats over His children's handicaps and wants them diseased so He can be "glorified" by their sickness? Is He the only one who prefers sickness to health? Who wouldn't heal His children when they get sick? Who chastens His children by causing their lungs to be eaten up by disease, their limbs to rot off, their lives to linger between life and death in the most intense suffering for years and years?\n\nIs He the only one who would make a creation only to let it end in suffering and defeat and die prematurely? Who wills these sin-cursed, disease-destroying forces to take their toll and cut people off before they finish their life's work? Is He the only one who doesn't require a high standard of physical fitness among His workers? Is He the only one whose highest will is shown through the sufferings, sicknesses, and helpless conditions of His people? Is He the only one who gets the best glory from people when they're physically, morally, and spiritually deprived of their normal functions?\n\nIf He were that kind of being, why didn't He make humanity sick from the start of creation? Why didn't He keep people sick in both Testaments instead of healing them? If sickness is God's will, why doesn't everyone have the same diseases so all could be equally in His perfect will? Is He a respecter of persons—wanting only some to be in His will through disease while others are well and therefore out of His will?\n\nWhy did Jesus die to take away our sicknesses? Why has God provided all the necessary means for healing and health? Why has He cursed those who didn't trust Him for healing?\n\nSuch ideas are not in harmony with the Bible or the character of God. It's God's highest will for everyone to "prosper and be in health, even as thy soul prospereth" (3 Jn. 2). The blame for sin and disease cannot be laid at God's door or blamed on His will. The blame falls entirely on humanity and the enemies of both God and people.\n\nInstead of meeting the conditions of the gospel to receive healing and stay healed, people continue to break the laws of God and nature, living in unbelief and rejecting God's way of healing and health in Christ. The sensible thing to do is learn the cause of the trouble, remove it, and keep it removed. Then you can be healed and stay healed.\n\n---\n\n## Ten Questions For Supplement Seven. Do You Know:\n\n- That the rapture of the Church is not a part of the second coming of Christ? The rapture is a distinct event in itself and takes place at least seven years before the second coming of Christ. The rapture happens before the Tribulation, and the second coming after the Tribulation. The rapture is when Christ comes for the saints (1 Thess. 4:13-17), and the second coming is when He returns to Earth with them (Zech. 14:1-5; Jude 14; Rev. 19:11-21). At the rapture, Christ takes the saints to Heaven (1 Thess. 3:13; 4:16; Col. 3:4), and at the second coming He leaves Heaven with them (Rev. 19:11-21). At the rapture, Christ doesn't come to the Earth (1 Thess. 4:16), but at the second coming He does (Zech. 14:4; Mt. 24:29-31). Since Christ doesn't come to the Earth at the rapture, it can't be called the second coming of Christ.\n\n- That the Bible plainly states qualifications for those who will go in the rapture? You must be "in Christ" (1 Thess. 4:16; 2 Cor. 5:17); be "Christ's" (1 Cor. 15:23; Gal. 5:24); do "good" and be "blessed and holy" (Jn. 5:28-29; Rev. 20:4-6).\n\n- That there have been and will be five raptures in the first resurrection? First, Christ and the saints resurrected after His resurrection going to Heaven (Mt. 27:52; Eph. 4:7-11; Acts 1:11); second, the rapture of those "that are Christ's at his coming" (1 Cor. 15:23; 1 Thess. 4:13-16); third, the rapture of the 144,000 Jews in the middle of Daniel's Seventieth Week (Rev. 12:5; 14:1-5; Dan. 12:1; Isa. 66:7-8); fourth, the rapture of the Tribulation saints (Rev. 7:9-17; 15:2-4; 20:4-6); fifth, the rapture of the two witnesses (Rev. 11:3-12).\n\n- That there are two main resurrections from the dead with a thousand years between them? The first resurrection is for the blessed and holy, happening before the thousand years. We read, "The rest of the dead lived not again until the thousand years were finished" (Rev. 20:4-15).\n\n- That we're living in the very last days of this age? This is proven by the fact that certain prophecies were to be fulfilled only in the last days—and since they're now being fulfilled, this proves we're in the last days. Read Mt. 24:37-38; 1 Tim. 4; 2 Tim. 3; 2 Pet. 3:1-9; Ezek. 37; Jer. 30:18-24.\n\n- That no prophecy must yet be fulfilled and no sign must yet take place before the rapture? This is proven because the early Christians were looking for the rapture at any time in their day without any particular prophecy being fulfilled, as shown in Phil. 3:20-21; Titus 2:11-13.\n\n- That all signs of the coming of the Lord point to the second advent and not to the rapture of the Church? This is proven because all signs of the Lord's coming were given in connection with the second advent, not the rapture (Mt. 24-25). Christians look for the rapture to happen at any moment without any particular sign coming to pass, as shown in Point 6 above. You can't look for an event to happen at any moment if certain other events must precede it—so associate all signs of the Lord's coming with His literal coming to Earth, not with the rapture, and everything becomes clear.\n\n- That "two in the field, one taken and another left" does not refer to the rapture? This teaching is connected with Christ's literal coming to Earth "immediately after the Tribulation," as proven by the word "Then" in Mt. 24:29-42. The statement "the flood came and took them all away" means "destroyed them all," proving that those taken away at the second advent will be those killed in the Battle of Armageddon. These will be the carcasses for the eagles to eat, as pictured in Mt. 24:27-28; Rev. 19:17-21; Ezek. 39:17-21. Those left will be those not killed in this battle—they'll be permitted to live on into the Millennium, as in Zech. 14:16-21. This will be made clear by many Scriptures in Lesson Forty-four.\n\n- That the fig tree of Mt. 24:31 does not refer to the Jewish nation? This was never Jesus' intended meaning. He was simply illustrating that just as we know summer is near when leaves appear on trees, so we'll know His coming to Earth is near when we see the signs of Mt. 24:4-26 come to pass. In Lk. 21:29 we read, "Behold the fig tree, and all the trees; when they now shoot forth, ye see and know of your own selves that summer is now nigh at hand. So likewise ye, when ye see these things come to pass, know ye that the kingdom of God is nigh at hand." If we're going to make the fig tree represent the Jews, then to be consistent we'd have to make all the other trees represent Gentile nations. If Gentiles are going to "bud forth" and be blessed like the Jews, then the old theory of Jews being blessed above Gentiles in the latter days loses its force. This isn't the thought at all, as anyone can see who takes the plain intended truth of these passages. If we want to teach something about the Jews, let's get plain Scriptures to prove it instead of using a few leaves of a fig tree as proof.\n\n- That the Bible teaches a first and second coming of God the Father to the Earth? In Dan. 7:18-22 we see that the Ancient of Days is coming to destroy the Antichrist. The Ancient of Days and the Son of man are two different persons, as proven in Dan. 7:9-14. If the Ancient of Days is not the Son of man, then He must be the Father. If He's the Father, then the Father is coming to Earth when Christ comes. This is also proven in Zech. 14:5; Titus 2:11-13. We call this the first coming of the Father to Earth to fulfill a mission. The second coming of the Father is at the end of the Millennium when the New Jerusalem—the capital city of God—comes from Heaven to Earth to be among people forever, as stated in Rev. 21:1-21. This is the second time the Father is pictured in Scripture as coming to Earth to fulfill a mission. This time He will be forever among humanity, and this planet Earth will be the site of His capital forever.\n\n---\n\nGod's authority in your life — whether over your circumstances, your body, or your sphere of influence — was given for His glory and others' benefit, not as a tool for self-advancement. So here's the question: In the areas where God has given you authority — over your health, your household, your work — are you using that authority in submission to His purposes, or are you building something with your name on it?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk48i0016ewhj981dw7gs	cmnknk48e000xewhj49ao5az0	Supplement 12 — For Lessons 23 & 24	SUPPLEMENT	867	8	\N	t	Sin is not just behavior — it's a condition. And the steps for getting free from both sin and sickness are more specific than most people have been taught. This supplement works through the practical side of what Lessons Twenty-Three and Twenty-Four established: not just what sin is and what healing provides, but how you actually walk into freedom.\n\nWe've studied the many aspects of what the Bible teaches about sin. In our last lesson, we explained how to get rid of sin and sickness.\n\nThe doctrines are clear, and the truths are laid out simply — but here's the thing: knowledge alone isn't enough to bring you the benefits you need and that God has promised. Now we need to put these lessons into personal practice.\n\nIt's time to take a personal stand with God, stand firm against these enemies of God and humanity, and step into the experiential reality of redemption.\n\n![Supplement 12 — For Lessons 23 & 24](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8Osz1qmddkWfugPJQnG59DLqiMUp7vN2m4Xbyal)\n\n## How to Identify Ourselves with Christ\n\n### 1. By Making Proper Confession of Sins\n\nThis starts with recognizing that Christ died on the cross to atone for your sins — that He actually bore them Himself. When you confess that you're a sinner and that He carried your sins on the cross, you identify yourself with Him in His work of atonement.\n\nThe result? Cleansing from all sin follows immediately (1 Jn. 1:9).\n\n### 2. By Crucifying the Old Man\n\nThe "old man," as we've seen, is the spirit and nature of the devil working in us before we're saved from sin. Even after salvation, the old man — or the devil — constantly works to regain control over us.\n\nSo we must stay on guard and refuse to give the devil any foothold (Eph. 4:27; 5:10-18; Jas. 4:7; 1 Pet. 5:8-9). Just because the devil seeks dominion and you're tempted to yield to sin doesn't mean you still have the old man in you or that you're somehow united with Satan's nature. It's only when you actually yield to sin again that you come back into harmony with the devil.\n\nBy "crucifying the old man," we simply mean recognizing that we're freed from him and refusing to obey him in any way. We must consider ourselves dead to him — and him dead to us.\n\nPaul puts it this way:\n\n> "How shall we that are dead to sin, live any longer therein? . . . Knowing this that our old man was crucified with him, that the body of sin might be destroyed, that henceforth we should not serve sin. For he that is dead is freed from sin. Now if we be dead with Christ, we believe that we shall live with him. Knowing that Christ being raised from the dead dieth no more; death hath no more dominion over him. For in that he died, he died unto sin once: but in that he liveth, he liveth unto God. Likewise reckon ye yourselves to be dead unto sin, but alive unto God through Jesus Christ our Lord. Let not sin therefore reign in your mortal body, that ye should obey it in the lusts thereof. Neither yield yourselves as instruments of unrighteousness unto sin: but yield yourselves unto God, as those that are alive from the dead, and your members as instruments of righteousness unto God. For sin shall not have dominion over you" (Rom. 6:1-14).\n\nYou must reckon — or count — yourself dead to sin and alive to God. That means you have nothing more to do with sin and everything to do with God. You're to consider sin a thing of the past, nonexistent as far as you're concerned. God is all-in-all, the only thing that truly matters in your life.\n\nThink of it like this: sometimes parents will cut off a child completely — removing them from the home and any inheritance. As far as those parents are concerned, that child is dead to them. In the same way, you must completely give up sin and count yourself dead to it. This happens by faith in Christ and by giving yourself wholly over to God and a life of holiness.\n\nThe old man and the devil's entire operation in your life must be ignored and rejected once and for all. That old life — which is nothing more than the devil working in you — must be renounced forever. We reckon ourselves new creatures in Christ, the old life dead, no longer living in the sins and lusts that destroy the soul.\n\nThe old life is past; the new life is here. The devil has no more part in you, so you count him dead (1 Jn. 5:18). The old sins are gone, counted as dead. You're alive to God now and recognize Him as your master.\n\nYes, the old life, old sins, old man, and the past still exist — but as far as you're concerned, they don't, because you are dead to them. When you served sin, you were dead to God — as far as you were concerned, He didn't exist. Now that you've turned to God and become alive to Him, you count yourself dead to sin and Satan.\n\nYou can't be alive to both. You can't serve two masters or live two different lives. When you're alive to sin, you're dead to God; when you're alive to God, you're dead to sin.\n\n### 3. By Faith in Christ\n\nThis includes faith in His name (Acts 3:16; 4:12), faith in His blood (Rom. 3:24-25), faith in His Word (Jn. 15:7; 2 Cor. 1:20), and faith in His death, burial, and resurrection (Rom. 6:1-8; 1 Cor. 15:1-19).\n\nYou must believe that what Christ died for becomes yours the moment you accept it. We identify ourselves with Him by faith in all His work, and then we receive the benefits for which He died.\n\n### 4. By Walking in the Light\n\n> "But if we walk in the light, as he is in the light, we have fellowship one with another, and the blood of Jesus Christ his Son cleanseth us from all sin" (Jn. 1:7-9).\n\nThis means that as you come to know truth, you accept it and obey it — and it sets you free (Jn. 8:31-32). It's similar to walking on a path that gradually becomes brighter — the more light you receive, the clearer your way becomes.\n\nThis is growing in grace and in the knowledge of God (2 Pet. 1:4-10; 3:18).\n\n### 5. By Taking Up Our Cross Daily to Follow Christ\n\nThis means forsaking everything the gospel requires you to give up and loving the Lord with all your heart and soul (Lk. 14:25-27, 33; Mt. 19:27-30). As you learn truth, you must conform to it daily.\n\nWhatever cross you're called to bear, you must carry it in conformity to God's will. All selfishness in obeying truth must be denied, and the gospel must be followed regardless of personal interests (Mk. 8:34-38; 10:23-31).\n\n### 6. By Walking and Living in the Spirit\n\nThis means seeking to know God's will through the Holy Spirit and the Word of God. It means always following the Spirit's leading and rejecting anything in your life that contradicts God's Word and your best spiritual interests.\n\nYou must put to death all the works of the flesh and cultivate the fruit of the Spirit (Rom. 8:1-16; Gal. 5:16-26; Col. 3:5-17; Jude 20-24; 1 Pet. 1:3-9).\n\n### 7. By Constant Prayer and Study of the Word of God\n\nIn Lesson Sixteen, we covered the need for prayer and how to get answers to your prayers. Along with prayer, there should be constant meditation in God's Word and cheerful obedience to it.\n\nThrough meditation and study, you learn the finer points of truth and God's spiritual leadings. We're required to "study" (2 Tim. 2:15), "search" (Jn. 5:39), "continue in," and "know" the truth (Jn. 8:31-36), "obey it" (Jn. 14:23-24; Acts 5:32; Rom. 1:5), and "meditate in it day and night" (Ps. 1; Josh. 1:7-8).\n\nIt's through prayer, study, and obedience to the Word that faith increases (Rom. 10:17). We're promised that if the Word abides in us and we abide in Christ, we can ask what we will and it shall be done (Jn. 15:7). This is how we learn the ways to identify ourselves with Christ — and how to actually do it.\n\n### 8. By Faithful Work for God and Consecration to Help Others\n\nWe should realize our responsibility — we're saved in order to serve. Find the most spiritual church in your community. Not necessarily the most popular one, because the popular church isn't always the most spiritual.\n\nChoose a church that upholds a genuine standard of Christianity and clean, holy living according to the gospel. Go to a church that expects its members to live free from sin and the bad habits that will destroy the soul. It will pay you great dividends — both now and in eternity — to choose wisely a church that develops your faith and encourages you in following the whole Bible.\n\nRegardless of which church you attend, cooperate with your pastor and the church's program for winning lost souls. Attend every meeting you possibly can. The Bible says:\n\n> "Not forsaking the assembling of ourselves together, as the manner of some is; but exhorting one another: and so much the more, as ye see the day approaching" (Heb. 10:25).\n\nBack your pastor in prayer and be the kind of member he can depend on. Get busy for God and use every opportunity to be a witness for Jesus — publicly or privately. Never turn down a chance to lead prayer meetings, visit the sick, help the needy, or do religious and social work.\n\n---\n\nIf you study the Bible, believe its promises, and follow the simple instructions we've given you, it will be impossible to fail God or miss the wonderful benefits He's promised. Believe in the reality of sin, sickness, Satan, demons, and God.\n\nLearn that the true source of help in life comes from God. Learn how to pray and what to pray for. Don't settle for living like many so-called Christians around you.\n\nStep ahead of the crowd. Be an example to others and never stumble over anyone. Here's the thing: if you stumble over a hypocrite, that person is actually ahead of you — otherwise you wouldn't be stumbling over them!\n\nPractice the constant presence of God. Overcome sin and bad habits. Believe that you can have healing, health, and everything God has promised. Seek God daily to experience these benefits, and your life will be blessed beyond anything you can imagine right now.\n\n---\n\n## Nine Questions For Supplement Twelve. Do You Know:\n\n- That the Bible tells us the exact day and month Abraham left Ur of the Chaldees? This was on the 15th of April, exactly 430 years to the day before the children of Israel left Egypt: "Now the sojourning of the children of Israel, who dwelt in Egypt, was four hundred and thirty years. And it came to pass at the end of the four hundred and thirty years, even the selfsame day it came to pass, that all the hosts of the Lord went out from the land of Egypt" (Exod. 12:40-41). Abraham started the sojourn 430 years to the day before Israel left Egypt, according to this passage. (See also Gen. 12:1-3.)\n\n- That men traveled through the air many centuries before the Wright brothers did? Ezekiel and Philip were both taken through the air by the Holy Spirit (Ezek. 8:3; 11:3; Acts 8:39). John and Paul were taken to Heaven and came back (2 Cor. 12:1-7; Rev. 4:1). Enoch and Elijah were taken to Heaven bodily and will come back and die at the hands of the Antichrist (Gen. 5:24; 2 Ki. 2; Heb. 11:5; Rev. 11:1-11; Zech. 4:11-14). Elijah came back in the days of Christ so he had at least two trips to Heaven, for he is there now (Mt. 17:3-4). Christ and all the Old Testament saints also were taken to Heaven as well as every person who has died in Christ (Acts 1:11; Eph. 4:7-11; 2 Cor. 5:8; Phil. 1:21-23).\n\n- That Saul was not the first king of Israel and that they had a king over 500 years before Samuel made Saul king? Moses was the first king in Israel, according to Deut. 33:4-5.\n\n- That the name of David's mother is given in the Bible? According to 2 Sam. 17:25 her name was Nahash, for there we find that Abigail and Zeruiah were daughters of Nahash. In 1 Chron. 2:13-16 it says that these women were the sisters of David. If they were David's sisters and if they were the daughters of Nahash, then David's mother's name must have been Nahash. It is argued that Nahash is a man's name. Be that as it may, Noah was a man's name but it was also a woman's name, according to Num. 27. The same could be true of the name of David's mother. Jesse was the father of David and these women mentioned, so if they were also the children of Nahash, then this must be the name of David's mother. That Nahash was a man, the stepfather of David, is unfounded and a mere theory of man.\n\n- That God did not create sin and He is not responsible for it? Sin is transgression of the law. God created creatures capable of laws and He made laws, but He did not make His creatures break the law. The ones who broke the law are the responsible ones and God holds them responsible. This has been made clear in Lesson Twenty-three, Point III, 9 and 10.\n\n- That the hinderer of lawlessness in 2 Thess. 2:7-8 refers to the Church? There are only three things in the world today that hinder lawlessness. They are the Church, the Holy Spirit and human governments. The hinderer that will be taken out of the world must refer to one of these three things. It could not refer to the Holy Spirit and governments for neither will be taken out of the world. It is clear that governments will not be taken for Antichrist will reign over the ten kingdoms during the Tribulation (Dan. 7:23-24; Rev. 17:8-17). The Holy Spirit will be here during the Tribulation, so He could not be the hinderer taken (Acts 2:16-21; Jn. 14:16; Rev. 7:14; 12:17; 19:10; Zech. 12:10). The Church will be taken out of the world (1 Thess. 4:16; Eph. 5:26-27; 1 Cor. 15:51-58), so this must be the hinderer referred to. If you cannot understand how the Church could be referred to as "he," see Eph. 2:15; 4:13 where the Church is called a "man." (See also Supplement Six, Questions 3 and 10; Supplement Seven, Questions 1-8; and Supplement Eight, Questions 1-3.)\n\n- That the mark upon Cain was not a physical mark or change of color? The Hebrew word for "mark" in Gen. 4:15 means "a pledge." The pledge was stated in the same verse, "Therefore whosoever slayeth Cain, vengeance shall be taken sevenfold." There is no statement that he was marked physically by a change of color or some other mark. (See Lesson Eleven, Point VII, 2).\n\n- That one man was healed of leprosy three different times? Moses was made a sign to Israel and to Pharaoh three times when his hand was made leprous and was healed (Exod. 4:6-7, 21, 30).\n\n- That Maher-shalal-hash-baz is not the longest name in the Bible, as is stated in all Bible statistics of men? There is another name equally as long found in the title of the 56th Psalm. (See Isa. 8:3 for the first eighteen-letter word.) Isaiah's son was called "Maher-shalal-hash-baz," meaning "haste ye, haste ye to the spoil," because Judah was soon to be spoiled by Babylon and the Jews taken into captivity.\n\n---\n\nFreedom from sin and sickness is provided in the atonement — purchased, settled, available. The only question is whether you're working the process that Scripture prescribes for receiving it. So here's the question: Is there a sin pattern or physical condition you've been carrying for months or years without seriously working through the specific biblical steps for freedom? What would change if you did?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk4bq0020ewhjcue865l3	cmnknk4bp001qewhjk5bcqnfj	Lesson 43 — The Rapture of the Church	READING	2958	9	\N	t	The Rapture is simultaneously one of the most debated and one of the most personal doctrines in prophecy. Debated because scholars disagree sharply about its timing. Personal because if it's pre-tribulational and imminent, it could happen before you finish reading this lesson. Not in the next generation. Now. That changes how you live — if you actually believe it.\n\nLast time we saw that the resurrection is bodily and that heaven is a real, physical place. Now we examine the event that begins the believer's journey there: the Rapture — the catching away of the Church before or during the Tribulation period.\n\nEven a single reading of these passages will prove that there *will* be such an event — the Lord descending from Heaven to take, in a moment of time, all the dead and living in Christ out of this world. Honestly, it's hard to see how anyone could fail to understand this if they'll just be honest and believe what's plainly written.\n\nFor example, look at 1 Thess. 4:16, 17: "For the Lord Himself shall descend from heaven with a shout, with the voice of the archangel, and with the trump of God: and the dead in Christ shall rise first: . . . Then we which are alive and remain shall be caught up together with them in the clouds, to meet the Lord in the air: and so shall we ever be with the Lord."\n\nWith language that clear, you'd have to deliberately deny what's written — or simply believe it will happen exactly as stated. There's really no room for misunderstanding here. If someone doesn't believe in the rapture, it's because they don't believe the Bible.\n\nNow, the rapture is called "the coming of the Lord," but it's never called the second coming of Christ. At the rapture, Christ doesn't appear visibly to people on Earth — He comes in the air *above* the Earth to catch up the dead and living saints, who rise together to meet Him there.\n\nThere are many different ideas about these two comings, and that's caused a lot of confusion. So many Scriptures have been misapplied to one coming or the other that you can see why people get tangled up. Here's the key distinction: The rapture is purely a New Testament doctrine and was first revealed to Paul as a special revelation (1 Cor. 15:51-58). The second coming, however, isn't just a New Testament teaching — it's one of the chief themes of the Old Testament, as we'll see in Lesson Forty-nine.\n\nThe Old Testament prophets never saw the New Testament Church, much less the rapture of the Church. So we shouldn't call the rapture "the second coming" or "second advent" of Christ — He doesn't come to the Earth at that time. And we shouldn't call it "one stage or phase" of the second coming, either. At the rapture, the Lord doesn't remain in the air during the tribulation and then complete a "second stage" by coming to Earth. No — the rapture is a distinct coming, not to the Earth, but in the air, where Christ meets the saints and takes them back to Heaven to present them blameless before God (Jn. 14:1-3; 1 Thess. 3:13; 4:16, 17).\n\nHere's where ministers sometimes confuse people. They'll say that many signs must be seen and many prophecies fulfilled before the second coming of Christ — and then, in the same breath, say He might come tonight or at any moment. How can both be true? If they wouldn't mix up the rapture with the second advent, the contradiction would disappear. The rapture can happen at any moment; the second advent cannot happen until certain events take place.\n\nThink of it like this: imagine you're expecting two different guests. One could knock on your door at any second — no warning needed. The other has told you they won't arrive until after certain things happen first. If you mix up which guest is which, you'll be thoroughly confused about what to expect and when!\n\nThese two comings simply cannot be mixed if we want to understand either one clearly. The Scriptures that apply to one don't apply to the other. Not one passage under the second advent in Lesson Forty-nine refers to the rapture, and not one passage we'll list below refers to the second coming. There's not a single Bible passage that treats both events as if they were one.\n\nThey are two distinct comings, separated by several years — not two "phases" or "stages" of the same coming. The rapture takes place several years before Christ's literal advent to the Earth, because the saints come back *with* Him at that time. The saints are in Heaven before God — not in the air — from the time of the rapture until they return with Christ to reign as kings and priests (Jude 14; Rev. 19:14; Zech. 14:5).\n\nThis seems clear from several facts: the saints are judged, given their rewards, and partake of the Marriage Supper in Heaven, not in the air (Rev. 19:1-10; 2 Cor. 5:9-10). Christ departs from Heaven at His second advent to Earth — not from the air (Rev. 19:11-21; 2 Thess. 1:7-10). The rapture must happen first — Christ must come *for* His saints (1 Thess. 4:16-17) before He can come back to Earth *with* them (Jude 14; Zech. 14:5).\n\nAt the rapture, the Lord comes from Heaven as far as the air, and the saints are caught up to meet Him there. At this event, the Lord isn't being raptured — the saints are. At the second coming, the saints aren't raptured and neither is Christ; both come back to Earth together.\n\nThe rapture takes place *before* the Tribulation; the second coming takes place *after* the Tribulation. The rapture may occur any moment, but the second coming cannot occur until all the signs in Lesson Forty-four come to pass and until certain prophecies are fulfilled.\n\nWe've separated these two comings because they *should* be separated — for the sake of clarity and because Scripture always keeps them distinct. The following points will help you distinguish the rapture from the second coming and give you a solid, scriptural understanding of this New Testament revelation.\n\n![Lesson 43 — The Rapture of the Church](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsvX8XMYFp6RzaLq0EW1HyN9UvhmSI5VtYXPn2)\n\n## I. The Fact and Manner of the Rapture\n\nYou don't need to be in the dark about this subject — it's fully and clearly revealed in many New Testament passages. Let's look at the fact and manner of the rapture as Scripture reveals it:\n\n"Watch ye therefore, and pray always, that ye may be accounted worthy to escape all these things that shall come to pass, and to stand before the Son of man" (Lk. 21:34-36).\n\n"And if I go and prepare a place for you, I will come again, and receive you unto myself; that where I am, there ye may be also" (Jn. 14:1-3).\n\n"But every man in his own order: Christ the firstfruits; afterward they that are Christ's at his coming . . . Behold, I shew you a mystery; We shall not all sleep, but we shall all be changed . . . In a moment, in the twinkling of an eye, at the last trump: for the trumpet shall sound, and the dead shall be raised incorruptible, and we shall be changed . . . For this corruptible must put on incorruption, and this mortal must put on immortality . . . So when this corruptible shall have put on incorruption, and this mortal shall have put on immortality, then shall be brought to pass the saying that is written, Death is swallowed up in victory . . . O death, where is thy sting? O grave, where is thy victory? The sting of death is sin; and the strength of sin is the law. But thanks be to God, which giveth us the victory through our Lord Jesus Christ. Therefore, my beloved brethren, be ye steadfast, unmovable, always abounding in the work of the Lord, forasmuch as ye know that your labour is not in vain in the Lord" (1 Cor. 15:23, 51-58).\n\n"That he might present it to himself a glorious Church, not having spot, or wrinkle, or any such thing; but that it should be holy and without blemish" (Eph. 5:27).\n\n"For our conversation is in heaven; from whence also we look for the Saviour, the Lord Jesus Christ: Who shall change our vile body, that it may be fashioned like unto his glorious body, according to the working whereby he is able even to subdue all things unto himself" (Phil. 3:20, 21).\n\n"But I would not have you to be ignorant, brethren, concerning them which are asleep, that ye sorrow not, even as others which have no hope. For if we believe that Jesus died and rose again, even so them also which sleep in Jesus will God bring with him. For this we say unto you by the word of the Lord, that we which are alive and remain unto the coming of the Lord shall not prevent [precede] them which are asleep. For the Lord himself shall descend from heaven with a shout, with the voice of the archangel, and with the trump of God: and the dead in Christ shall rise first: Then we which are alive and remain shall be caught up together with them in the clouds, to meet the Lord in the air: and so shall we ever be with the Lord" (1 Thess. 4:13-17).\n\n"Now we beseech you, brethren, by the coming of our Lord Jesus Christ, and by our gathering together unto him . . . For the mystery of iniquity doth already work: only he who now letteth will let, until he be taken out of the way. And then shall that Wicked be revealed whom the Lord shall consume with the spirit of his mouth, and shall destroy with the brightness of his coming" (2 Thess. 2:1, 7, 8).\n\nSee also these other passages on the rapture: 2 Cor. 5:1-8; Phil. 3:11; 1 Thess. 2:19; 3:13; 5:9, 23; Col. 3:4; Jas. 5:7, 8; 1 Jn. 2:28; 3:2; 1 Pet. 5:4.\n\nNot one of these passages refers to the second advent. We won't use any verse in Mt. 24 and 25 in connection with the rapture — there's not one verse in those chapters that refers to the rapture, as we'll see in Lesson Forty-four. We're dealing only with passages that refer to the rapture and that can be consistently explained only in that context.\n\nThere are two Greek words used in most of these rapture passages:\n\n**1. Parousia** means "personal coming or appearance" and is used of both the rapture and the revelation of Christ. At the rapture, Christ appears personally in the air to meet the saints; at the second advent, He appears personally to mankind on the Earth with His saints. This word *parousia* is generally translated "coming" — so both the rapture and the revelation are called "the coming of the Lord," but they are two different comings for two different purposes.\n\nThe word is used of the rapture in 1 Cor. 15:20-23; 1 Thess. 2:19; 3:13; 4:15; 5:23; 2 Thess. 2:1; Jas. 5:7, 8; 1 Jn. 2:28. All these passages clearly refer to the rapture, though 1 Thess. 3:13; 5:23; 2 Thess. 2:1 are used by some in connection with the second advent. But it's clear that 1 Thess. 3:13; 5:23 refer to when the Father pronounces the saints "blameless" before His throne in Heaven — *after* Christ has met the saints in the air, taken them to Heaven, and presented them "before God." So these refer only to the rapture.\n\n"The coming of our Lord Jesus Christ with all his saints" in these passages refers only to His coming back to Heaven with the saints at the rapture — not at the second advent (1 Jn. 3:1-3; Phil. 3:21; 1 Thess. 3:13; 5:23; Col. 3:4).\n\nThe last reference (2 Thess. 2:1) also refers to the rapture, as proven by the Greek word *episunagoge*, which means a complete collection or gathering of all the living and dead in Christ from all parts of the Earth, out of all denominations and dispensations, to meet Christ in the air. We are gathered "unto him" at the rapture, not at the second coming (Lk. 21:34-36; Jn. 14:1-5; 1 Cor. 15:51-58; 2 Cor. 5:1-8; 1 Thess. 4:13-18; 2 Thess. 2:6-8).\n\nIn Phil. 3:11, the rapture is called "the resurrection of the dead" — or literally, "the out-resurrection"; that is, the resurrection *from out among* the dead. The term "resurrection of the dead" is frequently used in the New Testament and includes the resurrection of both the just and the unjust (Jn. 5:29; Acts 24:19). But "out-resurrection" is used only in Phil. 3:11 and implies the resurrection of *some* — the just from the unjust (Rev. 20:4-6). The dead "rise first," then the living who are saved are "caught up together with them in the clouds, to meet the Lord in the air" (1 Thess. 4:13-18). If you keep these thoughts in mind, you can easily harmonize all the passages above — and the ones below — about the rapture.\n\n**2. Phaneros** means "to shine," "be apparent," "manifest," or "be seen," and is used in 1 Jn. 2:28; 3:2; 1 Pet. 5:4; Col. 3:4. The English translation is "appear" — meaning Christ is to appear to the saints in the air at the rapture. He won't appear to the world until His second coming (Heb. 9:28).\n\nThe rapture will include Old Testament saints and Church saints — everyone saved within the scope of redemption from Adam until the rapture, as explained above (1 Thess. 4:13-18; 2 Thess. 2:1). We're not saying the scope of redemption ends with the rapture — it's eternal, as we'll see in Lessons Forty-nine through Fifty-two.\n\nThe "trump of God" (1 Thess. 4:16) is not the same as the seventh trumpet of Rev. 11:15; 13:18. Here's how they differ:\n\n- One occurs at the rapture of the Church and the Old Testament saints (1 Thess. 4:16); the other is at the rapture of the manchild (Rev. 11:15–12:5).\n- One is the trumpet of God (1 Thess. 4:16); the other is the trumpet of the seventh trumpet angel (Rev. 11:15).\n- One heralds a single event that takes place "in the twinkling of an eye" (1 Cor. 15:51-58); the other heralds many events lasting days (Rev. 10:7).\n- One is a trumpet of blessing (1 Thess. 4:16-17); the other is a trumpet of "woe" (Rev. 8:13; 11:15; 12:12).\n- One is before the beginning of the Seventieth Week (2 Thess. 2:7-8; Dan. 9:27; Rev. 4:1); the other is in the middle of the Week (Rev. 11:15–12:6).\n- One is before the saints, represented by the twenty-four elders, are caught up in Rev. 4:1, as proven in Point VII below; the other is after the elders are already in Heaven (Rev. 11:15–12:5).\n- One is before the seven seals and first six trumpets (Rev. 6:1–9:21); the other is after them (Rev. 11:15).\n\nSo there's no need to confuse this trumpet and rapture with the seventh trumpet and the rapture of the manchild.\n\n## II. The Purpose of the Rapture\n\nThe purpose of the rapture is to resurrect the just from the dead and take all the saints out of the world *before* the Tribulation — so that God can fulfill in them the purpose for which He saved them.\n\nJesus told His disciples that some would escape the terrible things coming on the Earth in the last days. He said, "Pray that ye may be accounted worthy to escape all these things [of Mt. 24, 25; Lk. 21:1-19, 25-28] that shall come to pass, and to stand before the Son of man" (Lk. 21:34-36). This passage is practically the same in essence as Jn. 14:1-3.\n\nThese two passages (Lk. 21:34-36; Jn. 14:1-3) are the only ones in the Gospels that are clear concerning the rapture. Jesus didn't reveal this mystery in full — that was given to Paul many years later in 1 Cor. 15:51. The disciples had no idea *how* they were to escape unless they thought Christ would deliver them by His power. The "how" wasn't revealed or even mentioned before Paul explained it.\n\nNow, in the light of the mystery revealed, we can see that the rapture is what Christ had in mind when He spoke of some being worthy to escape. The Thessalonians were taught to expect the living to be taken out of the world, but some were confused about whether dead believers would share in the rapture. So Paul explained in his first epistle that both the living and the dead would be caught up to meet Christ in the air (1 Thess. 4:13-18). In this passage, the purpose is expressed: "so shall we ever be with the Lord." The rapture enables the saints to escape the Tribulation days and serve God in all eternity in whatever capacity He chooses.\n\nThis rapture before the beginning of the Week is the first in a series of raptures during the first resurrection. Besides this one, there will be:\n\n- The rapture of the manchild in the middle of the Week (Rev. 7:1-3; 12:5; 14:1-5)\n- The rapture of the great multitude of tribulation saints (Rev. 6:9-11; 7:9-17; 15:2-4; 20:4-6)\n- The rapture of the two witnesses at the end of the Week (Rev. 11:3-13)\n\nThe teaching of more than one rapture is not only stated in these passages, but is necessary to make clear what Paul meant when he said, "every man in his own order" (1 Cor. 15:20-23). The Greek word for "order" is *tagma*, and it occurs only here in the New Testament. It's used in the Septuagint (the Greek version of the Old Testament) of a body of soldiers and a whole army (Num. 2:2; 2 Sam. 23:13). It means "a company or body of individuals."\n\nFor every man to be raptured "in his own order" or company, there must be different companies of redeemed people, saved and raptured at different periods. This is proven by the following facts:\n\n> 1. **The Old Testament saints.** John the Baptist completed this company (Mt. 11:1-12; Lk. 16:16; Jn. 3:29). In these passages, both Jesus and John recognized it as separate from the Church saints. (See also Acts 7:38; Heb. 11:1-4; 12:1.)\n\n> 2. **The Church saints.** Passages like Mt. 16:18; 1 Cor. 12:27, 28; Eph. 1:20-23; 2:14-22; 4:12-16; 5:21-32; Col. 1:18, 24 prove that the Church of the New Testament did not begin before Christ's ministry on Earth — He is the head and founder of it.\n\n> 3. **The 144,000 Jews** are identified as a separate company from any others in Rev. 7:1-8; 9:4; 14:1-5. (See also Isa. 66:7-8; Dan. 12:1.)\n\n> 4. **The great multitude of tribulation saints** are proven to be a distinct company in Rev. 6:9-11; 7:9-17; 14:13; 15:2-4; 20:4-6.\n\n### The Purpose of the Rapture May be Summed up as Follows:\n\n> 1. To receive the saints to Himself (Jn. 14:1-3; Eph. 5:27; 2 Thess. 2:1).\n\n> 2. To resurrect the dead "in Christ" from among the wicked dead (1 Cor. 15:21-23, 51-58; 1 Thess. 4:13-17; Phil. 3:11, 20, 21; Rev. 20:4-6).\n\n> 3. To take the saints to Heaven where they will receive judgment for works done in the body, receive their rewards, and partake of the Marriage Supper (Jn. 14:1-3; Col. 3:4; 1 Thess. 3:13; 2 Cor. 5:10; Rev. 19:1-11).\n\n> 4. To change the bodies of saints from mortality to immortality (1 Cor. 15:21-23, 51-58; Phil. 3:20, 21; 2 Cor. 5:1-8).\n\n> 5. To present the saints before God the Father to be forever with Him (1 Thess. 3:13; 4:13-17; Eph. 5:27; 2 Thess. 2:1; Jn. 14:1-3).\n\n> 6. To make the saints "whole" in body, soul, and spirit (1 Thess. 5:23; 2 Thess. 3:13). The Greek word translated "unto" in 1 Thess. 5:23 should have been translated "at" to make the passage clear, just as it is translated in 1 Thess. 2:19; 3:13.\n\n> 7. To receive the fruit of the early and latter rain (Jas. 5:7).\n\n> 8. To cause the saints to escape the Tribulation and "all these things," and stand before the Son of man (Lk. 21:34-36; 2 Thess. 2:7, 8; Rev. 4:1; 1 Thess. 5:9).\n\n> 9. To remove the hinderer of lawlessness (2 Thess. 2:1-8).\n\n> 10. To permit the revelation of the Antichrist (2 Thess. 2:1-8).\n\n## III. Qualifications For Partakers in the Rapture\n\nWhat does it take to be part of the rapture? The qualifications are revealed in the passages we've looked at. The one and only necessary requirement — whether you're dead or alive — is to be "in Christ" (1 Thess. 4:16, 17; 2 Cor. 5:17; 1 Cor. 15:23; Gal. 5:24).\n\nThese qualifications are expressed in nine ways in Scripture. You must:\n\n> 1. Be "Christ's" (1 Cor. 15:23; Gal. 5:24).\n\n> 2. Be "in Christ" (1 Thess. 4:16, 17; 2 Cor. 5:17).\n\n> 3. Be "blessed and holy" (Rev. 20:4-6; Heb. 12:14).\n\n> 4. "Have done good" (Jn. 5:28, 29).\n\n> 5. Be in "the way, the truth, and the life" (Jn. 14:1-6).\n\n> 6. Be "worthy" (Lk. 21:34-36).\n\n> 7. Be in "the Church" or "body of Christ" (Eph. 5:27; 1 Cor. 12:13). The body of Christ and the Church are the same (Eph. 1:22, 23; Col. 1:18, 24).\n\n> 8. Purify "himself, even as he is pure" (1 Jn. 1:7; 3:2, 3; 2 Cor. 7:1; Gal. 5:16-26; Heb. 12:14).\n\n> 9. Be "without spot or wrinkle . . . and without blemish" (Eph. 5:27).\n\nIf you've met these scriptural qualifications, what more could you do? This means someone going up in the rapture is walking "in the light as he is in the light" (1 Jn. 1:7; 2:6, 9-11; 3:8-10; 5:4, 18).\n\nBeing "in Christ" means you're a "new creature: old things are passed away; behold, all things are become new. And all things are of God who hath reconciled us to himself by Jesus Christ" (2 Cor. 5:17, 18). To belong to Christ means you "have crucified the flesh (Gal. 5:19-21) with the affections and lusts" (Gal. 5:24). This means you're good, holy, blessed, and free from the sin business. What better qualifications could God require?\n\nNotice that these nine points come from Scriptures that specifically deal with the rapture. If there were other qualifications — more definite or more important — wouldn't they have been stated in those passages instead of these?\n\nWe conclude that it's not receiving other experiences (however scriptural they may be) that qualifies you for the rapture. It's maintaining a holy walk "in Christ" at the time of the rapture — or at the time of death, as the case may be.\n\nWhy should some be protected while others go through the Tribulation and be martyred? This is easily answered. At the rapture, everyone in Christ is taken up — there are no real Christians left at that moment. Those who are martyred in the Tribulation are those who refused to walk in the light and live "in Christ." They weren't ready to go at the time of the rapture; otherwise, they would have gone along with everyone else in Christ (1 Thess. 4:16-17; 1 Cor. 15:23).\n\nThey are saved *after* the rapture (Acts 2:16-21), having realized by then their mistake of not heeding the many warnings to be ready. They'll become determined to be faithful even unto death — which will be their only prospect of having a part in the first resurrection.\n\nThose who aren't martyred but are taken through the Tribulation and are on Earth at Christ's second coming will be permitted to enter the Millennium as an earthly people. They'll be part of the subjects of Christ's kingdom over whom the raptured saints will reign forever (Mt. 13:41-43; 25:34, 46; Isa. 2:1-4; 9:6-7; Lk. 1:32-35; Rev. 5:10; 11:15; Dan. 2:44-45; 7:13-14).\n\n## IV. The Time of the Rapture\n\nThe time of the rapture — like the second advent — isn't stated as to the exact day or hour. But we do know it will take place *before* the Tribulation and the revelation of the Antichrist, as we'll prove in Point VII below.\n\n## V. The Signs of the Rapture\n\nHere's something important: we can say, with full scriptural backing, that there are *no* signs of the rapture the way there are signs of the second advent. None of the signs and prophecies listed in Lesson Forty-nine as happening before Christ's second coming ever refer to the rapture.\n\nThere never was a sign that had to come to pass, nor a prophecy to be fulfilled, before the rapture. The rapture could have happened at any time in the past, and it can happen at any moment now or in the future — without any sign or prophecy needing fulfillment first. If certain prophecies had to be fulfilled beforehand, we'd be looking for those events instead of looking for the rapture.\n\nIt's possible some of the signs and prophecies might come to pass before the rapture, but that remains to be seen. We do know, by certain indications, that some prophecies are beginning to be fulfilled — showing us the second coming is very near. And if the second coming is near, the rapture is nearer, since it will take place at least seven years before the revelation (as we'll see below).\n\nThe rapture can take place at any time. This is proven by the fact that the early Christians were looking for it in their day (Phil. 3:20-21; Titus 2:13). They never said certain events had to happen first.\n\nSo all the modern theories are wrong that teach certain signs must come to pass, that certain prophecies must be fulfilled, that the Antichrist must first come, that the Tribulation must come first and be completed, or that a revival must first come to get the Church ready. All of that is completely out of harmony with the doctrine of the rapture as taught in Scripture.\n\n## VI. The Lord's Comings Are Practical Doctrines\n\nThe two comings of the Lord — the rapture and the second advent — are some of the most practical and fruitful doctrines in all of Scripture. They're real "incentives to holiness" (1 Jn. 3:1-3), "profitable in exhorting to watchfulness and faithfulness" (Mt. 24:25; 1 Jn. 2:28), "patience" (Jas. 5:7, 8), "death to self" (Col. 3:3-5), "endurance" (1 Pet. 1:7; 4:13), "holy conversation and godliness" (2 Pet. 3:11-13), "soberness and righteousness" (Titus 2:11-13), "consolation" (1 Thess. 4:13-18), "blamelessness" (1 Cor. 1:4-8), "pastoral diligence and purity" (1 Pet. 5:1-4), "sincerity" (Phil. 1:9, 10), "mildness" (Phil. 4:5, 6), and "hope" (1 Cor. 15).\n\nFor the saints, the rapture will be the beginning of eternal and ever-increasing joy and glory. For Israel and all righteous earthly nations, the second coming will be the beginning of eternal and ever-increasing blessing and favor.\n\n## VII. Reasons for the Rapture of the Church in Rev. 4:1\n\nWe believe that the Church and the Old Testament saints will be raptured in fulfillment of Rev. 4:1 — that this is foreshadowed by John's bodily ascension to Heaven, and that those raptured are represented in Heaven by the twenty-four elders throughout the remainder of Revelation. Here's the proof:\n\n### 1. A Marked Change in God's Attitude\n\nThere's a dramatic shift in God's attitude toward humanity — from mercy (Rev. 1-3) to judgment (Rev. 6-19). Revelation 4-5 pictures the raptured Church and Old Testament saints with God in Heaven *before* the Tribulation.\n\nWe know that from the Church's beginning until its rapture is a period of extended mercy without judgments from Heaven. But from the rapture until the second advent, judgments are predominant. The seals and first six trumpets (Rev. 6:1–9:21) take place in the first 3½ years of the Week (the "lesser tribulation"). The sixth seal and all seven trumpets reveal terrible judgments on Earth from Heaven, as do the seven vials during the last 3½ years (the "great tribulation").\n\nThe evil designs of the whore and the ten kings of Revised Rome, before the Antichrist gains control and destroys the whore (Rev. 17:12-17), cause the lesser tribulation, which calls forth the judgments in the sixth and seventh trumpets. The judgments themselves don't *make* the Tribulation — they're sent *because of* it.\n\nScarcely anyone believes the Church will undergo the Tribulation. Some who think the Church isn't caught up until the middle of the Week try to place the seals and trumpets in the last 3½ years — to match their view that the Church isn't here during that time.\n\nIt's true the Church won't undergo the seals and trumpet judgments. But taking them out of the first 3½ years and placing them in the last 3½ years is out of harmony with the plain, consecutive order of Revelation. These judgments happen in the order given — from the beginning to the\n\n---\n\nThe Rapture is imminent — no prophecy must be fulfilled before it can occur. It could happen at any moment. Paul expected it in his own lifetime (1 Thess. 4:17 — 'we which are alive and remain'). So here's the question you should never stop asking: If the Rapture happened in the next hour, what unfinished business would you leave behind — unsaid words, unresolved relationships, a person you kept meaning to tell about Christ? And what's stopping you from dealing with that today?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk4bq0021ewhj3i6js9ix	cmnknk4bp001qewhjk5bcqnfj	Lesson 44 — Exposition of Matthew 24 and 25	READING	3273	10	\N	t	Matthew 24 may be the most misapplied chapter in the entire Bible. It's been used to predict the end of the world in almost every generation since it was written. It's been applied to Rome's destruction in A.D. 70, to World War II, to every major political upheaval of the modern era. Most of those interpretations missed the obvious question: Who is Jesus actually talking to, and what specifically did He tell them to look for?\n\nLast time we examined the Rapture — the catching away of the Church. Now we dig into the chapter that contains Jesus's most detailed prophetic discourse: Matthew 24 and 25, where He answers His disciples' specific questions about the end of the age.\n\nHere's the interesting thing — these chapters in Mt. 24–25 are actually among the simplest to understand, yet they're also some of the most misunderstood passages in the entire Bible! They present a series of events in consecutive order, just as they're given.\n\nThe whole discourse began when the disciples proudly showed Jesus the magnificent stones of Herod's temple. His response must have stunned them: "There shall not be left one stone upon another that shall not be thrown down." This shocking statement prompted three specific questions from the disciples.\n\n![Lesson 44 — Exposition of Matthew 24 and 25](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsEfg5p7tdLWqJHyu8Q5MfmIRAjonSBKDvC0V2)\n\n## I. "Tell us when shall these things be?"\n\nThis first question (Mt. 24:3) refers directly to Jesus' statement about Jerusalem's destruction — something that was fulfilled in 70 A.D. by the Romans (Dan. 9:26; Lk. 21:20-24).\n\nYou have to understand why the disciples were so shaken by Jesus' words. The Jews had every right to be proud of their temple! It was made of snowy marble and gold, glittering brilliantly in the sunlight against the half-encircling green background of Mount Olivet.\n\nIn all his travels, no Jew had seen a city to match Jerusalem. Neither Antioch in Asia nor even Rome herself could compete with its architectural splendor. There simply has never been — in ancient or modern times — a sacred building equal to this temple, whether for location or magnificence.\n\nThe temple occupied an area of about nineteen acres and was considered one of the wonders of the world. Josephus tells us there were stones in the temple about 25 cubits (roughly 52 feet) in length, 8 cubits (about 16 feet) in height, and about 12 cubits (approximately 25 feet) in breadth.\n\nThe disciples were pointing out the nine gates overlaid with gold and silver, the one gate of solid Corinthian brass, the towering porches, the alternating blocks of red and white marble, and the vast clusters of golden grapes — each cluster as large as a man — hanging over the golden doors.\n\nYet Jesus declared that not one stone would be left upon another in the future destruction. Think about how improbable this must have seemed at the time! The world was at peace, the Jewish nation was subject to Rome and under its protection.\n\nBut within forty years, the prophecy was fulfilled to the letter. After a three-year siege by Vespasian and his son Titus, Jerusalem fell and the temple was destroyed in August, 70 A.D.\n\n## II. "What shall be the sign of thy coming?"\n\nThis second question (Mt. 24:3) is crucial to understand correctly. It does *not* concern the rapture of the Church to meet Christ in the air (1 Thess. 4:13-17). Instead, it's asking about the second coming of Christ to Earth with the saints — the event that happens *after* the rapture when He returns to establish His kingdom.\n\nThe disciples knew nothing about the rapture at this point, as we've seen in previous lessons. But they had often heard about Christ's literal advent to Earth (Mt. 18:1; 19:29 etc.).\n\nThe signs of the second advent are as follows:\n\n> 1. False messiahs before the middle of the Seventieth Week of Daniel, or setting up of the abomination of desolation (Mt. 24:4, 15; Mk. 13:5, 6; Lk. 21:8).\n\n> 2. Wars and rumors of wars (Mt. 24:6; Mk. 13:7; Lk. 21:9).\n\n> 3. Nations against nations (Mt. 24:7; Mk. 13:8; Lk. 21:10; Rev. 6:1-4).\n\n> 4. Famines (Mt. 24:7; Mk. 13:8; Lk. 21:11; Rev. 6:5-6).\n\n> 5. Pestilences (Mt. 24:7; Mk. 13:8; Lk. 21:11; Rev. 6:7-8).\n\n> 6. Earthquakes (Mt. 24:7; Mk. 13:8; Lk. 21:11; Rev. 6:12-17; 11:13; 16:17-21).\n\n> 7. Persecution of the Jews by all nations (Mt. 24:9; Mk. 13:9-11; Lk. 21:12; Joel 3; Zech. 14; Rev. 12:13-17).\n\n> 8. Many offences and betrayals (Mt. 24:10; Mk. 13:12-13; Lk. 21:16; 2 Tim. 3).\n\n> 9. False prophets before the middle of the Seventieth Week (Mt. 24:11, 15; 1 Tim. 4; 2 Tim. 3:1-8; 4:1-4).\n\n> 10. Iniquity abounding (Mt. 24:12; 1 Tim. 4; 2 Tim. 3:1-8; 4:1-4; Rev. 9:20-21; 13:1-18; 16:2, 6, 9, 11; 18:2).\n\n> 11. Love waxing cold (Mt. 24:12; 2 Tim. 3; Rev. 6:9-11; 7:14).\n\n> 12. The gospel of the kingdom preached into all the world as a witness unto all nations, not to each person of all nations (Mt. 24:13-14).\n\n> 13. The abomination of desolation set up (Mt. 24:15; Dan. 9:27; 12:7-11; Rev. 13:14-18; 2 Thess. 2:3-4).\n\n> 14. Flight of Israel into the wilderness (Mt. 24:16-20; Mk. 13:14-18; Isa. 16:1-5; 26:20-21; 63:1-5; Ezek. 20:33-44; Dan. 11:35-45; Hos. 2:14-23; Ps. 60:6-12; Rev. 12:6-17).\n\n> 15. The great tribulation days (Mt. 24:21-22, 29-31; Mk. 13:19-20; Dan. 12:1; Rev. 12:13-17; 13:1-18; 16:1–19:21).\n\n> 16. False messiahs after the middle of the Seventieth Week (Mt. 24:23-26; Mk. 13:21-22; 1 Tim. 4; 2 Tim. 3:1-8; 4:1-4; 2 Thess. 2; Rev. 13:1-10).\n\n> 17. False prophets after the middle of the Seventieth Week (Mt. 24:23-26; Mk. 13:22; 1 Tim. 4; 2 Tim. 3:1-8; 4:1-4; Rev. 13:11-18).\n\n> 18. Conditions as in the days of Noah and Lot repeated (Mt. 24:37-39; Lk. 17:22-37; 2 Tim. 3; 2 Pet. 3; Rev. 9:20-21).\n\n> 19. Fearful sights and distress on Earth (Mt. 24:4-31; Lk. 21:11, 25-28; Acts 2:16-21; 2 Tim. 3; Rev. 6:1–19:21).\n\n> 20. Great signs in the heavens and on Earth (Mt. 24:4-31; Lk. 21:11, 25-28; Acts 2:16-21; Rev. 6:12-17; 8:7–9:21; 11:1-13; 12:13-17; 13:1-18; 14:1-11; 16:1-21; 18:1-24).\n\nAll these signs — along with many others — must take place before Christ's second coming to Earth, which is exactly what the disciples were asking about. This proves the coming they inquired about was not the rapture, but the second advent. So we need to understand Mt. 24-25 in light of Christ's literal coming to Earth, not the rapture of the Church.\n\nThe first twelve signs will be fulfilled before the abomination is set up in Mt. 24:15. After listing the first six signs, Jesus said, "All these signs are the beginning of sorrows." The word "sorrows" here means "birth pangs" (Greek, *odin*), as used in 1 Thess. 5:3; Gal. 4:19, 27; Rev. 12:2.\n\nThink of it like a woman going into labor — these "birth pangs" refer to Israel's agonies under the future ten kings of Revised Rome and under the whore during the first 3½ years of the Seventieth Week. These pangs will continue throughout the rest of the Week until the whole nation is delivered by Christ at His coming (Zech. 12:10; 14:1-21; Mt. 23:37-39; 24:29-31; Rev. 19:11-21).\n\nIsrael's travail will actually begin *before* the Seventieth Week, for Antichrist will make a covenant with her to protect her for seven years — the entire Seventieth Week (Dan. 9:27).\n\n### Three Important Questions Answered\n\n**1. How do we know that Israel is the one primarily dealt with in Mt. 24–25, and not the Church?**\n\n> (1) Jesus is speaking to Jews and answering a Jewish question concerning their Messiah and His coming to deliver them from Gentile oppression (Mt. 24:34; Lk. 12:32; 22:29; Acts 1:6; Joel 3; Zech. 14).\n\n> (2) The deceptions by false messiahs primarily concern Israel (Mt. 24:5, 23-26; 1 Jn. 2:18).\n\n> (3) The seventh sign about Jewish persecution by Gentiles proves Mt. 24–25 concerns Israel. This sign is connected to the "birth pangs" by the word "then," showing the fulfillment happens at the same time as the sorrows.\n\n> (4) The seventh through twelfth signs are connected to the "birth pangs" by connecting words, showing all the first twelve signs will be fulfilled between these sorrows and the end of the Week. The first six signs mark the beginning of sorrows, so all twelve concern Israel during her travail-time at the end of the age.\n\n> (5) The fulfillment of all these signs happens during the last generation at the end of the age. Those who undergo these sufferings are blessed only if they endure to the end (Mt. 24:13-14). How could some endure to the end if they weren't living at that time? Who besides the Jews will undergo such sufferings at the end of the age? The tribulation saints couldn't be meant, because they aren't in Judea to flee like these afflicted people (Mt. 24:15, 16).\n\n> (6) "The gospel of the kingdom" is purely Jewish — it's the good news that the kingdom of Heaven is at hand and David's kingdom will soon be re-established because the King of the Jews is returning. This will be preached during the Tribulation "as a witness unto all nations," and then the end will come (Mt. 24:13, 14).\n\n> (7) The abomination of desolation concerns only Israel (Mt. 24:15; Dan. 9:24-27; 12:7-13; 2 Thess. 2:3-4; Rev. 11:1-2).\n\n> (8) The Jews fleeing from Judea when the abomination is set up in the middle of the Week proves Israel is being dealt with (Mt. 24:15-22; Rev. 12:13-17).\n\n> (9) The "sabbath day" — which would limit orthodox Jews' flight from Antichrist to about a mile — proves a Jewish connection (Mt. 24:15-22; Rev. 12:13-17).\n\n> (10) The great tribulation from the setting up of the abomination primarily concerns Israel (Mt. 24:15-22; Dan. 9:27; Jer. 30; Rev. 12:6, 14; 13:1-18).\n\n> (11) The "elect" in Mt. 24:21-26 whom Christ will fully regather when He comes after the Tribulation are Jews, as proven in Mt. 24:31; Isa. 11:10-15; Ezek. 37.\n\n> (12) Christ's coming referred to in this question (Mt. 24:3) is to deliver Israel and fulfill all prophecies of her restoration (Mt. 24:29-31; 25:31-46; Joel 3; Zech. 14).\n\n> (13) The judgment of nations at Christ's coming is based on how they treated Israel — His "brethren" — at the end of the age (Mt. 25:31-46).\n\n> (14) All the parables in Mt. 24–25 illustrate the end of the age in connection with Christ's literal advent, proving they refer primarily to Israel.\n\n> (15) In none of these passages could we insert the Church, because it's raptured "before all these things" (Lk. 21:34-36). This promise in Luke was given to believers *after* Jesus fully answered the disciples' questions, so it's separate from those answers. It shows some will escape "all these things" mentioned in the answers. Even if you believe the Church is raptured in the middle of the Week, you couldn't insert the Church in Mt. 24:15–25:46, because the middle of the Week is mentioned in Mt. 24:15 and everything afterward concerns events *after* that midpoint. This is proven by connecting words like "then" and "for then" (Mt. 24:16, 21, 23, 29-31, 40, 45; 25:1; etc.).\n\n> If the Church or Gentile Christians were being dealt with in Mt. 24–25, wouldn't you expect at least *some* plain reference to them, as there is with Israel in these fifteen points?\n\n**2. Are all the signs of Mt. 24-25 to be fulfilled in one particular generation, or throughout this whole age?**\n\nThat they're fulfilled in one generation at the end of the age is clear:\n\n> (1) This has already been proven in the fifteen points above.\n\n> (2) The parable of the fig tree (discussed below) proves this.\n\n> (3) It's definitely stated that "all these things" happen in one particular generation. "This generation shall not pass, till all these things be fulfilled" (Mt. 24:34). The phrase "this generation" appears sixteen times in the New Testament, and in every case it refers to a particular generation — not to a race of people (Mt. 11:16; 12:41-42; 23:36; 24:34; Mk. 8:12; 13:30; Lk. 7:31; 11:30, 31, 32, 50, 51; 17:25; 21:32). Compare "evil" and "adulterous" generation (Mt. 12:39, 45; 16:4; Mk. 8:38; Lk. 11:29), "faithless and perverse" generation (Mt. 17:17; Mk. 9:19; Lk. 9:41), and "untoward generation" (Acts 2:40). The word "generation" couldn't refer to a race passing away, since all people are eternal and will continue in the New Earth forever.\n\n> (4) "The days of Noah" refer to one generation only (Mt. 24:37-39; Gen. 7:1).\n\n> (5) Jesus plainly promised some will escape "all these things" — which couldn't be true if they were fulfilled throughout the age (Lk. 21:34-36).\n\n> (6) Some will "endure to the end" of the age — which couldn't be true unless those who endure are living at the end (Mt. 24:13).\n\n> (7) The abomination of desolation will be set up in the middle of the Week (Mt. 24:15; Dan. 9:27). Everything after Mt. 24:15 will be fulfilled after this. All the signs before verse 15 could easily be fulfilled in one generation.\n\n**3. If these signs are to be fulfilled during the last generation, what signs today show we're near the second advent and can look for the rapture before His literal coming?**\n\nMany signs in other Scriptures are being fulfilled today. Many prophecies prove we're in the last days and near Christ's second coming — but we're much nearer the rapture, which will itself be a sign of the second advent.\n\nThe signs in Mt. 24-25 refer to a definite period just before the age ends and *after* the rapture — namely, Daniel's Seventieth Week.\n\nHere's a summary of the answer about signs of Christ's coming: In Mt. 24:4-14 we have signs before the abomination is set up (Mt. 24:15). These, along with the signs of Mt. 24:15-26 (from the abomination to the second coming), will continue until Christ comes as pictured in Mt. 24:27-31. At that coming, the third question is answered (see Point III below).\n\nHere are signs *outside* Mt. 24-25 that prove the nearness of the second advent:\n\n> (1) **Travel and increase of knowledge** (Dan. 12:4). Under this heading you could list countless inventions proving we're living near Jesus Christ's return. While no specific invention is mentioned in Scripture, all of them can be included under this prophecy about increased knowledge.\n\n> (2) **The cry of peace and safety** (1 Thess. 5:1-9). This will be the unbelieving world deceived by Antichrist into thinking that when he conquers Jerusalem, there will be world peace — but instead, sudden destruction from Heaven will come when Christ returns (Zech. 14; Joel 3; 2 Thess. 1:7-10; Jude 14; Rev. 19:11-21).\n\n> (3) **Great deceptions and delusions** (2 Thess. 2:1-12; 1 Tim. 4:1-8; Rev. 13).\n\n> (4) **An abundance of false doctrines of demons** (1 Tim. 4:1-8; 2 Tim. 3:1-8; 4:1-4).\n\n> (5) **Many other signs** listed in Scripture: general lying, seared consciences, forbidding to marry, commanding to abstain from meats (1 Tim. 4:1-8), perilous times, lovers of self, covetous, boasters, proud, blasphemers, disobedient to parents, unthankful, unholy, without natural affection, trucebreakers, false accusers, incontinent (unrestrained in passions), fierce, despisers of good people, traitors, heady, highminded, pleasure-lovers more than God-lovers, having a form of godliness but denying its power, lustful, leading captive silly women, ever learning but never coming to truth, corrupt minds, reprobate concerning truth, destitute of faith, rejecting sound doctrine, heaping up teachers with itching ears, turning from truth to fables (2 Tim. 3:1-8; 4:1-4).\n\n> (6) **Increased labor troubles** (Jas. 5:1-8).\n\n> (7) **Scoffers mocking the doctrine of the Lord's coming** (2 Pet. 3:1-4). Anyone living today can see by all these signs — and many others — that we're in the last days before Christ's return. All other signs will be fulfilled after the rapture.\n\n> (8) **The second gathering of Israel.** The first regathering was after the Babylonian captivity (Ezra, Nehemiah). The nation scattered again in 70 A.D. (Lk. 21:20-24). Isaiah spoke of regathering "the second time" (Isa. 11:11-12; 14:1; 27:12; 43:5; 60:9; 66:20); many other prophets predicted this regathering (Jer. 30:10; 31:8; 32:37; 33:7; 46:27; 50:4; Ezek. 16:53; 20:33-40; 34:11; 36:24; 37:11-19; 39:25; Hos. 2:14; 3:5; Amos 9:13; Mic. 4:6; 5:7; 7:11; Zeph. 2:6; 3:18; 10:6-9; Mal. 3:18; Mt. 24:31).\n\nThis second regathering has been happening since 1948 when Israel became a nation again. It will continue only partially until Christ's second advent; then all Israel will be gathered from all lands to be an eternal nation under their Messiah. Angels will bring about the final regathering (Mt. 24:31).\n\nThis is really the first and greatest sign we can be definite about. There are many general signs, of course, as listed above. Some might argue we've had wars, earthquakes, famines, pestilences, persecutions, false prophets, and the rest in all past generations. So these alone might be questioned as definite signs.\n\nBut the regathering of Israel cannot be disputed as a sign of His soon return to reign on Earth!\n\n## III. "And of the end of the age?"\n\nThis "end of the age" is the same "end" mentioned in Mt. 13:37-42, 49-50; 22:13; 24:3; 25:31-46. Both the end of the age and Christ's literal advent were familiar to the disciples, but they knew nothing about the rapture at that time. So they couldn't have been asking about rapture signs, and Christ wouldn't have answered a different question than what they asked.\n\nThis third question — about what happens at the age's end — is fully answered in Mt. 24:27–25:46; Mk. 13:24-37; Lk. 21:25-33.\n\nAfter answering the second question about signs of His coming (Mt. 24:4-26), Christ explains the manner of His coming (Mt. 24:27) and what will happen when He comes (Mt. 24:28-46). Here are the events at His literal coming to Earth:\n\n> 1. Eagles will gather to the carcasses of men slain at Armageddon (Mt. 24:28; Lk. 17:34-37; Rev. 19:17, 21; Ezek. 39:17-21. Compare Job 39:27-30).\n\n> 2. The sun shall be darkened (Mt. 24:29).\n\n> 3. The moon shall not give her light (Mt. 24:29).\n\n> 4. Stars shall fall from Heaven (Mt. 24:29) — referring to a great meteoric shower, not falling planets.\n\n> 5. The powers of Heaven shall be shaken (Mt. 24:29).\n\n> 6. Then shall appear the sign of the Son of man in Heaven (Mt. 24:30).\n\n> 7. Then all Earth's tribes shall mourn (Mt. 24:30).\n\n> 8. Then they shall see the Son of man coming in clouds with power and great glory (Mt. 24:30; 2 Thess. 1:7; Jude 14).\n\n> 9. Then He shall send His angels with a great trumpet sound to gather His elect from Earth's four quarters (Mt. 24:31; Isa. 11:10-15).\n\n> 10. The ungodly will be destroyed (Mt. 24:32-42; 2 Thess. 1:7; Jude 14).\n\n> 11. Unfaithful servants will be judged and punished in eternal Hell (Mt. 24:43-51; 25:14-30; Mt. 13:30, 40-43).\n\n> 12. The nations will be judged (Mt. 25:31-46).\n\nSo you can see the Lord fully answered in Mt. 24:27–25:46 the third question about what will happen at the age's end.\n\n## IV. Prophetic Date-Setting (Mt. 24:36–25:46)\n\nThese verses definitely state and illustrate that no one will know the day or hour of Christ's second coming to Earth. All we may know is "the times and seasons" that prove the second advent is near (1 Thess. 5:1-3).\n\nTo keep His disciples from speculating about the exact timing, Christ compared the days of Noah to the days just before His coming. He stated that people before the flood "knew not until the flood came and took them all away; so shall also the coming of the Son of man be" (Mt. 24:37-39; 1 Thess. 5:1-3; Jude 14).\n\nCertain religious groups have spent much time setting definite dates for Christ's second coming, the rapture, and other prophetic events. These dates are all based on the fallacy that a day in prophecy means a year and vice versa. This has led some to predict the second advent at dates now in the past.\n\nWilliam Miller set one date for October 22, 1843, based on the 2,300 days of Dan. 8:13. His followers put on special ascension robes expecting to be caught up to meet the Lord. Nothing happened.\n\nInstead of giving up, Miller explained he'd miscalculated by a year and reset the date for October 22, 1844. The excitement was even greater the second time. One Advent message declared, "There is no possibility of a mistake this time."\n\nBut the second date passed too, and the results were heartbreaking. Many followers drifted into other groups or abandoned faith entirely. Those remaining tried to explain — Elder White even set the date up another year, and still Christ didn't come.\n\nIf all Miller's followers had been as honest as he was, the movement might have ended. But Mrs. Ellen G. White proclaimed that God "was in the proclamation of 1843 and His hand hid a mistake in some of the figures." This made it seem God had blundered along with Miller, but it was satisfactorily explained to the faithful. Christ was supposedly examining sins in the heavenly sanctuary.\n\nIn 1849, Mrs. White claimed to see the exact hour Christ would come and said His heavenly work was "nearly finished." Later it was revealed He would continue there until the seventh thousand years from Adam. But according to calculations, we're already over 143 years into the seventh thousand years since Adam. Christ supposedly entered the heavenly sanctuary in 1844, but Scripture says He entered there over 1,900 years ago (Heb. 8:1-2; Acts 1:11; 2:35; Rom. 8:34; Eph. 1:19-23; Col. 3:1; Heb. 1:3; 12:2; 1 Pet. 3:22; Rev. 3:21).\n\nMiller himself later wrote: "We expected the second coming of Christ at the time and now to contend that we were not mistaken is dishonest. I have no confidence in any of the new theories that grew out of the movement."\n\nAdventists explain the 2,300 days of Dan. 8:14 as 2,300 years from 457 B.C. through 1844 A.D. They explain the 1,260 days of Rev. 11:1-3; 12:6 as 1,260 years from 538 A.D. to 1798 A.D. But we've already seen that the 1,260 days are literal days relating to the future Tribulation when Antichrist reigns. The beast of Revelation is a man (Rev. 13:18), not the Catholic Church, and will be destroyed at Christ's second coming (Rev. 19:11-21; Dan. 7:11; 2 Thess. 2:7, 8).\n\nThe vision of Dan. 8 was to be fulfilled mainly at the time of the end (Dan. 8:17, 23-26). The Grecian Empire wasn't even formed by 457 B.C., much less its four kingdoms. The little horn was to come "in the latter time of their kingdom." The four divisions of Greece didn't exist until about 323-301 B.C. Since the little horn hasn't yet come, the 2,300 days are still future.\n\nThe 2,300 days are literally 2,300 evenings and mornings — 1,150 evenings and 1,150 mornings — referring to the morning and evening sacrifices in Jerusalem's temple. So the 2,300 days are only three years, two months, and ten days (using a 360-day year). This is clear because the prophecy concerns "the evening and the morning" (Dan. 8:26) and "the daily sacrifice" (Dan. 8:11-13), offered every morning and evening (Num. 28:3-4; 1 Chron. 16:40).\n\nThe 2,300 days refer to sacrifices that will be stopped in the future Jewish temple by Antichrist when he breaks his seven-year covenant with Israel and sets himself up as God in their temple (Dan. 9:27; Mt. 24:15-31; 2 Thess. 2:1-8; Rev. 13:1-18).\n\nThe end of these three years, two months, and ten days falls 110 days short of the 1,260-day great tribulation in Daniel's Seventieth Week's last half. This harmonizes perfectly with prophecy that Antichrist loses Jerusalem's control in those final days while warring against northern countries (Dan. 11:44).\n\nIt's similar to a military campaign where the general is off fighting distant battles and loses grip on home territory. When Antichrist returns from conquering the north and east, he must retake Jerusalem from the Jews who've come out of hiding in Edom and Moab. They evidently reclaim the city and cleanse the temple of beast worship during those 110 days. Antichrist conquers half of Jerusalem before Christ suddenly appears to defeat the nations and establish His kingdom (Zech. 14:1-21; Rev. 19:11-21).\n\nPastor Russell was another date-setter. According to him, the Millennium began in 1872 A.D. and "the times of the Gentiles" ended in 1914. Michael Baxter was another — according to him, the seals, trumpets, and vials of Revelation were fulfilled from 1907 to 1931 A.D.\n\nThis date-setting practice has been followed by many Bible students, all proven wrong. It's time for people to use better judgment than to set definite dates for specific events.\n\n**Setting dates is unscriptural.** No one can know when these events will happen until Antichrist comes and makes his seven-year covenant with Israel. Then, and only then, can we know the timeline.\n\nWhen Antichrist comes, everyone will know only seven years remain until the second advent. They can then know Rev. 6-19 will be fulfilled consecutively from the beginning to the end of Daniel's Seventieth Week, ending with Christ's second advent and the Millennium's beginning.\n\n## V. Rapture or Destruction—Which? (Mt. 24:40-42)\n\nThese verses are some of the most misunderstood in Scripture because many interpret them as referring to the rapture instead of the second advent. But it's clear from context that the rapture isn't referenced at all in Mt. 24-25.\n\nSo regardless of how much these verses *sound* like the rapture, they don't refer to it. They refer to Christ's literal coming to destroy the ungodly — similar to the flood — as made clear by the word "then."\n\n"Then [at the coming of Christ with the saints to end wickedness as did the flood] shall two be in the field; the one shall be taken and the other left . . . Watch therefore; for ye know not what hour your Lord doth come."\n\nWhy take these verses out of their proper context — Christ's coming *with* the saints — and make them refer to His coming *for* the saints? We have plenty of Scriptures proving the rapture without using this one. So why base a doctrine on a passage that doesn't concern it?\n\nIf these verses refer to Christ's literal coming, what do they mean? Where are the people who are "taken"?\n\nThe answer is in Lk. 17:34-37 — a parallel passage showing both refer to Christ's coming to Earth, not the rapture. Luke's verses conclude a discourse about "the day\n\n---\n\nMatthew 24-25 is primarily addressed to the Jewish remnant who will go through the Tribulation — not to the Church, which will have been raptured. But the principles of watchfulness, faithfulness, and readiness apply to every generation. So here's the question: Jesus said no one knows the day or the hour — but He rebuked the Pharisees for not recognizing the signs of the times. Are you watching? Not obsessively, not with anxiety, but with the sober awareness of someone who knows history is moving toward a fixed conclusion and that they have a role to play before it arrives?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk461000sewhjt1c55h1z	cmnknk45y000gewhjn5pl0kw9	Supplement 8 — For Lessons 15 & 16	SUPPLEMENT	1762	11	\N	t	God made an unconditional covenant with Abraham — one that bound only God. And Lesson Sixteen showed that the conditions for asking and receiving are clear and consistent. This supplement explores the intersection: What has God unconditionally promised, and what conditions must you meet to access that promise? The two aren't in conflict. They're the two sides of how covenant actually works.\n\nIn Lesson Fifteen, we saw how God began revealing more details about His plan during the time of Abraham. He started showing us more clearly who the promised "seed of the woman" would be and why He would come into the world.\n\nGod chose Abraham's descendants to be His representatives on Earth. Their job was to demonstrate God's will among all people — to show everyone that God loved them and wanted to bless them with holiness, health, wealth, and happiness. These blessings could never be fully enjoyed apart from God.\n\nGod also wanted to show that He had a wonderful future planned for everyone who would follow His ways. During this time — what we call the Dispensation of Promise — God began requiring something specific: absolute, unwavering faith in His plan.\n\nPeople were expected to believe that God wanted to remove want, sickness, and misery from the Earth. They were to trust that He would restore humanity to the state we enjoyed before the Fall. This approach to dealing with humanity made it possible for any individual who stayed true to God to receive everything He freely promised.\n\nHere's the beautiful part: faith still works the same way today. Even if everyone else in the world refused to believe God and follow His plan, one person who does believe can still receive all the benefits. This makes God perfectly fair to everyone and allows each person to reach what the majority might miss.\n\n![Supplement 8 — For Lessons 15 & 16](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsZ8N3xSdWQAzP5eOGYXjM0itdfBSUE9IN6u8o)\n\n## Unlimited Power in Prayer\n\nAs God's plan unfolds through these lessons, we can clearly see that the possibilities in prayer are unlimited. Lesson Sixteen showed us there are no limits to what we can receive from God according to His promises. There's no benefit God has promised that we can't obtain.\n\nWith this knowledge of God's will and the unlimited power of prayer, there's really no excuse for all the sin, sickness, disease, failure, poverty, and unhappiness in the world. It's honestly hard to understand why God's children settle for so much less than what's available to them when they could have abundance.\n\nYes, many have been taught wrong or limited ideas about God's grace. But that excuse doesn't work anymore — we're giving you the true light of God's Word in these lessons and supplements. From here on, it's not about ignorance or bad teaching. It will simply be a matter of willfully rejecting plain truth and refusing to put in the effort needed to receive these benefits.\n\nIn our previous lessons, we've given you plenty of scriptural proof that there's no limitation for believers in prayer. You can attain everything you want in this life and the life to come. Lesson Ten proved God's plan for meeting our needs. Lesson Twelve showed why that plan often goes unrealized. And Lesson Sixteen demonstrated how to attain the fullness of life simply by asking and receiving according to clear laws God has revealed in Scripture.\n\nWith all these wonderful truths plus the many helps throughout the lessons and supplements, you should be receiving God's abundance right here and now.\n\n## Inventory of Your Personal Progress\n\nLet's have a heart-to-heart talk about your progress. After all, it's your personal gain in life that you're interested in. That's why you wanted to study these lessons. That's why you want to continue through the whole series until you have the complete truth that will help you live in God's abundance.\n\nLet me ask you: what benefits have you actually received from these lessons? Take some time to review everything — the lessons and supplements — to see what you've gained in knowledge, wisdom, healing, prosperity, answers to prayer, and whatever you personally want and need in life.\n\nHave you faithfully followed the instructions from the very first lesson until now? Have you practiced the lessons on prayer and consecration to God's will? Have you surrendered your life to God? Are you really born-again? Have you given up the sin-business?\n\nHas your fulfillment of needs and wants been worth giving up sin for? Or have you been trying to attain God's benefits without actually becoming His child — benefits that are promised only to those who obey Him?\n\nDo you have a simple understanding of God's plan so you can follow His will? Have you conformed to the plan as you've learned it? Do you have faith in God, or are you still battling demon powers because of doubt and unbelief?\n\nOnly you can answer these questions. No one else can answer them for you except God. It's absolutely necessary that you conscientiously answer these questions before God and check up on yourself to see how you're progressing.\n\nIf you're making good progress, it's because you've obeyed the simple instructions to the letter. If you're not progressing as fast as you wish — if your needs for body, soul, and spirit aren't being supplied — it's because you've failed on some point. You're the one who needs to discover where you're failing. You're the one who needs to concentrate on those weak points. Even if you have to start all over again, the rich dividends will make it worth it.\n\nHere's what I suggest: make an outline of the necessary steps you need to follow. Write down the main points from the first lesson and supplement through Supplement Eight and see if you fully understand everything you're supposed to do. Check off the points you've been faithful to practice daily. Also check off the points where you've failed. Then begin to practice all the truth, not just part of it.\n\nIf after this you're still not getting the benefits you're entitled to, go to God about it. Quote the promises that cover your needs and wants. Seek Him about the failure. Make sure you haven't been doubting God while practicing these principles.\n\nIf you've been wondering or questioning why you haven't made better progress, that very wondering shows you've been doubting. And if you doubt, that doubt itself will defeat you. As long as you question and wonder why your prayers aren't being answered, you'll stay defeated.\n\nDon't mistake this! Don't let anyone deceive you! God's Word is clear on every detail. You can't miss the truth — it's too plainly written to leave anyone in ignorance.\n\nScripture is direct: "he that doubteth is damned if he eat, because he eateth not in faith: for whatsoever is not of faith is sin" (Rom. 14:23). And James tells us, "let him ask of God, that giveth to all men liberally, and upbraideth not; and it shall be given him. But let him ask in faith, nothing wavering. For he that wavereth is like a wave of the sea driven with the wind and tossed. For let not that man think that he shall receive any thing of the Lord" (Jas. 1:5-8).\n\nYour duty couldn't be made any clearer when it comes to getting answers to prayer. If you have sin in your life, no wonder you waver. If you don't have sin in your life, you have no business wavering — so quit it here and now.\n\nMake up your mind that you're done with wavering. Simply refuse to make God a liar anymore. It doesn't matter how hard evil spirits press you to doubt God and get discouraged — you don't have to yield to them even slightly. You must stubbornly refuse to doubt what God has said and resist these satanic forces.\n\nJas. 4:7 says, "Resist the devil, and he will flee from you." You couldn't bring yourself to believe that's a lie, could you? Would you dare say you've resisted and the devil hasn't fled? Would you dare say the Bible isn't true? No! You can't afford to say that if you want things from God.\n\nYou might wonder what to do next after you've done all this. The answer is simple: keep doing it until victory comes. And it will come. Never doubt this fact. God is true. The devil is a liar. You can believe God more than you can believe the devil.\n\nIf you're having a struggle with unbelief, that very struggle proves you have unbelief — otherwise you wouldn't be having any trouble. As long as unbelief remains, the fight must continue until you firmly and positively refuse to waver again.\n\nLet it be settled once and forever: you will not doubt. Since you know you won't get what you've prayed for as long as you doubt, it should be easy to cast all doubt aside and resist the very unbelief that causes failure.\n\nThink about it this way: you know you won't get the answer as long as you doubt. You also know there's a definite assurance you will get it if you won't doubt. So why be foolish and give in to unbelief? Even if there were the slightest possibility of not getting the answer even with faith, you'd still be better off not doubting — because doubt brings certain defeat. You certainly can't lose any more by having unwavering faith than by having doubt. So why not wholeheartedly cooperate with God and have faith in Him?\n\nJesus gave absolute assurance that you will receive if you have faith and don't doubt. He said, "If ye have faith, and doubt not, ye shall not only do this which is done to the fig tree, but also if ye shall say unto this mountain, Be thou removed, and be thou cast into the sea; It shall be done. And all things, whatsoever ye shall ask in prayer, believing, ye shall receive" (Mt. 21:21-22; Mk. 11:22-24; Jn. 15:7, 16).\n\nWhy should you be afraid to step out on God's promises? They will not fail. "All the promises of God in him are yea, and in him Amen, unto the glory of God by us" (2 Cor. 1:20). Being afraid to trust God and His Word shouldn't be tolerated in a Christian's life.\n\nThink of it like this: there was once a man crossing a frozen river. He was so afraid the ice might break that he crawled on his hands and knees. When he got out to the middle of the river, he heard whistling behind him. He looked around and saw a boy with a wagon full of corn pulled by a pair of husky mules, coming across the river like it was solid ground. The boy was carefree, with absolute confidence that the ice would hold the heavy load. But the poor man was afraid the ice would break under his own weight, so he crawled across in fear.\n\nGod's promises will hold you up. They will not fail. You'll see in due time that God means exactly what He says. One of the greatest surprises of Heaven will be seeing how abundantly God had provided for us in this life, even though we failed to receive those benefits.\n\nLet's make up our minds never to waver or limit God and His abundant promises for our needs in this life. God will not rebuke you for daring to ask anything He has promised. "Let him ask of God, that giveth to all men liberally, and upbraideth not; and it shall be given him" (Jas. 1:4-8).\n\nIf you're not progressing as fast as you'd like, get busy — but don't get discouraged. You're the one who's been hindering your own progress. You can progress as fast as you want. Read the promises over and over again until faith is generated in you enough to say, "It shall be done," "I do believe God," "I do believe He has heard my prayer and I have the answer."\n\nGod is more anxious for every one of His children to believe than any of them is to receive. God loves each one and deals with each one personally according to their faith. On this basis God deals with everyone. Every single person can get what they want and make the progress in God they desire.\n\nYou should be personally satisfied with your progress because you're the one solely responsible for it. There should be no complaining. If you want to develop in the business of asking and receiving, do what everyone has to do to succeed in this kind of business. God is no respecter of persons. He doesn't try to hold one back and push another forward. He doesn't will that any of His children should be defeated in any area of life.\n\n## Partnership With God in Business\n\nWe've seen above that there's unlimited power in prayer. A businessman needs God in his business to have guaranteed success. He shouldn't be ashamed to take God as a partner and let that be known. He should honor God in this respect and give Him credit for his success.\n\nHe should take God into consideration and pray about every phase of his life and work. He should be just as incurably religious and prayerful about his business as any preacher, and he's bound to succeed.\n\nAll men pray, even if some are ashamed of it. Even sinners and infidels find themselves praying to God when calamity strikes or when they're scared. They do it naturally under such circumstances — they don't have to be taught. They simply pray from the heart during those times, without regard to whether it will help or not. They must pray, proving that prayer is natural.\n\nBecause prayer is natural, let's all live a natural, normal life and not be ashamed of it. It should be considered the greatest honor to pray to the Supreme Sovereign of the Universe.\n\nThe businessman above many others should openly pray to God for help with his many problems. He should learn to let God run his business by following God's will in every move he makes. God is interested in every man's success and will help everyone succeed in life if He's consulted and obeyed.\n\nGod can run your business better than you can run it yourself. He knows the details of the business world and all the problems businessmen face. He can supernaturally work and accomplish things where all human efforts fail.\n\nIt doesn't matter what business you're in. If it's legitimate, you shouldn't feel it would be wrong to ask God to help you and lead you into unknown ways of making money and helping others through your work.\n\nIf you want to succeed in business, you must not only make God your partner, but also be willing to follow the laws of prosperity as discussed in Supplement Six. No one can possibly fail in business by following these suggestions. With God as a partner and by following the laws of prosperity, success in any kind of business is inevitable.\n\nGod can help the businessman by changing his life and conduct so he becomes capable of success. God can help by directing him into the proper channels and to the sources of help he needs. God can help by moving on people's hearts at every moment help is needed — to carry through some transaction during a crisis and bring success.\n\nPartnership with God is indispensable to any businessman and any Christian in any walk of life. The person who fails to seek God's help in every endeavor misses many blessings that partnership with God would bring. If you take God into your life and acknowledge Him in all things, you will be a success.\n\nProblems that seem impossible to solve — situations where you can't see any way out — if God is your partner, you can simply turn them over to Him, and He will make a way. Things can work out so easily and quickly when God acts that you'll be amazed how problems get solved.\n\nHow anyone can get along without God in their business is hard to understand. Another thing some find hard to understand is how godless people prosper in business without God. But this is easily explained when we consider that following the laws of prosperity will cause both sinners and saints to prosper materially.\n\nIf sinners can prosper by these laws without God, how much more will a Christian prosper with God as a personal partner? If Christians could see this, they wouldn't wonder at the prosperity of the wicked. If all businessmen could see that prosperity with God is greater than without Him, many would turn to God for supernatural guidance in business.\n\n## Tapping the Infinite Resources of God\n\nDwight L. Moody once said, "The world has yet to see what God will do in and for and with and through the man that is wholly yielded to Him." How true this is! The infinite resources of God have never been fully explored.\n\nThe average person has such a limited concept of God and His infinite love and provision for all creation that God Himself is limited in manifesting His great love to them. When we limit God, we naturally limit His blessings to ourselves. If people could wake up and see for themselves the abundance of God's provision, they would get so much more from Him.\n\nAs long as people argue that it's not God's will to do this or that, or that He cannot or will not do certain things, they'll come far short of God's blessings. It seems to me that just one reading of the Bible would be enough to correct most of the wrong ideas people have about God.\n\nThe purpose of these lessons is to help such misled people see for themselves what God's will is and what they're missing of the good things in life. Everyone should read the Bible for themselves and see what it plainly says on all these questions. Anyone who reads for themselves can see where they've been misled. The Bible says one thing, and what men teach contrary to it is easily detected.\n\nYou who are taking these lessons have no excuse, for your eyes are being opened to see the infinite blessings God has promised to those who love Him. You have no possible excuse to continue in unbelief and ignorance of what God wants you to have.\n\nGod has literally promised material wealth, physical health, and spiritual freedom from sin as well as from evil habits and failure in life. There's not one phase of life that God has ignored in His program. There is no want to those who fear God. There can be no failure for anyone who will tap God's resources. It's the duty of every believer to learn what God has promised and then be bold and aggressive in attaining the benefits provided.\n\nEnter wholeheartedly into fellowship with God. Love and obey Him, and you'll see for yourself that "No good thing will he withhold from them that walk uprightly" (Ps. 84:11).\n\nYou have before you sickness or health, poverty or wealth, failure or success. You have before you the infinite resources of God, and you can have what you want in life that is good. So forget the many false concepts of God and truth you've been taught and lay hold of the abundance of life. You can have anything you want by simply asking and receiving from your Heavenly Father.\n\n## Ten Questions For Supplement Eight. Do You Know:\n\n- **That the bride of Christ is a city and not the Church?** In Rev. 21:2, 9-10, it's stated as simply as possible that the bride of Christ is the heavenly city, the New Jerusalem. The bride then is not the Church or a special part of the Church. Neither is the bride Israel or a special company of Jews such as the 144,000 of Rev. 7 and 14. If the bride is a city, that should be accepted without question. In no place is the Church called the bride, a virgin, a lady, a woman, or referred to by feminine pronouns. On the contrary, the Church is called a "man" because it is the body of Christ, who is a man (Eph. 2:15; 4:13).\n\n- **That all saints of all ages will be a part of the bride of Christ?** If the bride of Christ is a city, then naturally all who will live in this city will be part of the bride. According to Heb. 11:8-16, the Old Testament saints are going to live in that city, so they'll be part of the bride. According to Heb. 13:14, present Church saints are going to live in the same city, so they'll also be part of the bride. The tribulation saints and the 144,000 Jews of Rev. 7 and 14 will also have part in the resurrection and live in this city, so these two companies will be part of the bride as well (Rev. 7:1-17; 14:1-5; 15:2-4; 20:4-6). Therefore, every company of redeemed who will live in the New Jerusalem will be part of the bride of Christ. Just as no single individual or group, or no single building or group of buildings that make up a city could be called that city, so no individual, group, or building in the New Jerusalem could be called the bride of Christ. All the resurrected redeemed saints of all ages and all the other inhabitants and buildings of the New Jerusalem will make the final bride of Christ, as we'll see in Lesson Fifty-two.\n\n- **That there is a difference between the kingdom of Heaven and kingdom of God?** The kingdom of Heaven in this age is the realm of profession as taught by the parables, wherein the tares and wheat and the good and bad are in the kingdom of Heaven. The kingdom of God is the realm of God in the vast universe. The kingdom of Heaven is only a part of the universal kingdom of God, as we'll see in Lesson Thirty-one.\n\n- **That the Church is not the kingdom of Heaven or kingdom of God?** The Church is the wheat-part of the kingdom of Heaven and only a part of the true servants of the universal kingdom of God. In other words, the tares in the kingdom of Heaven and the angels and other subjects of God in the kingdom of God are not part of the Church, as we'll see in future studies.\n\n- **That there will never be a revived Roman Empire, as taught by many Bible students?** The common theory is that the Roman Empire will be revived in the last days and that Antichrist will rule all this empire from Rome. But this isn't taught anywhere in Scripture. According to Dan. 7:23-24, there will never be a revival of the Roman Empire — which would mean one vast empire formed from the twenty-two states now inside the territory of the old Roman Empire. Instead of one empire ruled by one man from Rome, as in the days of the Caesars, there will be ten kingdoms ruled by ten kings from ten separate capitals inside this territory. In other words, ten kingdoms will be formed instead of one empire. There will be ten rulers instead of one. These ten are the same ones symbolized by the ten toes on the image of Dan. 2:31-45 and the ten horns on the beast of Rev. 17:8-17. That they'll exist at the second advent of Christ is clear from Rev. 17:14, where they fight against Christ the day He comes. This same truth is taught in Dan. 2:44-45, which speaks of the God of Heaven setting up His kingdom on Earth during the days of the ten kings.\n\n- **That Antichrist cannot be any man now prominent in world affairs?** This is clear from the fact that the "little horn," the coming Antichrist, cannot come until after the ten kingdoms are formed inside the territory of the Roman Empire. If the "little horn" represents the Antichrist coming after the ten kingdoms are formed and will come from among them, as plainly stated in Dan. 7:7-8, 23-24, then he cannot come until those kingdoms are formed. Therefore, he cannot be any man in world affairs until after they're fully formed. His coming, according to 2 Thess. 2:7-8, is after the rapture of the Church, as we'll see in Lesson Forty-three.\n\n- **That the Antichrist will be a future mortal man and not some supernatural resurrected man or some succession of popes, as taught by many?** That he'll be a man is clear from Rev. 13:18: "it is the number of a man; and his number is six hundred three-score and six." That he'll be mortal is clear from the fact that he'll be "slain" by Christ at His second advent (Dan. 7:11; Isa. 11:4; 2 Thess. 2:7-8). If Christ is going to slay him at the second advent, he must be mortal at that time. This proves he won't be a resurrected immortal man from the dead.\n\n- **That Antichrist cannot be Judas or any man that has ever lived on Earth before?** As we've seen above, he'll be a mere mortal man at the second advent of Christ, so he couldn't be a resurrected immortal man — which he would have to be if he were Judas or some other historical man coming back from death. There's no statement anywhere in Scripture that says he must be some historical man from the dead. This is mere supposition from prophetic sensationalists who try to get more from the Bible than it says. That both Judas and Antichrist are called sons of perdition proves nothing — this term simply means "sons of destruction" because both are destined to destruction. It's appointed unto men "once to die," not two or three times as would have to be the case if Antichrist were some historical man from the dead. Antichrist will die the day Christ comes, so he must be mortal at that time. If he's mortal then, he can't be someone from past history who died and has seen corruption in the grave for hundreds or thousands of years. Therefore, this theory isn't worthy of consideration from Scripture's standpoint.\n\n- **That Antichrist cannot come from Italy, the Vatican, Germany, Russia, or from any prominent country of today, or from Heaven or Hell?** According to Dan. 7:7-8, 23-24, he'll come from within the old Roman Empire and from among the ten kingdoms yet to be formed there. This alone proves he can't come from Heaven, Hell, Germany, Russia, or other countries some say he'll come from. According to Dan. 8:7-9, 20-23, he must come from one of the four divisions of the old Grecian Empire — known today as Greece, Turkey, Syria, and Egypt. When Alexander the Great died, his empire was divided into four parts, as any ancient history publication shows. Dan. 8:9 states, "Out of one of them" — out of one of these four divisions — the "little horn" would come, explained in verse 23 as a king of fierce countenance rising in the latter time of these four kingdoms. The four kingdoms of Dan. 8 will make up four of the ten of Dan. 7. This chapter narrows the Antichrist's origin geographically from ten kingdoms to four, so we can definitively say he won't come from any country outside these four kingdoms. Because he's coming from either Greece, Turkey, Syria, or Egypt, he cannot come from Italy, the Vatican, Germany, Russia, or other places many Bible students claim — none of these countries was part of the old Grecian Empire or its four divisions. If people had read these chapters and believed what's plainly revealed, we never would have had all the foolish speculation about Mussolini, Hitler, or other men. Neither would we have the present foolish speculation that the Pope or some current man will be the Antichrist.\n\n- **That the Bible definitely tells you where Antichrist comes from?** Daniel not only makes clear he'll come from the ten kingdoms of the old Roman Empire (Dan. 7:7-8, 23-24) and from the four divisions of the Grecian Empire (Dan. 8:7-9, 20-23), but also that he'll come from the Syrian division, as plainly taught in Dan. 11. The king of the north in this chapter is Syria; the king of the south is Egypt. Wars between these two divisions of Greece are pictured in Dan. 11:5-34. Verses 35-45 portray war between these same kingdoms "at the time of the end," showing the result of the last war between them. It states that Egypt shall not escape the king of the north in this last war, identifying Syria as the country from which Antichrist must come. If the king of the north were Russia, as many Bible students now teach, how could Dan. 11:44 be fulfilled? What countries are north of Russia that could fight against her, as this verse requires? There are no countries north of Russia, proving the king of the north can't be Russia. When we understand this refers to the northern division of the Grecian Empire — Syria — everything in the passage is clear. The Antichrist definitely comes from Syria and is called the king of the north who will fulfill all of Dan. 8:22-23; 11:35-45; 12:1-7.\n\n---\n\nGod's unconditional promises guarantee that what He pledged will be delivered — but your faith is the hand that receives what His grace has provided. So here's the question: Name one promise from the Abrahamic or Davidic covenant that belongs to you through Christ. Are you asking for it specifically, persistently, and in faith? Or have you left it as a doctrinal truth you acknowledge without ever personally claiming?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk48i0019ewhj8er5tbei	cmnknk48e000xewhj49ao5az0	Supplement 13 — For Lessons 25 & 26	SUPPLEMENT	810	11	\N	t	The Holy Spirit is a Person — not a feeling, not an atmosphere, not a force. And He produces both gifts and fruit in the lives of believers who are genuinely yielded to Him. This supplement takes the doctrinal truth of Lessons Twenty-Five and Twenty-Six and presses it into the practical question: What does it actually look like to live in the fullness of the Holy Spirit day to day?\n\nYears ago, when I was just a boy, I made a commitment to God. I consecrated my life to studying and teaching *everything* in the Bible — whether or not it lined up with my church or any other church. To me, this is the only honest and intelligent path to take.\n\nIf others choose differently, that's on them. They'll answer to God for it in the end.\n\nMy hope is that you'll continue approaching Scripture with a clear, rational mind. After all, isn't truth what you're really after? Don't you want to know what God Himself teaches?\n\nIf that's your heart, then studying these lessons and reading the Scriptures we provide can't possibly lead you astray. Every doctrine we cover is backed up by God's own Word.\n\n![Supplement 13 — For Lessons 25 & 26](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OswXC39qNv3tcSjXbK960OEaP1ghrDBudGzUTQ)\n\n## The Right Basis of Understanding Truth\n\nLet's acknowledge a few things together. The Bible is written in human language. There *must* be a basis of truth we can all agree on if we're willing.\n\nGod is certainly intelligent enough to make Himself clear on every doctrine He teaches. And it's simply impossible that there are dozens of "correct" interpretations of the same plain statements in Scripture.\n\nHere's what's really happening: there's a supernatural power at work, trying to confuse people and make them think the Bible can mean whatever anyone wants it to mean. This is too obvious to ignore!\n\nSo let's take a logical approach and ask ourselves some honest questions.\n\nIsn't the Bible God's inspired revelation? Isn't it written in plain human language so we can all understand it the same way — or is it really so complicated that we have to argue endlessly about what it means?\n\nWhy do preachers disagree about what the Bible teaches if it's simple enough for everyone to understand? What good does that division accomplish?\n\nWouldn't it be better to simply believe together what's plainly written? Why let the devil divide God's people?\n\nWe've already addressed these questions thoroughly in Lessons One through Three. We've shown how demon powers work to confuse people's understanding of Scripture. We've proven that the Bible is God's Word, that it's actually the simplest book to understand, and that there's no excuse for misunderstanding it — if we just take it literally, the same way we read any other book.\n\nOnce you grasp this foundation, everything else falls into place naturally.\n\n## The Abundant Supply House\n\nThroughout these lessons, we've demonstrated something remarkable: there is no known need or want in the human experience that the Bible doesn't promise to supply — when we meet certain conditions.\n\nThink of it like an enormous warehouse stocked with everything you could ever need, and God has given you the key. The supplies are there. The access is available. You just need to follow the instructions.\n\nIf you ever find yourself wavering on this point or questioning any detail, go back and review Lessons One through Three, Ten, Twelve, Fourteen, Sixteen, Twenty, and Twenty-two. Refresh your memory with God's abundant promises and His plan for getting these benefits into your life.\n\n## No Failure is Possible\n\nHere's a truth that should transform how you approach God: no failure is possible when you want to receive from Him — *if* you simply follow the instructions laid out in His Word.\n\nIt would be easier for heaven and earth to disappear than for even one of God's promises to fail, as long as we faithfully and literally obey the conditions He's made clear.\n\nJesus Himself said, "Heaven and earth shall pass away: but my words shall not pass away" (Lk. 21:33). And the psalmist declared, "For ever O Lord, thy word is settled in heaven" (Ps. 119:89).\n\nIt is literally impossible for God to fail anyone. No one has ever experienced that, and no one ever will. On the other hand, everyone who knows God — or who has heard about others who know God — will testify that He has never failed in a single thing He promised.\n\n## Where the Failure Really Is\n\nIf you're not getting what you want from God, the problem isn't far away. An honest self-examination will reveal it quickly. In fact, it's usually so obvious that no real searching should be necessary.\n\nThe failure is in *you*. And if you don't think so, that very attitude proves the point.\n\nThose who truly love God will give Him the benefit of the doubt. They'll check themselves to find out where the real trouble lies. And they'll discover that everyone who fails to receive what God has promised is, at the root, doubting Him.\n\nIf there's no other reason for failure, doubt is reason enough.\n\nHow can you tell if you're doubting? It's actually easy. The very fact that you recognize a failure, that you're questioning why you haven't been answered, that you're wondering about it at all — *that's* proof of doubt.\n\nSomeone who genuinely believes God will settle the matter once and for all. They won't question the how or why of any answer — or the lack of one. It's similar to how a child trusts a parent's promise completely, without constantly asking "but are you *sure*?" That child just knows it's going to happen.\n\nIf you truly believe, it becomes a settled fact. No questioning. No wondering. No reasoning it out. No discussing it with people or entertaining the devil's suggestions. No relaxing until the answer comes.\n\nSuch a person would rather die in faith than live in unbelief, constantly worrying and questioning. But here's the beautiful truth: if you refuse to waver, everything will work out. Unbelief, however, ties God's hands and keeps you from receiving what you want.\n\n## God is No Respecter of Persons\n\nGod has plainly stated the grounds on which He will — and will not — answer prayer. He must be faithful to His Word.\n\nHe will not deviate one inch from the conditions He's told us we must meet. He won't change His Word for any individual. If He did, He'd be showing favoritism like everyone else.\n\nGod is absolutely and eternally true to Himself, His Word, and His entire creation. He will not violate His own principles. If you want answers to prayer, do what God requires, and you will receive the right answers.\n\n## The Word of God is Plain\n\nWe've made this clear repeatedly: God's will and Word are plain, and we've stated the promises and conditions of answered prayer many times. There's no excuse for not knowing what to do, and there can be no excuse for not doing what you know you should.\n\nThese steps must be followed before prayer is answered, so settle this once and for all.\n\nIf there's any rebellion in your heart against certain Bible truths... if you've fought against accepting the whole truth as it reads... if you're still questioning whether what you're being taught might not be true — you must stop this right now.\n\nI understand that many of these truths are new to you and that the average church doesn't teach this way. But does that prove the Bible is wrong?\n\nYou can see for yourself that what we're saying is right there in plain English in your own Bible. You don't have to wonder about truth any longer — if you genuinely want it. It's there in your Bible, and it's not a question of whether everyone accepts it or whether it's popular.\n\nThe only question is: Do these statements appear in the Bible in plain human language? If they do, you're responsible to God for accepting or rejecting them.\n\n## Don't Sell God Short by Unbelief\n\nYou may even have to leave your church in order to believe the whole truth. I hope that's not necessary, but if it is — if that's what it takes to accept truth and receive its benefits — it will richly pay you to do so. Any church or preacher who won't accept the whole truth of the Bible is not worthy of your continued respect and support.\n\nLet me be clear: I'm not advocating that Christians leave their churches. What I *am* advocating is that all churches repent and return to the Bible.\n\nBut if it comes down to a choice, individuals in churches should not continue in fellowship with those who reject Scripture. It would be better to leave and join churches in your community that actually follow the Bible. This is a perfectly right position to take, because we must obey God's Word rather than human opinions.\n\nKeep this in mind: when churches become worldly, ungodly, and rebellious against the gospel, they're no longer truly Christian churches. They've become societies of sin and rebellion against God. Anyone who supports such churches will face God's judgment.\n\n## Your Final Court of Appeals is the Bible\n\nGo to it yourself. See if it says what we claim it does. We give you the references to read, and you can verify whether these things are there or not.\n\nDon't take the attitude that these teachings are just "our interpretation" and that another interpretation might be better. That's just an excuse, because you can still see for yourself whether the Bible says what we say it does.\n\nThis isn't about our interpretation versus someone else's. It's about what the Bible *actually says*. You need to wake up to this fact before it's too late.\n\nYes, my friend, we're being faithful to tell you what the Bible says — but it's up to you to accept it and receive the promised benefits. Don't blame God for your failure to get what you want. Blame yourself, correct what's wrong, and what you want will be yours.\n\nI'm asking you to reaffirm your faith in God and His Word. Consecrate yourself fresh to full and complete obedience to truth as you come to know it.\n\nRepeat the affirmations we've given you constantly. Grow in the kind of faith that dares to believe God for what you want.\n\nIn our next two lessons, we'll deal thoroughly with the subject of faith. Until then, I join with you in prayer that you might comprehend with all the saints the fullness of God and experience everything that is freely promised to you.\n\n---\n\n## Ten Questions For Supplement Thirteen. Do You Know:\n\n- **That sinners can commit the unpardonable sin?** This is clear from the fact that the Jews of Mt. 12:22-37 were sinners and Christ warned them of committing that sin. In Heb. 10:26-29 we have the fact that a person who has been sanctified by the blood of Christ can go back into sin and commit this sin. Blaspheming the Spirit and attributing to the devil the works of the Spirit, or speaking a word against the Spirit knowingly and willfully, is the unpardonable sin, and this any person can do whether he is a sinner or not.\n\n- **That one man in the Bible fasted longer than did Christ?** Moses fasted at least three times forty days each (Deut. 9:9, 11, 17-18, 23-25). In fact, it seems from Deut. 9:11-18 that Moses fasted eighty days without eating between the two forty-day fasts.\n\n- **That one man captured a whole army of multiplied thousands single-handed?** Elisha did this when he captured the whole army of Syria and led them captive to Samaria (2 Ki. 6:18-23).\n\n- **That Cain was not the first one to build a city on Earth?** He was the first to build a city on Earth since Adam (Gen. 4:17), but there were cities built on Earth by the pre-Adamites, as proven in Lesson Seven, Point VI.\n\n- **That resurrected men and angels can go back and forth from Earth to Heaven in one day?** Jesus after the resurrection went to Heaven and back in one day. This statement is proven by the fact that He forbad Mary to touch Him for He had not yet ascended to God but was going to God, and on the same day He returned to the Earth and appeared to the disciples (Jn. 20:17-23). If resurrected men can go to Heaven and back in a day, angels can do likewise. Other men have also gone to Heaven but how long it took them is not stated (See Supplement One, Question 9.)\n\n- **That people who go to Heaven will eat food forever?** In Lk. 22:14-18, 29-30; Rev. 2:7, 17; 19:1-10; 22:1-2 it is clear that saints will eat forever.\n\n- **That Egypt will never become great again?** The Bible is very clear that Egypt will never be a great power again as it used to be in the early days of Israel. The prophet Ezekiel said that Egypt would be "a base kingdom. It shall be the basest of the kingdoms; neither shall it exalt itself any more above the nations" (Ezek. 29:14-15). Egypt will be defeated by the future Antichrist from Syria (Dan 11:40-45).\n\n- **That the Arabs will give Israel protection from the Antichrist during the last 3½ years of this age?** Dan. 11:41 states that Arabia or ancient Edom and Moab will escape Antichrist. In Isa. 16:1-5; Ezek. 20:33-35; Mt. 24:15-21; Rev. 12:5-17 we read of Israel fleeing for protection into the wilderness of Edom and Moab, and since they escape the Antichrist and flee into this country for protection, then it is clear that the Arabs protect them during this time.\n\n- **That the Bible names the very city in Edom and Moab that Israel will flee to for protection during the Tribulation?** It is Sela or Petra, the ancient capital of Edom (Isa. 16:1-5; 26:20-21; 63:1-8).\n\n- **That God has a basis of dealing with the heathen who have never heard of the Gospel?** He will deal with them on the basis of the light they have received, and on the basis of obeying their own conscience (Rom. 2:12-16).\n\n---\n\nThe Holy Spirit is not a resource you access on Sundays — He's a Person who lives inside you every moment of every day. The gifts He distributes are for the Church's benefit. The fruit He grows is for your character. So here's the question: Are you actively cooperating with the Holy Spirit's work in both dimensions — seeking and exercising the gifts He's given you, and deliberately yielding to His fruit-producing work in your character? Or is one of those two areas being neglected?	\N	\N	\N
cmq76cgaf000510b8qnae1jyz	cmq76cga9000110b8juoxpkya	Lesson 2 — Lucifer's Fall: The Rebellion That Changed Everything	READING	900	1	\N	t	![Lesson 2 — Lucifer's Fall: The Rebellion That Changed Everything](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsbCl4JkpZWXUxpYCzVoHdTRw8N94g0J5LQu7D)\n\n## Lucifer's Fall — The Rebellion That Changed Everything\n\nThe origin of evil in the universe is not a mystery of metaphysics but a **recorded act of willful rebellion**. Lucifer, the highest and most beautiful of the created angels, chose pride over obedience — and in that choice, introduced evil into a world God had made perfectly good.\n\n### Who Was Lucifer?\n\nThe prophet Ezekiel gives us the clearest portrait, though he addresses the king of Tyre, the language soars far beyond any earthly monarch:\n\n> Ezekiel 28:12-15 — "Thou sealest up the sum, full of wisdom, and perfect in beauty. Thou hast been in Eden the garden of God... Thou wast perfect in thy ways from the day that thou wast created, till iniquity was found in thee."\n\nLucifer was created with extraordinary gifts: wisdom, beauty, musical ability, and a position of high honor. He was a **covering cherub** — one who stood in the immediate presence of God's glory. Yet these very gifts became the occasion of his downfall.\n\n### The Sin of Pride\n\nIsaiah records the fivefold "I will" of Lucifer's rebellion:\n\n> Isaiah 14:12-14 — "I will ascend into heaven, I will exalt my throne above the stars of God... I will be like the most High."\n\nPride is the foundational sin of hell. Lucifer was not tempted by an outside tempter; he tempted himself. He looked at his own beauty and splendor and chose to desire the glory that belongs to God alone. This is why pride remains the root of all demonic activity: every demon, in its own way, continues to say "I will" in defiance of God's "Thou shalt."\n\n### The Consequences of the Fall\n\nWhen Lucifer rebelled, he did not merely lose his position — he was **cast down**. Jesus says:\n\n> Luke 10:18 — "I beheld Satan as lightning fall from heaven."\n\nHis name was changed from Lucifer ("light-bearer") to Satan ("adversary"). He became the accuser of the brethren (Revelation 12:10), the deceiver of the whole world (Revelation 12:9), and the prince of the power of the air (Ephesians 2:2). The angels who followed him became demons — stripped of their holy office, condemned to wander the earth, and bound for eternal judgment.\n\n### The Fall and Human History\n\nSatan's fall did not end his activity. He immediately turned his hatred against God's new creation — humanity. In the Garden of Eden, he tempted Adam and Eve to the same sin that ruined him: the desire to be like God. And when humanity fell, Satan became "the god of this world" (2 Corinthians 4:4), exercising a usurped dominion over the nations until Christ's redemptive work would reclaim what was lost.	\N	\N	\N
cmq76cgb2000t10b86l560c2y	cmq76cgaw000n10b8wrzhfrh1	Supplement 4 — For Lessons 7 & 8	SUPPLEMENT	600	2	\N	t	![Supplement 4 — For Lessons 7 & 8](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OstN9YFGSSw8OsqyXxGpeBMuoLIEnWg5fdi9R1)\n\n## Supplement 4 — For Lessons 7 & 8: Deception and the Occult\n\n### Key Terms\n\n**Doctrines of Devils** — False teachings that originate from demonic inspiration, designed to lead people away from faith in Christ.\n\n**Divination** — The attempt to gain hidden knowledge by supernatural means apart from God.\n\n**Necromancy** — Communication with the dead; in reality, communication with demons impersonating the dead.\n\n**Familiar Spirit** — A demon that attaches itself to a family line, often through occult involvement.\n\n**Legal Ground** — The right a demon claims to operate in a person's life through sin, trauma, or occult participation.\n\n---\n\n### Scripture Memory\n\n> 1 Timothy 4:1 — "Now the Spirit speaketh expressly, that in the latter times some shall depart from the faith, giving heed to seducing spirits, and doctrines of devils."\n\n> Deuteronomy 18:10-12 — "There shall not be found among you any one that useth divination, or an observer of times, or an enchanter, or a witch... For all that do these things are an abomination unto the LORD."\n\n> 2 Corinthians 6:17 — "Wherefore come out from among them, and be ye separate, saith the Lord, and touch not the unclean thing."\n\n---\n\n### Modern Forms of Divination\n\n| Practice | Biblical Status | Common Manifestations |\n|----------|---------------|----------------------|\n| Astrology | Forbidden | Horoscopes, zodiac signs, star charts |\n| Fortune-telling | Forbidden | Tarot, palm reading, crystal balls |\n| Necromancy | Forbidden | Séances, Ouija boards, channeling |\n| Witchcraft | Forbidden | Spell-casting, curses, Wicca |\n| New Age | Forbidden | Spirit guides, energy healing, astral projection |\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Reflection\n\n1. Why does God condemn divination so strongly? What does it reveal about His character and His plan for His people?\n\n2. Have you ever participated in occult practices, even in jest? How should you address this in prayer?\n\n3. How can you lovingly warn others about the dangers of horoscopes, Ouija boards, and other "harmless" occult activities?\n\n4. What does it mean that demons have a "legal ground"? How does the cross of Christ break every claim they have?	\N	\N	\N
cmq76cgb3000v10b8zpkwql1x	cmq76cgaw000n10b8wrzhfrh1	Lesson 9 — Seducing Spirits and the Church	READING	900	3	\N	t	![Lesson 9 — Seducing Spirits and the Church](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsFkihX6bM1hRYQ2o4Z38BzgHNSP5KdLckVeFn)\n\n## Seducing Spirits and the Church\n\nThe Holy Spirit is not the only spirit at work in the church. Paul warned the Corinthians that false apostles and deceitful workers transform themselves into apostles of Christ (2 Corinthians 11:13-15). Satan's most devastating attacks do not come from outside the church but from within — through ministers who appear godly but preach another gospel.\n\n### The Spirit of Antichrist\n\nJohn warns that many antichrists have already come:\n\n> 1 John 2:18 — "Little children, it is the last time: and as ye have heard that antichrist shall come, even now are there many antichrists; whereby we know that it is the last time."\n\nThe spirit of antichrist does not necessarily deny God openly. It often simply ignores Christ — teaching about God, morality, and spirituality while leaving out the cross, the blood, and the necessity of personal salvation through Jesus.\n\n### Seducing Spirits in the Modern Church\n\n**The Prosperity Gospel** — The teaching that faith is a tool to obtain wealth and health. It turns God into a vending machine and the Christian life into a quest for personal comfort. It denies the clear teaching of Scripture that believers will suffer, be persecuted, and carry a cross.\n\n**Progressive Christianity** — The movement that redefines sin, denies the authority of Scripture, and blesses what God condemns. It presents itself as compassionate and enlightened, but it is the spirit of antichrist dressed in progressive language.\n\n**Hyper-Grace** — The teaching that repentance is unnecessary, that the cross covers all future sin automatically, and that believers have no obligation to obey God's commands. This transforms grace into a license for sin.\n\n**Emphasis on Experience Over Truth** — When churches prioritize emotional encounters, visions, and prophetic words above the plain teaching of Scripture, they open the door to demonic counterfeits. Satan can produce powerful religious experiences that feel genuine but lead people away from Christ.\n\n### Testing the Spirits\n\nJohn commands believers to test the spirits:\n\n> 1 John 4:1 — "Beloved, believe not every spirit, but try the spirits whether they are of God: because many false prophets are gone out into the world."\n\nThe test is doctrinal: Does the spirit confess that Jesus Christ has come in the flesh? Does it honor the Father and the Son? Does it submit to the authority of Scripture? Any spirit — or any teacher — that fails this test is not of God.	\N	\N	\N
cmq76cgbf001910b876wdxib6	cmq76cgb9001110b8mkvodphm	Lesson 18 — Deliverance and Exorcism	READING	900	3	\N	t	![Lesson 18 — Deliverance and Exorcism](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsNuZnzQUgPsHDqdkrK1w9XGRYBoiJIFQn0btc)\n\n## Deliverance and Exorcism\n\nJesus commissioned His disciples to cast out demons as a normal part of their ministry:\n\n> Mark 16:17 — "And these signs shall follow them that believe; In my name shall they cast out devils."\n\nDeliverance is not a relic of the early church; it is the ongoing ministry of every believer who walks in the authority of Christ.\n\n### The Ministry of Deliverance\n\nDeliverance is the expulsion of a demon or demons from a person or place through the authority of Jesus Christ. It may be dramatic — with convulsions, voices, and manifestations — or it may be peaceful, with the person simply feeling free.\n\nJesus performed deliverance with a word (Mark 1:25), with a command (Mark 9:25), and sometimes with prayer and fasting (Matthew 17:21). The method varies, but the authority is the same.\n\n### Preparing for Deliverance\n\nBefore attempting deliverance, the minister should ensure:\n\n1. **The person wants to be free** — Deliverance cannot be forced on someone who desires to keep their demon.\n\n2. **Sin is confessed and renounced** — The person must close every door they opened through repentance.\n\n3. **The minister is spiritually prepared** — Prayer, fasting, and a clean life are essential. The demons know whether the minister is walking in authority or presumption.\n\n4. **Aftercare is planned** — A delivered person needs discipleship, fellowship, and ongoing prayer. Deliverance without follow-up often leads to relapse.\n\n### Practical Steps\n\n1. **Identify the spirit** — Ask the Holy Spirit for discernment. The demon may reveal its name or function.\n\n2. **Bind the spirit** — Command it in the Name of Jesus to be silent and to come out.\n\n3. **Cast it out** — Do not negotiate. Do not converse. Exercise authority and command it to leave.\n\n4. **Fill the void** — The expelled demon must be replaced with the Holy Spirit, the Word of God, and Christian fellowship. An empty house is vulnerable (Matthew 12:44).\n\n### The Power of Prayer and Fasting\n\nSome demons are more resistant than others. Jesus said:\n\n> Matthew 17:21 — "Howbeit this kind goeth not out but by prayer and fasting."\n\nFasting weakens the flesh and strengthens the spirit. It is a declaration that the battle belongs to God, not to human will or technique.	\N	\N	\N
cmq6612vx001fgeo0cj4dbiy4	cmq6612vm0013geo0kqjj27k2	Supplement 8 — For Lessons 13 & 14	SUPPLEMENT	600	5	\N	t	![Supplement 8 — For Lessons 13 & 14](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsplH6cWMUGDFtHARxTo0frklQIVBOcXizMeJh)\n\n## Supplement 8 — For Lessons 13 & 14: Gabriel and Raphael\n\n### Key Terms\n\n**Gabriel** ("God is my strength") — Archangel of Annunciation; created to deliver the message of the Incarnation; patron of messengers.\n\n**Raphael** ("God heals") — Archangel of healing and accompaniment; patron of travelers, the blind, and medical workers; reveals himself as "one of the seven holy angels who present the prayers of the saints."\n\n**The Angelus** — Traditional Catholic prayer recited three times daily, commemorating Gabriel's Annunciation, the Incarnation, and Mary's *fiat*.\n\n**The Book of Tobit** — Deuterocanonical book containing Raphael's primary scriptural appearance; canonical in Catholic and Orthodox tradition.\n\n---\n\n### Scripture Memory\n\n> Lk. 1:26-28 "In the sixth month the angel Gabriel was sent from God to a city of Galilee named Nazareth, to a virgin betrothed to a man whose name was Joseph, of the house of David. And the virgin's name was Mary. And he came to her and said, 'Greetings, O favored one, the Lord is with you!'"\n\n> Dan. 9:21-22 "While I was speaking in prayer, the man Gabriel, whom I had seen in the vision at the first, came to me in swift flight... He made me understand, speaking with me and saying, 'O Daniel, I have now come out to give you insight and understanding.'"\n\n> Tobit 12:15 "I am Raphael, one of the seven holy angels who present the prayers of the saints and enter into the presence of the holy God."\n\n---\n\n### Gabriel's Ministry in Scripture\n\n| Event | Location | Content |\n|-------|---------|---------|\n| Vision of the Ram and Goat | Dan. 8:16 | Explains the vision of the four kingdoms |\n| The Seventy Weeks | Dan. 9:21-27 | The timeline pointing to the Messiah |\n| Announcement to Zechariah | Lk. 1:19 | Birth of John the Baptist foretold |\n| The Annunciation | Lk. 1:26-38 | The Incarnation announced to Mary |\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Reflection\n\n1. Gabriel says to Mary: *"Hail, full of grace, the Lord is with you"* (Lk. 1:28). This greeting addresses her by title ("full of grace"), not merely by name. What does this suggest about how God sees those He sends His messengers to?\n\n2. Gabriel prepared Israel for the Messiah through Daniel, then announced the Messiah's arrival to Mary. How does this pattern of *preparation then fulfillment* appear in your own spiritual life?\n\n3. Raphael traveled anonymously with Tobias for weeks before revealing himself. Have you ever experienced God's provision or guidance through someone who seemed "ordinary" — only later recognizing the grace at work?\n\n4. Raphael tells Tobit and Tobias: *"I was present"* when they prayed, and their prayers *"rose up to heaven"* (Tobit 12:12). How does knowing that an angel has been carrying your prayers before God change the way you pray?\n\n5. Mary's response to Gabriel — *"Let it be to me according to your word"* — is the defining act of receptivity in human history. What areas of your life most need that same *fiat* — that same complete surrender to God's word?	\N	\N	\N
cmq6612vj000zgeo0jfq5z7ju	cmq6612ve000tgeo0b9n1d4af	Supplement 5 — For Lessons 9 & 10	SUPPLEMENT	600	2	\N	t	![Supplement 5 — For Lessons 9 & 10](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsmpqMLlTjs8MAGw2JgFBiIXKDThNCyqSVfZL3)\n\n## Supplement 5 — For Lessons 9 & 10: The Second Hierarchy and Principalities\n\n### Key Terms\n\n**Dominations** (*kyriotetes*, "lordships") — The top choir of the second hierarchy; they direct the governance of all creation, channeling authority from the first hierarchy to the lower choirs.\n\n**Virtues** (*dynameis*, "powers/forces") — Associated with physical power, the laws of nature, and miracles; they sustain the cosmic order and execute supernatural interventions.\n\n**Powers** (*exousiai*, "authorities") — They defend the deep laws of creation and guard the integrity of the cosmic order; they introduce souls to higher realities.\n\n**Principalities** (*archai*, "beginnings, rulers") — The top choir of the third hierarchy; guardians of nations, peoples, and ecclesial structures. Michael is the Principality of the Church.\n\n---\n\n### Scripture Memory\n\n> Col. 1:16 "For by him all things were created, in heaven and on earth, visible and invisible, whether thrones or dominions or rulers or powers — all things were created through him and for him."\n\n> Dan. 12:1 "At that time shall arise Michael, the great prince who has charge of your people."\n\n> Rom. 8:38-39 "For I am sure that neither death nor life, nor angels nor rulers... nor powers... will be able to separate us from the love of God in Christ Jesus our Lord."\n\n---\n\n### The Nine Choirs at a Glance\n\n| Choir | Hierarchy | Defining Focus |\n|-------|----------|---------------|\n| **Seraphim** | 1st | Burning love — direct adoration |\n| **Cherubim** | 1st | Fullness of knowledge |\n| **Thrones** | 1st | Stability of divine authority |\n| **Dominations** | 2nd | Direct governance of creation |\n| **Virtues** | 2nd | Cosmic order; miracles |\n| **Powers** | 2nd | Defense of creation's laws |\n| **Principalities** | 3rd | Nations, peoples, the Church |\n| **Archangels** | 3rd | Chief heavenly missions |\n| **Angels** | 3rd | Personal ministry to souls |\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Reflection\n\n1. The Dominations, Virtues, and Powers govern the universe as a whole — not individual souls. What does it mean to you that God's care for creation extends even to cosmic law and natural order?\n\n2. The Virtues are associated with miracles. The parting of the Red Sea (Ex. 14:21), the calming of the storm (Mk. 4:39), the healing of the blind (Jn. 9:6-7). How does seeing these events as involving the Virtues change the way you read them?\n\n3. Michael is the Principality assigned to God's people — first Israel, then the Church. How does knowing this affect your understanding of the prayer to Michael?\n\n4. Principalities govern nations and civilizations. If every nation has an angelic Principality, what does this imply about how we should pray for our own nation?\n\n5. Paul declares that no "powers" can separate us from God's love (Rom. 8:38-39). Why is it important to worship God and not any angel, however exalted?	\N	\N	\N
cmq76cgbg001b10b8jkb68k7q	cmq76cgb9001110b8mkvodphm	Lesson 19 — Taking Away the Devil's Armor	READING	900	4	\N	t	![Lesson 19 — Taking Away the Devil's Armor](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8Osmbl2HNTjs8MAGw2JgFBiIXKDThNCyqSVfZL3)\n\n## Taking Away the Devil's Armor\n\nJesus taught that a strong man's house cannot be plundered until the strong man is bound:\n\n> Matthew 12:29 — "Or else how can one enter into a strong man's house, and spoil his goods, except he first bind the strong man? and then he will spoil his house."\n\nBefore a believer can walk in freedom, the enemy's hold must be broken. This lesson examines how to systematically dismantle Satan's strongholds.\n\n### Strongholds of the Mind\n\nPaul writes about "casting down imaginations, and every high thing that exalteth itself against the knowledge of God, and bringing into captivity every thought to the obedience of Christ" (2 Corinthians 10:5).\n\nA stronghold is a fortress of wrong thinking — beliefs, assumptions, and thought patterns that contradict God's Word. Common strongholds include:\n\n- **Fear** — Believing that God will not protect or provide\n- **Shame** — Believing that past sin defines your identity\n- **Rejection** — Believing that you are unlovable or worthless\n- **Unforgiveness** — Believing that your right to anger is greater than God's command to forgive\n- **Pride** — Believing that you are better than others or that you do not need God\n\nThese strongholds are not merely psychological; they are spiritual. Demons reinforce them with lies, memories, and emotions. To break the stronghold, the lie must be replaced with truth.\n\n### Binding the Strong Man\n\nBinding the strong man involves:\n\n1. **Identifying the entry point** — When did this stronghold begin? What sin, trauma, or decision opened the door?\n\n2. **Repenting and renouncing** — Confess the sin, renounce the lie, and break every agreement with the enemy.\n\n3. **Replacing the lie with truth** — Find the Scripture that directly contradicts the stronghold. Meditate on it, speak it aloud, and believe it.\n\n4. **Commanding the spirit to leave** — In the Name of Jesus, bind the demon and cast it out.\n\n5. **Maintaining the victory** — Fill the space with prayer, worship, fellowship, and the Word. Do not leave the house empty.\n\n### The Weapons of Our Warfare\n\nPaul says our weapons are not carnal but mighty through God:\n\n> 2 Corinthians 10:4 — "For the weapons of our warfare are not carnal, but mighty through God to the pulling down of strong holds."\n\nThese weapons include prayer, fasting, the Word, praise, testimony, and the blood of Jesus. Each is effective, but together they form an arsenal that no demonic stronghold can withstand.	\N	\N	\N
cmq76cgbi001d10b8k1wbqx50	cmq76cgb9001110b8mkvodphm	Supplement 7 — For Lessons 13 & 14	SUPPLEMENT	600	5	\N	t	![Supplement 7 — For Lessons 13 & 14](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsFMQ43igbM1hRYQ2o4Z38BzgHNSP5KdLckVeF)\n\n## Supplement 7 — For Lessons 13 & 14: Deliverance and Strongholds\n\n### Key Terms\n\n**Deliverance** — The expulsion of demons from a person or place through the authority of Jesus Christ.\n\n**Exorcism** — The formal practice of casting out demons; in the New Testament, a ministry both of Jesus and of ordinary believers.\n\n**Stronghold** — A fortress of wrong thinking reinforced by demonic influence that contradicts God's Word.\n\n**Binding the Strong Man** — Breaking Satan's hold on a person, family, or territory through repentance, renunciation, and spiritual authority.\n\n---\n\n### Scripture Memory\n\n> Mark 16:17 — "And these signs shall follow them that believe; In my name shall they cast out devils."\n\n> 2 Corinthians 10:4-5 — "For the weapons of our warfare are not carnal, but mighty through God to the pulling down of strong holds; casting down imaginations, and every high thing that exalteth itself against the knowledge of God."\n\n> Matthew 12:29 — "Or else how can one enter into a strong man's house, and spoil his goods, except he first bind the strong man?"\n\n---\n\n### Common Strongholds and Their Biblical Counter-Truths\n\n| Stronghold | Lie | Biblical Truth |\n|------------|-----|--------------|\n| Fear | God will abandon me | Hebrews 13:5 — "I will never leave thee, nor forsake thee" |\n| Shame | My past defines me | 2 Corinthians 5:17 — "If any man be in Christ, he is a new creature" |\n| Rejection | I am unlovable | Romans 8:38-39 — Nothing can separate us from God's love |\n| Unforgiveness | They don't deserve mercy | Ephesians 4:32 — "Forgiving one another, even as God for Christ's sake hath forgiven you" |\n| Poverty | God wants me poor | Psalm 35:27 — "The LORD... hath pleasure in the prosperity of his servant" |\n| Sickness | God wants me sick | 1 Peter 2:24 — "By whose stripes ye were healed" |\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Reflection\n\n1. What strongholds have you recognized in your own life? How did they begin?\n\n2. Why is it essential to "fill the house" after deliverance? What happens when a stronghold is broken but not replaced with truth?\n\n3. How does fasting specifically contribute to spiritual warfare? Have you ever fasted for a breakthrough?\n\n4. What Scripture will you use to replace the strongest lie the enemy has told you?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk460000oewhjmg531nqb	cmnknk45y000gewhjn5pl0kw9	Lesson 14 — Divine Healing and Divine Health	READING	5514	7	\N	t	There is a doctrine preached in thousands of churches that God uses sickness to teach His people spiritual lessons — that a loving heavenly Father sometimes inflicts disease as a form of discipline or character development. If this is true, then praying for healing is at best presumptuous and at worst a resistance of God's will. But if it isn't true — if this doctrine has no biblical foundation — then millions of believers are suffering needlessly because of a theological error.\n\nLast time we saw that human government, given divine authority, ended in collective rebellion at Babel. We now turn from dispensational history to something that directly affects your body today: what does Scripture actually teach about God's will concerning sickness and health?\n\n![Lesson 14 — Divine Healing and Divine Health](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8Os02gGQBYvRZPna4mAuYVdrFHOBK7LSwy8WioD)\n\n## I. What Divine Healing and Health is Not\n\nLet's start by clearing up what divine healing is *not*. It's not healing through natural remedies, imagination, willpower, or personal magnetism.\n\nIt's not metaphysics, demonology, or spiritualism. It's not a promise of immunity from death or an excuse for presumption or insubordination toward God's will.\n\nIt's also not "mind over matter," a denial of the reality of sin, sickness, and disease, or simply the body's natural ability to heal itself through inherent laws and creative powers.\n\n## II. What Divine Healing and Health Is\n\nSo what *is* divine healing and health? It's a definite act of God—something He does through your faith in Jesus, by the power of the Holy Spirit, the Word of God, and the precious blood of Christ.\n\nThrough this work, the human body is cured, healed, repaired, and delivered from sickness and its power. The result? You're made as whole, sound, and healthy as you were before the attack came.\n\n## III. Is Divine Healing and Health Scriptural?\n\nAbsolutely. There are at least fourteen Hebrew and Greek words found in hundreds of Scriptures that plainly teach the ideas of full salvation, deliverance, preservation, soundness, healing, health, and wholeness—for body, soul, and spirit through the gospel.\n\nThese words are used many times for the body *as well as* the soul. Sometimes they appear in the same passages referring to both, proving they aren't limited to spiritual healing as some modern Bible students believe.\n\nThere are some passages where these words speak of other things being healed—the soul (Ps. 41:4), a vessel (Jer. 19:11), land (2 Chron. 7:14), waters (Ezek. 47:8-11; 2 Ki. 2:21-22), and backslidings (Jer. 3:22). If healing is meant literally in these cases, then it should also be understood literally when it refers to the human body.\n\nTake a look at how often bodily healing is mentioned in Scripture:\n\n> 1. The Hebrew word **rapha** means and is translated "cure" (Jer. 33:6); "to make whole" (Job 5:18); "to heal" (Num. 12:13; Deut. 32:39; 2 Ki. 20:5, 8; 6:2; Isa. 19:22; 57:18-19; Jer. 17:14; 30:17; Hos. 6:1; Zech. 1:16); "be healed" (Gen. 20:17; Exod. 21:19; Lev. 13:18, 37; 14:3, 48; Deut. 28:27, 35; 1 Sam. 6:3; 9:15; 2 Chron. 22:6; 30:20; Ps. 30:2; 107:20; Isa. 6:10; 53:5; Jer. 17:14; Ezek. 34:4; Hos. 7:1; 11:3); "healeth" (Exod. 15:26; Ps. 103:3; 147:3; Isa. 30:26); "be thoroughly healed" (Exod. 21:19); and "physician" (Gen. 50:2; 2 Chron. 16:12; Job 13:4; Jer. 8:22).\n\n> 2. The Hebrew **marpay** means and is translated "healing" (Jer. 14:19; Mal. 4:2) "health" (Prov. 4:22; 12:18; 13:17; 16:24; Jer. 8:15); "cure" (Jer. 33:6); "remedy" (2 Chron. 36:16; Prov. 6:15; 29:1); "sound" in health (Prov. 14:30); and "wholesome" (Prov. 15:4).\n\n> 3. The Hebrew **yeshuwah** means and is translated "health" (Ps. 42:11; 43:5; 67:2); "deliverance" (Ps. 18:50; 44:4); and "salvation" (Ps. 91:16). The whole 91st Psalm teaches perfect healing and health physically; so the salvation of v. 16 includes the body as well as the soul.\n\n> 4. The Hebrew **arubah** means "restoring to sound health" (Isa. 58:8; Jer. 8:22; 30:17; 33:6).\n\n> 5. The Hebrew **chabash** means "healer" (Isa. 3:7) and "to bind up or heal" (Isa. 30:26; 61:1; Ezek. 34:16; Hos. 6:1; Job 5:18; Ps. 147:3).\n\n> 6. The Hebrew word **rifooth** means "health" (Prov. 3:8).\n\n> 7. The Greek word **therapeuo** means and is translated "heal" (Mt. 8:7; 10:1, 8; 12:10; Mk. 3:2, 15; Lk. 4:23; 6:7; 10:9; 14:3); "healed" (Mt. 4:23-24; 8:16, 17; 9:35; 12:15, 22; 14:14; 15:20; 19:2; 21:14; Mk. 1:34; 3:10; 6:5, 13; Lk. 4:40; 5:15; 6:18; 8:43; 9:6-11; 13:14; 14:4; Acts 4:14; 5:16; 8:7; 28:9); "cure" (Mt. 17:16; Lk. 9:1); and "cured" (Lk. 7:21; Jn. 5:10).\n\n> 8. The Greek **iama** means "healing" or "repairing" (1 Cor. 12:9, 28, 30).\n\n> 9. The Greek **iaomai** means and is translated "heal" (Mt. 13:15; Lk. 4:18; 5:17; 9:2; Jn. 4:47; 12:40); "healed" (Mt. 8:8, 13; Mk. 5:29; Lk. 6:17, 19; 7:7; 8:2, 47; 17:15; 22:51; Jn. 5:13; Acts 3:11; 28:8; Heb. 12:13; Jas. 5:16; 1 Pet. 2:24); "healing" (Acts 10:38); "whole" (Mt. 15:28; Acts 9:24).\n\n> 10. The Greek **iasis** means "healing" and "cure" (Lk. 13:32; Acts 4:22, 30; 28:27).\n\n> 11. The Greek **hugiano** means "sound health" (3 Jn. 2).\n\n> 12. The Greek **hugies** means "healthy," "sound" and "whole" (Mt. 12:13; 15:31; Mk. 3:5; 5:34; Lk. 5:31).\n\n> 13. The Greek **sodzo** means "to save," "deliver," "protect," "heal," "preserve," and "make whole." It is used 86 times in reference to saving the soul, but in the following passages it refers to saving the body and healing it of sickness (Mt. 9:21-22; Mk. 5:29, 34; 6:56; 10:52; Lk. 8:36, 48; 8:50; 17:19; Acts 4:9; 14:9).\n\n> 14. The Greek **dia-sodzo** means "to save" and "to heal thoroughly," "make perfectly whole" and is used of the body being healed (Mt. 14:36; Lk. 7:3).\n\nBy reading the 177 Scriptures above—and the many more we'll cover—you can see clearly that both divine healing and divine health are taught in Scripture for everyone who will meet the conditions of faith required by the gospel.\n\n## IV. Cases of Sickness and Healing in the Old Testament\n\n1. **Barrenness of the Philistine Women:** cause—divine intervention for Sarah (Gen. 20:1-7, 18); remedy—prayer (Gen. 20:7, 17); result—healing (Gen. 20:7, 17).\n\n2. **Barrenness of Sarah:** cause—unknown (Gen. 16:1–21:8); remedy—prayer and faith (Gen. 18:10; 21:1-8; Rom. 4:17-22); result—healing (Gen. 21).\n\n3. **Barrenness of Rebekah:** cause—unknown (Gen. 25:19-26); remedy—prayer (Gen. 25:21); result—healing (Gen. 25:21-26).\n\n4. **Isaac's Blindness and Sickness:** cause—old age (Gen. 27:1-2, 41); remedy—none stated, but evidently he was renewed in body by faith (Heb. 11:20); result—lived over twenty more years (Gen. 31:38; 35:27-29).\n\n5. **Barrenness of Rachel:** cause—unknown (Gen. 29:31; 30:1); remedy—prayer (Gen. 30:22); result—healing (Gen. 30:22-24; 35:16-20).\n\n6. **Jacob's Death:** cause—old age (Gen. 47:27-31; 48:1; 49:33); remedy—none, because of the divine appointment for all men to die (Heb. 9:27). The Hebrew word for "sick" in Gen. 48:1 is *calah*, meaning "to be worn," "weak." Jacob died of old age like an apple falling off the tree because of ripeness—not because of disease.\n\nThe Bible teaches that all true believers can live to a ripe old age (Ps. 34:12-14; 91:16; 1 Pet. 3:10-12). Not long ago, there was a woman 95 years old who was as healthy as could be expected at that age. One day she told her children she was going to die on a certain day. When that day came, after saying her goodbyes, she simply lay down, folded her hands, and gave up her spirit without a struggle.\n\nThis is how Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, Moses, and many others passed away. Think of it like this: a person doesn't have to die of a disease any more than an apple has to fall because of worms or rot. It's possible to live in health to a ripe old age and die a natural death. By faith in God, you can ward off many of life's ills and live long before natural death comes.\n\n7. **Boils on the Egyptians:** cause—hardness of heart and rebellion against God (Exod. 4:21; 5:2); remedy—unknown; result—unknown.\n\n8. **All Sicknesses of Israel:** cause—misuse of the tongue and rebellion (Exod. 15:24-26); remedy—the Word of God, prayer, and obedience (Exod. 15:26; Ps. 107:20); result—the whole nation made perfect in health (Ps. 105:37).\n\n9. **Plague in Israel:** cause—misuse of the tongue and idolatry (Exod. 32:1-6); remedy—prayer, atonement, and discipline (Exod. 32:7-28); result—many rebels died, but the rest were delivered from the plague (Exod. 32:27-35).\n\n10. **Plague upon Priests:** cause—drunkenness and disobedience; remedy—none; result—death by judgment from God (Lev. 10:1-9).\n\n11. **Plague in Israel:** cause—misuse of the tongue; remedy—prayer; result—plague stopped (Num. 11:1-3).\n\n12. **Plague in Israel:** cause—misuse of the tongue and lust (Num. 11:4-20); remedy—none because of judgment (Num. 11:19-20); result—death to thousands (Num. 11:33-35).\n\n13. **Plague of Leprosy upon Miriam:** cause—pride and misuse of the tongue (Num. 12:1-10); remedy—prayer and discipline (Num. 12:13-14); result—healing (Num. 12:14-16).\n\n14. **Plague upon the Ten Spies:** cause—unbelief and misuse of the tongue (Num. 13:26-33); remedy—none because of judgment; result—death (Num. 4:37).\n\n15. **Plague in Israel:** cause—misuse of the tongue and rebellion (Num. 14:1-19); remedy—none because of judgment (Num. 14:22-35); result—death (Num. 14:20-35).\n\n16. **Plague in Israel:** cause—pride and misuse of the tongue (Num. 16:1-14); remedy—judgment upon the rebels (Num. 16:21-30); result—death (Num. 16:31).\n\n17. **Plague in Israel:** cause—rebellion and misuse of the tongue (Num. 16:1-14); remedy—judgment upon the rebels (Num. 16:29); result—death (Num. 16:35).\n\n18. **Plague in Israel:** cause—rebellion and misuse of the tongue (Num. 16:41-42); remedy—atonement and judgment (Num. 16:46-50); result—14,700 were killed and the plague was stayed (Num. 16:49-50).\n\n19. **Plague in Israel:** cause—discouragement and misuse of the tongue (Num. 21:5); remedy—prayer, faith, and confession of sin (Num. 21:7-9); result—healing for all who obeyed and looked on the serpent of brass, a type of Christ (Num. 21:9; Jn. 3:14).\n\n20. **Plague in Israel:** cause—adultery and idolatry; remedy—judgment upon the rebels; result—24,000 were killed and the plague was stayed (Num. 25:1-15).\n\n21. **Barrenness of Hannah:** cause—the Lord caused her to be barren to test her and teach coming generations certain lessons (1 Sam. 1:2-9); remedy—prayer and humility (1 Sam. 1:9-19); result—healing and fruitfulness (1 Sam. 1:17-28).\n\n22. **Plague of Emerods upon the Philistines:** cause—taking the ark of God from Israel; remedy—trespass offering and ark sent back; result—unknown (1 Sam. 5:1–6:21).\n\n23. **Plague in Israel:** cause—looking into the ark; remedy—judgment upon the transgressors; result—many killed and the plague stayed (1 Sam. 6:19-21).\n\n24. **Insanity of Saul:** cause—disobedience and jealousy (1 Sam. 15:22-35; 18:8-30); remedy—inspired music (1 Sam. 16:14; 19:9-10); result—temporary relief (1 Sam. 16:14-23).\n\n25. **Sickness of an Egyptian:** cause—unknown; remedy—care and natural food; result—he was restored to health (1 Sam. 30:11-16).\n\n26. **Sickness and Death of David's Child:** cause—adultery and murder (2 Sam. 12:9-15); remedy—fasting and prayer (2 Sam. 12:16-23); result—prayer unanswered because of punishment for sin (2 Sam. 12:9-15).\n\n27. **Plague in Israel:** cause—David's sin in numbering Israel (2 Sam. 24:1-25); remedy—atonement, confession of sin, and prayer; result—70,000 were killed and the plague was stayed (2 Sam. 24:10-25).\n\n28. **Jeroboam's Withered Hand:** cause—attempted violence to a man of God (1 Ki. 13:4); remedy—prayer; result—healing (1 Ki. 13:6).\n\n29. **Abijah's Sickness:** cause—sins of the parents (1 Ki. 14:8-9); remedy—none because of judgment; result—death (1 Ki. 14:12-18).\n\n30. **Asa's Foot Disease:** cause—unknown; remedy—physicians; result—death for not trusting God (1 Ki. 15:23-24; 2 Chron. 16:12-14; Jer. 17:5-13).\n\n31. **Sickness and Death of the Widow's Son:** cause—unknown; remedy—prayer of Elijah; result—resurrection and healing (1 Ki. 17:8-24).\n\n32. **Ahaziah's Disease:** cause—fell from an upper story; remedy—trust in Baalzebub, the god of flies, instead of the true God; result—death (2 Ki. 1:1-18).\n\n33. **The Shunammite Woman's Barrenness:** cause—unknown; remedy—faith and prophecy; result—healing and fruitfulness (2 Ki. 4:1-17).\n\n34. **Sickness and Death of the Shunammite's Son:** cause—sunstroke; remedy—faith, prayer, and the power of God in Elisha's body; result—resurrection and healing (2 Ki. 4:18-37).\n\n35. **Naaman's Leprosy:** cause—unknown; remedy—trust in God and obedience; result—healing (2 Ki. 5:1-19).\n\n36. **Gehazi's Leprosy:** cause—greed, lying, and disobedience; remedy—none because of the curse upon him; result—no healing (2 Ki. 5:20-27).\n\n37. **Blindness of the Syrian Army:** cause—prayer of Elisha; remedy—prayer of Elisha; result—healing (2 Ki. 6:13-23).\n\n38. **Sickness of Ben-Hadad:** cause—unknown; remedy—unknown; result—might have recovered if he had not been murdered according to prophecy (2 Ki. 8:10-15).\n\n39. **Sickness of Jehoram:** cause—unknown; remedy—unknown; result—recovered but slain by Jehu (2 Ki. 9:14-29).\n\n40. **Sickness of Elisha:** cause—unknown, but could have been old age, for he was nearly 100 years old; remedy—unknown, but if the cause was old age, there could be no remedy; result—death (2 Ki. 13:14-20).\n\n41. **Death of an Israelite:** cause—unknown; remedy—touching the bones of Elisha; result—resurrection and healing (2 Ki. 13:21).\n\n42. **Uzziah's Leprosy:** cause—pride and usurping the priesthood; remedy—none because of judgment; result—no healing (2 Ki. 15:5; 2 Chron. 26:16-21).\n\n43. **Sickness of Hezekiah:** cause—pride and unthankfulness to God (2 Chron. 32:25-31); remedy—prayer and humiliation (2 Ki. 20:3; Isa. 38:2-3); result—healed and fifteen years added to his life (Isa. 39:5).\n\n44. **Jehoram's Bowel Trouble:** cause—many sins (2 Chron. 21:6-7; 10:13; 2 Ki. 8:18-19); remedy—none because of judgment; result—death (2 Chron. 21:12-20).\n\n45. **Plague in Israel:** cause—many sins; remedy—none because of judgment; result—many killed (2 Chron. 21:11-15).\n\n46. **All Sicknesses of Israel:** cause—breaking the law in eating of the Passover unworthily (2 Chron. 30:18); remedy—prayer and confession of sin; result—healing (2 Chron. 30:18-20).\n\n47. **Job's Boils:** cause—Satan (Job 1:6-22; 2:1-10); remedy—prayer and humiliation (Job 42:6-17); result—healing and prosperity (Job 42:6-17; Jas. 5:11).\n\n48. **David's Venereal Disease:** cause—sin (Ps. 38:3); remedy—prayer and confession of sin (Ps. 38:1-22); result—healed (Ps. 6:2; 30:2; 103:3).\n\n49. **Famine in Israel:** (Jer. 14:18); cause—sin and rebellion (Jer. 14:7, 10-22; Deut. 28:15-68); remedy—none because of judgment (Jer. 14:12-18); result—death and captivity (Jer. 14:11-18).\n\n50. **Sickness and Death of Ezekiel's Wife:** cause—made a sign to Israel (Ezek. 24:24); remedy—none; result—death (Ezek. 24:15-27).\n\n51. **Sickness of Daniel:** cause—long fasting; remedy—prayer, rest, and nourishment; result—complete recovery (Dan. 8:27; 10:2-3).\n\n52. **Blindness of Eli:** cause—old age (1 Sam. 4:15).\n\n53. **Blindness of Ahijah:** cause—old age (1 Ki. 14:4).\n\n54. **Blindness of Sodomites:** cause—sin; remedy—none (Gen. 19:1-11).\n\n## V. Cases of Sickness and Healing in the New Testament\n\n1. **Innumerable Cases of Healing by Christ** (Mt. 4:23-24; 8:16-17; 9:35-36; 11:5; 12:15; 14:14, 33-36; 15:30-31; 19:2; 21:14; Mk. 1:32-34, 39; 3:10-12; 6:5, 55-56; Lk. 4:40-41; 6:17-19; 7:21-23; 9:11; Jn. 2:23; 20:30-31; 21:25); cause—the devil and demons (Acts 10:38; 1 Jn. 3:8; Mk. 5; 16:17; Lk. 13:14); remedy—faith and the anointing with the Holy Spirit (Isa. 11:2; 42:1-7; 61:1-2; Mt. 12:22-32; Acts 10:38); result—healing in every case (Acts 10:35; Mt. 4:23-24; 8:16-17; 9:35; 12:15; 14:14, 35-36; 15:30-31; 19:2; 21:14).\n\n2. **Innumerable Cases of Healing by Believers Before Pentecost** (Mt. 10:1-7; Mk. 6:7-13; 9:38; Lk. 9:1-6; 10:1-20); cause—the devil and demons (Mt. 10:1-7; Lk. 10:1-20; Acts 10:38); remedy—faith and temporary enduement of power for the one mission representing Christ from city to city (Mt. 10:1-20; Lk. 10:1-20); result—healing in every case while on this one mission (Mk. 6:7-13; Lk. 10:1-20). The failure in Mt. 17:14-21 was after they had come back from this mission and before their permanent enduement of power at Pentecost.\n\n3. **Innumerable Cases of Healing by Believers After Pentecost** (Mk. 16:17-20; Acts 2:43; 5:12-16; 6:8; 8:7-13; 11:19-22; 14:3, 27; 15:4, 12; 19:11-12; 28:9; Rom. 15:18-19, 29; 1 Cor. 16:10; Heb. 2:3-4); cause—the devil and demons (Mk. 16:17; Acts 5:16; 8:6-7; 10:38; 19:11-12); remedy—faith and the anointing with the Holy Spirit (Lk. 24:49; Jn. 14:12-15; Acts 1:8; 3:6; 6:8; 19:12; 1 Cor. 12:7-11, 28-31); result—healing in every case (Jn. 14:12; Acts 5:12-16; 19:11-12; Heb. 2:3-4; Mk. 16:20).\n\n4. **Ten Cases of Demon-Possession Healed by Christ and Paul, and Others:**\n\n> (1) A maniac of Gergesa (Mt. 8:28-34)\n\n> (2) A maniac of Gadera (Mk. 5:1-21; Lk. 8:26-39)\n\n> (3) A dumb man (Mt. 9:32-34)\n\n> (4) A blind and dumb man (Mt. 12:22-32)\n\n> (5) An epileptic girl (Mt. 15:21-28)\n\n> (6) An epileptic boy (Mk. 9:17-29)\n\n> (7) A maniac (Mk. 1:23-28; Lk. 4:21)\n\n> (8) Mary Magdalene (Lk. 7:36-50; 8:2)\n\n> (9) An infirm woman (Lk. 13:16-17)\n\n> (10) A fortuneteller (Acts 16:16-24)\n\n5. **Twenty-Nine Cases of Healing by Christ, Peter, John, and Paul:**\n\n> (1) A leper (Mt. 8:1-4; Mk. 1:20)\n\n> (2) A centurion's servant (Mt. 8:5-13)\n\n> (3) Peter's mother-in-law (Mt. 8:14)\n\n> (4) A woman (Mt. 9:18-26; Mk. 5:22)\n\n> (5) Two blind men (Mt. 9:27-31)\n\n> (6) A withered hand (Mt. 12:9-14)\n\n> (7) Two blind men (Mt. 20:29-34)\n\n> (8) A deaf-dumb man (Mk. 7:31-37)\n\n> (9) A blind man (Mk. 8:22-26)\n\n> (10) A man with dropsy (Lk. 14:1-6)\n\n> (11) Ten lepers (Lk. 17:11-19)\n\n> (12) A nobleman's son (Jn. 4:46-54)\n\n> (13) An infirm man (Jn. 5:1-15)\n\n> (14) A blind man (Jn. 9:1-38)\n\n> (15) A lame man (Acts 3:1–4:22)\n\n> (16) A paralyzed man (Acts 9:32-35)\n\n> (17) A lame man (Acts 14:9-10)\n\n> (18) Father of Publius (Acts 28:7-9)\n\n6. **Six Cases of Resurrection from the Dead by Jesus, Peter, Paul, and Others:**\n\n> (1) Jairus' daughter (Mt. 9:18-26)\n\n> (2) A widow's son (Lk. 7:11-18)\n\n> (3) Lazarus (Jn. 11:1-44)\n\n> (4) Tabitha (Acts 9:36-42)\n\n> (5) Paul (Acts 14:19-20)\n\n> (6) Eutychus (Acts 20:7-12)\n\n7. **A Paralyzed Man:** cause—sin; remedy—faith in Christ; result—healing (Mt. 9:1-12; Mk. 2:3-12; Lk. 5:18-26).\n\n8. **Malchus:** cause—ear severed by Peter's sword (Jn. 18:10); remedy—power of Jesus; result—healed (Lk. 22:50-51).\n\n9. **Sickness and Death of Herod:** cause—pride and self-exaltation; remedy—none because of judgment; result—eaten by worms (Acts 12:20-25).\n\n10. **Blindness of Bar-Jesus:** cause—unbelief and rebellion against the gospel; remedy—none until Paul's curse was over (Acts 13:6-12).\n\n11. **Paul's Snakebite:** cause—accident; remedy—faith in Jesus Christ; result—healing (Acts 28:1-6).\n\nImmunity from poisons and snake bites is promised all believers (Mk. 16:17-20; Lk. 10:19; Ps. 91). This does not mean, however, that believers can tempt God and get away with deliberately drinking poison or getting bitten by snakes, as some foolish and ignorant people do today.\n\nThe Greek word for "take up" in Mk. 16:18 is *airo*. The way it's used shows its true meaning. It's translated "take away" when speaking of Christ taking away the sins of the world (Jn. 1:29, 1 Jn. 3:5); of branches being cut off the vine (Jn. 15:2); of the bodies of Christ and the thieves being removed from the crosses (Jn. 19:31, 38); of a fornicator being excommunicated from the Corinthian church (1 Cor. 5:2); of taking a man to prison (Mt. 22:13); of the removal of a stone from Lazarus' grave (Jn. 11:39); of the destruction of a nation (Jn. 11:48); and of the removal of knowledge and judgment (Lk. 11:52; Acts 8:35).\n\nIt's also translated "put away" concerning removing sin from one's life (Eph. 4:31); "took it out of the way" referring to abolishing the Law of Moses on the cross (Col. 2:14-17); "remove" referring to a literal mountain being cast into the sea (Mt. 21:21; Mk. 11:23); and "away with" referring to the destruction or murder of Jesus and Paul (Lk. 23:18; Jn. 19:15; Acts 21:36; 22:22).\n\nIt's never used for making a sideshow of the thing being removed or destroyed. So modern snake-handling fanaticism is not biblical. If people would remove snakes like Paul did—the only New Testament example of how to "take up" snakes—then it would be biblical. Paul shook the viper into the fire and destroyed it (Acts 28:1-6).\n\nIf people would use power—if they have real power from God—to heal the sick and do something truly worthwhile for humanity, they'd show some intelligence and be a blessing. You can go to India and other heathen lands and see better demonstrations of snake handling than from modern religious fanatics who make a sideshow of the gospel idea of power over all the power of the devil, as taught in Lk. 10:19 and Ps. 91. These passages simply mean that believers can have power over all enemies—but they never mean to bring reproach upon\n\n---\n\nGod's will for your body is the same as His will for your soul: wholeness, not affliction. The atonement secured both. The question is not whether God is willing — Scripture settles that. The question is whether you are. So here's what to consider honestly: Is there a sickness or physical condition in your life that you've accepted as God's will without ever seriously bringing it before Him in faith and claiming the provision of Isaiah 53?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk4bq0025ewhjx9x6vp21	cmnknk4bp001qewhjk5bcqnfj	Supplement 23 — For Lessons 45 & 46	SUPPLEMENT	1489	14	\N	t	The woman, the dragon, the manchild, the beast, the false prophet — these are not fantasy characters. They're the final cast of characters in the most serious drama in human history. This supplement helps you see the Tribulation period clearly: who's involved, what's happening, and what it means for how you live and prepare today.\n\nIf you're willing to study God's promises and build your faith to receive what Christ has already provided through redemption, you can break free from these afflictions. Sadly, many Christians endure these curses unnecessarily—often because of false teaching.\n\n![Supplement 23 — For Lessons 45 & 46](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsE5xgaJtdLWqJHyu8Q5MfmIRAjonSBKDvC0V2)\n\n## The Reason for Failure Among Christians\n\nWe've emphasized repeatedly that Christians suffer many things they don't have to because of unbelief and willful rejection of plain truth. If you continue rebelling against the very truth that would set you free, no one can help you (Jn. 8:31-34).\n\nHere's what needs to change: you need to lay aside those unreasonable, unscriptural theories—ideas like "it's God's will for me to live in defeat," or "I should be resigned to suffer the devil's work because others have suffered," or "I don't have the faith and power to help myself," or "it's just so hard to get things from God." Once you let go of these lies, you'll find yourself on the victorious side of life.\n\nIf you would quit cooperating with Satan and turn fully to God, refuse to be satisfied with the devil's work in your life, and try half as hard to have faith as you currently try to maintain unbelief, everything would change. If you'd set aside the many man-made fallacies that lead to defeat and seek excuses to receive God's benefits half as much as you seek ways to excuse your failures, you'd experience the glorious benefits of the gospel that are ready for all who believe.\n\n## Know the Truth That Sets Men Free\n\nJesus said to those who believed on Him, "If ye continue in my word, then are ye my disciples indeed; and ye shall know the truth, and the truth shall make you free . . . If the Son therefore shall make you free, ye shall be free indeed" (Jn. 8:31-36).\n\nThe first step is becoming acquainted with the truth. That's exactly what we've been making clear throughout these lessons and supplements—that salvation from sin, sickness, poverty, and all the works of the devil is the heritage of every child of God.\n\nIf you never learn this and freely accept it as truth, you cannot be helped to full freedom in Christ. As long as you believe and accept lies as truth, you'll remain bound and defeated. Think of it like this: just as truth makes you free, lies bind you. Just as truth builds up faith, lies tear it down and instill unbelief.\n\nFalse theories will never set you free because they keep you constantly questioning God's will. They produce fear, unbelief, doubt, and failure. They become a constant source of worry about whether you're on the right track.\n\nWhen you find yourself in such a state, it's clear evidence that you're following false theories instead of truth. But when you know the truth, you become settled in life and consecrated to obey it. You develop deep convictions about your duty toward God and man. Your actions become based on truth, and you're set free from worry, fear, constant complaining, doubting, and the unsettled conditions that caused so many failures before you knew the truth.\n\n## Is it God's Will to Save Souls Today?\n\nThere's not much argument about this question today. Most people in our land believe that Jesus Christ died to save all men from their sins.\n\nSome argue about whether people can really get a "know-so" salvation—the kind that actually saves them from the power and dominion of sin. But this cannot be disputed with Scripture. The Bible makes it very clear that God does save men from sin and keeps them from going back into the sin-business, provided they cooperate with Him.\n\n## Is it God's Will to Heal Sick Bodies Today?\n\nThe great question for many is whether God wills to heal today. The question isn't really about whether He *can* heal, but whether it's His *will* to heal.\n\nWhy anyone would question God's willingness to heal is beyond me, but doubt seems to arise whenever someone has been taught false concepts about God and His love. Any theory questioning God's will to heal should never have been stated in the first place.\n\nHere's the truth: the Bible not only teaches that it's God's will to heal, but that He will heal all men, in any age, who come to Him through Jesus Christ and have faith, nothing wavering. One of the main reasons God doesn't heal more often is that people don't understand it's His will to heal. God has made full provision to heal all who come to Him in faith.\n\nAs long as you doubt God's will in the matter of healing, you certainly won't receive it. James said, "Let him ask in faith, nothing wavering. For he that wavereth is like a wave of the sea driven with the wind and tossed. For let not that man think that he shall receive anything of the Lord" (Jas. 1:5-8).\n\nIsn't it clear from this passage what's required if you want answers to prayer? Do you really believe God will break His own law to answer you when you doubt? Do you think God is going to make a special pet of you and do what He said He wouldn't do?\n\nLet's be honest here: if you don't get an answer to prayer, it's because you don't believe. It's because you waver.\n\n## Is it God's Will to Prosper Men Materially?\n\nIn previous supplements and lessons, we've proven that it's God's will for men to be prosperous in material things. Scripture makes this absolutely clear, as shown in the Bible passages given in Lesson Ten and Supplement Six.\n\nSo we see from Scripture that it's undoubtedly God's will—not only for men to be saved from sin and free from bad habits—but also to be free from pain, sickness, poverty, and want. All these benefits of the gospel are for those who believe, but nothing is promised to the unbeliever. God is proving to people in all walks of life that these blessings are for them.\n\n## Sickness and Hindrances to Prayer the Work of Satan\n\nSatan is declared to be the source of sin, sickness, and oppression in the human race (Acts 10:38; Lk. 13:16; Jn. 10:10; 1 Jn. 3:8). It's the devil's work to cause men to sin and make them sick. It's Jesus' work to give life and give it more abundantly (Jn. 10:10). It's His work to save from sin (Mt. 1:21; Rom. 1:16; 8:1-13).\n\nThe devil also works to hinder prayer, preventing you from getting what you want in life. His work isn't just making people sin and get sick—he also causes them to fail in business and in everything they undertake. That Satan can hinder your prayers and the success of your work is clearly stated in Scripture (1 Chron. 21:1; Zech. 3:1; Rom. 15:22; Eph. 4:27; 6:10-18; 2 Cor. 10:4-7; 1 Thess. 2:18; 1 Pet. 3:7).\n\nWhen you get sick or are hindered in doing any good thing, you can rely on it—Satan is at the root of the trouble. If you would only realize this and immediately go to God for help and power to overcome these attacks, you wouldn't go through long sieges of defeat.\n\nWhen you delay resisting these powers, you give them time and opportunity to gain a strong foothold, making them more difficult to dislodge. It's similar to how a small fire can be easily extinguished, but if you wait, it grows into an inferno that's much harder to control.\n\nIn sickness, rebuke demons at once and healing will come. In hindrances to prayer, resist them from the first sign and your prayers will soon be answered. At any sign of business failure, go to God in earnest prayer, commit the matter to Him, and ask for help and guidance. God can and will undertake and correct the trouble. He can and will work out problems that you could never solve on your own.\n\nWhatever the unseen force is that hinders the progress of any good thing, it's the work of demon powers seeking to destroy the good being done. If they can't stop your good work altogether, they'll seek to hinder it as much as possible and discourage you from further attempts.\n\n## Demon Opposition and Oppression Are Real\n\nThe weakness of the average Christian in opposing demon powers is largely caused by failure to recognize the source of the trouble. Most Christians lack true understanding about this matter, leaving them helpless and confused in their warfare against demons.\n\nSo many times they conclude—without thinking scripturally—that God must be causing the trouble. They become resigned to defeat because they're consecrated enough that they don't want to fight against God.\n\nIf you would only use your intelligence in spiritual warfare like you do in everyday challenges, you would come out victorious and God would be glorified.\n\nJesus recognized that all sickness, opposition to truth, and defeat in life came from satanic forces fighting against the saints. He Himself was tempted by Satan for forty days and nights (Mt. 4:1-11). He always rebuked demons when healing people of various physical and mental troubles (Mt. 4:23-24; 8:28-34; 9:32-34; 10:17; 12:22-32; Lk. 18; Acts 10:38).\n\nJesus recognized satanic influence in Peter when Peter tried to stop Him from going to the cross (Mt. 16:21-23). He predicted that the Church would have to war against the gates of Hell, but would prevail (Mt. 16:17-20). He spoke of power to bind and loose, which must have referred to demon powers (Mt. 16:18-19). He spoke of Satan as the one who desired to destroy Peter at the time of the denial (Lk. 22:31-34). It was Satan who caused Judas to betray Christ (Jn. 13:2, 27).\n\nThe apostles taught the reality of satanic opposition to the gospel (Acts 5:3; 8:5-12; 13:10). Paul and others spoke of Satan as the great enemy of the gospel, stating that his devices were well-known by saints who fight him intelligently (Rom. 16:20; 1 Cor. 5:5; 7:5; 2 Cor. 2:11; 10:3-6; 11:13-15; Eph. 2:1-3; 4:27; 6:10-18; 1 Tim. 5:15; Jas. 4:7; 1 Pet. 5:8-9; Rev. 12:10-12).\n\nDemons are the great propagators of false doctrines (1 Tim. 4:1-8; 2 Thess. 2:8-12; Rev. 12:9). There are many kinds of demons that cause different sicknesses, as we've seen in Lesson Six, Point VII. The Bible also reveals that men enter into spiritual worship with demons (1 Cor. 10:16-22; Rev. 9:21).\n\n## How to Overcome Demon Powers\n\nDemons are disembodied spirits, and a legion can inhabit one man (Mk. 5). One or many can enter a person and gain control (Mt. 12:43-45). They enter an individual:\n\n- Through sin (Jn. 5:14)\n- Through failure to resist them (Eph. 4:27; Jas. 4:7)\n- Through failure to accept God's protection (1 Pet. 5:8-9)\n- Through some weakness in the human body or failure to keep God in your life (1 Sam. 16:14-15)\n- Through selfishness and yielding to demons (Prov. 4:14; Lk. 11:24-26)\n\nIf you want to overcome demon powers, first learn your true condition and acknowledge they're causing you trouble. Renounce all sins that cause weakness in resisting demons. Accept God's help by turning wholly to God (Acts 26:18).\n\nRefuse to give place to the devil (Eph. 4:27). Resist the devil steadfast in the faith, and put on the whole armor of God to quench all the fiery darts of the wicked (Eph. 6:10-18; 1 Pet. 5:8-9; Jas. 4:7).\n\nNo demon power can stand against the name of Jesus Christ or faith in the blood of Christ. It was on the cross that all principalities and powers of darkness were defeated, and it's through faith in Christ's work that personal victory over such powers can be gained (Col. 2:14-17).\n\nFaith comes by hearing, and hearing comes by the Word of God (Rom. 10:17). You must fully understand the reality of demons and the truth about their work in human lives. You must also understand Christ's work in destroying the works of the devil (1 Jn. 3:8; Jn. 10:10-18; Col. 2:14-17).\n\nAs long as you're ignorant about these truths, you're at a disadvantage in waging successful war against them. Review Lesson Six for better knowledge of demon powers, then take a definite stand against such power working in your life. All efforts of demons to work in your body, soul, and spirit can be defeated through cooperation with God and through the gospel.\n\n## Why Some Fail to Get Deliverance\n\nSome people get immediate and permanent deliverance from sin, bad habits, sickness, and other troubles the first time they pray and turn to God. Others seem to have an indefinite struggle with certain conditions. There's always a reason for this difference.\n\nThose who get deliverance easily simply believe the gospel and make a full and complete surrender to God. There's no reservation—no holding back anything they know should be given up. There's no hesitation about meeting the conditions plainly laid down in Scripture.\n\nThey don't even think of holding on to some sin or bad habit, and they don't concern themselves with the desire to do so. They're completely submissive to God's will. Their consecration is complete and definite enough to rid themselves of everything that would hinder God from answering their prayers.\n\nThose constantly struggling for complete victory over sin will find they're trying to hold on to some habit, or they're unwilling to forgive someone, or they're not willing to give up everything for God. They've made certain mental reservations and have limited God and His promises in their own hearts.\n\nThey think giving up their entire sinful past in a moment of time will be the hardest thing in the world. They're in a state of questioning and doubting from the start. Their faith isn't definite enough to make the full surrender all at once. They don't realize that complete surrender is necessary before they receive full deliverance.\n\nWhen temptation comes, they don't resist boldly and vigorously enough to overcome. To some extent, they reason with the evil spirits seeking to overthrow them. The love of their former pet sins and bad habits hasn't been fully broken, and they hesitate to make a total and eternal break.\n\nPicture it this way: it's like trying to break free from quicksand while still holding onto the very roots that are anchoring you in it.\n\nIf you would resist when temptation first comes, victory would be won. The battle will never be fully won until you renounce all sin and refuse to tolerate being bothered by demons. You cannot play around with sin and temptation and escape yielding to it for long.\n\nTemptation will grow stronger as thoughts are entertained about your former life. If you do have trouble with sin after turning to God, go to Him in earnest prayer. Read the Bible and build up your spiritual life to the point of attaining permanent and complete victory.\n\nConcerning sickness, some get healed immediately and others don't. There must be a cause when healing doesn't come, for there's no respect of persons with God. He will heal and save any and all who come to Him with their whole heart.\n\nThe cause of failure is not in God—it's in the individual. God has plainly stated what the cause is: unbelief. This was the cause in Mt. 17:14-21. This is plainly stated as the cause of failure in all prayer (Jas. 1:4-8; Heb. 11:6).\n\n## True Heart Faith Always Works\n\nIn Lesson Twenty-eight we studied the doctrine of faith and saw there's a difference between mental faith and heart faith. Heart faith is the active faith we've been learning about.\n\nYou can feel like you have lots of faith when you only have mental faith, but that's not enough. You must have true active faith that acts upon God's Word—faith that causes you to act as if prayer has already been answered the moment you pray. This kind of faith won't take no for an answer.\n\nIt won't blame God or try to excuse yourself for failure. It won't question or waver about the answer. It counts things done whether they're seen or not.\n\nMental faith, on the other hand, begins questioning God's will if the answer isn't given immediately. It reasons, wonders, begins to doubt, and constantly seeks ground to stand on—easily reconciled to defeat.\n\nYou can always tell your true state—whether you're exercising true faith—if you'll be honest and consider things in light of the Bible. True faith is taking God at His Word. It maintains that the answer to prayer has been granted regardless of what you see, think, or feel. It doesn't look to circumstances and possibilities in the least.\n\nThink of how Abraham acted: he simply believed God and was fully persuaded by God's Word alone that what God had promised, He was able to perform. Abraham's faith was built upon what God said, not what he could see or feel. He was occupied with what God had said, not what he thought his possibilities were.\n\nHe refused to reduce his confidence in God in the least degree. He held firm to his conviction that God was true to His Word and would do as He promised. He even glorified God before receiving the answer and acted as if the request was already fulfilled.\n\nTrue faith will always bring results. If you intelligently seek to know what God says, base your faith upon the Word of God, and determine to obey the Word of faith to the letter, you will have whatsoever you say (Mt. 21:21-22; Mk. 11:22-24; Jn. 14:12-15; 15:7, 16; 16:23-26).\n\nDon't be discouraged if you don't immediately receive what you ask of God. Keep reading the lessons on faith and prayer. Keep following the instructions in the supplements until your faith is built up to the point where you get what you ask.\n\nIt may take time to build up faith, but it's worth all the work necessary to develop strong, unwavering faith. It will provide rich rewards in the end. So keep up an intelligent program of finding out what to do and how to do it—and all the demons out of the Abyss cannot stop you from getting what you want from God in life.\n\n---\n\n## Ten Questions For Supplement Twenty-three. Do You Know:\n\n- **That there was sin on Earth before Adam and Eve and even before Lucifer's subjects rebelled?** There was sin in Lucifer himself before he caused his disciples to sin. This is revealed in Isa. 14:12-14; Ezek. 28:11-17 where we read of Lucifer being perfect in his ways as ruler of the Earth until iniquity was found in him. Jesus said he sinned in the beginning and was the father of sin (Jn. 8:44; 1 Jn. 3:8; Rev. 12:9; Mt. 25:41). All this happened before Adam and Eve's days, as proven in Lesson Seven.\n\n- **That the devil is a son of God?** He's a son of God by creation, like all the angels and Adam (Job 1:6; 2:1; 38:4-7; Lk. 3:38). He couldn't cease being a son of God, for then he would have to be uncreated—which is impossible. That he was a created being is clear from Ezek. 28:11-17; Col. 1:16-18. Sonship means nothing when sin is committed. God will damn His own sons when they rebel past reconciliation. This is clear because Hell is prepared for the devil and his angels, who are all created sons of God (Mt. 25:41, 46).\n\n- **That the Lord calls women to preach?** Currently there's major controversy about whether God calls women to preach, but Scripture clears this up if we believe what it says. In the Gospels, several women messengers proclaimed the good news (Mt. 28:1-10; Lk. 24:9-11; Jn. 4:28-30; 20:16-18). In Acts 2:16-21 we have a prediction that God would pour out His Spirit in the last days "upon all flesh; and your sons and daughters shall prophesy, and your young men shall see visions, and your old men shall dream dreams; and upon my servants and on my handmaidens I will pour out of my Spirit and they shall prophesy." Prophesying is explained in 1 Cor. 14:3-4 as "speaking unto men to edification, and exhortation, and comfort." The following passages prove to any unbiased mind that prophesying is for the Church (1 Cor. 12:1-31; 14:1-6, 12, 24-26, 29-33). In Acts 21:8-9 we read that Philip had four daughters who prophesied in the early Church. In Rom. 16 we find Phebe, Priscilla, Mary, Junia, Tryphena, Tryphosa, Persis—all female names—mentioned as laborers in the gospel. In Phil. 4:2-3, Euodias and Syntyche, two women, are mentioned as leaders in the local church at Philippi, referred to as "those women which labored with me in the gospel." Paul speaks of Corinthian women prophesying and praying in church (1 Cor. 11:4; 14:1-5, 23-26). When Paul said for women to "keep silence in the church," he wasn't speaking of women prophesying or praying, but referred to asking their husbands certain things at church. He said, "if they will learn anything, let them ask their husbands at home" and not in church (1 Cor. 14:34-35). But that they could speak, prophesy, and pray in church is clear from 1 Cor. 11:4; 14:3-4, 23-26; Acts 2:16-21. Women have been used by God in all ages (Exod. 15:20; Judg. 4:4; 2 Ki. 22:14; 2 Chron. 34:22; Neh. 6:14; Isa. 8:3; Lk. 1:39-56; 2:36). To say the least, God is sovereign and can use a woman when He pleases without asking any man whom He should use. Besides, if more men would obey God today, it might not be necessary to use so many women to preach.\n\n- **That literal angels will come down from the heavenlies to live among men in the future tribulation?** This will happen when Satan and his angels are cast out of the heavenlies to Earth to be among men for 3½ years (Rev. 12:7-12). The devil and his angels will cause the great woe of Rev. 12:12. The devil will likely come down visibly to men at this time—otherwise they wouldn't know he's among them in a different sense than now. As we've seen in Lesson Six, angels have appeared to men in visible form, which is no doubt what will happen then. God's angels will also be seen and heard visibly by men at this time, as proven in Rev. 14:6-11.\n\n- **That men cannot be saved after taking the mark of the beast?** This is plainly stated in Rev. 14:9-11, which says that when men take the mark, they're doomed to eternal torment—so they cannot be saved and still go to eternal Hell.\n\n- **That one-third of men on Earth will be destroyed in one hour?** This will happen under the sixth trumpet judgment when 200 million demon horsemen will be liberated from the Abyss to slay one-third of men at a certain hour (Rev. 9:12-21).\n\n- **That many men now living on Earth will personally stand before Christ to be judged before they die?** This will take place at the judgment of the living nations at Christ's second advent (Mt. 25:31-46). At that time many will be destroyed in eternal Hell (Mt. 25:41, 46; 13:40-42; 24:51). That we're living at the very end of this age, during the last generation before the second advent, is clear. Many signs point to Christ's soon return, and many prophecies are now being fulfilled, as we've seen in Lesson Forty-four. Many people living today will be alive when Christ comes to set up His kingdom—certainly many children now being born.\n\n- **That the dead are now in a conscious state between death and resurrection?** We've abundantly explained this in Lesson Thirty-seven, which can also be seen in Lk. 16:19-31; Rev. 6:9-11; Isa. 14:9; Ps. 16:10; 1 Pet. 3:19; Heb. 12:23; Mt. 17:3-5; etc.\n\n- **That none of the 144,000 Jews of Rev. 7 and 14 will ever die?** They will be translated to Heaven as the manchild of Rev. 12:5 without seeing death. They're all living Jews who will be taken up to Heaven from Israel when Antichrist breaks his covenant with Israel in the middle of Daniel's Seventieth Week, as we've seen in Lesson Forty-five.\n\n- **That it is not a sin today to eat pork?** Unclean animals were forbidden in the Law of Moses, but this law has been done away with, as proven in Lessons Seventeen and Thirty-two. Now under the New Testament, men are permitted to eat any animal, as proven in the following passages (Rom. 14:1-23; Col. 2:14-17; 1 Tim. 4:1-8).\n\n---\n\nThe Tribulation is real, and its cast of characters is already being prepared. The dragon has great wrath. The beast rises through deception. The false prophet gives religion to evil power. So here's the question: Are you developing the spiritual discernment that will help you recognize counterfeit authority — in theology, in politics, in spirituality — in the time before these events culminate? And is your discernment rooted in Scripture or in your gut?	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk4br0028ewhjjszyvrob	cmnknk4bp001qewhjk5bcqnfj	Supplement 24 — For Lessons 47 & 48	SUPPLEMENT	1922	17	\N	t	The beast with seven heads and ten horns is the final form of Gentile world power — and the ten-horn confederacy gives the Antichrist his global platform. This supplement works through the political and prophetic details so you can understand what you're watching when you see global power structures shifting.\n\n![Supplement 24 — For Lessons 47 & 48](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OsJ2fEwx4ZDlMH1zAnxErj0PQYB2bIe8GdaVWL)\n\n## Is Healing for the Body in the Atonement?\n\nMany modern preachers argue that bodily healing isn't included in the atonement. One writer puts it this way:\n\n> "This chapter is written to help the Christian who is seeking light on the subject of healing, and to expose the error which is taught by so-called divine healers. The author does not limit the power of God, nor does he deny the fact that at times God does heal a Christian. We believe that God heals whom He wills, when He wills, and how He wills. We do not believe that healing is in the atonement in the sense that each time a Christian is sick God must heal him and raise him up. God does not always heal and raise up Christians who are sick. It is not always the will of God to heal a Christian. If healing were in the atonement, a Christian would never die... There is not one verse in all of God's Word where atonement was made for sickness."\n\nLet's be direct here — these statements are completely unscriptural and misleading for anyone genuinely seeking truth about healing. The whole tone of this theory destroys faith in God and His Word.\n\nThink about what such statements actually imply: they make God out to be a liar, they make Him a respecter of persons, they limit His power to only a select few whom He chooses to heal at certain times, and they turn Bible promises into empty words and the atonement into a sham.\n\nAnd that argument about Christians never dying if healing is in the atonement? That shows a willful ignorance of the truth. Here's the thing — Christ atoned for the sins of the whole world, so by that same logic, we could argue that no sinner will ever be lost. Just because atonement is made doesn't mean everyone automatically receives its benefits.\n\nThink of it like a trust fund set up for you by a wealthy relative. The money is there, it's real, it's yours — but you still have to go to the bank and claim it. One must appropriate the blessings of atonement, and this is exactly why some people remain unsaved while others aren't healed or blessed — they haven't claimed by faith what Christ freely provided.\n\n## Atonement is Made for Both Sin and Sickness\n\nScripture couldn't be clearer on this point. Every prayer answered and every benefit received through faith comes because of Christ's atoning work. All faith in God springs from hearing His Word, and God's Word has always been rooted in the highest good for everyone, as well as in Christ's redemptive work.\n\nThe entire Bible tells one story: creation, Fall, and then redemption of humanity through Jesus Christ. God doesn't do anything based on our own merits — He does everything because of His grace that flows through Christ.\n\nThe Old Testament revealed faith in the coming Christ through animal sacrifices. In Gen. 3:21; 4:1-7; Heb. 11:4, we find the first recorded instances of blood being shed in faith, pointing forward to Christ — the Seed of the woman who would crush the serpent's head and restore humanity's dominion.\n\nFrom those first records throughout the Old Testament, people continued offering animal blood as evidence of their faith in Christ (Gen. 8:20; 12:8; 13:4, 18; 15:9-12; etc.). These altars where blood was shed stood as testimony that without the shedding of blood there can be no remission of sin (Heb. 9:22).\n\n## Sickness is the Result of Sin\n\nAll of Scripture makes clear that if sin had never entered the world, there would be no sickness — death came through sin (Rom. 5:12-21). When all sin is finally removed from the Earth, sickness will be gone forever (Rev. 21:1-7).\n\nSickness has always resulted from sin — not always from the personal sin of the sick person, but because sin exists in the world. This is explained in Lesson Fourteen, Point VI, and illustrated by many cases of sickness in Points IV and V of that same lesson.\n\nGod stated plainly in His covenants with Israel that sickness would come when sin is committed (Exod. 15:26; Deut. 28:15-68; 30:15-20). Plague after plague has struck humanity because of sin, as we see throughout Lesson Fourteen.\n\n## Remedies for Sickness Are Prayer and Atonement\n\nAs we've discussed before, all prayer is based on redemptive work. God doesn't promise to hear prayer except when it's offered in faith based on His Word and grace through Jesus Christ (Jn. 1:17; 3:14-16; Acts 3:16; Rom. 8:32; Gal. 3:6-29; Eph. 1:7; Col. 1:14-23).\n\nScripture records many instances of atonement being made during times of sickness (Exod. 32:1-35; Num. 16:21-30, 46-50; Num. 21:7-9 with Jn. 3:14-16; 1 Sam. 5:1–6:21; 2 Sam. 24:10-25). See "22 Proofs that Bodily Healing is in the Atonement" in Lesson Fourteen, point VII.\n\n## Jesus Bore Both Sin and Sickness in His Body On the Cross\n\nSince sickness entered the world as a result of sin, there can be no true remedy outside of Christ's redemption. Satan and demons propagate sickness (Acts 10:38). Nature has certain healing capabilities, and doctors can assist nature in many ways.\n\nBut when disease gets beyond what nature and medicine can handle, God must supernaturally intervene or the person will die. Honest physicians admit they can't actually heal — they can only assist nature. Drugs can destroy germs, and when that happens, people naturally get better.\n\nHowever, certain diseases have no medical cure. No single drug has been developed that cures every disease or kills every germ. Sometimes a physician's help simply isn't enough.\n\nOn the other hand, God can kill every germ, cure every disease, and completely repair the body regardless of the problem. But God will do this only on the basis of atonement — when proper prayer, confession of sin, and faith in Christ's blood has been exercised.\n\nThe following passages prove Christ bore the sins and sicknesses of all people in His body on the cross:\n\n> "They brought unto him many that were possessed with devils: and he cast out the spirits with his word, and healed all that were sick: That it might be fulfilled which was spoken by Isaiah the prophet, saying, himself took our infirmities, and bare our sicknesses" (Mt. 8:16-17).\n\nThis quote comes from Isa. 53, which according to the literal text reads:\n\n> "He was despised and forsaken of men; a man of pains, and acquainted with sickness... Surely he hath borne our sicknesses, and carried our pains, yet we did esteem him violently beaten, slain of God, and degraded. But he was slain for our crime, he was beat to pieces for our guilt; the corrections of our peace was upon him; and with his wounds we are healed... yet it pleased the Lord to beat him to pieces; he hath made him sick: when thou wilt make his soul an offering for sin" (Isa. 53:3-5, 10).\n\nAccording to Dr. Young, this passage reads:\n\n> "A man of pains and acquainted with sickness... Surely our sicknesses he hath borne, and our pains he hath carried them, and we—we have esteemed him plagued, smitten of God and afflicted... and by his bruise there is healing for us... And Jehovah hath delighted to bruise him; he hath made him sick."\n\nPeter expresses the same truth:\n\n> "Who his own self bare our sins in his own body on the tree, that we, being dead to sin, should live unto righteousness by whose stripes ye were healed" (1 Pet. 2:24).\n\nIf these Scriptures mean anything, they mean that on the cross Christ healed all who will believe. He took our pains and sicknesses in His own body on the tree so we wouldn't have to continue in them after we're redeemed. He was bruised, beaten, and wounded to heal our bodies. Healing is in the atonement.\n\nAll of our sins and sicknesses were forgiven and healed on the cross, and forgiveness and healing flow from the cross to all who believe.\n\nThe Hebrew word for "griefs" in Isa. 53:3-4 is *choli*. It's translated "disease" (2 Ki. 1:2; 8:8-9; 2 Chron. 16:12; 21:18; Job. 30:18; Ps. 38:7; Eccl. 6:2); "sick" (Isa. 1:5); "sickness" (Deut. 7:15; 28:61; 1 Ki. 17:17; 2 Ki. 13:14; 2 Chron. 21:15-19; Ps. 41:3; Eccl. 5:17; Isa. 38:9, 12; Hos. 5:13); and "sicknesses" (Deut. 28:59).\n\nThe root word from which *choli* comes is the common word for "sickness" and is rendered "sick" 34 times. It's never used of spiritual sickness — which some preachers claim is the only kind of sickness the atonement covers (Gen. 48:1; 1 Sam. 30:13; 1 Ki. 14:1; 2 Ki. 1:2; 8:7; 13:14; etc.).\n\nThe Hebrew word for "sorrows" in Isa. 53:3-4 is *makob*. It's translated "pain" (Job 33:19; Jer. 51:3); "grief" (2 Chron. 6:29; Ps. 69:26); and "sorrows" (Ps. 32:10; 38:17; Eccl. 1:18; 2:23; Jer. 30:15; 45:3; Lam. 1:12, 18; Exod. 3:7; Isa. 53:3-4). *Makob* comes from the root word *kaab*, meaning "to feel pain" (Job 14:22) and "to be sore" (Gen. 34:25; Job. 5:18).\n\nThe Hebrew words for "borne" and "carried" in Isa. 53:4 mean to "lift up, to convey, to carry away, remove, to take upon one's self the responsibility of, and to suffer that which one takes the responsibility to carry."\n\nThis certainly teaches that Christ took in His own body on the cross the sufferings caused by humanity's sin and rebellion. He became sin for our sin that we might be saved from our sins. He became sick for our sickness that we might be healed of all our diseases.\n\nTo teach that healing isn't in the atonement is to make the cross of Christ worthless as far as saving people from the sufferings caused by sin. He became our substitute for sickness just as He did for sin. He removed both sins and sicknesses in His own body on the cross so we might be free from both.\n\nThis is why it's just as easy to heal as it is to forgive sins — both were atoned for and borne by Christ so they could be completely removed from us.\n\nIf someone could prove Christ didn't bear our sicknesses in the atonement, they could also prove He didn't bear our sins in the atonement. Both our sins and sicknesses were borne by Christ on the cross at the same time.\n\nWhat does the atonement cover if not sins and sicknesses? This prophecy in Isa. 53 teaches the same substitutionary and expiatory work of Christ for sicknesses that it does for sins. We can teach with full authority that healing of the body is in the atonement.\n\nThe fact that both Matthew and Peter quote Isa. 53 in connection with physical healing proves the doctrine of bodily healing is in the atonement. Both writers claim it was our sins and our sicknesses that Christ bore. When someone takes upon Himself what another should bear, that's substitution.\n\nBearing diseases, therefore, is part of Christ's atoning work. This reveals that Christ is the Saviour of the body as well as the soul and spirit. Healing of the body is in God's will and plan, and it's the right of every believer in the atonement.\n\nIt's the duty of every God-called preacher to declare that he believes in healing and then practice it by praying for the sick to be healed just as much as he prays for sinners to be saved.\n\n## No Christian Needs to be Sick\n\nIn teaching this doctrine, we don't have to conclude — as the minister mentioned above does — that if healing is in the atonement, a Christian would never die. It's true that no Christian needs to be sick, but it's not true that death only comes through dreadful disease.\n\nUnder God's plan, people will continue to die until the rapture, when believers will be changed without experiencing death (Heb. 9:27; 1 Cor. 15:21-58). People can die — and should die — free from the diseases Christ bore for them.\n\nScripture gives many examples of people dying without having a disease. The first two people who died didn't die of disease (Gen. 4:8, 23). People can live long lives and die of old age without any disease or pain.\n\nThis is how Sarah (Gen. 23:1-2), Abraham (Gen. 25:7-8), Ishmael (Gen. 25:17), Isaac (Gen. 35:27-29), Moses (Deut. 34:7), and many other Bible characters died. Today, some still die of old age without disease or pain.\n\nLong life and health are promised to all believers (Ps. 91; Isa. 58; Mt. 21:21-22; Mk. 9:23; 11:22-24; Jn. 14:12-15; 15:7, 16; 1 Pet. 3:10-11).\n\nIt's similar to fruit on a tree — it's no more necessary for a person to die of sickness and disease than it is for fruit to be diseased and worm-eaten before it ripens and falls to the ground.\n\nIf people were taught the true gospel and Christians could have their faith built up to a healthy state, we would see multiplied thousands of victorious deaths without so much pain and suffering. But the average Christian today is taught so many fallacies that they're full of arguments against this truth. There's so much unbelief and doubt about the truth that we rarely find such victory in death.\n\n## Christ Healed Before and After the Cross\n\nSome argue that healing isn't in the atonement because Christ healed people before He died on the cross. This reasoning is illogical and unscriptural.\n\nBy the same logic, we could argue that forgiveness of sins isn't in the atonement because Christ forgave sins before He died on the cross (Mt. 9:1-8; Lk. 7:47-50).\n\nAs we saw in Lesson Eighteen, Point IX, sins were forgiven and sicknesses were healed throughout the Old Testament. This was done by faith in the coming redemption of the cross, just as we're saved and healed today by faith in the atonement, looking back to the cross.\n\n## Christ Heals Today As Ever\n\nIf Christ were unwilling to heal today during His High Priesthood and exaltation as He did during His humiliation, His Word wouldn't be true. He promised that every believer could carry on His work throughout this entire age and do the same works He did (Jn. 14:12-15; Mt. 28:20; Mk. 16:15-20).\n\nThe doctrine of healing in the atonement makes necessary the continuation of the ministry of deliverance to all people who will believe. The atonement is for all people of all ages, and when people return to the true faith of the gospel, they'll get results just as the early Church did.\n\nWherever and whenever people have had revivals of primitive faith and apostolic simplicity, there we've seen New Testament power confirming God's Word to believers.\n\nAny person today who will take God at His Word can have every promise in the Bible confirmed in their own experience. There's no reasonable excuse for continued unbelief and constant argument about the plain truths of God's Word being for us today.\n\n## Why Bear in Our Bodies What Christ Has Borne For Us?\n\nIf Christ did bear our sins and sicknesses in His own body, then we don't have to bear them any longer. In fact, the whole purpose of Christ bearing them was to take them from us — not to enable us to sin and be sick.\n\nIn Isa. 53:5, 6, 11 we read:\n\n> "He was bruised for our iniquities... the Lord hath laid upon him the iniquity of us all... he shall bear their iniquities."\n\nThe Hebrew word for "iniquity" is *avon*, meaning "evil," "sin," and "punishment." The Lord laid on Christ the punishment of us all — and this literally means sickness and all results or punishments for sin.\n\nThis word is translated "punishment" in Gen. 4:13; Lev. 26:41, 43; 1 Sam. 28:10; Ezek. 14:10; Amos 1:3, 6, 9, 11, 13; 2:1, 4, 6; Job 19:29; Ps. 149:7.\n\nIf Christ had all sins laid upon Him, then He also had all punishments for sin laid upon Him. Therefore, all people can go free from the punishment for sin. Sickness is a punishment for sin, as is clear from Gen. 20; Exod. 15:26; Lev. 26; Deut. 28; Isa. 58; etc.\n\nIf Christ took all of our punishments, this certainly includes sickness.\n\nSo in view of Christ's atonement for our sins and sicknesses, the big question now is: "Why are Christians sick?" If they don't have to bear sickness — which Christ bore for them — why do they?\n\nThe simple reason is they haven't come to know the truth that will set them free. As long as Christians listen to fallacies that build up unbelief in the atonement and its benefits, they'll tolerate sickness in their lives.\n\nThere's no need for them to have sickness, and they wouldn't have it if they would let Christ heal them and take away their diseases.\n\nTo be technical about it, it becomes sinful to bear in our bodies those things Christ has already borne for us. It's sinful to rob Him of the glory that rightly belongs to Him in healing people. It's wrong to refuse to let Him deliver us.\n\nHis work on the cross means nothing to us as far as the body is concerned until we let Him heal us — just like His work on the cross means nothing to people as far as forgiveness is concerned as long as they continue in sin.\n\nIn other words, the part of Christ's work that we fail to appropriate to ourselves — that part of the atonement means nothing to us.\n\nShall we limit Christ's atonement and refuse to accept the full benefits of the cross? Shall we believe we can have part of the benefits for which Christ died but not all of them? Shall we say Christ died to deliver us from all the penalties and consequences of sin except one?\n\nShall we believe Christ's work is incomplete and that part of our penalty will be paid by someone else later? Shall we believe God desires only to redeem the soul and not the body?\n\nAway with such false concepts of the atonement! Jesus went to the cross with His whole spirit, soul, and body to redeem the spirits, souls, and bodies of all who are under the curse of the broken law.\n\nEvery kind of sin, pain, sickness, failure, and need was fully met by Christ on the cross. Paul declares that the believer is complete in Christ (Col. 2:10).\n\n## Christ Has Redeemed Us From the Curse of the Law\n\nPaul teaches:\n\n> "Christ hath redeemed us from the curse of the law, being made a curse for us: for it is written, Cursed is every one that hangeth on a tree: that the blessing of Abraham might come on the Gentiles through Jesus Christ; that we might receive the promise of the Spirit through faith" (Gal. 3:13-14).\n\nThe blessing of Abraham was being justified by faith, having God as a friend to the extent that a whole nation was healed by prayer (Gen. 20), and having all the good things of this life that could come to a human being through the gospel (Gal. 3:6-9).\n\nDidn't the curse of the law include sickness and disease as the result of sin? This is certainly clear from Exod. 15:26; Lev. 26; Deut. 28 and many other Scriptures.\n\nIf Christ died to redeem us from the curse of the law, then didn't He redeem us from all sin and sickness? This means no one should be sinful or sick if they have faith in Jesus Christ's redemption.\n\n## Many Diseases Named as the Curse of the Law\n\nMany specific diseases are mentioned as being the curse of the law:\n\n- "terror"\n- "consumption" (tuberculosis)\n- "burning ague" (fever) that shall consume the eyes and cause sorrow of heart (Lev. 26:14-17)\n- "pestilence"\n- "consumption"\n- "fever"\n- "inflammation" (malaria)\n- "extreme burning" (prickly heat)\n- "botch" (elephantiasis Graecorum or leprosy)\n- "emerods" (piles and tumors)\n- "scab" (eczema and other skin diseases)\n- "itch"\n- "madness" (insanity)\n- "blindness"\n- "astonishment of heart" (heart trouble)\n- "oppression" (mental worries and depressions)\n- "great plagues"\n- "sore sicknesses" of long continuance\n\nBesides these named, we read "all the diseases of Egypt, and every sickness and every plague not written in this book" (Deut. 28:15-69; Exod. 15:26).\n\nMany other physical diseases are mentioned in Scripture:\n\n- "atrophy of the hand" (Mt. 12:10-13)\n- "blains" (inflammatory abscesses, Exod. 9:9-10)\n- "blemish" (physical deformities such as blindness, lameness, broken bones, crookback, dwarfishness, eye trouble, scurvy, and other imperfections, Lev. 21:17-23)\n- "boils," "carbuncles," "ulcers," and "sores" (Exod. 9:9-10; Job 2:7; Rev. 16:2, 11; 2 Ki. 20:7)\n- "deafness" (Lev. 19:14; Mt. 11:5; Mk. 7:32; 9:25)\n- "dropsy" (Lk. 14:2)\n- "dysentery" (Acts 28:8)\n- "dyspepsia" (1 Tim. 5:23)\n- "epilepsy" (Mk. 9:17-22)\n- "running issues" (Lev. 15)\n- "gout" (2 Chron. 16:12)\n- "hemorrhage" (Lev. 15:19; Mk. 5:25-29)\n- "murrain" (Exod. 9:3-6)\n- "paralysis" (Mt. 8:6; 9:1-8)\n- "scall" (Lev. 13:30)\n- "scurvy" (Lev. 22:22)\n- "spermatorrhea" (Lev. 15:16)\n- "stammering" (Isa. 32:4; 33:19)\n- "meningitis" caused by a sun stroke (2 Ki. 4:19)\n- "worms" (Acts 12:23)\n- "melancholia" (1 Sam. 16:14-23; 17:58)\n- "cataract" (Jn. 9)\n- "locomotor ataxia" (Jn. 5:1-9)\n- "atrophy of the muscles" (Lk. 13:11-17)\n- "spasms" (Mt. 8:6; Lk. 7:2)\n- "infantile paralysis" (Mt. 12:9-13; Mk. 3:1-5; Lk. 6:6-11)\n\nThese and all other diseases were borne in Christ's body on the tree so that people might be free from them.\n\nSince disease is part of the curse, healing of the body is part of salvation, as we saw in Lesson Thirty-three. Since sickness is a penalty for breaking the law, how could God will for us to remain under the curse of the broken law after we're freed from law breaking?\n\nWhy should anyone remain under the curse who is not under the old law but under grace? (Rom. 6:14). The fact that God has healed people throughout the ages proves that healing is in the atonement and is part of salvation. How could these people have been healed if God didn't provide for it in redemption?\n\n## Men Are Commanded to Glorify God in Their Bodies\n\nPaul said:\n\n> "What? know ye not that your body is the temple of the Holy Ghost which is in you, which ye have of God, and ye are not your own? For ye are bought with a price: therefore glorify God in your body, and in your spirit, which are God's" (1 Cor. 6:19-20).\n\nHe further says:\n\n> "If any man defile the temple of God him shall God destroy; for the temple of God is holy, which temple ye are" (1 Cor. 3:16-17).\n\nThese Scriptures certainly teach holiness of body — and that means freedom from disease.\n\nShall we believe demons are free to defile God's temple without facing God's judgment? Shall we believe the body is to be holy even though it's full of disease and corruptions of all kinds? Shall we believe it's God's will for us to live in sickly, diseased bodies in preference to clean, healthy ones?\n\nShall we believe God is unconcerned and has made no provision for bodily holiness? Can we possibly imagine that someone can glorify God better with a helpless, diseased, sickly body than with a well and healthy one?\n\nPicture it this way: Normal people like living in clean houses and take pride in them. Are we then to believe the Holy Spirit is the only person content to live in a corrupt and diseased house, and that human beings have more pride and respect than God?\n\nWould you rather live in a clean, healthy house than one that's unclean, diseased, and unfit for occupancy — or is God the only person who prefers this?\n\nShall we believe that after God bought us with such a great price, He's then willing to turn us over to demons to occupy our bodies and destroy us with torment, pain, and all kinds of diseases?\n\nWould you buy a house to live in and then be willing to share it with drunkards, thieves, harlots, and drug addicts? Wouldn't you drive out such occupants if you could?\n\nShall we believe God has the power to make His temples clean and healthy, but He'd rather not because He delights to live in filth and disease?\n\nSurely the logic of such theories seems impossible to believe, yet multitudes of Christians do believe these things about God. It's high time we wake up, display some intelligence in religion, and free God from all such accusations.\n\n## Healing is Absolutely Sure\n\nIf what we've discussed is true — and it is according to the Bible — then for no other reason than that the Bible says so, we should believe we were healed on the cross by Christ's stripes.\n\nIt's true because God said so. It's just as true as the fact that we were saved on the cross.\n\nEveryone can get healed now — right now by faith — just as much as they can be forgiven of sins now. The reason everyone isn't healed is because they don't believe this truth and accept it the way they accept forgiveness of sins.\n\nJust as forgiveness is received by faith in the atonement when someone surrenders their life to God, healing is also received by faith in the atonement the moment someone decides to accept it and refuses to be denied what was freely provided on the cross.\n\nBoth forgiveness and healing were atoned for on the cross, but they're appropriated individually by faith when someone meets the necessary conditions of repentance and faith in the atonement. All of Hell cannot rob anyone of either blessing if they refuse to let demonic forces defeat them.\n\nWe conclude that all God's promises in Christ are yes and amen to those who believe (2 Cor. 1:20; 2 Pet. 1:4).\n\nLet's lay aside unbelief in the gospel and fully appropriate the benefits of redemption through Christ according to our needs. Let's learn this lesson well: God is true and will meet His own obligations to each person who conforms to the gospel of Christ.\n\n## Ten Questions For Supplement Twenty-four. Do You Know:\n\n**That the Ten Commandments have been done away with?** This is such an important question that we felt it should be brought to your attention again. So many false doctrines are based on the teachings of the Law of Moses, and today we have so many cults that mix law and grace, that it can't be emphasized too much: the whole Law of Moses, including the Ten Commandments, was done away with and abolished in Christ on the cross. This is plainly stated in 2 Cor. 3:6-15; Gal. 3:19-25; 4:21-31; 5:1-5, 18; Eph. 2:15; Col. 2:14-17; Acts 15:5-29; Heb. 7:11, 12, 22, 28; 8:6-18; 9:1, 9, 10, 15-22; 10:1-18. In Lesson Thirty-two we abundantly proved this point.\n\n**That the fig tree of Mt. 24:32-33 does not refer to the Jews?** This parable is a simple illustration of the nearness of Christ's second advent, as is clear in the passage itself. Therefore, it doesn't refer to the Jews at all (see Lesson Thirty-four).\n\n**That Jesus will have scars on His hands and feet throughout eternity?** That He still has a flesh and bone body was demonstrated to Thomas and all the disciples (Lk. 24:39). That such scars on His body are for the purpose of convincing people forever that He actually died on the cross is plainly stated in Zech. 13:6. The phrase "in that day" in this passage refers to the time of the Messiah's eternal reign that we'll study in the next few lessons.\n\n**That there has been a flood on the Earth worse than the flood of Noah?** This flood is recorded in Gen. 1:2; 2 Pet. 3:4-8. In this flood, all life on Earth was destroyed, as we saw in Lesson Seven. In Noah's flood, both people and animals, as well as all vegetation on Earth, were preserved. This proves the first flood was more severe and of longer duration than Noah's flood.\n\n**That the devil now has access to Heaven?** The old theory that Satan can't go to Heaven because sin isn't allowed there is untrue. In both Job and Revelation, we have divine teaching that the devil has access to Heaven. In Job 1:6 and 2:1 we read that Satan appeared twice with the sons of God in Heaven. In Rev. 12:7-12 we read that he'll be cast down to Earth in the middle of Daniel's Seventieth Week (the future tribulation), and he'll never again have access to Heaven. We also read in this passage that he's now the accuser of the brethren and accuses them before God day and night. This proves he still has access to Heaven and will continue to until the middle of the Tribulation — 3½ years before Christ's second coming to Earth.\n\n**That saints will not spend eternity in Heaven?** The old-fashioned idea that all the redeemed will spend eternity in Heaven isn't taught anywhere in Scripture. It's true that at death, all righteous souls go to Heaven awaiting the resurrection of the body at the rapture. Then these people will be caught up to meet the Lord in the air to be forever with Him, as we proved in Lessons Thirty-seven and Forty-three. It's also true that the raptured saints will return from Heaven to reign with Christ on Earth for the Millennium. During this time, while their home will be in Heaven, they'll go back and forth between Heaven and Earth throughout the 1,000 years (Rev. 1:4-6; 5:10). Then after the renovation of the heavens and Earth, the resurrected saints will come to live on Earth forever when their home, the\n\n---\n\nThe global systems that will empower the Antichrist don't appear suddenly — they're being built now. Not through conspiracy, but through the natural tendencies of human power structures away from God. So here's the question: How does your understanding of Revelation's political prophecies shape how you engage with current events, global governance, and international institutions? Not with fear — with informed, biblical discernment.	\N	\N	\N
cmnknk4br002bewhjhhb4rqmu	cmnknk4bp001qewhjk5bcqnfj	Supplement 25 — For Lessons 49 & 50	SUPPLEMENT	1880	20	\N	t	Armageddon ends with Christ's return and the Millennium begins. A thousand years of direct divine government — Christ on the throne of David in Jerusalem, Satan bound, the earth transformed. This final supplement brings the entire fifty-two-lesson course to its conclusion: where history is going, what it means for you, and how you should live today in light of it.\n\nIf you'll just believe the truth about these doctrines, you'll never have to fear demons or go without these benefits.\n\n![Supplement 25 — For Lessons 49 & 50](https://hm8qhte0o7.ufs.sh/f/tgGixBSSw8OskNbTeNS8rZcMaXYe27SivEmH1t8Ad0OozjUK)\n\n## Prosperity, Healing and Full Salvation for All\n\nIf these blessings are for one person, they're for everyone, because God is no respecter of persons (Rom. 2:11). Think about it—would it be fair of God to give these blessings to some but not to all?\n\nCould it really be possible that so many modern ministers are right when they teach that it's not God's will for everyone to receive these blessings? The minister quoted in the last supplement says, "The author does not limit the power of God, nor does he deny the fact that at times God does heal a Christian. We believe God heals whom He wills, when He wills, and how He wills... God does not always heal and raise up Christians who are sick."\n\nWhat kind of reasoning is this? How could God be just and be no respecter of persons if He only decides to manifest healing at certain times for certain people? How could you ever be sure you're living at that particular moment when God feels gracious enough to do what He's promised—what He promises to do for all and what Christ died to freely give to all?\n\nHow can you know if you're a special pet of God or if you belong to some exclusive class to whom God will "at times" feel merciful? Such reasoning and contradictions to the Scriptures aren't worthy of an intelligent person's consideration, to say the least.\n\n## We Live in the Times of God's Mercy\n\nThe times of God's mercy have stretched from the fall of man until now, and they'll continue until the end of the Millennium when people will never be sick again. In other words, as long as there's a sick person, God's mercy will be extended to them if they believe the gospel and have faith in God to heal them.\n\nGod's salvation and compassion cover every human need now and forever. We saw in Lesson Fourteen that God has healed people in all past ages—before and after the cross—and that God has never turned down one person who came to Him in faith according to the gospel. We've seen that in the New Testament, there's provision for God's blessings for body, soul, and spirit—provision for prosperity, forgiveness, healing, and deliverance from all the works of the devil.\n\n## God's Program is to Bless All Men Here and Now\n\nYes, it's true that God heals whom He wills, when He wills, and how He wills—but God's program is written out in black and white. Scripture plainly tells us that He wills to prosper, heal, and save all people here and now who will believe in Him.\n\nIt's revealed that the time is *now* that God wills to bless people. It's also made clear *how* He wills to help those in need. His program works through faith in the gospel, in the atonement and work of Christ on the cross, and in the resurrection from the dead.\n\nPaul said by the Holy Ghost, "He that spared not his own Son, but delivered him up for us all, how shall he not with him also freely give us all things" (Rom. 8:32). We can therefore boldly and scripturally declare that all things are ours here and now.\n\nWhat good would a gospel be that provided benefits for people in the past and future but left out those living now? The Bible proves that any religion teaching there are no benefits for people of the present age—but that such benefits were experienced in the past and will be enjoyed in the future—is from the devil himself. It's been the program of demons in every generation to rob people of the blessings God has provided for all alike.\n\n## The Great Hindrances to All Blessings of God\n\nThe great hindrance to any blessing of God is uncertainty in the mind and heart concerning God's will. Some people raise the question of whether or not it's God's will for them to be saved, to be healed, or to prosper in material things. Others question whether it's God's time to bless them. Still others question whether such blessings would even be best for them.\n\nAll such questioning and uncertainty will rob you of the benefits that are freely promised to all alike. How can you have faith if you're uncertain about God's will or if there's any wavering at all?\n\nGod has definitely told us that no one should expect an answer where there's wavering faith. This is stated in Jas. 1:4-8:\n\n> "If any man lack wisdom, let him ask of God, that giveth to all men liberally, and upbraideth not; and it shall be given him. But let him ask in faith, nothing wavering. For he that wavereth is like a wave of the sea driven with the wind and tossed. For let not that man think that he shall receive any thing of the Lord. A double minded man is unstable in all his ways."\n\n## What To Do To Stop All Wavering\n\nThe first thing to do is study the Bible and get settled on what God promises for all people today. If He's made promises that cover every specific human need, then you can rely on it without further questioning—it *is* the will of God to give you what He has promised.\n\nGet those particular promises that plainly state God's will in granting what you desire, then refuse to tolerate any wavering on the question. Don't listen to conflicting thoughts that oppose God's promises, and don't argue the case with people or devils.\n\nSettle it once and for all that it is God's will to grant what He has promised. Also settle it that any time you truly pray in faith, the answer will be granted. This kind of attitude naturally implies that you believe God has made such provision—otherwise He wouldn't have made these promises.\n\nIt's impossible to receive God's blessings by faith if you're not sure He has promised them. Likewise, it's impossible to fail to receive what you claim by faith if you don't waver. God is true. His Word is yea and amen to those who believe, and no one can fail to get what they've asked for when they stand in faith and refuse to waver or question God's will as revealed in His promises.\n\n## The Law of Faith Must Be Obeyed\n\nThe law of faith is laid out clearly in Scripture:\n\n- "As thou hast believed, so be it done unto thee" (Mt. 8:13)\n- "According to your faith be it unto you" (Mt. 9:27-29)\n- "O Woman, great is thy faith: be it unto thee even as thou wilt" (Mt. 15:21-28)\n- "And all things, whatsoever ye shall ask in prayer, believing, ye shall receive" (Mt. 21:21-22)\n- "If thou canst believe, all things are possible to him that believeth" (Mk. 9:23)\n- "What things soever ye desire, when ye pray, believe that ye receive them, and ye shall have them" (Mk. 11:22-24)\n- "He that cometh to God must believe that he is, and that he is a rewarder of them that diligently seek him" (Heb. 11:6)\n- "But let him ask in faith, nothing wavering" (Jas. 1:4-8)\n\nIt's an unchangeable law of God that you must believe exactly what you ask in order to receive it. It would be impossible for a sinner to be saved unless they first believe it's God's will to save them and that salvation takes place when they confess their sin (Rom. 10:9-10; 1 Jn. 1:9).\n\nIn the same way, it's impossible for you to be healed unless you believe it's God's will and that it's done when you pray. This applies to prayer for prosperity, health, or whatever else you ask of God.\n\nFaith must rest upon the will of God that is fully known through the Word of God. It can't rest on mere human desires and wishes or on physical senses. True faith that appropriates what is asked isn't believing that God *can*—it's believing that God *will*.\n\n## "If It Be Thy Will" Should Be Forgotten\n\nThe statement "If it be thy will" should never be used in prayer concerning anything God has already promised. If God has promised something, then it's already His will—otherwise He wouldn't have made the promise.\n\nTo ask Him if it's His will to do what He's already promised and made clear is to accuse Him of being a liar. In effect, we're telling God we know what He's promised but we don't believe He's true to His Word. We try to convince God that He should be true to His Word. This is all foolish, unnecessary, and sinful to say the least. God will do what He has promised. It's already His will to do what He says He'll do.\n\nThink of it like this: suppose you had promised something to someone, and that person constantly threw it in your face that they didn't believe you were true to your word—that they didn't expect what was promised. Suppose they went around to others asking them to pray that it would be *your* will to do what you'd already promised, and that you would make it plain you'd actually made the promise.\n\nWhat kind of feeling would you have toward such an unbelieving person who let you—and others—know they had no confidence in your word? What kind of reaction do you think God has toward those who constantly accuse Him in prayer of being untrue to His Word?\n\nYou'd probably stop all dealings with a person who had no faith in your word and character. And so it is with God, who promises He will not hear anyone under these circumstances (Heb. 11:6; Jas. 1:4-8; Mk. 11:22-24).\n\n## Will Men Die if God Always Heals Them?\n\nCertainly people must die their appointed deaths on Earth until the time of the rapture when this law that all must die will be broken (Heb. 9:27; 1 Cor. 15:51-58). God didn't promise to keep people from dying their appointed deaths—that would be breaking His own Word.\n\nBut He *has* promised to heal them and keep them in health until natural death (Exod. 15:26; Ps. 91:1-12; 103:1-4; Mt. 8:16-17; 21:21-22; Mk. 9:23; 11:22-24; Jn. 15:7, 16; Jas. 5:14-16; 3 Jn. 2). He has promised to take away sickness from us and that the number of our days He will fulfill if we meet certain conditions (Exod. 23:25-26; Ps. 90:10; 91:1-12; 102:24; 1 Pet. 3:10-12).\n\n## What is Included in Christ's Will and Testament?\n\nWe made clear in Lessons Ten and Twelve and in other supplements what's included in the will of God through Christ. We've seen that God has promised people all they want and need in this life as well as in the life to come. We've given many Scriptures proving that prosperity, healing, health, and all wants are included in God's will and testament.\n\nAll we have to do is read His will. It's similar to a wife who is in possession of her late husband's will but constantly argues, reasons, doubts, and questions what her husband left her—or whether he left her anything at all. Or worse still, after reading the will she worries all the time whether it was really meant for her, or whether he really desired and willed her to have what he left.\n\nYou'd say such a woman is very foolish indeed. But she's really no more foolish than the majority of Christians who have the plain terms of God's will in the Bible yet won't read and believe that the benefits of the gospel are for them. They hear people preach and read these lessons that God means what He says—that every promise of God is yea and amen to those who believe—yet it never seems to really dawn on them. They never get it firmly settled in their minds that everything promised in the Bible is for them.\n\n## No Excuse for Ignorance of God's Will\n\nThere's no excuse for ignorance about God's will since everyone can read in the Bible what God has left for them through Christ. If you're in doubt about what you can have, read the will of God for yourself.\n\nIf someone is left things of value by a will and doesn't want to get the benefits simply because they don't want to touch or use any part of what's left them—that's their fault, not the testator's. If they constantly whine and worry about getting something needed that's been left to them, just because they don't believe it was actually left to them, then let them go without the benefits and keep their mouth shut about being in need.\n\nSo it is with Christians. If they don't want the benefits they read about in the Bible because they think it would be asking amiss or it might not please God, then let them go without these blessings. There's no excuse for lacking anything in life, now or hereafter, for all things are freely promised and abundantly provided for all in the Bible.\n\n## Everyone Can Have All That is Promised\n\nSince God is no respecter of persons, everyone can have all that is promised if they'll have faith in God. If you can have any part of what's promised, you can have all God has promised.\n\nIf some of God's promises are untrue, then we have no assurance that any of them are true. If you can't get one part of the benefits provided in the gospel, then there's no grounds for assurance you can get other promised benefits.\n\nPaul said, "All things are yours... things present, or things to come; all are yours; and ye are Christ's; and Christ is God's" (1 Cor. 3:21-23). All things are ours that God has promised, for again Paul said, "all the promises of God in him are yea, and in him Amen, unto the glory of God by us" (2 Cor. 1:20). What could be clearer?\n\n## All Answers to Prayer Are for God's Glory\n\nNot only are we told that all things are ours, but we're told the reason—"unto the glory of God" (2 Cor. 1:20). This is the chief reason for answered prayer, whether it's for prosperity, healing, health, salvation from sin, or whatever it might be.\n\nJesus said, "And whatsoever ye shall ask in my name, that will I do, that the Father may be glorified in the Son" (Jn. 14:12-15). When prayer has any other object, it's selfish and useless.\n\nThis doesn't exclude meeting your needs—even your wants—to the full. It might seem at first that if we ask for what we want, it's selfish and not for God's glory. But that's not the case. We've already proven it's God's will for people to ask for what they want. This isn't selfish—it's godly and perfectly natural. It's according to the divine and spiritual plan for people to ask what they desire (Mk. 11:22-24; Jn. 14:12-15; 15:7, 16).\n\nWhen you do this, you glorify God because it gives Him a chance to show His power, vindicate His own character, and prove His love for people. It's the only way to confirm the Word of God.\n\nHow can God be glorified as long as His hands are tied by people's unbelief? How can He fulfill His Word to them when they don't believe and see God's glory? How can He be glorified when people make Him a liar concerning His promises? How can God prove His love when people refuse it by failing to cooperate with His plan of being glorified?\n\nNo prayer for any good thing is selfish, for it's to God's glory to grant all that is asked according to His promises.\n\n## What is God's Desire for Men?\n\nGod's desire is that people yield completely to Him and cooperate wholeheartedly with Him for the highest good of all beings and of the universe. It's God's highest will to give to all people liberally without rebuking them for asking any and everything they want for their good and His glory (Jas. 1:4-8; 3 Jn. 2; Jn. 14:12-15; 15:7, 16; 16:23-26; Mk. 9:23; 11:22-24).\n\nWhen you ask, you can't ask amiss or selfishly when you're consecrated to the same end God is consecrated to—the best good of all creation. Those who ask amiss are described as adulterers and adulteresses who seek to consume God's blessings upon their own lusts (Jas. 4:1-5).\n\nIt's impossible to ask amiss when you ask in faith, nothing wavering, for you'll only ask in harmony with God and His promises. This implies you've been "born-again" and are fully consecrated to God and to the best good of your own being and the society in which you live. (See God's plan for the needs of man in Lesson Ten.)\n\n## Who Has Been Authorized to Change God's Will?\n\nIt would seem from modern teachings about the Bible that someone has been authorized to change God's will. Or perhaps we could say everyone has been authorized to change it to suit themselves according to whatever church creed they want the Bible to conform to.\n\nIt's a universal idea that you can get any meaning you want out of the Bible or prove anything by the Scriptures. It's also widely accepted that people are free to interpret Scripture any way they choose.\n\nWho gave them this authorization? It certainly couldn't be that God has authorized people to change His word and make it meaningless through every theory we can manufacture to excuse our sin and unbelief. If it wasn't God, then it must be the work of the devil making people think they're free to change God's Word to suit themselves.\n\nIt thus becomes clear that demons have inspired such fallacies that lead people away from the plain truths of the Bible and make the Word of God of no effect—worse than the fables of men. When you believe any part of any doctrine that contradicts God's Word, you deny truth to that extent.\n\nGod has no part in cheating people out of what He's willed them through Christ. Christ is the mediator of the New Testament. He's our advocate—our lawyer—and He won't cheat anyone out of any part of the will as earthly lawyers sometimes do.\n\nSince the will has never been changed by God, all changes are made by demons and people who seek to break the will and keep the rightful heirs from their inheritance.\n\n## Both Testaments Promise the Same Blessings\n\nAs we saw in Lesson Thirty-two, the Law of Moses has been abolished, but this doesn't do away with the many blessings God has promised people in all ages before the cross. Before the law was given, God promised to bless all nations through Jesus Christ (Gen. 12:1-2).\n\nGod enlarged upon His program for humanity as He revealed more of His will to bless people when the law was given. In the five books of the Law of Moses, we have many promises of God's providence in every phase of human life (Exod. 15:26; 23:25-26; Deut. 5:16, 29, 33; 6:3, 18; 12:28; 19:13; 28:1-14; Lev. 26:1-13).\n\nIn other books of the Old Testament, God continues to promise people prosperity, healing, health, salvation, and even the desires of their heart (Josh. 1:5-9; 1 Sam. 2:7-8; 1 Ki. 2:3-4; 1 Chron. 29:12; Ezra 8:22; Ps. 1:1-3; 23:1; 34:10; 84:11; 91:1-12; 103:1-4).\n\nThe Law of Moses—or the Old Covenant recorded in Exodus, Leviticus, Numbers, and Deuteronomy—is the only part of the Old Testament's thirty-nine books that is abolished. The many truths in these books before and after the law was given that were given to people in general and that are taught in the New Testament are still applicable today.\n\nEven the truths of the law that were made part of the New Covenant are still in force. It's only those parts of the Old Covenant that weren't made part of the New Covenant that aren't in force today for Christians.\n\nSuch truths as faith in God, salvation from sin and bad habits, healing of the body, health, prosperity, answers to prayer, and those benefits God has made clear are for people of all ages—these are still for us today even though they were part of the Old Covenant. We don't get these benefits by obedience to the old law but by obedience to the New Covenant.\n\nAll these blessings have been brought into the New Covenant and made part of it. Scriptures in the Old Testament can be used to prove doctrines that are clear in the New Covenant. Even Scriptures recorded in the Law of Moses can be used to prove that it's always been God's will in every age to bless people with all these good things.\n\nIn the New Covenant, however, we have an abundance of proof that we can get all the universal blessings of God that are for people of all ages—past, present, and future. In fact, the glory and blessings of the New Covenant are so much greater than those of the Law of Moses that there's no comparison.\n\nThis is what Paul taught in 2 Cor. 3:6-15 when he said the glory of the Old Covenant had no glory compared to that of the New Covenant. So you can see that all the benefits God has promised in all ages can be experienced today in greater fullness because of the power and glory of the New Covenant.\n\n## We Have Exceeding Great and Precious Promises\n\nThis is what Peter taught in 2 Pet. 1:4:\n\n> "Whereby are given unto us exceeding great and precious promises: that by these ye might be partakers of the divine nature, having escaped the corruption that is in the world through lust."\n\nHe also said these promises give us "all things that pertain unto life and godliness" (2 Pet. 1:3-4). Such promises in the following passages reveal that nothing is impossible to any believer and that you can have whatever you desire (Mt. 6:31-33; 7:7-11; 21:21-22; Mk. 9:23; 11:22-24; Jn. 14:12-15; 15:7, 16; 16:23-26; 1 Jn. 3:21-22; 5:14-15; etc.).\n\nIt would be absolutely impossible for anyone to fail to get what they wanted in life that God has promised if they would take these promises and meet the conditions of their fulfillment. You can have full salvation from sin, sickness, poverty, and want by the gospel of Jesus Christ.\n\nBut you must accept these truths and get rid of all questioning, argument, unbelief, and doubt that such benefits are promised by God to any and all believers. Until this is settled, no aggressive faith is possible to attain these blessings.\n\nYou must freely and wholeheartedly accept these truths as God's will for you before such doubts can be erased from your mind. When this is settled and there remains no argument against this fact, then you're on sound footing to ask and receive.\n\n## Common Sense Must Be Used\n\nDon't expect that God will lay a million dollars at your feet the moment you pray, particularly if you're not being obedient to the laws of prosperity. God will begin to answer prayer and open up doors that will lead to prosperity.\n\nIf you do all you're supposed to do to get into God's will and be led by Him in business, and if you follow the laws we've given before, you'll soon see that these truths are practical and beneficial.\n\nIf you want salvation from sin, bad habits, and sickness, you can go immediately to God as we've explained before and receive immediate deliverance. In these things you must expect to do your part. You must renounce all sin and destroy all things that pertain to bad habits and have faith in God for deliverance before it will be realized.\n\nYou can't expect deliverance if you get up from prayer and start back into sin again. You can't start drinking, smoking, chewing, dancing, swearing, pleasure-going, or anything you've asked forgiveness and deliverance from and expect an answer. You must refuse to go back into sin if you want God to break the power of sin in your life.\n\nIt's not fair to God or to yourself to ask God to forgive and deliver you from the power of these sins and then go right on living in them when you get up from prayer.\n\nIt may be true that temptations will come at first. Such temptations will be weak or strong depending on your personal hatred and renunciation of the sinful habits and whether you mean business with God or not.\n\nGod won't supernaturally pick you up by the nape of the neck and keep you from going into any place of sin. He doesn't ordinarily knock the tobacco, whisky, or dope out of your hand if you're determined to continue in sin—unless by some special intervention to wake you up.\n\nGod can do all things, but we simply mean that in His ordinary dealings with people He demands cooperation to receive deliverance. When you're earnest and seek divine help to deliver you from these habits or to cleanse you from all sin, God will supernaturally deliver you from the power and dominion of sin.\n\nIn case of sickness, you must know that healing is for you and that God is your only help. You must turn to God with your whole heart and rebuke the disease and resist demons by faith in the name of Jesus Christ and His atonement.\n\nDon't tolerate sickness and disease in your body. Claim healing and full deliverance and maintain that it is done—and it will be manifested.\n\nIn other words, if you want God's salvation for any trouble in life, you must meet the plain conditions laid down in connection with the promises and you will get full deliverance. Let's all have faith in God and His Word and ask in simple, unwavering faith for those benefits of the gospel that are for us all.\n\n---\n\n## Ten Questions For Supplement Twenty-five. Do You Know:\n\n- **That there will not be a general judgment for both the righteous and the wicked?** As we saw in Lesson Thirty-eight, there will be a judgment for the righteous before the Millennium and one for the wicked after the Millennium. So contrary to common opinion, there won't be a general judgment for both classes at the same time and place. Only the wicked dead will be judged at the final judgment, as is clear in Rev. 20:7-15.\n\n- **That Jesus had brothers and sisters?** In Mt. 13:55 and Mk. 6:1-3 it's clear that He had both brothers and sisters. His own home town people that knew Him said, "Is not this the carpenter, the son of Mary, the brother of James, and Joses, and of Juda, and Simon? and are not all his sisters here with us?" He had at least four brothers (that is, half brothers). He also had more than two sisters, for if there were only two, the word "all" would have been "both." Jesus is called Mary's firstborn (Mt. 1:25; Lk. 2:7), which would imply she had other children. In Ps. 69:8-9 it's stated of the Messiah that He would be a stranger and an alien to his brethren, His "mother's children." That this passage refers to Christ is clear in Jn. 2:17. His brethren and mother came to him seeking to get him away from the crowds (Mt. 12:46-50). In Jn. 7:3-10 we read they showed lack of sympathy for His work. They later believed in Him and were greatly used of God (Acts 1:14; Jas. 1:1; Acts 12:17; 15:13-21; 21:18; Gal. 1:19; 2:9, 12; 1 Cor. 9:5). The natural meaning of "his brethren" never would have been questioned if it hadn't been for the doctrine of exalting Mary as the mother of God and to identify her with the great goddess of paganism, as we've seen in previous lessons and in my book Revelation Expounded.\n\n- **That the devil knows he will be defeated?** This is clear from Rev. 12:12: "the devil is come down unto you, having great wrath, because he knoweth that he hath but a short time." His defeat was predicted by God as far back as Gen. 3:15. From there on are many prophecies of his defeat. His doom is predicted in Rev. 20:1-10; Mt. 25:41. God always fulfills His Word, so it's clear that Satan knows his coming defeat and doom.\n\n- **That we are at the very end of this age?** As we saw in Lesson Forty-four, there are many signs now coming to pass that prove we're near the end of this age. Many prophecies are also coming to pass proving this fact. One of the most notable signs is the return of the Jews to their own land, as predicted by the prophets (Jer. 30:18-24; 31:1-40; 32:37-44; Ezek. 37; Hos. 2:4-5). This is now taking place and no one can dispute this fact. This and Mt. 24:37-39; 1 Tim. 4; 2 Tim. 3; 2 Pet. 3:1-3 and other passages prove we're living at the very end of this age.\n\n- **That Satan will be bound for 1,000 years and sin will still continue on the Earth?** It's hard for some to understand how sin will continue on the Earth during the time that Satan will be bound for the 1,000 years of Rev. 20:1-10, but this is easy to understand when we see that sin is transgression of the law and that people will still be free moral agents during that time. There will be laws to obey in the Millennium just as there are today, and people will become sinners by transgressing them just as they do today. That there will be sinners in the Millennium is clear from Isa. 65:20-25; 1 Cor. 15:24-28; Rev. 20:7-10; Zech. 14:16-21.\n\n- **That there is more than one paradise mentioned in Scripture?** One paradise is mentioned as the place where Christ and the penitent thief went, which was in the lower parts of the Earth (Lk. 23:43; Mt. 12:40; Eph. 4:7-11; Ps. 16:10). Another paradise is mentioned as being in the third Heaven (2 Cor. 12:1-3). Then the Garden of Eden is generally understood to be a paradise (Gen. 2:8-25; 3:1-19).\n\n- **That we shall know each other in both Heaven and Hell?** That we'll know each other in Heaven is generally understood and is proven in 1 Cor. 13:12; 15:34-58, but that people will know each other in Hell isn't so universally known. That people will look into eternal Hell is clear from Isa. 66:22-24; Rev. 14:9-11, and as people will be fully conscious forever, as proven in Lesson Thirty-nine, it's also clear they will know each other in Hell.\n\n- **That animals have souls and spirits?** As seen in Lesson Four, Point I, 9, the soul is that which feels and the spirit is that which knows. Animals have both feelings and knowledge, but on a lower scale than people. They weren't created in the likeness of God as people were. They're a lower type of life and have souls and spirits in the sense of feelings, emotions, appetites, desires, passions, and a low form of knowledge, as can be seen in many Scriptures in Lesson Six, Point IX, 2 and 3.\n\n- **That both Jews and Gentiles will live on the Earth forever?** In the next chapter we'll see many Scriptures proving eternal generations of natural peoples of both Jews and Gentiles. God created all people to live forever on the Earth. The Fall of Adam cut off all humanity from natural life on the Earth forever, but in the final restitution of all things, natural people will again live on the Earth as intended by God before the Fall. They'll be the subjects of the resurrected saints in the eternal kingdom forever. See Scriptures in Supplement Two, Question 2, for Scriptures proving eternal generations of natural people.\n\n- **That infant baptism is not taught in Scripture?** There's not one word that mentions any such practice. On the contrary, it's clear from Scripture that baptism comes only after people have repented and have been born-again by the Spirit of God, as we've seen in Lesson Twenty-nine, Point VIII. Infants aren't lost because they're not old enough to be responsible for their sins and therefore couldn't repent of known sins. Therefore, they can't be fit candidates for water baptism, which is a symbol of the death, burial and resurrection of Jesus Christ and a testimony to the world that the individual has been saved from a life of sin and is walking in newness of life. Only personal disciples and followers of Jesus Christ were commanded to be baptized (Mt. 28:19-20; Mk. 16:15-16; Acts 2:38-39).\n\n---\n\nThe entire arc of human history — from the original creation to the Millennium — is moving toward the moment when God's will is done on earth as it is in heaven. You're living in the penultimate chapter. The final chapter is already written. So here's the question that closes this entire course: Now that you've seen the whole plan — from eternity past to eternity future — what is one specific thing about how you're currently living that needs to change to align with the reality you've just studied? Not a theological adjustment. A life adjustment. What is it, and when will you make it?	\N	\N	\N
